ainst the odds.\par \par \uc2 I want to tell you about just one of those examples is in the rural area of Southwestern Mississippi. Jefferson County it`s called, home to just about 7,000 people, 85 percent of whom are Black, nearly 30 percent of residents live in poverty.\par \par \uc2 And the state of Mississippi as a whole routinely ranks at or near the bottom in all kinds of health care outcomes. So, when the COVID vaccines first became available early this year, uptake in Jefferson County was as you might expect, pretty slow.\par \par \uc2 Vaccinations in the county lag behind the state and then Mississippi lag behind the rest of the United States.\par \par \uc2 But then, there was a drastic change. Jefferson County`s vaccination rate surge, surpassing the state`s pace, it is now the most vaccinated county per capita in Mississippi. Nearly 80 percent of the eligible population receiving at least one dose. It`s among the highest vaccinated rural counties in the entire country.\par \par \uc2 So, how did Jefferson County do it?\par \par \uc2 Well, local health care providers went out into the community to meet people where they are, literally, physically. Healthcare workers from the Jefferson County Comprehensive Health Center took their mobile unit to town centers, apartment complexes, local churches, even a correctional facility.\par \par \uc2 The sheriff medical director estimate that 95 percent of the 270 inmates and more than 50 employees there have now been vaccinated. The mobile unit made multiple trips to Jefferson County High School.\par \par \uc2 According to the superintendent, approximately 94 percent of district staff are now fully vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 Medical Director of this effort Dr. Crystal Cook second from the right in this photo, also credits her relationships they built and the trust they earned with the community for their success.\par \par \uc2 [21:00:08]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It is a real triumph. Through sheer will and effort, the health care workers and leaders of Jefferson County were able to protect their community.\par \par \uc2 They should be held up as a great example of beating the odds and a reminder that good and difficult things are within our grasp with will and diligence.\par \par \uc2 That is ALL IN for this week. "THE RACHEL MADDOW SHOW" starts right now. Good evening, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020211211ehca00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for December 10, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Andrea Mitchell, Michael Beschloss, Garrett Haake,  Pete Williams, Barbara McQuade \par \uc2 7,971 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 December 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 REVEREND RANDOLPH HOLLERITH, DEAN OF WASHINGTON NATIONAL CATHEDRAL: -- Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit beyond you and remain with you and this world in which we live this day and forevermore, Amen.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Let us still forth in the name of Christ.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Thanks, be to God.\par \par \uc2 LEE GREENWOOD, SINGER-SONGWRITER, (SINGS "YOU`LL NEVER WALK ALONE"): When you walk through a storm, hold your head up high and don`t be afraid of the dark.\par \par \uc2 Because at the end of a storm, there`s a