bsidies.\par \par \uc2 But there`s so much money there that it will jumpstart in important ways the move towards renewable energy. It`s not a home run, but it`s probably enough to keep the game alive and send us into extra innings and maybe enough to salvage at least something out of this Glasgow conference.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Bill, that dynamic you`re talking about in terms of all the sticks, all the punitive or required actions are taken out and all the incentives are put in, mostly in the form of tax incentives, it did make me wonder if those initiatives, such as they are, might ultimately be more sustainable than more restrictive or punitive measures. Even if -- obviously, you`d want both. You`d want them to go further, but the kinds of things they did choose, because they are incentives to people who are designed to economically provoke more renewable energy and related technologies, those might be the sort of things that will be hard for Congress to get rid of or let lapse after a few years.\par \par \uc2 MCKIBBEN: Well, I think what you`re saying in a way is since our political system is run in a lot of ways by money, once solar power and wind power guys have enough money to buy their own congressmen, the process will be somewhat different than it is at the moment. We watched yesterday as \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  and his oversight committee tried to hold the CEOs of the oil industry to account. They were slippery and they dodged, but Katie Porter and AOC and people got in some good shots.\par \par \uc2 And what they`re seeing sort of in action is a re-jiggering of the balance of power here. Movements have grown strong enough that they have begun to challenge the previously unchallengeable political power of the fossil fuel industry and that`s getting stronger. The important thing today were these demonstrations around the world aimed at banks.\par \par \uc2 I was in Boston today with a bunch of colleagues from Third Act, this progressive organizing outfit for people over the age of 60. We were backing up the young people outside of Chase Bank, the biggest fossil fuel lender on Earth.\par \par \uc2 I think that message is starting to get through. The problem is time is extraordinarily short. The scientists have told us we have to cut emissions in half by 2030, which is eight years and a little loose change away.\par \par \uc2 This is the last probably push we`re going to get out of the Congress, if history is any guide. And so now we`re also going to have to take this fight not just to Washington but to Wall Street in a really serious way.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: \uc2 Bill McKibben\uc2 , climate expert, one of the founders of grassroots environmental group, \uc2 350.org\uc2 , Bill, safe travels, thank you for joining us tonight and good luck.\par \par \uc2 MCKIBBEN: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: We`ll be right back. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [21:52:13]\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: So Halloween is on Sunday. I decided to wear my costume tonight as a preview as you can see, going as a -- as a middle-aged childless lesbian wearing a $12 blazer. Spooky, right? Nailed it. Nailed it.\par \par \uc2 I love Halloween. I love my eternal costume. \uc2 Baboom\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But because I am in addition to being lazy about Halloween also a nerd, I also like to think of this time of year as oral argument season. October is the start of the Supreme Court`s new term each year. And on Monday morning we are going to get arguments at the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  on the Texas abortion ban which went into effect at the start of last month.\par \par \uc2 One challenge to the Texas abortion ban has been brought by a clinic in Texas called Whole Women`s Health and another challenge brought by the Biden administration, by the Justice Department. But the Supreme Court is going to hear arguments back-to-back Monday morning one at 10:00 a.m. eastern and the other at 11:00 a.m. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 Now, this is a really big deal, right? When the Supreme Court let the Texas abortion ban go into effect, it was the first time any state has been able to effectively ban any abortion since Roe versus Wade supposedly established a precedent that wouldn`t allow that back in 1973. These Monday arguments are going to be a really big deal.\par \par \uc2 And there are no cameras allowed inside the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 , but they do stream the audio of the hearings live on the Supreme Court website, which is very cool. So, on Monday morning, I`m just saying, it will be possible to plug in a pair of headphones and curl up with those oral arguments starting at 10:00 a.m. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 There`s two thing you should know if you`re going to do that on Monday morning. First the Texas abortion ban being challenged here quite directly violates a woman`s right to get an abortion as established by Roe versus Wade and subsequent Supreme Court decisions. That is not likely to be the direct legal question the Supreme Court grapples with on Monday. Instead they`re going to deal with the really unique and novel parts of the Texas abortion ban, specifically the vigilante part.\par \par \uc2 They wrote this law in such a way that the state of Texas is not directly in charge of enforcing it. Instead, individual private citizens are empowered to bring lawsuits against anybody who provides a woman with an abortion in Texas or helps her get one in any way. Texas says that because their state officials aren`t the ones enforcing the law that the state of Texas can`t be sued over it either, and in fact maybe no one can.\par \par \uc2 So that`s the legal question that judges are going to be considering Monday. The other thing to know ahead of the oral arguments is just how quickly we got here. The Supreme Court is not a fast moving entity. It is the slow loris of the judicial world.\par \par \uc2 [21:55:05]\par \par \uc2 It`s like it`s a horse and buggy but the horse is super old and has to keep stopping and taking notes. I mean from start to finish, it very often takes years before the Supreme Court, you know, takes up a case, hears oral arguments, gets anywhere near to making a ruling.\par \par \uc2 But in this case of the Biden administration suing over the Texas abortion ban, it took the Supreme Court ten days -- ten days to schedule oral arguments once they agreed to take up the case. That is unheard of. The legal scholars we have talked to about that say they have heard of almost zero cases in the modern world in which the Supreme Court has moved that quickly to prioritize a case. The last time anybody remembers the court moving this fast on a case was for Bush v. Gore in 2000.\par \par \uc2 But nevertheless, oral arguments on the Texas ban are going to start 10:00 a.m. Monday. Each side will get a half-hour to argue their case. If you`ve never listened to a Supreme Court case before you`ll be amazed how much everybody interrupts. The Texas clinic challenging the law will be up first. The Justice Department will go second at 11:00 a.m. I was going to say watch this space, but listen to this space live at the Supreme Court website Monday morning.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Just one last thing before we go this fine Friday night. We`re doing something a little bit different tonight here on MSNBC. Usually after me is "THE LAST WORD." But tonight, we`re doing something different. We`re airing this new super thought provoking acclaimed documentary which is called "Civil War or Who Do We Think We Are."\par \par \uc2 It`s really well-done. The executive producer is Brad Pitt, which is the Brad Pitt, which I think is a first for an MSNBC production. It`s called "Civil War, or Who Do We Think We are" and it starts right now. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document TRMADS0020211030ehat00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for October 29, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Garrett Haake, Ali Vitali, Kelly O`Donnell, Heidi Przybyla, Gary  Grumbach, Kathy Park, Guad Venegas, Ellison Barber \par \uc2 8,275 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 October 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, MSNBC HOST: If it`s Friday, as President Biden meets with world leaders abroad, divisions in his party have delayed again his agenda here at home. The good news for Democrats is that they all want a deal. The bad news is they still don`t have it nailed down.\par \par \uc2 And it comes as Democrats in Virginia could use a boost. It`s a true toss- up with just four days to Election Day. In a race that is being watched for much more than just who wins the governor`s mansion. We`re live on the ground there ahead.\par \par \uc2 And later firefighters, police officers, and other New York City workers have just hours to go before a vaccine mandate goes into effect or risk losing their jobs as the city prepares for a possible shortage of first responders.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY, I`m Garrett Haake.\par \par \uc2 A deal is an insight for Democrats that`s for sure. How much it`ll help the party politically, though, that`s a lot less sure. The good news for Democrats right now is that President Biden as he begins his trip abroad is nearing the finish line on nearly $3 trillion worth of agenda items here at home.\par \par \uc2 As the dust settles on a topsy-turvy mixed up last 24 hours, it`s clear that virtually every Democrat wants to get to yes on these deals.\par \par \uc2 Progressives endorsed the President`s reconciliation framework. Manchin says he`s OK with the size of it. Sinema is finally talking directly with House Democrats.\par \par \uc2 There`s a widespread agreement on the left that failure is not an option. And Biden reminded his party behind closed doors yesterday that their congressional majorities and his presidency are on the line.\par \par \uc2 But the bad news for Democrats is that there is no deal yet and that leaves Biden in Europe for a big climate change summit without congressional sign- off on his climate agenda.\par \par \uc2 It leaves the White House without a key victory at a moment where they badly need some wins as the President`s approval ratings continue to decline. And it leaves Democrats in Virginia twisting in the wind in a governor`s race where Republicans seem to have all the momentum.\par \par \uc2 A new Fox poll out of Virginia has Republican Glenn Youngkin up eight points. Eight points over Democrat Terry McAuliffe. That pulls an outlier.\par \par \uc2 But even if the poll`s off by 10, it`s still a toss-up race and toss-up is exactly where the new Washington Post-Schar SCHOOL poll has this contest. Folks, this is a dead heat.\par \par \uc2 And there`s also the political reality facing Democrats that passing a huge piece of legislation ahead of a President`s first midterm cycle isn`t always the political life preserver the party in power hopes it will be.\par \par \uc2 Democrats found that out the hard way after passing the Affordable Care Act. And then Republicans learned the same lesson all over again after signing Trump`s tax reforms into law.\par \par \uc2 In both cases, the party in power still got hammered hard in the following midterms. The bottom line here is Democrats have a lot of work ahead of them.\par \par \uc2 Joining me to break it down, NBC`s newly named Senior White House Correspondent, Kelly O`Donnell, is following President Biden in Rome. Congratulations, Kelly on that new title, from one senior to another. And my colleague, Ali Vitali is on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 And Ali, I`ll start with you. The President`s visit on Capitol Hill yesterday, obviously, did not create the surge of momentum that Democrats were necessarily looking for but it does seem like maybe it broke the dam.\par \par \uc2 Is the feeling now on the Hill that this is a matter of when not if that BBB framework passes as a Build Back Better law?\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Garrett. I think that`s the right read on the week that we`ve had here on the Hill. It`s not a matter of if but when. And the when is really a sliding target.\par \par \uc2 We`ve heard Progressives, even at the beginning of this week say that if this took a little bit longer than before the President went overseas, they were fine with that because they want to get it right.\par \par \uc2 What yesterday was, in some ways, was a redux of this argument that we`ve seen before, Progressives holding the line to maintain their leverage here on this larger Social Spending Package.\par \par \uc2 But it`s also a new era in terms of where these negotiations stand because although Progressives still held the line and prevented that Bipartisan Infrastructure vote yesterday, they also managed to show that we have a clearer picture of what`s in this bill than we`ve ever had before.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 VITALI: There is an actual framework here. What happens without an actual framework is sort of where this story is going to go next.\par \par \uc2 We`ve heard from Congressman Pramila Jayapal, for example, that dozens of members within her Caucus need more than just a text. They need assurances from the Senators on the other side of this equation to say that they will back this framework in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 When you talk about the hangover from 2010 in Obamacare, it`s less than Democrats in the House think that they`re going to be punished for what`s in this bill, and more for the fact that they don`t want to get left hung out to dry by the Senate.\par \par \uc2 So, that trust deficit is manifesting in the pause on the Bipartisan Infrastructure Bill actually coming to a vote in the House.\par \par \uc2 But what we`re probably going to see next week is more movement on this. And as one Senator -- as One Republic -- as one Democratic Congressman told me yesterday, as they were leaving, she has good vibes for November. Take that to mean what you will.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Frameworks aren`t laws, vibes aren`t frameworks, but it all kind of goes together here. So, Kelly O, the President -- our reporting is never really made the hard sell for a vote yesterday, and he didn`t get one.\par \par \uc2 What`s your reporting say about whether or not that was the plan? And what`s the White House is thinking? What`s their -- what are the vibes the White House is feeling as the President is in Europe now after what happened yesterday, and what didn`t happen on the Hill?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`DONNELL, MSNBC SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, I`m struck by today and yesterday, both having similar themes, faith and friction.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is counting on Democrats to come along, he didn`t put the hard clothes on the House Democrats, expecting they will, in fact, deliver for him, he didn`t get it in the timeline that he wanted.\par \par \uc2 The friction is there to be sure. He would have liked to have had more to show for it here in Rome. Faith demonstrated through his meeting with the Pope, perhaps that fulfilled a little bit of the spiritual deficit that he might have needed.\par \par \uc2 And there`s been friction in some of the meetings he`s had already with, like President Macron of France, and there are other meetings that he`ll be having. And does that lack of a deal back in the United States affect the meetings he will have going forward.\par \par \uc2 So, for the White House, what they have argued is that the world stage understands that what`s happening in Washington can be read by other world leaders.\par \par \uc2 Politicians know politics. They understand that there is a long lead here. it has been many months of negotiation. And that the Ark of this appears to be going in the President`s favor, even though it`s not locked in and that can be part of the negotiations here.\par \par \uc2 It certainly would have strengthened the President`s hand to have very specific, achievable things to bring as he tries to talk to other world leaders to get their buy-in.\par \par \uc2 So, as much as they are encouraged by this, it certainly would have been better for the President to have this done. Also, it would have freed up oxygen to move on to other topics when he returns to Washington and to free up what Democrats back home need as well.\par \par \uc2 So, in many ways, the President has to compartmentalize here. What is happening in DC needs to stay there as he focuses on new topics. Is there a drag on his momentum here? Perhaps, that`s something we`re watching very closely, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Kelly, thanks. Yes, I think you know, Terry McAuliffe, across the river in Virginia, may be the person most upset about the timing of how this is all played out. We`ll talk about that later in the show.\par \par \uc2 Ali, I got to ask you about some retirement news out of the House today. Adam Kinzinger, one of the two Republicans on the January 6 Committee, one of just a handful of outspoken critics left in the Republican party when it comes to former President Trump said he`s not running for reelection. And he had some pretty poignant words in the video announcing that decision. Let`s listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM KINZINGER, (R-IL): Because in this day, to prevail or survive, you must belong to a tribe. Our political parties only survive by appealing to the most motivated and the most extreme elements within it. And the price tag to Power has skyrocketed. Dehumanizing each other has become the norm. We`ve taken it from social media to the streets.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: You know, one thing Kinzinger didn`t talk about in this video is the redistricting in Illinois put him in a really tough spot, had he chosen to run for reelection.\par \par \uc2 But what do you think his retirement means for this Congress overall and for the potential next Congress, which will look a lot Trump year no matter how you slice it?\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Well. look a lot Trump year because while Kinzinger was one of the casualties of Illinois redistricting, which is controlled by Democrats, there are plenty of other states across the country that are controlled by Republicans in this process, and they`re doing the opposite of what`s happening in Illinois, which is bolstering their Republican district.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 VITALI: So, there are implications to the larger redistricting conversation that are important for the next Congress, and the Congress after that, and the Congress after that. It`s shoring up for both political parties that they can keep the seats and then continue to keep winning.\par \par \uc2 For Kinzinger, though it`s also part of the conversation about what it means to be a Republican in the post-Trump era and it`s hard not to read this in any other way other than he`s the second of the 10 Republicans who voted to impeach the President for his role in the January 6 insurrection, he`s the second of those Republicans to resign his seat and retire, the first of them, Anthony Gonzalez in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right, Kelly and Ali, thank you both.\par \par \uc2 And joining me now is one of the 50 Senators who will have to agree to that White House Build Back Better framework. It`s Massachusetts Democratic Senator Ed Markey.\par \par \uc2 So Senator, first of all, what are your top-line thoughts on this framework? A lot of stuff to like in this bill if you`re a Democrat but some priorities are left out. Are you -- are you a yes on this if this framework could be voted on today?\par \par \uc2 SEN. ED MARKEY, (D-MA): Well, I support the framework. I think it embodies the goals of the Build Back Better program of Joe Biden.\par \par \uc2 But at the same time, on immigration, on family leave, on prescription drug pricing, we have to continue to advocate until this final text, actual language to fill in the framework is completed.\par \par \uc2 So, the framework is actually excellent but it can be improved, and we`re going to continue to advocate for that improvement until that bill finally reaches Joe Biden`s desk.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: One of your colleagues who we`ve all been puzzling over the last couple of weeks seems to want more work on this bill, too. That`s Joe Manchin. He tweeted yesterday.\par \par \uc2 As we work through the text of this legislation, I would hope we`ll all continue to deal in good faith and do what`s right for the future of the American people.\par \par \uc2 President Biden wants Democrats to trust that Joe Manchin and Kyrsten Sinema are on board for this framework. Do you believe Joe Manchin and Kyrsten Sinema are on board for this framework?\par \par \uc2 MARKEY: Well, again, the framework is an outline. It`s like the frame of a house. But you have to fill in the rest of the house before it is completed.\par \par \uc2 That`s what we`re in the process of doing, that`s what the actual legislative text is all about, that`s what Joe Manchin is talking about looking at the text.\par \par \uc2 So, we have to continue to work with Senator Manchin in good faith towards the goal of completing the actual language, remembering that the devil and the angels are in the text.\par \par \uc2 And so, we have to understand fully what is going to be included and continue to negotiate both with Senator Manchin and with Senator Sinema.\par \par \uc2 But I think we can do it. I think that we have a real pathway now to completing this legislation. We just have to work out the remaining details. And I think that a big historic success is potentially within our grasp within the next couple of weeks.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: How should we, in the rest of the political world, look at what happened yesterday? How important was this marker from the White House, even if it`s not a bill to say this is what we believe will pass?\par \par \uc2 MARKEY: Well, it was historic. For example, you know, I`m very concerned about climate change. The legislation has $555 billion to deal with the climate crisis.\par \par \uc2 And again, young people all across our country rose up in 2020 as an incredible, political, powerful force. And this bill vindicates all of their efforts.\par \par \uc2 10-year tax breaks for all clean energy technologies, a civilian climate cooler that I`ve been advocating for is in there that will employ hundreds of thousands of young people in our country of a climate bank that I`ve been fighting for, that will unleash $200 billion of additional investment in clean energy technologies. So, on climate, on so many other issues, this bill is historic.\par \par \uc2 And compared to anything we`ve ever done in our history, really, you have to go back to the -- to the New Deal and Franklin Roosevelt`s era to find anything that`s comparable.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: So, this passes your much-repeated no climate no-deal test?\par \par \uc2 MARKEY: Yes. When President Biden is talking to other world leaders in Glasgow next week, he`ll be able to say confidently, that the combination of what`s in the framework of this bill and what he can do in the executive branch, increasing fuel economy standards for the vehicles that we drive, for example, that the United States will reduce its greenhouse gases by 50 percent by the year of 2030.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Senator.\par \par \uc2 MARKEY: And that`s the kind of -- that`s the kind of negotiating leverage that will help him with other countries in the world.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And Senator, you wrote this morning that traveling to Glasgow without a sealed deal on climate action would be an abdication of our responsibility to the next generation, a generation that`s taking to the streets and countries around the world to demand a Livable Future. You don`t have that sealed deal is this an abdication?\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 MARKEY: Well, I do believe that President Biden is completely committed to all of the provisions in this bill. And so I think, to the extent to which President Biden needs to have something that he can point to with the leaders in the rest of the world, what we now have is, in fact, all that he`s going to need to deliver.\par \par \uc2 A very powerful message that the United States is back as a leader and not a laggard, that we`re not preaching the temperance form of our school. We`re going to be doing it ourselves. And we`re going to expect the rest of the world to follow our leadership.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right, Senator Markey has been a leading voice on these climate issues. Thanks for coming on and talking about it with us today.\par \par \uc2 MARKEY: Glad to be on. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And up next, we`re on the ground in Virginia with just four days ago before an election, that will be a key indicator about which way the political winds are blowing in this country. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. The lack of a done deal on Capitol Hill this week could have some ripple effects across the Potomac in Virginia where voters will decide between Terry McAuliffe and Glenn Youngkin as their next governor four days from now.\par \par \uc2 As we showed you earlier, two new polls illustrate just how precarious that race is for Democrats in a state Joe Biden won by 10.\par \par \uc2 A Fox News poll of likely voters has the Republican, Youngkin, ahead of the Democrat McAuliffe by eight. And a Washington Post poll has McAuliffe up by just one point.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Heidi Przybyla joins me now from Richmond, and Gary Grumbach is in Charlottesville both covering this race.\par \par \uc2 So, Heidi, forget whether or not there has been a vote on Biden`s agenda. How frustrated, how engaged our voters there about the reconciliation framework, that Build Back Better agenda we were just talking about?\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HEIDI PRZYBYLA, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Look, Garrett, there is no question that the women here in Virginia, the same sort of suburban women who vaulted Democrats to power in 2017, today, are much more fatigued.\par \par \uc2 They say they care just as passionately about the issues but they don`t see Washington getting the job done. They sat down with a cross-section of these women, actually. And here`s a little bit of what they had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY MANNELL, VIRGINIA VOTER: Gun control is a huge issue that I`m really passionate about, that I have really strongly.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: For me, the biggest issues are public education and gun safety laws. My kids go to public school, I attended public school, my mom taught for 30 years in public school. It`s important. And most kids in Virginia go to public school, so it`s something we really need to invest in.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`ll be voting for Glenn Youngkin on November 2, and education is the top issue for why I`m voting the way that I am.\par \par \uc2 I think that virtual schools didn`t do any kids any favors. I think that it`s really important for, in school, education to be happening for -- quality education to be happening.\par \par \uc2 I don`t think that critical race theory has any place in our public schools.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I grew up in Texas, and a lot of the things that are going down in Texas and some of the other southern states, really concern me. Voting rights, women`s, productive -- reproductive rights, those things really concern me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PRZYBYLA: So Garrett, Democrats do have a pretty big voter registration advantage here. But the question is, with President Trump no longer in power, that was a big referendum for them in 2017 when they waited in those long lines, are they going to do that again this time? Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right, there was the first opportunity for so many frustrated Democrats to come out and vote anywhere against Donald Trump in some capacity.\par \par \uc2 Gary Grumbach, the Youngkin campaign is trying to walk a bit of a tightrope when it comes to Trump too. Keep him a little bit at arm`s length, but use him in some ways to fire up their supporters.\par \par \uc2 What`s their feeling as they look at these polls that have them -- I have to think exactly where they want to be in the closing days of this campaign?\par \par \uc2 GARY GRUMBACH, MSNBC REPORTER: Yes, it really is a fine line and Glenn Youngkin is walking that fine line between being conservative and Republican, but not too conservative, or too Republican.\par \par \uc2 And of course, in Virginia --in the Commonwealth of Virginia, you can`t win the election of Governor by just getting the bluest of the blues or the reddest of the reds, you really need the people in the middle.\par \par \uc2 And the issue, when I talk to voters, that Republicans are saying that they care the most about more than the economy, more than the environment, more than the Second Amendment, is education, and namely, critical race theory.\par \par \uc2 It really has become one issue of race when it comes to the Republican side of critical race theory. And that is, I think, something that`s really interesting.\par \par \uc2 Another point that I`ve noticed from voters is the idea that many of them, Republicans, they`ve already voted. There`s 45 days of early voting here in the Commonwealth.\par \par \uc2 And unlike in 2020, when you had national Republicans saying, don`t early vote, vote on election day, you`ve got Glenn Youngkin, in adventurer strapped up just behind me saying get out and vote today. Go to the polls and make that happen.\par \par \uc2 He`s also suggesting that his supporters go and register and volunteer to become poll watchers to make sure that the election runs smoothly, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Forget just 2020. Every election I`ve ever covered, the rule was always the Democrats voted early Republicans didn`t but it kind of pandemic has changed that. And I think a lot of ways and it`s going to be an interesting dynamic to follow.\par \par \uc2 Heidi, with a little bit of time we got left here. Does the McAuliffe campaign feel hung out to dry by Democrats on this side of the Potomac that they didn`t get something more to help close -- you know, a closing message in the last couple of days? What are they trying to close with now?\par \par \uc2 PRZYBYLA: Well, look, there`s no question that they would have preferred to have had this deal. No question. However, the Democrats are doing what they can within their power.\par \par \uc2 They`re bringing in all of the heavy hitters like President Obama, both of the Biden`s, the House Majority Whip. Clyburn will be here this weekend. And they say at this point, it really is about turnout.\par \par \uc2 Look, Democrats have a 450,000 voter registration advantage here and they`re hoping that those suburban women among other blocks that turned out big time for them in 2017 are still going to be passionate about the issues, especially when you see what`s happening.\par \par \uc2 For instance, it`s Texas with women`s reproductive health, they look at the way COVID is being handled and some of these other Republican-run states, Garrett, and they don`t want that to happen here.\par \par \uc2 So, even though they say they`re fatigued, every single one of them who I talked to said, I`m tired but yes, I`m going to vote.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right, fascinating stuff. It`s going to be an interesting one to watch. Heidi and Gary, thank you both.\par \par \uc2 And be sure to tune into our Meet the Press election night special hosted by Chuck and Kristen Welker. That`s going to be Tuesday beginning at 9:00 Eastern streaming on our streaming channel, NBC News Now.\par \par \uc2 And turning out from the race to be Virginia`s next governor to the fate of New York`s former Governor, Andrew Cuomo, is now facing a criminal charge for allegedly touching a woman inappropriately. Cuomo has been charged with forcible touching according to the documents filed in Albany City Court yesterday.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: That`s a misdemeanor punishable by up to a year in jail or three years of probation. He`s scheduled to appear in court next month.\par \par \uc2 According to the complaint, the former governor touched the alleged victim at the governor`s mansion last December. The Albany County Sheriff filed the complaint but the County District Attorney whose office would actually have to prosecute the case said he was surprised to learn what the Sheriff`s Office`s done.\par \par \uc2 Cuomo`s attorney says the former governor never assaulted anyone and that this confusion between the Sheriff and the DA proves the case against the governor is purely political. The complaint does not identify the victim.\par \par \uc2 It was filed more than two months after New York Attorney General Letitia James filed a 165-page report detailing assault or harassment allegations against Cuomo by 11 different women.\par \par \uc2 Cuomo deny many of the allegations laid out in that report but resigned a week later amid pressure from Democrats, including President Biden.\par \par \uc2 And up next, a showdown over vaccine mandates in New York City where police officers, firefighters, and other city workers have just hours to prove they`ve gotten their first vaccine shot or risk losing their jobs. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. In just a few hours from now, New York City`s vaccine mandate will go into effect for the City`s entire workforce.\par \par \uc2 Mayor Bill de Blasio announced last week that the city`s roughly 160,000 workers would have to get their first vaccine dose by 5:00 today or be placed on unpaid leave starting on Monday.\par \par \uc2 [13:30:08]\par \par \uc2 Tens of thousands of employees from agencies including the FDNY and the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  remain unvaccinated. And at the Department of Sanitation where vaccination rates are some of the lowest, trash collections have already slowed down ahead of today`s deadline, leading to heaping garbage pileups across the city.\par \par \uc2 All of this raises questions over whether the Big Apple will soon be facing a big staffing crisis and what one would actually look like.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now with the latest from New York is NBC`s Kathy Park.\par \par \uc2 So, Kathy, what are the city department leaders saying about the mandate and what the city might look like or smell like come Monday?\par \par \uc2 KATHY PARK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Garrett. Well, I will begin with Mayor Bill de Blasio. He`s actually hopeful that more people will get vaccinated in the next couple of hours because as you mentioned that mandate is coming up. It`s at 5:00 p.m. tonight where people have to get at least one dose of the vaccine, and the reality is, if they don`t, they won`t be getting a paycheck come Monday morning.\par \par \uc2 And we have received some new numbers. It appears that the numbers are trending upward as far as vaccination numbers go. More than 12,600 people have received at least that one dose of the vaccine ever since that mandate was announced. And -- but the reality is that 33,000 city workers still have not been vaccinated and that is a big concern. Right? Thousands of emergency responders including officers as well as firefighters remain unvaccinated.\par \par \uc2 And yesterday we saw hundreds of firefighters and their supporters outside the mayor`s residence. They`re outraged, they`re saying they`re not against a vaccine. However, they are against the mandate. And they said the reality is we could face some staffing issues come Monday morning. Take a listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDREW ANSBRO, UFA PRESIDENT: I`ve been saying that on November 1st there`s going to be a crisis in this city. If 30 percent to 40 percent of city firefighters are sent home, we will have to close houses. This is going to be a manufactured crisis. 30 percent to 40 percent of the police department will be sent home.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PARK: Now the city has said that there are contingency plans in place in case there are any sort of staffing issues, so there will be mandatory overtime as well as extra shifts to fill in any sort of gap. So the mayor has assured New Yorkers that the city will be safe and he believes, like I said, the next couple of hours things will turn around with more people getting that first dose -- Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Big test on mandates coming up in New York. Kathy Park, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, vaccines for younger kids are almost here. A top pediatrician joins us next to talk about who can get the jab and when. Plus questions of safety and side effects.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:36:03]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. By this time next week 28 million children between the ages of 5 and 11 could be eligible for Pfizer`s pediatric vaccine. On Tuesday the CDC`s Advisory Committee will begin a two-day meeting to review data and ultimate vote on whether the shots should be recommended and for who. This comes after the FDA`s Independent Advisory Committee found that the benefits of vaccinating kids outweighed the risks earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 The FDA is expected to make a decision on Emergency Use Authorization in the coming days prior to the CDC`s vote. So if it`s approved, Pfizer`s shot would be the first COVID vaccine to hit the market for younger children.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now to discuss this is the president of the American Academy of Pediatrics, Dr. Lee Savio Beers.\par \par \uc2 And, Doc, what will you be watching for in next week`s CDC meetings? Obviously there`s a lot of debate around the FDA committee vote. Do you think there`s going to be enough data to support widespread vaccination kids 5 to 11?\par \par \uc2 DR. LEE SAVIO BEERS, PRESIDENT, \uc2 AMERICAN ACADEMY OF PEDIATRICS\uc2 : Right. Thank you so much and thank you for having me. You know, I think absolutely we`ll see what the discussion is, but I think looking at the findings of the FDA scientists and the discussion from their advisory meeting, I think we all feel incredibly encouraged that this is a safe and effective vaccine, and for which the benefits really do outweigh the risk.\par \par \uc2 And so we`re really looking forward to next week to hear what they say. But I think if things go as we expect, we should have a vaccine very soon that`s recommended for younger children.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Kids are the largest block left of unvaccinated people in many places. How big of an impact do you think pediatric vaccine could have on the pandemic overall across this country?\par \par \uc2 BEERS: Yes. I mean, your point is very good. Actually right now about a quarter of the new infections with COVID-19 are in children. So certainly it could have a big impact on ending the pandemic, but I would also say there are, you know, tremendous benefits to individual children and their families, and I think, you know, as parents that`s often what you want to know. You want to see the pandemic end, but you also want to know that this is a benefit for your child. And I think that`s what the data showed us.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Talk to me about what we could see potentially in schools here. I mean, if you`ve got first shots start going into arms next week, are we looking at a potentially dramatically different situation in terms of, you know, masking and how schools are conducting their business by the spring semester?\par \par \uc2 BEERS: Yes. I mean, certainly that`s the hope. Right? You know, but I think the next step is to make sure that parents are able to have their questions answered, that they have access to the vaccine. You know, we know there are a lot of parents who are really eager to get this vaccine and there are some who still have questions. And so when we look at the vaccine and take for adolescents, you know, about half of adolescents have gotten the vaccine. And so we still have work to do there.\par \par \uc2 So right now I think one thing that`s just incredibly important so that we can get to that place in the schools is to make sure we`re making the vaccine accessible and available for everyone when it`s authorized, and that we`re providing that education and opportunities to have your questions answered.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I`m glad you brought up that idea about half. There`s a recent poll that said only about a third of parents said they`d get their kids vaccinated right away. Do you see any role for mandates like what we were just talking about in our last segment when it comes to vaccinating children, or is that too much of kind of a third rail especially for Emergency Use Authorization approved drug?\par \par \uc2 BEERS: You know, I think certainly this question comes up a lot, and the way we`ve been thinking about this is really right now we want to focus on answering parents` questions because that`s what we are hearing from the surveys is that there`s a lot of information out there. Some of it`s good. Some of it`s not so good. And, you know, what`s really important is to provide those opportunities for parents to have their questions answered so that they can make those decisions for their kids to -- so that they can be safe and protected.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:07]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: One of the interesting things that I thought came out in this FDA meeting, the CDC`s data said something like 42 percent of kids between 5 and 11 had some kind of immunity, essentially could have already been exposed or had COVID. Does that number surprise you?\par \par \uc2 BEERS: Yes. It surprises me a little bit, but maybe not. I think, you know, when we think about the arc of the pandemic, particularly early on where testing wasn`t as available and, you know, and we know that overall, this COVID can be very serious in children, but for many children, it is milder. So they may not have come in to be tested, and so I think, you know, it`s very possible that that`s true, but I think it`s also really important to emphasize that, you know, regardless of that, you know, we still absolutely recommend the vaccine for -- you know, for anyone who is eligible for it.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right. Doctor Lee Savio Beers, thank you. This is going to be a moment of such relief for so many parents, I think, if and when that decision is made next week.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we go county to county as we continue this week`s kickoff to a year-long reporting project here at "MEET THE PRESS" and at NBC News. From evangelical voters in the southeast to Latino voters out west, what`s driving the political debate on the ground ahead of the midterms? That`s next.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS daily.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:45:09]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. This week we launched the 2022 edition of our county- to-county project. We`re going to be keeping a close eye on seven counties in seven states. Each to help us take the pulse of a key part of the American electorate. Today we`re going to zero in on two of those counties and two of those key demographics.\par \par \uc2 First evangelicals in Chattooga County, Georgia, a deep red county that`s part of Marjorie Taylor Greene`s district that went overwhelmingly for Donald Trump, and Latinos in Washoe County, Nevada, a swing county that`s recently broken for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Guad Venegas is in Washoe County and Ellison Barber is in Chattooga County, Georgia. They`ve both got a look into how these voters view the infrastructure talks that have consumed all of us here in Washington.\par \par \uc2 So, Guad, to you first. What issues are important to these Washoe voters and how are they reacting to what`s been going on here in Washington?\par \par \uc2 GUAD VENEGAS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Garrett, it`s important to understand what Washoe County is like. This used to be a red county and then a lot of people moved here from California. You have people taking jobs. It`s turned a little more blue but it`s still considered a purple county. One-third Republican, one-third Democrat, one-third nonpartisan or other. But then also, when you analyze the fact that almost 16 -- almost 16 percent of eligible voters here are Latino, this is a county that is up for grabs.\par \par \uc2 We`ve been speaking to some of the voters from those that support the current administration to those that are not happy with what is happening in Washington. This is what they had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM DAIN, WASHOE COUNTY BUSINESS OWNER: So much crap into it, but yes, I don`t think we should -- we`ve been doing infrastructure, start repairing some freaking power lines and stuff. Do what we do in other countries. We want to really make something happen, not any of the social stuff.\par \par \uc2 LINO ORTEGA, EL MUNO LATINO OWNER: (Speaking in Foreign Language)\par \par \uc2 VENEGAS: So he says all Hispanics want immigration reform. So that was the most important issue for him. He`s waiting for immigration reform, and that`s the reason why he selected the candidate that he chose.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VENEGAS: Now when you look at what`s happening in Washington right now, right, you have this infrastructure bill that they`re trying to put it through. And then some Democrats continue to push immigration reform in that bill even though it`s very difficult, and this voter is telling us exactly what many of the Hispanic voters here in Washington and other parts of the country are saying, that immigration continues to be one of the main issues on the top of their mind. And I showed that, that in all of Washoe, housing and the economy are also very important to voters. Garrett?\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Yes. I can`t tell you how many campaign events I`ve covered in Reno or in Sparks, somewhere out in Washoe County is a fast-changing area.\par \par \uc2 Ellison, Chattooga voters are obviously very different from what Guad`s seeing in Washoe. What are you hearing about the priorities there? What do they think about what`s going on with these negotiations in Washington?\par \par \uc2 ELLISON BARBER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, I mean, this county is about as red as you can get. President -- former president Donald Trump won this county last cycle with 80 percent of the vote. And that was while the population was declining here. Whenever he was on the ballot, even though the population was declining, voter turnout went up. So when we say this is a red county, we really mean it. And when you ask people here to summarize the political beliefs of people in this community, you tend to hear them say two words. God and freedom.\par \par \uc2 They hate the idea of socialism here, and they genuinely believe and fear that this country is going to become a socialist or communist nation similar to China or Cuba, and for them, the things happening in Washington, the discussions, the cost of an infrastructure deal of the latest spending proposal, that plays into that fear. Listen to some of what we`ve heard.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARBER: Somebody was telling you they really don`t -- they really feel like a Venezuelan like country is not far off here. I think a lot of people will hear that and say that`s absurd.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOSELEY, CHATTOOGA COUNTY, GEORGIA VOTER: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 BARBER: What do you think and do you get the sense here that there really is a genuine concern that this could become --\par \par \uc2 MOSELEY: Yes. If you look just a few years ago, we were talking about passing bills that was multibillion dollar and people was going out of their mind going, I can`t believe how this big -- how large this bill is. And now I think today they came out with another one, we`ll just cap it at $1.75 trillion.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARBER: So, Garrett, we`re not in this county because we think it could potentially flip and turn blue or even purple. We know that this county is going to stay red. But in a newly purple state like Georgia, if Republicans want to do well in the midterms and take back that Senate seat they lost, they have to see voters turn out in communities like this. There`s been some discussion around this idea that the former president`s false attacks on the election claiming that it was rigged, that that could impact his supporters and actually hurt turnout for the Republican Party in the upcoming election.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:12]\par \par \uc2 When I talk to people in this county, they will tell us that they do not trust the election process. But every single person we`ve spoken to they still say they plan to vote. And two big issues driving that are their opposition to abortion, this is a very evangelical community, and then their fears that this country is headed in a direction towards socialism which is something they do not support, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right, Ellison and Guad, in two states that are going to have big Senate races this cycle. One under the radar, one very much not. Thank you both.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, an audience at the Vatican unlike any before. The Pope and the president. A meeting that`s just as personal for Biden as it is political.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. President Biden kicked off his foreign trip today with a trip to the Vatican and an audience with Pope Francis. Today`s one-on-one lasted nearly 90 minutes and according to the Vatican readout, the two discussed far-reaching global issues from poverty to refugees to the COVID- 19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 For President Biden, only the second Catholic president, today`s meeting with Pope Francis wasn`t just political. It was clearly personal, too. In the Senate, on the campaign trail, and in the White House, Biden has really leaned into his faith even as his views on abortion put him at odds with some Catholic leaders here in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:10]\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is "Politico`s" senior staff writer Ruby Cramer. She`s written extensively about the president and his faith.\par \par \uc2 So, Ruby, you`ve reported on the president`s clash with the Catholic Church in the U.S. over abortion. The president said he didn`t talk about abortion today and dodged a question about the U.S. Conference of Bishops. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: Mr. President, did the issue of abortion come up at all?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: No, it didn`t. It came up. We just talked about the fact that he was happy I was a good Catholic, and I could keep receiving communion.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: He said that you should keep receiving communion?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: Did you discuss the Conference of Bishops?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: That`s a private conversation.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: You can see the testiness here, Ruby, I know you know the background about how the president views this particular issue. Explain it.\par \par \uc2 RUBY CRAMER, POLITICO SENIOR STAFF WRITER: It`s just one of the most interesting, complex aspects of his presidency, really, because Biden is someone who can`t be understood without talking about Catholicism, the way that he was raised. He loved the institution of the Catholic Church, the nuns that taught him at school, going to family mass. Every, you know, other word, sometimes it feels like is some Bidenism that has to do with Catholic social doctrine or a saying that his dad told him.\par \par \uc2 And yet when he`s pressed about these issues, like you just saw in the video that you played, he`s extremely careful with his words. He almost shuts down a little bit, he`s not as voluble as he usually is. And often he`ll say that`s a private matter or that`s just my personal life, or like he just said, that`s a private conversation. He does not want to interfere at all with the U.S. hierarchy. The church U.S. hierarchy. And it`s sort of odd, a dichotomy between sort of how he publicly expresses his persona as a political figure which is wrapped up in everything about his faith versus this sort of ongoing tension with the Catholic Church which frankly you just -- he just refuses to address.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Well, and the politics of it are fascinating, too, because religious voters, particularly evangelicals but also practicing Catholics and other religious voters in many cases have drifted away from the Democratic Party. Now you`ve got Biden also facing some criticism from Catholics that he`s somehow not Catholic enough because of his stance on abortion. Right?\par \par \uc2 CRAMER: Right, and obviously the historical context here is important because we know that President Kennedy, the only other Catholic president we`ve ever had, was criticized for being Catholic and sort of went out of his way to emphasize the separation between church and state and say that he wasn`t going to be a Catholic president, he was going to be a president who happened to be Catholic.\par \par \uc2 And Biden, you know, 63 years -- 60 years later is sort of facing in a way the opposite problem. He has a leadership of bishops in this country who have taken the issue of abortion as the sort of preeminent issue of the church and believe that people who are out of step with church teaching on abortion should not participate in its holiest sacrament, the eucharist, the taking of communion.\par \par \uc2 And Biden is someone who is observant to the point of going to mass every weekend, even though, you know, he`s in the White House now, he`ll go in D.C. and oftentimes he goes to his home parish in Delaware. And so the act of him participating in the fundamental rite of the Catholic Church has become politicized. There are protesters outside of his home parish St. Joseph on the Brandywine almost every Saturday or Sunday that he`s there.\par \par \uc2 So it`s overtly political in the sense that these clashes are sometimes so in your face. I mean, his home parish is this sleepy, small little church in a suburb of Wilmington and sometimes you hear screams -- not screams, but yelling. It`s a very visceral, jarring moment to hear strangers yelling at Joe Biden as he`s going in to participate in a sacred moment. You`re a fake Catholic, you`re not a real Catholic, you know, God save Beau Biden`s soul, which was, you know, in one incident. They were calling out about his son.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CRAMER: It`s this jarring aspect of his presidency. And you`re right, it is political, whether or not he wants it to be.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: You really cannot understand Joe Biden without understanding or trying to understand his relationship with his faith.\par \par \uc2 Ruby Cramer, you`ve done a great job breaking it down. Your piece in "Politico" magazine is excellent. I tweeted it for anyone who wants to go do a little homework reading this weekend. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 CRAMER: Thank you, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Ruby, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And thank you all for being with us this hour. We will be back Monday with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. And if it`s Sunday, it`s "MEET THE PRESS" on your local NBC News station. MSNBC coverage continues with Geoff Bennett, my friend, right now.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT, MSNBC HOST: It is great to be with you. I`m Geoff Bennett. In this Friday there is high drama from Capitol Hill all the way to Rome.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020211030ehat00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE RACHEL MADDOW SHOW for October 22, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Rachel Maddow \par \uc2 8,170 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 October 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Rachel Maddow Show\par \uc2 TRMADS\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [21:00:06]\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST: Pakistan has 12 weeks I think of leave. I just saw it today.\par \par \uc2 Robert Reich\uc2  and Betsy Stevenson, thank you both. That is "ALL IN" for this week.\par \par \uc2 THE RACHEL MADDOW SHOW starts right now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Chris. Thanks, my friend. Have a fantastic weekend.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You too.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Thanks at home for being with us on a Friday night. Happy Friday.\par \par \uc2 Here is the thing I do not get to say very often. Today was a day full of good news when it comes to whether or not, we`re going to stay a democracy in our lifetimes. We have been covering this beat pretty relentlessly for the past several years now as you know.\par \par \uc2 The Republican Party over the past several years, they have more and more overtly embraced Donald Trump`s denunciation of elections, the promotion of false claims about elections to undermine faith in them, the rejection of clear election results, the promotion of changes to election administration that would allow Republicans to subvert election results that they don`t like. It has been a bleak thing to cover, right? The news on this beat is always bad.\par \par \uc2 If January 6th was supposed to be a shock to the system that was going the reset the Republican Party away from this very dangerous course, it worked for like one day and then it really did not work. The embrace of all this very dark anti-democracy, you know, rule by force stuff in the Republican Party has just gotten worse and worse.\par \par \uc2 Even since January 6th. It is a drag. It has just been a drag to cover it, I will tell you honestly.\par \par \uc2 But today, bright spots. Not being sarcastic. Bright spots on a bunch of different fronts. Let me tell you about some of them.\par \par \uc2 First of all, in terms of accountability, CNN first to report tonight that one of the key alleged plotters with Trump in the senior levels of his Justice Department is finally going to come forward and testify.\par \par \uc2 This is Jeff Clark. Mr. Clark with Trump hatched this scheme by which the Justice Department would hold a press conference after the election and declare at that press conference that there were serious fraud problems in the election in multiple states and the Justice Department was investigating these very serious problems. He then wanted to send a letter to swing states that Biden won, a letter from the Justice Department, advising officials in those states that the Justice Department thought there were serious problems with the elections in those states, and Republican state legislators should therefore, you know, start investigating the election results or whatever. They should get to work on whatever they needed to do to not send Biden electors to the Electoral College even though in reality Biden won those states.\par \par \uc2 That was the Jeff Clark plan. We know from the Senate Judiciary Committee`s investigation of these matters. And we know that the plan, while it had a lot of detail in it and they took a lot of acts of furtherance toward it, the reason it fell apart was because the then-attorney general, a guy named Jeff Rosen, wouldn`t sign on to it.\par \par \uc2 When Rosen wouldn`t side off on it, wouldn`t allow this plot to go forward, Trump then tried to replace Rosen with Jeff Clark, tried to install Jeff Clark himself as attorney general, so the Clark plan would go ahead. Again, to have the Justice Department declared serious fraud in the election and to direct states to therefore not send their Biden electors. It was absolutely insane.\par \par \uc2 It was also a rational plan, that they didn`t just spit ball about. They didn`t just fantasize about it. They drafted the letter to the states. He did insist on the press conference to other Justice Department officials. The Justice Department officials even tried to on-board U.S. attorneys, federal prosecutors in the relevant states to back them up on these spurious fraud claims they were going to use to keep Trump in power even though he lost the election.\par \par \uc2 Just saying this, right. Like I said, news on this front has been absolutely terrible as we learned of all of the things they tried. But in today`s news a little glimmer with the news that Jeff Clark has apparently finally agreed to testify to the committee that is investigating the January 6th attack on the U.S. Capitol.\par \par \uc2 I mentioned that a lot of the Clark plot emerged both from public source reporting, particularly in "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " but also from the Senate judiciary committee`s investigation into what happened at the Justice Department around this time.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  has been investigating how Trump used the Justice Department to try to seize power and stay in office after losing the election.\par \par \uc2 Interestingly, for their preliminary report, which was released a couple of weeks ago, they had a lot of information about what Jeff Clark did but all of the information they had about him was from other people. They couldn`t get Clark to testify himself because Republican senators on the Senate judiciary committee apparently blocked the committee from giving a subpoena to Jeff Clark. They wouldn`t allow a subpoena to him to go forward.\par \par \uc2 [21:05:01]\par \par \uc2 Republicans didn`t want to hear from him, and so Jeff Clark did not speak to them. But the House Committee that`s investigating the January 6th attack, they apparently do not have that problem, and so, Clark is going to be coming in this day next week to speak to the January 6th investigation. Progress.\par \par \uc2 And this news comes, of course, just after the full house voted yesterday, all the Democrats and nine Republicans voted yesterday that another important witness for that investigation, maybe a subject of that investigation, I don`t know, should be referred to the Justice Department for potential prosecution for him defying a subpoena to give testimony to that committee. That criminal referral of Trump adviser \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  is now at the U.S. attorney`s office in Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 They will consider it independently and make their own decision about whether or not to bring charges. We are waiting to see if and when they will put \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  on trial in that matter. But, again, the prospect of accountability there, that`s a glimmer.\par \par \uc2 Those glimmers today also arrive alongside a bizarre turn today for a Trump lawyer named John Eastman. He`s an obscure figure who got famous through this whole scandal when he wrote the notorious Eastman memo which laid out the theory of the case basically behind the January 6th attack on the \uc2 Capitol\uc2 . What Trump demanded of the crowd at that rally January 6th, before his supporters rushed to the \uc2 Capitol\uc2 , what Trump demanded of them, what people like Eastman himself and other speakers that day told the mob they would accomplish if they, in Trump`s words, fought like hell that day was a specific thing. It was this scenario that was laid out in Eastman`s memo in which he said the vice president, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , shouldn`t accept the electors from swing states that Biden won. The memo laid out how vice president \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  had the right not to accept those electors, and that`s how the electoral count could be stopped, that`s how the naming of Biden as the winner of the election could be technically stopped.\par \par \uc2 John Eastman is the one who laid out the memo for how the January 6th attack on the U.S. Capitol, which did stop the counting process for some time, he laid out the memo for how the attack on 