me. Shocked to find out a ballot had been cast in his dead wife`s name. Shocked, it made no sense. Now that he knows it happened, he just went there, this obviously lends credence to all these claims you are hearing about, about all of the voter fraud in the election. There must be tons of it. It just makes me wonder how pervasive this is.\par \par \uc2 Now that man, the husband, has been charged with two felony counts of voter fraud because according to prosecutors, according to Nevada state attorney general`s prosecution division, he is actually the one who cast the ballot in the name of his dead wife in addition to his own ballot, which means if the prosecutors in this case are correct he probably wasn`t all that shocked. Shocked and in disbelief to learn that her ballot had been cast since he`s the one who cast it.\par \par \uc2 But he nevertheless put himself out there to become the poster boy for these false claims of fraud that Republicans and the conservative media used and are still using to claim that the election was stolen. He`s an executive at a company that had taken a very positive and public pro-Trump stance and actually hosted an event for president Trump in Nevada just before the November election last year. He`s been charged with these two felony counts. He is facing up to eight years in prison. He will have his initial court appearance next month in Nevada.\par \par \uc2 This beat, this effort by Republicans and the conservative media to make Americans not belief in election results anymore, it is still a depressing beat and we will stay on it because it is the most important story in modern history. But some days, you know, some days, even as this beat is relentlessly depressing and it does feel like it just keeps getting worse, some days are good days.\par \par \uc2 Some days schemes get exposed. Schemers get caught. Good people stand up, bad guys are caught and the real facts come out. Some days are good days.\par \par \uc2 This is a good day.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [21:27:30]\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Let me start here by saying this is not a typical story that we cover on this show, not our usual thing. But it is a compelling story and it is all right. It is a legitimate mystery and I want to tell you it connects with something we`ve been covering intensely recently.\par \par \uc2 Recently in the show, we have been covering how Americans are going on strike all over the country, more than 10,000 people who work at the John Deere company are in their ninth day of their strike against that company for better wages and benefits, 1,400 Americans working at the Kellogg`s Cereal company are in the third week of that strike in multiple states. Tens of thousands of people who are health care workers at Kaiser Hospital System in California, Oregon and Hawaii, they may also go on strike. I mean, that would be tens of thousands of people. That would be huge. That decision could come at any time.\par \par \uc2 This week 300 flight attendants working for a subsidiary of American Airlines voted to authorize a strike. In Philly, we learned that transit workers there may strike. And in Hollywood, another huge one. It was last week when 60,000 stage crew workers in Hollywood came within hours of launching what would have been the biggest strike in that industry since World War II.\par \par \uc2 The union IATSE represents the vast majority of Hollywood stage crew, everybody from makeup artists to camera and microphone operators to stage hands of all kinds. Last week just hours before the 60,000 people were due to walk off the job, the studios did offer up an improved deal for those workers. Whether it is a good enough deal, those workers are still going to have to decide, still going to have to vote on whether they`re going to accept the new terms. Until they do, the threat of that historic strike still looms.\par \par \uc2 But that is turning out to be part of the relevant backdrop for this truly bizarre and terrible story that we all woke up to this morning that nobody in the country seems to be able to avoid talking about all day long. By now you have seen the headlines.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday on the set of a western filming in New Mexico, the actor Alec Baldwin fired a gun that was supposed to be a prop in a scene. The resulting shot killed the film`s cinematographer, 42-year-old woman named Halyna Hutchins. She was considered to be a rising star in her field. Again, she was the cinematographer.\par \par \uc2 The film`s director was injured in this incident. He has since been released from the hospital. When it comes to figure out how it could happen, the questions to the fore are mystifying. How does a prop gun result in a real shooting?\par \par \uc2 [21:30:02]\par \par \uc2 In some cases, a prop gun is something that`s not a gun at all, it has no working parts, just made out of rubber and plastic designed to have a gun shape. But if you are watching a movie or TV show and you see somebody actually fire a weapon, you hear the bang, you see the flash, chances are that is either special effects or that could be a real gun firing a blank bullet.\par \par \uc2 What is a blank bullet? This is a diagram "\uc2 USA Today\uc2 " posted today which I think is useful. For an actual piece of ammunition, one that is designed to shoot someone, there`s several parts. There`s the casing, the primer, the powder, gun powder, and the bullet which is at the tip of the projectile. That`s the thing that`s supposed to be able to kill you, right.\par \par \uc2 The primer and the powder are the things that make the bullet propel out of the gun. That`s the stuff that goes bang and lets off the flash, but it is the bullet that is the actual projectile that`s fired out of the end of the barrel.\par \par \uc2 Now, if a gun is loaded with a blank, that just means it has all of the other component pieces that a real piece of ammunition has minus the bullet itself, minus the projectile designed to actually kill you. Just because they`re blanks though doesn`t mean they`re not dangerous.\par \par \uc2 I mean go back to that diagram showing the blank for a second. Can we pit it back up? You see where it says "wad sealed with plastic or paper, released like a projectile." there isn`t a chunk of metal on the end, a bullet, but you need to stick something in there to seal in the gun powder. They use wadding or paper, it poses less danger than a bullet but if fired at close enough range a blank can injure or kill someone.\par \par \uc2 It is technically missing the bullet but even a blank can be dangerous, even a blank can be deadly. It happened on movie sets before. In 1984, an actor named John Eric Hexum was joking around on the set of a film with a real prop gun that was filled with blanks. He shot himself in the head at close rang. The force of that blank fractured his skull and he died.\par \par \uc2 There`s also the potential to misload a gun. This happened on a movie set in 1993. The actor Brandon Lee, the son of the great Bruce Lee, was starring in a movie where his character was supposed to get shot in the stomach, but when another actor fired a real prop gun in his direction, the prop gun loaded with blanks, turns out the gun had been misloaded. It hadn`t been checked properly when it was loaded with blanks. There was an actual bullet, a real bullet accidentally lodged in the chamber of that prop gun. When the gun went off, it did fire a blank but it also dislodged the real bullet which hit Brandon Lee in the abdomen and killed him.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, they are prop guns of various kinds used in various ways, but these things are so dangerous a lot of productions don`t use them at all anymore. The show "Mayor of East Town" that was on \uc2 HBO\uc2  earlier this year, Kate Winslet plays the police detective, it is not a spoiler but a lot of guns are fired on that show. Kate Winslet is a cop and on "Mayor of East Town" for that whole show they decided every firing of every gun would all be CGI, it was all special effects.\par \par \uc2 They didn`t use prop guns loaded with blanks. They, you know, maybe lost the authentic recoil and the live muzzle flash that they would have gotten from using blanks, but they decided it wasn`t worth the risk. They added in all of the effects after the fact, and a lot of productions do that now.\par \par \uc2 But a lot of movie and TV sets still use real guns. The prop master who handles all props for all purposes on the set, if there are guns being used, that person can sometimes be assisted by a specialist, an armorer, somebody who specializes in weaponry for film and TV sets.\par \par \uc2 Now, as you can imagine, the entertainment industry has really strict rules for using firearms onset. This is some of the -- from the industry wide labor management safety commission, these are some of the safety bulletins they`ve put out about guns onset. Look what this one says right at the top in all capital letters, blanks can kill. Treat all firearms as though they are loaded. Then it is pages and pages of rules trying to head off any tragic accidents like the one we saw yesterday in New Mexico.\par \par \uc2 Number one, refrain from pointing a firearm at anyone including yourself. If it is absolutely necessary to do so on camera, consult the prop master. Number two, never place your finger on the trigger until you`re ready to shoot. Keep your finger alongside the firearm and off the trigger. Number three, know where and what your intended target is. This goes on and on and on.\par \par \uc2 To state the very obvious, clearly something broke down on this film set yesterday in New Mexico when a crew member was killed and another seriously injured when an actor discharged a prop gun. We don`t know where the breakdown of safety protocols occurred, but how did it -- how could it have happened?\par \par \uc2 [21:35:07]\par \par \uc2 Just in the last hour, we learned from the "AP" it was the assistant director of the film who reportedly handed the prop gun to Alec Baldwin before the incident happened. According to court documents obtained by the "AP", the assistant director did not know the weapon contained a round, a live round. I should tell you in movie speak a live round can also mean a blank. For the purposeless of being on a movie set, anything that can be fired out of a gun, blank or bullet, is considered to be a live round.\par \par \uc2 In terms of what killed the cinematographer and injured the director, we don`t even know if it was a blank that was discharged from the weapon. Could it have been an actual live bullet as was the case with Brandon Lee? It is being investigated now by the Santa Fe County Sheriff`s Office.\par \par \uc2 But let me bring it back to the beginning now. Beyond the immediate tragedy here and the absolutely flummoxing mystery of what went wrong and how it could have gone wrong, this story does also intersect in an interesting way with the story we`ve been watching unfold over the country in recent weeks, right, where American workers of all kinds in all sorts of different industries have been advocating for changes in their workplace, advocating for, you know, better pay, better benefits, but also safer and more sustainable working conditions. Like I said, this activism has included the entertainment industry just this past week. It has only been a couple of days since tens of thousands of workers in Hollywood`s biggest union threatened to walk off the job for better and safer working conditions.\par \par \uc2 We are learning tonight and getting information this dynamic of workers all over the country demanding better working conditions, that dynamic was on play on this movie set where this happened and it was at play hours before the fatal incident occurred. "The L.A. Times" is reporting tonight just hours before this incident where the cinematographer was killed and the director was injured, just hours before, half a dozen camera crew workers walked off that same set to protest working conditions.\par \par \uc2 Quote, the camera operators and their assistants were frustrated by the conditions surrounding the low-budget film, including complaints of long hours and complaints about getting their paychecks. So, the morning of the incident, the camera crew arrived for their 6:30 a.m. call time but according to "The L.A. Times", they packed up their gear and walked off the set.\par \par \uc2 One crew member telling "The Times" that the cinematographer that was killed had been using her clout onset to advocate for better working conditions for her team. One of the camera crew workers left the set in protest -- excuse me. Once the camera crew workers left the set in protest yesterday, "The L.A. Times" reports that the film production brought in nonunion crew members to replace them and ordered all remaining union workers to leave for the day.\par \par \uc2 Now, typically, onset the prop master, the person in charge of making sure guns are handled and used safely, traditionally that itself is a union role but today the local union covering the prop master says no member of their union was onset at the time of the incident. It gets more worrying.\par \par \uc2 NBC News is reporting this evening among the safety concerns raised by the workers who walked off the set yesterday were concerns over the fact that the prop gun involved in this incident, the same gun, had reportedly misfired multiple times previously on this set. "The L.A. Times" has further reporting on this tonight. I will quote from what they just posted. Baldwin`s stunt double accidentally fired two rounds on Saturday after being told the gun was cold, lingo for a women that doesn`t have any ammunition including blanks. That`s according to two crew members who talked to "The L.A. Times" about it.\par \par \uc2 One crew member telling "The Times", there should have been an investigation into what happened, there were no safety meetings. There was no assurance that it wouldn`t happen again. All they wanted to do was rush.\par \par \uc2 Again, according to "The L.A. Times" tonight, one colleague was so alarmed by the prop gun misfires he sent a text message to the unit production manager saying, quote, we`ve now had three accidental discharges, this is super unsafe.\par \par \uc2 Look, there`s so much we do not know right now. We have no idea where or when or why the breakdown in gun safety protocol happened, we don`t even know the basics of what was fired out of the prop gun. We know there was trouble onset preceding the incident, as recently as the morning before it happened with crew members complaining about safety conditions onset including around that gun. We don`t know if that played an immediate role at all in how things fell apart here, fatally.\par \par \uc2 What else can we know at this point about exactly what happened here?\par \par \uc2 We`ve got an expert on hand to help us out with this next.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [21:44:04]\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: This was a picture that Halyna Hutchins posted to her \uc2 Instagram\uc2  account last week. It is a picture of the cast of the movie "Rust" and it is captioned, standing in solidarity with our crew here in new Mexico on "Rust." Again, "Rust" was the name of the movie.\par \par \uc2 When she is referencing solidarity was the labor action by the film and television workers union who were at the time threatening a massive strike in Hollywood, the biggest strike since World War II among -- over among other things what they saw as inadequate workplace safety for film crew members.\par \par \uc2 We still don`t know if IATSE is going to go on strike, that labor action is sort of still pending while its members decide whether or not they`re going to vote to accept new terms they received from studios last week. But this has a whole different cast now.\par \par \uc2 Halyna Hutchins died on the set of that movie yesterday after a prop gun that shouldn`t have been able to hurt anybody fired a projectile of some kind which shot and killed her while she was working on set.\par \par \uc2 [21:45:09]\par \par \uc2 It is a set that reportedly had labor problems of its own, including a walk-off by some IATSE members as recently as yesterday morning before the incident happened. We are learning more tonight, that there was reportedly a series of issues with the prop guns on set that were cause for safety concerns among crew members before yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Still lots of unanswered questions here. Joining us is Steve Wolf. He is a weapons safety expert for films. He was involved in the investigation into the accidental shooting death of Brandon Lee during a movie seat in 1993.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Wolf, it is a pleasure to have you with us tonight. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 STEVE WOLF, WEAPONS SAFETY EXPERT: Rachel, it is a pleasure to be with you. I almost feel like you don`t need me. You`ve really got this.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Well, let me ask if I explained any of this wrong when I was talking about blanks and the difference between blanks and bullets, that`s the way it is supposed to be explained?\par \par \uc2 WOLF: You nailed it. You could have done, you know, ten times the job that was done on the Baldwin set.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Well, let me ask you about this.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: This is --\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Go ahead, please.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: This is the thing that would be a blank round, just a casing and some gun powder. This is live ammo , the thing used to shoot at things. So, you know, hard to get these wrong, which is which.\par \par \uc2 One issue though has been referring to this weapon as a prop weapon. A prop is anything that an actor touches. If an actor touches their cellphone, you know, this is a prop.\par \par \uc2 Well, if I touch this gun it is a prop. That doesn`t make it a prop gun though. It is a gun that`s being used as a prop. A prop gun has been modified so that you cannot introduce live ammo to it.\par \par \uc2 Live ammo simply -- sorry about that. Live ammo simply won`t go into a prop gun, only blanks will go in. So it is not that they were killed with -- you know, he was killed with a prop gun. He was killed with a real gun, and presumably now what we`re hearing, you know, actual live ammo .\par \par \uc2 Also, when we say going live onset as you said, going live doesn`t mean we are using live ammo. It means we are about to do something noisy, that we`re really doing the real thing. We`re going live, we`re going to blow up the car. We`re going live, we`re loading the gun with the blanks.\par \par \uc2 You know, it doesn`t mean we are putting real ammunition, bullets into guns capable of firing them and then handling them onset.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Steve, because of all of the safety protocols that are used including as you just described, like making sure you are using a realistic looking weapon that is operable but it can`t be loaded with live ammunition, the kind of safety rules that I just showed that are standard in terms of what people are -- how people are trained to work around these kinds of firearms onset because of what we know about this sort of industry standards around safety briefings for everybody involved in a shoot that`s got a firearm involved in it in any way, it just seems very surprising given the current state of how these things are handled in the industry that this could happen. It seems like there`s so many safeguards this should be impossible.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Yes. It is to the point where you can`t hurt somebody by breaking a rule. You can hurt someone if you break multiple rules. So when you use an unmodified firearm that can accept live ammo.\par \par \uc2 When you put that live ammo in it, when you point the gun at a person, you have now broken three rules that lead you down the road towards accidental homicides. Any one of these things -- you know, let`s say you have a live gun and put real ammo in it but when you pointed it and observe the rule you don`t point guns at things you don`t want to put holes in, there wouldn`t have been a fatality. A bullet whizzing past everyone`s head, oh, my God, it is an unsafe set and I`m leaving, but no one would have been killed. You have to break a bunch of rules.\par \par \uc2 Also, talk about the gun misfiring, when a gun misfires it doesn`t go off. So, the gun didn`t misfire on the previous days. It was accidentally discharged.\par \par \uc2 I`m not sure how it can accident -- you know, having had guns around my whole life, I have never seen a gun go off by itself. Guns go off when you -- let me check it is clear. Guns go off when you press the trigger. It is an intentional act, and so we shouldn`t confuse intentional acts from accident.\par \par \uc2 The gun didn`t accidentally go off. The gun was put in the hands of a person, the person did not check if there was anything in it, and then they pressed the trigger twice. That`s why the gun went off.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Hmm.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: It was unsafe handling and unsafe supervision.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: And the supervision and the labor conditions here are going to dovetail in terms of consequences of this act are, but we should keep in mind --\par \par \uc2 [21:50:08]\par \par \uc2 WOLF: True.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: -- we should keep in mind first and foremost right now, there`s a police investigation into what happened here, and we expect that the Santa Fe Sheriff`s Office will let us know more than we know now.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Yeah. From what I`m looking at now, you know, I believe that charges should be filed. To introduce live ammo with bullets in it to a movie set is absolutely unconscionable. It put that into the hands of an actor and not tell the actor, under no circumstances should you point this at anyone unless you intend to commit murder.\par \par \uc2 I`m pretty sure that Alec Baldwin, had he been told that, would not have pointed the gun at anyone. No one told him that, and if they did, they weren`t watching to see he was following directions.\par \par \uc2 So, we have all the safety meetings (ph) you want, but if we`ll are going to break the rules, then those meetings mean nothing, and the rules mean nothing. Simple common sense, don`t bring live guns on set. Don`t bring live ammo on set. Don`t point guns at people that you don`t want to put holes into. It`s not much more complicated than that (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Steve Wolf, professional stuntman, film weapons safety expert, spelling this out in a way that is very, very clear. Steve, thank you for helping us understand tonight. I really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Rachel, thanks so much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: All right. We`ve got more ahead tonight. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [21:55:29]\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: The \uc2 Supreme Court of the United States\uc2  is not a vehicle that is built for speed. Deciding whether to take a case, setting a date for arguments, eventually ruling, it`s like cold molasses, even on urgent issues, except today.\par \par \uc2 Today, the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  did something quite remarkable. They agreed that they`re going to hear the Texas abortion ban case, okay? But they agreed that they`re going to hear it a week and a half from now. That`s lightning for them.\par \par \uc2 Law professor Steve Vladeck pointing out today he can`t think of any recent precedent except for Bush v. Gore where the court acted so stunningly fast.\par \par \uc2 The arguments are going to be on November 1st, which is very, very soon. Still the court deliberately allowed the Texas abortion ban to stay in place while they consider the matter, and they didn`t have to do that.\par \par \uc2 Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  offering a blistering dissent, saying in part today, the promise of future adjudication offers cold comfort for Texas women seeking abortion care who are entitled to relief now. These women will suffer personal harm from delaying their medical care and as their pregnancies progress, they may be even unable to obtain abortion care altogether because every day the court fails to grant relief is devastating both for individual women and for our constitutional system as a whole. She says, quote, I dissent.\par \par \uc2 Abortion rights on the chopping block this term for the high court and very, very fast.\par \par \uc2 Watch this space.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: All right. That is going to do it for us tonight. I do want to mention before I go that MSNBC has a new documentary that`s going to air this weekend on Sunday night. It`s called "Civil War, or Who Do We Think We Are?"\par \par \uc2 It`s really good, really well done, really thought provoking. The executive producer is Brad Pitt, if that tells you anything. That is going to air right here on MSNBC this Sunday at 10:00 p.m. Eastern Time. You should watch it.\par \par \uc2 That`s going to do it for me tonight.\par \par \uc2 It`s time for "THE LAST WORD" with Ali Velshi filling in for Lawrence tonight.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Ali.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document TRMADS0020211023eham00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Interview with Representative Barbara Lee (D-CA) about Voting Rights Bill; Supreme Court Agrees to Hear Challenge to Texas Abortion Law; CDC Gives Final Approval for Moderna and Johnson & Johnson Booster Shots; Florida Election Official Warns Against Election Falsehoods, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Geoff Bennett, Pete Williams, Kavita Patel, Ali Vitali, Peter  Alexander, Guad Venegas \par \uc2 8,584 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 October 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WENDY IHAMA, CITY COLLEGE OF NEW YORK STUDENT: -- inspirational because it goes to show that it is possible.\par \par \uc2 SHEINELLE JONES, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Alejandro Espinosa is a senior, studying climate policy.\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO ESPINOSA, CITY COLLEGE OF NEW YORK STUDENT: It`s about Colin Powell`s legacy. It`s about the fact that he wants his legacy to be inherited to our generation. And I think it`s our task to inherit -- to pass it forward to our children and grandchildren.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Carrying that legacy, a future lawyer, diplomat, policymaker, and military officer.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, MSNBC ANCHOR: And that indeed was his greatest legacy. Thank you. This is Andrea Mitchell Reports, MTP DAILY starts right now with Geoff Bennett.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: If it`s Friday, the White House tries to get things back on track after another week of falling poll numbers and intraparty disagreements over President Biden`s agenda. We`ll have the very latest from the White House coming up.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we`ll go one on one with Congressman Adam Schiff after the House votes to hold Steve Bannon in contempt for defying the January 6 Committee`s demands for documents and testimony.\par \par \uc2 And later, an update on a bizarre and tragic story we`re following from New Mexico after actor Alec Baldwin discharged a prop firearm on a movie set killing one crew member and injuring another. What he`s now saying about that incident, ahead.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY, I`m Geoff Bennett in for my friend Chuck Todd.\par \par \uc2 And we start this hour with the two stories consuming Capitol Hill right now. One, the ever-evolving talks over President Biden`s ambitious domestic agenda, the other, the investigation into what led to the January 6 attack on the U.S. Capitol.\par \par \uc2 Negotiators are continuing to progress on the President`s social spending package with both House Speaker Nancy Pelosi, and Senator Chuck Schumer expressing optimism that a deal could soon be reached.\par \par \uc2 Now, Pelosi had breakfast at the White House this morning, with Schumer joining virtually. Pelosi has set an October 31 deadline for passing the trillion-dollar infrastructure deal, already passed in the Senate. Biden, himself, predicted a victory during a CNN Town Hall last night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We`re down to four or five issues which I`m not going to negotiate on national television, as you might guess --\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER, CNN ANCHOR: We`d be like hearing in for some hearing and that you want--\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No, no, no, -- no, I know. But all kidding aside, I think we can get there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Now, President Biden also addressed concerns over Senators, Joe Manchin, and Kyrsten Sinema, who continue to push back against some of the reconciliation plan`s more Progressive elements, as well as how to pay for the deal.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Joe is not a bad guy, I mean, he`s a friend. And he`s always the end of the day, come around and voted.\par \par \uc2 She`s very supportive of the environmental agenda in my legislation, very supportive. She`s supportive of all -- almost all the things I mentioned relating to everything from a family care to all -- to all those issues. Where she`s not supportive, is she says she will not raise a single penny in taxes on the corporate side, and or on wealthy people, period.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Now, that might be an oversimplification of Senator Sinema`s position, but it certainly speaks to the level of frustration felt by some Democrats.\par \par \uc2 So, as they await a potential deal on Biden`s domestic agenda, Democrats are also awaiting word from the Justice Department on whether Trump confidant, Steve Bannon, will be prosecuted for failing to cooperate with the January 6 Committee.\par \par \uc2 The House voted yesterday to refer the contempt of Congress resolution to the DOJ. Nine Republicans voted with every Democrat in favor of that resolution.\par \par \uc2 Now in a moment, I`ll be joined by January 6 Committee member, Congressman Adam Schiff. But first, let`s go to NBC News Chief White House Correspondent Peter Alexander, and NBC News Capitol Hill Correspondent, Ali Vitali, it`s good to have both of you with us.\par \par \uc2 So, Ali, you`ve got the Majority Leader, Steny Hoyer, he`s telling House members that they`re going to aim to vote on both the social spending bill and the infrastructure bill next week.\par \par \uc2 The House Speaker told reporters that everybody is on the path to an agreement toward reconciliation, but Senator Manchin is saying an agreement`s not going to happen anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 So, give us a bit of a level set. There`s lots of momentum, lots of optimism, but where are we really in this process?\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: There is both all of the momentum and also none of the momentum to get this done on a truncated timeline, which is sort of the way that it`s been playing out over the course of the last few weeks.\par \par \uc2 Everyone agrees here that they would like to see -- vote on the bipartisan infrastructure bill as soon as possible. But the mechanism to get there has always been, for Progressives and also for the White House, has been to move forward on this larger social spending package.\par \par \uc2 It feels like, in the last few days, we`ve seen more progress on that, and it really was underscored last night when we saw the President very succinctly lay out what`s in the bill, and what`s out of the bill.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Where Senator Joe Manchin stands, where Senator Kirsten Sinema stands, that`s something that has sort of been nebulous for -- to a lot of lawmakers on the Hill. They feel like they haven`t even been clear on where those two key Senators stand.\par \par \uc2 The President last night in the Town Hall, laying it out, and we can see if we look at what`s in and what`s out, kind of where the contours of this deal could come together.\par \par \uc2 It`s clearer now than it`s ever been when you look at things like federal paid leave being tacked down from 12 weeks to four weeks, universal pre-K, still in that bill, the child tax credit, albeit only for a one-year extension, which has drawn the ire of some who wanted to see it pulled out for a longer period of time.\par \par \uc2 But we`re also starting to see from the President some of the concessions that could be made for things that aren`t currently in this bill. You see there, for example, possible $800 vouchers for hearing, that`s because Medicare expansions on hearing, dental, and vision, likely aren`t going to be in this reconciliation bill.\par \par \uc2 You also heard the President last night talk about increases to Pell Grants. That`s because free community college has fallen out of these reconciliation negotiations. The President last night saying expanding Pell Grants isn`t going to get you the whole way there in the way that free community college would have, but that at least gets you closer.\par \par \uc2 You also see here, the clean electricity performance program. President Biden says that that`s still in, but Senator Joe Manchin has said that it is fallen out. So, it sort of depends which Joe you want to take the word of on that one.\par \par \uc2 And then, of course, tax rate hikes on corporations, Senator Kyrsten Sinema is -- has long said that she does not want to see rates hiked on corporations or the wealthy individuals. These are now the contours of the deal that we`re dealing with.\par \par \uc2 I also think that it`s important as we move forward here on whatever timeline happens on the Hill, the way that Anderson Cooper consistently last night, talked about Senator Joe Manchin, and Senator Kyrsten Sinema, they`ve been central to our conversation.\par \par \uc2 But just the fact that the President spent so much time responding to what their demands could be and are, is really striking and underscores what he said there, which is that in a Senate, with margins this close, everyone can be a President.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Yes, it was a really revealing line delivered by the President there last night.\par \par \uc2 So, Peter, I was talking to a member of Congress who has been in direct talks with President Biden, and this person told me that the President made clear to them that he would like to have an agreement before he heads off to this global climate summit in Glasgow next week.\par \par \uc2 Help us understand the political pressure created by the political calendar, you`ve got the climate summit, you`ve also got this big gubernatorial race in Virginia.\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, MSNBC CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, you`re exactly right. And as we heard from Gavin Newsom in a conversation with our colleague, Chuck Todd, within the last 24 hours or so, he said to those here in Washington, Democrats specifically, just get something done.\par \par \uc2 That he`s over this political Death March as he describes it, where one is up, and another is down, Manchin, Sinema, and the rest of them.\par \par \uc2 He says that most Americans and certainly Democrats want to see real progress on this, and the White House agrees with that.\par \par \uc2 I know there is a timeline, even if they don`t want to give one specifically, we`ve heard the language change here in the last week, Jen Psaki, and other aides saying that the timetable for this is not unlimited, it`s not limitless.\par \par \uc2 They want to get it done before that crucial vote to takes place in Virginia, which is a pretty good arbiter of where things are and gives you a good heads up of where the Midterm may go as well.\par \par \uc2 But the President certainly recognizes that. And we did hear from Nancy Pelosi, that`s why she said after the meeting today that she, Chuck Schumer -- and Chuck Schumer virtually had, with the President, that she was optimistic. She said that on the environmental side, things were resolved. She said in terms of health care, there were still a couple of outstanding issues.\par \par \uc2 But the bottom line for Democrats is that at the end of the day, this is what`s going to define the President`s spurs -- certainly his first two years in office, can he take real action on these issues, which was the point behind the effort last night to make it less about that top-line number and more about what`s in there, which is why Pelosi today said, no matter where the number ends up, it`ll be bigger than anything we`ve ever done for the American people.\par \par \uc2 That`s the message they are hoping to deliver. But to get there, they do have to get something done, Geoff.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Yes, absolutely. Peter, and Ali, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 And as we mentioned at the top of the show, the Justice Department is now deciding whether to prosecute Steve Bannon after the House voted yesterday to refer a contempt of Congress resolution to the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 Now, Bandon has refused to cooperate with the January 6 Committee despite being subpoenaed. Now, he`s citing executive privilege.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Congressman Adam Schiff, Democrat from California, member of the January 6 Committee, and Chair of the House Intelligence Committee.\par \par \uc2 He also served as an impeachment manager for Donald Trump`s first impeachment trial. He`s also the author of the new book, "Midnight in Washington: How We Almost Lost Our Democracy, and Still Could". It`s good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 And we`re told that Congress`s criminal contempt referral of Steve Bannon was accepted by the U.S. Attorney`s Office in DC yesterday evening around 5:30. Now, the ball is in Merrick Garland`s court. What`s your expectation for how quickly the DOJ will move on this?\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF (D-CA): Well, it`s my hope and expectation that they will move quickly, that they understand the urgency here.\par \par \uc2 And I think there are good signs in what the Justice Department did with respect to some of the witnesses we wanted to talk to, a former top-ranking justice officials, they did not assert executive privilege, they did not assert any kind of work product privilege, and made it clear that they view the public interest in getting all the facts out about that violent attack on our democracy to be paramount.\par \par \uc2 I think that same logic ought to apply here. So it`s our hope is our expectation that they will move quickly.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: I want to play for our viewers something that Congresswoman Liz Cheney, who`s the top Republican on the January 6 Committee, something she said on the House floor yesterday, as she explained why Steve Bannon`s testimony and her view is so critical. Look at this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY, (R-WY): Mr. Bannon`s own public statements make clear he knew what was going to happen before it did. And thus, he must have been aware of, and may well have been involved in the planning of everything that played out on that day. The American people deserve to know what he knew and what he did.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: So my question is, how much of what Steve Bannon knew is really a proxy for what Donald Trump knew? And can you get that information from other witnesses if executive privilege is tied up in the Court?\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: Well, first of all, we think that Steve Bannon clearly has relevant information, he was reportedly in conversation with the President, he did - - apparently predict that there would be all hell breaking loose on January 6, as in data turned out all hell would break loose.\par \par \uc2 But whatever insights he have -- he has are only going to be a subset of the information about the President`s conduct in the days leading up to that, and including on January 6, so he is certainly not the sole source of information about what took place. And we are approaching others that can share information about that.\par \par \uc2 But I will say this also, his executive privilege claims are very remote and improbable of any success. What`s more, he failed to show up. And that`s not an executive privilege issue, that`s a claim of absolute immunity, and no one enjoys absolute immunity, the Courts have held that repeatedly. So, it`s a very strong case to prosecute, and we certainly hope that Justice Department will soon.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: On the standing that he may or may not have when it comes to executive privilege, Chuck Rosenberg, who is a friend of this show, former U.S. Attorney for the Eastern District of Virginia, he guided our team to a 2007 OLC office of the legal counsel opinion that potentially could pave the way for an executive privilege argument for Bannon.\par \par \uc2 I`m going to read some of it to you, it says this. That the communications involve individuals outside the Executive Branch does not undermine the President`s confidentiality interests. The Communications at issue occurred with the understanding that they would be held in confidence, and they related to decision making regarding U.S. Attorney removals or replacements, or responding to congressional or media inquiries about the U.S. Attorney matters.\par \par \uc2 So, this is a very specific issue relating to 2007, but this idea that the subject didn`t necessarily have to be a part of the Executive Branch, which Bannon certainly wasn`t at the time, is that in any way, a complicating factor?\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: No, I don`t think so at all. That OLC opinion, and of course, is not binding on the current Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 And as far as I`m aware, no court has ever held executive privilege to apply to a Presidential communication with someone who`s not even in the administration.\par \par \uc2 Even for people who are in the administration, it generally doesn`t apply unless they`re top-ranking officials. And even then, when it does apply, it gives way in cases of extreme public interest.\par \par \uc2 And it`s hard to imagine a more extreme public interest than an attack on the Capitol on the seat of our democracy. So, Steve Bannon`s claims are going to fail, they will inevitably fail.\par \par \uc2 But the whole point is to delay and this is why the criminal prosecution is so important. Donald Trump, for four years, did everything he could to stonewall all the subpoenas and delay, this is merely a continuation of that effort, but they can`t have the same success in terms of stonewalling when it comes to prosecution and that`s why our hope and expectation, as I mentioned, is that the Department will pursue it and with vigor.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: I want to ask you about these other Trump loyalists whom the Committee says are cooperating, that`s Mark Meadows and Kash Patel. When the Committee says that these two gentlemen are engaging with the Committee, what does that mean exactly? What level of engagement?\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: Well, it really differs with each of the witnesses that we have subpoenaed, and I can`t go into the particulars, but I can tell you that we`re determined to secure their testimony.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: If we reach the point with any of them where we don`t think that they`re negotiating in good faith, that they`re really trying to run the clock, then we will pursue the same path we did with Steve Bannon.\par \par \uc2 But if we can get to yes, and I think with the -- with some, there is certainly the prospect that we will that, is our preferred course, but we will only wait so long.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Congressman, Adam Schiff, thanks so much for your time. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Coming up next, an update on that tragic fatal movie set shooting as actor Alec Baldwin is now responding to his firing of a prop firearm that resulted in the death of one crew member, and the hospitalization of another.\par \par \uc2 And later, big news from the CDC as the agency expands eligibility for Americans looking to get a COVID booster. That`s all ahead. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Welcome back, tragedy struck a film set in New Mexico yesterday after actor Alec Baldwin discharged a prop gun, killing the film`s director of photography and injuring its director.\par \par \uc2 The Santa Fe Police confirmed that Halyna Hutchins was killed in the incident, the cinematographer was 42 years old. And the spokesperson for director Joel Souza confirmed that he is out of the hospital after receiving emergency treatment.\par \par \uc2 It`s still unclear exactly how the incident unfolded, but a statement from Baldwin`s spokesperson called the shooting an accident and a "misfire" of a prop gun with blanks.\par \par \uc2 Baldwin commented on the incident and Hutchins death for the first time this morning on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , writing this.\par \par \uc2 There are no words to convey my shock and sadness regarding the tragic accident that took the life of Halyna Hutchins, a wife, mother, and deeply admired colleague of ours. I`m fully cooperating with the police investigation to address how this tragedy occurred and I`m in touch with her husband, offering my support to him and his family. My heart is broken for her husband, their son, and to all who knew and loved Halyna.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Production of the film has been suspended, and the police investigation is now ongoing. For more on this story, I`m joined by NBC`s Guad Venegas.\par \par \uc2 So, Guad, this is the first time we heard from Baldwin, what more do you know about the circumstances surrounding this tragic shooting?\par \par \uc2 GUAD VENEGAS, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Right, Geoff. So, the details about what happened have not been revealed. We also now have a statement from the district attorney that says the case is still under preliminary investigation, they are assisting the Santa Fe County Sheriff`s Office, and they have offered their full support. They do also note in that statement that at this time, they don`t know if charges will be filed.\par \par \uc2 Now, Halyna`s agent also sent a statement today, they`ve said that everyone -- innovative artist is heartbroken, the family is mourning and they hope that this tragedy will reveal new lessons on how to better ensure safety for every crew member on set.\par \par \uc2 Now, we know from Halyna`s website that she was originally from Ukraine, where she studied journalism as she worked as an investigative journalist in Europe before coming to the United States where she eventually studied at the \uc2 American Film Institute\uc2  here in Los Angeles, and eventually became a director of photography.\par \par \uc2 Also, from her social media, we know that she had just shared a video on her \uc2 Instagram\uc2  page, just days ago, riding horses on the ranch, saying how happy she was to be shooting at this location because she was able to ride a horse on her day off, and you can just see how much she was enjoying shooting of this film.\par \par \uc2 Now, the details as to what happened exactly in this incident, like I said have not been shared, we just know that the Sheriff`s Office is now involved. And of course like we mentioned, the district attorney is assisting them, Geoff.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Yes, just a tragic case. Guad Venegas, thanks so much for that live update.\par \par \uc2 Up next, with the GOP`s efforts to undermine democracy looming over Washington, I`ll speak with Congresswoman Barbara Lee, the former Chair of the Congressional Black Caucus, about the future of voting rights reform on the Hill. That`s next. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Welcome back. The Biden administration`s fight to expand voting rights is in a precarious position. Earlier this week, GOP Senators successfully filibuster the Democrats` Freedom to Vote Act, leaving the future of that bill up in the air.\par \par \uc2 Senate Majority Leader Chuck Schumer plans to bring Democrats` other voting rights bill, the John Lewis Voting Rights bill, to the floor next week.\par \par \uc2 Right now, Senator Lisa Murkowski is the only Republican who supports it. Voting rights advocates have criticized President Biden for letting the infrastructure packages and other legislation overtake voting rights as a major priority.\par \par \uc2 And at a CNN Town Hall last night, just hours after he spoke at the 10 year anniversary of Washington`s MLK Memorial, the President agreed with his critics.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I tell you what my greatest regret is. My greatest regret is I have these -- had these three major pieces of legislation that are going to change the circumstances for working-class folks, and African-Americans as well, that I`ve been busting my neck trying to pass.\par \par \uc2 But what is done is prevented me from getting deeply up to my ears, which I`m going to do once this is done in dealing with police brutality, dealing with the whole notion of what are we going to do about voting rights, it`s the greatest assault on voting rights in the history of the United States for real since the Civil War.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: For more on voting rights and the fight over the Biden agenda, I`m joined by a Democratic Congresswoman from California, Barbara Lee. Thanks for being with us.\par \par \uc2 And I want to start with your reaction to President Biden basically saying his focus on securing congressional Democratic support for his, you know, economic agenda, really distracted him from pursuing voting rights legislation and policy reform. This is something that his supporters who have been critical of his approach to voting rights have been saying for months.\par \par \uc2 REP. BARBARA LEE, (D-CA): Yes, Geoff, and I think my first reaction is this President is very honest, and he has a lot of integrity. Secondly, we`ve been working on this, though, in Congress in terms of the freedom -- I call it the right, but it`s been changed to the Freedom to Vote Act, as well as the John Lewis Voting Rights Advancement Act.\par \par \uc2 And so, that`s not to say -- I mean, his message, of course, was very clear. But believe you me, the House and the Senate have been working very hard on these bills.\par \par \uc2 And the President has been juggling a lot, he`s building back better on a lot of fronts, and so he was very candid and honest last night on a variety of issues. And this is one that he told the truth.\par \par \uc2 But having said that, let me just say he also talked about the filibuster, and how we`re going to get there. And that`s the path that we`ve been trying to figure out, especially in the Senate for months now.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think you`re going to see all hands on deck, but just know that a lot of work has been put into this up to now to get it positioned and ready for the President to weigh in.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Yes. And let`s play that sound for our viewers who might not have seen that Town Hall. The President talking about how he`s open to scrapping or amending the filibuster if that`s what it takes to enact voting rights.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Well, that remains to be seen. Exactly what that means in terms of fundamentally (INAUDIBLE) or whether or not we just end the filibuster straight up.\par \par \uc2 There are certain things that are just sacred rights. One is a sacred obligation that we never going to renege on a debt, we`re the only nation in the world. And we have never ever reneged on a single debt.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER, CNN ANCHOR: But when it comes to voting rights you --\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Voting right uniquely is consequential.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: -- When it comes to voting right, just so I`m clear, though, you would entertain the notion of doing away with the filibuster on that one issue. Is that correct?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And maybe more.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: And maybe other issues?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: So, if it`s not amending the filibuster, are there any other strategies Democrats are considering right now to make an end-run around the Republican obstruction when it comes to voting rights?\par \par \uc2 You know, might it be having to break out specific elements of the Freedom to Vote Act and trying to pass them one by one?\par \par \uc2 LEE: Well, right now, you see what happened with the Republicans not even allowing the freedom to vote act, to even have a debate. And so, we know that we`ve got to do something because night -- what is the 19 states have passed now, 33 laws that will disenfranchise many, many millions of voters throughout the country.\par \par \uc2 [13:30:00]\par \par \uc2 So we must do something. I think that many have proposed a carveout because our democracy is at stake. It`s very fragile. And so we`ve got to do something.\par \par \uc2 So that may mean carving out a process that allows for the filibuster to be set aside so that we can pass both bills, the Freedom to Vote Act and the John Lewis Voting Rights Advancement Act, or some form of carveout or some form of a waiver or some form of a process that will allow at least the bills that are critical to our democracy, and critical to our right to vote, critical to getting dark money out of politics, critical to making sure our election system is fair, and the gerrymandering stops.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we have got to do something. And so whatever process we come up with, I`m sure that the president will be part of this, and we`ll make sure that he leads us in making sure we do protect our very fragile democracy.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: While we have you, I also want to ask about the president`s domestic agenda and this social spending plan. The White House makes the point that the top line number isn`t as important as what`s in it. I take them at their point. But the overall price tag has been knocked down some $4 trillion since the talks started. In your mind, is it still significant, this package? Does it still meet the moment as Democrats see it? Especially if you`re not able to include the Medicare expansion that the president ultimately initially wanted?\par \par \uc2 LEE: Well, listen, we knew that this was going to be a negotiation from day one. And Geoff, I just have to say this is really an economic agenda. This is the Biden economic agenda because this agenda central to it is the creation of good paying jobs. Good paying union jobs. Ensuring that women and men get back to work because of the childcare provisions.\par \par \uc2 You know, right now over -- close to two million women can`t get back into the work force because they cannot afford childcare. So this really is an economic agenda. And of course, as I`ve been -- I`m a member of the progressive caucus and have had the privilege to meet with the president on three occasions now, we`ve been negotiating, we`ve been talking, and we know that we have to come to consensus with all of our Democrats.\par \par \uc2 It`s a tragedy, the Republicans won`t support the Build Back Better bill, because we know their constituents also are going to create -- be beneficiaries of the good paying jobs. But having said that, we talked a lot about the shortened timeframe. But these investments they are extremely important. You just look at housing. Congresswoman Maxine Waters has been fighting day and night to make sure that we have more pathways to ownership, that we have affordable rental housing and that we preserve and rehab the current rental houses, Section 8 Housing, in many respects is dilapidated. And so you look at all of the issues and investments, we`re negotiating now.\par \par \uc2 But we`re saying that we`re getting close. We know we`re getting close, but the dollar amount, again, for myself, is still not that important, because we have to make sure that these critical investments are going to be immediate for American families are in the Build Back Better bill.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Yes. Congresswoman Barbara Lee, thanks so much for your time. Appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, a big news from the Supreme Court over Texas`s controversial abortion law. More on that after this.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:36:37]\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Welcome back. We have some breaking news out of the Supreme Court. The court has agreed to review Texas`s controversial abortion law that bans all abortions after six weeks. Oral arguments are scheduled for November 1st. But that`s not the only action the court took on this case today.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now with the latest is NBC News Justice correspondent Pete Williams.\par \par \uc2 So, Pete, the Justice Department tried to prevent this law from taking effect. So where does it stand now?\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, NBC NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: The Justice Department that hoped to keep the law from staying in effect of course initially did then it was blocked, now it`s back in effect again. So there`s sort of three moving parts here, Geoff. First of all, the court is denying the federal government`s request for a stay. That means SB-8 will remain in force. And I would think this means it`s going to remain in force for at least several more months while these cases work their way through the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Secondly, the Supreme Court agreed to take on a fast track, a separate appeal that was filed by abortion rights groups in Texas who are challenging the constitutionality of SB-8. That`s one of the cases the court will hear on November 1st. Then the court will also hear a part of the federal government`s challenge to SB-8. There the question isn`t so much SB-8 itself as whether the federal government has the right to bring this lawsuit against the state and if so whether the state court judge had the right to issue an injunction that applied to state court judges, clerks and so forth.\par \par \uc2 So there`s basically two questions the court will consider on November 1st. Is SB-8 constitutional in the state case? And then in the federal government, can the federal government sue over a law like this? And of course the reason that`s important, the federal government said to the Supreme Court earlier this week and again today in a reply brief, they said to the Supreme Court in essence, look, if you let Texas get away with this, then states can pass laws in essence, nullifying Supreme Court decisions but handing off the enforcement to private parties and you`ll never be able to do anything about it.\par \par \uc2 Surely you don`t want to stand around with your hands in your pockets and let that happen. So that was the government`s argument. The Supreme Court now is going to hear these two cases and then, of course, on December 1st, the court takes on the big case of the term on abortion which is the direct challenge to Roe v. Wade that`s brought by Mississippi and its state law that would ban abortion after 15 weeks. So to recap, the Mississippi law is not in effect now. It`s put on hold by the courts. The Texas law is in effect now and will probably remain so for several more months while the court takes up these other questions.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Yes. It would mean in total the court would have three abortion- related cases before it this term.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Pete Williams, thanks so much for that live update.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: At least. Three and counting.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: At least, yes.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: All right. Coming up, some encouraging vaccine news as the CDC expands eligibility for booster shots and signs off on the mix and match approach. What it all means for you, your family, in tackling the pandemic as a whole, is coming up next.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:43:21]\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Welcome back. We`ve been talking about boosters all week and bringing you all of the regulatory updates as they come. And it`s now officially official. The CDC signed off on Moderna and \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  booster shots yesterday. Plus they approved a mix and match approach that allows some folks to get a boost from any three vaccines currently available in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 So who`s eligible for the extra shots? If you got Moderna`s vaccine at least six months ago, you can get a booster if you`re over the age of 65. Adults with underlying medical conditions or frontline jobs are also eligible. For \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  boosters are recommended for all adults over the age of 18 that got the single shot vaccine at least two months ago.\par \par \uc2 Now in today`s briefing the White House COVID Response Team said millions of people fall into those groups and now have access to the extra shots.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is a former policy director under President Obama and MSNBC medical contributor, \uc2 Dr. Kavita Patel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It`s great to see you. Great to have you with us. So you`ve seen the data. Is it just plain better to take the mix and match approach and get some other shot than the one you initially received?\par \par \uc2 DR. KAVITA PATEL\uc2 , MSNBC MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, Geoff, it`s a great question. And the answer is for certain people, absolutely. And I would say that those people are 15 million people who got \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2 . Many of whom have had it for over two months. So they should, 18 and above, as you said, get a booster. And it does look like either of the MRNA vaccines are really great.\par \par \uc2 I know a lot of people, by the way, Geoff, everyone is flooding our offices with calls asking to get Moderna because it felt like looking at the data, Moderna was overwhelming. That was a half dose, just keep that in mind. Not a full dose.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Got it.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:02]\par \par \uc2 PATEL: So eighter way, but yes. Mixing and matching also makes physiologic sense. Different technologies yield kind of a different way of priming the immune system. But again, really the \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  folks stick out. Other folks, I would say get what`s most available around you. Don`t obsess too much between \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  and Moderna. It`s pretty much the same type of effect with those two vaccines.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Yes. As you talk about people rushing your office, there was a headline out today saying that more people are getting their COVID boosters than are getting the first shot. I want to ask about where we are overall in the life of this pandemic. Because even as the numbers are trending in the right direction here at home, overseas, cases in the UK are spiking. Russia is in total lockdown mode right now. Is this pandemic further away from being over than we think?\par \par \uc2 PATEL: It is. And Geoff, there`s good news in having the ability to boost ourselves and get better protection writ large. And then we`re going to have a conversation next week I`m sure about pediatric vaccines. That`s up in front of the FDA advisory committee. But to your point, even though 6.2 billion people have received at least one dose, many of those doses have lower efficacy. So we`re seeing like you said hospitalizations.\par \par \uc2 That`s what`s driving a lot of the UK, we think. A combination of decreasing efficacy from their original shots and then the relaxation of all the restrictions. And then to your point, I think the rest of the globe is incredibly vulnerable. They haven`t had a shot. And so this gives us an environment that`s ripe for those variants much like the Delta variant that could potentially even escape our immunity.\par \par \uc2 That`s kind of the fear that keeps a lot of us in public health up. Haven`t seen any evidence of it yet. That`s good news. But we can`t kind of put the cart before the horse, so to speak. Because we`ve got a world to vaccinate. I don`t think we`re going to get there until 2023. But the United States is going to certainly benefit from our rates of vaccination as long as we can keep them going up and not down.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Yes. When it comes to the rates of vaccination, I want to ask about COVID shots in kids. Because \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  reported data today showing that its COVID vaccine had a 90.7 percent efficacy rate in preventing symptomatic COVID-19 among kids ages 5 to 11. What are you going to watch for as the FDA really starts to pick apart this data?\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Yes. The most important thing I think are going to be some of those safety signals. We`ve talked you and I about myocarditis, inflammation of the heart. We see that in about one in 15,000 cases. But particularly in younger adults of certain age. We`re going to want to look for those rates in children. Not as common in children. But it`s something that parents are likely going to consider. So number one, kind of side effects, and then number two, how can we judge, you know, relatively speaking, how many kids need to get vaccinated to prevent one hospitalization? Symptomatic COVID is very important.\par \par \uc2 But those are the conversations parents are going to be asking. They`re also going to be asking about whether or not they should wait. Lots of parents I know are asking should they wait several weeks? I think they`ll come up in the conversation next week should we have a certain subgroup be prioritized amongst 5 to 11-year-olds so just it ready for all of them. I`ll be looking to see the discussion around that as well.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Doctor Patel, thanks so much as always. Appreciate that great context and clarity.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, dire new warnings from Florida election officials to elected leaders and candidates. Stop undermining democracy. We`ll speak with the Republican leading that fight coming up next.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:51:29]\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Two lawyers that backed former President Donald Trump`s lawsuits over the 2020 election results now hold two of the most powerful positions in Texas. One of them, Jon Scott, was just named secretary of state by Governor Greg Abbott. Now he briefly joined Trump`s baseless legal challenges to Pennsylvania`s election results before the lawsuit was thrown out. Mr. Scott will now oversee Texas elections including the limited 2020 election review Governor Abbott ordered in four of the state`s counties.\par \par \uc2 Then there`s Ken Paxton. The outspoken Texas attorney general who tried unsuccessfully to get the courts to overturn Joe Biden`s victroy in four states. Paxton was in El Paso yesterday doubling down on Trump`s election lies. At one point agreeing that Joe Biden`s victory amounted to a, quote, "overthrow."\par \par \uc2 This rhetoric is a danger to democracy, but many Trump supporters and Republican leaders continue to embrace it. It`s gotten so bad that even in Florida, a state that Donald Trump won by more than 350,000 votes, the most Republican supervisors of elections say they`ve received threats. They wrote a letter this week imploring all candidates and elected officials to, quote, "tone down the rhetoric and stand up for our democracy, to commit themselves to the goal of fighting falsehoods and strengthening voter trust. Our democracy depends on it."\par \par \uc2 Wesley Wilcox is the Republican president of the Florida Supervisors of Elections, which is the bipartisan association behind that letter, and he joins us now.\par \par \uc2 So your letter, and I`ve read it is urgent. You say democracy is being undermined as we speak. Are you afraid that Florida voters aren`t getting that message? They don`t share that sense of urgency?\par \par \uc2 WESLEY WILCOX (R), PRESIDENT, FLORIDA SUPERVISORS OF ELECTIONS: I`m afraid so, Geoff. You know, this is really not a Republican or Democratic issue. This is a bipartisan issue. You know, if we allow a few to convince our voters that their voting voice is not being heard or it`s irrelevant to the outcome, what`s their next logical step? They stay home and they don`t participate. You know, this country was built on participation.\par \par \uc2 Too many people have fought and died for us to have that opportunity. And I`m just not going to stand idly by and watch these vocal few destroy our country.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: So you`re a Republican. I mean, what do you make of your party`s embrace of Donald Trump`s election lies? I mean, do you see that as a danger to democracy itself?\par \par \uc2 WILCOX: You know, once again, I don`t see it as a party embracing. You know, I`ve heard our governor, I`ve heard our secretary of state, myself, you know, the election that we conducted in the state of Florida was by far the most secure election we`ve ever done. In the words of Governor DeSantis, you know, the way Florida did it should be the role model for the rest of the country, and I firmly believe that.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Your letter actually appears to purposely not name the former president by name, for example you write this. "During and after the 2020 presidential election, the integrity of our democracy has been challenged by misinformation, disinformation, and mal-information that sows discord and undermines trust in America`s electoral process. Many of us have been threatened by our fellow citizens who have been led astray by these deceptions."\par \par \uc2 There is a lot of passive voice in that first statement. Why not call Donald Trump out by name?\par \par \uc2 [13:55:00]\par \par \uc2 WILCOX: Actually if you look at former President Trump`s statements, he goes on record, just as recently as four or five weeks ago, stating the way Florida did it, once again, ran a clean election. So I don`t believe it`s our former president. I believe other groups that are leading many good people astray with misinformation. And so that`s kind of our take on it, that it`s words out there that are doing -- that are attempting to perpetuate this fallacy and this misinformation, disinformation. And it`s not doing any of us any good.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: What`s your response to what`s happening in Texas, that the Texas state attorney general, Ken Paxton, calling Joe Biden`s victory, an election he won fairly, an overthrow?\par \par \uc2 WILCOX: I`m not going to comment on that. Texas, they do their stuff. I`m a state of Florida election official. And I`ll keep my comments to the state of Florida.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WILCOX: I know -- I think this is the best place in the country, so no disrespect to Texas, though.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: So this letter that you sent it was only sent to elected officials and the candidates there in Florida. Why not send it to Florida voters?\par \par \uc2 WILCOX: That`s coming. That will be our next step. Next week, we are sending a letter to the voters of the state of Florida ensuring them of the integrity of our system. You know, a lot of people forget that we, too, all of us elected officials here, are voters here in the state of Florida. And what`s more important than your own personal vote? And, you know, so I`ve got integrity in the system.\par \par \uc2 I use the system, I vote by mail, I voted at an early voting site. You know, so that letter to the voters in general is coming next week.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: All right, Wesley Wilcox, thanks so much for your time, appreciate you joining us.\par \par \uc2 And one last thing before we go, another sneak peek at Chuck`s interview with California Governor Gavin Newsom airing this Sunday on "MEET THE PRESS." Among the difficult decisions facing him right now is deciding the fate of Sirhan Sirhan. A two-person panel ruled in August that the man who assassinated \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy\uc2  in 1968 is suitable for parole. The California Parole Board staff is now reviewing that recommendation and the governor can then approve, deny, or amend the decision. Chuck asked the governor about that pending decision.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: The Sirhan Sirhan decision, is this a decision?\par \par \uc2 GOV. GAVIN NEWSOM (D), CALIFORNIA: That`s a tough one.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What makes it tough?\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: I mean, it`s interesting you ask the question. I don`t laugh to be dismissive, I laugh because that`s a hard one for me.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s all on you.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: \uc2 Bobby Kennedy\uc2  is my political hero. I mean, you look at my house. Looks like a shrine to the Kennedy family. And Bobby in particular.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And we wonder why some of us ask you if you`re interested in running for president.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: Well, I`m just -- but I`m attached to the evocative idealism of the `60s. I mean, there`s something about that service contribution. I mean, there`s something beautiful there that we need to attach ourselves to again. But that said, this is hard because I believe in redemption, I believe in second chances. At the same time, man, he took away dreams. He took away a lot of hope. And this country, this world, is radically changed as a consequence. And I have to factor that in. So I --\par \par \uc2 TODD: What about the family?\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: This is hard.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But the family. Some you could say they`re not divided evenly. Does that matter to you?\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: Profoundly. Yes. Come on, Bobby`s wife.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: If I want to preview a point of view, that`s going to be profoundly determined.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ethel Kennedy matters a lot.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: If Ethel Kennedy call me up, and express her point of view, there`s weight and there`s a different level, and weightiness of weight. And that`s a call from Ether Kennedy. And I`m not looking for that call.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: But I`m very desirous to get a better understanding of where she is (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 TODD: When do you have to make this decision?\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: It hasn`t been formally brought to my desk so it`s a matter of months but it`s coming up and it`s stirring a lot of interest. I`ll tell you, it`s really -- you asked this question. I`ve gotten e-mails and text messages from folks that are some of the most heartfelt and deep and emotional.\par \par \uc2 TODD: On both sides of the issue?\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: On both sides of this issue. It hits home because --\par \par \uc2 TODD: John Hinckley is out right now. Does that matter to you in this decision?\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: People brought that up.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: It matters. But Bobby`s loss carries a different weight. And again, I`ll remind you, not only do I have a shrine of sorts, that`s exaggerated, but pictures of \uc2 Bobby Kennedy\uc2 , but the most valuable thing I own in my life is a picture of my dad, who is passed away, with \uc2 Bobby Kennedy\uc2 , signed by mother, who passed away, "Tessa from \uc2 Bobby Kennedy\uc2 ," and it was done just a few months before he died. So this one is a tough one.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: And that does it for us this hour. We`ll be back Monday with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020211022eham0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE RACHEL MADDOW SHOW for October 18, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Rachel Maddow, Michael Beschloss \par \uc2 8,180 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 October 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Rachel Maddow Show\par \uc2 TRMADS\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [21:00:05]\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST: It boosts your protection against reinfection and also provides a comprehensive way to boost immunity in the future that is not just, you know, a natural reinfection.\par \par \uc2 DR. SYRA MADAD, INFECTIOUS DISEASE EPIDEMIOLOGIST: That`s absolutely much more reliable, much more durable. We`ll probably see the longevity of our immune response. You`re able to boost more in case that`s needed, but it`s better than severe hospitalization or death.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right. Dr. Syra Madad, that was very helpful. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 That is "ALL IN" for this Monday night.\par \par \uc2 THE RACHEL MADDOW SHOW starts right now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Chris. Thank you, my friend. Much appreciated.\par \par \uc2 And thanks to you at home for joining us this hour. Happy to have you here.\par \par \uc2 President Biden tonight has ordered flags across the country flown at half staff to honor the legacy of General Colin Powell who has died today at the age of 84. Because of General Powell`s remarkable record of service to the United States, his status is just a singular figure in modern public service, American flags will fly at half staff in Colin Powell`s honor not for a day but for every day this week until sunset on Friday night.\par \par \uc2 General Powell`s legacy is an important, powerful, complex thing that operates on a lot of different levels. We`re going to have much more on his passing coming up over the course of this hour. He`s just about the only American I can think of who was not a U.S. president but for whom you nevertheless need to consult a presidential historian to talk about his role in our country and his role in modern history, how he changed this country as a singular figure. We`re going to do that later on this hour. We`ve got much more ahead on that coming up.\par \par \uc2 We are also following a number of developing stories tonight. The committee investigating the January 6 attack on the U.S. Capitol tonight has released a formal report recommending something that hasn`t been done in nearly 40 years.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump`s campaign manager and one-time White House advisor \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  has lately settled into a sort of marginal role as a right- wing podcaster and effectively a Trump surrogate. He travels around the country promoting the myth that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  really didn`t win the 2020 election. Former President Trump is going to somehow be reinstated. \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  has become like the My Pillow guy but without the pillows.\par \par \uc2 It was less than a year ago that Trump pardoned him for the felony fraud charges. He was facing for his role in a fake border wall fundraising scheme that appears to have separated lots of gullible Trump supporters from lots of their money. Prosecutors that money just ended up going into the pockets of \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  and the co-defendants. The co-defendants, interestingly, were not pardoned by Trump. And so, the case against them continues. But Bannon got off scot-free.\par \par \uc2 Bannon has since decided to refuse to comply with a subpoena to appear before the committee investigating the January 6 attack. And subpoenas are not requests, right? They`re not optional. They`re mandatory things.\par \par \uc2 But Bannon has decided to defy the one that he received from the January 6 investigation. And tonight, the January 6 -- the committee investigating the January 6 attack, they just released a report tonight which recommends that Bannon should, therefore, be referred to the Justice Department for prosecution. Now, the committee is going to formally vote tomorrow night to make that referral for prosecution. Then what will happen is that the Justice Department, specifically the U.S. attorney for Washington, D.C., will have to decide what to do about it.\par \par \uc2 It has been nearly 40 years since a former government official has been prosecuted for denying a subpoena like this. But hey, you never know. Couldn`t happen to a nicer guy.\par \par \uc2 That high drama tonight, around Trump`s former campaign manager, he has already had to pardon once, that drama comes as former President Trump himself brought a lawsuit this afternoon against that same committee investigating the January 6th attack. He is basically trying to stop the investigation from getting access to his White House records. Records related to the attack, he filed that lawsuit tonight. We shall see.\par \par \uc2 But he filed that lawsuit tonight on what is turning out to be a big day for his legal fees already. Before he filed the lawsuit tonight, earlier this afternoon, Trump completed a four and a half hours sworn deposition in one of the roughly ten civil lawsuits that is currently pending against him. This lawsuit relates to anti-Trump protesters who say that they were subject to violence from Trump`s private security guards, at a protest outside of Trump tower or in 2015.\par \par \uc2 [21:05:02]\par \par \uc2 These plaintiffs allege that Trump encourage violence against people who protested against him. They allege that Trump is liable for the violence that they experienced in part that, because the private security guards, involved in this violence, worked for him and he is responsible for his employees conduct. The suit has been around for several years. It has been pending against Trump for a long time. In this case and in several of their cases he was able to delay having to give any deposition, or any other testimony in these cases, because he was serving as president. But now that he`s no longer serving as president the floodgates are starting to open, and starting to appear for these things.\par \par \uc2 For example, he`s facing another potential deposition between now in the end of the year. In a defamation case brought against him by a woman named Summer Zervos. Ms. Zervos says Trump defamed her when he denied her claims that Trump had sexually assaulted her. It is due to be deposed for that case by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 He is facing another potential deposition, and potentially a DNA test, and a similar case brought by a different woman, E. Jean Carroll, who also sells that Trump defamed her when he denied her claims that he raped her.\par \par \uc2 He is facing another potential deposition in a major fraud case brought against him, by people who said that he was duped into investigating in a bunch of worthless schemes because Trump endorsed them, that case again ongoing, they`ve been seeking a step position now that he is no longer president. That may even have been.\par \par \uc2 No, there is a bunch more of these. I can go on. There`s a lot of these.\par \par \uc2 Before today, the last time that he was actually deposed in one of the civil suits was in 2015. When he was elected in 2016, so that`s before he became president. And that case, he was deposed in conjunction with another fraud case brought against him related to his Trump University scam, which he ran for years.\par \par \uc2 That case did not end well. He gave that deposition as part of his defense in that lawsuit, but ultimately, in the end, he paid out $25 million to settle that case, paid out $25 million. Right before he was sworn in as president.\par \par \uc2 I almost felt like that was sort underplayed us for Trump ascending to the presidency. We should`ve noon at the time that that was auspicious for right before his presidency. Who else pays out $25 million to settle a massive fraud suit against him right before becoming president? We should`ve known, that should`ve giving us an inkling as to how things were going.\par \par \uc2 But that was in 2015. And the Trump university fraud case that cost him $25 million, that was the last time he had to do one of these depositions before the one he did today. All the other ones were put on pause while he was president. As of today, they have started back up. So we are watching that.\par \par \uc2 Also as of tonight, we got eyes on the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 , the \uc2 U.S. Justice Department\uc2  has gone into the Supreme Court on the issue of Texas`s abortion ban. You`ll recall that the Supreme Court allowed that Texas abortion ban to go into effect in September, last month. The Justice Department is fighting it.\par \par \uc2 Today, DOJ basically asked the Supreme Court to look at the case from a different angle. Then the way they previously looked. That Justice Department wants the Supreme Court to block the law basically on the grounds that Texas can`t do something that deliberately violates the U.S. Constitution, and block the federal courts from reviewing it.\par \par \uc2 So again, the Justice Department could`ve gone back to a lower appeals court, they have decided instead to try their luck with the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 . It appears that the Supreme Court has some appetite to potentially fold that in with the other major abortion cases that is coming their way. Before the end of the year, a Mississippi abortion case with antiabortion candidates, will be the vehicle by which come conservative Supreme Court`s finally eradicate the protections of Roe v. Wade once and for all.\par \par \uc2 We shall see. Eyes on the Supreme Court in that case, tonight as well.\par \par \uc2 So there`s a lot going on. And on top of all of that, when it comes to the Democratic Party, and the Democratic Party trying to get its act together, trying to unite around a strategy to pass the legislative agenda for which President Biden was elected, on this long saga that we have been watching of the Democrats, mostly coalescing around President Biden`s agenda, while some holdouts basically stop progress towards these things actually passing Congress. Today, a remarkable thing happened. Today, the people you actually want to be in the same room together talking one-on-one, fighting it up between them, today those people finally did get in the room together, personally, one-on-one.\par \par \uc2 [21:10:01]\par \par \uc2 Let me explain what happened. These are the first real signs that the heretofore, frozen negotiations about how to pass President Biden`s agenda, those frozen negotiations maybe starting to thaw.\par \par \uc2 Here is the situation. Tomorrow, the White House is scheduled to hold another round of meetings, separate meetings, with Democratic moderate, and Democratic progressives, to talk about path forward of the Biden agenda, for the Build Back Better Act. Today, ahead of those meetings, President Biden met separately with one key progressive Democrat, Congresswoman \uc2 Pramila Jayapal\uc2 , who is the chair of the progressive caucus.\par \par \uc2 Throughout this whole progress, Congresswoman \uc2 Pramila Jayapal\uc2 , has been the lead negotiator for the progressives. She successfully mobilized the progressive caucus to withhold their support for a much smaller bipartisan infrastructure bill as a means for making sure that the much Bigger Build Back Better act would also get a vote. That it would not be killed in the \uc2 United States senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now, she has been the lead on the progressive side. On the moderate side, on the centrist side, there is not really a need for a single lead negotiator, because all the centrist Democrats trying to trim down this bill could frankly fitted into a forester and could not fight over who gets known cup holder. There just aren`t that many of them.\par \par \uc2 On the progressive side, we`ve got Congresswoman \uc2 Pramila Jayapal\uc2 , effectively acting as an emissary, for the nearly 100 members of the progressive caucus. But really for all the Democrats in Washington, who are all but united around want to see President Biden`s agenda pass as possible. For that reason alone, it`s notable that Congresswoman \uc2 Pramila Jayapal\uc2 , was at the White House today for more talks directly between her and President Biden.\par \par \uc2 What is even more interesting in newsworthy, as what she did after her meeting with President Biden. It was NBC News`s \uc2 Garrett Haake\uc2 , who was first to report this afternoon, that Congresswoman \uc2 Pramila Jayapal\uc2 , after meeting with President Biden today, she then held a two-hour meeting with the other guy had named Joe who`s in the middle of this trauma. Congresswoman \uc2 Pramila Jayapal\uc2  met for two hours with Senator \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 . Finally, the two people who need to be in the same room fighting this out are in the same room fighting this out.\par \par \uc2 Senator Manchin is, of course, one of the two, count them, two, Democratic senators who have been blocking this thing, who have been insisting that President Biden`s agenda should not pass. He and Kyrsten Sinema, of Arizona, want to shrink the presidents build. The Build Back Better bill, they want to strip out some of its provisions, or we aren`t exactly sure how much they want to strip, out or what they want to, shrink or what they want to be smaller, or if they care about it being smaller. Depends on what they want, depends on what kind of move they`re in.\par \par \uc2 Over the weekend, it looked like Senator Manchin was starting to ratchet up his resistance to present the president`s agenda at all. "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " reporting on Friday that the core climate provision of the bill we`re likely to be stripped out of it because of Joe Manchin`s singular opposition to those.\par \par \uc2 On Sunday, yesterday, "Axios" reported that he had come out with a whole new list of ways that he wanted to cut down the very popular child tax credit. That Democrats passed on the COVID relief bill and want to make permanent.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, everybody else at the party`s frustration with \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  seemed to have reached a bit of a tipping point. Friday night, Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 , another key progressive in this negotiation out of the budget committee and the Senate, he wrote an up at for Senator Manchin`s hometown paper in West Virginia, "The Charleston Gazette Mail" explaining why he thought that was Virginians would benefit from the key provisions of the Build Back Better Act, which Senator Manchin opposes.\par \par \uc2 The stuff that Senator Manchin says he does not, like Senator Sanders wrote that all of those things would help West Virginia. He wrote, quote, pull off their poll shows overwhelming support for this legislation. Get the political problem we face is that in a 50/50 Senate we need every Democratic senator to vote yes. We now have only 48 yes votes, two Democratic senators remain in opposition, including, West Virginia Senator \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Again, Senator Biden -- excuse me, Senator Sanders, publishing that in Senator Manchin`s hometown paper in West Virginia, which Senator Manchin very much did not like, he doesn`t like anybody else talking to West Virginia except for him. He responded to that in a sniffy statement that said in part, quote, this isn`t the first time an out of state or has tried to tell West Virginians what`s bad for them, despite having no relationship to our state. I will not vote for a reckless expansion of government programs. No op-ed from a self-declared independent socialist is going to change that.\par \par \uc2 So, all of that made it seem like the progress for Democrats figuring something out, coming to some kind of resolution seemed like the prospects were getting more and more remote, as Senator Manchin seem to be more and more dug in, and more and more prickly about it all the time.\par \par \uc2 [21:15:11]\par \par \uc2 But then, the time today happened. I mean, for the first time since these talks began, you have the lead progressive negotiator, Congresswoman \uc2 Pramila Jayapal\uc2 , somehow managing to get into the same room as one of the two elusive senators holding up this deal.\par \par \uc2 From the very beginning, it was felt those of us who aren`t politician, those of us who watch this because we care about the policy, and because we care about the human drama of it, those of us who aren`t politicians have sort of instinctively felt, couldn`t Jayapal and Manchin just talk about this, and kind of figure it out? They both seem like they have an argument to make, what if they just made it to each other without anybody else there, couldn`t that get us there? Couldn`t Senator Sanders and Senator Manchin`s in the same room, talk about it, one-on-one?\par \par \uc2 Obviously, they`ve got both got points to make. If they talk about a one- on-one, in the same room, at length, could they get somewhere? Those of us watching from the cheap sheets have sort of had that instinct all along.\par \par \uc2 While today, those conversations finally happened. Sources telling NBC News tonight, that during the conversation between Jayapal and Manchin, each of them laid out their priorities for the Build Back Better bill. They reportedly did not get into a specific back and forth over how they were going to resolve their differences. But they each laid out their case to one another, directly, in the same room, at length.\par \par \uc2 And again, this was the first meeting between Jayapal and Manchin and it went on quite a while apparently, about two hours.\par \par \uc2 And, also, today Senator Manchin held another one-on-one meeting, with senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 . Following that meeting, a notably should chipper Senator Manchin, even stop to pose for a picture with Senator Sanders while the two were leaving the capital walk into their cars.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. BERNIE SANDERS\uc2  (I-VT): I want to get a picture of us?\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN\uc2  (D-WV): We`re talking.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: We`re talking.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: You`re going to have a resolution by the end of the week?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: We`re talking.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: We`re talking.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Senator, are you prepare to make some deep cuts to climate change and other things like that?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Sort of heard Senator Manchin sign of say something to Senator Sanders there as they both got in their cars.\par \par \uc2 According to NBC News Capitol Hill producer Frank Thorp, who was there at the moment, what`s Manchin said there, to senator Sanders was quote, never give up Bernie. What does that mean?\par \par \uc2 Just before we got on the air, CNN reported that in addition to Senator Manchin meeting with Senator Jayapal today, I`m meeting with Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  today, Senator Manchin also apparently, had a direct phone call with President Biden today. Does this mean that things are changing? What does it mean that they keep progressives in this fight are now meeting one- on-one, and directly, with Senator Manchin? Who was the guy who was -- got his thumb in the dike here.\par \par \uc2 What`s he saying to them? One of these meetings like? Do these indicate progress?\par \par \uc2 I have just a person asked. Joining us now is Congresswoman \uc2 Pramila Jayapal\uc2 . She is chair of the Congressional Progressive Caucus, and she has had quite a day.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Jayapal, it`s a real pleasure to have you here. Thanks for making time.\par \par \uc2 REP. PRAMILA JAYAPAL\uc2  (D-WA): It`s great to see you, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: So, again, I`m watching from the cheap seats, as is everybody in the country who is a stakeholder in this fight, who cares about this policy, who knows how transformative they have this bill would be if it passes. And even people who aren`t invested in the policy, who just can`t believe the drama and have this ruling out across personalities and across time.\par \par \uc2 What can you tell us about that meeting with Senator Manchin, and how we should understand it in terms of whether this thing is going to get done?\par \par \uc2 JAYAPAL: Well, Rachel, I have said for sometime that we`re going to get them both done. We are going to get them done. It`s a messy process.\par \par \uc2 Democracy is not always easy. Negotiation is not always easy. There are differences, everybody knows that there are differences. We got to bridge them and we have to come together, because at the end of the day, we have to deliver both of these bills. The infrastructure bill and the Build Back Better act to the president`s desk.\par \par \uc2 I have always been to happy to talk to anybody. It was great to spend time with Senator Manchin today. I am not going to get into the details of what we talked about, but I just think it is important for us to be talking to each other. I felt that way for some time. And, I think that those conversations are important to have. Because we`re not going to be able to make progress endless we all talk to each other.\par \par \uc2 And so, that`s what today was about. Also, meeting with the president and really, again, this is the president`s agenda and I`m really proud of the progressive caucus for what we did to actually get the Build Back Better Act back on the table.\par \par \uc2 [21:20:01]\par \par \uc2 And have us negotiating on it. Have a real discussion around it. Because unlike the bipartisan bill, which you and I talked about, and I think we were both kind of skeptical honestly that it was going to get done, but it took five months, and eventually it got done. But, this also has really only been two weeks of negotiation. It`s only since the progressive caucus said we`re not going to do one without the other that we then were able to get people to take this negotiation seriously. And come to the table.\par \par \uc2 And that`s what we`re doing right now. I`m not saying it`s going to -- I don`t know that it`s going to be done