even though he thought there might be WMDs.\par \par \uc2 And at the same time, if you were trying to torture Colin Powell, what is the one thing you would take away from him? His authority. His credibility. And that is what that speech did.\par \par \uc2 It wasn`t winning because he believed that the intelligence that he had studied, they had discard a lot of intelligence of what was wrong, but the result was that the one moment of that decade that he would be remembered for would be that speech to the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . and he bore that grief to his grave.\par \par \uc2 [21:45:10]\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Indeed. Michael Beschloss, NBC News presidential historian, thank you so much for being here, Michael. It is just a remarkable loss for our country but his legacy is going to be studied for a long time. I mean, the good and the bad of it. But thank you for helping us do it tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 BESCHLOSS: He was flawed, great man. Thank you so much Rachel. Be well.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: It was ten days before \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was inaugurated as president, when "\uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2  News" published what has come to be known as they dossier, a series of intelligence reports compiled by a former British spy named \uc2 Christopher Steele\uc2 , alleging coordination between the Trump campaign and the Russian government, as Russia was trying to help get \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  into the White House.\par \par \uc2 [21:50:01]\par \par \uc2 The allegations in that dossier have been debated and litigated and investigated and demagogued ever since. To this day, it`s still being investigated by Bill Barr appointed special counsel, \uc2 John Durham\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But now, two years after the release of the Mueller report, and after being relentlessly attacked and dragged through the mud by President Trump and his allies, \uc2 Christopher Steele\uc2  himself is finally speaking for himself, in an 