notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020211015ehae00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Moderna, J&J Boosters Could Roll Out Within Weeks; Anti-Trump Republicans Announce Support for Democrats; What`s Happening to Evangelicals in the Post-Trump Era, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Anne Thompson, Sahil Kapur, Pete Williams, Monica  Alba \par \uc2 8,799 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 October 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: -- him and I are doing everything we can. But I`m calling on more businesses to step up. I`m calling on more parents to get their children vaccinated when they are eligible. And I`m asking everyone, everyone who hasn`t gotten vaccinated, please get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 That`s how we put this pandemic behind us and accelerate our economic recovery. We can do this, I said many times. God bless you all, and may God protect our troops. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: You just watched President Biden there do an update on the vaccination program selling the mandates.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, he wants to keep encourage more mandates. We know the federal government is going to be writing its rules soon. He wanted to reassure that there were plenty of shots as well. And obviously he didn`t take any questions.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to have more on the pandemic itself coming up, and the latest on the booster shot situation.\par \par \uc2 But right now, on the other end of Pennsylvania Avenue, we`re watching a number of subpoena deadlines come and possibly go.\par \par \uc2 And today is that deadline. It`s the House`s January 6 Committee that seeks a trove of witness, testimony, and documents from Trump allies tied to the January 6 insurrection.\par \par \uc2 The former President has told them all not to cooperate, so the Committee is not holding its breath for members of the former President`s inner circle, including Steve Bannon, Mark Meadows, Dan Scavino, and Kash Patel.\par \par \uc2 I`m not sure if they were going to comply with subpoena deadlines today and tomorrow for testimony. And that`s just the tip of the iceberg in terms of who the Committee is targeting.\par \par \uc2 Members of the Committee have crisscrossed the airwaves in the halls of Capitol Hill in recent days who are warning Trump allies that if they don`t cooperate, there will be consequences.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF, (D-CA): If witnesses don`t appear when they`re supposed to if they don`t produce the documents, we intend to move very quickly to have a vote in the House, to hold them in criminal contempt or for it to the Justice Department for prosecution.\par \par \uc2 REP. STEPHANIE MURPHY, (D-FL): I would recommend the full extent of consequences. Jail time, fines, we need to make sure that these people understand that this is not acceptable.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN, (D-MD): Americans across the country should ask themselves what they would do if they had a subpoena to turn over documents or materials were to appear in Court. They would go where they understand that the marshals would show up at their door the next day.\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY, (R-WY): Every single person on the Committee recognizes how important it is for us to make sure that we ensure -- we enforce our subpoenas and that we do so expeditiously.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: For what it`s worth not complying, only raises the specter of what do you got to hide? But will they go after them in a big way?\par \par \uc2 It`s all big talk right now, but to make good on most of those threats, the January 6 Committee is going to need the blessing of Biden`s Justice Department and the Attorney General Merrick Garland because any criminal prosecution for stonewalling a subpoena would come from federal law enforcement, not Congress.\par \par \uc2 So, the big question is, is Garland on board with this? Democrats on the Committee are basically saying he`s going to have to be.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF, (D-CA): When Bill Barr was Attorney General, there was no way he was going to prosecute someone who was covering up for Donald Trump. He viewed the Justice Department as essentially Trump`s criminal defense firm.\par \par \uc2 It`s different now, different Attorney General, one that believes in the rule of law. So, we intend to enforce these subpoenas.\par \par \uc2 I think if someone thumbs or knows at the law doesn`t comply is prosecuted, it will be a powerful message to others that they darn well better comply.\par \par \uc2 REP. STEPHANIE MURPHY, (D-FL): I fully expect this Department of Justice to uphold and enforce that subpoena because I think this Department of Justice believes that nobody is above the law.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: NBC`s Sahil Kapur is on Capitol Hill, and with me set, our Justice Correspondent Pete Williams.\par \par \uc2 Sahil, the deadlines are today. Steve Bannon was holding a rally in Virginia, so he was close. He`s within driving distance of showing up today. What do we know? And when do -- when does the Committee decide they haven`t complied? Is it five o`clock?\par \par \uc2 SAHIL KAPUR, MSNBC NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER: Chuck, there is a growing likelihood that this criminy -- that this Committee is going to put out criminal referrals to the Justice Department to prosecute these individuals.\par \par \uc2 Steve Bannon is the first to make abundantly clear in a letter from his legal team that he`s not going to comply.\par \par \uc2 He is citing executive privilege claims made by former President Trump which the White House does not accept, which this Committee does not accept.\par \par \uc2 Now, you heard what those Democrats are saying, they`re making it abundantly clear that they`re going this route and that they`re going to send a message to everyone that they need to comply.\par \par \uc2 Their word is going to be tested here because it`s not just the Committee, it`s not just the House that has to do it, but as you point out, the Justice Department, a U.S. Attorney in DC has to make those prosecutions happen, has to issue those fines, those jail times to send that message.\par \par \uc2 Now, of course, Bannon`s reason for refusing to comply has nothing to do with the proximity to Washington D.C. He can show up here and he can talk to the Committee if he wants to. He feels like he doesn`t need to, and he feels like he can get away with it, it`s going to be up to the Committee and the DOJ to prove him wrong.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Mark Meadows, Sahil, is a former member of Congress, Kash Patel worked on Congressional Committees since, been on the other end of oversight requests.\par \par \uc2 All of the conversations around those two has been they`re talking. What is -- are they try -- are they trying to find a compromise to comply?\par \par \uc2 (13:05:00)\par \par \uc2 KAPUR: It is notable, Chuck, that those two men, Kash Patel and Mark Meadows have been described by the January 6 Committee as engaging.\par \par \uc2 Now, that could mean a lot of things. They haven`t clarified whether that means fully cooperating. They haven`t said whether they intend to -- whether they have provided those documents that they had a deadline of doing last week.\par \par \uc2 Patel is supposed to testify today, the Committee hasn`t said whether he is showing up, Meadows is expected to testify tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Meadows of course, yes, a member of Congress, he knows these halls very well, he`s walked them for many years, but he is also now firmly on the side of Donald Trump. Put up -- hoop -- by the way, put out a statement last night lavishing praise on --\par \par \uc2 TODD: I saw all that.\par \par \uc2 KAPUR: -- Meadows as Chief of Staff. I don`t want to try to get in the President`s head here, but it seems unlikely to me that Donald Trump would do that if he thought Mark Meadows was going to comply. So, a lot of this happening at the same time, and the pressure increasing here on this January 6 Committee.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. You know, that had the feel of the boss putting his arm around Mark Meadows, remember who you work for. I saw that same release and had the same reaction.\par \par \uc2 Sahil Kapur on Capitol Hill for us, Sahil, I know you`ve got some more reporting to run to and I`ll let you go.\par \par \uc2 Let me bring in Pete Williams here. All right, Pete, we know when they -- it looks like they`re going to have at least one criminal referral for Steve Bannon, possibly more. So, walk us to the next step. Now it goes to where`s the U.S. Attorney of the District of Columbia, technically?\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, MSNBC JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: Yes. So, Congress votes to find someone in contempt. There`s a statute that was passed in 1857 that says when Congress does that, the U.S. Attorney in Washington shall present the matter to a Grand Jury.\par \par \uc2 Now, since 1984, the Justice Department`s position has been what you can`t make us do anything, we`ll consider that it`s a matter of our own discretion. The fact is, the Justice Department has never ref -- never prosecuted anybody who cites executive privilege.\par \par \uc2 But this is a complicated case because this is a former official of a former President, whose assertion of executive privilege, at least in one instance, has already been rejected by the current President, and the Supreme Court has said the current President is very influential in this process, doesn`t -- isn`t totally in control --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, it is. The office of the presidency gets executive privilege, not the individuals who were President, right?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: -- that`s right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: But we know from the Nixon case in 1977, when he sued the archives over this, that -- a former President has some right to assert executive privilege. But when the current President goes the other way and undercuts -- doesn`t completely eliminate their claim, but it undercuts them.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Steve Bannon hasn`t worked for -- didn`t work in the White House for almost three years.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK. So, it seems to me that the executive privilege on that is very hard to defend. I don`t think he`s got much of a case. So, what happens next? Is this going to court, they arrest him, Grand Jury indicts him, what is the process?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: So, you know, the Justice Department`s view of course -- of course, when any prosecution is, well, we`ll take it case by case. If I had to guess, directly a guess, I would think this is a pretty good likelihood that the Justice Department, in fact, will file charges here.\par \par \uc2 Because look, what they`ve done so far, they`ve already waived executive privilege on their own, former officials testifying before Congress.\par \par \uc2 We know what the Biden`s view is on the latest assertion of executive privilege over documents. So, yes, he would be indicted by a grand jury and he would go to trial.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It seems Mark Meadows being those --\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: And by the way, if he`s convicted, you get -- you can serve up to a year and -- in jail.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Meadows is being the actual Chief of Staff at the time of all of this. He seems to potentially have a stronger executive privilege case than anybody else.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Remember, the executive privilege claim is the President`s and not the -- not the officials so much, it alls -- it`s all about him, his right to receive candid advice without fear that it`ll become public.\par \par \uc2 But it`s -- you know, you`re -- the further away you get from a sitting President and the President himself in his inner circle, the weaker the claim becomes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is there a chance it would go to trial?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, sure.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM: I mean, the last person to be cited for contempt of Congress was Rita Lavelle during the Reagan administration over the whole EPA Superfund thing. She was tried and she was acquitted.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And that trial would be in the District of Columbia?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes, DC -- federal court here in DC.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`d be a -- it`d be a jury trial?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes, most likely.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, one would assume this thing isn`t going to go to trial?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, if he`s -- if he`s charged, I mean, he has a pretty serious choice then of deep to plead guilty or not.\par \par \uc2 TODD: This also takes up time. Yes.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, how much time are we talking here of something like this? I mean, is this month? Before a resolution?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: For sure, but months at least.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And that makes -- that also --\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: By the way, it just occurred to me. Supposed he`s charged with contempt of Congress, and he`s -- he pleads guilty or he`s found guilty, I`m not sure that that means he still has to testify.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Then what? I was just going to say he serves time. Does he end up stolen never have an existent file?\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I don`t feel the answer to that question.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, it`s possible he can avoid testifying if he`s willing to sit in jail for a year?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, I mean, the -- I guess the problem is, every time he refuses to testify, he can get charged all over again and becomes a new offense. And it would just -- I can`t imagine that it would just go on forever like that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: How fast do you expect the Justice Department to act here?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Oh, I think pretty promptly. I mean, they`ll get the referral from Congress here. I would think Congress will act very quickly, and then the U.S. Attorney will act within. I would think a matter of days.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, Pete Williams, explaining how this could work. Pete, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let me bring in somebody who`s familiar with the investigation into the January 6 attack. It`s Pennsylvania Congresswoman Madeline Dean.\par \par \uc2 She`s Vice-Chair of the House Judiciary Committee. She was an impeachment manager for this year`s Senate trial of former President Trump. Yes, the second impeachment happened this calendar year for those with sort of COVID calendar brain.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, I`m sure you have that too. Stuff either happened yesterday or three years ago, nothing in between. How quickly will Congress refer this as a criminal complaint to the Justice Department in your view?\par \par \uc2 REP. MADELINE DEAN (D-PA): I`m confident it will be a quick referral, and I`m certain the Committee is fully prepared for that. We have seen this stonewalling by some of these actors over the course of the many -- last few years under the former administration.\par \par \uc2 But it is a new day, and peaches (PH) laid it out. This is a different Department of Justice, this is a different administration, thanks to that good lord.\par \par \uc2 And I believe the referral will come quickly. I think a vote on the floor could come quickly. I think that this will be met with much more urgency and swiftness of response.\par \par \uc2 After all, this is about the subpoena power of an equal branch of government. The former administration has some notion that he and he alone was the preeminent branch of government. Well, he`s got a few other things coming through on whether he learns it or not.\par \par \uc2 We are co-equal branch, we do have legal subpoena authority, and those like Bannon who will try to skip town on it, good luck. We will get you now\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are you could -- speaking of Bannon, are you concerned, Congresswoman, that he almost it appears to be embracing this fight, that he wants to be a martyr and frankly, sadly, there`s an entire ecosystem that will cheer him on and frankly, fun to his fight, if you will for this?\par \par \uc2 You know, we`ve gotten in such a bizarre place, obviously, in our toxic politics, that there is a growing wing of one party that would cheer something like this on. How much does that complicate things?\par \par \uc2 DEAN: To me, Chuck, I see it as a sign of extraordinary insecurity and weakness. Any one of these people shouldn`t need a subpoena at all, as an American, as a patriot, as a citizen, who suffered the insurrection that was not an attack on Democrats in Congress. It was an attack on the entire government, including the Republican sitting Vice President.\par \par \uc2 So, to me, it`s just a sign of their weakness, obviously, to your question earlier on, what on God`s name are they hiding? After all, this insurrection was spontaneous. They must have had nothing to do with it.\par \par \uc2 Well, if you`ve read some of the Senate Judiciary reports about the desperate measures that the President was taking in the days leading up to the insurrection, we will know that many of these actors know much, much more, and we will learn what their responsibility was for an attack on our government, by Americans holding, and wielding, and beating people with the American flag and with the Trump flag.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m glad you brought up the Senate Judiciary report because I`ve been thinking about this quite a bit. We discussed it on the Sunday show with a Senator who`s on the Judiciary Committee, who was a part of this investigation.\par \par \uc2 You are on the Judiciary Committee in the House, part of the impeachment investigation. If you had had Jeffrey Rosen`s testimony in February, and that was part of your -- I mean, you guys had plenty of evidence but this is even more damning than ever now, the six days between January 1 and January 6, and all of his actions that we now have, had you had all that? Do you think -- do you think it would have improved your chances of winning a (INAUDIBLE)?\par \par \uc2 DEAN: Sadly, I think we had plenty of facts to reach all 100 Senators. Sure, I wish we had had more of the information of the literally 3-hour meeting in the Oval Office that took place on January 3rd, three days before the insurrection.\par \par \uc2 And then the following day, the meeting with Attorney Eastman, with the Vice President, with the six-page memo trying desperately on the part of Donald Trump to overturn the full fair results of an election, sure it would have been good to have.\par \par \uc2 But now, we will have it on the record through the Senate, through the Select Committee, and the American public will know it.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 DEAN: But you know, if you look back to that impeachment trial that I was honored, solidly honored to be a part of, we had Senators who we showed them the evidence.\par \par \uc2 The evidence was compelling, and it was not refuted by Donald Trump parties. I don`t know what more we could have brought in there that would have changed the result of the 57-43 vote.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, certainly now where this party is, I don`t know whether anything would have moved the quest. They were perhaps a bit more movable back in February.\par \par \uc2 Let me turn quickly to sort of the ongoing debate over the President`s agenda. I don`t know if you caught Terry McAuliffe`s sort of, exasperation at Congress.\par \par \uc2 He obviously is on the ballot in neighboring Virginia, he`s experienced this before, he almost lost his first race for governor because of sort of Congressional dysfunction, if you will, or the appearance of it.\par \par \uc2 How concerned are you about this, impacting that race and therefore creating this sort of domino effect? And do you think we`re going to get closer before the end of this month?\par \par \uc2 DEAN: Well, I honor Terry McAuliffe, in his run to become governor again. And I certainly understand his frustration, and the timing is coming close on his election, certainly.\par \par \uc2 But I would like to push back on this notion of total dysfunction. I was literally at the White House yesterday with the President in a bipartisan way. We had a meeting and had the chance to talk a little bit about Build Back Better.\par \par \uc2 I want people to recognize we have two incredibly important transformative bills that are on a dual track. The bipartisan infrastructure bill, which I am confident, will come forward, and we will pass, and it will be meaningful to state after state after state.\par \par \uc2 But we also have the chance in a much more transformative way with Build Back Better. And the substance of that bill is what we should be talking about, not the process, not the date, not the top line number.\par \par \uc2 Think of how different our world will be in terms of educational opportunity and economic opportunity if we put those things in place, and I have a confidence we`re going to do both, that will reflect well on Democrats, while all of the big lie and what has going on in the past reflects seemingly on Republicans.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The problem, Congresswoman, is on the -- on this larger bill, the reconciliation bill, we don`t know what`s in it. And there seems to be a debate of, OK, we can`t have every program that we want to do.\par \par \uc2 Generally, it certainly looks like there is a larger chunk of the party that says if you`ve got to pick, then pick the things that help children, pick the things that help young parents, and that Medicare expansion may be a bit too expensive. Is that your sense of where things are headed?\par \par \uc2 DEAN: Well, Chuck, I`m in an interesting spot, and I`ve spoken to Leadership about this. I`m both on the Progressive Caucus and the new Democratic Caucus, and so I`ve had the opportunity to be a part of both of those kinds of conversations.\par \par \uc2 And I`ll tell you what I keep telling including the Speaker this week. What I care about is making a difference for the children in terms of education. So, it`ll be in negotiations, that we decide which programs and for how long these will be funded.\par \par \uc2 But I want to remind the American people, we actually have a plan to pay for it. So, don`t fall for this notion of oh, the Democrats just want to run up a deficit. We actually can pay for this.\par \par \uc2 And then, for example, I just want to feature a couple. Universal pre-K, what an engine that will be for educational opportunity and equity for all of America`s children?\par \par \uc2 Paid family leave, we`ve already seen that make an extraordinary difference in people`s economies, in people`s ability to get back to work.\par \par \uc2 Childcare, affordable childcare, and then one of my real beliefs is free community college. I believe that will be an economic engine. It`s called - - the package was called America`s College Promise.\par \par \uc2 So, when we do things that have to do with the education and economic opportunities around families, and we make those programs last, we will be transforming our economy and the futures for our children.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Congresswoman Madeline Dean, Democrat from the Judiciary Committee, appreciate your coming on sharing your perspective.\par \par \uc2 And you`re only reinforcing this notion, I think -- I think we know where the focus of this bill is likely to be. Congresswoman, thank you.\par \par \uc2 DEAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Up next, we`re going to dig into those remarks we just heard from the President over the Coronavirus pandemic, as the FDA meets today to debate whether or not to approve more booster shots. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. We just heard from President Biden, and he gave some remarks in the state of the pandemic highlighting recent dropping cases, deaths, and hospitalizations nationwide.\par \par \uc2 Is that quite the Independence Day-like speech on this one? I think everybody is being cautious right now about this current situation. It looks good, let`s hope it continues.\par \par \uc2 The President also touted some encouraging data from private companies. Many have seen employee vaccination rates increased by more than 20 points as a result of new vaccine requirements that are coming, and have already come in some places.\par \par \uc2 But there`s still 66 million eligible Americans that have not yet received even their first shot, and infection rates still remain far too high to say definitively that we`ve turned the corner.\par \par \uc2 All this comes as an FDA Advisory Committee meets today to consider recommending Moderna booster shots for many of the nearly 70 million Americans who already received the first parts of those two-dose vaccines.\par \par \uc2 If authorized, Moderna boosters could roll out for those over 65, and high- risk adults with underlying conditions or certain frontline jobs, the same groups of people already eligible for the third \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  shot.\par \par \uc2 Day two of the meeting continues tomorrow to consider boosters from \uc2 Johnson and Johnson\uc2  and mix and matching vaccine doses. And questions have already been raised about the data presented by both manufacturers here.\par \par \uc2 Briefing documents made available prior to the meeting show the FDA`s mixed opinion on whether extra shots are necessary and if the data is sufficient.\par \par \uc2 Plus, a new NIH study suggests that \uc2 Johnson and Johnson\uc2  recipients may be better off getting a \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  or Madonna booster, not another J&J anyway.\par \par \uc2 So, there`s a lot to get into with my guest today. We got Monica Alba at the White House for us, also joining us as vaccine expert, Dr. Peter Hotez.\par \par \uc2 He, of course, directs the Center for Vaccine Development at Texas Children`s Hospital, and he`s the Dean of the National School of Tropical Medicine at Baylor College.\par \par \uc2 Monica, I want to start with you. It was interesting to see that the White House -- that the President chose to come out today to talk today about this. Do you have some further insight as to why they thought today was a good day for this?\par \par \uc2 MONICA ALBA, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Yes. And usually these speeches, these COVID updates, Chuck, do come tied to some more administrative action or a bigger message that they really want to push.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 ALBA: You remember just a couple of weeks and months ago when the President unveiled his six-prong strategy, or they wanted to give that initial rollout of the boosters, today`s was more of an overarching message, a progress report, a check-in, and a warning where the President really wanted to make sure people hear that, yes, things are absolutely trending in the right direction but this is not the time to lit up.\par \par \uc2 So, even though we`re heading into a fall with a lot of new tools in the toolkit, the President is essentially reminding Americans that we want to make sure that people are taking advantage of that.\par \par \uc2 So, he did say, for anybody who`s already eligible for a booster, make sure you`re going to get that. For anyone who might soon be a parent of a youngster aged five to 11, if they do indeed get approved by the FDA, which could happen in the next few weeks, the President said -- he said, we want parents to be ready and planning for that possibility.\par \par \uc2 And then as you just mentioned, this potential J&J and Moderna booster question mark of what may be best, will there be a mix and match, he said he wanted people again, to be vigilant and say, once there is a green light, do be sure you go out there and get it.\par \par \uc2 Now, the President also wanting to pull back and talk about this Department of Labor emergency rule. Remember when we talked about this, we said, how`s this going to work? How`s this going to be enforced? What if businesses don`t comply?\par \par \uc2 We now at least know the rule has been written. We haven`t seen the text of it, but that`s one step closer and it could come as soon as next week, and then we`ll really know whether businesses will be fined or how that`s going to work.\par \par \uc2 So, the President talking about what he sees as the effectiveness of the vaccine mandates so far, but of course, there still is economic woes. Everything we talked about yesterday, with the pandemic, still a major concern.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Monica, is basically the vaccine mandate from the federal government about the only plan the Biden team has left to try to get vaccine resistors to take it?\par \par \uc2 ALBA: I think that`s their most large lever of government that they have left, yes. And they feel that they are prepared for the legal battles and challenges that are already coming.\par \par \uc2 But this is something that, remember, months ago, the President said he was hesitant to do these kinds of requirements and mandates. Now, they`re saying this is actually, maybe exactly to your point, the only thing that will move some people off the sidelines.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, Monica, at the White House for us, Monica, thank you. Let me bring in Dr. Peter Hotez. And Dr. Hotez, I`m just going to put up here just the various quotes from some red flags and an FDA briefing document about the J&J booster in particular, because there`s a lot of booster confusion this morning -- this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Data sets were not submitted in sufficient time for FDA to conduct an independent review to verify the sponsors` analyses, the FDA is not independently verified the data from the final efficacy, these analyses were limited by small sample sizes, PsVNA assay used for the immunogenicity analysis is not validated or not qualified.\par \par \uc2 What is all of that tell you Dr. Hotez? Are they not going to approve this booster?\par \par \uc2 DR. PETER HOTEZ, DIRECTOR, CENTER FOR VACCINE DEVELOPMENT, TEXAS CHILDREN`S HOSPITAL: Well, there`s a possibility. And in fairness to J&J, you know, for the Pfizer BioNTech vaccine, we had the advantage of all of that data for vaccine effectiveness coming out of Israel and the U.K.\par \par \uc2 And that`s what really, I think, helped push this over the edge with the FDA. And the Moderna and J&J vaccines, are not being used in those two countries, and we just haven`t really collected vaccine effectiveness data as much -- as much as we`d like.\par \par \uc2 I do think it is a good vaccine, and -- but there are some deficiencies in the packet. And I think there`s also some misunderstanding about the paper that came out yesterday in terms of J&J as a booster.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you this. You`ve seen there was a report this morning that perhaps it`s -- you`re better off getting a another -- getting one of the mRNA vaccines as a booster if you took J&J, explain why that is. What that could give?\par \par \uc2 HOTEZ: Well, so that`s what I was -- so that`s exactly what I was referring to. So, this was a preprint that came out yesterday that showed that it looked at all the different permutations and combinations of the three vaccines, the two mRNA vaccines from \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 , BioNTech, and Moderna, and the J&J vaccine.\par \par \uc2 And what it showed is that as a booster, the mRNA vaccines tended to give a much more robust level of virus-neutralizing antibody compared to the J&J vaccine.\par \par \uc2 But what I was referring to before was the fact that you know, with the kinetics of the antibody response of the J&J vaccine, are quite different.\par \par \uc2 If you looked at the phase, one phase two data, you know, the antibody levels don`t peak till around 60, 70, 80 days afterward. And this study cut it shorter on 30 days.\par \par \uc2 So, it kind of stacked the deck against looking at the effectiveness of the J&J vaccine. So, I think that has to be considered.\par \par \uc2 Also, the J&J vaccine in two doses gives you also a different type of immune response on the CDA positive cells that are very strong.\par \par \uc2 So, put on -- all in that together in the phase one, phase two data, I still think the J&J vaccine as a two-dose vaccine is still a very good vaccine.\par \par \uc2 TODD: How comfortable are you -- look, I know that \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 , Moderna, J&J have pretty good scientific reputations. That said, how comfortable are you that we`re relying on their data sets, their collection of data?\par \par \uc2 [13:30:00]\par \par \uc2 Shouldn`t this be done independently? Shouldn`t we be asking maybe it`s you to run an independent -- you know, maybe it`s another academic institution? But shouldn`t we be collecting the data on these vaccines that way?\par \par \uc2 HOTEZ: Well, typically it would be a combination of the two. There would be independent studies and, you know, this is why \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 -BioNTech had the advantage of the data collected by the Ministry of Health of Israel and a cadre of Israeli scientists. Same with the U.K. So you are right. I think, you know, this has been a problem that we`ve had with CDC that we`ve not really collected vaccine effectiveness data here in the U.S. at the level that we should have. And so that`s not working to the disadvantages of the companies.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And one other question about -- as we`re getting closer to seeing emergency authorization for \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  at least for kids 5 to 11, I know overseas some countries have just approved only one dose for kids 5 to 11. There are quite a few doctors that have brought up the idea that could two dose cause an increased risk of heart issues for younger folks during development stages. Where are you on this issue?\par \par \uc2 HOTEZ: So, you know, the myocarditis does increase in frequency. It`s still a very rare event in the adolescents compared to the young adults. And so the concern is that as you get down to younger ages the rate of myocarditis will subsequently increase. We have no evidence of that. You know, I think one of the issues is the data packet being submitted to the FDA has about 3,000 kids in it, which would be not enough to really assess that myocarditis safety signal.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, the problem is a single dose of the vaccine is very modest in terms of its protective effect.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 HOTEZ: Some saying only 30 percent against the Delta variant. And remember what we`re trying to do here, Chuck. We`re also trying to prevent not only hospitalizations but long COVID and neurological deterioration. And I think that`s the big unknown is how extensive does this filter down to kids?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 HOTEZ: And how important is it to both prevent hospitalizations as well as long COVID.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. And is long COVID a worse long-term risk frankly than the threat of myocarditis.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, Dr. Peter Hotez, as always, sir, it`s great to get your expertise on the show. So thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, from John Deere to the film industry, some major labor pains are being felt across the United States right now as thousands of union workers take to the picket lines or threaten to. Workers have all the leverage these days. We`ve got the details next.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:36:27]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. If the last week has shown us anything about the current state of the labor market is that workers may have more leverage over their employers now than at any time in recent history. The Labor Department reported this week that 4.3 million people, nearly 3 percent of the workforce, quit their jobs in August with the prospects of better and higher paying jobs available.\par \par \uc2 And workers in unions have also used the current climate to push for better conditions for themselves. Hollywood crews are threatening to strike next Monday if no deal is reached with studios over better hours, safer conditions and better benefits. About 1400 Kellogg employees who want better pay and benefits have been on strike for more than a week. And about 10,000 John Deere employees went on strike this morning after rejecting an offer from the company last night. That`s the largest public sector strike in two years.\par \par \uc2 Each of these strikes is very different with workers having very different demands. But they all point to the same thing. With a nationwide labor shortage, workers have the upper hand over management. And right now in this environment, they`re not afraid to use it.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, is it time for America to establish a third political party? I`ll speak with a former Republican governor about the future of the GOP as she calls on moderate members of her party to team up with Democrats for now.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:40:44]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. A little bit of breaking news. The January 6 Select Committee has just announced it will meet Tuesday to vote on adopting a contempt report against Steve Bannon after the Trump ally failed to comply with his subpoenas. The announcement only mentioned Bannon, not Mark Meadows, Kash Patel or Dan Scavino, who also face similar deadlines. This would set up a vote in the full House, then refer the matter to the Justice Department for potential indictment and prosecution. We will continue to monitor those developments with the committee as we get them.\par \par \uc2 Staying on this topic, for Republicans opposed to Donald Trump now would seem to be an opportunity to start a third party. But some prominent Republicans say the key to eradicating Trump from their party is to support Democrats.\par \par \uc2 It`s a fascinating debate as former New Jersey governor Christine Todd Whitman and former Trump administration official Miles Taylor wrote in the "New York Times" this week. "Rational Republicans are losing the party`s civil war. And the only near-term way to battle pro-Trump extremists is for all of us to team up on key races and overarching political goals with our longtime political opponents, the Democrats."\par \par \uc2 Well, the organization Whitman and Taylor are helping to lead, Renew America Movement, took the next step in that pledge, endorsing 11 moderate Democrats ahead of next year`s midterms and I believe 10 moderate Republicans, all of them on the House side, voted to impeach the president.\par \par \uc2 Christine Todd Whitman joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Governor Whitman, it`s always a pleasure to talk with you. Look, I want you to respond to -- this is an interesting debate. Jonah Goldberg wrote the following about this debate. He`s part of the never Trump movement as well on "The Dispatch." And he said this, "Asking right of center voters to vote for Democratic senators and representatives who take the opposite positions on abortion, guns, foreign policy and tax and spending issues for democracy`s sake is a heavy lift, even if you can convince them that the democracy is really at stake. Biden has made that lift far heavier by making it clear that he wants right of center voters to compromise on everything while he compromises on little or nothing."\par \par \uc2 So what do you say to that part of this debate in this small tent of never Trumpers?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE TODD WHITMAN (R), FORMER NEW JERSEY GOVERNOR: Well, we know it`s going to be a difficult battle. But I have to tell you, Chuck, and by the way, it`s always good to see you. Since we announced or put out that op-ed, I`ve been amazed at the number of Democrats that have reached out to say, we`re ready for something like this. We`re worried about how far to the left our party is going. And if you look at registrations across the country, you have 50 percent of registered voters who are now registered independents or unaffiliated.\par \par \uc2 And a large majority of those I am willing to bet are Republicans. And if you look at the numbers of Republican registration versus Democrats, you`ll see that the Republicans have been losing more. So I think the time is ripe to tell people look, what we`re after is supporting those candidates who will stand up to the big lie, who are not part of this concerted effort to undermine the public`s confidence in our electoral process, who will -- they`re not going to agree on everything and we`re not going to agree with them.\par \par \uc2 I`m not going to agree with them, Miles won`t agree with them, he and I won`t agree on everything. But we agree on enough that has to do with our core principles, our respect for the rule of law and our respect for the Constitution to actually make this happen.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s clear to me that everybody has the same goal here, which is eradicate Trump from the Republican Party. And I guess the question is, what is the best way to do it? And why do you believe the best way -- it sounds like you think the best way to do it is starve the Republicans of power and maybe they`ll reform themselves? Is that the hope?\par \par \uc2 WHITMAN: Well, we`re doing both. We are supporting those Republicans -- incumbent Republicans right now. In the list that we released it`s an even number of incumbent Democrats and incumbent Republicans that we`re supporting because they are all people of principle, who have stood up to the big lie on both sides.\par \par \uc2 And we`ve been strategic in picking those races where it`s going to be close, where an endorsement and -- you know, bringing them to the attention of the voting public as people who are principled, who are willing to work for both sides and to get things done, willing to work for the American people, putting aside their party affiliations. Most of the public does that anyway. People split their ballots.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:00]\par \par \uc2 They are ready now to see something different happen so that they can feel that we`re actually getting these problems solved and not ending up in this continuous loggerheads that we find ourselves with Congress. We can`t seem to get anything major done for the American people.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, right. It does seem as if there`s a reward structure for dysfunction. There`s a reward structure for obstruction. There`s not a reward structure for success right now. I mean, look at --\par \par \uc2 WHITMAN: Well, that`s what we`re trying to provide.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I mean, there is more money to be made by Steve Bannon by not cooperating than cooperating. Like that is what has broken our system.\par \par \uc2 WHITMAN: I don`t disagree with you. But what we`re saying is, OK, now is the time to stand up and support those who are willing to take that risk of stand on principle, knowing that they`re going to be attacked. I mean, these incumbents that we have endorsed are all going to be in tight races because they`re in those districts that are swing districts or that are, you know, marginal districts in their favor.\par \par \uc2 But they are the kind of people that we want to see in Congress, we believe. And the public can look at these lists and give us some information as to who they should support and where, if they have the financial ability to do so. They can provide some financial support as well because you`re right, nobody has had the backs of those who are willing to stand up to leadership. Frankly, in neither party.\par \par \uc2 Because what leadership can do both through their PAC and just through the power they have and rules of the House and the Senate is to basically emasculate anybody who stands up to them. And that`s what these representatives have been willing to -- been willing to take that chance.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Do you want --\par \par \uc2 WHITMAN: And that`s something we should support.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you hope that your willingness to essentially say, look, Republicans want to support Democrats here right now because of this threat of Trump, threat of Trumpism, threat to the democracy? In turn, do you want the Democratic Party to run on that issue, make that more front and center than anything else?\par \par \uc2 WHITMAN: Well, it`s an important issue but we`re also asking Democrats to cross and support Republicans. If a Democrat has the choice between a far- left Democrat candidate versus a centrist Republican, we`re saying, vote for the centrist Republican. It`s not just on the Republicans. It`s also on the Democrats so that we can start to get a balance, the kind of balance that we need.\par \par \uc2 Because, you know, what`s happening, Chuck, and you`ve seen it and you know about it and you`ve talked about it is a concerted effort right now by the Trump faction to undermine the public`s confidence in our electoral system.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 WHITMAN: And that is just an idea to make it easier to overturn the 2022 and the 2024 elections, although having said that and the president is apparently telling Republicans not to vote, I don`t understand where that thinking is. But, you know, be that as it may, people are going to vote. We want to send a strong message.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Christine Todd Whitman, longtime Republican, former governor of New Jersey, appreciate you coming on and sharing your perspective on this fascinating debate taking place between Republicans --\par \par \uc2 WHITMAN: It`s not over.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you help the Democrats or do you start a third party. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, a sneak peek at something pretty exciting coming soon from us here at MEET THE PRESS. Stay with us to learn more.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:51:49]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back here on MEET THE PRETTY DAILY. We are happy to announce the "MEET THE PRESS FILM FESTIVAL." The AFI Fest is returning for its fifth year on November 11th with in-person screenings this year in Los Angeles along with virtual screenings online. The festival, which you normally have here, we`ve got some COVID issues so we`re very excited to be doing it in L.A. The festival will showcase more than 15 short documentary films. Early bird passes are available right now at fest.afi.com.\par \par \uc2 Tickets to in-person and virtual screenings will be available to the general public beginning Wednesday, October 20th at fest.afi.com. You won`t want to miss it. We have a tremendous slate of films again. And as you know, what you`ll see here, you`ll eventually see nominated for Oscars. It`s always a really good slate and AFI is a great partner.\par \par \uc2 Up next, what`s happening to evangelical voters in the post-Trump era? Our latest episode of "MEET THE PRESS REPORTS" digs into just that and we`ve got the details next of what`s a growing divide.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:55:52]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. If it`s Thursday, it is time for yet another episode of our streaming show "MEET THE PRESS REPORTS." This week we are looking at specific cohort of Republican votes, white evangelicals. My colleague Anne Thompson asks the burning question, what`s happening to evangelicals in the post-Trump era and found out very quicky at one church that there is no post-Trump era. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANNE THOMPSON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Peters is leading one side of the battle for the soul of the evangelical church.\par \par \uc2 KEN PETERS, SENIOR PASTOR, PATRIOT CHURCH: Some of the evangelical church I think it is soft. I think they`re cowardly and they`re trying to ride the fence between the left and the right. And so that`s kind of where the divide is.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Here in the heart of the bible belt.\par \par \uc2 DR. PHIL NORDSTROM, LEAD PASTOR, LIFE CHURCH: I would say the person of faith who`s been around church all my life that the bible belt is unbuckling.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Across town, Pastor Phil Nordstrom leads the Life Church.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): What do you mean that the bible belt is unbuckling?\par \par \uc2 NORDSTROM: The branding of Christianity has suffered. I think that our association with political extremism has especially turned off a younger generation toward evangelicalism. So one of the challenges we face right now is, who are we, what does it mean to be an evangelical?\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON (voice-over): Nordstrom is decidedly old school, a pro-life pastor pointedly staying away from partisan politics on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 NORDSTROM: The funny thing is I`m probably personally pretty conservative. So I don`t come across as like I`m a liberal evangelical. But people get the spirit -- get the feeling pretty quick that we`re pretty inclusive church, that everyone is welcome, that we`re trying to not fight the culture wars from the pulpit.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Nordstrom builds his church with passengers he meets driving for Uber and \uc2 Lyft\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NORDSTROM: You have a wonderful day.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NORDSTROM: All right. Appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Under his soft-spoken demeanor, a growing anger that the things he loves are misconstrued.\par \par \uc2 NORDSTROM: I think the easy thing to do would be to say, I`m no longer an evangelical or like I`m no longer a patriot. No, I`m a patriot and I`m an evangelical. And I`m proud of it. I still believe in a personal relationship with Jesus Christ which is what an evangelical in my view is.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Anne Thompson is here with more.\par \par \uc2 And Anne, it`s been a fascinating dive that you and I have been taking on this. And the stat that I think is most explanatory of this conundrum evangelicals are in, more people identify at white evangelical today and fewer go to church.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Yes, because I think what increasingly evangelicalism has become is not so much a religious movement. It`s become a political movement. And it`s a political movement that is certainly at home and very much in the camp of the Republican Party and not just the Republican Party, but the extreme right wing of the Republican Party. It is becoming a movement of Donald Trump. And that you see at the Patriot Church.\par \par \uc2 Why Donald Trump? I mean, they admit he is an imperfect messenger. He`s not the kind of Christian that they all hope to be. But they very much support him because of his positions. His pro-life position, his position on illegal immigration. And they want to go a step further, they believe there are only two genders, there is no transgender and they are very much anti- gay marriage.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, it`s interesting. Russell Moore told me for an interview for our show that`s going tonight, he said, in many ways Donald Trump is actually very much like a Jimmy Swaggart or a Jim and Tammy Faye, that he comes from that sort of lineage of the movement that he admits has always been a part of the movement that has been uncomfortable for the more devout evangelicals.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: He does. He embraces the prosperity gospel and in many ways he would seem to be the personification of the prosperity gospel. And the one thing Donald Trump has always been is he`s been an extraordinary salesman, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Anne Thompson, it`s terrific work. Folks are not going to want to miss it.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020211014ehae0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD WITH LAWRENCE O`DONNELL for October 13, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lawrence O`Donnell, Jill Wine-Banks, Matt Miller, Zerlina Maxwell \par \uc2 7,370 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 October 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:37]\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 And I was listening to your reporting on what`s going on in Alaska, and you showed the video of people in these meetings threatening these elected officials who are considering how much mask mandating do we have to do and what situations. We`re going to be joined tonight by Jennifer Jenkins who has gone viral in the last 24 hours on this issue. She`s a school board member in Florida.\par \par \uc2 And what she has endured by simply by believing that masks are a pretty good idea to try to limit the spread of COVID in public schools, what she and her daughter, her family have endured is just horrific, and she finally spoke about it for the first time yesterday. She`s going to join us tonight.\par \par \uc2 And I just marvel at the bravery of these people. The astonishing thing in your reporting is at the end of everything that we see here, they then vote 9-1 in favor of masks and against the wishes of the people who were threatening them with violence.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST, "TRMS": That`s right, and public service is sort of always a small H form of heroism, always. To be a public servant is to do a good thing by the world. But it should not actually take literal bravery. It should not take physical bravery to do these kinds of jobs, to be a city councilor, to be a school board member, to do these things.\par \par \uc2 But the way the political right has dealt with COVID is by bringing threat, including physical intimidation and physical harm very, very, very close to the surface almost immediately for the public servants who are dealing with these questions just as part of their job in public service, and it is -- I saw the footage that you`re referring to from your guests tonight. I`m really looking forward to what she has to say about it.\par \par \uc2 She shouldn`t have to be that brave to do that job. That`s on us as a country.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Exactly, exactly. And no one signing up for the school board was ever signing up for this kind of thing, and so --\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: That`s the other part of the marvel of it is the -- people continuing in these jobs in the face of this.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I mean, they are in a sense part of that first responder force that the people who must come up with ways of responding to COVID because of their jobs right away, and people involved in the schools have all been part of that, and now, with the school year underway, successfully, there`s this -- instead of celebrating how successfully the school year has gone so far, there is all this rage.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Rage and threat and literal violence and there`s going to have to be both accountability for it but there`s also got to be strategies to deescalate this stuff, that will have to involve responsible figures on the right. That`s what we`re not seeing yet.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yeah, we`ll have to be very patient waiting for them to emerge. That`s the one that`s going to happen.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Thanks, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  is promising to become a criminal again tomorrow. But he won`t have \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to pardon him this time. In the last months of the Trump presidency, \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  was arrested and charged with federal crimes for the way he embezzled money that he raised from pathetically gullible Trump voters who actually gave money to \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  because \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  lied to them and told them that he would use that money to privately build the wall on the southern border that the president of the United States \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was not able to build.\par \par \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  would build it if you just sent him your money. \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  defrauded Trump voters, got caught by the feds, was charged with those crimes, but didn`t have to go to trial in federal court because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  pardoned him, pardoned him for defrauding Donald Trump`s own voters.\par \par \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  has been subpoenaed by the special House committee investigating the attack on the capitol to testify tomorrow, and he has already become the first person in history who is trying to hide behind presidential executive privilege even though he wasn`t even working in the White House or the government during the period that the committee is investigating.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:07]\par \par \uc2 He was a private citizen.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has become the first private citizen and former president in history to try to exert executive privilege in a congressional investigation and felt today once again, President Biden smacked down that false and illegal claim by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The White House released a letter today that was actually written last week to the archivist of the United States where all of the presidential records are kept. That letter says President Biden does not uphold the former president`s assertion of privilege. President Biden instructed the archivist to provide the House committee with all, all of the information that the committee has requested.\par \par \uc2 Last week, Steve Bannon`s lawyer sent a letter to the committee saying since these privileges belong to President Trump and not to Mr. Bannon, until these issues are resolved, Mr. Bannon is legally unable to comply with your subpoena requests for documents and testimony.\par \par \uc2 Like all Trump world lawyers, Steve Bannon`s lawyer is one of those lawyers where you wonder if he really finished law school. He is so wildly wrong.\par \par \uc2 Executive privilege no longer belongs to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . It now belongs to President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and so when \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  does not show up for his deposition tomorrow, the House committee is going to recommend to the Justice Department that \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  face criminal charges for contempt of Congress.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF (D-CA): I think that \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  has been pretty public about his unwillingness to cooperate in any way and from my point of view, it`s not up to him. He is required to show up. He`s required to testify, and if he doesn`t and doesn`t have a reason, a legal reason to proffer, which he doesn`t, then we will hold him in criminal contempt and we will refer that to the Justice Department for prosecution. That will be true of the other witnesses as well if they do not comply.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: That was Adam Schiff on this program last night. He`s a member of that committee. \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  and other members of the committee have also said that the committee is unanimously supportive of enforcing its subpoenas with criminal contempt charges. The committee made news at the close of business today by revealing that they have subpoenaed former Acting Assistant Attorney General Jeffrey Clark.\par \par \uc2 In a letter to Clark accompanying the subpoena, the committee said that they have credible evidence that you attempted to involve the Department of Justice in efforts to interrupt the peaceful transfer of power. You proposed that the department send a letter to state legislators in Georgia and other states suggesting that they delay certification of their election results and hold a press conference announcing that the department was investigating allegations of voter fraud. These proposals were rejected by department leadership as both lacking a factual basis and inconsistent with the department`s institutional role.\par \par \uc2 Lacking a factual basis, so Jeffrey Clark knows that the committee already knows that he was lying about the election in discussions inside the justice department and inside the White House. Jeffrey Clark is ordered to testify in a deposition on October 29th, and that gives him about two weeks to practice invoking the Fifth Amendment.\par \par \uc2 The committee obtained direct incriminating evidence against Jeffrey Clark today in the testimony of Jeff Rosen, who was Donald Trump`s final acting attorney general. If Jeff Rosen told the House committee the same things that he told the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  about Jeffrey Clark, then Jeffrey Clark is living in fear of criminal charges tonight of attempted election fraud and conspiracy to commit election fraud.\par \par \uc2 Joining our discussion, Jill Wine-Banks, former assistant Watergate prosecutor, and Matt Miller, former spokesman for Attorney General \uc2 Eric Holder\uc2 , both are MSNBC contributors.\par \par \uc2 And, Jill, I want to begin with you and I want to begin with this key question that I think has left a lot of us confused. Why were you so effective in getting compliance to subpoenas against President Nixon and his administration during the Watergate investigation? And the Democrats in Congress have been so ineffective in relative terms in pursuit of the same kind of subpoenas against team Trump?\par \par \uc2 [22:10:05]\par \par \uc2 JILL WINE-BANKS, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: It`s really a very simple answer. Because President Nixon, despite his crimes and despite all the wrong that he did, actually believed in our system of laws and government. And when confronted with a Supreme Court decision, he agreed to comply.\par \par \uc2 So he never tried to stop people. He did try to claim executive privilege, but the Supreme Court said, no, you have to balance it. And in this case, the needs for the criminal investigation far outweigh the need for executive privilege.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, it is clear that even in the case cited by Bannon`s lawyer, this is a privilege of the current incumbent. It goes with the office. It does not go with the former office holder, so there is no possible executive privilege for any of the people and maybe most especially for Bannon who never was at the time of these conversations a part of the White House.\par \par \uc2 But even if he was, there wouldn`t be a legitimate claim of executive privilege. And so I think we have to look at the fact that the Republicans now are willing to evade all laws and to just simply stone wall in a way that is completely contrary to our democracy.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Wasn`t there another element in the enforcement of your subpoenas in the 1970s, 1974 in the Watergate investigation, and that was the speed of the courts. I mean, it seemed like you would issue a subpoena, the subpoena for the tapes, for example, the presidential tapes, that case went right up to the \uc2 Supreme Court of the United States\uc2 , got a ruling, and the ruling was enforced, and it seemed to take a matter of weeks to go from subpoena to the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  to a final ruling?\par \par \uc2 WINE-BANKS: It did. We`re clearly living in an alternative universe now. We issued the second subpoena for 64 tapes for the trial on April 16th. It was argued in the Supreme Court July 21st, I believe, and on July 24th, the Supreme Court ruled in our favor.\par \par \uc2 We got the tapes, and by August 9th, so we`re talking about days later, the Republicans had gone to the White House and said we saw the tapes, we heard them. We cannot support you. If you do not resign, you will be convicted in a Senate trial.\par \par \uc2 So that`s why it happened from April, August 9th he was out of office, and it could happen again if the Supreme Court would stop all of the political nonsense that they have been engaged in, if they would take this seriously. This is a case that cannot be left to linger for years, as it has recently. There have been other subpoenas and it has taken up to six years for a decision.\par \par \uc2 That can`t be allowed to happen because justice delayed is justice denied, and this delay would be a killer because we need to get this information to prevent any possibility of this happening again. We need the facts that are being asked. Everything that has been subpoenaed, everything requested in the letters seems very clearly relevant to legislative actions.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Matt, it seems Jeff Clark, my guess about him is that it`s very likely that he will show up to testify. It`s also very likely he will take the Fifth Amendment to virtually every question.\par \par \uc2 MATT MILLER, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: I think that`s likely. I think certainly he`s going to have to show up and testify. He really is on an island by himself. If you look at the way he was behaving in those final weeks.\par \par \uc2 He was acting completely beyond his authority. He`s supposed to be in charge of environmental crimes and was conducting his own investigations or supposed investigations into voter fraud. He was violating multiple department rules, having contacts with the White House, having contacts with members of Congress, both of which he was not allowed to do on his own, and of course violating his oath to the Constitution by trying to overturn the election.\par \par \uc2 And he really is, you know, out there by himself because, you know, we`ve seen \uc2 Jeffrey Rosen\uc2  and we`ve seen one of Jeffrey Rosen`s subordinates testifying before Congress because the Justice Department has already told Rosen and Clark and other DOJ officials from this time period that the department is waiving all of the relevant privileges. Usually the department blocks its officials from coming up and testifying for matters like this, and they`ve waived all of them. That`s true for Jeffrey Clark.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, if he wants to avoid a criminal referral and be charged with criminal contempt of Congress, you know, he needs to come up and comply with the subpoena and testify because otherwise he`s in blatant contempt of Congress. Now, to your point, if he wants to take the fifth, of course, that`s his right to -- that`s his right.\par \par \uc2 [22:15:05]\par \par \uc2 He can do so, but he doesn`t have the ability to just blow off the subpoena like he seems to want to do.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So, Matt, one of the changes in the 21st century compared to the Nixon era is just how breathtakingly slow the courts are, at the district court level, the appeals court level on all of these enforcement issues on congressional subpoenas. What about -- the Justice Department will now be tested when the Congress refers to the Justice Department for criminal prosecution of contempt of Congress. We don`t know how quickly the Merrick Garland Justice Department will be able to move on this, and in, you know -- in the past these kinds of things have been able to be handled very, very quickly.\par \par \uc2 What is your expectation of a timetable at the Justice Department for this?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: I would expect they could move pretty quickly. Look, the Justice Department has a rule that they will not prosecute someone for contempt of Congress if they are refusing to testify because there has been a legitimate claim of executive privilege by the president of the United States. It ought to be pretty easy for the attorney general to pick up the phone, call the White House counsel and say, Dana, did the president -- President Biden invoke executive privilege for Jeff Clark or \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  or any of these other within witnesses? And when the answer is no, as we know the answer is, it`s a pretty straightforward prosecution.\par \par \uc2 And I actually think the Justice Department could do more, by just making it clear that if you are not the subject of an executive privilege assertion by the sitting president of the United States, not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  but \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and you blow off the congressional subpoena and refuse to testify, you are not immune from prosecution and we will take those referrals very seriously.\par \par \uc2 These witnesses are acting with impunity because for years they got away with it with no consequences, and if DOJ sent that kind of signal right now, I think you`d see some of these people coming up and testifying even before it got to a subpoena and not kind of playing chicken with Congress and playing chicken with the Justice Department the way they`re doing now.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Matt Miller and Jill Wine-Banks, thank you both for starting us off tonight, really appreciate it. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, Zerlina Maxwell and Alex Wagner will be joining our discussion. But, first, we`ll be joined by someone who went viral this the last 24 hours by showing us just how bad the anti-mask protesters can be, just how vicious and how much bravery and strength it takes for one school board member in Florida to stand up to them. Jennifer Jenkins will join us and we`ll tell you what she and her daughter have had to endure, and it is much, much worse than you think. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:22:06]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Our next guest, Jennifer Jenkins, has now had the experience of going viral in the last 24 hours. When I saw the video of Jennifer Jenkins speaking at a school board meeting in Florida, I was in awe of her courage and her calm because Jennifer Jenkins thinks that masks are a good way to limit the spread of COVID-19 in public schools. Jennifer Jenkins and her family, her daughter, have been targeted for nothing less than public torture by Florida`s anti-mask fanatics.\par \par \uc2 In Florida, it is the Department of Children and Families that investigates child abuse, the DCF. No one knew that the DCF was going to come up at a public meeting of the school board in Brevard County, Florida, southeast of Orlando. The school board discussed whether it would be possible to allow home coming dances this year. The home coming dances have been postponed indefinitely because of COVID-19.\par \par \uc2 When the agenda turned to a resolution about threats against school board members, Jennifer Jenkins found herself talking about something she never wanted to talk about publicly.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER JENKINS, BOARD MEMBERS: In spite of what people believe, I am not opposed to people practicing their First Amendment rights, even when it`s outside of my home on a public property. I`m not. I`m not. I think it`s a silly method. I think it`s ineffective. It doesn`t move me or motivate me in any way, but I`m not opposed to it.\par \par \uc2 What I reject is this effort to create fear and division in the community that leads to credible threats of violence against me and my family, and there`s a lot of things that I haven`t shared with all of you up here.\par \par \uc2 I`ve tried not to talk about this stuff publicly. I don`t reject people coming here and speaking their voice. They do it all the time. We don`t stop them from doing that. I don`t reject them standing outside my home.\par \par \uc2 I reject them following me around in a car, following my car around. I reject them saying that they`re coming for me, that I need to beg for mercy. I reject that when they are using their First Amendment rights on public property, they`re also going behind my home and brandishing their weapons to my neighbors.\par \par \uc2 That they`re making false DCF claims against me to my daughter, that I have to take a DCF investigator to her play date to go underneath her clothing and check for burn marks. That`s what I`m against, which is a credible threat and calculated.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is Jennifer Jenkins, a member of the Brevard County, Florida, school board.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for joining us tonight. I am very, very sorry for what you have endured and for what you`re here to tell us about tonight.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:07]\par \par \uc2 How long has this kind of harassment and public torture been going on?\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Thank you so much for having me, first and foremost.\par \par \uc2 You know, to be honest with you, this is something that started within Brevard County back in April for me. It started with a minority group of individuals who are part of an organization that was founded by my Republican opponent, who have decided that their voices are going to be used to terrorize me.\par \par \uc2 And you know, the majority of people support me, and they reach out to me and they tell me that they`re there for me and they support the things that I stand for. But unfortunately, it doesn`t just take -- it just takes one person to terrorize you.\par \par \uc2 And when there`s no consequences for it, it becomes the norm, and it becomes acceptable and to be perfectly honest with you, I feel like this is the new pandemic here in the state of Florida for us to focus on. It`s going across the nation as well.\par \par \uc2 Our school board members and our educators are being attacked and threatened for doing what they were elected to do, what I was elected to do, to keep our students and our staff safe and what educators were hired to do to protect our children.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You`re in a state where 60 percent support what you`re doing, 60 percent support requiring students, teachers, and staff to wear masks in school, but you`re in a Republican area where that polling might be very different.\par \par \uc2 You left out a lot of details of what`s happened to you that I know about because of conversations that we`ve had with you today including some of the vandalism that occurs at your home. They`ve attacked your plants and your trees. It`s a very active kind of thing. It took the police an hour and 40 minutes to come to your home one time while one of these things was going on.\par \par \uc2 What is it like to be in your home with your daughter watching that stuff?\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: It`s really difficult. The last protest where they came outside of my home, again, I`m not against people using their First Amendment rights. They`re on public property. That`s okay.\par \par \uc2 But I`m against them terrorizing my family. There was 25 individuals standing outside my home in a small beach town in Florida. They were there for hours.\par \par \uc2 Police presence didn`t come right away. It started to get dark out. It was time for me to put my daughter to bed, and as I`m sitting trying to read her a story, these individuals are walking right by her bedroom saying vile things.\par \par \uc2 And as I followed them around the corner to where their cars were parked into a parking lot right behind my home, police presence wasn`t following them until I was there. They got in my face. They coughed in my face telling me they were going to give me COVID. They swung flags in my face, nearly missing me by a couple inches. They said horrible disgusting things to my neighbors.\par \par \uc2 I eventually went back inside, but they brandished their weapons in the parking lot in front of my neighbors, my daughter to this day at least twice a week, mommy are those mean people going to be outside again. It`s been really difficult.\par \par \uc2 Yeah, they did, they vandalized my property. They wrote obscenities in my grass with weed killer. They cut down some of my plants. They did the DCF claim which was really difficult for me. I had to take the DCF investigator to a play date of somebody who I am just an acquaintance of, I don`t even know very well.\par \par \uc2 So, here I am a school board member walking back to a play date I had just dropped her off to with a DCF investigator with no announcement for them to look underneath her clothes to see if she has burns. It`s a really uncomfortable position to be in, and unfortunately even though the police department is doing their best to have subpoenas sent to the state level to figure out who made that unfounded claim, there`s only so far they can go.\par \par \uc2 And so, currently, I still have a document that says I had an unfounded DCF claim against me. It`s not something that`s going to go away.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: What did you tell your daughter about that incident with the DCF during that play date?\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: You know, the DCF investigator was fantastic. And so, she had no idea what was going on. I really appreciated that person understanding this was very unlikely an unfounded claim. The police department came with her and let her know what`s going on.\par \par \uc2 She handled it really, really well like a pro, and I was really happy that she was there.\par \par \uc2 [22:29:51]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Why did you want to become a member of the school board and do you want to continue as a member of the school board?\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: I ran to become a member of the school board because I`m an educator. My husband`s an educator, and my daughter just turned five years old. She just entered kindergarten.\par \par \uc2 And so I am invested in public education professionally and personally and I truly cared. I cared about making a difference for our educators. You know, we`ve had some serious fights for significant wage increases here in Brevard County and in Florida overall. That was one of my passions.\par \par \uc2 And the equity across my district, I really cared about our students making sure that they all had equal opportunities to the same academic outcomes and success.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately COVID got in the way in the middle of my campaign and that really became the focus of the conversation. But I was really grateful to be there because the person I was running against was completely against every mitigation strategy and ultimately that is why I ended up winning my election.\par \par \uc2 And I`m really excited and proud to be on this board and proud to continue to stand up for the safety and health of our students and staff.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I understand that, but have you -- at what point do you run into a kind of torture for doing this job that would make you say to yourself and your family, I cannot continue to do this?\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: I would be lying to you if I didn`t say I have had some really, really dark moments. This has been so difficult for me and my family, and the people who care about us and love us. It has been -- it has been horrible.\par \par \uc2 But I have done my best to not put that out in the public. I wanted people to know that I am strong, I am here for them, and I will continue to fight for them. And I will never give up on that.\par \par \uc2 But I also didn`t want the people to know who were doing this to me that it did affect me because it will never break me and I will never ever step back on the promise I made to the residents of Brevard County. I`m going to be there, I`m going to fulfill my term, and if the timing is right and everything is right, maybe I`ll do it again.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Jennifer Jenkins, thank you very much for joining us tonight. And thank you very much for the serving the voters who elected you in exactly the way you promised you would. I`m very, very sorry for what that has cost you so far for you and your family and your daughter.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: I appreciate that. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And joining our discussion now Alex Wagner, co-host and executive producer of Showtime`s "The Circus". She`s contributor and writer at the "Atlantic". Also with us Zerlina Maxwell host of the program "ZERLINA" that airs on Peacock.\par \par \uc2 And I just want to hand this over to both of you. I`m kind of speechless at the moment. Zerlina, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 ZERLINA MAXWELL, HOST, PEACOCK: That was really hard to listen to, Lawrence, because I feel, you know, in my heart we`re all living through a pandemic. And nobody`s really done this before. And so we`re all sort of flailing about and couldn`t we just be a little bit nicer and kinder to each other.\par \par \uc2 And unfortunately what has happened is we`ve lived through four years of a presidency where so much bad behavior was normalized -- racism, misogyny, homophobia, bullying. And so nobody is raising their kids to behave in this way, the way adults are behaving in some of these school board meetings and across the country.\par \par \uc2 And it`s so unfortunate because so much life has been lost because masks and mitigation measures and vaccinations, it`s been politicized when it should never have been.\par \par \uc2 And it is -- it is a crime against humanity frankly that so much of this has been put up for debate as if science is not fact, and there is too much loss of life as a result.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Alex Wagner, I do not know how to praise Jennifer Jenkins enough. The one thing I can say just by comparison is I am sure that I could not endure what she has endured to do her job.\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, EXECUTIVE PRODUCER, SHOWTIME`S "THE CIRCUS": Yes, as a mother of a child who is not vaccinated because he`s not qualified to be vaccinated yet, who`s in the very same age bracket of her child, I think all of us moms who believe in science are very grateful that Jennifer is serving on the school board.\par \par \uc2 So what struck me in all of this, Lawrence, this isn`t about a surgical mask. I really don`t believe that this is about a mitigation strategy. I believe that this is about what, you know, the right sees as cultural, an existential cultural battle that needs to be waged with every tool, every tool in the arsenal, if you will.\par \par \uc2 [22:34:43]\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: This isn`t about masks. This is about a way of life. This is a pushback against what they see as an encroaching liberal majority that believes in science and the social compact but that is fundamentally out there to end a white Christian way of life, the quote -- unquote, "American way of life". And masks are just a proxy for that.\par \par \uc2 And that belies another truth, which is that rage is central to the Republican Party right now. It is, in fact, the ideology of the Republican Party. It doesn`t matter what the issue is, whether it`s immigration or whether it`s COVID or whether it`s, you know, infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 The response is rooted in rage. And there is no particular policy platform at this point. There is only this emotional center of anger and, boy, is it animating.\par \par \uc2 When you watch these videos of these school board members getting attacked, you hear Jennifer`s testimony, this is about something much larger and more pernicious and deeply emotional that sits at the center of conservatism in the 21st century.\par \par \uc2 And I don`t really know how you combat it. It`s not about, you know, being bipartisan, it`s not about reaching across the aisle. It`s not even about some kind of persuasive argument that people like Jennifer can make because what you`re running up against is this deep existential rage and fear that is being stoked on a daily, monthly, hourly basis by one of our two political parties in this country.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yes, Zerlina, what Alex has just framed for me has made me now see this as she was speaking as the kind of local version of the Trump election loss because the person who`s running this torture program against Jennifer is the person she beat in the election, the Republican she beat in the election.\par \par \uc2 And it used to be the Democrat beat the Republican, the Republican waited around for the next election or went off into the real estate business or something else, right?\par \par \uc2 But now the Democrat beats the Republican and the Republican goes into attack against the Democrat who beat the Republican, and you get things like January 6th, and you get things like the continuing Trumpian madness that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  spews every day about the election that he lost, and this would be the local version of that and the local January 6th version is they`re on your front lawn.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: Yes, Lawrence, but one of the things that does help me, I don`t know, wake up every day and put one foot in front of the other is that this is the minority of the American public. This is a small minority of the American public.\par \par \uc2 They are very loud. They are angry, as Alex said. She is right about that, but they are not now, nor will they be the majority of American voters. Now, will Republicans be able to rig the election process in their favor despite the math? That I think is the question for the Democrats in the senate, namely Senator Manchin and Kyrsten Sinema right at this moment.\par \par \uc2 But I think in the big picture, we have to remember that they are not the majority of the people. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lost two elections, the popular vote and two elections, and in the second one it was by about three times the amount as the first time.\par \par \uc2 So the American public is clear on how they feel about this approach to American politics and they do not want it. The question is are we going to be able to marginalize these angry voices, these bullying voices on the local level, but also on the national level?\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I want to switch to the situation in Texas that you`ve been covering, Alex on the new Texas abortion law because I saw some of your interview, which we ran here with Dr. Dermish (ph) and I want to run some of it again because it is so powerful with Dr. Dermish explaining to you what she`s already experienced with this Texas abortion law. Let`s watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The first patient who I saw after the law went into effect, when I told her that we could see cardiac activity, you know, she just curled up into a ball on the table and just started sobbing.\par \par \uc2 And she asked if she could hold my hand.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And that was all I could do for her in that moment. She was a college student, had a birth control failure, you know, she was a person who wasn`t in a situation where she could be pregnant.\par \par \uc2 Women in Texas have been punished legislative session after legislative session. These are people you know who are getting turned away, and this could be you next.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Alex, I`ve seen nothing on television that delivers more powerfully the reality of what`s happening in Texas.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Lawrence, the average woman in Texas would have to drive 200-and I think-75 miles to get an abortion, and let`s just be real, nobody wants to get an abortion.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:50]\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: This is not something that patients are running after to get. This is a wrenching decision, a wrenching decision, that the state of Texas has made, you know, for the last decade basically very, very difficult to see.\par \par \uc2 And at this stage, effectively impossible. There`s -- you know, there are a number of boundaries women need to clear, but now if their pregnancy shows signs of cardiac activity, which is usually around five or six weeks, which is before most women even know they`re pregnant, they can`t get an abortion in Texas, which means that out of state clinics are being flooded with calls, requests, there are women taking matters into their own hands.\par \par \uc2 I mean, Texas is a case study in what happens when you don`t have Roe v. Wade, and what is so shocking to me is the fact that we have really lost sight of humanity in this hour, whether it`s a school board member or a college student whose birth control failed her, we have lost our humanity.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yes, that is what this segment has been about. Alex Wagner, Zerlina Maxwell -- thank you both very much for joining this discuss tonight. Really appreciate it. Couldn`t have done it without you. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Thanks, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: Thanks, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, Donald Trump`s company is now a criminal defendant in a tax fraud case in Manhattan. According to federal rules, the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  can now be barred from doing business with the federal government, which means \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  should immediately lose the rights to operate his Washington, D.C. hotel on federal property.\par \par \uc2 That`s next.\par \par \uc2 [22:41:25]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Last week the House Oversight Committee released documents showing that Donald Trump`s Washington, D.C. hotel lost more than $70 million during his presidency. And tonight there are new reports that the hotel converted from the still federally-owned old Post Office Building in Washington will be sold.\par \par \uc2 According to "Wall Street Journal", the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  is in advance discussions to sell the rights to its Washington, D.C. hotel in a deal worth more than $370 million. The lease deal could ultimately fetch closer to $400 million which would represent roughly doubling of the money the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  spent to convert the government building into a luxury hotel.\par \par \uc2 The House Oversight Committee`s report on the Trump hotel found in 2018 \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  provided President Trump with a significant financial benefit by allowing him to delay making principal payments on the Trump Hotel`s $170 million loan.\par \par \uc2 The House Oversight Committee also found that the documents provided by the \uc2 General Services Administration\uc2 , quote, "raised new and troubling questions about former President Trump`s lease with GSA and the agency`s ability to manage the former president`s conflicts of interest during his term in office."\par \par \uc2 One of the initial conditions for holding a lease on that federal property was that no government official would be allowed to be involved in leasing the building. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  became a government official after he obtained that lease.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday Citizens for Responsibility and Ethics in Washington and the project of government oversight sent a letter to five federal agencies who have done business with \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  entities including the \uc2 General Services Administration\uc2  saying, quote, it is standard practice to suspend contractors under indictment. In fact, the government suspends and debars thousands of contractors each year including small businesses with limited financial means based on allegations far less serious than the criminal charges facing the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Trump Organization\uc2  has been indicted by a grand jury in Manhattan and is now facing criminal prosecution for tax fraud in New York. A former director of the U.S. Office of Government Ethics, Walter Shaub will join us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:48:12]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now, Walter Shaub, senior FX fellow at the project on government oversight. He`s the former director of the U.S. Office of Government Ethics in the Obama administration. He resigned in protest from that position during the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 Walter, thank you for very much joining us tonight. I know you`ve been focusing on this Trump Hotel since the transition days between the Obama administration and the Trump administration. Your reaction to this possible pending sale while the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  is under indictment in Manhattan.\par \par \uc2 WALTER SHAUB, FORMER DIRECTOR, U.S. OFFICE OF GOVERNMENT ETHICS: Yes. It`s absolutely crazy. He should not even have this lease. The GSA`s determination back in 2017 that he could keep it flew in the face of the language of the contract.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, the lowly contracting officer who made that decision was so taken with the starry eyed experience of working with the first family that he actually wound up asking \uc2 Ivanka Trump\uc2  to get coffee with him when all was said and done to sort celebrate the future that they had with the government.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yes. It`s -- I mean so it started off as a violation that a government official could even own this lease. That government official being \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now you`re at the stage where that company that owns that lease has been indicted and there are federal rules that say, when something like that happens to your company, the federal government can cancel all dealings with you.\par \par \uc2 SHAUB: Yes, they absolutely should suspend the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 . They`ve done it for less. Arthur Anderson was suspended on an indictment alone during the \uc2 Enron\uc2  matter. And they could do that here. You`ve got the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  been indicted, it`s entered into settlements with the New York Attorney General involving claims of fraud and false filings.\par \par \uc2 There`s no reason the federal government should run the risk of any new contracts being awarded to the \uc2 Trump Organization\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And let`s clarify what the Trump company actually owns. They don`t own the building. They own a lease, a long lease, and that lease is with the federal government and they can sell that lease to someone else.\par \par \uc2 SHAUB: Yes. If GSA approves the buyer, they can sell it. It`s nearly a 100- year lease, when you count the options for renewal. So this is an enormous commodity that the federal government gave and then illegally allowed the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  to retain and then simply failed to conduct any meaningful oversight during the Trump era.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:55]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And what about now? What is preventing any meaningful oversight now?\par \par \uc2 SHAUB: Well sadly, I think timidity. It`s the same thing we`re seeing with the Justice Department. There seems to be this fear that they`ll be viewed as engaging in retribution, politically motivated, or that they may face retaliation themselves from a future administration.\par \par \uc2 But I think it`s a failure to grasp that we`re so far outside of the norm and in such dangerous territory, that the greater risk in my view is the impunity from a lack of the government holding anyone accountable.\par \par \uc2 Its sentences us a nation to endure more of this because future bad actors will know they can get away with it.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Walter Shaub, thank you very much for joining us tonight. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 SHAUB: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:59:59]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The LAST WORD team has already posted our interview tonight with Florida school board member Jennifer Jenkins, so that you can send it to anyone who might have missed it.\par \par \uc2 You can find it on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  @TheLastWord.com, you can find it at MSNBC.com.\par \par \uc2 That is tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH BRIAN WILLIAMS" starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020211014ehad00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD WITH LAWRENCE O`DONNELL for October 12, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lawrence O`Donnell; John Heilemann, Eugene Robinson \par \uc2 7,397 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 October 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:37]\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 And it will be midnight in Washington in a couple of hours, but it`s going to be midnight in Washington right here on THE LAST WORD with Adam Schiff - -\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST, "TRMS": Oh, very nice.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: -- talking about his new book.\par \par \uc2 And thank you, Rachel, for sending overall those questions that you used last night that`ll get me through my entire interview with Adam Schiff tonight.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: If I didn`t write 100 questions for every five-minute interview that I do, I can get on other things in life. But you know me, I overwrite.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Uh-huh. And, you know, he actually made some other news today, so there`s a bit more to talk about than just that.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Fantastic. Go with God. Indeed.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Thanks, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Today, our first guest, Congressman Adam Schiff, said that the special House committee investigating the attack on the \uc2 Capitol\uc2  on January 6 will pursue criminal contempt charges against anyone who tries to defy the committee`s subpoenas. Insurrectionists in suits and ties is how Adam Schiff describes the Republican members of the House whose rhetoric encouraged and helped provoke the attack on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 One of the witnesses subpoenaed by the January 6th committee, \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 , in Adam Schiff`s new book, "Midnight in Washington: We Almost Lost Our Democracy and Still Could," he tells a story of \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  testifying to the House Intelligence Committee, while he was a member of the White House staff. This was the first year of the Trump`s presidency, the House of Representatives was controlled by Republicans, and the chair of the Intelligence committee was then republican \uc2 Devin Nunes\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On page 153, Adam Schiff writes: \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  showed up for testimony one afternoon with a list of only 25 questions he would deign to answer in the entire investigations, notwithstanding the fact that he was a key witness. More stupefying, he admitted that the list had been written by the White House, complete with a one-word answer to each questions. No.\par \par \uc2 That is what the January 6th Committee can expect if and when Bannon actually shows up to testify before the committee. A year before, Adam Schiff stood on the Senate floor, on the third day of the first impeachment trial of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF (D-CA): No Constitution can protect us. Right does not matter anymore. You know, you can`t trust this president do what`s right for this country. You can trust he`ll do what`s right for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He`ll do it now. He`s done it before. He`ll do it for the next several months. He`ll do it in the election if he`s allowed to.\par \par \uc2 This is why if you find him guilty, you must find that he should be removed, because right matters, because right matters. And the truth matters. Otherwise, we are lost.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: In his new book, Adam Schiff says during the attack of the \uc2 Capitol\uc2 , members of the House kept saying to him, you warned us. On page 12, he writes, fear was receding and anger at the president was quickly taking at its place, one of my Democratic colleagues came up to me, and was the first to say, what I would hear versions of for weeks to come, you said this would happen, she said.\par \par \uc2 Adam Schiff describes what he was feeling when Capitol Police was trying to move members of Congress to safety. He wrote, you need to get out a police officer shouted. Move, I made my way down to the well and joined the remaining members and staff filing out looking back at the doors being hammered to the rear of the chamber, glass now shattering, police officers pushed large cabinets in front of the doors and would soon draw their weapons.\par \par \uc2 You can`t let them see you, a Republican member said to me.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:01]\par \par \uc2 He`s right, another Republican member said. I know these people. I can talk to them. I can talk my way through them. You are in a whole different category.\par \par \uc2 In that moment, we were not merely members of different political parties, but on opposite sides of a much more dangerous divide.\par \par \uc2 At first I was oddly touched by these GOP members and by their evident concern, but by then I had been receiving death threats for years and that feeling seem gave way to another. If these Republican members had enjoying the president and falsely attacking me for four years, I wouldn`t need to be worried about my security, none of us would.\par \par \uc2 Adam Schiff writes that in the first impeachment trial of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in which Adam Schiff was the lead prosecutor in the Senate trials, Republicans proved to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , that the Republicans in Congress would never confront, him never constrain him, and had been fully and successfully cowed.\par \par \uc2 None of the Republicans have been more cowed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  than the House Republican Leader Kevin McCarthy who will become speaker of the, House if the Republicans win back control of the House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 In his book, Adam Schiff writes this about Kevin McCarthy: I learned all I would ever need to know about Kevin McCarthy on a plane, or to be more precise, shortly after landing. About six months before the 2010 midterm elections, we were both heading back to Washington from California happened to be seated together on a \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  flight.\par \par \uc2 Although we are both from California, our districts are separated by almost 100 miles and we will never really had the opportunity to get to know each other. While Democrats were still in the majority, the elections were shaping up to be tight, our margin was small, and there was growing doubt about whether we would retain that majority. During the flight, I express confidence that the economy would remain positive and we would continue to leave the House. Not surprisingly, he took the contrary view. It wasn`t much of a conversation, just the type of small talk you would have all waiting for a movie, any movie to star on a plane.\par \par \uc2 I thought nothing of it. The next morning I picked up one of "The Hill" newspapers. Everyone knows that Republicans are going to win the House, McCarthy and told a group of reporters after arriving at the \uc2 Capitol\uc2  from our flight. He said that he spoke to me on the plane and even I admitted Republicans would take back the majority.\par \par \uc2 Kevin, if we were having a private conversation on the plane, I would`ve thought it was a private conversation. But it wasn`t, you know I said the exact opposite of what you told the press. I know, Adam, was his reply, but you know how it goes.\par \par \uc2 I was incredulous. No, Kevin. I don`t. You just make stuff up, and that`s how you operate? He just shrugged.\par \par \uc2 That was simply how we operated, and how he still operates today. The man who warned us, in the first Trump impeachment trial, that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would do it again, has a new warning for us.\par \par \uc2 Republican legislatures, all across the country have continued Trump`s big lie about massive voter, fraud initiating phony new audits of the election results and replacing independent election officials, with the ones beholden to partisan legislatures. They are preparing the battlefield, and the struggle to overturn the next presidential election, if it doesn`t go their way, and should they regain the majorities in congress, they just might be successful.\par \par \uc2 The Trump presidency was a stress test for our system, for the results must wake us up, the survival of our institutions, and the rule of law, depends on what we do next.\par \par \uc2 Leading off our discussion tonight is Democratic Congressman Adam Schiff of California. He`s the chair of the House Intelligence Committee and a member of the January 6th Select Committee. He was the lead impeachment manager in the first impeachment trial of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . His new book is "Midnight in Washington: How We Almost Lost Democracy and Still Could."\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for joining us tonight. I really appreciate.\par \par \uc2 I want to begin with that story you tell about Kevin McCarthy, because we see in that story, in 2010, 11 years ago, behavior, that is purely Trumpian, if you just substitute \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for Kevin McCarthy in that story, and usage around the, plane and you had that conversation, and then he went out and just lied about it, everyone would just go well sure. Of course \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did that.\par \par \uc2 And so, the idea that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had to somehow turn Kevin McCarthy, corrupt Kevin McCarthy into something he wasn`t, is no longer my view, after reading your book.\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM, SCHIFF (D-CA): Well, I think that`s absolutely right.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:01]\par \par \uc2 Certainly, Kevin McCarthy was made for a moment like this, but he is who he is, and he`s always been a suspect, who he is today. In fact, one of the running themes as you know throughout the book, is something that Robert Caro, the historian once said, that power does not corrupt as much as reveals, it may not reveal the best, of us but it reveals a lot about who we are.\par \par \uc2 And power has revealed more and more, about who Kevin McCarthy is. It revealed on Sunday who Stephen Scalise is when he was unable on Chris Wallace`s program, to simply state the fact that the election was not stolen. It revealed about Bill Barr. It revealed a lot frankly, and the very inspiring and positive side about people like Maria Yovanovitch, and Alexander Vindman.\par \par \uc2 It`s not all stories of villainy. There are some real heroes that have emerged from this period, but you`re absolutely right, Kevin McCarthy was ahead of his time, but he has really made for a presidency that believes that truth is not true. That everyone is entitled to their own alternate facts, in the idea that he would ever step foot in the speaker`s office, is just a terrifying one.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Well, tell us about that idea, if the Republicans win, and get the House back in the next, election next year, Kevin McCarthy becomes speaker, what does that mean for the country, in the next two years of the Biden presidency?\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: Well, I think what that means is a couple of things. One is people have speculated well could \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  actually be the speaker, because you don`t need to be in Congress to be speaker, the reality is, he would need to be the speaker, because essentially, Kevin McCarthy would do anything \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  told him to do. I think that`s all too apparent.\par \par \uc2 And, one of the most profound indications of that is what took place after the insurrection. For a moment, you could see Kevin McCarthy putting his finger to the winds, and sensing that the direction of the wind was running against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , he adjusted incited a violent attack against the \uc2 Capitol\uc2 , and he made statements attributing responsibility to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . But it took all of a matter of, days before he went down to Mar-a-Lago to pledge his undying fealty to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  once again, because it was clear that the wind was blowing in the other direction.\par \par \uc2 And I have to say, among the most painful parts of this past several years to me, Lawrence is the fact that after we had to endure that terrible insurrection, and we might I have turned the corner as a country. We had the absolute wrong person leading the Republican Party in the House, in Kevin McCarthy, and even \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 . Who you can see struggled with, this and wanted to throw \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  overboard, lack the courage and conviction to do, and now we are forced to suffer, this again, until we can expunged him from the political scene.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So, did \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , in your view, corrupt the Republican Party in Congress, or did he give them a chance to reveal who they really are?\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: I think really both, it was really astounding to me, that someone is deeply flawed as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , in a matter of for sure years or less, could completely remake one of America`s great political parties, in his flawed image, but he did it. And the reason he was able to do it, was because all too many of the Republican leadership in the Congress, did not turn out to care anything about anything they said they cared about.\par \par \uc2 One of the most frequent questions I get from constituents and others around the country`s to the Republicans really believe what they`re saying publicly, when you talk to them privately? In the answer is all too often no, they don`t. They don`t believe in the ideology that they profess, they don`t believe in conservative, values they don`t believe in family values.\par \par \uc2 It turns out they only believe in the maintenance of their own position and power. That is where power has revealed, in these last four years. And to me, it was a terrible epiphany, that these members, that I`ve worked with, McCarthy per se, but others who I respected, some of who I admired, because I believed that they believed what they were saying. It turned out not to believe it at all.\par \par \uc2 And, to me, it doesn`t matter how brilliant the constitution, is or how well crafted era laws are, if -- as you played in that clip from the impeachment trial, if people don`t give content to the provisions in the Constitution, if they don`t live the spirit which they were written, if they don`t apply right and wrong, and the truth, then none of it is good enough to protect us.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: If Democrats lose control of the, House in the next election, that means, that the January 6th committee, really only has about 13 months left, in which, to do its work, complete its work. What can you tell us about, where you are in that first round of subpoenas? There are reports indicating that two of the first subpoenaed witnesses are in some contact with the committee. \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , Kash Patel, but it`s not clear at all whether \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  in the other two are in any contact with the committee about the subpoenas, and how will you enforce them?\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: Well, I think that \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  has been pretty public about his unwillingness to cooperate in any way. And from my point of view, it`s not up to him. He is required to show up. He`s required to testify. And if he does not, and does not have a reason, a legal reason to proffer which he doesn`t, then we will hold him in criminal contempt, and we will refer that to the Justice Department for prosecution.\par \par \uc2 That will be true of the other witnesses as well, if they do not comply. And, I think the reason why \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 , you know, you read it from the book, the reason why he feels empowered to do, that is because for four years, we had a Justice Department that did not apply the law equally. In Bill Barr, we had someone who was essentially running the presidents the, former president`s personal criminal defense firm.\par \par \uc2 But the situation has changed. We now have an attorney general of integrity, we have a Department of Justice that believes no one is above the law, we have an administration that is not asserting privilege to stymie the public from finding out what led to January 6th, and that gives me every hope and expectation, that not only will we move quickly, but that the Justice Department will also.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Well, what has not changed is that full legal enforcement and the congressional subpoena like this does take time, and if it becomes a matter of criminal prosecution, for defying that subpoena, we could be talking about years.\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: Well, civil litigation, Lawrence, you`re absolutely, right. The civil litigation took us years, took us almost two years to get the testimony finally. The weight of a criminal prosecution, I think, has a very different impact on witnesses, and potential witnesses.\par \par \uc2 And you`re right, they could take even a long time to prosecute a criminal case. But you prosecute one criminal case, and every other witness that looks at that says, okay, the Department of Justice is serious. The Congress is serious. I better comply, because I do not want to face prosecution.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s a big club. And it`s necessary, because for four years, effectively the rule of law was undermined, by the leadership of that department. In those days are gone. To me, this will be a real test of the recovery of our democracy, if the Justice Department does not view anyone`s being above the law, and that`s fully my expectation that they will uphold that principle.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So if you`re \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  sitting home tonight watching this, ignoring the subpoena that you and your committee absent to him, and he`s laughing at this, how long will \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  be able to stay at home laughing about this before he feels the weight of some kind of enforcement of that subpoena?\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: We intend to act very quickly, with uncooperative witnesses. So I can`t be specific as to any one person, but without any dissent, without any disagreement, all the members of the committee are quite uniformed, that we will not abide delay, we will not abide efforts to play rope-a-dope with us as we saw in the past. And, look, \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 , this one story about the last administration to me is so.\par \par \uc2 The man runs for president on a platform building a wall that he says Mexico will pay for. It`s an absurd promise of course. He doesn`t build a wall. Mexico doesn`t pay for it. A bunch of his fellow grifters, including \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 , and start up a fund, a private fund to build the wall. They steal money from the supporters of the president and what is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  do? He pardons Bannon.\par \par \uc2 No wonder \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  feels that he can go on with the grift. But it`s not Donald Trump`s Justice Department anymore. It`s the Justice Department that believes it serves the public interest, not the interest of the person of the president. And if \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  does not have that message, yet he is not paying attention.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Chairman Adam Schiff, thank you very much for starting us off tonight. I really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Chairman Schiff`s new book is midnight in Washington, it is "How We Almost Lost Our Democracy and Still Could".\par \par \uc2 And coming up, the \uc2 American Medical Association\uc2  has filed an appears court brief in opposition to the new Texas abortion law. The attorney rule that brief, Shannon Zeldin, will join us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Last night, the \uc2 U.S. Justice Department\uc2 , asked a federal appeals court, to prevent Texas from enforcing the states new abortion law, while that law is being challenged in federal court. In its pleading, the Justice Department call the law and unconstitutional scheme, and said, if Texas is scheme is permissible, no constitutional right is safe from state sanction sabotage of this kind.\par \par \uc2 Last week in Texas, Federal Judge \uc2 Robert Pitman\uc2  blocked the law from being enforced, while it was being challenged in federal court, but two days later, federal appeals court reversed that decision and allow the Texas law to remain in effect.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:02]\par \par \uc2 Bridget Amiri, deputy director of the ACLU Reproductive Freedom Project, told "\uc2 USA Today\uc2 ", a legal fight has quote created devastation and chaos, in Texas, where patients were given a glimmer of, hope before their appointments began getting canceled, it`s really hard on staff and providers and patients, to have this whiplash you said, it`s destabilizing, it`s dehumanizing, and it really should not be happening.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 American Medical Association\uc2 , along with over a dozen other medical organizations, filed an amethyst, brief in the case written by our next guest. The brief says that the Texas abortion law not only endangers the health and when being a pregnant women seeking care, but also forces medical professionals to choose between long establishments effect ethical in clinical standards of care, and their personal and professional risk of being bankrupted by lawsuits.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Sharon Selden, who serves as counsel to the 19 medical organizations, and the amicus brief filed against the Texas abortion law.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for joining us, tonight we really appreciate it. Your brief is fascinating because one of the issues, in bringing this legal challenge, has been the challenge of standing, and standing generally meaning, you have standing to sue, and bring legal action, if you are somehow being harmed. And it seems that the medical profession, is the target of this law, you are representing in a sense the medical profession in this amicus brief.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON SELDEN, REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS ATTORNEY: That`s right, in this amicus brief, the medical organizations, which include the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2 , the \uc2 American Medical Association\uc2 , American Academy of Pediatricians, and many, many other leading medical organizations, are weighing in, to support the government`s case against Texas. So, this case, the medical organizations are not asserting their own briefs, they`re not bringing their own suit, but they are weighing in to say, these issues are essential to the members of our organizations, and the patients that we serve, because the Texas law cuts at the very heart of the doctor-patient or clinician-patient relationship, interferes with safe ethical, medical evidence based care.\par \par \uc2 And that`s something that we should all be concerned about, both patients are seeking abortions to end, unwanted or unintended pregnancies, but also everyone who is or could be pregnant in Texas is vulnerable because of the way in which this law interferes with the safe evidence based provisional medical care.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You`ve seen the arguments that the state of Texas is making in this case so far. What is the most challenging argument, that you have to meet? That the state of Texas is making here. What is the legally most difficult points that you have to get over?\par \par \uc2 SELDEN: For the government, for the United States, government and its fight with Texas, the initial fight is really a threshold question, can the \uc2 United States government\uc2  go into court and sue the state of Texas, because the state of Texas has stripped away individual rights, protected by the United States Constitution. And, you see the United States in its brief, so the Department of Justice in its briefs, relying on some older cases, this is not something that frequently happens.\par \par \uc2 But it does happen, and it happens in ways that the courts have recognized, and in ways that the courts have recognized that the \uc2 United States government\uc2  has an equitable right, a general equitable right, not written in the Constitution, not written in federal statute, but one that has long been recognized, by federal courts in this country. That the United States can go into its federal courts, to protect the constitutional rights of its citizens, and that`s with the department of justice`s saying it has to do here.\par \par \uc2 That`s a tough argument, not because it`s conceptually difficult, that`s kind of the promise, at the very core of our constitutional scheme. It`s a tough argument because whereas to be made. It has to be made in the circuit, may actually be made in the Supreme Court. It`s the made in courts that are attending is seen in other cases, challenging as the Texas abortion law to side with the state of Texas, and to defer to it, and to allow it to adopt and enforce this unconstitutional ban on abortion.\par \par \uc2 So, I think it`s a well rooted argument, but it`s one that faces particular challenges because of the deference that these courts have given to the state on this issue.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Let`s listen to what Dr. Amna Jermish said about her experience with one patient.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. AMNA JERMISH: The first patient who I saw after the law went into effect, when I told her that we could see cardiac activity in her, she just curled up into a ball on the table and just started sobbing.\par \par \uc2 And she asked me if she could hold my hand. And that was all I could do for her in that moment. She was a college student, had a (INAUDIBLE) failure. You know, she was a person who was in a situation where she could be pregnant.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Will the appeals court recognize the timing urgency in a case like that when stories like that are being told every day.\par \par \uc2 SELDEN: I certainly hope so. It is hard to think a constitutional that is more time sensitive, more intimate than the ability to decide whether or not to continue a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 And I`m so glad that we are hearing this stories directly from doctors and some of the briefing in the U.S. versus Texas case, you hear some of the stories of patients who have been looking to get care during this period.\par \par \uc2 And this law is just unconscionable in the way that it treats women and girls in Texas. It is isolating. It separates pregnant women and girls from their family, from their friends, from their (INAUDIBLE), the resources they could talk to about their pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Why? Because it puts all of those people into liability to a significant monetary damages. And patients know that by talking to their doctors, their family or friends are putting them in jeopardy and it takes away basic essential reproductive care which is what abortion is.\par \par \uc2 And you know, abortion is among the safest ones of reproductive care and an essential part of kind of reproductive care that doctors provide to their patients all the time. And in banning it, Texas is really endangering the health and the well-being of women and girls throughout the state.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Shannon Selden, thank you very much for joining us tonight. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 SELDEN: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, John Heilemann and Eugene Robinson will join us next on the politics of the Biden legislative agenda in Congress.\par \par \uc2 [22:32:27]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: After what may have been only a temporary slump in polls, President Biden`s polling numbers appear to be recovering with the latest Reuters poll showing his job approval at 48 percent approval with disapproval at 47 percent.\par \par \uc2 And with voters perception of the economy being closely linked to presidential approval it is good news for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  that the most recent Gallup poll finds that 57 percent of Americans say their financial situation is good or excellent, the highest in 19 years.\par \par \uc2 After the first pieces of the Biden legislative agenda flew through the Congress, seemingly quickly and delivered what felt like immediate COVID relief assistance to voters, the progress of the Biden infrastructure package has appeared to be slow going and noisy by comparison as Democrats publicly with increasing frustration have been negotiating with Democrats.\par \par \uc2 "Washington Post`s" Eugene Robinson writes, "What I see is a pretty normal exercise in legislative give and take except that it`s all happening within the Democratic Party while Republicans swoop, holler and obstruct from the peanut gallery.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to Congress, things never go as quickly as they might and there always comes at least one moment when it appears that all is lost.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now Eugene Robinson associate editor, Pulitzer-prize winning columnist for the "Washington Post" and John Heilemann, host and executive producer of Showtime`s "The Circus" and host of the "Hell and High Water" podcast from the recount. Both are MSNBC political analysts.\par \par \uc2 And Eugene, I love your column about this because it does actually looks like the normal legislative process. And the first speed through legislation that the Biden administration, the Democrats were doing did not look like the normal process at all because it was going so very smoothly. That may have gotten some spectators out there spoiled into thinking that`s the way it`s always going to work.\par \par \uc2 EUGENE ROBINSON, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: I think they got some people -- you know, Lawrence from your experience in the Senate that is not the way things work.\par \par \uc2 You know, $1.9 trillion does not just zoom through the Congress in a matter of, you know, days or mere weeks. It takes a long time to work this sort of thing out. There`s a lot of give and take and the Democrats have very slim majority, you know, 50/50 Senate, three-fourth votes in the House.\par \par \uc2 So yes, I think the ease with which the rescue package went through, spoiled some people or made them forget what legislating is really like.\par \par \uc2 And then the other thing that`s sort of turbo charge this, I think largely false narrative of Democratic doom and a Biden collapse was a poll about a week ago showing his approval at 38 percent.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:44]\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: \uc2 Quinnipiac\uc2  poll, good pollster but that poll increasing looks like an outlier for all the poll since show essentially what that Reuters IPSOS poll showed. The Biden above 48.7 or 49.46 percent or 50/50.\par \par \uc2 None of them show him under water much less way underwater there (INAUDIBLE) \uc2 IPSOS\uc2  poll showed. Nonetheless, my newspaper, our network, other news outlets continue referring to that shocking \uc2 Quinnipiac\uc2  poll in (INAUDIBLE) the sort of definitive status check on President Biden which it now -- do we have evidence that it isn`t.\par \par \uc2 And it`s -- you know, I don`t like to do media criticism because there are lots of people who do that. but this just seems kind of crazy to look at the world in a way that it really just isn`t.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: John, my coverage of the Biden two-track infrastructure plan started off with just I could not fathom how you could do this. I don`t understand how these two 747s, you know, land on the same runway at the same time, I just didn`t get it.\par \par \uc2 And then I watched it go remarkably smoothly. And kind of very, very surprised especially when they got to the point where they actually voted in the Senate -- all 50 of them voted for the $3.5 trillion budget resolution and if you have got a problem with the 3.5. That`s the time to have the problem. And they didn`t. They voted for it.\par \par \uc2 You know, Joe Manchin and \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  and so that made me think this thing was going really smoothly and now they`re arguing about the 3.5, they`re kind of deciding to do that at the end of the process instead of the beginning after they already voted for it.\par \par \uc2 And so now we are seeing what I consider like the realistic section of the legislation.\par \par \uc2 JOHN HEILEMANN, MSNBC NATIONAL AFFAIRS ANALYST: Yes. I mean look, I mean Lawrence there`s the Senate as it should and there`s a Senate as it is. I think you are familiar with both. I mean the Senate as it should is the Senate where all the Democrats vote for that budget resolution and then don`t quibble over numbers going forward.\par \par \uc2 But the Senate as it is, is the one where Joe Manchin and \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  just voted for that thing inside ok, whatever, negotiate, use our leverage later.\par \par \uc2 So here we are and, you know, at a pile a little bit of the same analysis, and I think, you know, look, I don`t think there is a lot right now, a lot of Democrats who have gone through this process and gotten to this point.\par \par \uc2 I number one, think most of them think that eventually they believe this, I am not saying it`s true, but they think eventually it will -- both these bills will get passed.\par \par \uc2 But there`s also now a lot of them who have an enormous sense that there`s a lot of momentum, and there`s a lot of trust between the two sides, which is a little bit of a problem right now because the reality is that moderates and Democrats both feel betrayed in various directions and they have to eventually come together if they`re going to get this done.\par \par \uc2 I`d say my political analysis goes a little bit in the same direction in terms of Eugene`s assessment which is there`s the world as it is and the world as it should be. It makes no sense perhaps and I did not -- I`m the last person who`s going to say \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is in collapse, he is not.\par \par \uc2 I don`t think the Democrats are in disarray, they are not. They`re on normal levels of whatever they are. But I will tell you right now if you go around and talk to House Democrats in competitive districts, they`re looking at their numbers and they`re not happy right now.\par \par \uc2 And there is a lot of concern about turn-out next year, about the base being frustrated and about voters in the middle of the party being frustrated for a slightly different -- for slightly different sets of reasons.\par \par \uc2 And so what I just hear over and over again from my reporting is that House Democrats are terrified about what`s going to happen in 2022.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yes. And one of the challenges is how to describe what they are doing and last night on this program, Anat Shenker-Osorio (ph) had what so far is the line of the week, at least up until this segment when she said the Democrats have to stop selling the recipe and start selling the brownies.\par \par \uc2 And Eugene, someone`s got to teach them how to do that pretty quickly.\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: Yes. Well, you know, I have complained for years and years, the Democrats are just lousy at modern communications and strategic communications. They never come up with a phrase. They never emphasized, you know, what this is going to do for you rather -- you know they`re talking about these imaginary $3.5 versus $1.8.\par \par \uc2 I mean talk about what it`s going to do for you, for the constituents. And please, that`s all I can say. Please.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Eugene Robinson and John Heilemann, we`ll have brownies for you next time. Thank you very much for joining us. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 HEILEMANN: Please.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HEILEMANN: Thank you Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And coming up, Jason Fuhrman was the chair of President Obama`s Council of Economic Advisers and he says the reconciliation bill Democrats are negotiating in congress might be the last chance Democrats have to advance their agenda for a very, very long time. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 [22:44:46]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The president feels that we`re continuing to make progress. We are having important discussions of what a package that is smaller than $3.5 trillion would look like, those conversations have to happen with a range of members.\par \par \uc2 We are at the point I should where there are choices that need to be made. That`s the point we`re at now. Given there will be fewer dollars that`ll be spent.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Last week in the meeting with House Democrats, President Biden told them that they will have to lower their target to around $2 trillion to get a reconciliation bill that can pass the Senate as part of the Biden two-trac infrastructure package.\par \par \uc2 Today Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  acknowledged the lower overall spending target for that legislation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: I`m very disappointed that we are not going with the original $3.5 trillion which was very transformative.\par \par \uc2 We have to make sure we have a bill which I also said is that we have to have something that`ll pass the House and pass Senate. Pass the House and pass the Senate and I am not asking members to vote for something that has no chance to pass in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [22:50:00]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Democratic \uc2 John Yarmuth\uc2 , chair of the House Budget Committee has suggested that the Democrats could take up another reconciliation bill next year to expand on what they might pass this year.\par \par \uc2 Jason Fuhrman served as the chair of President Obama`s Council of Economic Advisers told the "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " quote, "The problem now is this may be the last train leaving the station for a long time -- that could be five, ten, 20 years before there`s another shot at a lot of these issues.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Jason Fuhrman, professor of the practice of economic policy at Harvard`s Kennedy School. He served as chair of the Council of Economic Advisers during the Obama administration.\par \par \uc2 Professor Fuhrman, thank you very much for joining us tonight. What do you mean five, ten, 20 years? How could there possibly be that kind of blank space between these kinds of legislative efforts?\par \par \uc2 JASON FUHRMAN, HARVARD`S KENNEDY SCHOOL: Look, Lawrence, most presidents get one big fiscal thing done. You know, President \uc2 Obama\uc2  got two. He got the Recovery Act and the Affordable Care Act.\par \par \uc2 President Trump got one. His tax cuts.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is on track to getting three done. Enormous rescue plan, an infrastructure bill and this reconciliation bill which focuses around climate change and childcare. So it`s a lot.\par \par \uc2 I`d love to see more next year. I`d love to see more the year after. I just don`t think anyone should count on that.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Well, yes. You know, when I read that quote of yours today, I looked back at and remembered that the Clinton health care bill, comprehensive health care reform bill, Democrats dream, 1994, it fails in the House and Senate. Doesn`t pass. Doesn`t even come to a vote in the House. Doesn`t come to a vote in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 And it`s 16 years later that the Democrats take up health care in the Affordable Care Act. And in between, nothing. Not a single sentence of health care reform did the Democrats try to advance in Congress.\par \par \uc2 FUHRMAN: Yes, that`s absolutely right. Or look at the Waxman Markey climate change bill in 2009. It passed the House. It is held in the Senate. It`s now 12 years later the first serious legislative effort at climate change.\par \par \uc2 You can always cobble things together. President \uc2 Obama\uc2  did some things on administratively using his regulatory authority. We had some tax credits extended. But you know, it`s taken 12 years since the failure of the last major climate change legislation.\par \par \uc2 Again, I hope that`s not the case. Democrats should keep trying again next year if they need to compromise with Republicans next year or the year after. They should try to do that too. Anything to move the ball forward.\par \par \uc2 I just think you want to try to get all of your big things in now. You want to lock them in to be permanent. Don`t leave them at the wiles of the future Congress. I mean unfortunately you`re going to prioritize and make sure you do some things really well so that they contribute a lasting (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I think part of the importance of what we`re talking about right now and what you`ve I can identified in here is that this is what the argument is about. This is why the people who`ve been fighting for the bigger package, the $3.5 trillion have been fighting as long as they`ve been fighting and why they`re trying to hang on as long as they can to as much of it as possible because I think they recognize what you`re talking about. This is their only chance for a very long time.\par \par \uc2 FUHRMAN: Yes. Look, from an economic perspective, I`m an economist. We can afford $3.5 trillion. We could afford even more than $3.5 trillion. Including paying for some of it with tax increases, Medicare reforms and also some borrowing because these are investments and interest rates are really low.\par \par \uc2 From an economic perspective, it can be big. But you know, Lawrence, if all we can get is $2 trillion, you can do a lot with $2 trillion. You can dramatically reduce child poverty. You can take a gigaton of emissions out -- carbon emissions every year. You can make health care more affordable for millions of people.\par \par \uc2 So $2 trillion isn`t just better than nothing. $2 trillion, you could do really meaningful things if you prioritize and spend that money well.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Well, yes. And that is what the final legislative decision comes down to. There will be a number. It might be 2. It may be somewhere in the neighborhood of two or around there. Where everyone stares at it and realizes this is it. We vote for this or we get nothing. And that`s going to be the moment when the Democrats make their final choice about what they`re voting for.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:54]\par \par \uc2 FUHRMAN: Yes. $2 trillion is not just better than nothing. $2 trillion could be historic for climate change, for children, for health care. Again, if can you get more than $2 trillion that would be even better. You get $3.5 -- that would be wonderful.\par \par \uc2 I just don`t think you can right now. You heard Speaker Pelosi say you can`t get that. So focus not on the money, not on the dollars but what outcomes you`re trying to achieve. And you know, as the price tag shrinks, you`re going to need to be smarter about that. You`re going to need to prioritize. You`re going to need to figure out how to do a few things well. Either it`s focus on children, on climate change and on, you know, coverage of health care.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Professor Jason Fuhrman, thank you very much for joining us tonight. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 FUHRMAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:59:56]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Earlier tonight, the House passed the identical debt ceiling increase that the Senate passed last week which will extend the debt ceiling into early December. The House vote was 219 to 206.\par \par \uc2 Every Republican voted against raising the debt ceiling that would cover the $8 trillion in increased debt that created by the Trump presidency a debt that they approved during the Trump presidency.\par \par \uc2 That is tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH BRIAN WILLIAMS" starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ M