 of our COVID response. We are using military assets as we talked about, Rachel, to actually fill short gaps, because the biggest limiting step right now is not the availability of ventilators or beds. It`s on stuff beds. It`s the availability, so we can see a lot more of this critically whole patients from point A to point B.\par \par \uc2 Hopefully, this is -- we don`t see a lot more of it, but I expect we will in the coming months.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Right, as an important thing that we don`t see as visibly, we see those military teams and those federal response teams turning up in rural hospitals around the country. That the other part of it is that there`s military transport assets., developed a force in the combat context of being used now for American civilians needing to be moved to care within our country.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Gupta, I really appreciate you being here as always, thank you for your service, thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 GUPTA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: All right. We`ll be right back. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: This batch is from the federal appeals court in Texas today. Quote: an abortion provider in Houston cried with her first patient after Texas`s abortion ban passed. The patient had detectable embryonic cardiac activity on the day of her scheduled procedure after having none the day before. She was unable to get an abortion.\par \par \uc2 Provider in Houston recalls a patient with five children, two of them have disabilities, who had embryonic cardiac activity at just five weeks, four days pregnant. The patient frantically pleaded, what am I going to do? What is going to happen now?\par \par \uc2 One provider had a patient say put oil in her vagina to try to terminate her pregnancy and worries the abortion ban will force more people into, quote, back alleyways.\par \par \uc2 One provider in Houston spoke of a 12-year-old patient who came in with her mother, a single working mother