f VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020211012ehab00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for October 11, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Garrett Haake, Andrea Mitchell, Shaquille Brewster, Leigh Ann  Caldwell, Kelly O`Donnell, Yamiche Alcindor, Pete Williams, Nahid Bhadelia,  Ken Dilanian \par \uc2 9,021 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 October 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 SHAQUILLE BREWSTER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: That is the focus that they`re having today but you have a lot of the cheers, a lot of the celebrations that the Boston Marathon is back, Andrea.\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, MSNBC ANCHOR: Shaq Brewster, wish we were all up there with you. And all the Marathon action was on NBCSN on Peacock today, and you can catch an encore presentation of the race tonight on the Olympic channel.\par \par \uc2 And that does it for this edition of "Andrea Mitchell Reports." Garrett Haake is in for Chuck Todd right now when MTP day -- "MTP DAILY" starts.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, MSNBC HOST: If it`s Monday, it`s a democracy in crisis because as we`ve seen over the last few days, the lie that incited an insurrection over the last election is now a rallying cry for Republicans trying to take back Congress and the White House in the next elections.\par \par \uc2 Plus, a steady dose of hopeful news in the battle against COVID as the U.S. sees declines in infections, hospitalizations, and deaths, and the FDA considers emergency approval of a new antiviral drug.\par \par \uc2 And a remarkable story of espionage, nuclear secrets, and peanut butter sandwiches as the \uc2 FBI\uc2  accuses a U.S. Nuclear Engineer and his wife of plotting to pass State`s secrets to foreign government sources.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY, I`m Garrett Haake in for Chuck Todd.\par \par \uc2 The last 48 hours or so have been a sobering reminder about the current state of politics in America and the unstable future that lies ahead for us.\par \par \uc2 It`s not just that Republicans in Congress and across the country have acquiesced to Trump, despite him losing the election, it`s what they seem to be acquiescing to, that the future of the party rests with lies and misinformation like this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The election was a fraud, and if we want to save our country and make America great again, we have only one choice, we must elect strong unyielding America-first Republicans at every level, and we must pass a complete overhaul of our entire election system to ensure it`s free, fair, honest, and able to be fully and quickly audited.\par \par \uc2 The single biggest issue, the issue that gets the most -- the most polls, the most respect, the biggest cheers, is talking about the election fraud of the 2020 Presidential election. Nobody`s ever seen anything like it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: What the President said there in Iowa about our elections on Saturday night is not true, but you can`t dismiss that he keeps saying it and more and more people seem to be believing it. It also comes following comments he made last week falsely claiming that the real insurrection happened on election night.\par \par \uc2 Alongside the former President, this weekend was Senator Chuck Grassley, that`s the most senior Republican in the Senate, the ranking member of the Judiciary Committee, and the man who would be President pro tem if Republicans took back the Senate. That makes him third in line to the presidency.\par \par \uc2 It was notable to watch him up there listening to Trump`s attacks on democracy and not pushing back at all. And why isn`t he pushing back? Well, Grassley, who is running for re-election gave us a pretty clear window into why.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. CHUCK GRASSLEY, (R-IA): I was born at night, but not last night, so if I didn`t accept the endorsement of a person that`s got 91 percent of the Republican voters in Iowa, I wouldn`t be too smart. I`m smarter enough to accept that endorsement.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GRASSLEY: I got you.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: I love you, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: That pretty much sums up the entire dynamic in the Republican Party right now, so does this interview with the House`s number two Republican, Steve Scalise.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WALLACE, ANCHOR, FOX NEWS CHANNEL: Do you think the 2020 election was stolen from Donald Trump?\par \par \uc2 REP. STEVE SCALISE, (R-LA): Oh, Chris, I`ve been very clear from the beginning, if you look at a number of states, they didn`t follow their state passed laws that govern the election for President. That is what the United States Constitution says.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: I understand you think there were regularities and things that need to be fixed. Do you think the election was stolen?\par \par \uc2 SCALISE: And it`s not just the regulated, states that did not follow the laws set which the Constitution says they`re supposed to follow.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: That`s the number two Republican in the House and a possible candidate to be Speaker if Republicans take that chamber back.\par \par \uc2 Folks, the bottom line here, amid the day to day drama in Washington over the fate of the Biden agenda, the debt limit brinksmanship, the government shutdown threats, and the inter-party squabbles on the left, an exceedingly potent threat to the long-term viability of U.S. democracy instability is finding serious, serious traction on the right. And ignoring this isn`t going to make it go away.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got to talk about it and report on it. And here to do that, NBC`s Leigh Ann Caldwell on Capitol Hill, NBC`s Kelly O`Donnell outside the White House. Also joining us is "PBS NewsHour" White House Correspondent, and MSNBC Contributor, Yamiche Alcindor, and "Politico" National Political Correspondent, Meridith McGraw.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: So, Leigh Ann, I`ll start with you. You heard Chuck Grassley`s rationale for hitching his political future to former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 He`s doing the same thing in the halls of Congress where we talked to him too after his committee detailed the former President`s quest to overturn the election before being buffed by the d -- being buffed by the DOJ in early January, he wrote.\par \par \uc2 President Trump did what we`d expect the President to do on an issue of this importance. He listened to his senior advisors and followed their advice and recommendations.\par \par \uc2 Now, this is on a meeting about how best to overturn election results, meaning a lot of people think that the President shouldn`t even be having.\par \par \uc2 Leigh Ann, Chuck Grassley might know better, but if this is the most senior Republicans saying these sorts of things in the Senate, do we have to give up the idea that there`s somehow anti-Trump or wish it wasn`t Trump winning in Congress anymore? Or is this kind of where we`re at with the Republican Party?\par \par \uc2 LEIGH ANN CALDWELL, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: I think we have to do give up on that idea, Garrett, because think of the people who aren`t running for reelection, and they made a choice. Do they have to try to win over the Trump voters, or do they just fell out?\par \par \uc2 You have people like Senator Richard Burr of North Carolina, Senator Roy Blunt of Missouri, and they knew that it was going to be very difficult for them to win reelection, especially if they had a tough primary, which was expected.\par \par \uc2 So, in addition to looking at how people are running their races to get reelected, you also have to look at the people who decided to make that choice to actually not move further to the right, not embrace the former President`s lies about the last election.\par \par \uc2 And as far as Senator Grassley is concerned, a source told me that he`s getting a lot of blowback in Iowa from his -- for his vote on the bipartisan infrastructure bill.\par \par \uc2 And so, he has to -- even though he`s served, you know, seven terms in Congress -- in the Senate, he still has to shore up that Republican, that Trump-base in order for him to win this election, he thinks. And that`s why you saw him on this stage with Senator -- or President -- former President Trump just this weekend.\par \par \uc2 Now, there is some concern from some Republicans, though, because they worry that the more that the former President is in the picture, the more focus that Democrats can put on the former President instead of Republicans being able to focus on the current President who is underwater in a lot of these states, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Kelly, I want to bring you in on that point because I feel like we heard this from President Biden all the time when he was candidate Biden, the idea of, you know, don`t compare him to the almighty, compare him to the alternative.\par \par \uc2 How important is it to the Biden White House, to Democrats` political efforts, that the alternative still be the former guy, as they like to call it former President Trump being out there creating that contrast?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`DONNELL, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: It certainly is a big part of the approach from the Biden White House. And we know how unusual this is, we have not had a potential rematch in presidential politics before, we`ve not had someone who has left the office conduct himself the way that Donald Trump has, and remained so much in the national political conversation.\par \par \uc2 So, in many ways, what brought President Biden to this position in the White House was the unusual nature of Trump --\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: -- and what he`s got to do to stay there and to fight for his own agenda, still has a lot to do with President Trump.\par \par \uc2 And the narrative that Trump is able to build among not just Republicans, but independent voters are important to the Biden agenda.\par \par \uc2 And certainly, Republicans on Capitol Hill would be better off trying to take shots at what this current President has done or not done during his time in the office going into 2022 compared to having to re-litigate the Trump era. But that`s not the hand that has been dealt to everyone involved here.\par \par \uc2 And certainly the Biden White House is concerned about the President`s poll numbers coming down and wants to see action on things like its own agenda, as a way to make that more real for voters to improve his poll numbers.\par \par \uc2 They would argue if people can see benefits in their own lives and communities with infrastructure, and all the social programs that are related to this, if you can hear protesters, there are climate protesters outside, that`s a big part of the package as well, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Meridith, I want to go back to kind of a 30,000 vote -- foot view here. You were at the Trump rally in Iowa with these mainstream Republicans standing by the former President as he was making those remarks we played at the top, but what else were they standing by?\par \par \uc2 I mean, you kind of own what President -- former President Trump says as it comes out of his mouth when you`re up there on stage. What do these mainstream Republicans now own?\par \par \uc2 MERIDITH MCGRAW, NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, POLITICO: Well, at this rally, a lot of what the former President talked about was criticisms towards the Biden administration, towards his immigration policies, what`s happening at the border, and about his withdrawal from Afghanistan, but the main focus of it was on his claim that -- his false claim that he won the 2020 election, and that there was widespread voter fraud that led to Biden getting into the White House.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 MCGRAW: But another thing was two, he was going after people like that Minority Leader Mitch McConnell, on saying he didn`t have the courage to overthrow the election. He talked about his deal with the Democrats to raise the debt ceiling and was slamming McConnell for that.\par \par \uc2 So, while these folks are up on stage, they`re also standing with a lot of the sharp elbows Trump has had towards anyone who criticizes him, who criticizes his agenda, and what he stands for.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Yamiche, I want to play for you something that former Trump National Security aide Fiona Hill said on this network on Friday, we`ll talk about it on the other side, take a listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FIONA HILL, FORMER NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL OFFICIAL: The real danger is that we`re moving forward on the basis of a lie. If we don`t get to the bottom of what happened on January 6, if we don`t have an honest conversation about that, if members of Congress in the Republican Party right now don`t look themselves in the mirror, you know, fess up to what actually happened there and to speak the truth, we`re heading down a path that many other countries have tried before towards the degradation of our democracy.\par \par \uc2 And you know, very, unfortunately, on a path towards authoritarianism and autocracy. And you know, I don`t say that lightly now that has anyone else, but we`re in a very dangerous moment right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I want to ask you based on that, a kind of a version of the same question I asked Meridith, which is how -- to what degree do Republicans own the events of January 6 as the memory hold them, and as they pretend in many cases, that what happened on January 6, wasn`t really what happened or wasn`t really as bad as we all witnessed it being, what does that do to that party as they try to figure out how they do want to go forward?\par \par \uc2 YAMICHE ALCINDOR, WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT, PBS NEWSHOUR: Well, for Republicans, what it does is continue the threat to democracy. And it --and in some ways, it provides a home for the sort of existential crisis of our democracy in the Republican Party. So, what Fiona Hill is talking about when she`s talking about the degradation of democracy, this is how democracies end.\par \par \uc2 I`ve talked to so many immigrants over the last few months and years, really, in covering the Biden administration -- I mean, covering the Trump administration, rather, and they all say the same thing, and this was even before January 6.\par \par \uc2 They were saying the way that this happens where you have someone like President Trump -- former President Trump, who was riling up his base, who was feeding them lies, who is saying, if you are my political opponent, you`re not just someone who I disagree with, you`re someone who I will try to end, you`re someone who I will villainize, you`re someone who I will try to make sure doesn`t have any sort of power by any means necessary.\par \par \uc2 That is how you end up in situations with dictatorships, like in Haiti and in Venezuela. And they said all this to me before January 6, and I remember Haitian immigrants telling me, you know, all of the -- all the former President has all these people who are angered by his light, and they could get violent, and they could sort of taking out their anger on lawmakers. And that`s exactly what happened on January 6.\par \par \uc2 So, what Fiona Hill is talking about, it`s something that has already happened in so many other countries. And I watched that Trump rally on Saturday, and one of the things that I thought really stuck out to me was that he`s upping the ante former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 He`s not just saying that the 2020 election was stolen, he`s saying we need to take back our country now, he`s saying our country will not be here in three years if we don`t change the who`s in power right now.\par \par \uc2 So, he`s really feeding people this idea that not only do they need to sort of try to take back whatever power they have but that they need to, by any means necessary, take the power from President Biden right now. That`s a scary thing to say.\par \par \uc2 And when you look at Chuck Grassley, someone who was very, very critical of former President Trump after January 6, and you see the about-face that he has made because he`s up for reelection, it tells you that Republicans are just scared of former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 He has the power, he has the base, and you have lawmakers who, even when they had a little backbone on January 6, they`ve simply looked at this and said, the calculation is I`m going to hold on to political power, instead of calling out sort of lies.\par \par \uc2 And one last thing, I think you can look no further than the former Vice President Mike Pence to see the change on January 6. This is someone who had to be evacuated, the crowds were chanting about hanging him, and he is on TV now trying to sound how -- somehow downplay January 6.\par \par \uc2 That tells you that this isn`t just the people who are loyal to President Trump, who are in his orbit, but it`s also any Republican who wants power again.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Yes, that`s sort of the -- like the Simpsons Boo-urns, you know, they intentionally mishearing that they were out here, chanting hang Mike Pence.\par \par \uc2 Meridith, you`ve covered the statewide efforts around this country to change how elections are dealt with, something like 92 Republican lawmakers now in different places calling for a nationwide audit. You know, this goes to Yamiche`s point. Look, Joe Biden`s going to be President until 2024 like there`s not going to be a Trump reinstatement.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: But how much of what we`re seeing right now is based on setting the table for 2022 and making it so that anyone who loses an election on the Republican side who wants to, can say we need an audit or recount, or to throw out the votes altogether, and to -- and to win elections that you didn`t win on the back end?\par \par \uc2 MCGRAW: Think that`s certainly an element of it. You know, so much of this is jumping off of the demands that the former President has had. And I think too, Governor Greg Abbott in Texas, responding within hours to a demand by the former President to hold audits and for counties.\par \par \uc2 But you know, when I was in Iowa on Saturday, I talked to over a dozen Trump supporters there. I was asking them, you know, do they want Trump to run again in 2024? Who would they want his potential VP to be? What are the issues that are important to them?\par \par \uc2 The issues that were important to them are things that the Republican Party would probably rather Trump be talking about. Things like immigration, the economy, inflation, pocketbook issues.\par \par \uc2 But the other question that I asked all of these people was, do they believe that the election was stolen? And overwhelmingly, all of them believe that they said that they brought up the fact that they`re concerned about the integrity of our elections, that that`s something that they`re really worried about in elections going forward.\par \par \uc2 So, this isn`t something anymore that, you know, is a focus of the former President, the focus of the people around him, this is something that has become a mainstream belief for Republicans across the country, and I talked to some of them on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: It`s just going to be a huge problem for the next election, every subsequent election when that`s out there. And to that point, Kelly O., you know, I think the one thing from the Trump`s sound that we played at the beginning where he talks about, we need to change our laws, and how we conduct our elections.\par \par \uc2 Democrats agree with that, they just want to change them in a very different way than Republicans do. And when I talk to House Democrats, for example, they are at the tip of the spear here.\par \par \uc2 They want to see President Biden really get out there and push for changes in how we vote in this country to protect democracy.\par \par \uc2 Does the Biden White House understand the fervor there is on this issue on both sides and the demands from Democrats to protect small d democracy while they can?\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: It`s been raised many times with the President. They talk about it being a top priority. A core of his leadership is that he wants to have protections for voting rights to make certain that all Americans can exercise that right without fear of voter suppression or being knocked off the rolls for reasons that are unfair, being denied their franchise.\par \par \uc2 One of the challenges is that is a hard path right now in the Senate, and the President still does not believe in changing the filibuster, so that makes it difficult.\par \par \uc2 And of course, he`s tasked his Vice President, Kamala Harris, with work on voting rights, too. So, it is an ongoing tension point between the President and his own party over voting rights.\par \par \uc2 He agrees with them, but he is not using as much of his time talking about that issue publicly. And it`s been one of the criticisms Democrats and Progressives, in particular, have had of the President is using the time of the presidency and the bully pulpit on the issues that they think are most important.\par \par \uc2 Certainly, you can point to times and places where the President talks about these issues. But could he do more of that? Many Democrats say yes, he could, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Yes, many Democrats I talked to see it as kind of an asymmetrical war.\par \par \uc2 Yamiche, let me give you the last crack at this year. There are still some areas where we see elected Republicans go against Trump`s will.\par \par \uc2 You know, we talked -- we talked about the infrastructure vote, for example, in the context of Chuck Grassley, the debt limit vote last week was an interesting example of that.\par \par \uc2 A lot of our viewers don`t like to hear this, but Mitch McConnell has actually stood up to Trump on things like you know, blowing up the filibuster when Trump was in charge or on doing this debt limit vote against his opposition.\par \par \uc2 But the next Congress could be very, very different. Roy Blunt is out, you could have Eric Greitens in that seat, you`re going to have the likely to have more Trump allies taking over in the House as well.\par \par \uc2 What did things look like in the next Congress where you have even fewer institutionalists around to provide what minimal guardrails they were able to bring to our government?\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Well, it`s a great question, and it really goes to the heart of what the Republican Party is morphing into. There are still some Republicans who got their seats without having to sort of show their loyalty to former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 But as they start to Trump either retire out because they don`t want to deal with being in the minority or is it they`re chased out in the case of Representative Gonzales, someone that I`m sure you`re familiar with.\par \par \uc2 He says that he had to hire private security, that he had to try to protect his family against death threats and all sorts of threats because he voted to certify the election and won against President -- former President Trump`s wishes to try to take away the election that was won fair and square by President Biden.\par \par \uc2 So, you have Republicans who are facing increasing pressure to either get in line with President Trump or retire.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: And as that continues to happen, if President Trump continues to have this sort of power that he has in the GOP, you can imagine that the people who are going to be elected into Congress in next year and in two years after that, that they are going to continue to be people who are more in line with President Trump and more in line, frankly, with the lies that he`s telling the base.\par \par \uc2 I think the question now will be, will Republicans want to at all even try to at all step away from President Trump, if, let`s say in the -- in the next election, in the 2022 midterms, they don`t see the sort of shift that they want to see? Because let`s remember, it`s not just that President Trump lost the 2020 election, he also lost a House in 2018.\par \par \uc2 So, he`s really shown one time in 2016, that he had a lot of electoral power, so Republicans, who I think, have to also balance that reality.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Our first trend Tim Cook this morning called this a really significant problem that we`re not paying enough attention to. We`re going to stay on this, and I thank all of you for your reporting. Leigh Ann, Kelly, Yamiche, and Meridith, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, I`ll speak to a leading Democrat about navigating fights within the Democratic Party, even as the threat to democracy looms larger.\par \par \uc2 And up next, the latest threat -- twist rather in the legal back and forth over the Texas Law that effectively bans all abortions. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. The Biden administration has until tomorrow to respond to a Federal Appeals Court decision to temporarily reinstate Texas strict Abortion Law.\par \par \uc2 A three-judge panel on the Fifth Circuit Court of Appeals issued an administrative stay, late Friday night, putting the Law back in place until the full court can consider it.\par \par \uc2 That came just two days after a U.S. District Judge suspended the Law leading some abortion providers in Texas to resume conducting their procedures.\par \par \uc2 NBC Justice Correspondent Pete Williams joins me now. And so, Pete, what is that administrative stay? That isn`t a paperwork issue that has a legal meaning, right?\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, MSNBC JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: Sure. It basically is a -- is a ruling by an Appeals Court to block a Lower Court ruling without saying anything about who`s right or wrong in this fight.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: It basically sort of intended usually to preserve the status quo. Now, you could argue the status quo was putting the law on hold, I think in this case, the Texas argument is the status quo was we were able to enforce it. So, it`s -- it grants to stay, but it says nothing about who`s right or wrong.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: So what do we expect to see happen at this deadline?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: So, the Justice Department will file a brief saying why the Judge`s ruling should be upheld. This is on a -- an injunction, this is not the merits of the case, but you still get into the merits of the case because, in the Federal Courts, the way to win an injunction is to say you`re likely to win when it goes to trial. So, we`re still in the early stages here. The government will respond, and then the three-Judge panel will issue a ruling.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: So, this is going to take a long time to adjudicate. Is the onus on the state of Texas to say why they need this law in place while this goes to the courts? Or is the onus on the Justice Department to say, we think this law is so damaging to women`s health, you got to -- you got to set it aside while we fight this out?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: So, the real issue at the heart of the case is can a state do this?\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: The Justice Department`s argument here is that it`s unconstitutional. You can`t pass a law, the Justice Department says, that deprive someone of a constitutional right, and not give them a chance to challenge that in court.\par \par \uc2 What Texas says is that the Judge went way off the reservation. He said the government had the authority to intervene in the case when it really didn`t, that this is all about State`s rights. So, that`s the issue before the Court.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, it is a brand new issue because this law was you have to say with no matter how you come down on this issue, very cleverly devised to make it hard to do this.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: So, amateur legal scholars like, myself, are wondering when does the Supreme Court gets involved in something like this? I think a lot of people were surprised that they didn`t take up the opportunity to review this sooner.\par \par \uc2 Are they wait -- is the Supreme Court, would they rather just wait and deal with the Mississippi case and only have to touch this issue once? How do they view when and where and how to kind of get involved in a case like this?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I`m sure they would rather not have the Texas case because they`ve already got two abortion cases. One big abortion case, one abortion-related case. I`m sure they think this is the last thing we need.\par \par \uc2 And this is a novel question here, this whole area of states devising a law to have other people carry out enforcing the law.\par \par \uc2 What`s going to happen here is though, it -- whoever loses before the three-Judge panel has a choice either to go the full Fifth Circuit or to the Supreme Court, one way or another, this case is coming back to the Supreme Court either on the injunction or on the merits of the case itself.\par \par \uc2 So, I would -- I wouldn`t be surprised if it`s back before the Supreme Court one way or the other before the end of this term, whether they like it or not.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And we`re already seeing other Republican-control states try to draft similar legislation, so I think we`ll probably be talking about this more sooner rather than later here.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Which if the government loses before the Fifth Circuit would be an argument on why to take this case now. That wind will be at the Justice Department`s face because their basic -- there`s no split among the Circuits yet, it`s very early, and that`s the normal reason the Supreme Court would take a case. They usually like to let these things percolate in the lower courts before they jump in.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right, Pete Williams, thank you very much for breaking that down. I know we`ll talk about this a lot more soon.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Anytime.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Up next, I`ll talk to a leading Democrat about that Catch-22 we told you about last Friday. The party needs the President to be popular to pass his agenda, and they need to pass this agenda so that he can be more popular.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:31:56]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. With most of President Biden`s domestic agenda still in limbo, the divisions within the Democratic Party have been on full display for the last month. Negotiations on that package are continuing even as Congress begins a two-week recess, and they seem to maybe kind of possibly be closing in on a $2 trillion price tag.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is one of the key Democrats in the House, Kentucky Congressman John Yarmuth. He`s the chairman of the House Budget Committee.\par \par \uc2 And Congressman, if we stipulate that Democrats are close to agreement on that price tag, we know that the number`s got to come down. Right? And there seems to be a pretty significant disagreement on how to do it. Do you do a few things for longer? Do you do what Pramila Jayapal wants to do, kind of everything that`s in the package now but for a shorter period of time? Where do you come down on that?\par \par \uc2 REP. JOHN YARMUTH (D-KY): Well, we actually had a discussion about this last week among committee chairs, and I would say the committee chairs were pretty much split. I come down on the side of taking down several of the most critical priorities that we have, and concentrating on them. We can do another budget reconciliation package next year. So, for instance, and this is just hypothetically, the dental provision we are proposing to add to Medicare doesn`t go into effect until 2028 as it`s currently written.\par \par \uc2 So we could put that in a budget reconciliation package next year. So that`s what I would prefer to see. There are others who think we ought to do all of the things that we proposed in the larger Build Back Better Act, and do them for shorter period of time or else impose some limitations on who is eligible for the benefits. So -- but there`s no consensus on either of those things, and even if you agreed with me, there`s not total consensus on which the most important priorities are. So we`ve got a lot of work to do.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Is there consensus building around the idea of another big reconciliation package next year? I think so much talk has been about the idea that this package right now could be the last train leaving the station. But if Democrats think there`s time to do another big package, that changes the conversation significantly.\par \par \uc2 YARMUTH: Well, I think it does. And I think -- I don`t know that there`s a consensus on it. I`ve talked to a number of my colleagues who seem perfectly comfortable with doing one next year. It would be right in the middle of the midterm elections, but that might pose an advantage for us, actually, forcing Republicans to oppose some really popular initiatives. So it might be -- I think we should look forward to doing that.\par \par \uc2 With this environment where we have virtually no hope at all of getting bipartisanship, we have to look to every opportunity we have to make progress for the American people.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Politically speaking, is the size and scope of what you`re trying to do which has been obviously problematic in crafting it, is that a political benefit? Is this package as far as Democrats look at it, too big to fail? You know, you kind of have to do all of it because everybody needs some piece of it?\par \par \uc2 YARMUTH: Well, that`s a really good point, and that`s at the heart of some of our negotiations right now.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:03]\par \par \uc2 We know that there are millions of kids who would benefit from early childhood education. Millions of seniors who need homecare to get them -- have them live comfortably. Childcare is a critically important issue to so many of our families. Again, expanded Medicare benefits. Paid family medical leave is incredibly important. Extending the child tax credit which pulled so many children out of poverty this year, and could do it for a number of years to come.\par \par \uc2 These are incredibly important things. And they`re incredibly popular. The big problem we`re having now, and I think it was illuminated very well in a poll that was on MSNBC this morning, that only about between 25 percent and 30 percent of the American people actually know what`s in the proposal. They know the proposed cost, but they don`t know the elements.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right.\par \par \uc2 YARMUTH: Those elements are extremely popular.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Last week Leader Schumer said Congress was going to try to get both of these tracks done by the end of October. Obviously you have a deadline on the infrastructure bill to get that done by the end of October. How realistic do you think that timeline is? And do you see -- I mean, you mentioned that there`s not a consensus in the House. Is the bigger challenge getting the consensus on the House side or is the bigger challenge what they`re dealing with over on the Senate side to reaching those goals?\par \par \uc2 YARMUTH: Well, it seems to me that we have challenges on both sides. It seems to me the challenge is more with two members on the Senate side. You know, in my conversations with the various caucuses in the -- on the House side, and I`ve been trying to herd cats for about four months now, there`s very little disagreement on the overall objectives of what we`re trying to do.\par \par \uc2 Yes, there will be some concern about the overall price tag, but, again, you know, this is money that`s being spent over 10 years. And it`s all being offset by tax cuts -- tax increases, so, you know, I think there`s not a great deal of resistance on the House side to doing something soon and doing something significant. So I would say maybe the Senate side a little bit more than the House side.\par \par \uc2 But again we still have to resolve some differences. I don`t think they`re really differences that are strongly held. I just think people are looking for the best path forward.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: As you would expect. Let`s stay on the Senate side for a minute. Your fellow Kentuckian Mitch McConnell wrote a letter to the president on Friday in which he says, "Your lieutenant," speaking of the president on Capitol Hill, "The time they claimed they lacked to address the debt ceiling through standalone reconciliation and all the tools to do it. They cannot invent another crisis to ask for my help."\par \par \uc2 He`s talking about you there, I think, as one of the lieutenants here who could handle a debt ceiling lift through reconciliation. Is that the path we`re now on here? And would it be as easy as one Republican aide suggested to me last week that it could be an amendment vote in the vote-a-rama? Could be as simple as that for Democrats to handle the debt ceiling in reconciliation if you so choose?\par \par \uc2 YARMUTH: Well, first of all, I don`t think you can trust anything that any Republican says because they can`t control all 50 of their members. And it only takes one or two to object on the Senate side, as you know, Garrett, to foil any of the guarantees that they might make to us. One thing that I need to make -- and I get furious thinking about Mitch McConnell that he`s my senator, because he`s being so deceitful with the American people.\par \par \uc2 Right now, you know, he says, well, this is -- what they`re asking us to do is pay for future debt. Right now every minute we are incurring more debt because of the Republican passed tax cuts in 2017. CBO projected at the time that between 2022 and 2028 the Republican tax cuts would add $800 billion to the national debt. So that`s their debt. That`s not anything the Democrats had to do. We all voted against it. That`s their debt and now they don`t want to pay for it.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right. Congressman Yarmuth, proudly supporting his F-rating from the NRA on his lapel, we will leave it there with you for now.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a potential breakthrough in the treatment of COVID.\par \par \uc2 YARMUTH: Thanks, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Thank you, Congressman.\par \par \uc2 The anti-viral pill that experts say would be a game-changer and bring us one step closer to becoming widely available.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I`ll make more sense in the next segment.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:42:32]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. A big development today in the fight against COVID. Merck officially asked the FDA to authorize its oral treatment for COVID-19 this morning. The first step in getting an entirely new weapon on the table to fight the pandemic. If the FDA grants emergency use, Molnupiravir would be the first anti-viral pill to hit the market to treat COVID patients at home.\par \par \uc2 Clinical data showed the pill cut hospitalizations and deaths in half among unvaccinated, at-risk adults diagnosed with mild to moderate disease. The news comes as the FDA is set to consider authorizing booster shots of Moderna and \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  vaccines later this week. Plus Pfizer`s vaccine for children could get an FDA approval before Halloween.\par \par \uc2 Altogether, this means the U.S. may well be heading into winter with a whole new arsenal of tools to help keep the worst of the pandemic behind us.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is MSNBC medical contributor and founding director of Boston University`s Center for Emerging Infectious Diseases, Policy and Research, Dr. Nahid Bhadelia.\par \par \uc2 So, Bhadelia, I won`t ask you to say the name of the pill again, but I want to talk about this Merck news. How much of a game changer could a pill treatment be for ending the pandemic or at least cutting down on the deaths?\par \par \uc2 DR. NAHID BHADELIA, MSNBC MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, Garrett, you know, the whole part of dealing with this virus at this point now that we know that we are not going to be able to get eradicate it at least in the near future, is to tame it. And that taming means that even if you end up getting an infection, despite the vaccine, you know, despite all our efforts trying to reduce community transmission, that we take the things out of this disease and people don`t end up in the hospital.\par \par \uc2 And something by mouth is particularly important because, you know, even this weekend I had an immunocompromised friend who had -- who started developing symptoms, was positive, couldn`t find access to a place that was open to get monoclonal antibody infusion. Right? Long weekend. A pill makes a huge impact because it sort of improves people`s access to be able to protect themselves if they find out despite those layers of mitigation that they ended getting to -- you know, getting the infection, that they don`t end up in the hospital.\par \par \uc2 But here are a couple of things that might really impact whether or not it`s a game-changer or not. One, who is it going to be for? The study is for high-risk folks who wanted to see who benefitted, who`s failed therapy? Right? 50 percent still ended up in the hospital. Who were the 50 percent that still ended up in the hospital? Two, how much of a supply will this be? Merck has said government has bought about 1.7 million courses of this illness, just to give you a sense, or 100,000 infections per day, right?\par \par \uc2 [13:45:04]\par \par \uc2 And so that`s a lot. And Merck has said they might have enough for about 10 million people, and last, it needs to be linked to testing. We need to do earlier testing so people know if they`re positive so they can take the pill. So all of those will really decide how big of an impact they make.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: My understanding is that Merck only tested this pill on unvaccinated adults with at least one risk factor. So should we assume that that`s who it`s likely to be approved for, or what about vaccinated people who might experience a breakthrough infection?\par \par \uc2 BHADELIA: Yes. So vaccines reduce, you know, even in the age of Delta, they reduce chances of infections by five times. However, there are breakthrough infections in people who are high risk. I don`t see this just being approved for people who are vaccinated. It`s likely it will be approved for anybody who has confirmed COVID which is where, you know, which is where the data was taken from. It`s people who had a test that was positive. And that`s why that linking with tests will be important.\par \par \uc2 The other thing that`s going to be interesting is whether there will be further data either from this product from Merck and there are two other, you know, oral antivirals that are being looked at by Roche and \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 . If any of those may also be used as a post exposure, which means that you could give it to people after they`ve had a high-risk exposure so you don`t even have to sort of wait until the test is positive. And that might be another layer of how we make an impact. But we`ll have to wait to see what the data shows.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: The -- \uc2 Scott Gottlieb\uc2  who was the former FDA commissioner, \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  board member. was on CNBC earlier today. He was talking about the idea that this review could likely take longer than usual, a couple of months. Is that your understanding of what we`re looking at here, potentially an end of the year rollout for this pill?\par \par \uc2 BHADELIA: Well, we haven`t seen the data. You know? It`s hard to know. If the data holds up, the thing that holds promise for me is that we haven`t seen the data, but there was an independent safety and monitoring board that basically said look, you`ve got to stop the trial. The results seem to be very clear, and that gives some signal that an independent group has looked at the data but the data is not out yet.\par \par \uc2 And so I think it will depend on whether or not, A, the data holds out that FDA gets from Merck, what it looks like. But B, as you`ve seen, FDA has a lot on their plate. You just listed the number of meetings. And so you might see that some delays because of that. But I don`t see that it would be any different, for example, than the evaluation for monoclonal antibody if the data holds out. It may just be getting a thorough evaluation and getting it on the market.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: A lot of good news, a lot of new tools. Doctor Bhadelia, thank you.\par \par \uc2 BHADELIA: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And coming up, a serious spy craft story with a silly twist. A Navy engineer with a top-secret clearance allegedly trying to leak national security secrets in a peanut butter sandwich. Boy, is he in a jam?\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:51:27]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. Today is the International Day of the Girl. A day dedicated to empowering girls and lifting up the stories of women and girls around the world. The need to shine a light on the challenges girls face worldwide in achieving equal rights and equal opportunities is as urgent as ever especially as the Taliban`s takeover in Afghanistan threatens millions of girls` access to education.\par \par \uc2 Malala Yousafzai is one of the brave activists fighting for the future of Afghan women. She was shot by the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  in Pakistan for speaking out on her right to learn. But she survived and has gone on to become a leading activist for the education of girls and the youngest ever Nobel Peace Prize laureate. Malala sat down with my colleague Savannah Guthrie on the "TODAY" show this morning to share her message. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MALALA YOUSAFZAI. WOMEN`S RIGHTS ACTIVIST: The Taliban`s narrative that, you know, this is so-called Islamic thing that women cannot be equal. Women are challenging that. So this is something that gives me hope and that is why we need to listen to the voices of Afghan women and girls.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC NEWS HOST, TODAY SHOW: What`s your message to girls who want to make a difference in their own communities?\par \par \uc2 YOUSAFZAI: Believe in yourself. Believe in your voice. Believe in the dedication and determination that you hold for the things that you believe in. You can make it happen.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Malala`s nonprofit, the Malala Fund, has invested close to $2 million in Afghanistan for the education of girls.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:56:26]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. A mysterious package sent to another country, a peanut butter sandwich and a band-aid wrapper. Federal investigators say these were all parts of a Navy engineer`s effort to sell nuclear submarine secrets. That engineer and his wife were arrested Saturday in West Virginia. Details of the case against them were unsealed yesterday.\par \par \uc2 For nearly a year, investigators say, Jonathan and Diana Toebbe were selling information to who they thought was a foreign power. It was actually an undercover FBI agent.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Ken Dilanian joins me now.\par \par \uc2 So, Ken, what`s more intriguing about this case, the kind of broad strokes of it or the details of what happened here and how the \uc2 FBI\uc2  caught these folks?\par \par \uc2 KEN DILANIAN, NBC NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: So because of the way this unfolded, we have far more detail than we ordinarily do in this spy case because the \uc2 FBI\uc2  had control of this the whole time. This was essentially a sting operation. They were talking to this couple, or to Jonathan Toebbe, and he was laying out in extraordinary detail of the nature of his alleged crime. He even said that he`d been planning and secretly gathering this information for years.\par \par \uc2 He was very explicit that he wanted money. They paid him $100,000 in cryptocurrency and they induced him to do what`s called in the spy world a dead drop or multiple dead drops where he placed a memory card in one case inside a peanut butter sandwich. That was how he transmitted the information.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you can`t make this stuff up except it`s really deadly serious because these charges carry life in prison.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: This does seem like the kind of thing \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents in a breakroom would be like joking about. What`s the most ridiculous thing we could make them do the dead drop? Do we know anything about Toebbe and what his motivation might have been? The whole case is weird to me, the money involved and so -- yes.\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: He had these secrets because he was a Navy nuclear engineer. We don`t know a lot about his motivation other than he wanted money. I feel like there`s something missing here. His wife is a PhD in philosophy who teaches at a tony private school in Annapolis. They`re in their 40s. They have young children. Why were they providing this information to, we think, an allied country, not an adversary?\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: That`s interesting.\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: That`s how the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , we believe, got access to this in the first place. And there was a moment in the criminal complaint where the \uc2 FBI\uc2  was sending a signal at the embassy, presumably they wouldn`t be able to do that if it was the Chinese or the Russian embassy, in order to ensnare this couple. So I think a lot left to be known about it.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: So the potentially allied company was in on this sting operation, too, is what you`re inferring here?\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: That`s what it looks like. Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: What role did his wife play in all this? It`s interesting that they`re both arrested here.\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: So the \uc2 FBI\uc2  says in the criminal complaint that she acted as a lookout during these dead drops in these rural areas. And they say that he entrusted the details of this operation to her, they say that explicitly in this criminal complaint. So she`s on the hook for all these very serious charges.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Are we likely to learn anything more about this or is this the kind of case with the national security implications where we know about as much as we`re going to find out?\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: I suspect that that is how it`s going to unfold because if there is a plea agreement, which in a kind of case where the evidence is strong, you expect would happen, we`re not going to hear very many more details about this.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right. Well, we`re stuck with our peanut butter -- but no jelly. Our peanut butter sandwich version of the story.\par \par \uc2 All right, Ken Dilanian, thank you.\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And thank you for being with us in this hour. Chuck will be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. MSNBC coverage continues with my friend Geoff Bennett right now.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT, MSNBC HOST: It is good to be with you. I`m Geoff Bennett and this is the number two Republican in the House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WALLACE, FOX NEWS HOST: Do you think the 2020 election was stolen from Donald Trump?\par \par \uc2 REP. STEVE SCALISE (R-LA): Well, Chris, I`ve been very clear from the beginning. If you look at a number of states, they didn`t follow their state-passed laws that governed the election for president.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: So you think the election was stolen?\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020211012ehab00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION WITH \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2  for October 10, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Richard Lui \par \uc2 6,488 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 October 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [17:00:01]\par \par \uc2 YASMIN VOSSOUGHIAN, MSNBC HOST: But now, I`m going to turn it over to my friend, Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2 , and "POLITICSNATION."\par \par \uc2 REV. AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Good evening, and welcome to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s lead, what happens in Texas does not stay in Texas, because, right now, Republican politics in the Lone Star State have galvanized the resistance among nationwide Democrats.\par \par \uc2 The resumption of Texas` near-total abortion ban after a federal appeals court decision this weekend has underscored the reproductive jeopardy that women in the state face after last month`s Supreme Court indecision. And that has teed up another SCOTUS case, from Mississippi, where abortion rights are also under threat. And other red states are watching and hoping that they`ll be able to follow suit.\par \par \uc2 Of course, Republican legislators in those states are also looking to gerrymander their congressional districts and they can look again to Texas as the template, where GOP state lawmakers have unveiled a new congressional map designed to protect their pro-Trump incumbents by diluting the number of black and brown voters and pitting Democrats of color against each other. And against that backdrop, the midterms and 2024 loom, as we learned more today about just how far down the conspiratorial rabbit hole Trump and his Republican Party are willing to go in pursuit of power.\par \par \uc2 But we start tonight deep in the divided heart of Texas. Joining me now, Congressman Al Green and Congresswoman \uc2 Sheila Jackson Lee\uc2 , both Democrats of Texas.\par \par \uc2 Now, Congresswoman, let me go to you first. Many thanks to both of you for being here, but I want to ask you first, Congresswoman, specifically. I want to get both of your responses to the latest assault on democracy in your state.\par \par \uc2 This new Congressional map unveiled by your Republican-led state senate last week, as one expects, it adds to the number of Republican districts protecting GOP incumbents in counties that would have gone for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And even though Texas gained seats in Congress, it also subtracts from the number of districts where \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  would have won, heavily black and brown, heavily Democratic, and it effectively pits you both against each other by overlapping your districts.\par \par \uc2 While we were happy when you both agreed to come on tonight, Congresswoman \uc2 Jackson Lee\uc2 , I`ll start with you, Congressman Green, we`ll come you to, but I Congresswoman \uc2 Jackson Lee\uc2 , am I misreading this? Aren`t they looking like they`re trying to eliminate a district and try to pit two of our most high profile, highly effectively black Congress people against each other?\par \par \uc2 REP. SHEILA JACSON LEE (D-TX): Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2 , what a pleasure to be with you, and my colleague, Congressman Green. Thank you for giving us this national platform for a national story that is going to permeate redistricting across America. Let me first say how grateful we are that little Christopher was found, God is good, after four days of being lost.\par \par \uc2 But we are, in essence, the examples of what a brutal approach to redistricting can be. The redrawing of the 9th and the 18th with glee of putting two African-Americans of the same party in the same district that has never been done in my lifetime is amazingly wrong.\par \par \uc2 This is a Barbara Jordan`s district. My colleague`s district is 30 years old. The Barbara Jordan district, which she sweated and teared for, among others is 50 years old. And she became the first holder of a seat along with Andy Young to go to the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2  since reconstruction.\par \par \uc2 The drawing of these districts, Reverend, met no redistricting principles. It didn`t protect incumbents. It didn`t leave in place districts that had not disturbed anyone. It didn`t hold communities of interest. And it was racially gerrymandered.\par \par \uc2 It looks like a tool of racialness was taken to draw in this map. And to add insult to injury, when our state senator, Senator Boris Miles, tried to correct it with procedure, timeliness, and offered the amendment, and all his colleagues had to do is say, you`re right, this is wrong, it is racially gerrymandered, and it is excluding people of color out of their ability to elect a person of their choosing.\par \par \uc2 [17:00:14]\par \par \uc2 I`ll just finish with this point. The idea of these districts, the bloodshed on the Edmund Pettus Bridge by \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2 , and \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  and many others, was to ensure that everyone could have the right to vote for a person of their choosing, even though I represent people from all backgrounds, it was to allow that. It has now been dismantled and destroyed. And I ask my colleagues in the statehouse, they have seemed receptive, please fix this injustice. It is not fair and just. It is unjust and unfair.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Congressman Green, your turn. Do you that this is nothing but racial gerrymandering?\par \par \uc2 Congressman Green, I can`t hear you.\par \par \uc2 REP. AL GREEN (D-TX): Thank you, Reverend. I`m honored to be on your program, and I`m especially honored to be on with my colleague, \uc2 Jackson Lee\uc2 . I think that our appearing here together is evidence of the fact that we do not intend to allow this plan to pit us against each other to succeed.\par \par \uc2 It appears to me that this was done with some degree of malice aforethought. And my hope is that the people of our congressional districts, as well as the people across the length and breadth of this country, will understand, as you have indicated, that this is just the genesis of this, that there are many revelations that may come to us as we proceed with this redistricting across the country, and that we have to stand together. We have to unify ourselves to prevent this from becoming pervasive.\par \par \uc2 A bit more empirical evidence, I believe, is important. Texas is the only state, the only state to have two new congressional districts accorded it by virtue of the census. In fact, the population growth was about 16 percent in the state of Texas. The state of Texas had growth that was 95 percent among minority persons, 95 percent. Yet minority population, generally speaking, is not acquiring new congressional seats. They`re going to the Republican Party and they will be going to Anglos for the most part.\par \par \uc2 I believe that the international district I represent deserves better. And I say it deserves better because it`s being severed, Reverend, the international district, where we speak multiple languages, about 80 languages, the international district, where the ballots are printed in English, Spanish, Vietnamese and Chinese, it is being severed of separating the community of interest that was built over years with people working together. It is an exemplar of what diversity is all about and I do regret that this is happening.\par \par \uc2 Finally, again, Congresswoman \uc2 Jackson Lee\uc2  and I have talked and we have said and we would announce it publicly that we will not be running from the same congressional district so they can pit us against each other. We are going to protect the 18th Congressional District and the 9th Congressional District in the courts. We`re going to protect them if we have to negotiate more, if we have to protest a lot and if we have to litigate until we get to the Supreme Court of the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And let me say this, I want to go to other subjects, but I wanted to have both of you on and I want the people in the Texas senate to see these picture of you two together. We have seen the national impact as well as the constituency services and many issues that both of you fought for. And we want to see exactly what we see on this screen, both of you to remain, as those Congress people, until the voters in your district decide otherwise or you make other moves in your career.\par \par \uc2 So I want people to take a look at this picture. We`re not talking about consolidating it into one or having some fight that is orchestrated by those that want to reward people with some kind of Trump-sized districts, when -- as you just said, Congressman Green, when the growth has been more on the people of color in the state, yet how do you go from having more population, black and brown, but you`re going to lessen the amount of representation from that state of black and brown representatives who have done an effective job.\par \par \uc2 Let me go to you on this, Congresswoman \uc2 Jackson Lee\uc2 . My question now is about the six-week abortion ban. It`s now resumed after the court stated that federal appeals court`s decision going into this weekend.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:03]\par \par \uc2 I`m sure it is having an effect on your constituents. I wonder if you can tell us what you`re hearing from those constituents, specifically women of color. Because I read some numbers from The Houston Chronicle and the state`s health department, Hispanic Texans received 30 percent of abortion procedures in 2020 while making up nearly 40 percent of the state`s general population. Black Texans are 30 percent of the procedures while accounting for 13 percent of the population.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, is that same Chronicle piece that I read, that I read that more than 80 percent of women that requested abortions last year in your district, Harris County, Houston, identified as black, Hispanic, Asian, or native Americans? The disproportion is clear. What are women in your district telling you?\par \par \uc2 LEE: They`re telling me that they`re desperate. And I thank you so very much for bringing this up. And may I as well say that part of the tragedy of putting two members of Congress in the same district, Reverend, is to silence voices who have been outspoken and have stood by the most vulnerable groups in our community in spite of our unbelievable growth in population.\par \par \uc2 So, what we are seeing is this dastardly act that contravenes a woman`s right to choose with her faith leader, her family member, and her health provider, has now been extinguished in this state. Who is impacted? Poor women. And certainly poor women are in my congressional district, and certainly districts around our community and, yes, they are woman of color.\par \par \uc2 And let me tell you what else is sad. Women who are of better means are able to travel to other states. Oklahoma`s increase in number of abortions has exceeded one`s imagination. Other states are seeing women from the state of Texas with means are able to get to other states for an abortion. Poor women, women with children, women with children that they love and nourish, that they`re trying to make sure they can provide for, but they just cannot proceed, or there are other reasons, or, Reverend, it is because of incest or rape, because the Texas abortion law, the most strict in the nation, does not even allow for any exceptions for incest and rape. How cruel can you be?\par \par \uc2 But having seen the coat hanger time of life, when women had to go to back alleys and use -- and have procedures performed on them that may have caused the loss of their life or their inability to give birth in years to come, we`re now at that stage. And with this new appeals court decision, when just a moment, maybe 24 hours, the clinics opened up, and barely were able to help desperate women, and then they were shut down again by the Trump-appointed fifth circuit. And so we need the Supreme Court to do its job.\par \par \uc2 And let me just say this. Can you imagine, Reverend, a mother, a sister, a daughter, having a stalker, a vigilante go after you so they can get a bounty of $10,000? It reminds me of the horror days of slavery. And so I`ve introduced legislation to stop these vigilantes and make it a criminal act if you interfere with a woman`s right to choose and get health care and get a $10,000 bounty. But we are suffering in Texas and the Supreme Court must do its job now.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Congressman Green, let me give you the last question. I have black voters asking me more and more about how or if officially historically black colleges will benefit from the president`s infrastructure plan to the extent that he promised. I know that last month, some of your Congressional black caucus members were already threatening to withhold their support for the president`s overall infrastructure bill over HBCU funding concerns. And the $45 billion in addition to funding in the president`s initial reconciliation budget has gone down to $2 billion.\par \par \uc2 It`s a casualty of politics and still $2 billion in new funding, but it`s still a number I`ve had to explain on the street and on my daily radio show all week. Explain to us, give us your response, Congressman.\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Well, thank you very much again, Reverend. And allow me to say quickly, there are some places, with all due respect, that the long arm of the law should not go. And the long arm of the law has no place in a woman`s womb. The law ends where the woman`s body begins when it comes to this question. This is a question for women to decide along with their most trusted advisers.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:02]\par \par \uc2 I support this and I`m going to stand with the women of this country so that we can prevent the long arm of the law from intruding into places it should not go.\par \par \uc2 With reference to the HBCUs, Reverend, I went to \uc2 Florida A&M\uc2 , \uc2 Howard University\uc2 , Tuskegee Institute of Technology, and received my juris doctor degree from \uc2 Texas Southern University\uc2  School of Law at the time, now Thurgood Marshall School of Law. HBCU saw something in me that I did not see in myself. It gave me the opportunity and it allowed me to be tutored and nurtured to a certain extent by persons who truly cared about my destiny. I am grateful forever to the HBCUs for what they have done to give me the opportunities that I had and give me the opportunity now to serve in Congress.\par \par \uc2 I want to single out, if I may quickly, \uc2 Texas Southern University\uc2 , because Congressman \uc2 Hank Johnson\uc2  is a grad of the University`s School of Law, Senator Craig Washington in the statehouse and the United States congressman was a graduate of Thurgood Marshall School of Law, Mickey Leland, a graduate of Thurgood Marshall School of Law. So, these institutions serve us well.\par \par \uc2 Here is where I stand on this issue. I don`t think too much is enough for HBCUs because of the historic, invidious discrimination that has been perpetrated and perpetuated. HBCUs were born out of racism and bigotry. And as a result, they have not received the proper funding over the years that they richly deserved. And as a result of this, I stand for more money for the HBCUs. Yes, $2 billion for the HBCUs is a large amount of money. But when we look at the amount of suffering that has taken place, it is not enough. So I stand for more money for HBCU`s.\par \par \uc2 And, finally, I want you to know, Reverend, I cannot tell you how much I appreciate your having the two of us on this evening together. It means a lot to send this positive message to the masses, that we stand together, we will not be separated, we will not run against each other, we will not run out of the same Congressional district. And if we had preclearance, we wouldn`t be suffering this. The suffering is being perpetrated upon minorities.\par \par \uc2 But preclearance, the \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  voting rights act, the advancement act, were passed, Reverend. We wouldn`t have this. We`ve got to pass it and we`ve going to end the filibuster. I yield back to you, Reverend.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I think that, that is the exact point to end on. We must pass that voting rights act. This would have been pre-cleared had we had that \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  voting act in. And I certainly agree with you on HBCUs. I`m in Washington, D.C. tonight because I preached at Rankin Chapel for Dr. Richardson this morning, and \uc2 Howard University\uc2  produced us a vice president of the United States, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . Thank you both for being with us. Thank you both -- there was no resistance, in fact, you all were anxious to come on together to show this united front of fighting the real opposition. Thank you, Congresswoman \uc2 Sheila Jackson Lee\uc2 . Thank you, Congressman Al Green.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, discrimination and racism came first even within the most respected institutions in this country. I`ll explain how you can rise up together to douse the flames of bigotry.\par \par \uc2 Plus, more damning information about Trump`s disgraceful inaction during the January 6th insurrection. I`ll discuss with my panel.\par \par \uc2 But, first, my colleague, Richard Lui, with today`s other top news stories. Richard?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LUI, MSNBC HOST: Rev, a very good Sunday to you.\par \par \uc2 Some of the stories that we`re watching for you this hour, authorities say no charges will be filed against the passenger whose behavior prompted an emergency landing at La Guardia Airport Saturday. American Airlines Flight 4817 stopped on the tarmac yesterday as emergency crews restrained the passenger who witnesses say had been acting erratically. Investigators say there was no threat to the aircraft.\par \par \uc2 One person is dead and 14 injured after a shooting overnight at a bar in St. Paul, Minnesota. Three male suspects are in custody. Around midnight, police received several 911 calls about gunshots at the Seventh Street Truck Park Bar. One victim was pronounced dead at the scene.\par \par \uc2 And a crash involving a Russian plane carrying skydivers has left at least 19 people dead and three injured, according to Reuters and Interfact sources. It happened in a region where cosmonauts actually train. The twin- engine plane came down after the pilots reported one of the engines had failed.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION: with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:20:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: For today`s Rise Up, I want to talk about discrimination and its unfortunate ability to lurk in plain sight, even within to some of the most esteemed institutions in this country. For instance, inside the New York City Fire Department which recently handed down its biggest wave of suspensions following a scandal that uncovered vile, racist messages being circulated among some of its white firefighters. And it`s not just New York. Fire departments across the country have struggled with racism, sexism and other discrimination for years, usually behind the firehouse doors, where female firefighters and firefighters of color are left to deal with the fallout.\par \par \uc2 The culture of discrimination is so bad in some places that people are hesitant to call 911 in an emergency.\par \par \uc2 [17:25:00]\par \par \uc2 People of color have long known that calling 911 might bring police brutality instead of assistance. And widespread reports of racism and sexism from fire departments doesn`t restore confidence in emergency responders.\par \par \uc2 Nationwide, nearly 80 percent of firefighters are white, far higher than the share of white people in the general population, meaning that people of color are underrepresented across the board. And the gender split is even worse, with women, who make up half the population, accounting for just 4 percent of firefighters in the United States.\par \par \uc2 Fire departments must do better. Firefighters are some of the most courageous people in the world, with one of the most dangerous and important jobs in the world. Firefighters of color and female firefighters should not have to worry about bigotry and discrimination while facing down the flames.\par \par \uc2 This is a nationwide problem. But its solution must be local. Demand your local fire department institute zero tolerance policies when it comes to discrimination. And find out what they`re doing to make sure the firefighters in your community reflect the spirit and values of that community.\par \par \uc2 Firefighters are among the bravest and the best of us. So we must honor their work by rising up together to douse the flames of bigotry.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:30:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION. I have a lot to get to with my political panel. Joining me now is Sarah Longwell, Director of Republicans for the Rule of Law and Publisher for The Bulwark, and Kurt Bardella, Adviser to the DCCC and former Spokesperson for House Oversight Committee.\par \par \uc2 Let`s start with the disgrace former president, yet another book is coming out to shed light -- some light on his behavior, this one from Reporter \uc2 Jonathan Karl\uc2 . Perhaps most damning is a revelation that Trump boasted about the size of the crowd at the insurrection on January 6th, even as the rioters were committing violence in the \uc2 Capitol\uc2  and the strangest tidbit involved the former president believing and absurd in baseless conspiracy theory about wireless thermostats manipulating Georgia voting machines.\par \par \uc2 Sarah, what is your reaction to these allegations? And how can the Republican Party keep this man as its standard bearer?\par \par \uc2 SARAH LONGWELL, PUBLISHER, THE BULWARK: Well, they shouldn`t. They should have gotten rid of him a long time ago. But the real problem is that there`s a new poll showing that 44 percent of Republicans still want \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to be the President and run again in 2024. And that`s why it`s so difficult to get rid of him.\par \par \uc2 But it is gob-smacking. I mean, we knew all of these. We know that he thinks January 6th was no big deal. Recently, he came out and he said that the real insurrection was actually the election on November 3rd. And so, you know- -- and he is the reason right. He`s such a conspiracy theorist. And he`s the reason now that there`s so much of this conspiracy nonsense really kind of taking over the party in creating this toxic environment and the GOP where so much of what they say is just -- you know, it`s built on a lie.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You know, meanwhile in the House, even as the former president is fuming over the Biden administration`s refusal to keep its record secret, the January 6th committee released a letter promising to, quote, swiftly consider advancing a criminal contempt of Congress referral for those people defying subpoenas. Kurt, does that mean we can expect those referrals this week? Why didn`t the committee immediately issued such referrals when the deadline to present evidence and come in for testimony had already passed?\par \par \uc2 KURT BARDELLA, ADVISOR, DCCC: Yes, Rev. I think it`s been very clear from the Trump, Bannon posture that their entire intention this entire time is to not comply with the January 6th committee to try to obstruct its proceedings and to try to hide behind Republicans and hope that they can run the clock out on the committee`s work. And Democrats cannot let this happen.\par \par \uc2 I mean we are seeing, reference to \uc2 Jonathan Karl\uc2 , like what we have heard from the Woodward/Costa, example after example of how \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was more than just a conspiracy theorist, he`s the ring leader. He`s the reason why January 6th happened.\par \par \uc2 And we need to use every tool at our disposal, every legal tool, whether it`s putting people in jail holding them in inherent contempt of Congress, every single possible mechanism we have to get to the truth of January 6th we need to used. Because if we aren`t willing to use and deploy those resources to get to the bottom of a domestic terrorist attack, I don`t know really what we are doing here, Rev. because all it does is send a signal to Republicans, to be polite, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his enablers and his accomplices, that they are not willing to do enough to hold us accountable so they`re going to try to do it again.\par \par \uc2 January 6th was a dress rehearsal. And the more that we`ve seen from other Republican acolytes, like the governor of Texas, Gregg Abbott, like \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  in Florida, like Youngkin wants to be in Virginia, they want to try to continue the work that they tried to start on January 6th and do it all across this country. And we can`t allow them to get away with it.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, let me raise this, since this capitulation on the debt ceiling vote last week. Minority Leader Mitch McConnell has been facing anger from his own party led by the former president, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  himself.\par \par \uc2 [17:35:07]\par \par \uc2 And this is in apparent attempt to save face. He sent a letter to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  complaining about Majority leader, Chuck Schumer, for identifying Republicans as the culprit in holding the economy hostage over the debt ceiling. McConnell promised in his letter that Republicans would have struck the process again in December.\par \par \uc2 But, Sarah, didn`t we all assume that was going to happen anyway? If anything, doesn`t this early confrontation that Democrats will have to do this alone strengthen their position within their own party?\par \par \uc2 LONGWELL: Maybe. I`m not sure about that. I mean -- look, I think that Mitch`s histrionics are ridiculous and that they were absolutely being obstructionists, but 11 Republicans did ultimately voted to raise the debt ceiling, as they should have. It was the responsible move. And I think that Schumer, it wasn`t a great look for him to turn around and attack those Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Like what I wish that he had done is thank the Republicans who worked with him, who voted to raise the debt ceiling, who did the responsible thing. Because, you know, I`m so concerned about how toxic the relationship is between Republicans and Democrats in Congress when we`re facing down these incredibly serious issues like whether or not we`re going to default on our debt.\par \par \uc2 And so they need Republicans they can work with. And so when Republicans do work with them, I feel like attacking them afterwards is not the most beneficial strategy for a good working relationship going forward.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. It`s not an election year for most states, but Virginia does have the odd year statewide elections. And the commonwealth is less than a month away from electing a new governor. A recent polling in a governor race shows the gap narrowing between Democrat \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  and Republican Glenn Youngkin. Kurt, what are the stakes in this race and do you think it deserves more national attention than we are now seeing focused at this point?\par \par \uc2 BARDELLA: 100 percent, Rev. I mean, listen, I cannot underscore how important and impactful this race is going to be to what happens going forward. The last thing we need right now in this country is for Republicans, like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Glenn Youngkin, to actually believe that their path is the way to get back to electoral success.\par \par \uc2 If we lose Virginia, if \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  is not successful, we will have the combination of going backwards on our efforts to try to mitigate COVID. The economy will then take a huge tumble. Republicans will get the message that the incentive that their anti-science, ant-democratic, anti-woman platform is a winnable platform for them.\par \par \uc2 This is a high stakes game that we`re playing right now. And we don`t need Virginia to try to turn into a mirror of Texas or Florida, and have the extreme right-wing Republican agenda hijack this state and take us backward.\par \par \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  demonstrated during his first tenure as governor what success can look like for a state like Virginia, how you can grow the economy, how can you create innovation and new tech and address climate and still preserve fundamental constitutional rights.\par \par \uc2 And I tell you, if the rest of the state wants to see your rights get revoked, if you`re a woman living in Virginia and you want to see what happened in Texas happen here, sit this one out. This is an all hands on deck situation. The national media needs to tune in to this. This is far more impactful and important, frankly, than the circus we just saw back in California with the recall. We need all eyes and ears on the race right now. It is an incredibly important bellwether for what`s going to happen going forward politically.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Absolutely. Lastly, the question of abortion, the right to choose has become a judicial and political football with courts going back and forth on the issue several times in recent weeks at that. And the conservative control of the Supreme Court preparing to hear a case that experts say could lead to the end of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Sarah, the vast majority of Americans think abortion should be legal in some or all circumstances. And they have sensed the polling on that, that question began, they`ve been saying this all along. If the Supreme Court ends Roe v. Wade, how do we approach a court that is so out of step with so many Americans?\par \par \uc2 LONGWELL: Yes. Look, if they overturn Roe v. Wade, this is going to be one of those issues where Republicans are going to feel like the dog that caught the car, because this will actually -- I mean, electorally speaking, this is one of those things that could really backfire against them.\par \par \uc2 You know I do focus groups with swing voters all the time, and the fact is this is one of those wedge issues within the Republican Party itself. Yes, there`s a socially conservative base that would love to see Roe v. Wade overturned.\par \par \uc2 [17:40:01]\par \par \uc2 But there`s a whole other part of the party, the moderates, the people who aren`t socially conservative, the economic conservatives, for whom especially the college-educated suburban women which Republicans are sort of desperate to win back after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  drove them away, this is one of those issues that really alienates them. Because while they favor some restrictions on abortions, they`re mostly would consider themselves pro- choice.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. Sarah and Kurt, thank you both very much for being with us tonight as our political panel.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, school boards across the country are facing confrontations and threats of violence over a variety of issue. I`ll have the president of the American Teachers Association with me to discuss mitigation strategies, that`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: The Department of Justice announced that for 30 days they will be meeting with law enforcement leaders on both the federal and the local levels to discuss the rise in harassment and threats of violence toward school board and teachers, from anti-mask protest to hysterics about misunderstanding what it means for students to be thought critical race theory. It appears the adults are acting more like children than the children are acting.\par \par \uc2 Now, taking measures into their own hands, the DOJ also will produce specialized training and guidance for local school staff so they now know how to respond to the -- or report all of these threats and counter them.\par \par \uc2 [17:45:16]\par \par \uc2 But will that be enough to keep our school safe?\par \par \uc2 Joining me is the president of the American Federation of Teachers, \uc2 Randi Weingarten\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Randi, the DOJ announced this week that they would be looking into threats directed towards school boards and teachers all across the country. The task force will look into how they`ll use federal tools to help prosecute criminal activity leveled at school personnel. The statement went on to say that they hope to provide training to state governments and teachers to guide school staff on how to preserve evidence and report issues when they occur.\par \par \uc2 Now, as someone who helps advocate for teachers, is this a step in the right direction to give school staffs the protection that they really need?\par \par \uc2 RANDI WEINGARTEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT, AMERICAN FEDERATION OF TEACHERS: Yes, of course. And, you know, what`s interesting, Rev, is that every time there`s an issue that has affected more than one district, one state, we ask the federal government to step in, to help with guidance and help with training because it has more resources, because Secretary Cardona, and the (INAUDIBLE) department, \uc2 HHS\uc2 , the Justice Department, can help create some guidance in how to deal with issues.\par \par \uc2 And when we had school shootings, in fact, 55 school shootings in the month of September in schools, a number that is way out of bounds, it shouldn`t happen in terms of COVID. It happened before in so many different ways we were trying to create some basic public health guidelines for schools across and communities throughout the country. So it`s kind a shocking for the Republicans to find a way to undermine the health and safety of all. Criticism of school board policies is fine but violence is not.\par \par \uc2 And let me just say, as a social studies teacher, one more thing, which is we are about the freedom of everyone. And to have the freedom to think and the freedom to speak, you need to actually know how to critically think, and that`s what we do in schools. So to threaten us about that is just plain wrong.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Senate Minority Leader Mitch McConnell responded to the DOJ`s announcement with a letter to Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  stating that he was in favor of parents telling their school boards what to teach and that those interactions are the basis of representative government. I think constructive conversations between schools and parents are one thing, threatening violence is another.\par \par \uc2 WEINGARTEN: Right.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I mean, is he missing the point here?\par \par \uc2 WEINGARTEN: He totally missed the point in the letter. Of course, constructive conversation is the right thing. Look, and you I, how many times have we been on the street doing constructive protests, fighting for things that we think weren`t right? But there is a huge line between constructive conversation, between demonstrations and the incitement or the engagement in violence. And the issue right here is the incitement and the engagement in violence. And, frankly, (INAUDIBLE) should want to take the side of no violence, no hate, no violence.\par \par \uc2 What we need to do is we need to engage kids. We need (INAUDIBLE). We need to engage them in productive conversations. But the first thing is you`ve going to keep them safe. And that`s part of the reason that these school boards have had masking policies, vaccination policies. And then don`t get me started on the whole issue of, we have to teach kids about our history. We have to teach kids about --\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Well, let me go right there with you, because --\par \par \uc2 WEINGARTEN: Sorry.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: No, but let me go right where you`re going. Many of the attacks against school boards are about so-called critical race theory. Even though many of the supposedly concerned parents and citizens, they even struggle to explain what that term means. What do you think is really lurking behind this right-wing effort to suppress discussion of race in our classroom?\par \par \uc2 WEINGARTEN: So, look, I just read -- this is in The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  this weekend. This is a superintendent who just talked about this in such graphic ways, which is, in the aftermath of George Floyd`s murder, we had and have to have a real reckoning of what is happening in terms of race in America and what`s happening now in terms of ensuring that everyone has a safe and welcome place to be, in school and in the street.\par \par \uc2 [17:50:12]\par \par \uc2 And what we`re seeing in this kind of made-up -- these are hard conversations, but you have to have them in order to have kids have a more perfect union, and that`s what is really going on right now.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And when you are facing a critical situation, you need to deal with it in a way that makes it clear what we are talking about so that you can get past it. You cannot have healing without dealing with what is the ailment or the problem.\par \par \uc2 WEINGARTEN: Exactly. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: \uc2 Randi Weingarten\uc2 , thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Up next, my final thoughts. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:55:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: As I stated, I have am in Washington, D.C. this -- today where this morning I did the sermon at Rankin Chapel at \uc2 Howard University\uc2  for Dean Richardson, and my challenge to them is my challenge to you. Don`t miss this moment.\par \par \uc2 Yes, we are facing very serious things, infrastructure, whether we are going to do the larger human infrastructure, what are we going to do about getting voting rights back on the front burner and bring in a vote that will secure voting rights for people? How do we bring sides back to the table and get a real passing of police reform with the George Floyd voting rights bill?\par \par \uc2 How do we stop this gerrymandering that`s going on? We cannot say that we`re so overwhelmed it looks like things are going all wrong. We cannot have political pity parties. It is time for you to say I am not going to miss my moment to stand up like others have stood up in the past. Every time we made progress, it was because people took times like this to show their strength, their determination that they will fight back and not let people bring us backwards. Don`t miss your moment. Stand up. Fight back.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:00:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That does it for me. Thanks for watching. I`ll see you back here next weekend at 5:00 P.M. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020211011ehaa00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION for October 9, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Juanita Tolliver, Susan del Percio, Richard Lui \par \uc2 6,312 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 October 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 YASMIN VOSSOUGHIAN, MSNBC HOST: I`m Yasmin Vossoughian. I`ll be back tomorrow, 3:00 p.m. Eastern. Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2 , "POLITICSNATION" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 [17:00:07]\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Good evening and welcome to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s lead: Democracy versus despotism. Right now, our political life is riven by two factions that defy easy category. On the one side, you have the most Democrats committed to the rule of law and at least participating in the business of governing; on the other, you have Republicans still beholden to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and a few Democrats beholden to their own agenda. I`ll get to those Democrats shortly.\par \par \uc2 Because Trump`s former presidential aides have yet to comply with congressional subpoenas related to the events of January 6, because our former President literally told them not to. President Biden, of course, isn`t having it, declining his predecessor`s executive privilege as we got yet another damning report of just how far Trump tried to go to stay in office.\par \par \uc2 But even out of office, the Trump stamp on the courts endures with a Federal Appeals Court last night removing the stay on the Texas near total ban on abortion after our highest court chose not to get involved. We`ll have the latest out of Texas tonight.\par \par \uc2 And while negotiations of every sort continue on the Hill, those of us in the Civil Rights community are wondering about the future of police reform, as cities do what they can, and the G.O.P. legislators do what police unions want them to do. I`ll talk to black America`s Attorney General, \uc2 Ben Crump\uc2  later in the show.\par \par \uc2 But first, let`s bring in my political panel early. Juanita Tolliver is a Democratic strategist, Susan del Percio is a Republican strategist, both are MSNBC political analysts.\par \par \uc2 Let me start with going to the question of the infrastructure legislation, a massive political project that will affect all Americans if Democrats can get it done. Recent polling shows 62 percent of Americans support the smaller roads and bridges bill, and 57 percent support the larger social infrastructure bill.\par \par \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  of West Virginia and Kyrsten Sinema of Arizona are single handedly holding up the process. Even as the bill -- both bills have high approval in their states. Juanita, does this thing get done? What`s really behind Manchin and Sinema`s obstructionism?\par \par \uc2 JUANITA TOLLIVER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Look, Rev, that`s the same question that I think a lot of us have been asking, and I appreciate the way Representative \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2  puts it when she says, "Who is your priority? And what are you doing to make sure they get what they need?"\par \par \uc2 Because it`s clear that every time Manchin and Sinema make a demand, they are not prioritizing Americans in need, the most vulnerable communities across this country. And as you showed in the polls, the people who want and support all of these provisions, instead Manchin and Sinema are going to Biden and saying, hey, we`ve got to cut some of this stuff.\par \par \uc2 And when they do that, with every provision that is either sunset earlier or cut entirely, they are saying that no, mothers do not need paid family leave. No, children don`t deserve pre-K. No, our elders don`t deserve quality elder care or vision and hearing care as part of Medicare, right? Like they are creating these issues within the caucus.\par \par \uc2 But I do have hope that it will be resolved soon, Rev. But I also want to appreciate what you mentioned about Republicans sitting on the sidelines throughout all of this. They have essentially obstructed or filibustered any other substantive legislation in Congress right now, and so, what we are seeing is also a result of their inaction.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes, and what continues to bother me is that they are talking more about the figures than the services and what are being paid for. And even if you want to bring the figures down, tell us how the services will still be done.\par \par \uc2 But now let`s talk about the debt ceiling fight, and the figures around -- it revealed within the Republican Party, Minority Leader Mitch McConnell caved and allowed a vote to raise the debt ceiling until December and was immediately attacked by the former President and his allies, even though this vote was to pay the bills racked up primarily under his leadership, during the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 Trump is still the most influential person in the Republican Party. He is holding a rally in Iowa tonight. Susan, can McConnell continue to thread this needle? Or is it Trump`s way or the highway?\par \par \uc2 [17:05:16]\par \par \uc2 SUSAN DEL PERCIO, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I do not think that \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  acted because of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in any measure since \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has left office, frankly. But what`s interesting here is -- now, let me first say, I wish the Democrats had a governing partner, they do not.\par \par \uc2 The Republicans have no interest of stepping up and trying to govern with this President or with their Democratic counterparts. They are sitting on their hands and it is despicable how close we got to the debt ceiling coming -- going literally off a cliff.\par \par \uc2 That being said, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , the self-proclaimed Grim Reaper does not care, nor can he be shamed. He also wrote a book called "The Long Game," which is what I think this December 3rd, I think date is about to delay the debt ceiling until then, and the reason why I believe he did it, again, he is only acting with political will, Rev -- is that \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  thought, it looked like a potential to blame the Republicans because there wasn`t enough time on the calendar because people woke up to the debt ceiling fight late, even though \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  said he wasn`t doing anything three months ago.\par \par \uc2 However, now, he is given warning, and now he is saying, I will not do a thing. Is it right? No, but it`s a fact, and he is hoping that the Democrats do not pass infrastructure before then, do not pass a social service agenda then. And then everything comes together on December 3rd, and is hoping for a mess.\par \par \uc2 The best thing the Democrats can do to fight \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  right now is to pass the hard infrastructure, give \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  a much needed win, so he can go out and sell the second half of the social infrastructure, if you will, the human infrastructure and come up with a deal with Manchin, a $2 trillion and a carve out for voting rights.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, let`s move to January 6 insurrection and the subpoenas sent out by the committee that have so far been ignored. The disgraced former President is trying to claim executive privilege over White House documents, and even the testimony of people like \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 , who wasn`t a White House employee at the time.\par \par \uc2 But Trump is not -- is no longer the executive, and the Supreme Court has historically limited that privilege when it comes to former Presidents, reasoning that it should be invoked only for the benefit of the Republic. Now, Juanita, Trump and his enablers have a long history of using flimsy legal arguments to drag out legal proceedings and avoid accountability. What can Democrats do to stop this?\par \par \uc2 TOLLIVER: Look, I`m glad that the White House came out full force and essentially told the archives to do what they`ve got to do. We know this is likely going to go to the courts, and so what I`m looking to the Select Committee to do now is to start to enforce those contempt charges against uncooperative witnesses to compel their testimony because as Bannon signaled, he is following Trump`s lead.\par \par \uc2 I think we can expect other Trump loyalists to do the same, and that`s why they need to make good on what Chairman Thompson said was on the table, these criminal contempt charges or civil contempt charges which the D.O.J. could enforce.\par \par \uc2 And so it`s important that they set a standard here and a tone here that not only communicate their seriousness to witnesses and future people they would subpoena, but also to voters that they are using every tool available to them to get to the truth, to make sure that they hold Trump and whoever else is accountable, as well as to ensure that this never happens again, Rev.\par \par \uc2 It is critical that they deliver on that mandate when 60 percent of the public recognizes what happened on January 6th as a terrorist attack, because no one wants to see this again, especially when we know that the GOP laid out this slow coup approach, right, like all of this points to a bigger mission that sadly likely did not end on January 6th, and that could again, come to harm future elections.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now finally, a new report out this week from the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  revealed that even before the day of the insurrection, the disgraced former President spent weeks planning for his attempted coup d`etat. On at least nine separate occasions, Trump tried to pressure the Justice Department into investigating baseless fraud claims in support of his big lie.\par \par \uc2 Susan, \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  has a lot on his plate, but should he be prioritizing this weaponization of the D.O.J.? How worried should we be get the current D.O.J. personnel were involved in this scheme?\par \par \uc2 [17:10:13]\par \par \uc2 DEL PERCIO: Oh, we should be very worried as a country, Rev, that`s not a Republican or Democrat issue.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did so much harm to the D.O.J., not just internally what he expected of them at the end of his administration, but just the way he tried to knock them and the conspiracy theories et cetera.\par \par \uc2 It is a critical that \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  set up new directives that are approved by the President and if necessary, voted on by Congress. D.O.J. needs to go back as a place where it`s safe for all Americans to go and to know that they are taking care of all of us and that it is not a political arm of the White House.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, I`m going to have to leave it there. Thank you both, Juanita Tolliver and Susan del Percio.\par \par \uc2 Now to a major new development in the fight over reproductive rights. Last night, a U.S. Appeals Court reinstated the controversial Texas law representing a near ban on all abortions after a lower court blocked it from being enacted just two days earlier.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, a case involving another abortion law out of Mississippi with major implications for Roe versus Wade will soon be heard by the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is podcast host of "Boom! Lawyered" and senior editor of law and policy for Rewire News Group, Imani Gandy.\par \par \uc2 Imani, there`s been so much back and forth between the courts with the Texas anti-abortion law. Can you explain exactly what has happened and where things go from here?\par \par \uc2 IMANI GANDY, PODCAST HOST OF "BOOM! LAWYERED" AND SENIOR EDITOR OF LAW AND POLICY FOR REWIRE NEWS GROUP: Well, what has happened is that Texas has essentially decided to act like a mob boss. They`ve enacted this law that confers standing to anyone in the country to sue providers, to sue abortion funds, to sue anyone in the Access Pipeline. And by doing that, they have sort of shoved off their own duty to enforce this law.\par \par \uc2 They did this so that they could evade an injunction. They did this so that they could prevent people who would be affected by this law, people who would be sued under this law from seeking a remedy in court.\par \par \uc2 And so what the District Court in this case did on Thursday, I believe it was, is say, wait a minute, this is not proper. In the case that the United States has brought against Texas, the United States has said that Texas is in quote, "open defiance of the law." They`ve essentially created a law specifically to skirt the regular judicial process to skirt the litigation process and to prevent people from accessing a constitutional right.\par \par \uc2 The District Court said this is not going to stand. In 113 pages, the court offered many different reasons. But I think the biggest one is that somebody has to be able to sue Texas to block this law. If not to block it, to actually have a court figure out what people`s rights are, whether the law is constitutional, whether the enforcement mechanism is constitutional or proper, and that`s what the Federal District Court did.\par \par \uc2 The Fifth Circuit expectedly yesterday issued a temporary stay. So it is basically a temporary pause on the District Court`s order to give the Fifth Circuit time to more fully develop its arguments, its claims, and then they`re going to issue a fuller order probably this week, I suspect.\par \par \uc2 So you know, the Fifth Circuit basically stepped in yet again, and took over, yet again, the work of what the District Court did and said, wait a minute, we`re going to stop this, we`re going to let this law go into effect.\par \par \uc2 And I really cannot stress enough how truly just shocking it is to me that the Supreme Court let this law go into effect. Under the court`s current precedent, six-week abortion bans are patently unconstitutional. There is no Federal Appeals Court that has upheld one of these laws.\par \par \uc2 So what the district court did --\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes, but -- and I`m clear on it being unconstitutional. But I want to go back to the ruling earlier this week that briefly blocked the Texas law from going into effect. The Federal Judge in that decision put out 100-page ruling with some important legal insights.\par \par \uc2 I understand you read that ruling yourself.\par \par \uc2 GANDY: I did.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: What was the biggest takeaways from it?\par \par \uc2 GANDY: The biggest take-away from it is that under the court current precedent, this law is unconstitutional. While this new enforcement mechanism is novel and no court has really had a chance to address it, the very fact that the underlying law is unconstitutional should have impelled the Supreme Court to block it.\par \par \uc2 So, the Federal District Court kind of, you know, to kind of -- to use a colloquial phrase, threw shade at the Supreme Court by saying, what are you doing? You know, this is your own precedent.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:11]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And if the Judge said -- if the court said that it was unconstitutional, it would seem to me as a layperson, that that would have required the Supreme Court to step in if we are talking about Constitution, and I don`t see how they play any other role in this matter if it is raised to that level, as well as the Supreme Court has agreed to hear several other high-profile cases this term including a Mississippi abortion ban after 15 weeks of pregnancy, a massive departure from Roe versus Wade, which currently sets the limit for abortions at around 24 weeks.\par \par \uc2 How likely do you think this roll back on women`s rights will happen?\par \par \uc2 GANDY: It`s going to happen I believe, next year, next June after the Court hears oral arguments in this Mississippi case, Dobbs vs. Jackson Women`s Health. They`re hearing oral arguments in December. It`s a huge case. In these huge cases, those rulings come down usually at the end of the term. That`s going to be next June.\par \par \uc2 This is the case where they`re going to have to show their work, they`re going to have to explain to the American people why in this other Texas case involving an equally unconstitutional ban, the court decided to just throw up its hands.\par \par \uc2 I mean, there is no other higher court in the land, as you said, if it`s not the Supreme Court, who is going to address this and look at this and say, wait a minute, this is either constitutional or not? And here`s why. Then I don`t know who else is supposed to do it.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Well is very, very frightening. Well, thank you Imani Gandy.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next on -- coming up soon on POLITICSNATION, as police reforms remains stalled in Capitol Hill, police unions are pushing back on efforts to end qualified immunity, which has blocked true justice in so many brutality cases. We will bring you the latest.\par \par \uc2 Plus more than a few U.S. senators seem to have forgotten who they work for. So, I`ll take a look through time to remind them.\par \par \uc2 But first, but colleague, Richard Lui with today`s top news stories -- Richard.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LUI, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Rev, a very good Saturday to you. We`re going to start with some breaking news.\par \par \uc2 Just hours ago, an \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  plane had an emergency landing at New York`s LaGuardia Airport. The New York Port Authority saying that fliers reported suspicious behavior from another passenger. Here are passengers as they deplaned on the tarmac.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We think -- well, it is not a medical emergency at all. Not a medical emergency. Now, where are we walking, sir?\par \par \uc2 That wasn`t no medical emergency.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LUI: There`s the plane you see there, Flight 4817 from Indianapolis to New York. It did not have mechanical issues and there were no reported injuries amongst the 76 passengers and four crew members.\par \par \uc2 More details next hour as we learn more details on this.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. now has over 44 million COVID cases, the death toll nears 717,000. This comes as the C.D.C. now estimates approximately 140,000 children in the U.S. lost their caretakers due to COVID-19.\par \par \uc2 The Spanish island of La Palma still under a state of emergency as volcanic activity continues there. Since the initial eruption on September 19th, upwards of 800 buildings have been destroyed, 6,000 people have evacuated the area while lava and ash continue to pummel that region.\par \par \uc2 And investigators continue to look into what caused the oil spill off of the coast of Southern California. The Coast Guard stated that an underwater pipeline off of Huntington Beach shows signs of damage, but the exact cause of the spill is under inspection.\par \par \uc2 The pipeline has leaked more than 144,000 gallons of oil into the ocean killing local wildlife. Shoreline cleanup is underway as multiple Orange County beaches remain partially off limits to beach goers this weekend.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  right after a short break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: For this week`s Got-cha, I`m taking aim at the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  and a few specific senators, -- well, West Virginia Democrat \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  is not the only problematic member of the Senate, I`m going to start with him, because he made a statement this week that sums up the issue neatly.\par \par \uc2 When challenged by his colleague, Senator Bernie Sanders, to come to the negotiating table in good faith and list specific issues he has with this infrastructure bill, Manchin refused. Then to add insult to injury, he accused Sanders of wanting to make this country quote, "an entitlement society." That`s some impressive projection from West Virginia`s senior senator, a man who thinks his multimillion dollar wealth entitles him to ignore the wishes of his own constituents.\par \par \uc2 West Virginians support the legislation Manchin has been maligning by huge margins.\par \par \uc2 Senator Manchin who lives on a private yacht when he is in Washington finally faced some of those constituents last week, but only when they literally paddled up to him in kayaks. But to Manchin`s credit, he led those West Virginians say their piece, something that his Arizona colleague Senator \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  flatly refuses to do.\par \par \uc2 Sinema has a scant three years in the Senate, but somehow feels entitled to avoid town halls and phone calls with her constituents. Indeed, it seems the only way to have your voice heard by Sinema in Arizona is to attend multi-thousand dollar fundraisers.\par \par \uc2 [17:25:10]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And just in case you thought this entitlement was solely a Democratic issue, let me direct your attention to Minority Leader Mitch McConnell, a man who feels his own political goals, entitles him to threaten a worldwide economic crisis by promising to filibuster a routine debt ceiling vote.\par \par \uc2 This man feels entitled to hold the global economy hostage when he was sent to the Senate by a state with less than two percent of the U.S. population. It is not just McConnell who wields outsized power, it`s the Republican caucus as a whole.\par \par \uc2 Currently, Democrats represent 40 million more Americans than their Republican counterparts, and that landslide victory earned them only the slimmest majority. It is not a new problem either, Republicans have not won a majority of Senate votes nationwide since 1996, but they control the Senate for 18 of those 25 years, as if entitled to permanent minority rule.\par \par \uc2 Between Republican dedication to anti-majoritarian power and some Democrats refusal to listen to their own constituents, the Senate has long since abandoned any pretense of being a truly democratic institution. All of Manchin`s whining about entitlement is clear that these senators feel like they are above us all, entitled to adoration and respect for their positions alone, it is like an American House of Lords.\par \par \uc2 But there was a whole revolution to ensure that this country would have no nobility class, and it`s time for the Senate to remember that they are a government of the people, by the people, and for the people.\par \par \uc2 So to Manchin, Sinema, and McConnell, and any other senator with similar superiority complexes, if you`re going to act like a House of Lords, you either need to dress the part or remember that you work for us.\par \par \uc2 I got-cha.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:31:27]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: A month after police reform negotiations collapsed in Washington, developments in the cases that drove those talks continue to frustrate reform activists. As the City of Minneapolis prepares to vote on the future of his policing nearly 18 months after George Floyd`s murder, and a new "Washington Post" analysis has found that dozens of state level efforts to rein in police conduct have been quietly killed for the exact same reason that Federal efforts died.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is the Attorney General of Black America, Civil Rights attorney, \uc2 Ben Crump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now Attorney Crump, you haven`t -- we haven`t had you on the show since negotiations behind the Federal police reform collapsed on Capitol Hill last month, though you and I talk often as we deal with these issues and matters with the families.\par \par \uc2 The main sticking point between parties being, of course, qualified immunity for police officers, Democrats generally wanting individual officers to be subject to civil rights suit, Republicans enthralled to police unions, of course do not. And so largely because of that, what would have been \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  Justice and Policing Act is dead now, or at least for now, the time being.\par \par \uc2 Now that those watching who might say we have to look at the states and build victories there, I point you to this week`s "Washington Post," which found that at least 35 state bills to end or amend qualified immunity have died in the last 18 months. So, what is the next front in the war, counselor? And what does that look like to you?\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN CRUMP\uc2 , CIVIL RIGHTS ATTORNEY: Thank you, Reverend Al, but this issue -- continuing to focus on this issue, Reverend Al is very important.\par \par \uc2 When we talk about qualified immunity, I feel it`s very important that we always give a historical analysis to how we got in this quandary. It was in 1871 that the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2  passed what was known as the \uc2 Ku Klux Klan\uc2  Act, which gave newly freed African-Americans a legal pathway to bring civil actions and collect monetarily against racist government officers for violating their constitutional rights and commit brutal violence against them.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Right.\par \par \uc2 CRUMP: And it was in 1967, Reverend Al, in the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  in the Freedom Riot Desegregation case of all things, rendered qualified immunity to be able to oppose this protection that they gave to black people to hold those government officers who brutalized them accountable.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Right.\par \par \uc2 CRUMP: And made it baseless. And so, it`s so ironic when we think about it, Reverend Al. We are fighting to say that a law that was passed to give black people access to whole government officers who could perform racist acts and commit violence against them a legal pathway to get redress.\par \par \uc2 If that`s the issue that they`re saying they don`t want to address, they want it to be where those protections are not given any sufficient rights for minorities to say, you are violating my constitutional rights, and we have to make police and other governmental entities say that is not allowed to do to any American citizen.\par \par \uc2 [17:35:26]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And in many ways, leave the cities to pay these civil suits, but in many ways having taxpayers pay for the brutality of individuals that won`t be liable at all for their brutalities. Now, you recall the unrest in Minneapolis last year after George Floyd`s murder, we were all there, and then this week, you and I saw those new body camera videos captured five days after that murder, in which Minneapolis police officers arrived to deride black protests.\par \par \uc2 One officer heard to say that he appreciated that his peers were quote, "out hunting people." Capping it off with a quote, "F these people." And of course, it gets better, one video shows an officer firing rubber bullets into a crowd of people screaming that they are unarmed. As the people screamed they unarmed, he is firing rubber bullets.\par \par \uc2 The people in Minneapolis will soon vote on a ballot initiative that would essentially remove the mandate that their city have a police department as we know it. To those opposed to this measure, how do you view videos like these? And then say that more training is just the answer -- just training -- when you have people that have these kind of attitudes on the force?\par \par \uc2 CRUMP: Reverend Al, it is the intellectual justification of discrimination when you see videos like this and then you have a legal doctrine of qualified immunity to say that what those officers did was legal and there is no recourse for the citizens.\par \par \uc2 We must always remember, Reverend Al that we will overcome this. It is going to be challenging, it may be a multi-year approach, but I am still optimistic that as long as the people keep getting in good trouble, like they did in Colorado after Elijah McClain was killed and \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  and they actually got qualified immunity abolished in that state.\par \par \uc2 More states have to continue to be pressed. The people have to flood the Senate chambers and say, we demand that we make the Declaration of Independence apply to everybody, not just white people, but to black down, to brown people, Asian people, and we have to keep doing that, Reverend Al. That is the only way we will get rid of the --\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Sustained -- sustained indignation, I call it, like we got the Eric Garner law a state law here in New York.\par \par \uc2 Were you surprised -- I am out of time -- but I have to ask you, were you surprised that the Justice Department declined to pursue criminal Civil Rights charges against the Kenosha Wisconsin police officer who shot Jacob Blake seven times in the back citing insufficient evidence to establish that the officer used excessive force.\par \par \uc2 Now, let me remind our audience, Blake was shot seven times in his back as he left the scene of a domestic incident in August of last year. The officer was found not guilty of state charges earlier this year. Blake, of course, remains paralyzed from the waist down. Your response, Attorney Crump.\par \par \uc2 CRUMP: I was disappointed, but not surprised, Reverend Al, and this underscores the reason why we have to continue to push to end qualified immunity because the only justice that Jacob Blake, Jr., now paralyzed may ever receive in life is civil justice since the criminal justice system has once again fail to give justice to this person, this --\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And as I said, he is paralyzed. He has children and a wonderful mother and father.\par \par \uc2 Ben Crump\uc2 , thank you as always, Mr. Attorney General for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Up next, congressional infighting over the debt ceiling continues and could this finally be the moment to bust the filibuster? Congressman \uc2 Emanuel Cleaver\uc2  joins us. Stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:43:32]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION. Joining me now Congressman \uc2 Emanuel Cleaver\uc2  of Missouri, a Democrat. Congressman, Reverend, thanks, as always for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 We wanted you with us tonight because you sit on \uc2 the House Financial Services Committee\uc2 . What can you tell us about where negotiations stand on the drama of the week raising the debt ceiling?\par \par \uc2 REP. EMANUEL CLEAVER\uc2  (D-MO): Well, I think that we`re going to probably be able to do that on Tuesday of this week, but it would be malpractice if I did not say, particularly to you, Reverend, that this is going to be a short-term situation, and there is a lot of damage going to be done to the United States economy even though we`re going to raise it for a short period of time.\par \par \uc2 The reason is that we have had a reliable economy in our country. We have the international currency. The dollar is the international currency, and we`re making the people around the world doubt the value of the dollar and whether or not the United States is going to be able to pay its bills. That`s not good for our credit rating.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Congressman, I`ve read where you called the potential failure of the President`s infrastructure plan, a quote, "existential threat to democratic majorities" in both chambers. I certainly won`t refute your view from the chamber.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, but for black voters who got \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  elected, they watched the police reform die, and as I just discussed with \uc2 Ben Crump\uc2 , and they`re now wondering why this infrastructure bill is cast as the make or break for Democrats, instead of the George Floyd bill or the \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  Voting Rights Act. What do you tell them?\par \par \uc2 [17:45:28]\par \par \uc2 CLEAVER: Well, they ought to be angry, I can`t -- I`ve got to tell you, I`m not happy at all myself. And one of the reasons we`re having this problem is because we have some idol worshipers in the Senate that they`re practicing idolatry. They believe somehow that the filibuster was brought down from Sinai, and that they`ve got to, you know, not trespass on filibuster.\par \par \uc2 And if they rid of the filibuster, except for criminal reform, voting rights, and this emergency Build Back Better and our, also bipartisan infrastructure bill, we can do what we need to do to move people into a better place.\par \par \uc2 Of all the things that I think just irked me was seeing Senator Sinema do the McCain thumbs down on a $15.00 minimum wage that people who are poor and black and brown, that`s a slap at us.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: No doubt about it, and she doing that, and then these talks around police reform and voting rights are going down. I told President Biden in our meetings with him with Civil Rights leaders that you told America that blacks had your back, you`d have ours. Well, they`re stabbing us in the back.\par \par \uc2 Let me bring you to this -- earlier this week, you and Congresswoman \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2  protested the state execution of Ernest Lee Johnson who was convicted of killing three people during a robbery in 1994. Johnson was intellectually disabled from birth, a fifth of his brain medically removed in 2008. His execution drawing protests from anti-death penalty activists and all the way to Pope Francis himself.\par \par \uc2 And you wrote on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  that quote, "State sanctioned execution of the intellectually disabled is both morally reprehensible and egregiously unconstitutional." I know that a House Bill to prohibit capital punishment for Federal crimes was introduced earlier this week by your colleagues \uc2 Ayanna Pressley\uc2  and \uc2 Adriano Espaillat\uc2 . Do you see Democrats taking this up as a priority? And by Democrats, I mean, the Congressional Black Caucus?\par \par \uc2 CLEAVER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Since you and Congresswoman Bush both drew the line from Jim Crow to what happened this week, Congressman?\par \par \uc2 CLEAVER: Absolutely. The Congressional Black Caucus, hopefully the Progressive Caucus will join in with us and the Hispanic Congressional Caucus. We all need to join forces in this because what happens is if you study or look at the capital punishment situation, if an African-American or a brown person kills an African-American or brown person, they stand a very, very small chance of getting the death penalty. It is when you kill someone white that the death penalty is very likely going to be your sentence.\par \par \uc2 And killing this gentleman, this man, Ernest Johnson is like killing a six- year-old. Reverend, I don`t even believe he understood when they told him he was going to be put into an electric chair. I don`t think he had any knowledge of understanding of what was even happening to him.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Wow. Well, Congressman, thank you. And I remind people he is not only a Congressman, he is one of the best preachers in the country. I`m preaching at \uc2 Howard University\uc2  in the morning. I`m using one of your sermons, so don`t watch it on FaceTime.\par \par \uc2 Emanuel Cleaver\uc2 , thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, my final thoughts. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:53:21]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: In my final thoughts tonight, I want to pay tribute to a great person, Reverend Jesse Jackson, who celebrated his 80th birthday on yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Reverend Jesse Jackson has given his life fighting for justice and fairness all over the world. It was Jesse Jackson that when Dr. King was killed, joined other allies like Dr. Ralph Abernathy and others to keep Dr. King`s dream going in nonviolent social protests and brought it into the North, which was basically a southern base movement.\par \par \uc2 It was Jesse Jackson that came out and made private corporations and the private sector accountable. It was Jesse Jackson that from the Rainbow Coalition before we ever used the term intersectionalism and brought people of different silos and backgrounds together.\par \par \uc2 It was his run for President in `84 and `88 that registered voters that elected black governors and blacks to the Congress and black mayors. It was Reverend Jesse Jackson that went to foreign lands and brought back hostages without a portfolio from the government, and without even being thanked in many cases by those that were in office.\par \par \uc2 He has helped change America and we should salute and give him his flowers while he is yet among us and going strong.\par \par \uc2 I personally have been touched all my life by Reverend Jackson. When I was 12 years old, my mother and Pastor brought me to him. He was twice my age then, he was 26, and he has been a father figure and as I got older, a Big Brother figure, he didn`t want me in my 50s and 60s call him a father figure.\par \par \uc2 [17:55:08]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: He has guided me and many others that are known and unknown with the work ethic and the commitment that you put the cause ahead of your own agenda.\par \par \uc2 I wanted to say tonight, Happy Birthday, Reverend Jackson. Some of us will never forget what you`ve done not only for us, but for this nation and the world.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That does it for me. Thanks for watching. I`ll see you back here tomorrow at 5:00 p.m. Eastern for another live hour of POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Now let`s go to my colleague, Alicia Menendez.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 [17:59:48]\par \par \uc2 ALICIA MENENDEZ, MSNBC HOST: Thank you so much, Reverend Sharpton.\par \par \uc2 Hello, everyone. I`m Alicia Menendez.\par \par \uc2 Later this evening in Iowa, a return to the stage by you know who? \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  stumping for Senator Chuck Grassley`s re-election. NBC`s Gary Grumbach spoke with Trump supporters a short time ago as they wait for Trump to take the stage, and safe to say support of him has not waned since the January 6th insurrection he incited and was impeached for.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020211010eha900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE RACHEL MADDOW SHOW for October 8, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Rachel Maddow \par \uc2 8,811 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 October 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Rachel Maddow Show\par \uc2 TRMADS\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [21:00:09]\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST: That`s "ALL IN" for this week.\par \par \uc2 THE RACHEL MADDOW SHOW starts right now. Good evening, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Chris, have a fantastic weekend, my friend, thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You too.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: And thanks to you at home for joining us this hour. Happy Friday. Very happy to have you here.\par \par \uc2 This is one of those Friday nights when the news gods are saying, neener, neener, we don`t believe in weekends.\par \par \uc2 Today, we have got eyes on the country`s most conservative federal appeals court, the Fifth Circuit U.S. Circuit -- Fifth Circuit U.S. Court of Appeals sits in Louisiana. Ideologically, Republican presidents have so stacked that particular court with their appointees that it`s now the judicial equivalent of like a combination anti-vaccine rally and gun show.\par \par \uc2 The Fifth Circuit includes the state of Texas. And tonight, the state of Texas has told the Fifth Circuit that they want their abortion ban back. Texas, as you know, earlier this year passed a law that effectively banned abortion in Texas. The conservative Republican appointed majority on the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  amazingly let the Texas abortion ban go into effect, even though there`s a ruling called Roe v. Wade that`s supposedly still standing precedent, that supposedly blocks states from banning abortion.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 , despite that, let the Texas abortion ban go into effect last month. The Biden administration, the Justice Department under U.S. Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , they sued Texas in federal court, asking a judge to step in and block implementation of the Texas abortion ban while the case is heard in the courts.\par \par \uc2 Two nights ago, a federal judge in Texas did just that, blocked the ban, which has meant for the past two days or so, the Texas abortion ban has been suspended and Texas women could once again, at least theoretically, get an abortion in that state.\par \par \uc2 But as of tonight, Friday night, we are watching that ultraconservative, ideologically uniform, far right federal appeals court, because tonight, the state of Texas has asked them to jump in and reinstate Texas` abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 I am not a lawyer, do not take this to the bank or anywhere where it might be perceived as currency, but my expectation from what I know of that appeals court is that that appeals court probably will find a way to reinstate Texas` abortion ban. And it`s probably just a question of when, in the expectation that it might be as early as tonight, tonight we do have eyes on the Fifth Circuit. We`ll keep you posted as we keep watching that matter.\par \par \uc2 Now, midnight last night was the deadline for these four former Trump administration officials to comply with the -- the guy at the bottom, that is not a joke, he made that face of his own accord, do not blame me. Sorry.\par \par \uc2 The deadline was last night at midnight for these four to comply with subpoenas from the committee that`s investigating the January 6th attack on the U.S. Capitol.\par \par \uc2 Now, of these four, the committee says that two of them are responding thus far. The January 6th investigator said today that these two, former White House chief of staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  and Mr. Goggly Eyes, Kash Patel, they are, quote, engaging with the January 6 Committee that they received. Okay.\par \par \uc2 That said, this guy on the lower left hand corner, Trump social media guy Dan Scavino, he apparently is not engaging with the committee. He`s not responding to the subpoena despite the deadline, because apparently the committee can`t find him. I don`t know if it`s fair to say he`s on the lam, but apparently nobody can locate him to actually serve the subpoena and until they can physically find Dan to give it to him, we don`t know what`s going to happen there. Which of course leaves this guy, upper right corner, who has been found, one of the only pictures known to humankind in which \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  is wearing only one shirt.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  is not engaging in the committee even though he has been found and he has been served with a subpoena. \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  sent the January 6th investigators a letter today saying he won`t comply with their subpoena. And, you know, presumably Mr. Bannon is doing that because one of the tried and true lessons that was learned in the Trump era by all of the many, many accused felons and subpoenaed ones witnesses in Trump`s orbit was, play dumb, delay, don`t respond, resist subpoenas, say you won`t testify, see what happens, nothing ever happens.\par \par \uc2 Through the Mueller investigation into, you know, Russia interfering in 2016 to get Trump elected, to the impeachment investigation over Ukraine, to the other Trump impeachment investigation, to all the other serious investigations in between, all the Trump guys think they`ve figured it out.\par \par \uc2 [21:05:06]\par \par \uc2 As long as you just drag things out, refuse to cooperate, the hints and the threats that something bad might happen to you if you defy a subpoena, that never really comes to much, nobody ever gets in trouble, don`t ready about it. Better to risk that inchoate and feeble theory than help anybody investigating Trump`s alleged crimes.\par \par \uc2 So Bannon is defying the subpoena. The only problem with Mr. Bannon taking this tack with the January 6th investigators is that those investigators also all lived through the Trump investigations of the past five years. They`re all wise to this as a Trump tactic of delay and distraction during investigations. That`s why the January 6th investigators have been saying so insistently that they`re not going to get played the way other investigators have been. The chair of the investigation, Congressman \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2  of Mississippi, has been threatening from the very beginning that the January 6th investigation is not going to just let people defy its subpoenas.\par \par \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  is apparently happy to test that. That`s why you`ve got dramatic headlines like this one in "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 ," January 6th panel threatens to pursue charges against \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 . We shall see.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we are also going to speak with Susanne Craig, Pulitzer Prize winning investigative reporter for "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 ." We`re going to speak with her about new documents and materials just released by the oversight committee today which are honestly sort of shocking. Not very much shocks me in this type of investigation anymore, but this is amazing. The documents they produced today show that while he was president, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  apparently received tens of millions of dollars from the foreign owned bank \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 . Just a massive financial windfall he got from \uc2 deutsche bank\uc2  without publicly disclosing it while he was president.\par \par \uc2 The oversight committee turned up evidence of that previously secret arrangement between Trump and \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 . Again, this happened while he was president, and it`s never been publicly disclosed before. They also turned up evidence that they say shows that the former president lied materially about his financial status when he applied to the federal government to lease the building that he then turned into the Trump Hotel in D.C.\par \par \uc2 Materially misrepresenting your financial standing in order to get a thing of value from an entity like a bank, an insurance company, tax authorities, the federal government, that can be a serious matter. But honestly, shocking stuff released today by the oversight committee. Susanne Craig dropped the anchor down through the opaque morass of Trump`s finances, which led to, among other things, the criminal charges the Trump businesses are facing in New York. Susanne Craig is going to join us live in just a moment to talk about these new revelations as well.\par \par \uc2 All that have tonight, plus, we`ll have an update for you on how it is that we still have a postmaster general, a dude running the U.S. post office nationwide, while personally under \uc2 FBI\uc2  criminal investigation and while he`s now putting in place new post office rules specifically to mess with your Christmas this career. And while he`s continuing his quest to make the post office provide fewer services more slowly and at higher cost. Why are we still beset with this man now that there is a new administration?\par \par \uc2 I do not know. But the effort to stop him picked up steam in an interesting way today. We`re going to have more on that tonight as well. I told you, this is a Friday, which the news gods just will not let go.\par \par \uc2 But I also want to show you this tonight. This is a sitting member of Congress, a Republican member of Congress, making his case this week at an official congressional hearing. As you know, Republicans in Congress have almost universally at this point, at one level or another, gotten behind this crusade by former President Trump to say that the election was stolen from him, that he should rightfully still be president, that there was something wrong with the last presidential election.\par \par \uc2 Their exhibit A, their prize case study for something having been wrong with the election, with Arizona. Well, here is a congressman from Arizona, \uc2 Paul Gosar\uc2 , explaining in his own words exactly what he knows went wrong in the Arizona election, just making it clear as day, spelling it out for all the stupid, stupid other members of Congress who don`t get it even though it`s so clear to him what happened. He wants you to know, it`s so obvious. At this hearing this week he just decided he was going to lay out all the evidence so we could all follow along with him because he says it`s all very, very simple.\par \par \uc2 [21:10:02]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. PAUL GOSAR\uc2  (R-AZ): From the standpoint that we see this, there`s problems. How about me? Where do you come for me? The day after the election, I was contacted by two individuals. One had security and fraud -- jobs with the banking world. The other one from fraud from the Department of Defense. They were monitoring the election through Edison, the amalgamator, who was providing information to the media.\par \par \uc2 What they saw in Arizona, they were watching the secretary of state at the same time, but what they saw from Arizona drew their attention quickly first. They saw numbers of 90-some thousand, 60-some thousand, 40-some thousand ballots drop into Donald Trump`s category and they quickly come out verbatim. There may be a reason for that. We don`t know. But then they started watching and looking to the dumps.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Then they started looking to the dumps, hmm, the dumps, hmm.\par \par \uc2 My favorite part is when he said there may be a reason for that, we don`t know. But the 90,000, 50,000, 60,000, 40,000 ballots dropped in Trump`s category and came out verbatim. Do you know what verbatim me? The Edison amalgamator. How about me?\par \par \uc2 Lest you think I`m taking Congressman Gosar out of context here and cherry picking the one part where he seems to be off in his own haze, trust me, it`s all like this from him.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOSAR: The results we see here supposedly don`t change the outcome. But there`s -- there`s more to the story. It`s how those votes could be manipulated by the machine. And then the -- the calibration or the certification of those ballots is covered up by the machine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Was covered up by the machines. The machines are covering up the Crayola markers. Calibration, certification, what, what?\par \par \uc2 This is the best case that can be made for the election was stolen, right? This is all the investigation that he`s done in Arizona. This is testimony in Congress.\par \par \uc2 By the end of it, Congresswoman \uc2 Carolyn Maloney\uc2 , chair of the Oversight Committee who is presiding while Mr. Gosar gets to explain himself here, by the end of it, she`s like, yeah, what are we going to do with this? Watch the look on her face as he wraps up.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOSAR: And provide different mechanisms, so they allow a full canvass. They didn`t have access to the routers. They didn`t have full access for voter signature acuity and documentation and accuracy.\par \par \uc2 REP. CAROLYN MALONEY\uc2  (D-NY): The gentleman`s time --\par \par \uc2 GOSAR: There`s troubling problems here and hopefully I`ll be able to get some time yielded to me so I can explain even more.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Oh, yes, sir, hopefully you`ll get much more time so you can explain even more. It`s going great. We`re all right there with you. Just following right along. Tell us more about the amalgamator Crayola certification with the what about me?\par \par \uc2 That`s the state of the argument. That`s also a sitting congressman. Arizona Republican Congressman \uc2 Paul Gosar\uc2 , leading the charge for the election was stolen from Trump cause at the House Oversight Committee`s hearing this week on Arizona`s so-called audit. They held an oversight hearing on Arizona having reviewed its election results with this partisan, weird, QAnon-promoting Cyber Ninjas Trump audit.\par \par \uc2 And Congressman Gosar, yes, this is the same guy who claimed last week that somebody from the CIA fraud department reached out to tell him how hundreds of thousands of votes were stolen from Trump in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 The CIA fraud department? What`s their phone number? Who runs the CIA fraud department? Tell me that.\par \par \uc2 This is the state of the argument for this central tenet of the Republican Party now.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, at this same hearing, again, oversight committee looking into what exactly they did in Arizona based on these claims that Congressman Gosar is explaining so clearly, two other Republicans testified. And these were elections officials, county officials from Maricopa County, Arizona. They testified as to what they have been experiencing as public officials ever since this so-called audit mishegoss got going there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL GATES\uc2 , VICE CHAIRMAN, BOARD OF SUPERVISOR, MARICOPA COUNTY, ARIZONA: Senators, they threatened to jail me and my colleagues on the board of supervisors. The Senate then hired the cyber ninjas. They chased conspiracy theories. They threw out false claims.\par \par \uc2 And worst of all, they accused our good elections workers of committing crimes. They said that they deleted files, that these were files that the Cyber Ninjas just couldn`t find. Now, this was either an out and out lie or a level of incompetence by the Cyber Ninjas that was staggering.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [21:15:08]\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: A level of incompetence that was staggering.\par \par \uc2 That was local Arizona official, Maricopa County official, \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2 , not that \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2 . He`s a Republican. Telling the House Oversight Committee this week how Republicans in Arizona, Republicans in the state Senate in Arizona, threatened to put him and his colleagues in jail for not handing over more materials to the Cyber Ninjas who they had hired to do this ridiculous review of the presidential election results in that state.\par \par \uc2 I should note now that the guy who is the head cyber ninja, the QAnon promoter who is the CEO of the Cyber Ninjas company that carried out this audit, he was also asked to testify at this hearing this week. He refused to testify. Instead of testifying to Congress, he spent that time he was supposed to be in Congress instead doing a conspiracy theory podcast hosted by the guy who started the conspiracy theory that Dominion voting machines were a secret Antifa plot. That`s what he spent his time doing instead of testifying to congress, because he was very, very busy with the Antifa plot Dominion voting machines guy.\par \par \uc2 But this ridiculous audit thing, as laughable and increasingly incoherent as it is to the rest of us, it is still picking up steam in the Republican Party. It is becoming more and more central to how they are moving forward as a party and what they are working on. And it`s in multiple states now.\par \par \uc2 You will recall, our reporting recently that the My Pillow guy, N Mike Lindell, CEO of the My Pillow company who is a key player in the nonsense elections around the country, last month, he told Idaho, a state Trump won by 30 points, that he had proof, proof, that there was massive fraud and computers were hacked in every single county in Idaho to move votes from Trump to Biden and he demanded a state investigation. Even in the counties where there aren`t computers involved in the voting process at all. Still, computers were hacked, it was every county.\par \par \uc2 The Republicans in the Idaho secretary of state`s office decided for whatever reason that they were going to take this seriously enough to follow up on his allegation and they actually did recanvass two counties in Idaho. The recanvass of those two counties in Idaho actually gave \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  I think it was eight more votes than they thought he had had in the previous certification.\par \par \uc2 Idaho, interestingly, though, has decided they`re not happy that they went ahead and did this. Now they`ve asked Mr. Lindell to pay for the recanvassing that they did because of his allegations. The chief deputy secretary of state in Idaho saying today, quote, we will be sending him a bill. He also said his office is considering legal action against Mr. Lindell for him having made these false allegations.\par \par \uc2 Okay. He made false allegations. But did you have to follow them up and go check? Them, really, did you?\par \par \uc2 The craziness is not letting up, though. The Mr. Pillow guy is claiming he has discovered that one of the people whose vote was recorded in the 2020 election was 850 years old. And doesn`t that seem suspicious? Now, he won`t say in which state this 850-year-old superhuman voted. But he is sounding the alarm that that was definitely not kosher.\par \par \uc2 Someone from the year 1170 stayed alive for nearly nine centuries just to cast a ballot -- for Trump? I don`t know -- in the 2020 election. He really is alleging that. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 Remember, he also says Trump will be back in the White House as president by Thanksgiving. Okay. The Republican audit circus, as ridiculous as it is, continues to spread around the country, though. Wisconsin, the Republican leader of the state assembly in Wisconsin hired a former Wisconsin judge, a former judge who spoke at a stop the steal rally in November alleging that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had secretly won the election. They hired him to oversee a very, very objective audit in the state of Wisconsin as the Wisconsin legislature`s official special counsel to investigate these matters.\par \par \uc2 This is an audit that is set to cost Wisconsin taxpayers nearly $700,000. The man who is in charge of this audit has made sure to keep conspiracy theories directly in the orbit of what he`s doing. He attended a symposium put on by the My Pillow guy, the guy who says that an 850-year-old person voted in the election.\par \par \uc2 He`s also apparently been consulting with a former political candidate who also was part of the Arizona audit. This is a guy who says the election was stolen from Trump and he can tell you that because he has found it by reading between the lines of "The Hitch Hiker`s guide to the Galaxy," that`s where he got the numerical algorithms that prove what votes were stolen and when.\par \par \uc2 It`s against that backdrop that the guy who is running the Wisconsin audit decided to randomly show up at a Green Bay, Wisconsin council meeting this week, and answer questions in public for the time about what it is he`s doing in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 [21:20:12]\par \par \uc2 When he was asked to explain his comments that education there had been election irregularities in Wisconsin, this was him sort of struggling to explain himself.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALDER RANDY SCANNELL: You said that there was information to indicate that there had been some inappropriate action taken by district. Is that public information or is that private information?\par \par \uc2 GABLEMAN: Oh, yes. I mean, please look at controversies, if you have -- I see you have a laptop there. The Internet I suppose you have. If you look at issues concerning the Wisconsin election, a plethora, a plethora of issues will come up.\par \par \uc2 ALDER SCANNELL: In that plethora of information you believe there is some in there that is valid? You have --\par \par \uc2 GABLEMAN: I`ll find out. We`ll find out and I will share them with you.\par \par \uc2 ADLER SCANNELL: So as of right now, then, you don`t know if there is?\par \par \uc2 GABLEMAN: I think there are indications that there may be which is why the legislature has authorized this investigation.\par \par \uc2 ADLER SCANNELL: That`s what I`m trying to get at. What indications? Because from what I can see, all there is are a plethora of accusations, but I see no evidence supporting those, so --\par \par \uc2 GABLEMAN: Okay. All right. No, no. I`m happy to answer your question. You got a few hours?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: You got access to the interwebs? You`ll find a plethora of information. Give me a few hours, and ooh, boy, I`ll just bore you with it. I don`t have it at hand but I`ve got hours of it ready to go some other time, with my plethora, which has an remora, which is heartbreaking.\par \par \uc2 This was the headline from "Milwaukee Journal Sentinel" this week, on that former judge who`s been running the audit. Quote, former Supreme Court Justice Gableman, head of Republican review of Wisconsin election, says he does not understand how elections work. This is not the onion and this is not a hyperbolic headline. The paper quotes this judge telling them in an interview that he, quote, like most people, quote, does not have a comprehensive understanding or even any understanding of how elections work.\par \par \uc2 He says he has no understanding of how elections work. He is running the election audit for Wisconsin Republicans, at taxpayer expense.\par \par \uc2 And that is just the tip of the iceberg when it comes to the sort of comedy of errors that are surrounding this election audit that he`s trying to do. Last week, the same guy issued subpoenas. This is the first time Wisconsin lawmakers have issued subpoenas from the legislature in nearly 50 years. He sent out subpoenas to election officials and county officials across the state, subpoenas that were riddled with mistakes.\par \par \uc2 In one subpoena to Green Bay, the Green Bay city clerk`s name was misspelled. And the subject line of the email issuing the subpoena, also misspelled the legal phrase related to producing the documents. And a subpoena sent to the city clerk of Milwaukee who is not even responsible for elections in Milwaukee, this guy asked for documents related to the elections in Green Bay. Why would the Milwaukee clerk have election information about green bay, especially when the Milwaukee clerk doesn`t work on elections at all?\par \par \uc2 These subpoenas directed officials they should come meet with this guy at a rented office suite in a co-working space that is also listed as the address for a liposuction clinic. Sure, seems legit, turn up, we`ll give you a Johnny, it opens up in the back.\par \par \uc2 This all follows the special counsel, this guy reaching out to city clerks asking them to preserve election materials. He reached out to them from a Gmail address that populated everybody`s emails as John Delta. Nobody knows who John Delta is.\par \par \uc2 This request also included an attached PDF with this blurry clip art logo that appears to have been a copy and paste job from a website. This is what he says his letter was.\par \par \uc2 The metadata from that document telling election clerks what they needed to hand over to him, that document wasn`t even authored by this guy, Michael Gableman, who is supposedly overseeing the election audit. It wasn`t authorized by John Delta, whoever that was. It was apparently authored by an Andrew Kloster. We chased down, trying to figure out who that Andrew Kloster was.\par \par \uc2 There is an Andrew Kloster who worked as a lawyer in the Trump administration, in the Trump White House. We didn`t know for sure of it was him. We asked him for comment. We never heard back from him. But, today, "The Milwaukee Journal Sentinel" confirms that yes, that Trump White House lawyer has been hired by this guy to work on the Wisconsin audit of the election results.\par \par \uc2 Oh, yeah, that seems totally fine. You`re auditing the election results, you`ve put in charge of the audit a guy who says it was stolen from Trump, who can`t tell you anything he`s actually investigating, who says he has no idea how elections actually work, and he`s hired a Trump White House lawyer to help him with it.\par \par \uc2 That John Delta Gmail address email was sketchy enough and concerning enough to Wisconsin`s clerk that it prompted some of them at least to reach out to the \uc2 U.S. Department of Homeland Security\uc2  for guidance on if or how they should comply with these demands.\par \par \uc2 The demands are -- in terms of the breadth of what this guy is demanding from the city clerks, it`s just bewildering. Here is what those subpoenas asked for from all the county clerks in the state. Quote, all documents contained in your files and/or in your custody, possession, or control pertaining to the election. All? All?\par \par \uc2 That`s asking for every single thing in any way related to the election that has ever passed through any county clerk`s office in any capacity. Really? You want all of that? And should we send it to John Delta`s Gmail address or do we just drop it off at the liposuction clinic?\par \par \uc2 The guy running the audit said this week he is also going to subpoena the mayors of five of the largest cities in Wisconsin. Again, planning to request everything in any way related to the election that`s ever passed through any of their offices for any reason at any time. And he said this week that those subpoenas were going out to the mayors of the five largest cities in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 But then last night, "The Milwaukee Journal Sentinel" reported that this guy, the counsel who is overseeing the audit, he changed his mind. And even though he announced that he was going to subpoena and demand testimony from all of these mayors, he changed his mind, he`s backing off those requests, he`s cancelling all the interviews at the co-working space that he shares with the liposuction clinic.\par \par \uc2 He no longer wants to do the interviews and he`s backing off all the new subpoenas, the ones he just announced, he doesn`t know what he`s going to do there. He said, quote, they could simply provide him with copies of records they`ve already made available under the state`s law. Again, not the onion.\par \par \uc2 That was last night, this guy doing an about face, walking back the subpoena requests, walking back these subpoenas that he announced in public apparently before sending them out. Today, he reversed course again. Today, he said never mind, about 180 that I did yesterday, today I`m doing another 180, which results in me doing a 360 which means I`m still going.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOST: So you haven`t cancelled scheduled mayors or anything like that?\par \par \uc2 GABLEMAN: Absolutely not.\par \par \uc2 HOST: So this is a 100 percent fake story.\par \par \uc2 GABLEMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: One hundred percent fake story.\par \par \uc2 That same interview with a conservative radio host, this guy decided to attack "The Milwaukee Journal Sentinel," the paper who broke the news yesterday that Gableman was reportedly walking back the demands. This was the way he chose to attack the newspaper.\par \par \uc2 Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOST: You will never hear me call "The Milwaukee Journal Sentinel" a straight news organization without a political agenda.\par \par \uc2 GABLEMAN: This is the last time I`m going to respond to their propaganda. You keep in mind, Joseph Goebbels, who was Adolf Hitler`s head of propaganda in Nazi Germany, Goebbels said, you give me control of a country`s media, I will turn any people into a herd of pigs. That, there, you know what, what they`re doing over at the journal would make Joseph Goebbels blush.\par \par \uc2 HOST: That`s a pretty strong comparison.\par \par \uc2 GABLEMAN: Do you disagree with it?\par \par \uc2 HOST: I mean, I don`t think it`s entirely appropriate to compare "The Milwaukee Journal Sentinel" --\par \par \uc2 GABLEMAN: Why not?\par \par \uc2 HOST: I generally shy away from comparisons to Hitler and the Nazis. I understand the point, please don`t misunderstand. I would say they`re more like a Pravda from the Russian government.\par \par \uc2 GABLEMAN: Okay, Pravda, we`ll settle on Pravda. I retract my statement about Joseph Goebbels and you and I agree on Pravda.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Why do you disagree with my Nazi comparison? You`re wussing out? You`re wussing out? You make Goebbels blush?\par \par \uc2 This is the guy who was leading Wisconsin`s nonpartisan objective audit to just make sure there`s a, you know, sincere and unbiased look at those very troubling election results in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 It really seems as if the Wisconsin audit might be even more ridiculous than the one they did in Arizona. Arizona got lots of national attention for their ridiculous Cyber Ninjas thing and what happened, right? But the Wisconsin one is also, again, taxpayer funded, organized and put in motion by Wisconsin Republicans in the state legislature.\par \par \uc2 [21:30:07]\par \par \uc2 Not getting any national attention thus far, but they are trying to use it in just the same way. That guy leading it, to put some sort of spotlight on the Wisconsin presidential election, is to make it seem like Biden didn`t really win.\par \par \uc2 Today, a Wisconsin judge in response to a lawsuit filed by the watchdog group American Oversight ordered the Republican state assembly speaker in Wisconsin to hand over records and documents related to this election investigation. Maybe we`ll finally find out what exactly led to this and what exactly Republicans in Wisconsin are up to with this. As much attention as Arizona got for what they did, they are doing the same thing in Pennsylvania, they are doing the same thing in Wisconsin, and it`s worse. They just tried to do it in places like Idaho, they`re trying to do it in Texas.\par \par \uc2 This is what the Republican Party stands for right now. \uc2 Paul Gosar\uc2  is the captain of the team at this point. If you pretend it is otherwise, you`re missing that because it is too ridiculous to believe, but it is what we are doing now.\par \par \uc2 Watch this space.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: To be perfectly clear from the outset, this is not a martini. What this appears to be is a wine glass, maybe a red wine glass, filled with ice, some clear-ish murky liquid, and a sad little abused olive plopped into the bottom there. This meets zero of the physical properties of a martini.\par \par \uc2 [21:35:02]\par \par \uc2 But it was in fact sold as one, for $15, to a reporter who ordered the martini from the Trump grill in New York City, in the Trump tower, in 2016. Ordering a drink at Trump`s restaurant was one reporter`s sideways attempt to try to get a closer look at the inner workings of Trump`s business empire after he was elected president.\par \par \uc2 In a way, that sorry excuse for a martini turned out to be a pretty good scene setter for what it would be like over the next couple of years for reporters trying to learn more about what the president`s business really was, what it was he was selling, and what it was people were buying.\par \par \uc2 The lesson being that once you start peeking under the hood, a lot of what Trump said made his businesses so successful appeared to potentially be the product of fraud. During Trump`s presidency, reporters at "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " published a series of deeply reported investigations into Trump`s business and private finances. Much of their reporting was based on the access to Trump tax documents.\par \par \uc2 "The Times" found Trump appears to have engaged in a series of tax schemes, tax evasion schemes, instances of what appeared to be outright tax fraud in order to boost his bottom line. Prosecutors are currently investigating the Trump business and his close associates for similar allegations of fraud that Trump`s business potentially hid the truth about his finances in order to con financial institutions into giving him money he otherwise wouldn`t have been able to get. That`s of course in addition to the felony charges currently pending against his company for tax fraud.\par \par \uc2 But there`s also the unique example of the president`s hotel in Washington, D.C. Trump`s D.C. hotel is actually an old U.S. Post Office building. So, it`s owned by the federal government. Trump`s company pays the government to lease that building so he can then rent it as a hotel to make money off of it. That arrangement, importantly, gives Congress oversight of this particular slice of his business, because the building is federal property.\par \par \uc2 The House Oversight Committee has been investigating whether or not Trump`s lease of that federal building constituted a conflict of interest given that while he was president, he was leasing that building from the federal government that he himself was in charge of.\par \par \uc2 Today, the committee released new findings based on hundreds of pages of financial documents that tell an incredible story. What they`ve turned up appears to fit the other patterns we have seen from hard-won investigative reporting about potential fraud and obfuscation and financial slight of hand, the things that have become synonymous with Donald Trump`s businesses.\par \par \uc2 According to the oversight committee today, Trump, quote, concealed certain debts from the federal government when he applied to lease the post office. The committee says he omitted hundreds of millions of dollars in debt, essentially to make himself look like a more qualified applicant to take over that lease. Once the hotel was up and running, Trump posted that the hotel was a huge financial boon for his business. He said he made more than $150 million off that hotel during his presidency.\par \par \uc2 But based on financial reports obtained by the committee, they say Trump`s dc hotel actually lost more than $70 million during the course of his presidency. We also learned from the committee today about the existence of a sort of inexplicable and until now, secret multimillion dollar favor paid to the president from a foreign owned bank called \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In order to actually turn the old post office into a luxury hotel, Trump`s business borrowed $170 million from \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 . Under the terms of that loan, Trump was due to start repaying the principal of that loan in 2018. But look what the committee found out. Quote, in 2018 the terms of the loan were changed to allow Trump to defer any principal payments on the loan by six years.\par \par \uc2 Under these revised terms \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  allowed the Trump hotel to make just interest payments for an additional six years and delayed any principal payments to 2024. That`s a multimillion dollar handout to the Trump hotel and to President Trump personally, who personally guaranteed those loans. And that was never previously known before.\par \par \uc2 Why is a foreign owned bank doing a multimillion dollar personal favor to a sitting president while he`s president?\par \par \uc2 These revelations today are the latest in a string of revelations. Every time somebody takes a slice of the Trump business world and puts it under the microscope, it`s not like good gin and vermouth. It`s like water-down who knows what in the wine glass and I would not need that olive.\par \par \uc2 Is it profit as a result of fraud and accounting tricks and careful omissions? Is that what this is? If so, is that legal? Does the D.C. hotel and its revelations portray a different portrait than what we`ve seen is it more of the same?\par \par \uc2 Joining us is Susanne Craig. She`s an investigative reporter for "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 ". She`s one of the lead reporters on "The Times" Pulitzer Prize- winning investigation into the president`s finances. She obtained Trump`s tax information regarding the D.C. hotel as part of her reporting.\par \par \uc2 [21:40:03]\par \par \uc2 Susanne, it`s nice to see you tonight. Thanks for making time to be here.\par \par \uc2 SUSANNE CRAIG, THE \uc2 NEW YORK TIMES\uc2 : Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Is the Trump hotel a meaningfully different thing than the rest of the Trump empire? It seems like some of these revelations laid out by the committee today are troubling in terms of potential legalities here and in potential consequences for the president, both in business terms and in criminal terms.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG: No, I don`t think it`s any different at all. When I think of it, I was reading it today, it sounds like a money pit. It reminds me a little bit of that movie from the 1980s. This is just costing him a huge amount of money to keep this going. Not only is he losing money, he`s plowing his own capital into it.\par \par \uc2 We`re seeing this in other properties. You sort of wonder the code names inside the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  for these properties, like money pit number 1, money pit number 2. Even Doral, he`s lost millions and millions of dollars, he`s had to put in hundreds of millions of dollars in his own capital to keep it going, that`s just one example.\par \par \uc2 These businesses are under a lot of pressure. We`ve had visibility in his taxes up until 2018, so we saw 20 years of personal and corporate tax returns. We haven`t seen the last two. But I can imagine what`s there.\par \par \uc2 He`s gone through COVID, he runs a very hospitality-centric business. Today with these numbers, we got a sense that it`s not getting better. The old post office is a flagship property of his and it`s not getting better.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Now, presumably it`s not a crime to lie to the public about your financial status or to brag about money that you say is coming in that`s not really coming in. But if you lie about your financial status in order to get a lease from the federal government, if you lie about the income that you say you`re getting from property like this when in fact you`re dumping tens of millions of dollars into the property, not getting any of it out, if you lie about those things on an ethics disclosure as a public official, presumably there could potentially are problems for the president here.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG: There could be. There could be. And -- and there`s a lot of public documents out there now that we`ve seen because he`s had to release them as part of his obligation when he ran for president and what he calls -- or what we call income, he would call revenue. He was putting in a very high figure that didn`t -- that didn`t account for any expenses, for example, that he might incur.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, we`ve seen that, we know that -- we`ve been able to match up his taxes. We know that trick he does just to make it look better than it is.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Susanne, one of the more baffling revelations out of these House oversight disclosures today was I think this information about how \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  appears to have done Trump a multimillion dollar favor while he was president, letting him delay the repayment of this loan that he took to renovate the hotel. Those payments, he was supposed to start making them in 2018. They apparently gave him a deferment on those payments for six years which had the effect of letting him keep tens of millions of dollars in his pocket that he otherwise would have had to lay out.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of that, how do you see that?\par \par \uc2 CRAIG: I think on its face it looks like potentially that the terms of the loan could have been changed. There was a lot of pushback today from \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  on that. And another theory could have been simply that the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  filed something potentially incorrect before and then corrected it and the loan actually didn`t change.\par \par \uc2 The problem with these things, and it`s so frustrating, you can imagine, it`s just -- we don`t know, because there`s just so little disclosure about this. It`s maddening, because \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  is saying, well, there`s a lot of things wrong, but won`t tell us what. Same with the Trump organization. You know, if this loan was changed, I think we should know about it. It`s a foreign bank involved in, you know, a huge loan to the president.\par \par \uc2 And these loans, he`s going to have hundreds of millions of dollars, 400 plus million dollars of loans coming due in the next several years that he has personally guaranteed. $300 million of that is owed to \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 . This is a fellow who is now -- his organization has been indicted.\par \par \uc2 Once that happens, there`s collateral consequence. He may not be able to -- \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  has indicated that they don`t want to do business with him anymore. But he may not be able to get any bank to do business with him because of this indictment.\par \par \uc2 This is serious stuff. The disclosures matter. As you can tell, I`m frustrated by it because we don`t know what`s wrong or, you know, all these things are being said, and it gets frustrating to watch.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Well, it`s frustrating but it`s also of huge public consequence, particularly if he`s going to run for president again, the idea that somebody would be running for president while owing hundreds of millions of dollars to mostly foreign entities with no evident way to pay it back, and it all coming due, like that`s not somebody who you would want in charge of parking meters in your little town let alone a major country.\par \par \uc2 [21:45:16]\par \par \uc2 CRAIG: There`s one way you can stave off the loss, you can start selling assets, and we`ve heard the old post office is for sale. But these are things -- yeah, he`s running for president once again, his finances to most of the world are not clear. And you have these things happen today where something pretty serious was alleged about \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 , that they had secretly changed the terms of a loan to a sitting president that was a great deal to him.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, and then they`re all saying, it`s not right, but they won`t tell us what`s not right about it.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Yeah, it can`t stand. This is -- this is -- this has to be unsustainable. Susanne Craig, investigative reporter --\par \par \uc2 CRAIG: Multiply the Old Post office where we actually have a window, because the government is the landlord, multiply that 40 or 50 or however many things he owns. It`s not that many, but go one after the other, and you incur the same problem, the same losses and the same opaqueness for most people.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately I`ve seen the tax returns, we`ve reported on it, we`ve done a lot of work on it. I think people deserve better from this from president, they deserve disclosure on his finances so we can understand the pressures that the sitting president is under.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: The pressures that he`s under and the potential blackmail that he`s open to, yes.\par \par \uc2 Susanne Craig, investigative reporter for "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " -- Susanne, thank you for being here, much appreciate it. It`s good to see you.\par \par \uc2 Since we`ve been on the air in this segment, I have to tell you we have breaking news to get to. Just moments ago, as I mentioned at the top of the show, just moments ago the Fifth U.S. Circuit Court of Appeals has reinstated Texas` abortion ban. Now, as I mentioned at the top of the show, we knew there was a decent chance that conservative federal appeals court would do that. Now it has. We`ll have more on what that means coming up.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [21:51:19]\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: So we do have breaking news tonight. Now, I mentioned at the top of the show we were watching for this and that this might be happening. It has, in fact, how happened.\par \par \uc2 The way the United States federal court system works, you got the Supreme Court on top. You got circuit courts, appeals courts below that and district courts below that. The Fifth Circuit Court is the most conservative circuit court in the country. The Fifth Circuit includes Texas.\par \par \uc2 The state of Texas today appealed to that Fifth U.S. Circuit Court of Appeals asking them basically to reinstate Texas`s abortion ban. The Fifth Circuit has now said yes. That means Texas`s draconian abortion ban is as of right now back into effect.\par \par \uc2 This is a law that was passed by Texas earlier this year. It outlaws abortion after six weeks, a point which most women wouldn`t know they were pregnant. The \uc2 U.S. Justice Department\uc2  had sued Texas to block this law after the Supreme Court allowed it to go into effect. The Justice Department asked a federal judge in Texas to block the law while the justice department`s case could be heard in court. That Texas judge this week agreed to block the law in a 113-page blistering ruling, putting the abortion ban on ice.\par \par \uc2 But tonight, the Appeals Court, the fifth U.S. Circuit Court of Appeals has undone that and put law back into effect. The order tonight gives the Justice Department the chance to reply by Tuesday, that means the Texas abortion ban is now in effect, at least until Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 There are options for the \uc2 U.S. Justice Department\uc2 , including potentially appealing directly to the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 , although nobody thinks they`re going to be a particularly hospitable venue for anything related to abortion rights with this Constitution of the court right now.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now on the phone to react to this news is Molly Duane. She`s senior staff attorney at the \uc2 Center for Reproductive Rights\uc2 . They filed their own lawsuit to block the Texas ban.\par \par \uc2 Molly, thanks for joining. I know it`s short notice to get you. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MOLLY DUANE, \uc2 CENTER FOR REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS\uc2  (via telephone): Thanks for having me, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: So I`m explaining this sort of ad-libbing, explaining this as a layman, how I observe this. Let me ask you if any of that I explained seems wrong to you or this is basically how this has happened?\par \par \uc2 DUANE: No, you explained it correctly, unfortunately.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Does this mean that Texas`s abortion ban is in effect as of now, as of this hour?\par \par \uc2 DUANE: That`s exactly what it means. And so, you know, what we saw was judge pitman issue a thoroughly reasoned 113-page order laying out clearly why this law was unconstitutional and needed to be enjoined immediately. And what we saw was providers across the state resume services. And now what we know and while it is expected, it is still disappointing, but what we know now is that the law is back in effect.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: For women in the state of Texas who may have gotten an abortion while the law was temporarily on ice while providers were temporarily, you know, day and a half, two days able to start providing services again, are they at risk for retroactively being sued, retroactively being pursued for having provided abortions or helped women get abortions during that brief period when that ban was enjoined?\par \par \uc2 DUANE: Yes. The providers and people who assisted those providers in providing pregnant women and pregnant patients with critical abortion care during those two days were fully aware of the risks that they were taking. But they took oaths to their patients, to their field of medicine to provide that care, and so they provided that care, consistent with the court`s order.\par \par \uc2 [21:55:09]\par \par \uc2 And so, what happens next is as good -- is up to really what happens in the state of Texas. But what we know is that our attorneys will be standing behind our providers, as we have for decades, and behind the pregnant people of Texas who are harmed every day this law is in effect.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Molly, in terms of the Justice Department`s options here, obviously, this was the U.S. Justice Department`s case against Texas which resulted in judge pitman`s ruling that put the ban on ice for a couple of days. It`s back in effect. Does the Justice Department have any other option rather than waiting until the end of the day on Tuesday to file its own papers with the Fifth Circuit and seeing things through? Or could they leapfrog this right to the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  and try to resolve this there instead?\par \par \uc2 DUANE: Based on the order we just received from the Fifth Circuit, yes, the Department of Justice could go straight to the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 . That is what we did a couple weeks ago when the exact same thing happened, so that is an option.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Would you advise them to do it? Do you think they ought to?\par \par \uc2 DUANE: Well, what I would advise them stood what they`re already doing, which is everything they can to protect the constitutional rights of pregnant people in Texas. And so I have communicated with them, obviously, and I urge them to continue in that fight. It`s far from over.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Molly Duane, senior staff attorney at the center for reproductive rights at the forefront of these legal battles, thanks for joining us on short notice. This is going to mean a lot of work for you tonight and in the days ahead. Thanks for helping us understand.\par \par \uc2 DUANE: Thanks, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Again, we`re covering the breaking news tonight that the Texas abortion ban having been put on advance with a federal court ruling a couple of days ago in Texas, the Texas abortion ban is back in effect with a ruling tonight from a very conservative Fifth Circuit Court of Appeals tonight. As you heard there from the senior staff attorney for the \uc2 Center for Reproductive Rights\uc2 , this is not the end, this is the beginning of this fight. But the ban is back in effect. Roe versus Wade is, in effect, dead in this country.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: That is going to do it for us for tonight. I`ll see you again on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Now, it`s time for "THE LAST WORD," where the great Zerlina Maxwell is in for Lawrence O`Donnell tonight.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Zerlina. It`s good to see you.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document TRMADS0020211009eha800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Last Word, October 8, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Zerlina Maxwell, Dr. Ebony Hilton, Brian Williams \par \uc2 7,216 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 October 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST: All right. That is going to do it for us for tonight. I`ll see you again on Monday. Now it`s time for THE LAST WORD where the great Zerlina Maxwell is in for Lawrence O`Donnell tonight. Good evening, Zerlina. It`s good to see you.\par \par \uc2 ZERLINA MAXWELL, MSNBC HOST: It`s good to see you, too. Thanks, Rachel. And this is such important breaking news about abortion. It reminds us how important it is to protect the federal bench. \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  prioritized it and progressives should think about doing that.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Exactly. Seriously. Thanks, my friend.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: Thank you. We`re going to be talking to Congresswoman Barbara Lee momentarily about that breaking news. But I want to begin tonight by talking about where we`re at. We`re at an inflection point because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  might be gone, but the stress test he`s putting our democratic system through is still very much in progress.\par \par \uc2 And what we do right now in this moment will have a lasting impact on our democracy. And it will dictate whether our democracy survives. You think I`m being melodramatic? Trust me, I am not melodramatic. Ask yourself this question. When else in our history has one party been so concerned only about winning and completely consumed with one single man`s ego?\par \par \uc2 So much so that they`ve been willing to lie, cheat, and steal just to stay in power. That`s the Republican Party in 2021. Sure, politicians in both parties have been corrupt before, but never have we seen an entire political party bend to the corruption.\par \par \uc2 So what we do now matters to our democracy in the long run. More specifically, what Democrats do now matters. And that`s why it`s so refreshing to see them stand up and have the power to stand up. Today, the democratic effort to protect the rule of law landed on President Biden himself.\par \par \uc2 The Biden White House formally blocked an absurd attempt by former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to withhold documents from Congress about the January 6th attack. Congress could soon have access to a trove of documents from the National Archives related to the Capitol insurrection. And that`s only because there`s a Democrat in the White House.\par \par \uc2 You better believe \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would have blocked those documents if he was still the president. And it`s only because Democrats have the majority in the Se0nate that we learned new details about Trump`s relentless and shameless crusade to bully the Justice Department into helping him overturn the 2020 election results.\par \par \uc2 And it`s only because Democrats have a majority in the House that we just learned that Trump lied to hide his corruption involving his hotel in Washington, D.C. Today, the Democrats on the House Oversight Committee uncovered that while Trump was in office, his hotel received $3.7 million in payments from foreign governments, which means \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  possibly violated the law and illegally profited off of the presidency.\par \par \uc2 In this moment, the survival of our democracy depends on whether or not we hold officials to account for a corrupt and anti-Democratic behavior. No one is above the law, not even a former president. It would be awesome if members of both political parties wanted to do this.\par \par \uc2 But aside from \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  and \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2 , Democrats are out here all by their selves. The rest of the Republican Party is either looking for another way of actively blocking attempts to save our democracy, like how \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  directed former officials to not comply with subpoenas from the January 6th select committee, a request that \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  complied with.\par \par \uc2 Where`s the GOP outrage that subpoenas are being ignored? Where is the GOP outrage that their president was actively working to use the levers of government to overthrow the election that he lost? Where`s the GOP outrage that a president might have illegally profited from the presidency?\par \par \uc2 [22:04:58]\par \par \uc2 Where is the GOP that was all about law and order? Let`s remember that if we survive this moment and if our democracy survives, it`s because Democrats did the hard and necessary work to save it. And the Republicans? Forget good government. Even basic democratic government does not seem to be a party priority after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Leading off our discussion tonight is \uc2 Steve Schmidt\uc2 . A former Republican strategist, he is the co-founder of the Lincoln Project. And Steve, the Trump hotel story is really the canary in the coal mine. It`s one of those stories that comes back around to the beginning. And you can`t be a little pregnant and you can`t be a little anti-democratic.\par \par \uc2 There is no country on earth where a strongman or a dictator oversees a fair law-abiding society. So, why don`t Republicans see how dangerous this is for all of us?\par \par \uc2 STEVE SCHMIDT\uc2 , FORMER REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST: Well, I think that we crossed that bridge five years ago, Zerlina. We have a Republican Party that by order of magnitude is more radical and more extreme than it was on the day of the insurrection. I think it bears mentioning that in the hours afterwards, even Kevin McCarthy expressed regret, condemned Trump`s actions, assigned blame to him.\par \par \uc2 He wavered momentarily, but very quickly got his sturdy sea legs under him. And so whether it`s the hundreds of pieces of legislation that seek to restrict voting rights, even more worrying than that, to nullify the results of valid elections at the whim of Republican legislatures, all of this is aimed at the next election at this point, not last one.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the Republican front-runner. He`s the presumptive nominee of the party. We see the intimations of violence being mainstreamed in the party. And so when we look at all of it as we come up on the year anniversary of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  being elected the 46th president of the United States, we have one of the two major political parties, the third oldest in the world that has given over -- given itself over completely to this autocratic project.\par \par \uc2 And in doing so, has broken faith. He`s broken the compact that we Americans have had with each other since the beginning about how we share power.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: One of the things I`ve been thinking a lot about is, you know, we`ve been talking a lot this year about the filibuster and Senate tradition. But what about the tradition of our democratic system? What have Republicans done to protect our democracy? They`re standing in the way saying they care about the filibuster and Senate tradition, but what about American tradition and our democracy?\par \par \uc2 SCHMIDT: I think the -- I think the question, Zerlina, is what have the Democrats done to harden the infrastructure of American democracy? It was self-evident that the filibuster was going to have to go when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  took the oath of office, at least as it pertained to all of these voting rights bills that are being passed all over the country.\par \par \uc2 On any one of a number of issues, what ethics laws have been hardened? What has been strengthened? What has been confronted? What has been dealt with? When you look at this honestly, Republicans have had a very, very good year.\par \par \uc2 They have been able to whitewash the insurrection. They have convinced 80 percent of the members of their party that the election was stolen and have turned a huge segment of the country against democracy. Huge segment of the country is fully invested in an autocratic movement, in a political extremism, and it has not been met.\par \par \uc2 Winston Churchill before the Second World War said this. He said the malice of the wicked was aided by the weakness of the virtuous. And at the end of the day, life isn`t fair and it`s not fair for the Democratic Party in this moment.\par \par \uc2 They have to govern. They have a lot of responsibility, but there`s no higher priority and responsibility than protecting democracy. And the reality is, after someone tells you who they are for the 10,000th time and shows you that they`re operating towards you with malice and bad intent, take them seriously.\par \par \uc2 And the simple fact is, the Democratic Party has been reluctant to exercise all of the tools in the kit, to exercise all of the power at its disposal, and the result of that is that this autocratic movement has gotten worse, not better, over the course of the last year when we would have hoped that in the shock of the aftermath of all of it that it would have receded.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: Well, we will see if Democrats heed your message and certainly we`ll see if \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  is listening to the words -- your words tonight. \uc2 Steve Schmidt\uc2 , thank you so much for joining us tonight. And joining us now is Democratic Congressman \uc2 Eric Swalwell\uc2  of California. He served as a House Impeachment Manager in the second impeachment trial of President Trump, not to be confused with the first impeachment trial of President Trump. And he`s a member of the House Intelligence and Judiciary Committees.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Swalwell, do you agree with Steve that the Democrats need to use all the levers of power to do everything they can to protect our democracy? Essentially, I think this moment is an inflection point, as I said in the open, and a stress test for the other branches of government.\par \par \uc2 REP. ERIC SWALWELL\uc2  (D-CA): Absolutely, Zerlina. And right now we are squarely in extra innings. We thought -- and I`ll use that analogy as the Giants are beating the Dodgers right now here in the Bay Area. But we thought that the 2020 election would resolve this matchup between Trumpism and democracy. Instead, we are in extra innings and everything matters.\par \par \uc2 And making sure we protect access and voter rights at the polls that matters. Making sure we understand what happened on January 6th so that no candidate can ever aim a mob violently at the capitol. That matters. And those have to be the priority. And I cannot stress enough to our president that getting the Senate to break the filibuster to pass voting rights, we have to do that in extra innings.\par \par \uc2 Getting this January 6th commission to press these subpoenas, even if it means criminal contempt, we have to do that because if we lose, we don`t just lose an election, we lose everything.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: And when you think about all of the different investigations that are out there, obviously we got new information today from the house oversight committee as well that found that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  reported his hotel in downtown D.C. brought in $150 million in income while he served as president. But the hotel actually incurred more than $70 million in losses.\par \par \uc2 And this was an issue that came up during the campaign, in the beginning of his presidency in terms of potentially profiting off of the presidency. He also received in a postponement of payments from \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 , the permission to postpone a $170 million loan. What is wrong with this picture? This feels very much against what the founders had intended.\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL: That`s right, Zerlina. Even though \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is no longer in office, corruption, sexism, racism, misogyny, they`re still at play. And yes, they were manifested in \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as president, but there will be more competent, corrupt, sexist, racist, misogynistic president who could take the Oval Office.\par \par \uc2 And if we don`t learn from what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did, it`ll be much worse. And so Adam Schiff`s legislation, the Protecting our Democracy Act actually go a long way to preventing what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was able to do financially, benefiting from being president while he was in the Oval Office, having foreign actors stay at his hotels. So, there`s an urgency here. We can`t just say, well, that`s the past, we should move on, because a more corrupt, competent president could come into the Oval Office.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: What do you think your House colleagues on the Republican side would say if President \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  profited off the presidency in a hypothetical world?\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL: That`s right. There would be endless hearings after hearings. We`d never stop hearing about it. And so that`s why I think it`s actually in Republicans` interest to join us in making sure that we have more good government legislation in place. If they have any concerns about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , I certainly don`t. I think he`s one of the most honest persons to be president.\par \par \uc2 But if they have concerns and want to hold him accountable, well, this is not aimed at \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  anymore. It`s aimed at the current occupant and future presidents. So join us and make it bipartisan in our interests to make sure you no longer have a corrupt president benefiting from the Oval Office, not only personally, financially, and politically, but also for what it means in their ability, as I said, to try and aim a mob at the capitol to hold on to power if they were to lose an election.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: And to that point about the insurrection, the January 6th committee has sent out many subpoenas. \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  defying the subpoena. I mean, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  and \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2  are the two Republicans on that committee, so it`s bipartisan. What do Americans need to know about the rationale behind \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  defying the subpoena and Donald Trump`s efforts to use executive privilege in a moment where a bipartisan committee is trying to prevent another attack on the capitol?\par \par \uc2 [22:15:02]\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL: If he was innocent, he would cooperate. The reason he`s not cooperating is because he does not have an innocent explanation for his conduct that day, plain and simple. It`s a consciousness of guilt. And Americans need to know that Republicans have to own the two deadly lies they continue to spin, the lie that the election was stolen that continues to lead to political violence in this country, and the lies about the vaccines that make our children vulnerable to getting sick where 700,000 Americans have died.\par \par \uc2 Two deadly lies are what the Republicans must own, and that`s what`s at stake and it`s on us to make sure Americans know when they go to the ballot box next November that those two deadly lines, if we give them the right to govern, will continue to make us vulnerable at the ballot box where we could lose our democracy and at hospital beds where we could lose family members.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: Congressman \uc2 Eric Swalwell\uc2 , thank you for joining us tonight on this Friday night and enjoy the game, the rest of the game. Good luck.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, breaking news, Texas extreme anti-abortion law is back in effect tonight after an appeals court stay. We`ll have more on that after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:20:00]\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: Breaking tonight, the Fifth Circuit Court of Appeals has allowed Texas to temporarily resume banning most abortions just one day clinics across the state began rushing to serve patients again for the first time since early September. On Wednesday, U.S. District court judge Robert Pitman issued an order suspending the Texas law, calling it an offensive deprivation of constitutional rights of women to have abortions.\par \par \uc2 But tonight, the appeals court quickly granted Texas` request to set aside Judge Pitman`s order for now while the case is reviewed. And joining us now is Democratic Congresswoman Barbara Lee of California. And I just want to get your reaction to this breaking news. You testified recently about your own experiences. What is your reaction to the latest out of Texas?\par \par \uc2 REP. BARBARA LEE (D-CA): Well, I`m glad to be with you tonight. Sorry about this horrible news that we have just learned. Let me tell you, women`s lives are at stake. And, you know, women, people all over the country are sick and tired of our lives being in jeopardy in so many ways.\par \par \uc2 And it almost feels like the courts and the state laws are just playing games with us. These are our bodies, our lives, and we have the right to make our own personal decisions about our own reproductive health care, including abortions.\par \par \uc2 And so forth for these courts to continue to put women in jeopardy and to really force them to scramble to who knows what will take place next in terms of what they do when in fact they want to and have made a decision to have an abortion.\par \par \uc2 Finally, I`ll just say, yes, I remember the days of back-alley abortions. I always said last week, you know, at age 16, I had to go to Mexico. Fortunately, it was a clinic and compassionate and competent doctor, but so many women died during that period and now people are going to die again to find new ways of trying to have abortions if in fact these laws are not taken off the books and the courts need to be on the side of people everywhere and on the side of the constitution.\par \par \uc2 So, this is a very, really, dark moment, I think, for people in the country and for our constitutional rights.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: I think it`s so important for people to understand what it was like before Roe v. Wade and that`s why your testimony about the pre-Roe realities was so, so important to this conversation. You know, what do you want voters out there to do in this moment, the Women`s Health Protection Act passed the House of Representatives, but, as we know, it`s held up the same way all the other bills are held up in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 So going forward, you know, in the state of Texas, women are not able to get abortions, but as soon as the Supreme Court decides, there are trigger laws that will affect about a dozen other states. So, what is the recourse in this moment for voters who support abortion rights?\par \par \uc2 LEE: Well, first, we`ve got to get -- you know, make sure the senators understand that they need to support the Women`s Health Protection Act. And this involves, Zerlina, political action. This result (ph) of grassroots action. This involves making sure that we hold members of Congress, both House and Senate accountable to people who deserve to be able to exercise their constitutional rights.\par \par \uc2 What they`re trying to do is turn the clocks back to the days that I know so well, that so many people know so well where women died. And in fact, we have to move forward and make sure we hold the elected officials accountable. And in these states we need to elect members of state houses who are going to support reproductive freedom and we need to make sure that those who don`t are not re-elected.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this boils down to a political struggle. We`re in a war. This is a fight for our lives. This is a fight for our freedom. And they`re playing with our lives. They`re playing with our bodies. And I am telling you, people are going to die if these states continue with what they`re doing and if these courts continue making these decisions against people`s reproductive freedom, their constitutional rights.\par \par \uc2 And finally let me just say it`s your personal decision. And as I said in my testimony, it`s nobody`s darn business what I do with my body. The only reason I came forward is because I want people to understand what it was like before Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:00]\par \par \uc2 And to know that I shouldn`t have to even slip there and talk about it. Nobody should`ve to do that. But I was compelled to do it because I`m sounding the alarm on what can happen. So many women died. That was the primary reason for the deaths of African-American women, septic abortions, during days. So, this is a really sad and dangerous moment, but it better compel us to engage in political action like we`ve never done before.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: Before it is breaking news, we had scheduled you to talk about the Build Back Better agenda. And one of the things I think is related is one fact and one question, which is a majority of women who have abortions in this country already have children. One of the central components of the Biden agenda is child care.\par \par \uc2 Child care is central to the Biden agenda. Talk about why it`s so important for families to have affordable child care, especially since in Texas and other places you don`t have access to abortion that you may need because it`s an economic consideration.\par \par \uc2 LEE: Sure. And then they try to deny us access to contraception also and family planning and comprehensive sexual education in our schools. What the heck do they want? I mean, this is crazy. It doesn`t make any sense. And so we just got to make sure that as we move forward we insist that people are respected and that our rights are respected and that election officials are not going to destroy our lives.\par \par \uc2 And that`s what they`re trying to do. And when you look at our Build Back Better bill, yes, child care, women want to work. Black and brown women especially are disproportionately low-wage workers. Child care is expensive. And so why in the world are those who are trying to stop our reproductive freedom and our rights to have an abortion, why are they now denying us access to good quality child care and trying to stop the Build Back Better bill.\par \par \uc2 They don`t want us to have child care either. They don`t want women to get back into the workforce. I mean, come on. This has got to go. This is an inflection moment. This is the time when we have to fight on all fronts for our rights on so many efforts -- in so many efforts.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: Congresswoman Barbara Lee, it`s been great to have you tonight. Thank you so much for joining us and please stay safe.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, one Democrat has an idea of how to beat back the Trump insurrectionists. This ad is already a candidate for the best political ad of the 2022 campaign. We`ll show it to you next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:31:47]\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: In just three days, a North Carolina congressional candidate`s campaign ad has been viewed by more than 5 million people. In the ad, North Carolina State Representative Charles Graham, a Democrat, describes what happened when his hometown came together to stand up against hate and what good citizens can do today in the wake of the attack on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STATE REP. CHARLES GRAHAM (D-NC): When I was a young boy, the KKK announced a night rally in my home county. A cross burning with hundreds of Klansmen to terrorize the blacks and Lumbee (ph). We were a poor farming community - - black, white, and Indian. My parents and grandparents were sharecroppers like many.\par \par \uc2 The police chief warned the Grand Dragon, these people don`t want your trouble. The Klansmen called us mongrels, half breeds, and told him the Klan would him how to handle people like us.\par \par \uc2 That night they rolled with their cars, their crosses, and a single light bulb hooked to a car battery. 50 Klansmen, not a bad turnout on a cold night. The problem is, they were surrounded by 400 Lumbees.\par \par \uc2 Simeone Oxendine had been a tail gunner in a B-29 during the war. Verdia Locklear was four months pregnant. Neil Lowery was the local barber. Hundreds of normal folks deciding to stand together against ignorance and hate.\par \par \uc2 Lowery shut out the light. The Klansmen scattered. By the time the sheriff arrived to fish them out of the swamp, the press was running with the story. The battle of Hayes Pond where one town beat the Klan.\par \par \uc2 A piece of forgotten history worth remembering, especially today. In Washington, lies turn to violence, and the biggest lie is that America is at war with itself. That you can`t trust your neighbor. That they want something that`s yours. That you must live in fear of them.\par \par \uc2 But the people who stood up at Hayes Pond refused to be afraid. I grew up with their story. And the lesson is, human dignity is a human right.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: Joining us now is State Representative Charles Graham, a Democrat from North Carolina. He`s running for congress in North Carolina`s 9th district.\par \par \uc2 So your ad clearly hit home with people who watched it. It`s super cool to see that part of American history, especially for those who hadn`t heard it. What do you want voters to take away from that ad? What does a Hayes Pond response look like in today`s very divided political climate?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Well, thank you Zerlina, for having me on your program tonight. First of all, let me say I`m a proud Lumbee Indian and the son of sharecroppers. I would be the first American Indian elected to congressman of North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 [22:34:42]\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Now let me tell you about the story of Hayes Pond. This story is about my community, a community that came together in 1958 to fight hatred and bigotry.\par \par \uc2 And what we`re seeing today is the same fight like on January 6th. The only difference is the insurrectionists were not wearing sheets. My representation will be about unifying our country against bigotry and hatred and in my congressional district. My campaign will be about working and promoting working families and supporting businesses.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: In terms of the divided nation and the way Hayes Pond can be a lesson, I think one of the lessons is evolution and evolving on certain issues. You`re running against Republican Representative \uc2 Dan Bishop\uc2  who sponsored this so-called Bathroom Bill back in 2016. That`s the bill that prevented trans individuals from using the restroom of the gender that they identified with.\par \par \uc2 You voted for that bill. This week you apologized for that vote. Is that an example of the possibility of evolution on issues and why do you think that was important for you to do?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Well, you know, part of that evolution is, Zerlina, that I voted to repeal that bill. You know, the HB2 bill after the fact, after I had cast that vote, I realized that it was hurtful. It was hurtful to the transgender community. And that is exactly why I worked hard to repeal HB2.\par \par \uc2 I am an indigenous person. I`m an American Indian. As I was growing up in my developmental years I confronted discrimination, as a young man who enjoyed playing sports, baseball in particular. As I played and most of my teammates during that summer, I can remember it very vividly. They were Caucasian, and what those young men did during that time -- and I was the only person of color on that team. They shielded me from discrimination. They protected me from discrimination coming out of the stands. And I will never forget that.\par \par \uc2 It was as if there was just recently that those folks were screaming racist remarks to me, against me, and that`s why I`m running for Congress as someone who will unite and not divide. But, you know, of course I`m proud of where I`m at today on HB2.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: In terms of your particular race, your district is actually a district that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won. How do you think you can turn that around? What message are you going to bring to the campaign trail in 2022 that will attract that voter that came out in 2020 and supported \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Yes. Thank you. That`s a good question, Zerlina.\par \par \uc2 You know, one of the things that I`ve been successful at -- and I`ve served in General Assembly of North Carolina. This is my sixth term. I`ve been able to get out and work in a primarily minority House district.\par \par \uc2 This past election I was the only Democrat from all the candidates -- state, national, that was re-elected in my county. My message has always been that I will stand strong and listen to my constituents. And that has been my motto throughout my tenure in the North Carolina General Assembly.\par \par \uc2 And I just want folks, whether it`s the 9th congressional district or any other district, to know I will be a representative of the people, and that`s where our campaign is going to produce. I`m going to put ideas out there that I think are uniting our communities.\par \par \uc2 For example, my opponent, if it`s congressional district 9, did not support the (INAUDIBLE) legislation. One of the things that`s important to my community and important to rural North Carolina, Zerlina, is broadband. He voted against that.\par \par \uc2 So my message is going to be, throughout the campaign is, I`m going to compare and contrast myself to what we can have as a congressional member to what we`re getting right now, and that is not very much. And that will be my message throughout the campaign. And I am very proud of that.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: I can`t overstate the need for broadband expansion in rural areas.\par \par \uc2 North Carolina State Representative Charles Graham, thank you so much for being here tonight and please stay safe.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:58]\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: Coming up, Senator \uc2 Chuck Grassley\uc2  is going to rally with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tomorrow in Iowa after putting his name on a Republican report yesterday that made the claim \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did not exert improper influence over the Justice Department after the November election.\par \par \uc2 How did the state that launched Barack Obama`s presidency in 2008 become Trump country? And what can Democrats do to win it back? That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will hold a rally in Des Moines, Iowa tomorrow, fueling speculation about whether or not he`ll run for president in 2024. Iowa, of course, is the traditional launch pad for the presidential primaries of both political parties.\par \par \uc2 [22:44:44]\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: Joining Trump at his rally will be Iowa Senator \uc2 Chuck Grassley\uc2 , rankings member of the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  who just yesterday signed a report excusing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for his attempts to overturn the election and subvert the will of American voters. Trump`s visit to Iowa comes on the heels of positive polling in the state.\par \par \uc2 The latest Des Moines Register/Mediacom Iowa poll shows 53 percent of Iowans, including 91 percent of Republicans have a favorable view of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . 45 percent have an unfavorable view. Democratic Party leaders in Iowa are critical of Republican support for Trump`s visit with Iowa Democratic Party chairman Ross Wilburn saying at a news conference, "For now we`re just focusing on reminding Iowans why \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not just bad for Iowa, but he`s toxic for our democracy. And it`s been well established that he did nothing to stop his supporters from violently attacking our capitol, even while his own vice president was inside."\par \par \uc2 So what will it take for Democrats to reclaim the state that launched the presidency of \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  but voted overwhelmingly for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the last two election cycles?\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is former Democratic Congresswoman, \uc2 Abby Finkenauer\uc2 . She`s running for the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  in Iowa in 2022. And Republicans in Iowa may have a favorable view of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but actually that doesn`t mean they think he should run for president.\par \par \uc2 This is from "The Wall Street Journal". "My preference perchance that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has a role in the direction of our country and party and some influence over candidates, but I think it would be best for someone else to become the standard bearer." And that was Mark Lundberg a financial adviser and former Republican chairman in northwest Iowa`s Sioux City. And that`s one of the more conservative areas of the state.\par \par \uc2 I mean, he was twice impeached. Is he really, you know, a popular figure for president in Iowa now?\par \par \uc2 Abby Finkenauer\uc2  (D-IA), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: Look, I watch him and I know that Chuck Grassley tomorrow is going to go stand on a stage with the former president. This guy, who told his supporters on January 6th to march to the \uc2 Capitol\uc2  while they were wearing Trump hats, Trump T-shirts, and they beat our \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  officers with Trump flags and American flags.\par \par \uc2 And Chuck Grassley saw that. He saw that happen. Our governor saw that happen. And now tomorrow, they`re going to go stand on a stage with him and essentially endorse that behavior. It`s despicable. It is not who we are.\par \par \uc2 That is why this race is so dang important. I mean, I know Iowa. I grew up here. I know what we did in 2018 when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wasn`t on the ballot the last time. We flipped two congressional seats. We had three out of the four congressional seats that went to Democrats. Three out of the six statewide races went to Democrats, and the Republicans in Iowa know that they can`t win without \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And love it or hate it, he`s not on the ballot in 2022. And we`re going to make sure that we win this thing.\par \par \uc2 We need folks with us. Please go to Abbyfinkenauer.com and join us. And make sure we hold these folks accountable. They are afraid.\par \par \uc2 They know they`re vulnerable and they`re selling essentially democracy down the line because of the fact that they cannot stand up to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . The lies that led to that day and they`re now going to stand on stage next to him endorsing it.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: What do you think it is about the Iowa voter that leads them into Donald Trump`s camp twice? You grew up there. What happened between 2008 and 2016 and 2020?\par \par \uc2 FINKENAUER: Again, you know, 2020 was tough, but it was tough everywhere. In 2022 \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  isn`t on the ballot. In 2018 we flipped these seats. We were able to win in Iowa as Democrats, and we can do it again.\par \par \uc2 These folks are my friends and my family in Iowa. I know who we are. I`m somebody who grew up a daughter of a union pipe fitter welder. My mom was a public school employee. I`m a first-generation college grad here. And I know what it`s like to have people in Washington, D.C., look down their nose at you and think they`re better than you and go think that they know the policy that`s actually going to help move your life forward.\par \par \uc2 I am fighting for those people in Iowa. It is who I am. It is who I fight for, and it is how we`re going to win in 2022 because Chuck Grassley has gone to Washington, D.C. for nearly five decades and has become the Washington politician that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has told all his supporters to hate over these years.\par \par \uc2 He is in the pocket of big special interests like the pharmaceutical companies. And as I was in Washington, D.C. as a congresswoman passing historic prescription drug reform, Chuck Grassley was over in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  stuffing it.\par \par \uc2 This is the type of stuff where we can hold him accountable. We will win here, but we need folks with us as we do it.\par \par \uc2 [22:49:55]\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: \uc2 Abby Finkenauer\uc2 , candidate for the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  in 2022 in the state of Iowa, thank you so much for being here tonight. And please stay safe.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the vaccine for kids 5 to 12 could be available by Halloween. But there`s one thing doctors and parents should be doing right now before the shots are available.\par \par \uc2 We`ll talk about what that is, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: We`re ending on some good news tonight. \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  has officially asked the Biden administration to authorize the use of their COVID vaccine for children ages 5 to 11. And I know parents out there, it`s like music to their ears.\par \par \uc2 And while some of those parents are more than ready to set up vaccination appointments, surveys suggest a larger number still need maybe a little bit more convincing.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:46]\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: A \uc2 Kaiser Family Foundation\uc2  poll conducted last month found just 34 percent of parents of children between the ages of 5 and 11 years old are ready to vaccinate their child immediately after the vaccine is authorized. 32 percent want to wait and see. 24 percent are firmly opposed to their child getting the shot.\par \par \uc2 Last month in "The Washington Post" Dr. Rachel Pearson, a mother and pediatric hospitalist wrote about what she says to convince parents to vaccinate their children. She says in part, "My feelings are unhelpful, and beside the point. The point is that kind, consistent factual communication from physicians might help lift us from this nightmare. So instead of unloading my feelings or telling the stories of worst-case scenarios, I try to offer something useful. A conversation focused on this parent`s child, this specific child, who will probably be fine either way but who will enjoy a near 100 percent certainty of not being hospitalized with or dying of COVID if she gets the vaccine. The child`s flourishing is our shared goal always."\par \par \uc2 And joining us now are Dr. Rachel Pearson, a pediatrician in the Long Island Long School of Medicine and UT Health San Antonio and Dr. Ebony Hilton, MSNBC medical contributor and associate professor of anesthesiology and critical care medicine at the University of Virginia Healthcare Systems.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Pearson, I`ll start with you. Tell us what you see in the hospital when children get COVID-19.\par \par \uc2 DR. RACHEL PEARSON, PEDIATRICIAN: Thank you, Zerlina. I see a mix. I see little kids under the age of one who often come in because they`re having trouble breathing, and they need oxygen.\par \par \uc2 The other big group we see is teenagers, some of whom also have difficulty breathing and some of whom have severe complications from COVID.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: You said under the age of one. That`s a baby.\par \par \uc2 DR. PEARSON: That`s babies, yes.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: Ok.\par \par \uc2 DR. PEARSON: Can be as young as a few weeks old.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: Oh wow. So Dr. Pearson, just a follow up to that, are parents asking you already for the vaccine for those young children or any of the younger children that are coming in?\par \par \uc2 DR. PEARSON: Not yet. A lot of parents have questions about the vaccine in general, though. Most parents know that kids 12 and up can get vaccinated now. And like you talked about in that Kaiser report, a lot of parents have questions or worries.\par \par \uc2 They want to talk to a pediatrician about it. And they want to know, are there -- what are the side effects like? They really want to know more than anything else, is this right for my kid? And that is the question that we`re here to help you figure out.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: And Dr. Hilton, we`ve had conversations often on my Peacock show about how these conversations need to happen in communities of color. So what is the nuance here? How are these conversations happening in communities of color?\par \par \uc2 Because you`re not only going to have to convince the parents to get vaccinated who are hesitant, but also the children of those hesitant parents.\par \par \uc2 DR. EBONEY HILTON, MSNBC MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Right. I think what we have to do is, for one, we have to counteract the narrative that we push early in this pandemic that children were immune or some type of way protected from COVID-19.\par \par \uc2 What we know is that in the last four weeks alone, a million children were diagnosed with COVID-19. What we know is that although largely, kids are protected from dying from COVID-19, we do have individuals and babies like Mikaela (INAUDIBLE) in Mississippi, eighth grader, within days of starting school, who is now no longer with us. And that`s a tragedy that we should avoid at all costs because kids at large do not die in childhood, right.\par \par \uc2 So how do we approach this in a way that we`re making parents aware that at this point, six million children have been diagnosed with COVID-19 and we know there`s long standing impact of just infection alone, not just death.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: And just yes or no, you know, folks are saying the delta wave is over. Do kids still need to get this vaccine, Dr. Hilton?\par \par \uc2 DR. HILTON: The answer is yes. And the reason why is again, what we know is that delta will not be the last variant that we see.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: Right.\par \par \uc2 DR. HILTON: What we also know is that if we`re thinking about other countries, we have not done a great job of vaccinating globally. In fact at this point, for the poorest income countries, only 2.4 percent of those people in those countries have actually received one dose of the vaccine.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: Right.\par \par \uc2 DR. HILTON: So we have a ways to go. So the best thing that you can do is prepare your child for, if there is another variant that comes through that makes delta look weak, that their body is already prepared at day one to be able to fight.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Rachel Pearson, and Dr. Ebony Hilton, we`ll have to leave it there. Thank you so much for taking the time to join us on this Friday night.\par \par \uc2 [22:59:47]\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL: That is tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for watching. You can catch me every week night at 6:00 p.m. Eastern on The Choice from MSNBC exclusively on Peacock.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH BRIAN WILLIAMS" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN WILLIAMS, MSNBC HOST: Well, good evening once again.\par \par \uc2 Day 262 of the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020211009eha800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour with Brian Williams for October 08, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Brian Williams, Anne Thompson \par \uc2 7,722 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 October 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 BRIAN WILLIAMS, MSNBC HOST: Well, good evening once again, day 262 of the Biden administration. And there is more breaking news tonight out of the state of Texas on that state`s restrictive abortion law. The U.S. Fifth Circuit Court of Appeals tonight has temporarily reinstated the Texas six- week abortion ban after a federal judge with some ceremony blocked the ban on Wednesday. As the Fifth Circuit is known as one of the most conservative appeals courts in the country, the ruling means a ban on abortions after six weeks is now back in force. Part of this case that appears destined for a rendezvous with the Supreme Court. We`ll have much more on this story just ahead.\par \par \uc2 But first here tonight, the fight between Donald Trump and that congressional committee looking into the inner insurrection that he led is now heating up. And today the current president played a role in this fight.\par \par \uc2 NBC News first to report earlier today that the Biden White House has decided in fact, not to block the release of documents related to Trump, his presidency and the insurrection. The move sets up a legal and potentially lengthy showdown between two presidents current and former over the issue of executive privilege. Earlier today, White House Press Secretary Jen Psaki was asked about this decision.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI, WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The President has determined that an assertion of executive privilege is not warranted for the first set of documents from the Trump White House that had been provided to us by the National Archives. As we`ve said previously, this will be an ongoing process. And this is just the first set of documents, and we will evaluate questions of privilege on a case-by-case basis.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: This comes as the 1/6 Investigative Committee is considering its next moves now that yesterday`s deadline for four former Trump aides to provide documents to the committee has now passed.\par \par \uc2 Chairman, Congressman Bennie Thompson, Vice Chair, Liz Cheney released a statement today, threatening former Trump adviser Steve Bannon with criminal contempt while noting that other witnesses are cooperating, they say, "Mr. Bannon has indicated he will try to hide behind vague references to privileges of the former president. We will not allow any witness to defy a lawful subpoena or attempt to run out the clock and we will swiftly consider advancing a criminal contempt of Congress referral."\par \par \uc2 New York Times reports Bannon`s attorney sent a letter to the 1/6 committee saying he would not comply with the subpoena. The committee made no mention of former Trump aide Daniel Scavino in their statement, he held the title Deputy Chief of Staff while basically running Trump`s social media. He was one of the original groups subpoenaed and Politico reports he was served with his own just today.\par \par \uc2 As the investigation into 1/6 continues, the government has made public even more evidence of the brutality of the assault on our Capitol nine months ago. Dramatic video new to us released today shows rioters viciously assaulting police officers during that attack that we all watched unfold on live television.\par \par \uc2 There are also new developments on a separate congressional investigation into the former president. House Oversight Committee accusing Donald Trump of providing, "misleading information" about the financial state of his hotel in Washington while he was in office. The committee says Trump reported his D.C. hotel brought in $150 million during his time in the White House. But after reviewing documents, lawmakers say the hotel actually suffered over $70 million in losses.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  spokesperson called the assertions, "intentionally misleading and unequivocally false."\par \par \uc2 Also, this as the current president is trying to defend a disappointing September jobs report just out today, the economy added 194,000 jobs in the month of September. That`s a problem because it`s below the Dow Jones estimate of half a million new jobs. Earlier today, Biden offered this take on today`s numbers.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, U.S. PRESIDENT: In just eight months since I became President, in the midst of a grave public health and economic crisis, the unemployment rate is now down below 5% -- at 4.8%. Let me just repeat that, today`s report has the unemployment rate down to 4.8 percent, a significant improvement from when I took office and a sign that our recovery is moving forward even in the face of a COVID pandemic.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Our country also temporarily avoided an economic disaster this week when the Senate voted to lift the debt limit until December but, of course, in a few short months expect a whole new standoff.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:04]\par \par \uc2 Today Senate Majority Leader -- Minority Leader rather, Mitch McConnell sent Joe Biden a letter warning that Republicans won`t help raise the debt limit next time. It reads, "I will not be a party to any future effort to mitigate the consequences of democratic mismanagement. Your lieutenants on Capitol Hill now have the time they claim they lack to address the debt ceiling." So, we have that to look forward to, of course.\par \par \uc2 And with that, let`s bring in our leadoff guests, our starting line on this Friday night. Eugene Daniels, White House Correspondent for Politico co- author of each day`s edition of the Politico Playbook, Jeremy Peters, Veteran Political Reporter for The New York Times and Cynthia Alksne, former Federal Prosecutor in the Civil Rights Division of the DOJ.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, and welcome to you all, Cynthia, counselor, you get to go first tonight because of our lead story, what do we need to know about this Texas reversal on appeal and where this case might be headed?\par \par \uc2 CYNTHIA ALKSNE, FORMER FEDERAL PROSECUTOR: Well, the case is headed to the Supreme Court and it`s nothing but bad news for women of Texas and women everywhere. And then I hesitate to sum it up this way, in a colloquial way. But basically, you know, we`re screwed. That`s just true. The Fifth Circuit has reinstated this law. That is an overturning of Roe v. Wade, that takes away a woman`s right to control her own body two weeks after she`s missed a period. Most women don`t know they`re pregnant at that time. And now it`s up to the Fifth Circuit. People say, oh, not to worry, it can be appealed to the Supreme Court. Well, it can be appealed to the Supreme Court. But remember, it`s the Supreme Court that let this law go in to effect in the first place through a shadow docket late at night and without even mentioning Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court is extremely hostile to Roe v Wade. They have five votes to overturn it. They have a case in front of them the Mississippi Dub`s case, and it looks like they`re either going to overturn Roe v. Wade, or so restricted that none of us are going to recognize it anyway.\par \par \uc2 So, I see nothing but bad news. It`s infuriating. It`s infuriating to me that none of these judges either on the Fifth Circuit or on the Supreme Court are listening to stare decisis. And adhering to that basic principle. I find that infuriating, I also find it infuriating that the Congress has had 50 years to pass a woman`s right to control her own body, and they haven`t done it. And we`ve never been important enough to them. And it`s time to have a little bit of a revolution.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: All right, Eugene, those are sobering words. And as I don`t need to explain to you stare decisis used to be the bedrock of conservative jurisprudence, as we once knew it. So let me ask you if your reporting sheds any light on any reaction we might expect from DOJ.\par \par \uc2 EUGENE DANIELS, POLITICO WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Yeah, I mean, we`ve already seen the DOJ making very clear that they are going to protect a woman`s right to choose but excuse me protect their constitutional right to choose. And they`ve said that over and over again, I think you`re going to continue to see, and they`ve said that they will sue where they need to, they will go where they need to, to get this done and to do as much as they can to protect, but like was just said, you know, this is going to the Supreme Court and all everything, every single indicator tells us that it looks like Roe v. Wade, for the most part will be taken apart or go away. We will all be surprised if that is not the case, whether that`s Mississippi Dug, or through this through this Texas law. And I think that is something that has been really interesting to watch, you haven`t seen a lot politically, you haven`t seen a lot of Republicans support the Texas law. They were worried that the Texas style seemed a little silly. But it looks like thanks to the Fifth Circuit, that this law is one that is going to the Supreme Court and is going to stick around for some time. But the DOJ, like I said, has made it clear that they`re going to continue to do whatever they can, but how much they can actually do is what we`re what we`re lacking to see at this point.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Jeremy, you covered the Trump years and as Trump would say you covered them very strongly. I thought of you today, with Trump remaining on brand and effect telling his former aides not to rat and with Mr. Bannon a man you`ve spent a lot of time with remaining on brand, telling his followers Trump will still be reinstated after the election is decertified, perhaps even before 2022 that`s not a thing. But you have to admire everyone staying on brand, Jeremy.\par \par \uc2 JEREMY PETERS, THE NEW YORK TIMES POLITICAL REPORTER: Right and this, Brian, is exactly the ground that Trump and all of his followers want 2022 and 2024 to be litigated upon.\par \par \uc2 [23:10:08]\par \par \uc2 Trump wants the people who are in Congress after 2022 to be people who buy into his lie that the election was stolen. And he wants to run his 2024 campaign, if there is one, which, at this point, I think we would all be naive not to expect that that`s going to happen. He wants it to be all about the myth of this stolen election.\par \par \uc2 And what I think, you know, we`ve all seen a lot of, the more marginal figures in the Republican Party, Matt Gaetz, Marjorie Taylor Greene, Lauren Boebert, like all these people go along with this. What`s been more interesting to watch are the mainstream Republicans who are sitting -- not exactly saying they think the election was stolen, but saying with a wink and a nod, that they think that there is election fraud, and that there`s ought to be something done about it, even though there`s no evidence of that. There`s no evidence of it on a widespread systemic basis. But they`re saying, oh, OK, well, the Democrats sent out all of these ballots in the mail that no one asked for, and we don`t know what happened to these ballots. And that is the way, if I were advising people to look for ways in which the Republican Party is mainstreaming fringe attacks that`s what I would look at. Because they are basically saying that the election is stolen without saying the election is stolen. And that is going to be our political reality for the next two or three years.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Cynthia, back over to you, once and for all, is there any scenario under which a former president in a non-national security area can declare privilege over documents once they`ve left office?\par \par \uc2 ALKSNE: We`re sure he can declare it, but that doesn`t mean it`s enforceable. I mean, what`s going to happen today, what`s happening now is that Biden is said, I`m going to release these documents. And then under the law, he has 30 days, Trump has 30 days to complain. And if he doesn`t do anything, the documents will be released, what`s likely to happen is on about day 29 and a half, he`s going to file a lawsuit and gum up the process. Because Trump`s whole game, it doesn`t matter if it`s -- if he`s legally correct, it only matters to him that he can slow the process down and slow walk the process, because after all, that`s really worked for him. And it worked for him and impeachment by stalling and not getting witnesses. It`s worked for him with his taxes. And that`s what Bannon is counting on that it`s going to work for him with the subpoena process. So no, he`s not going to win the argument. But yes, he is going to try and make it to slow the process down.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: And Eugene, back to your beat, was it regarded as pro forma or a big deal that Biden decided the way he did on this tranche of documents?\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: I mean, I think it was unsurprising, right? We expect as things move on that the Biden ministration is not likely to exert executive privilege for the former president and for his aids. But what they continue to say is this is case-by-case basis. I think part of that is to make sure that when it`s time for the Biden administration to assert executive privilege, over their own things they did, they haven`t really -- you know, they haven`t softened, what that means. But this White House has a vested interest in helping the January 6, select committee figure out what happened that day, not just because they`re the campaign that turned into a White House that Donald Trump and his allies say stole the White House, which they did not they won the election, but because they see it as bigger than them, they see it as bigger than the election, they see it as, you know, protecting democracy. And that`s something that you heard from Jen Psaki today. They are concerned privately publicly about what January 6 meant for that day.\par \par \uc2 And so, what`s really interesting is when you talk to folks inside, they are the last line, right, of whether or not we`ll see some of these documents. And what this administration experts believe, and it seems to be very, very true, is that executive privilege lies in the office and that is something that a lot of people learn today, including Donald Trump. It`s not what the man, right, leaves office, the executive privilege stays behind. And so, by him asserting something that he thinks he has, that`s not true. It`s why this administration had to refuse, who had that to assert, excuse me, had to refuse this privilege this time around on behalf of Donald Trump, right, because it lies in the office and I think that is something that Trump wasn`t expecting, he wasn`t expecting, he expects that he has that privilege for as long as he lives as the former president, but that is not the case and the Biden administration made that very clear today.\par \par \uc2 [23:15:10]\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Hey, Jeremy, take us back to Republican politics. There is a term for what Trump is doing to all the "would be" presidential candidates in his party, but we`re a family broadcast and can`t use said term, but we both know what it is. And it reduces all these potential candidates to the bootlicking role. For some of them, they`re in their fifth year of it. Talk about what this is doing to the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 PETERS: So as Donald Trump goes to Iowa tomorrow afternoon to campaign in a rally, one of many rallies that he`s been holding over the course of this past six months, he is basically daring any Republican who wants to be president to challenge him. And so far, they`ve all blinked. You have Nikki Haley, who gave a speech earlier this week at the Reagan Library, which the theme was ostensibly the future of the Republican Party. And she spent most of it talking as if she were a Reagan Republican trapped in 1984, talking about socialism and capitalism and communism. And, you know, it sounded as if she was speaking from a Republican Party that doesn`t exist anymore, and it doesn`t.\par \par \uc2 This is Donald Trump`s Republican Party. And there is, frankly, Brian, no way that I see any Republican who wants a political future challenging him if indeed he does run in 2024 doesn`t mean he will. He`s notoriously fickle. We don`t know what he`s going to do. But the fact that all of these Republicans are afraid to dare challenge him, from Ron DeSantis, Nikki Haley, Mike Pence, they`re all basically running scared of him right now. And he is the leader of the party, the nomination is his to lose and if he runs, I think that that`s what we`re going to be dealing with for the next four years.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, ladies and gentlemen, there you have it. We are greatly indebted to our starting line on this Friday night. Eugene Daniels, Jeremy Peters, Cynthia Alksne, many thanks for starting us off. It`s a lot.\par \par \uc2 Coming up for us, he lost the election but as the former president with an ironclad grip on his party, winning the battle to avoid responsibility for what happened on 1/6. Two political insiders standing by. We`ll talk about what to watch for next.\par \par \uc2 And later, Fiona Hill stepped into the spotlight and the first impeachment of the last president. She is with us here tonight and she has a warning if Trump wins again, she says, "democracy is done." All of it as the 11th Hour is just getting underway on this Friday night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:21:14]\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: It`s been over nine months since the insurrection and the country remains of course bitterly divided. Our friend Susan Glasser at the New Yorker puts it this way, and we, "The political crisis today is worse than it was not better. The unacceptable has been accepted by a shockingly large part of the population and its political leadership."\par \par \uc2 Back with us tonight, Don Calloway, Democratic Strategist, Founder of the National Voter Protection Action Fund. And Michael Steele, former Chairman of the Republican National Committee, former Lieutenant Governor of the great State of Maryland, also happens to be host of the Michael Steele podcast.\par \par \uc2 Gentlemen, great to have you both. Good evening. Michael, I`d like to begin with you, our mutual acquaintance, Rick Wilson, stirred up some, you know what, today on social media when he wrote a thread saying basically, the 1/6 committee is already done, it`s over, don`t expect anything, he went on to say, this is staffed wrong, lead wrong and a gutless exercise to get back to talking about infrastructure. They`re not taking the risk seriously. They`re not taking the data before them seriously. And they`re eager to run out the clock, he received some strong pushback from the committee. I`ll say that, but Michael, is there evidence to support his conclusion?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STEELE, FORMER RNC CHAIRMAN: Well, you know, I don`t know exactly what Rick was basing his conclusions on. But I guess there is a genuine concern about just how hard this committee is going to press the Trump individuals who are brought or subpoenaed or asked to appear? You know, how seriously they`re going to take this? How much effort are they going to put behind actually dragging these people into the halls of Congress to sit before this committee to testify to what they know, their role in it, et cetera. And I think there`s a legitimate concern about the level of pressure they`re going to bring in that regard.\par \par \uc2 You had already those who`ve been subpoenaed, basically blow it off. And so, the question now becomes, all right, the deadline has passed, what are you going to do? And I think that`s driving a lot of Rick`s frustration, the frustration of a lot of folks who do want this commission`s work to be legitimate and to reach the results of finding out exactly what happened.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: And Don, you see, you know, the history of this, you see why people are suspicious, even when Liz Cheney promises that they will follow up on these subpoenas, the folks watching in the cheap seats, say to especially the Democratic leadership, when have you done this before? When have you ever insisted? When have you put the force of law behind a subpoena? They couldn`t get John Bolton into the Capitol if they`d offered him a new car.\par \par \uc2 DON CALLOWAY, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Yeah, that`s right. But at the same time, you know, that is not House leadership`s problem, that is not the Democratic committee`s problem. What that is, is a problem with the integrity of the Republican Party who would want their subpoenas respected. They`re going to subpoena Barack Obama, as soon as the House