LSHI: Tell me what you`re expecting, first of all, as it relates to abortion in this court.\par \par \uc2 [21:40:00]\par \par \uc2 The court has not decided. What they did in Texas was not actually hearing the case on the merits. The Mississippi case in December 1st is probably going to be the big abortion hearing that we`ve got at the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 WEINSTEIN: That`s right. And the analysts who are looking at that case, Ali, I would say are ranging from despondent to despondent in terms of their expectations of what will be left of "roe v. Wade" after the Supreme Court decides that case.\par \par \uc2 Now, there is some disagreement about whether the court will actually overturn "Roe" or just gut it. But here`s the thing that`s really clear. That law in Mississippi, just like the Texas law, is unconstitutional as the constitution is currently understood by the Supreme Court. The Supreme Court has said that the state can`t put an undue burden on the right to choose before viability. And these are both effectively bans on abortion well before viability.\par \par \uc2 So, they`re really designed to test whether Roe can survive, whether Casey, which followed on Roe, can survive or needs to be modified or eliminated, and that`s what`s at stake.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: You just said something that was really interesting. I think it was really legally smart, but I want you to expand on it. You said they`re both unconstitutional as the constitution is currently understood by the Supreme Court. I think that`s what most of our viewers think. Except the question on our minds is, as the new session starts, will the Supreme Court see the Constitution differently under the way the Supreme Court is actually constituted? That I think is what`s worrying people.\par \par \uc2 WEINSTEIN: Exactly, because Mississippi, in its abortion case, has asked the Supreme Court to see the Constitution differently. And it`s given it a menu of options. It has said, there is no right to