ate your time tonight.\par \par \uc2 WEINSTEIN: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Still ahead, an environmental disaster as at least 126,000 gallons of crude oil fouls the California coast. The question is how is this still happening in 2021? We`ll talk about that after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [21:48:05]\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: On Friday, the first thing people noticed was the smell. Joggers on the Huntington Beach boardwalk about 40 miles south of Los Angeles reported smelling something, quote, rotten like old meat. In nearby Newport Beach, residents also reported smelling a, quote, horrible and strong tar- like odor that night. Then the next morning on Saturday, workers from Amplify Energy which owns around oil pipeline off orange county saw signs of oil in the water, while they were doing a routine inspection.\par \par \uc2 They reported to the Coast Guard at 9:00 in the morning, Saturday. By noon that day, the Coast Guard announced it was actively responding to an oil spill. \uc2 The city of Newport Beach\uc2  tweeted that day, quote, the city is monitoring the large oil spill, about three to four miles off the coast of Newport Beach. The spill is expected to dissipate through wind, sun, and wave action and is not expected to come ashore.\par \par \uc2 It did not dissipate. It kept spreading, stretching 13 square miles. This is what the Orange County coast looked like by Sunday, ripples of black people wash ashore. People trying to dodge the oil as they inspected the damage. Experts expect the oil to come ashore over the next several days.\par \par \uc2 So far, at least 126,000 gallons of oil have spilled from a pipeline connected to the platform. The company that owns both the platform and the pipeline, \uc2 Amplify Energy Corporation\uc2 , has launched an investigation into what caused this breach. The \uc2 U.S. Coast Guard\uc2  is also continuing its investigation.\par \par \uc2 As of yet, we don`t know for sure the underlying cause of the spill. But there are multiple reports tonight that Amplify Energy believes that a ship anchor striking the pipeline might be responsible for the breach. State and federal officials have launched a criminal investigation into this leak.\par \par \uc2 And "The L.A. Times" reported just within the past hour that a branch of the \uc2 California Department of Fish and Wildlife\uc2 , quote, observed sheen in federal waters several miles off the coast of Huntington Beach as early as Friday night.\par \par \uc2 [21:50:04]\par \par \uc2 But the Coast Guard was not notified until Saturday when the company that owns the pipeline reached out.\par \par \uc2 So all hands on deck to try to contain this thing right now, but every time there is a spill like this, it makes you wonder whether our obsession with oil is worth the inherent danger of the infrastructure that fuels it.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Jacqueline Savitz, chief policy officer for North America and Oceania, an international advocacy organization focused on ocean conservation.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Savage, thank you for being with us tonight.\par \par \uc2 And look, I say this as somebody who consumes oil. I`ve got a carbon footprint like we all do. We have to understand this. There are leaks all the time. We`re lucky when there`s one close enough that you can smell it or see it. But oil leaks from pipelines under the water and under the ground all the time.\par \par \uc2 JACQUELINE SAVITZ, OCEANA NORTH AMERICA CHIEF POLICY OFFICER: Yeah, that`s right, Ali, when we drill we spill. It`s not a matter of if we`re going to spill the oil. It`s just a matter of when, and we`ve seen that time and time again. Yet, at the same time, our government continues to let oil and gas companies do what they want to do in our coasts, even though when there`s a problem like this, it ends up hurting the coastal economies, and that`s before we even start talking about the fact that oil and gas are driving climate change. It makes absolutely no sense to continue with our sort of fossil fuel age when we know we need to be making that history.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: And the distinction you make is important. We can have this conversation, and on this channel we have it a lot about climate change and what we need to do about that, but the actual fact that after the Deepwater Horizon, the Gulf of Mexico, it was mind boggling to most of us that the oil companies had no plan. They had no way to deal with the amount of oil that was going into the ocean. Private enterprise, kids in schools were trying to figure out whether they could put detergent in water to get oil out. Why does this still happen in 2021?\par \par \uc2 SAVITZ: You would think that after we watched day in and day out that picture of the oil gushing into the Gulf of Mexico and just wrung our hands because there was nothing we could do about it that we would have learned something from that and begun to make the transition away from offshore oil and gas. Instead, we didn`t. We continued to sell leases in the Gulf of Mexico. We continued to allow drilling off of California, and you know, I think it`s really clear now as we sit and watch this, in this moment we have an opportunity to finally get permanent protection for our coasts. It`s being discussed in Congress now, and this is really a stark reminder that we absolutely need to make that happen.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: A lot of places along the gulf coast, you can stand onshore, look out, see platforms and see rigs. Same thing in California, California you see them on land and off land.\par \par \uc2 What do protections look like? For people in these places that look out and see a platform on the horizon, an oil rig on the horizon, what do we want? What does success look like?\par \par \uc2 SAVITZ: Well, there`s a question about whether or not we should continue to expand offshore drilling, which we absolutely should not do. What we`re pushing for in Congress right now, by permanent protection, we mean stop all leasing for any new oil and gas development offshore. If we can get that done, we can prevent some future spills. We still have this historic legacy that also needs to be addressed. That`s going to require better inspections. We`re going to need penalties that actually create an incentive to prevent spills. Right now they don`t really.\par \par \uc2 And the fact of the matter is the oil and gas industry is sitting on so many active leases for oil and gas that 75 percent of what`s out there right now that they have their hands on if they can drill, if they are ready to do that, 75 percent of it is either unused or not producing, and that amounts to about 7,000 to 8,000 unused approved premise to drill.\par \par \uc2 And so when we talk about stopping the expansion of offshore drilling, the oil and gas industry, you know, seems to think that`s such a horrible thing, but in the meantime, they have more leases to drill than they could ever possibly even use.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: So we`re not even capping the capacity of what we would drill.\par \par \uc2 Jacqueline Savitz, thank you for joining us tonight. Jacqueline Savitz is the chief policy officer for North America and Oceana.\par \par \uc2 SAVITZ: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [21:58:26]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Not only are Republicans refusing to do their job, they`re threatening to use the power, their power, to prevent us from doing our job, saving the economy from a catastrophic event. I think quite frankly, it`s hypocritical, dangerous and disgraceful.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: President Biden today criticizing Republicans in Congress for refusing to allow Democrats to raise the debt ceiling. Congress must raise the debt ceiling by October 18th in order to keep the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  from defaulting on its debts and many fear consequences could come even sooner than that.\par \par \uc2 Today, Senate Democratic leader Chuck Schumer wrote in a letter to his colleagues quote, we must get a bill to the president`s desk dealing with the debt limit by the end of the week. Period.\par \par \uc2 As Democrats work to try and overcome Republican obstruction to save the U.S. economy from catastrophe, they`re also still working overtime to try and reach a deal on passing President Biden`s agenda. Today, at the same press conference, President Biden told reporters that he`s closed the deal with 99 percent of his party and that only two senators, I assume you can guess which two he`s talking about, are not yet on board.\par \par \uc2 The question is what will it take for them to get on board with the rest of their party?\par \par \uc2 That does it for us tonight. We`ll see you again tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 It`s time now for "THE LAST WORD" with Lawrence O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Lawrence, good evening.\par \par \uc2 You are I think starting off with really the biggest story today, and that is the question of abortion rights in this country.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Yes, we`re going to begin with the Supreme Court tonight, but we also are going to have one of the 48, Ali, we`re going to have one of the 48 senators who \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has closed the deal with.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Right.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And maybe he can tell us what`s going on with the other two.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: That is an artfully written tease, Lawrence. You said one of the 48. I`m going to have to stay here and watch until I see which one it is.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yeah, I`m not going to tell you which one, it`s a very, very important member of the 48 will be joining us.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Have an excellent show, I`ll be watching.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you, Ali.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document TRMADS0020211005eha400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for October 4, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Garrett Haake, Leigh Ann Caldwell; Kelly O`Donnell, Kavita Patel,  Erin McLaughlin, Pete Williams \par \uc2 8,778 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, MSNBC HOST: It`s Monday, President Biden warns of economic catastrophe amid a standoff with Republicans over raising the nation`s debt ceiling as he tries to navigate a standoff with Democrats, two in particular, over the fate of his legislative agenda.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the whistleblower behind a trove of leaked documents and damning revelations on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  has gone public accusing the platform of a betrayal of democracy as she gears up for Congressional testimony tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 And officials are about to speak in California with an update on that 13- square-mile oil spill off the coast, which has environmental experts warning of an ecological disaster.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY, I`m Garrett Haake in for Chuck Todd.\par \par \uc2 And to give you a sense of just how much is on the administration`s plate right now, the President ended last week trying to break this stalemate with Democrats over his legislative agenda.\par \par \uc2 And now, he begins this week with a warning on the economy amid a stalemate with Republicans over raising the debt limit, which will be breached in just two weeks, if not sooner.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The days ahead, eve before the default date, people may see the value of their retirement accounts shrink. They may see interest rates go up, which will ultimately raise their mortgage payments and car payments.\par \par \uc2 And the American people -- look, I just say it this way. As soon as this week, your savings in your pocket -- your pocketbook could be directly impacted by this Republican stunt.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: It`s pretty serious stuff. And the President urged Republicans to stand down on their threats to default because right now Democrats don`t intend to try to raise the debt ceiling without them.\par \par \uc2 So, Biden basically told reporters that the future of the economy might lie with one person, Mitch McConnell. The bottom line here is there`s a dual urgency for the White House right now. Avoid an economic catastrophe if the U.S. defaults and avoid a potential political catastrophe if his agenda fizzles (PH).\par \par \uc2 This comes after the President met with Democrats on Friday, giving Progressive Democrats a big boost in the negotiations by agreeing to link passage of his Infrastructure and Reconciliation bills together, even if it takes weeks or months to pass them.\par \par \uc2 He also tried to appease Moderates by telling the party that the price tag for the Reconciliation package will have to come down probably by more than $1 trillion.\par \par \uc2 But Progressives definitely came out of those meetings in a stronger position than the Moderates, which is going to ratchet up pressure on those two Democratic Senators, Joe Manchin, and Kyrsten Sinema, more about them later. The President all but called them out today for holding things up.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Why were you unable Mr. President to close the deal with members of your own party on key part -- key parts of your legislative agenda last week? Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I am close a deal on 99 percent of my party, two, two people.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It sounds like you`re putting the blame squarely on two U.S. Senators for your inability to close that deal, Senator Sinema and Senator Manchin, am I incorrect? Is that who the blame lies with?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Look, I need 50 votes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: With so much on the line for the President and for Democrats, that pressure is only going to increase.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, NBC`s Leigh Ann Caldwell with the latest from Capitol Hill, and NBC`s Kelly O`Donnell at the White House.\par \par \uc2 And Leigh Ann, I`ll start with you. It`s a new week in Congress. A lot of members went home to kind of lick their wounds at the end of last week, which was pretty trying for a lot of folks, there`s also now a new deadline on Infrastructure. So, what`s the state of play for these discussions as we`re starting to look at the end of this month?\par \par \uc2 LEIGH ANN CALDWELL, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, there`s a new deadline, Garrett, October 31. And so, what Democrats are doing this week is trying to reset. We know that the House of Representatives is still at home, they are not coming back, they`re on recess for this week but these negotiations continue.\par \par \uc2 We also know that Senator Schumer, the Majority Leader, is holding his normal weekly Leadership meeting this afternoon, and on his leadership team are two key Senators, Senator Joe Manchin, and Senator Bernie Sanders.\par \par \uc2 So, we expect in that meeting to be a lot of discussion about the path forward on this multi-trillion dollar human Infrastructure bill and how those two sides can reach an agreement.\par \par \uc2 But it is undeniable that there is increased pressure on the Arizona Democrats Kyrsten Sinema. She`s getting a lot of pressure from activists, she`s getting a lot of pressure at home, and she`s getting a lot of pressure from her colleagues up here on Capitol Hill to be more transparent about what she wants and where she stands.\par \par \uc2 Although people close to Senator Sinema insist that Pres. Biden and Leader Schumer have known exactly what she wants since early August.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: So, while she doesn`t negotiating the press, they insist that the people who need to know knew what she wants in this legislation.\par \par \uc2 But it`s still extremely difficult balancing act as these Democratic leaders in the White House try to maneuver to get these two sides on the same page, especially when Senator Sinema and Senator Manchin have a lot of power to hold things up.\par \par \uc2 Now, the alternate argument is people like Senator Warren and Senator Sanders, they say they also have an equal amount of power to direct the path of this legislation. But so far, Manchin and Sinema seem to be outmaneuvering them. And we know that just last week President Biden told House Democrats that the $3.5 trillion is going to have to be scaled back, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Leigh Ann, let`s talk a little bit about the debt ceiling piece of the puzzle here. Earlier, Schumer had a letter this morning saying that he wants to deal with it this week. Obviously, he got a little bit of air support from President Biden holding that press conference and focusing on the issue here.\par \par \uc2 But how do Senate Democrats plan to attack that debt ceiling issue this week? And if they don`t get it done this week, they`re kind of running out of time for the last worst-case scenario option of trying to fix it through Reconciliation, aren`t they?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Yes, they are. My sources are telling me that it should take about seven days if they want to add the debt limit to this Reconciliation bill. Now, that is an enormous amount of time, since there wasn`t a lot of time left before this October 31 -- all the other things that they have to deal with, let`s just leave it at that.\par \par \uc2 But there is more than seven days left before the October 18 deadline where the country would default according to the Treasury Secretary. So, they do have an option that`s not an option that Democrats want in part because if they do it through the Reconciliation process, as you have reported, they can`t just suspend the debt limit, they have to put a very scary number of what they want that debt limit to be.\par \par \uc2 Now, Leader McConnell has released a letter just a little bit ago saying once again, that they are going to play no part and pointing out that President Biden, and during the Bush administration, said that he took the same position, then that McConnell has now that it should be a partisan process because of a partisan agenda moving forward, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And Kelly O`Donnell at the White House, I`m curious, what were your takeaways on both the kind of top-line pressure from President Biden to solve this debt ceiling piece and the fact that he has laid the challenges of getting his agenda passed squarely at the feet of those two Moderate Democratic Senators?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`DONNELL, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, Garrett, I was in the State Dining Room for the President`s event, and I asked him about the letter from Leader McConnell about the debt ceiling and the President says he has seen it and intends to talk to McConnell about his position.\par \par \uc2 The President also wanting to sort of set the public awareness that Democrats, may, in fact, have to do this on their own. That separately Democratic voter, who have been engaged on this issue interested in it probably needed to hear from the President to try to get them all in line with the steps they need to take.\par \par \uc2 And then on the issue of his agenda items, the President was clear that he didn`t want to negotiate publicly, we`ve seen plenty of that from all kinds of officeholders. But he was also clear that the two Senators are really the final two yeses he needs to somehow obtain.\par \par \uc2 Where that number is? Somewhere around 1.5 trillion as what Senator Manchin has said publicly, we know that Progressives want it to be higher. So, where is that point? The President wouldn`t get into specifics publicly.\par \par \uc2 But this was really an opportunity for the President to start the week by exerting some pressure from the White House in places where he hopes it will do some good, and at the same time trying to inform the public by talking about things like the impact of the debt ceiling on 401 (k), and retirement plans.\par \par \uc2 That sometimes gets people dialing their lawmakers. We know, all of us, who`ve covered Capitol Hill, how influential that can be, and to try to set a bit of the expectation for what still needs to come on his own agenda.\par \par \uc2 The President will be traveling tomorrow heading to Michigan, other stops coming this week to talk about the substance of what`s in his spending priority wish list, and trying to get the public to understand what they could gain if Senators do fall in line on the Democratic side, and if they can get this through.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s a lot of work still for the President to try to bring his own party together on key issues that will really have a big impact on many Americans` lives, Garrett. HAAKE: Yes, after a couple of weeks on the defensive, interesting to see the White House that they`re trying to set the agenda and really focus the conversation on the things that they want to be talking about. Kelly, Leigh Ann Caldwell, thank you both.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by a Democratic Congressman from California, John Garamendi. He is also on the House Transportation and Infrastructure committee intimately involved with the negotiations around both of these bills.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: So, Congressman, you got to start a new week, you`ve got a new deadline --\par \par \uc2 REP. JOHN GARAMENDI, (D-CA): Sure.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: where are the negotiations, at least on the House side go now?\par \par \uc2 GARAMENDI: I think they`re going to go very, very well, but not immediately. It`s not going to be done in the next couple of days, but it`d be done during the month of October.\par \par \uc2 But we have heard, and we got to listen carefully to what people are saying and -- but move aside from all of the other things. Manchin is -- wanted a lower number. He`s also wanted some specific things as has Sinema. Sinema wants to be -- wants some more on the climate piece of it. All of those are possible. Also, some of the Progressives in the House side have said, well, maybe we can move off the $3.5trillion.\par \par \uc2 So, there`s room for negotiations here. You can change the dates. That`s a 10-year program spread over 10 years, 3.5 trillion, cut it to five years, suddenly you`re down to 1.75 trillion.\par \par \uc2 But you got to talk about the individual programs that are here. The largest tax cut ever for middle-class Americans is in the Reconciliation bill, also, the child tax credit, all of those things, specifically, 39 million families will benefit.\par \par \uc2 Climate change issues, money for the kinds of things that we need to do to move away from petroleum and coal, all of those things are in the bill, so yes, the President is going to go out and talk about it.\par \par \uc2 With regard to the debt limit, keep in mind it is the Republican filibuster that is blocking the bill that is presently on -- in the Senate. We passed that bill actually twice, it`s sitting over there in the Senate, it`s a filibuster, it is McConnell`s unwillingness to release his members to allow the Democrats to vote on it.\par \par \uc2 He is blocking that he can say that. He can say what he wants to say the fact, so it is a filibuster by the Republicans that is blocking the 50 Democrats, plus the Vice President.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: That`s tough when you -- the other side doesn`t have something they want to negotiate for. You touched on this a little bit, I think in your answer just now. I think there`s this debate going on around Democrats as you try to figure out how this package will get smaller, and everyone I think acknowledges that it will get smaller.\par \par \uc2 Whether that means taking certain programs and saying we`re not going to do these because we can`t do them as well, for less money? Do -- where do you fall on the idea of kind of shrinking programs, setting deadlines a little bit shorter, or if they`re things that you look at in that Reconciliation bill as it exists right now that you have to say, you know what, maybe we just -- we set this aside for another day?\par \par \uc2 GARAMENDI: We`re not going to set it aside. We`re going to move things forward. The House of Representatives, all of our key committees in the House, Ways and Means, all of them, even the Transportation Committee, we worked months and months, particularly two months, August and September, on drafting the Reconciliation bill to meet the needs of Americans.\par \par \uc2 It`s in the bill. Would we cut anything out? I certainly don`t want to cut out the climate, I certainly don`t want to cut out the child tax credit, I certainly don`t want to cut out -- well the other piece of it is major corporations, the wealthy paying their fair share. So, the bill is balanced as it is today.\par \par \uc2 I think the best way to do it is to reduce the number of years. Five years would cut it in half. A six-year is OK, we can go up to seven, seven and a half years, whatever the agreed-upon number.\par \par \uc2 But the programs are what is essential here that pet the increased taxes on the wealthy to pay for all of this, it`s 100 percent paid for. 3.5 trillion, cut it in half and it`s still 100 percent paid for, and -- go ahead.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: It-- as I was just going to say it`s always easier to create these programs and then force a future Congress to decide to try to get rid of a program that you hope would be popular, right?\par \par \uc2 I mean, that`s by setting these up even on a shorter timetable, you`re essentially telling future Congresses, look, we put this program in place, do you keep it or do not keep it? And once these programs are on the table, they tend to sort of stick around.\par \par \uc2 I want to ask you about the timing of all of this, though. You-- House Democrats got themselves in a little bit of trouble last week, obviously, with a deadline that wasn`t a real deadline, at least in terms of an absolutely necessary deadline to pass the Infrastructure framework by the end of last week, and I know you were someone who wanted to pass it right away when it was ready.\par \par \uc2 Are you confident that you can get both pieces of this done in time to meet what is a little bit more of a real deadline at the end of this month? And are you frustrated that the President has essentially put you in a place where now you do have a deadline to do both of these things in this month?\par \par \uc2 GARAMENDI: Well, I remember Professor back in college days that he set a deadline, it was called the final exam, and he really meant it. Is the October 30 a real deadline? No, it`s not as probably a midterm if you would use the same.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got the time to get this done, and we`re going to get it done. That`s the key piece of it. The American public wants the Infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 They want their streets, they want their roads. They want their water system. They want to get rid of lead pipes. They want to make sure that we`re building for the future an electrical system that will actually meet the needs of a society that`s not dependent on coal and oil.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 GARAMENDI: They want those things. Will we deliver it? Yes. Does it have to be delivered during the month of October? That would certainly be the wise thing to do. We`re not going to die on a date. We`re going to die on -- we`re not going to die at all, we`re going to get this thing done.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I certainly, hope not.\par \par \uc2 GARAMENDI: I want to keep in mind -- I want to keep in mind that we`ve got to deal with the reality of the Senate, in which case there is two Senators over there.\par \par \uc2 Also, there are four Democrats among the 222 of us that could kill the bill in the House of Representatives. So it`s very, very things should go we`ve got to put together the compromises.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I -- believe me, I hear that and I`ll be following those members, especially those two Senators as closely as I can this week while they are here in Washington, DC. But Congressman Garamendi, I have to leave it there. Thank you for coming on.\par \par \uc2 GARAMENDI: My pleasure. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right. Up next, a major ongoing outage at \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , and WhatsApp, just one day after the \uc2 Facebook\uc2  whistleblower went public after leaking a trove of documents that`s exposed some damning revelations about the platform. The latest on her allegations and the social media giant`s response coming up.\par \par \uc2 And later, new reporting on former President Trump`s plans for 2024, and how his team had to talk him out of a formal campaign launch over the summer. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. \uc2 Facebook\uc2  is facing a pair of crises today. Operationally, it`s dealing with an ongoing global outage that`s preventing users from accessing three of its platforms, \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , and WhatsApp. We`ll keep you updated as we learn more about what`s behind that.\par \par \uc2 And politically, it`s preparing for potentially damning Senate testimony tomorrow by a former employee turned whistleblower.\par \par \uc2 Frances Haugen, who revealed our identity in a 60 minutes interview last night, left the company in May and took tens of thousands of documents with her. She says the documents prove \uc2 Facebook\uc2  cares more about its bottom line than about society`s well-being.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FRANCES HAUGEN, WHISTLEBLOWER, \uc2 FACEBOOK\uc2 : \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , over and over again has shown it uses profit over safety. It is subsidizing is paying for its profits with our safety.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Haugen also says \uc2 Facebook\uc2  has profited off of polarization at the expense of democracy at large.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FRANCES HAUGEN, WHISTLEBLOWER, \uc2 FACEBOOK\uc2 : When we live in an information environment that is full of angry, hateful, polarizing content, it erodes our civic trust, it erodes our faith in each other, it erodes our ability to want to care for each other. The version of \uc2 Facebook\uc2  that exists today is tearing our societies apart and causing ethnic violence around the world.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: \uc2 Facebook\uc2  is defending its efforts, releasing a statement that reads.\par \par \uc2 Every day our teams have to balance protecting the rights of billions of people to express themselves openly with the need to keep our platform a safe and positive place. We continue to make significant improvements to tackle the spread of misinformation and harmful content. To suggest we encourage bad content and to do nothing is just not true.\par \par \uc2 How these revelations were the basis for the Wall Street Journal`s six-part investigative series into \uc2 Facebook\uc2  last month? Deepa Seetharaman is one of the reporters behind that Wall Street Journal series, and she joins me now.\par \par \uc2 So, Deepa, we`ve got this Senate hearing tomorrow, what is left for lawmakers to ask Frances Haugen tomorrow? And I guess if you are a Senator, and knew you had this witness under oath in your Committee, what would you want to ask?\par \par \uc2 DEPPA SEETHARAMAN, SOCIAL MEDIA AND POLITICS REPORTER, THE WALL STREET JOURNAL: Well, we`ve only really seen the tip of the iceberg when it comes to what`s in these documents. I mean, you know, we`ve done a lot of reporting around these documents, we do. There`s more reporting to come. But there is a wealth of information in here, and a lot of which we haven`t quite touched on yet.\par \par \uc2 You know, I`m not a Senator, I`m not going to be able to tell you what I think they should ask, but there`s just an extraordinary number of themes, and numbers, and, all kinds of things that need to be uncovered, and we`ll see what she says tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Is that -- of the themes that present themselves here, do you think it`s more interesting, kind of the -- what we`ve learned about \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , or what we`ve learned about Facebook`s effort to make sure we didn`t learn those things about \uc2 Facebook\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 SEETHARAMAN: So, I think what you`re asking is, is, you know, to what extent is it -- is it important that \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , that this is -- this research happening within \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , or is it more compelling that \uc2 Facebook\uc2  saw this research and then did nothing about it? And I think --\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: That`s a good way to ask the question, too.\par \par \uc2 SEETHARAMAN: I -- you know, honestly, I think it`s a ladder. And I think a lot of the -- a lot of the kind of anger and outrage, at least, you know, the mail I`m getting in my inbox is about the fact that they didn`t know about this from \uc2 Facebook\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 You know, just taking the teen girl research, just as a once -- one specific example. The research doesn`t say that \uc2 Instagram\uc2  is bad for all teen girls. That`s not the sort of the underlying basis of it. And what it says is that for some teen girls, especially girls who are dealing with issues of depression, anxiety, or have body image issues, that they -- that for them, \uc2 Instagram\uc2  can be pretty harmful, right.\par \par \uc2 And so the company, up until now, has been talking about how, you know, for -- there are good effects for a lot of teen girls, and there are, you know, positives to social media, and, you know, yes, there`s some bad things, but what we really need to do -- and we need to do a lot to address them, but on the whole, it`s good.\par \par \uc2 And what this research shows is that there`s more nuance and depth to that research that for some teens, there are really damaging effects. And so, there needs to -- they`re -- what`s happening now is sort of a level set in the conversation where people aren`t just taking Facebook`s word for it. They want to know a little bit more about what the company knows and what their research shows.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I want to stay on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  here for a minute and play for you something that Frances Haugen said on 60 minutes last night about \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , and what \uc2 Facebook\uc2  knows about its effects. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FRANCES HAUGEN, FACEBOOK WHISTLEBLOWER; And what`s super tragic is Facebook`s own research says, as these young women begin to consume this eating disorder content, they get more and more depressed, and it actually makes them use the app more. And so, they end up in this feedback cycle where they hate their bodies more and more.\par \par \uc2 Facebook`s own research says it is not just that \uc2 Instagram\uc2  is dangerous for teenagers that harms teenagers, is that is distinctly worse than other forms of social media.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: So \uc2 Facebook\uc2  is a public company too, they`ve got a profit motive a fiduciary duty to their investors, and all that, but how do they deal with challenges like this?\par \par \uc2 I mean that`s pretty damning stuff. Where do they think their responsibility is both morally -- and I suppose even as a -- you know, just you want to be like totally lizard-brain about it. As a business practice, you can`t do this kind of thing to your customers and continue to succeed as a business.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 SEETHARAMAN: I think, you know, one thing that`s kind of important to know as you step back, there was a ton of really good, conscientious people that work at \uc2 Facebook\uc2 . Nobody wants -- none of the people that I know want their \uc2 Instagram\uc2  to harm the mental health of teen girls. That`s not the objective.\par \par \uc2 But the problem is, is that it`s inconvenient. And so they`ve got all this research that shows that certain -- that certain types of content can be triggering, you know, celebrity content, fashion content. That cuts directly against some of the areas that \uc2 Instagram\uc2  has been investing in.\par \par \uc2 You know, they are encouraging more celebrity content, they are encouraging more fashion content, they like that stuff because it holds people on the platform. And so, there`s a tension there between, you know, here are this -- here are things, here are topics that a lot of people like and aren`t armed by, but then the subset is deeply harmed by. And how do you tease out, how to -- how do you -- how do you help them or, and -- but at the same time, not lose lots of money?\par \par \uc2 And we see this again, and again, and again. And the documents, it`s that there`s a certain amount of harm that`s exposed through the research. And then \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , not quite knowing what to do because some of the solutions might harm engagement.\par \par \uc2 And that trade-off that Frances is talking about is, one that means all my colleagues who`ve been covering \uc2 Facebook\uc2  for a few years, and now, have heard at various times, that this is an important tension within the company.\par \par \uc2 They have this responsibility to investors, but then they also have this responsibility that they talk about a lot to individual users to, you know, to make sure that the product minimizes the bad and amplifies the good. And -- but here, those two goals are at tension.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Can I ask you also about the controversy here over the content that related to the January 6 attack on the Capitol just outside our window, my workplace here, and the accusations that \uc2 Facebook\uc2  essentially turned off its own moderating algorithms, and what have you -- might have led to a spike in the kind of content that motivated those people? What is \uc2 Facebook\uc2  saying about that? What is your reporting from these documents indicated?\par \par \uc2 SEETHARAMAN: So, are reporting from all these documents show? Well, I guess maybe take a step back and think about the fact that \uc2 Facebook\uc2  is a platform that`s very much informed by Mark Zuckerberg`s views on speech. And for him, more speech is better than less speech.\par \par \uc2 He`s -- he talked about this a lot. He says that there is a lot of value in making sure that people have a voice whether or not you disagree with them.\par \par \uc2 And that philosophy has sort of filtered through the company and filtered down to a lot of its policies. You know, it`s taken a long time for \uc2 Facebook\uc2  to do things like ban or ban Holocaust denial, right? Because it`s been this debate within the company, whether that`s right, whether it`s right to limit and curtail people`s speech like that.\par \par \uc2 But over time, as there`s been this connection between speech and violence, you know, not just here, brilliant (PH) Myanmar, and a lot of countries around the world, the company has sort of, stepped up its policies in -- to meet those evolving responsibilities.\par \par \uc2 But when it comes to something like the 2020 election, you know, they put in all these different great -- break glass measures, as what they call them, to ensure that violence didn`t percolate, that there were major issues, but then, you know, that, at the same, time cuts against the underlying philosophy of the company, which is that, yes, there -- within parameters -- within the parameters that they set, there should be free speech.\par \par \uc2 And so, there`s this push-pull within the company about what that looks like and what those responsibilities are. And -- but up until now, they`ve always erred on the side of more speech. And now what Frances is bringing up is that this inherent tension and what happens when it potentially could lead to violence.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right, Deepa Seetharaman, we got to leave it there. Thank you for coming on and for the excellent work you`re doing on this topic for the journal.\par \par \uc2 SEETHARAMAN: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: This story is going to continue to develop as this whistleblower testifies tomorrow on Capitol Hill and we will keep following it.\par \par \uc2 Up next, cleaning up the latest Coronavirus confusion. What Dr. Fauci is now saying about Americans going home for the holidays? You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:32:56]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. Here`s some news I`ve wanted to stay for a while. We start this week with the pandemic trending in the right direction. The daily average of new cases nationwide has decreased every day for the past two weeks bringing us to a daily average of 108,000. That`s a 35 percent decrease in the last month alone.\par \par \uc2 And in vaccine news, the FDA announced a number of key meetings in the coming weeks, among them is a two-day advisory committee meeting set for mid-October to consider booster shots for people who got the Moderna and the \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  vaccines.\par \par \uc2 Despite all this good news, Dr. Fauci says now is not the time to let our guards down. He was on CNN earlier today clarifying some confusion over some comments that he made yesterday about the holidays. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. ANTHONY FAUCI, CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISER TO PRESIDENT BIDEN: The best way to assure that we`ll be in good shape as we get into the winter would be to get more and more people vaccinated. That was misinterpreted as my saying we can`t spend Christmas with our families, which was absolutely not the case. I will be spending Christmas with my family. I encourage people particularly the vaccinated people who are protected to have a good, normal Christmas with your family.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Dr. Fauci`s un-Grinching of himself comes as the CDC has put out its own list of holiday recommendations urging some safety precautions for indoor gatherings like getting vaccinated and keeping spaces well ventilated.\par \par \uc2 Let`s dig into those recommendations and all the other COVID developments of the day and the week with \uc2 Dr. Kavita Patel\uc2 , primary care physician and a former policy director under President Obama.\par \par \uc2 So, Doc, a lot of people are starting to make their holiday travel plans. They`re starting to request those days off. What do you make of Dr. Fauci and the CDC`s guidelines around holiday recommendations? There was obviously kind of a cleanup on aisle seven situation here, but what do viewers really need to know as they`re starting to think about their holiday gatherings?\par \par \uc2 DR. KAVITA PATEL\uc2 , MSNBC MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, Garrett, I think the key thing is that we don`t know what the holidays will bring unless you can kind of guarantee that you keep the crowd small, limit it to mostly vaccinated people, and, of course, with children`s vaccines potentially being one of those key meetings during the FDA`s month of October.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:06]\par \par \uc2 PATEL: That could be a big game changer. I mean, a lot of what Dr. Fauci has had to give pretty sound advice on has been because under 12 people are not vaccinated. Immunocompromised people are basically unprotected. The more people in a household holiday gathering that are vaccinated, the better the protection, the higher the confidence, and of course if case rates can stay low, then we`re going to have an incredible holiday season because we all -- let`s be honest. If we can vaccinate more people, we deserve to reward the benefits come from those kind of hard-earned vaccinations and what we`ve been doing the last 20 months.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: When I first heard Dr. Fauci`s originally comments, I was reminded of something that Andrew Cuomo said during one of his daily press briefings early on in the pandemic where he talked about the idea that from a public health perspective, your mandates and things that you put in place are only as good as knowing that people will be willing to follow them.\par \par \uc2 As the pandemic goes longer, how much harder is it for public officials like Fauci to put recommendations or mandates in place when people are just so exhausted by the rules that they have been following for so long? Does it get more challenging from a public health perspective to even recommend things that you know people aren`t going to want to follow?\par \par \uc2 PATEL: It does. I mean, look, Garrett, I`ve been reading and keeping up with all the guidelines, and even I am getting exhausted at the idea of another virtual Halloween, another virtual Thanksgiving, any of these holidays. So I think your point is correct. So we have mandates, we have vaccines now. That`s a huge game-changer from last holiday season.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right.\par \par \uc2 PATEL: So it has to be the mandates this. But we also have to -- we really do need to send a signal that is true. These vaccines work. So we need to have some of that confidence come back. It`s a fine line. It`s not the nuanced messaging that the CDC is used to giving. But I think that`s why we`re going to continue to watch the case rates. Continue to encourage vaccination. And then like I said, I`m looking -- I`m not expecting every child to get vaccinated, but the advent of pediatric vaccines really will signal a shift that I think will put a lot more consumer confidence just back out there.\par \par \uc2 And we need that, and we need to message that as scientific officials. Follow the science. But also follow the science to know that we`re safe and we`re protected when we`re vaccinated and what that means if your household can celebrate which we can if we`re vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Of the two sort of October meetings, the J&J, Moderna, questions about boosters, and the possibility of pediatric vaccines, I think I know the answer to this, but is the pediatric vaccine the bigger potential game changer for the long-term trajectory of the pandemic?\par \par \uc2 PATEL: It is. But I look for those other FDA meetings. It`s not just about the boosters. It`s also about the mix and match. We`re all waiting because we`re seeing global data, Garrett, on how if you got Moderna, you can get a \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  booster and vice-versa. We`re going to see more data coming from the NIH. They`re small trials but they`re going to be important because the real world of giving out vaccines like I did to patients is that we`ll need to have this flexibility.\par \par \uc2 So all three meetings are going to be key. Moderna, J&J and the kids vaccines. But I do think that culturally those pediatric vaccine meetings are going to feel like the beginning of the end for our country. And it`s well-deserved.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I know so many parents who will be breathing so much easier when their kids can get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 Doctor Patel, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And up next, breaking news on the oil spill off the coast of California as questions grow about the impact to wildlife, the economy, and the speed of the initial response.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:41:37]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. We`re following breaking news out of California where officials are warning of a potential ecological disaster after an oil pipeline leaked spewing more than 126,000 gallons of oil into the waters off of Newport Beach. Several beaches have been closed and the pipeline believed to be the source of the leak has been shut down. Officials just finished giving an update on the situation and NBC`s Erin McLaughlin joins me now from Newport Beach.\par \par \uc2 So, Erin, what have we learned here this morning?\par \par \uc2 ERIN MCLAUGHLIN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Garrett. That`s right. Officials just gave an update with respect to the impact on the local wildlife. And they`re saying that they`re cautiously optimistic. Particularly with the impact on the number of birds in the area, which is always a concern when it comes to oil spills. They say that they believe that there have been less of an impact on birds in the area than originally feared.\par \par \uc2 That said, it`s really only one component when you look at the overall damage from an oil spill of this size. Overnight officials expanding the area of impact from Huntington Beach all the way Dana Point, shutting down beaches, shutting down fisheries as well as marshlands and wetlands. They say that the oil has crept into those areas, although they`ve had a really all-hands-on-deck approach in terms of the cleanup, deploying thousands of buoys.\par \par \uc2 So far they say they`ve collected some 1200 gallons of oily water which is really a drop in the bucket when you consider some 126,000 gallons of water had been dumped. They`re particularly concerned with the type of crude oil dumped into the bay. It`s post production crude. They say that`s hazardous not only to wildlife and also to people in the area, urging anyone who has any sort of side effects, dizziness, nausea, headaches, they`re concerned about the air quality, to seek medical attention. Garrett?\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right, Erin McLaughlin. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And up next, the new term of the Supreme Court starts today with the future of "Roe v. Wade" at stake.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:47:12]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. The Supreme Court is back in session today kicking off what`s set to be a blockbuster term, taking on gun rights, religion, and the most consequential abortion case in 30 years. And for the first time in 18 months the justices are back in the courtroom hearing oral arguments in person. Justice Kavanaugh is the only justice absent after testing positive for COVID-19 late last week.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is NBC News justice correspondent Pete Williams. And Pete, what are these big cases that we`re looking out? Which ones are on your, you know, calendar must-watch most consequential cases?\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, NBC NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: I think everybody would say the Mississippi case. This is a state law that would limit abortion after 15 weeks. It would basically ban it after 15 weeks. Now that is a direct challenge to "Roe v. Wade" for this reason. Roe and follow-on Supreme Court decisions have said yes, states can restrict access to abortion during the period of pregnancy before viability which is generally thought to be 23 to 24 weeks. But they can`t ban it in that period.\par \par \uc2 So along comes Mississippi, and it would ban it before that. So that`s a direct challenge to "Roe" and indeed the state in its brief says the Supreme Court should overturn "Roe v. Wade." So, you know, "Roe v. Wade" is on the chopping block this term.\par \par \uc2 You mentioned the gun case. It`s a test of what does the Second Amendment mean when it says there`s a right to keep and bear arms. You wouldn`t think of bearing arms in the house. The Supreme Court said in 2008 that there is an individual right in the Second Amendment to have a gun at home for self- defense. This asked the questions, well, what about outside the home?\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: On the abortion case, you`ve got also this Texas law awaiting in the wings. It`s likely to potentially end up in front of the Supreme Court. Are there other state laws that are being almost designed for this purpose to become other challenges to Roe depending on what the court rules on the Mississippi case?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, both of these fit that definition. They were very much passed to directly challenge Roe, and yes, there are other cases pending. But the Supreme Court is not going to do anything. If other appeals come, they will hold those until they decide the Mississippi case. It really is the -- it`s the central question.\par \par \uc2 The Texas case, you`re right. The Supreme Court a month ago allowed the law to take effect. But it`s undoubtedly going to come back before this term is over one way or the other.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And in terms of the timeframe, when do we start seeing these decisions being rolled out?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: When your grandchildren are born.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Oh, good. All right.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: We`ll probably not see them until the end of the term. These contentious cases, they go back and forth. I wouldn`t think we`d see the abortion decision before the end of June. The gun case maybe a little earlier. The abortion case is argued December 1st. The gun case is argued in November. There`s a case about religion in public support for religious schools. That could come out a little earlier, too.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Just in time to be major animating factors in the midterms?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right. Pete Williams, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: You bet.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And coming up, Donald Trump`s 2024 presidential plans. What his candidacy in waiting means for the Republican Party and American democracy?\par \par \uc2 [13:50:02]\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Are you going to run again, Mr. President?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: Oh, that`s a tough question.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Actually for me it`s easy question.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: OK.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: I mean, I know what I`m going to do but we`re not supposed to be talking about it yet from the standpoint of campaign finance laws which frankly are ridiculous, OK, you want to know the truth. But we have to live with it. But I think you`re going to be happy.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. That was former President Trump last month teasing a run in 2024 when asked about it on the 20th anniversary of September 11th, of all days. According to new reporting in "The Washington Post," Trump considered formally announcing his campaign around that time following the chaotic Afghanistan withdrawal but his advisers convinced him that such an early entrance was not worth the political risk or the legal headaches.\par \par \uc2 Still, the groundwork is clearly being laid behind the scenes for the next election as Trump and his allies continue to push the lie that the last election was rigged against him. It leaves potential Republican presidential hopefuls not named Trump all holding their breath and democracy advocates worrying about what a Trump 2024 comeback could mean for the country.\par \par \uc2 Here now is "Washington Post" national political reporter Michael Scherer, one of the reporters behind "The Post`s" deep dive on the Trump 2024 operation.\par \par \uc2 So, Michael, the key question, right, is how serious is former President Trump about trying to take back the White House and what kind of groundwork are we talking about here? What does his campaign in waiting look like?\par \par \uc2 [13:55:02]\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL SCHERER, THE WASHINGTON POST NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER: Well, I think at the moment he`s very serious. And with President Trump you always have to sort of put an asterisk on statements like that because he does change his mind.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right.\par \par \uc2 SCHERER: It doesn`t mean he`ll still be here as serious in two years. In terms of the apparatus right now, he has built a rather large leadership PAC. He`s fundraising aggressively. That money is not easily transferred to a presidential campaign but it does allow him to support other candidates right now. He`s also started to hire up a political staff including two key operatives in Iowa, including Eric Branstad, who is the son of the former governor of Iowa, who`s about as big a get as you could get if you want to compete in an Iowa caucus in 2024.\par \par \uc2 And then he`s going around the country. I mean the discussion that happened in August with his aides was whether it would be better for his 2024 election to announce now, tie himself to the midterms directly, allow Democrats to campaign against him, because the moment he announces, a midterm election becomes wholly about President Trump, or whether it`s better for him to just support candidates that he likes in the hopes that he`ll get payback down the road, that he can sort of build his network beyond even where it is now, and then declare victory.\par \par \uc2 If Republicans retake the House, retake the Senate, there`s zero doubt that President Trump will claim 100 percent of the credit. And in the meantime, he`s going to continue traveling the country. He`s got another rally this Saturday in Iowa of all places, at the Fairgrounds. And almost certainly you will hear him deliver the same sort of wink and nod hints as the clip you played at the top of the segment, you know, suggesting we have a great victory coming in 2024, you`re going to be very happy, things like that, without actually going all the way.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right.\par \par \uc2 SCHERER: And trigger the campaign finance laws.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I think there`s probably nothing that would make congressional Democrats up in 2022 happier than having a Trump 2024 campaign already going to run against him without him being on the ballot. It would be the dream scenario like they saw in 2018.\par \par \uc2 I have two questions, one, about a primary. Who does the Trump operation, who does the former president worry about if he does choose to run in a primary, if anyone? Right? I can`t imagine a Mike Pence or a Mike Pompeo gets any traction with the former boss still in the race.\par \par \uc2 SCHERER: It`s a very crowded primary if Donald Trump is not in the race.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right.\par \par \uc2 SCHERER: If Trump is in the race, we don`t really know who`s going to stick around. Right now the former President Trump is telling people he`s not worried about any of them. And aides have told us that he`ll sort of tick through his potential rifles, everyone from Ron DeSantis in Florida, you know, Rick Scott from Florida, Mike Pence, his former vice president, who are all making moves, all going -- meeting with donors or visiting Iowa. And there`s a dozen other people you can add to that list as well.\par \par \uc2 But tight now the president says he`s not worried about any of that group. I think, you know, at the current status, it`s unlikely any of those people would be able to make the argument which basically is the only way to beat former President Trump in a primary, which is that President Trump is a great guy, we love what he did the last four years but he`s just not the best person to represent the party in 2024, he`ll lose it for us.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Yes, and the party --\par \par \uc2 SCHERER: We`re just not in an environment you could make that argument.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: No.\par \par \uc2 SCHERER: Although, you know, after the midterms, that could change.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Yes. I mean, the party has so fully embraced him at this point. So, moving on to a general election matchup here, I mean, you`re talking about a guy who lost by seven million votes. How do you see a Trump campaign dovetailing with all of these, you know, voting changes, issues in state legislatures trying to change the way so many states like a Georgia, for example, might count votes and could put him over the line even if the votes aren`t necessarily there?\par \par \uc2 SCHERER: It`s really hard to tell at this point. You`re going to have legal challenges on the state level. You also have the real possibility that Democrats in Congress figure out a way to pass some voting reforms of their own before the midterms. And if that happens, that complicates things further. I think what we do know is that especially if President Trump is on the ballot, the legitimacy of the election will be in doubt for a big part of the country.\par \par \uc2 And the legitimacy of the election will be even more on the ballot than it was in the last cycle. And I think that theme is just inescapable at this point thanks to former President Trump. I mean, he brought it up in his rally in Georgia. It`s going to be a centerpiece no doubt at his rally in Iowa. He keeps hammering, much like he did the sort of myth about Obama`s birth before his first run, this misinformation about the last election not being conclusive against him. And for him, it`s, you know, incredibly beneficial to rallying his base.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: A fascinating echo of the last Trump -- the last Trump campaign that worked, I suppose.\par \par \uc2 Michael Scherer, thank you.\par \par \uc2 SCHERER: It worked.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And thank you all 