unny that you said big pharma because I remember looking at your ads in 2018. So, you run in 2018 against an incumbent Republican. And you did a lot on taking on big pharma over oxycontin and opioids. I mean, that was a real thing.\par \par \uc2 Now, you`re running in a district that was a Trump district. I mean, Trump had won the district, right?\par \par \uc2 ROSE: True.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That was an issue that you felt both I think passionate about substantively and was politically advantageous for you in a district that was -- that was a district Trump carried.\par \par \uc2 ROSE: Right, right. People cannot listen to their donors right now.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROSE: They cannot listen to their donors, and they have got to listen to the American people, all right.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROSE: The American people -- that`s what makes this also shocking. The American people are united about this.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You`re talking about the prescription drug benefit particularly.\par \par \uc2 ROSE: Oh 100 percent. And here`s what`s amazing about that, OK. That`s the thing that actually provides them revenue to do others things.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 ROSE: OK, so --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You get rid of it, the deficit gets bigger. Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROSE: Right.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Because it saves the government money.\par \par \uc2 ROSE: You save Medicare hundreds of billions of dollars which then you use to pay for universal childcare, universal pre-k, expanding Medicare, and combating climate change.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Faiz, on the Senate side, I mean Tester -- you know, Tester is interesting because he`s an interesting player in there. He`s, you know, next to Manchin who probably represents the most conservative state but is much less of a kind of caucus contrarian, I think, that the Manchin is. And Manchin, I think, has his own sort of thing. But having worked for Reid and worked in that Senate, and know Bernie well and work with Bernie, like, what is the approach here to get -- because you need all 50 to agree on a number and on what`s actually in it.\par \par \uc2 FAIZ SHAKIR, POLITICAL ADVISER TO \uc2 SEN. BERNIE SANDERS\uc2 : Well, the problem is, Chris, we`ve been having a fake negotiation. We`ve been into this for months and months and months. And at this point, you would think that if you were on the other side of Bernie or whatever, you would have a proposal.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: And yet, what the surprising thing for Manchin or Sinema is there is no proposal. You`re negotiating with the shadow. And it`s very hard to have a "compromise" when progressives are both advertising the North Star where we need to go and then offering the compromise, and yet begging, what is your alternative to this? What is it? And to this point, I`ve been talking about this for months. They cannot offer a specific alternative.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is a really good point because anyone who`s ever been through a negotiation, it goes like this. There`s an offer, there`s a counter, there`s an offer, there`s a counter. You need a counter. And I`ve been in negotiations where you`re waiting on the counter and it drives you nuts. Where`s the counter? Where`s the counter?\par \par \uc2 There`s no -- I mean, that`s the thing. Manchin`s counter is a strategic pause, or maybe we don`t do anything at all, but if he -- maybe he`s doing this privately, but if Manchin and Sinema has a counter, you`re right, they should say what the counter is.\par \par \uc2 SHAKIR: Yes, I mean, I face this every night with my children. It`s kind of like debating with children. Like, if you start with a here`s what you should eat for dinner, maybe carrots and broccoli. How about mac and cheese? How about the spaghetti and meatballs? They say, no, no, no, I need you to work with me. You can`t go to bed hungry. It`s like, you got to give me something to work with. And I 