 Los Angeles Times\uc2 " was there at the scene to witness the people streaming in, to witness the huge crowds converging, ready to make themselves heard.\par \par \uc2 That reporter, Del Quentin Wilber, from "The L.A. Times" was able to capture the scene as we closed in on that noon witching hour. He captured the scene this way at 11:33 a.m. Yeah. That was it.\par \par \uc2 And this isn`t just like a shot that was taken in the wrong direction or a shot taken of the sidelines. See what we`re pointing out there? That is the big screen and that`s the stage. That`s the center of the main event less than a half hour before the whole thing was supposed to kick off. Where is everybody?\par \par \uc2 Now, to be fair, this was 11:33 a.m. Things did fill out a little bit by the time noon actually rolled around. But not by that much. This was as big as the whole thing ever got on Saturday. This was around 1:00 p.m. about an hour into it.\par \par \uc2 When there is a big march or rally in Washington, a big gathering sometimes news outlets will use like, you know, crowd size experts or aerial views of the crowd to do a bunch of complicated math to try to approximate the size of the crowd.\par \par \uc2 In this case you could just literally just kind of eenie, meenie, miney moe, count them all up by hand. \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  said in total, there were around 400 people or so milling around that site at the front of the \uc2 Capitol\uc2  on Saturday, but most of the 400 were not actually protesters. If you actually zoom in on the crowd, this is what it looked like. It was a dozen or so people with nice cameras, dozen or so reporters all pointing cameras at like lone protester because there were many more members of the media at this thing than actual pro Trump protesters.\par \par \uc2 This guy was counted as two though because of him and his badger. Why wouldn`t you count the badger?\par \par \uc2 "The Washington Post" reported this weekend that the attendance was so small 