n terms of the way that challenge is going to go whether or not actual legislation is passed on Capitol Hill to bolster abortion rights, again, another one of these things that could not be more timely. All -- all really just has to happen all at once.\par \par \uc2 All right. Much more ahead tonight. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Court hearings like this one are not usually very exciting. Today, if you were like penciling it into your calendar wondering if you should pay attention to it today was technically a scheduling hearing which means it was a hearing to schedule future hearings, right? Not generally gripping stuff.\par \par \uc2 But then in the middle of today`s hearing to schedule more hearings, the defense lawyer held up a manila envelope in hand in the courtroom and announced it was represented to us by the district attorney that this package includes documents that were found in co-conspirators` basements. Tax documents that go directly to the issues.\par \par \uc2 New, key documents just handed over to the defense today. Documents found in basements. Basements plural from co-conspirators plural. This is getting more interesting.\par \par \uc2 This court hearing today was the first court appearance by the top financial executive at Donald Trump`s family company. A man named Allen Weisselberg as the first court appearance by him since he and the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  were arraigned this summer.\par \par \uc2 Both he and the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  are charged with a 15 year multi-million dollars tax evasion scheme, if you want to boil it down. Weisselberg himself has pled not guilty as have lawyers for Mr. Trump`s business.\par \par \uc2 But as to the question of whether there is going to be more coming from prosecutors in this case, well, that same defense counsel who held up the envelope today and said these key tax documents were just recovered from co-conspirators` basements, the same counsel also told the judge today, quote, we have strong reason to believe there could be other indictments coming.\par \par \uc2 We don`t know why Mr. Weisselberg`s attorney thinks that or who those might apply to. New York prosecutors have reportedly been investigating other senior \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  executives but who knows? Whatever is going on right now in case against Allen Weisselberg and the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 , the former president certainly appears to be quite upset about it, firing off an incoherent statement this weekend attacking the Manhattan district attorney by name and the New York attorney general by name, going after one of the district attorney`s lawyers by name, objecting to how many documents from his company they have examined, saying the American people will not stand for this. I think they will. We`ll see.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Dan Goldman, former assistant U.S. attorney for the Southern District of New York, in Manhattan. He is lead counsel for the first House impeachment trial into President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Goldman, it`s nice to see you. Thanks for taking time tonight.\par \par \uc2 DAN GOLDMAN, FORMER ASSISTANT U.S. ATTORNEY: Great to be with you, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: As you look at these revelations in court today, as a non-lawyer, I look at the assertion that new evidence has been found in multiple co- conspirators` basements and just handed over to the defense, the proclamation for defense counsel that there are likely more indictments coming. And I as a non-lawyer take those things on face value and think oh, looks like this case is about to get bigger and new search warrants must be executed.\par \par \uc2 [21:45:08]\par \par \uc2 Are those reasonable inferences to draw from what happened in court today?\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: You`ve hit the nail on the head, Rachel. I think you`re right. This wouldn`t ordinarily be a newsworthy conference but a lot of the statements made by Weisselberg`s attorneys to c