hildren.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Dan, when you look at the way this is proceeding, I was struck by the fact it is going to be nearly a year according to the judge today before the trial happens that just I think coincidentally has potentially sort of political implications in its timing since it is right before next year`s midterm elections if things proceed the way it looks like they are proceeding it will mean the president`s company is on criminal trial right before those elections.\par \par \uc2 The other thing that struck me is that former President Trump making these very belligerent statements about what is going on, naming the da, naming the attorney general, and then naming a specific lawyer who is working on the case in the D.A.`s office, is he running any risk by doing that, saying the American people won`t stand by this, calling it a witch hunt, proclaiming prosecutorial misconduct, naming these individual lawyers and prosecutors is he flirting with anything that might potentially get him in trouble or might be worse for the case against him by doing this in this public way?\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: Well, certainly, you know, his statements as the chief executive of the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  could in theory be used against the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 . So anything he says could be used at a trial against the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And, yes. You cannot threaten prosecutors in any way, shape, or form. That is a crime. The question, you know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has for many years escaped a lot of liability or guilt because he speaks like a mob boss. And he doesn`t say things directly but he says things with enough of a hint or inference so that the people who are following him or around him understand him. \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  has talked about this at length.\par \par \uc2 So I don`t think that statement itself is chargeable but if it continues and gets worse, yes. You potentially have some degree of obstruction of justice or threats against state officials that is illegal.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Dan Goldman, former assistant U.S. attorney in the Southern District of New York, Dan, it`s nice to see you. Thanks for helping us understand this tonight.\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: Thanks, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: We`ll be right back. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [21:54:01]\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: This is so well done. Here`s the headline on the editorial. An appeal to vaccinated "Detroit Free Press" readers. About 51 percent of Michigan`s 10 million residents are fully vaccinated against COVID. If you`re reading today`s Detroit free press, you`re probably one of them. The pandemic that`s extinguished 660,000 American lives continues, but you`ve done the most important thing ordinary citizens can do to slow its transmission and limit its terrible toll. We are all confronted a deadly problem, you`ve chosen to be part of the solution.\par \par \uc2 Now we need you to play a bigger part. The impact of your initiative will be significantly diminished unless a significant number of the approximately 3 million eligible Michiganders who are so far declined vaccinations overcome their reservations. And after nine months in which neither the manifest risk of death nor the pleas of epidemiological experts, elected leaders and mainstream media have turned the tide, it`s increasingly apparent that you, the vaccinated are the last best hope to overcome the hesitance and distrust of the unvaccinated minority.\par \par \uc2 [21:55:03]\par \par \uc2 We are seeking to enlist you in a benevolent experience, a team effort to persuade our unvaccinated friends, family members and work colleagues to join in the unfinished war against COVID.\par \par \uc2 "The Detroit Free Press" this weekend asking its readers to join a benevolent conspiracy of vaccinated people in the state to persuade the unvaccinated people in their lives to please get the shot. And alongside the editorial they 