rs or destroying the definition of public property because they were victims then and now, political prisoners.\par \par \uc2 As always, we have a lot of news to cover tonight, but I need you to know why today`s rally disgusted me to no end.\par \par \uc2 In the last decades, where we`ve marched and protested, just take the last few years when we protested whether it was around George Floyd`s murder last year in Minneapolis or Michael Brown`s killing by policemen in Ferguson, Missouri, and some went out and looted and destroyed property. We denounced it.\par \par \uc2 We never protested that they who were prosecuted for looting or prosecuted for destroying property were somehow political prisoners. We said that is not what we`re about.\par \par \uc2 If we had, imagine if National Action Network, my group or Black Lives Matter, or \uc2 NAACP\uc2 , had called for rallies to defend those that burned stores in in Minneapolis last year and we had a rally calling them political prisoners, we would have been totally castigated, and rightfully so, and that`s what we saw today.\par \par \uc2 The takeaway from that story today should not be that yes, they didn`t have a lot of violence, just a few arrests. And yes, their numbers were embarrassing. The takeaway is they were there defending insurrectionists, violent people who had a day that caused the death of five people, that Capitol Police were injured.\par \par \uc2 They were calling for the death of the sitting Vice President of their party, and the Speaker of the House. That`s the takeaway, and for those people to rally around them, like they were worthy of some political prisoner status is disgusting.\par \par \uc2 But those of us that just three weeks ago, we brought 50,000 and not one utterance of violence and not one support of it.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got another big show as the Justice Department prepares to go all in on Texas, over abortion and voting rights, and the continued inequities around COVID-19 vaccination and mask mandates were brought to the forefront this week, not by politics, but pop culture.\par \par \uc2 All of that tonight on POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 But first, joining me now is Congressman Troy Carter, Democrat of Louisiana. Congressman Carter, first of all, our thanks for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Watching the so-called Justice for J6 rally today, would you agree that the response from law enforcement, conservative media, and even some of your Republican colleagues in Congress would be vastly different if it was, instead of an overwhelmingly white Trump supporters and right-wingers, that was a Civil Rights group or Black Lives Matter or National Action Network out there earlier today, advocating on behalf of black extremists who showed up with the gallows and stormed the United States Capitol and desecrated its offices and threatened Republican lawmakers including the Republican Vice President with bodily harm, even death. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 REP. TROY CARTER (D-LA): Reverend Sharpton, I think we know the answer to that. We would have a massacre. We would have bloodshed. You would have the kind of atrocities that would have given us memories of the past where there were mass beatings of African-Americans where we are fighting for civil rights where there was non-violence, but we were met with violence and bloodshed.\par \par \uc2 It`s amazing that the level of violence that came from the insurrectionists were met with such kid gloves.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:14]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Let me go to this, Congressman, I sat out with Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  this week along with \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2 , and his wife, Mrs. Arndrea Waters King, and as President of the National Action Network, I was there and our press to key policing reforms priority rise in Congress, specifically those contained in the \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  Justice and Policing Act. We also talked about voting rights, but we focused on this Policing Act, which was stalled in the Senate since passing the House in June.\par \par \uc2 Now, according to grio.com, Senators \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  and Tim Scott will meet next week to discuss the bill, while "Rolling Stone" Magazine reports President Biden is leaning on Manchin and Senator \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  to abandon the filibuster, so this bill along with voting rights protections can pass without any Republican support.\par \par \uc2 Can you explain to our audience the political strategy and the urgency behind all of this inactivity?\par \par \uc2 CARTER: Well, Reverend Sharpton, the reality is we can`t afford to wait. We can`t afford to have logistics or gamesmanship, denied justice any further. So using whatever resources we can, manipulating the process to make things work, to advance issues that are important, such as the \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  Policing Act, making sure that filibustering and those kind of manipulations don`t get in the way.\par \par \uc2 We know how important this is, and this is important to our entire society. So, people have to lean on their Members of Congress, black, white, Republican or Democrat, to understand the significance of letting justice find its place through this process.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, let`s go to your home state. Congressman, right there where you are, where for a month you and other state Democrats have been calling on the Justice Department to investigate the disproportionate use of force and the detention against black residents by the Louisiana State Police, often with bodycam footage that is long withheld from public view, whether it`s Ronald Greene`s death in custody two years ago, the Justice Department now investigating whether the State Police covered for the officers involved in Greene`s arrest.\par \par \uc2 And then last month, we saw footage of another encounter that occurred just three weeks after Greene`s death, in which motorist, Aaron Larry Bowman was beaten 18 times with a police flashlight, despite being heard, repeating quote, "I`m not resisting. I`m not resisting" in the graphic bodycam footage that was only recently obtained by the Associated Press.\par \par \uc2 I`ve learned from the AP`s investigation that nearly 70 percent of State Police arrests are of black people. What is going on in Louisiana, Congressman?\par \par \uc2 CARTER: I think this department has a pattern, a culture of abuse. Listen, all of the men and women are not bad, but we certainly have a culture of abuse that has been allowed to fester. And now, it`s time that we take the necessary steps to remove the investigation from the hands of the very people who have violated it.\par \par \uc2 I`ve asked the Attorney General to do a patterns of practice investigation to determine and evaluate those practices that we have seen all too often in Louisiana, where people`s civil rights have been abused, where we`ve seen cover ups that we continue to see each day, each month, a little bit more come out that demonstrates that this pattern and this culture is real, and we have to stop it and we can no longer have the fox guarding the henhouse.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Congressman, you sit on the House Transportation and Infrastructure Committee. Back in May you met with President Biden as he stumped in Louisiana for his infrastructure plan, which you endorsed and voted for.\par \par \uc2 Three months later, your state has been battered by Hurricane Ida, as it has been countless storms and that`s predicted to worsen due to climate change. What do you tell your constituents weary from seeing their lives disrupted again, when they ask you how this plan will improve their lives?\par \par \uc2 [17:10:00]\par \par \uc2 CARTER: Well, I would say that the one thing that we will do more than anything else is make sure that we correct the inequities that we saw during Katrina, where communities of black and brown who were disproportionately left out of the discussion or certainly not given the same level of resources as others, and we are going to make sure that doesn`t happen again.\par \par \uc2 We`ve had great conversations with the President on his recent visit, as we walked through communities in my district, with the Administrator of F.E.M.A. Just a few days ago, we had Secretary of Housing and Urban Development, Marcia Fudge here on the ground, walking through and seeing firsthand demonstrating that it will not be business as usual. We have gone round and round with F.E.M.A. in making sure that we`re tweaking and we`re fixing many of the problems that we found.\par \par \uc2 I`m confident that the President of the United States when he says that he is going to put the full faith and credit of the United States behind rebuilding Louisiana, and not only rebuilding it, but building it back better.\par \par \uc2 This infrastructure plan that we`re navigating now is a plan that should be, by all accounts recognized as a bipartisan once in a lifetime opportunity to rebuild our infrastructure, our roads, our bridges, but also those social infrastructures, housing, opportunities for broadband, creating opportunities for economic development, and making sure that all parts of the community have an opportunity to participate in the rebuilding of our devastated state.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I`m out of time, but Congressman, quickly, please. You represent St. James Parish, home to several of the industrial plants that make up your state`s so-called Cancer Alley. We had E.P.A. Administrator Michael Regan with us last weekend, and I brought up the recently stalled push to bring a $10 million plastics complex to the region viewed by many residents, activists, and elected officials as an immense environmental threat in a district where cancer rates are 50 times the national average according to the E.P.A.\par \par \uc2 The permit for the project was suspended last month by the Biden administration pending a review. But as you weigh economic needs versus environmental justice, is it realistic that even a smaller version of such a project could come to your district and not add to sickness like this?\par \par \uc2 CARTER: Listen, we know that there has been significant issues with plants up and down the river. We know that the residents have had legitimate concerns about the carcinogens that are released into the air, but we also know that with proper enforcement, with the use of technology, carbon capture and many others, that we`re able to balance that to make sure that a person should not have to and will not have to literally die for their job.\par \par \uc2 When we balance economy over the environmental concern, we have to make sure that we balance it and put the community at the forefront to make sure that people aren`t dying for the jobs, making sure that we are bringing those polluters to justice and cleaning neighborhoods, while recognizing that commerce will be commerce at least to support it, but not over the safety and wellbeing of people who live in those communities.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And the lives of the people in those communities.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Congressman Troy Carter for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is my political panel. Michelle Goldberg, "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " columnist and MSNBC political analyst, and Rina Shah, former Republican strategist and founder and principal of Relaxed Strategies.\par \par \uc2 The Department of Justice filed suit this week in a Texas District Court for an emergency injunction against their unconstitutional abortion ban. The filing included the stories of actual harm done to women seeking abortions, according -- including one woman facing violence from her husband and trying to sell personal items to discreetly leave the state.\par \par \uc2 Michelle, will the inclusion of real-time harms in the filing move the needle on this decision? Will it have a meaningful impact as well on public opinion on this?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE GOLDBERG, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: So I do think it will have a meaningful impact on public opinion. I watched a focus group recently and was really struck, you know, people who follow politics closely, I think, are aware of just how draconian and cruel this ban is.\par \par \uc2 But one of the clever things that the Supreme Court did by sort of, you know, making it a procedural issue that they -- you know, a procedural thing that went out in the middle of the night is that you didn`t have the headlines you would have if Roe versus Wade were overturned and there is a surprising number of people who just don`t know what`s going on in Texas.\par \par \uc2 As for whether the Justice Department lawsuit will be successful, I will make no predictions because the people I know who know the most about the Supreme Court were quite sure that they were going to enjoin this law because it is so outrageous.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:23]\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: And even after the Supreme Court initially refused to act, people were saying, well, just give it a few days. You know, at some point this week, I`m sure that they`re going to stay it, again, because it`s just such a blatant violation of precedent, and yet we saw the Supreme Court refuse to hear the -- refuse to hear the people who are asking to put this law on hold.\par \par \uc2 And so I think that, you know, the train has left the station, I don`t think we know what the Supreme Court is capable of.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Republicans in Pennsylvania subpoenaing the private data of every voter in the state information including name, date of birth, driver`s license, number, address, date of last voting activity, and even the last four digits of their Social Security number to hand over to an unnamed private contractor in their, quote, "investigation" of the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 The "Philadelphia Inquirer" is refusing to call this scheme an audit on the advice of nonpartisan experts. Rina, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won Pennsylvania by more than 80,000 votes and that result was already audited. So, what`s the point of this?\par \par \uc2 RINA SHAH, FORMER REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST: This is performative. This is for a base that is absolutely, completely engrossed with the past, and I`ve been part of the local party and I know exactly how these people function. They`ve been part of parties for a long time, the structure, the leadership structure is what I`m talking about, and they are frustrated by the lack of ability, they have to overturn the free and fair election, which elected \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  from the State of Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 This is what we`re talking about here is the sham audit that many Republicans think are still acceptable in the year 2021. We are nearly one year past this election and these Republicans continue to grasp at straws. Why? Because this is going to continue to help them fundraise. This keeps their base angry and almost sort of mobilize, because this is exactly what we see when we look at rural pockets of Republicans, this anger and this frustration.\par \par \uc2 So I`ll say this, this is a nothing burger. It`s a black mark on the face of the Pennsylvania G.O.P., which I thought in previous years used to be very sensible. But the reality is this, you attend a Conservative Political Action Conference every year, and you would see this exact type of frustration that these party leaders carry, cycle into cycle.\par \par \uc2 They fundraise off of it, they mobilize voters off of it, and frankly, they`re even activating donor voters with it.\par \par \uc2 I think a lot about this, Rev. This is a question that is hitting time and again, and particularly this past week with Congressman Gonzalez announcing his retirement, talking about the toxic state of the party, nationally. We look at it regionally and also locally. You see that same toxicity.\par \par \uc2 I`ve been thinking a lot about, is the Republican voter of today the Republican voter tomorrow? And perhaps I`m a little too optimistic, but I think the answer is no. I think there will come a point at which the pendulum will swing and these younger folks who have been mobilized with anger and frustration and revere will say, no more, we need to do something different.\par \par \uc2 And that is what I`m hopeful for. I really hope we`ll see some of those strides in different parts of the country, particularly maybe Western Republicans, as we lead up to 2022 midterms.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Today`s so-called rally in Washington was organized by a former Trump campaign official and meant to valorize the insurrectionists who tried to stop the certification of the election on January 6, and while there were not elected Republicans in attendance like they were on January 6th, the G.O.P. has also refused to condemn these people, Michelle.\par \par \uc2 Briefly, please, are they offering tacit endorsement of political violence by not doing so?\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: I don`t think they`re offering tacit endorsement. I think, they are offering open endorsement in many cases. You know, it`s true that some -- that no Republican Members of Congress spoke. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  warned people away as a setup, but he did it in a statement in which he also spoke about how unfairly these insurrectionists were being treated.\par \par \uc2 You know, there are many people on the right, including Members of Congress who have spoken about people who were arrested for the January 6th riot as political prisoners who have tried to turn Ashli Babbitt, one of them, into a martyr.\par \par \uc2 And so I think that that -- you know, the minority position, the fringe position in today`s Republican Party are the few Members of Congress who are condemning that riot outright.\par \par \uc2 [17:20:11]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Michelle Goldberg and Rina Shah, thank you both.\par \par \uc2 Coming up on POLITICSNATION, Republicans are licking their wounds after a botched attempt to unseat California Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , but they seem to have a new ally in their misinformation campaign against COVID-19 vaccine.\par \par \uc2 I have some words for my friend, Miss Nicki Minaj that you`re going to want to hear.\par \par \uc2 But first, my colleague, Richard Lui with today`s top news stories -- Richard.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LUI, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Rev, a good Saturday afternoon to you.\par \par \uc2 Stories that we`re watching for this hour: The United States has reached over 42 million cases of COVID. Idaho health officials say over there in the state, there`s only ICU beds of 91 open statewide. Wednesday, more than 700 residents were hospitalized with the virus. This, as an F.D.A. panel recommends booster shots for those at high risk.\par \par \uc2 SpaceX`s first tourist space voyage is about to end in a couple of hours. The spaceship is expected to splash down off Florida`s coast around 7:00 p.m. Eastern. Four private citizens took part in that three day trip, including billionaire Jared Isaacman.\par \par \uc2 And the season`s 15th tropical storm has formed in the Mid-Atlantic. Odette should remain at sea and not make landfall, but the Mid-Atlantic area expect to see strong rip currents and rough surf conditions this weekend and early next week.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  right after a short break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: For this week`s "Got-cha," I want to talk about the most recent failure of the Republican Party, their botched attempt to recall the Governor of California because there are lessons to be learned from this spectacular misfire, and what happened to Republicans in California, the home of Hollywood magic, could be a preview of coming attractions.\par \par \uc2 So first, let`s review. Knowing they had no chance of success in a regular election, California Republicans tried to recall Democratic Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  and the voters gave the idea two thumbs down, way down.\par \par \uc2 Nearly two-thirds of voters wanted Newsom to stay in office and the Republican effort to make this recall a referendum on Newsom`s COVID response backfired spectacularly. They tried to portray Newsom like some maniacal movie villain, drunk with power and overstepping his authority when it came to pandemic regulations.\par \par \uc2 But the voters saw a different story. Forty seven percent of them thought Newsom`s approach was right on and 18 percent thought it wasn`t strict enough. Confronted with the ragtag ensemble of anti-vaxxers and anti- maskers on the Republican slate, those voters decided to stick with Newsom.\par \par \uc2 Just about two thirds of the state population is fully vaccinated, almost exactly the margin that voted for Newsom in the recall election, because most Californians take this pandemic seriously, and most Americans do, too.\par \par \uc2 Nearly two thirds of the adult population is fully vaccinated and three quarters of us have at least one dose. And we are tired of watching so many Republicans indulge the small minority of people who refuse to take the pandemic seriously putting us all at risk.\par \par \uc2 But just because vaccinated Californians overwhelmingly chose to keep the Democrat in power, does that mean the rest of the country will follow suit? Recent polling on President Biden`s vaccination initiatives show majority support for vaccine mandates for school workers, healthcare workers and government employees, all while the loudest wing of the Republican Party seems to take the side of the virus, and that`s where the recent spate of voting suppression laws are coming from.\par \par \uc2 Republicans know that their policy approach is unpopular, and in a state like California, where everyone gets a no excuse mail-in ballot, they can win. They`ve been saying as much in public on camera for years, when every eligible American can vote, Republicans know they will lose. That`s why it`s so imperative that we overcome their scheming and their lies, and pass voter protection laws.\par \par \uc2 Every American deserves a voice and a vote, and those of us who want this pandemic to end can see which cast of characters are taking it seriously.\par \par \uc2 So to Republicans, when you lose in 2022 and beyond, you can`t blame Hollywood magic, but good all American perseverance, and patriotism, and your own hubris.\par \par \uc2 I got-cha.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:33:16]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION. As we near yet another fall season while battling coronavirus, the F.D.A. announced that those who are 65 and older or at high risk should get a third jab of the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  shot. However, the advisory committee did not recommend the booster dose for the rest of the population.\par \par \uc2 All this happening as many Americans remain unvaccinated and hospitalizations continue to overwhelm many ICU across the country. Joining me now to unpack this all is MSNBC medical contributor and founder of Advancing Health Equity, Dr. Uche Blackstock.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Blackstock, as of today, one in every 500 Americans have died from COVID-19. That`s a grim statistic as the U.S. has seen an overall total of more than 676,000 deaths. Your thoughts about these numbers a year and a half into this pandemic.\par \par \uc2 DR. UCHE BLACKSTOCK, MSNBC MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Thank you for having me on, Reverend Al. You know, these numbers are a travesty, and I think that they signify a failure of our government to adequately protect its citizens.\par \par \uc2 You know, we are seeing death rates in cases that rival earlier this year and that are higher than times last year. And we did think with the change in administration that there would be changes and improvements, but you know, I think that the delta variant and the fact that vaccinations have stalled are factors, but also we need to consider other policies like mask policies, capacity restrictions.\par \par \uc2 [17:35:08]\par \par \uc2 BLACKSTOCK: We are essentially wide open as a society, and that`s why we`re seeing the case numbers that we`re seeing.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, this week, rapper, Nicki Minaj and others in entertainment told fans various things in that she said she did not attend the Met Gala since she was not vaccinated and remained skeptical. To say the least about why she has not gotten the vaccine, citing a story about a side-effect her cousin`s friend who got vaccinated had was actually factually called incorrect by some authorities.\par \par \uc2 Let me tell you my thoughts. I`m not a friend, but I am a fan of Nicki Minaj. I have a lot of respect for her artistry, and she broke through barriers. And I`m not going to get into the fact that the right-wing have embraced an attack for that, because I don`t think that serves what we really are concerned about on all sides, and that is the life of people disproportionately in our community.\par \par \uc2 But I am going to say to name call \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2 , someone that I`ve known and have seen even before she came to this station, I`m not saying this because she`s a colleague, I knew her in Black Radio in Florida 20 years ago, and she`s put her career and neck on the line to cover stories and bring things mainstream, that`s across the line.\par \par \uc2 I think that if people want to raise questions, fine, but leave the name calling out of it, particularly someone that has had the, in my opinion, the model example of journalism that Joy Ann Reid has.\par \par \uc2 They are two black women that have gotten to the top of their field. We don`t need name calling. We need solutions as we see disproportionate people die in our communities, and in every community in the country.\par \par \uc2 Now, let me get back to this, Doctor. I know you and your sister wrote an op-ed about Miss Minaj. Tell us your thoughts.\par \par \uc2 BLACKSTOCK: Yes. So Reverend Al, my thoughts are that we absolutely need to keep the lines of communication open and what I`m saying is that in our op- ed, we said that as black physicians and healthcare professionals, we are here to give out education that is accurate and responsible about the COVID vaccine, and we are asking that like music artists, those with platforms use their platforms responsibly.\par \par \uc2 We`re here to give them that information, but we also need to hold them accountable for putting out potentially inaccurate information, that we need to work together as a community to encourage COVID vaccine uptake among black Americans because as we know, we have been disproportionately impacted in this pandemic. And you know, we`re seeing worse and worse numbers, especially even right now, disproportionately still affecting black Americans.\par \par \uc2 So we need to work together, come together to really end this pandemic.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And if you`ve got a big platform, you`re blessed, you should not misuse your blessings. You should really try to do what you can even if you`ve got to ask questions. I asked questions at the beginning of this, but you should be careful about the information you put out.\par \par \uc2 Now President Biden has announced a vaccine mandate for all Federal employees. On Wednesday, he met with top business executives, companies like \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  and \uc2 Disney\uc2  that have also pushed vaccine mandates for employees.\par \par \uc2 The meeting was meant to encourage other companies to show that vaccine mandates are good for the economy. What are your thoughts on that?\par \par \uc2 BLACKSTOCK: So yes, so I think vaccine mandates are just good for the economy. They`re good for public health. You know, we saw vaccinations stalling and we needed them -- the vaccine requirement as a mandate to increase the number of those people vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 Look, we need about 80 to 90 percent of the population vaccinated, and we are far from that. And so these vaccine requirements or mandates are absolutely necessary to keep workplaces safe and to help us -- one of the ways for us to get out of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Dr. Blackstock, thank you for being with us tonight.\par \par \uc2 BLACKSTOCK: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Up next, Hispanic Heritage Month is in full swing and so are Republicans` efforts to suppress the Latino vote. Our next guest explains the powerful impact of the Latinx community after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:43:06]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Democrats in California are breathing a little easier this weekend as the Golden State voters shot down a Republican-led recall election against Governor Gavin Newson earlier this week. But while Newsom clinched his victory in a landslide, his support from California`s Latinx voters was underwhelming when compared to previous years.\par \par \uc2 The Governor attributing the gap to disproportionate impact of COVID on brown communities telling CBS News that, quote: "Latino voters felt left out of the recovery."\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Maria Teresa Kumar, President and CEO of Voto Latina, also an MSNBC contributor. Miss Kumar, always glad to have you join us before we get to your group`s legal actions against Texas over voter suppression law, which I want to get to, some Democrats are wanting a canary in the coal mine with regards to what they view as a lackluster showing from Latinx voters in this week`s recall election in California with our own NBC News exit polling suggesting that Latino voters sided with Newsom and against the recall 60 to 40 percent.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " is reporting that 63 percent of people of color and 60 percent of Latinos who supported Newsom is quote, "the weakest for a California Democrat in memory."\par \par \uc2 Is it fair to suggest that the Democrats need to do more to reach out to voters of color specifically America`s brown voters?\par \par \uc2 MARIA TERESA KUMAR, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: We`ve had this conversation, first of all, thanks for having me on, but we`ve had this conversation before. Part of the challenge is that campaigns come in and they start talking to brown communities and black communities towards the end of it.\par \par \uc2 Voto Latino, we did get a call from the Governor`s office and we -- from his recall campaign, excuse me, not from the government -- from the recall campaign, the Stop the Republican Recall, and we came in mid-August, and we invested in getting out the Latino vote, and we did see a surge at the end.\par \par \uc2 [17:45:21]\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: But our recommendations always start early, start as soon as you can, because as you and I both know, African-American and Latinos and Asians, we have been at the frontlines, we`ve been essential workers we are trying to manage so much. And this idea then that we`re supposed to, at the same time, pay attention to politics, it may sound easy, but when you`re just trying to put food on the table and making sure you`re keeping your house over, you know, ahead over your children and they are back to school, there`s a lot of different forces that people seem to take for granted that black and brown communities are dealing with every single day, and that is just survival.\par \par \uc2 And so our recommendation to the Democrats has always been the same. You have to talk to Latinos year-round, and you have to engage them constantly. And that`s why you did see at the end of the campaign, a surge. Again, it`s not -- it`s -- you know, the circumstances at this time because it was a nail biter, we would have preferred early, but we didn`t know that when you start talking to Latinas and talk about the stakes and talk about the contrast of \uc2 Larry Elder\uc2 , my goodness, there`s no competition right there\par \par \uc2 And I know that while we are having, you know, these difficulties of trying to get out of the pandemic, \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  has been able to start delivering to the Latino community making sure that people are vaccinated, and he still talks about the importance of eviction moratoriums, and he is talking about making sure that people have access to healthcare, regardless of their legal status.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes, well, I tell the Democrats when it comes to black and brown voters, Latino voters come early and stay late. But now it`s unfortunate that Latinos in Texas must celebrate this year`s Hispanic Heritage Month under legislative siege.\par \par \uc2 Your organization, Voto Latino, is one of several Latino civil rights groups that filed a Federal lawsuit against Texas this month over its new voter suppression law, contending that the electoral changes stemming from this law are explicitly designed to restrict black and Hispanic voters` access to the franchise in the state`s most diverse jurisdictions.\par \par \uc2 Can you explain how this law specifically goes after Latino voters in Texas?\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: I will share with you something. In Texas last year, it is the hardest to vote state in the Union, full stop. Yet, they were able to certify a free and fair election saying that the election results of 2020 were legitimate. That should give us all pause. Why now are they trying to take the vote away?\par \par \uc2 Because we saw a huge increase in youth in Latino, in Asian, and black voters in Texas. And just right now, Reverend, we have -- just when you look at the Latino community, we have an additional quarter of a million Latino youth that will be eligible to vote by mid next year when Greg Abbott is on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 The reason Voto Latino is suing is that they have made it very clear that they do not want brown and black individuals enfranchising themselves. They are taking -- as an example, they are taking away the drop off ballot box only in Harris County, the most diverse county in the country.\par \par \uc2 They are not taking those drop boxes away in the surrounding counties that are predominantly white. We must step back and ask ourselves why.\par \par \uc2 This orchestrated process that`s not just hitting Texas, it`s hitting Georgia, it`s hitting Arizona, and the biggest culprits behind it are the Heritage Action Fund, part of \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2 . This is a bill in a box.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, before I lose you, another pressing issue, abortion.\par \par \uc2 The abortion battle in Texas, and I was in Houston last weekend, it is directly impacting other states, with as many as 7,000 non-residents coming to California for access to reproductive freedom.\par \par \uc2 Maria, briefly, please, you sit on the National Board of Planned Parenthood. What do you know about the population of women and girls whose reproductive rights are being restricted, many of which are low income black and brown residents.\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: The majority of the people that are getting restricted are black and brown women because we are disproportionately at the lower end of the economic scale. If I were to be, you know, if I were to talk to a girlfriend of mine who was white, she is more than likely going to be far more fluent than the people that aren`t being targeted right now, and that is why we should all be locking arms, all women, because they start with the poor, but it doesn`t mean that`s where they end.\par \par \uc2 And this idea that a woman has to drive from Texas to California already tells you who can afford to do that? And that tells us that right now and for folks that may not be aware the Texas law is so foul that an Uber driver, they technically get fined for bringing a woman --\par \par \uc2 [17:50:18]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: An Uber driver, it`s amazing. It is that repressive. Maria Teresa Kumar, thank you for being with us as always.\par \par \uc2 Up next, my final thoughts.\par \par \uc2 But first of quick programming note, my colleague, Ayman Mohyeldin is taking his reporting to the weekend. His new show kicks off tonight where he will dive into the pro-Trump rally earlier today and the status of negotiations surrounding the economic bill stalled in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 You can watch "Ayman" Saturdays at 8:00 p.m. Eastern Time, Sundays at 9:00 Eastern Time only on MSNBC. Tell Ayman that the Rev said Amen to Ayman.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: About 10 days or so before the big march on voter rights march of August 28th where 50,000 marched in Washington and thousands around the country, we met with Senator \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  -- Martin Luther King, III and Arndrea Waters King and I -- to talk about he needed to vote for the \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  Voting Advancement Act. He said he wanted to work on a bill that would bring in everyone and they`ve come up now with the Freedom to Vote Act, which is mostly good, things he could live with.\par \par \uc2 [17:55:14]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: He says, I think I can craft a bill with others that he is working with, that I can get Republicans to vote, and I even jumped at him at the end of the meeting, saying I`m a man of faith, not a man of fantasy. I don`t believe you can get Republicans, we`ll see.\par \par \uc2 Well, they came up with a bill this week, and so far, not one -- nada -- Republican has said they would vote for this bill.\par \par \uc2 You have until the beginning of next week, Senator Manchin, and if you can`t come up with a Republican, you have the moral obligation, you have the real weight of this nation to say, I did my best, people in the civil rights community gave me the benefit of the doubt. Now that I can`t come up with 10, not even two Republicans that I will support the changing of the filibuster, eradicating it, or at the least to carve around it, our right to vote cannot be stopped.\par \par \uc2 And you have been given the belief by some of us that we give you the benefit of the doubt. Now, doubt has showed up and the Republicans have made it clear. They do not care about the right to vote for all Americans.\par \par \uc2 Now, you have to show you do.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That does it for me. Thanks for watching. I`ll see you back here tomorrow at 5:00 p.m. Eastern for another live hour of POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 My colleague, Alicia Menendez picks up our news coverage now.\par \par \uc2 [18:00:24]\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020210919eh9i00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Last Word for September 17, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Zerlina Maxwell, Melissa Murray, Ebony Hilton, Maria Theresa  Kumar \par \uc2 7,452 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 September 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST: I hope you have a wonderful weekend. I`ll see you again on Monday. Now it`s time for "The Last Word" where Zerlina Maxwell is in for Lawrence tonight.