us information to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , about Russia and Trump in an effort to get investigative activity to hurt the 2016 campaign. Quote, there`s been no public sign that he`s found any evidence of that theory.\par \par \uc2 So, what that apparently leaves Durham with, is a lawyer, who gave the \uc2 FBI\uc2  some information, he thought they should have.\par \par \uc2 [21:25:04]\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FBI\uc2  chased that down and decided and probably nothing, and maybe that lawyer fully revealed who is representing at that time. Does it matter who is representing at the time?\par \par \uc2 As for why this case is being brought now, well, look at the clock. The meeting in question here, happened on September 19th 2016. What`s today`s date? September 15th, 2021.\par \par \uc2 Because of a five-year statute of limitations for such cases, Mr. Durham has a deadline of this weekend to bring a charge over activity from that day. Literally the statue of limitations, expires on Sunday, here it is Wednesday, and he`s rushing to get this done before the statue of limitations expires. This indictment is apparently not been filed yet. We do not have all the facts, yet whether this lawyer told the \uc2 FBI\uc2  untrue, about who his client with, whether it matters who his claim was when he gave the information for them to look into. That is something that may end up being hashed out in court.\par \par \uc2 But the big picture here, the substance and suspicion around the \uc2 Alfa Bank\uc2  stuff, is this might be evidence of some means of convert communication between a U.S. presidential campaign in a foreign entity that was trying to influence our election. If you came across that, if you had some cybersecurity expert guy who was your client and he brought you that data, whether you are working for \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  or you are working for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or you are working for Donald Duck, if you are a computer scientist or experience cybersecurity lawyer, who had