 HOST: And that`s tonight`s REIDOUT. "ALL IN WITH CHRIS HAYES" starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST: Tonight on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GAVIN NEWSOM (D-CA): We defeated Trump, but we haven`t defeated Trumpism. And I want folks to know that`s what`s really behind this recall.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The huge political stakes of California`s recall election as a Republican Party radicalizes behind the big lie.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Whether or not you win or lose, will you accept the results of the election tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 LARRY ELDER (R-CA), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: I think we all ought to be looking at election integrity. Then, the thin line between anti-vax mandate and just anti-vaxx erased in real time in Florida.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The vaccine changes your RNA, so for me, that`s a problem.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Definitely not true, but how Republican governors are trying to square the circle on COVID vaccines. And as the newest justice standing next to Mitch McConnell claims the court isn`t partisan. The oldest justice still refuses to retire.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN BREYER\uc2 , ASSOCIATE JUSTICE, U.S. SUPREME COURT: I don`t intend to die on the court. I don`t think I`ll be there forever.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The ridiculous state of the Supreme Court when ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York, I`m Chris Hayes. Today is the last day of the California gubernatorial recall campaign. And you may not live in California. You`re asking well, OK, what about me? But it is the first eve of the first big political test of the post-Trump era because elections one year into a new president`s term, they are often a very early indicator of which way the wind is blowing, which way politics are forming for the new administration in the White House.\par \par \uc2 You may remember during President Barack Obama`s first year in office, it`s the last time that we had a first-term Democratic president, in that fall of that year, we saw this huge surprise upset in the race for governor of New Jersey with the election of Republican Chris Christie which, you know, foretold some troubles ahead for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 So, there are huge political stakes to tomorrow`s election and also obviously huge substantive ones. California voters are choosing who will lead the fifth largest economy in the entire world, the most populous state in the union with 40 million residents. They`re choosing whether or not among other things to continue the battle to suppress the COVID virus or to essentially wave the white flag as some pro-Trump Republican governors have done.\par \par \uc2 Those pro-trump right-wing forces are the ones behind this election. They organize a recall against Democratic Governor Gavin Newsom. And on the campaign trail, the governor has been framing this race as a fight against Trumpism.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM: Trumpism is still alive and well in this country. We defeated Trump but we haven`t defeated Trumpism. And I want folks to know that`s what`s really behind this recall. And that`s why we have to reject it and vote no.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, what Gavin Newsom is saying is true but of course it is also in his interest, obviously, to frame the race as a referendum on the former president because Donald Trump lost California by nearly 30 points last year. The state has become a Democratic stronghold last voting for a Republican presidential candidate in 1988 and last electing a Republican governor in a recall race in 2006. And there`s a good reason for that.\par \par \uc2 The kinds of candidates who are getting the most traction in the current Republican Party like Donald Trump are increasingly anathema to general election voters in states like California, not to mention places like New Jersey, Virginia where Republicans held statewide office just a few years ago.\par \par \uc2 In California, the candidate that is sort of popping out is uh front runner Larry Elder. He`s a Republican, a conservative talk radio host with zero political experience, zero governing experience. He mentored former Trump advisor Stephen Miller. He`s against abortion rights, the minimum wage, and vaccine mandates.\par \par \uc2 And the polling shows that those positions are not appealing to voters in California, not surprising. Governor Gavin Newsom has been steadily gaining ground particularly since he announced vaccine mandates for health care and education workers last month and contrasted that with the flailing approaches of Republican governors in places like Texas and Florida.\par \par \uc2 The current polling average of those who want to keep him in office is 57.5 percent to 40.8 and want him to remove him according to FiveThirtyEight. Of, course those are polls and polls can be wrong. We know that. But if we were covering this race in another state, if a Democrat were running nearly 20 points behind in a weird special election in say Idaho or South Carolina, I think we`d all be saying well, you know, that`s politics, conservative states. But that is not how the Trump Republican Party were.\par \par \uc2 No, this is now automatic. This is canon. They are already screaming fraud. The twice-impeached former president put out this statement today asking, does anybody really believe the California recall election isn`t rigged? Millions and millions of mail-in ballots will make this just another giant election scam, no different, but less blatant than the 2020 presidential election scam."\par \par \uc2 That`s interesting, less blatant. I don`t know. It`s hard to get your head around that one. Larry Elder, OK, who is the leading Republican candidate has been making the same kind of bogus claims on Fox News.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:07]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ELDER: The 2020 election in my opinion was full of shenanigans. And my fear is they`re going to try that in this election right here in recall. So, I`m urging people to go to elect elder.com. And whenever you see anything, hear anything suspicious, go to my Web site. We have a battery of lawyers. We`re going to file lawsuits in a timely fashion this time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: OK, lawsuits in a timely fashion. Go to electelder.com. In fact, Elder has been asking his supporters to go to that Web site and to sign a petition demanding the California legislature hold a special session to investigate the "the twisted results" of this 2020 recall -- 2021 recall election. The election hasn`t happened yet, just to be clear. It`s tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Today, MSNBC Correspondent Jacob Soboroff pushed elder about whether he would accept the election results and he refused to answer.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACOB SOBOROFF, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Whether or not you win or lose, will you accept the results of the election tomorrow? I think we all ought to be looking at election integrity no matter whether you`re a Democrat, an Independent, or Republican. Let`s all make sure that the election is a fair election.\par \par \uc2 So, let`s all work together no matter what the results are to make sure that the results are valid and legitimate and everybody who voted should have voted. Let`s all do that together.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Is that a commitment to accept the results of the election tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 ELDER: Let`s all do that together. Let`s all work together on both sides of the aisle to make sure that the election is a fair election.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Is that a commitment to accept the results of the election tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 ELDER: Let`s all work together to find out whether or not the election tomorrow is a fair election.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: One of my favorite things on being a political reporter is when you`re dealing with a politician who has clearly been sort of media-trained to answer one question with a certain phrase and then you see it over. Remember Marco Rubio doing that in that debate? Let`s all work together. Let`s all work together.\par \par \uc2 So, we have learned since the Trump era that whether or not people perceive elections to be free and fair, right, depends a lot on whether or not the bested party acknowledges their legitimacy. And we`ve seen this disease spreading lies about fraud and scams and illegitimacy continue to increase before our very eyes even when Trump is gone.\par \par \uc2 Just next door to California, in Nevada, Republican Senate candidate Adam Laxalt is talking about mounting legal challenges in his election which is not until next year.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ADAM LAXALT (R-NV), SENATE CANDIDATE: With me on top of the ticket, we`re going to be able to get everybody at the table and come up with a full plan to do our best to try to secure this election. Get as many observers as we can, file lawsuits early if there are lawsuits that we can file to try to tighten up the election.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, we`re going to sue early when I lose which is a weird message. The lie has become central not only the campaign of Republican candidates but also the identity of their voters. A new polling shows that 59 of Republicans say that believing that Donald Trump won the 2020 election is very or somewhat important to what being a Republican means to them.\par \par \uc2 So, that`s the strange state of our politics. You would think the Republican Party would be running away from a one-term loser of a president who oversaw the deadliest year in American history. But because Donald Trump Has so effectively manufactured this destructive big lie about the election and fraud, he still has grips on the party. And that means that our democratic norms continue to erode in extremely terrifying ways.\par \par \uc2 But it also means that he remains a big political target in ways that are probably helpful to Democrats in races like the one taking place in California tomorrow. MSNBC Correspondent Jacob Soboroff conducted that interview with Larry Elder earlier today and he joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Jacob, just to level set on this. I mean, that`s a standard question politicians get asked and the standard answer is of course, right? Could you sense his hesitancy to say that obvious thing like yes of course?\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Yes, no doubt about it, Chris. And I`m actually -- I`m glad you played that Laxalt sound as well because it brought back memories from me straight from the Trump playbook. I was in Nevada in Las Vegas when Adam Laxalt, Matt Schlapp, and Rick Grinnell, I chased the three of them to a waiting van that spirited them away when they alleged that it was an improper election in Nevada without answering a question.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, my next question for Larry Elder was, so what specifically are you alleging is wrong with the election here? I mean, I myself was inside the LA County vote by mail processing center. Is it a vote-by-mail issue? You know, we have the largest election jurisdiction in LA, not just in the state but in the nation. I saw the vote signature verification process myself.\par \par \uc2 But it`s not about specifics. And I think that`s the point. This is not about specifics for Larry Elder. It was not about specifics for Adam Laxalt, Matt Schlapp, or Rick Grinnell when they ran away in Nevada after the election. This is about saying that the election should not be trusted ahead of the election even happening.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. And there`s also an aspect to it that I find fascinating just from a straight political standpoint which is, you know, we don`t know -- first of all,. let`s be clear, we don`t know what`s going to happen tomorrow. You know, we`ve had huge polling misses in the past, right?\par \par \uc2 But to the extent the polling is accurate, right, this has not been a successful effort. It looks like it`s -- it hasn`t been successful. And normally, the story now would be, well, what did we do wrong with the messaging we should have done this and that. But if the -- if you`re already saying the story that the election was fraudulent, then you never have to deal with the normal stuff that people do, consultants, politicians, all those folks around election that doesn`t work out.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:24]\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: It feels like they know that this is slipping away because don`t get me wrong, there are very serious issues to talk about -- there are many issues you and I have talked about before in California. You have the greatest economic inequality in the nation. COVID hit the state particularly hard. Wildfires driven by climate change are raging here.\par \par \uc2 And before Democrats started to tune in and pay attention, before folks like the President of the United States showed up at events like the one that I`m at tonight, this was a much closer race in a 2-1 Democratic state.\par \par \uc2 But everything about the way that Larry Elder seems to be behaving, to me says this is an election that`s slipping out of reach, an election that might have been within reach just a couple weeks ago but they no longer feel that way.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: As someone who`s on the ground there, what changed in the terms of the dynamic? Was it just people paying attention? Was it sort of leaning into referendum on COVID, Donald Trump, etcetera?\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: You can see it behind me. The message is, this is a Republican recall and Larry Elder, the opponent, the front-running Republican opponent of Governor Newsom will be to the right. And that`s a direct quote on Donald Trump. That`s what Gavin Newsom says.\par \par \uc2 And I think that there is an all-out effort by Democrats, national Democrats. Bernie Sanders recorded a message. President Obama recorded a message. We saw Elizabeth Warren and Amy Klobuchar come out here over the course of the last couple of weeks. I think people were not paying attention here in California. People were rightly disenchanted with the way things have been going over the course of the last 18 months in California. Governor Newsom said that to me directly.\par \par \uc2 But I think that the governor has done an effective job of painting what the alternative would be, and Larry Elder is that alternative.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, Jacob Soboroff, great reporting out there. Great interview today. Thank you so much for making a little time for us.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Thanks, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: We`re now less than an hour away from the last big campaign push for Governor Newsom before tomorrow`s recall election. President Joe Biden will be joining him in long beach making his first trip to California since taking office.\par \par \uc2 The biggest Democratic names have been pulling all the stops for Newsom, Vice President Kamala Harris, of course, former senator from California, that`s her home state, campaigned alongside Newsom on Wednesday of last week. And former President Obama recorded this campaign ad.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA, FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Hello Californians. you`ve got a big choice to make by September 14th. Governor Newsom has spent the past year and a half protecting California communities. Now, Republicans are trying to recall him from office and overturn common sense COVID safety measures for health care workers and school staff.\par \par \uc2 Your vote could be the difference between protecting our kids and putting them at risk. Helping Californians recover or taking us backwards.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Juan Rodriguez, the campaign manager for California Governor Gavin Newsom`s anti-recall campaign. He joins me now from Long Beach, California where Joe Biden is going to join the governor later tonight.\par \par \uc2 I noticed one in that ad and much of the messaging for the campaign, there`s been a lot of talk about COVID and COVID restrictions. How central does your campaign want that to be on the minds of voters?\par \par \uc2 JUAN RODRIGUEZ, CAMPAIGN MANAGER, NEWSOM`S ANTI-RECALL CAMPAIGN: It`s absolutely central. I think from the moment we started the campaign, Republicans have tried to discuss this campaign about being -- you know, how the governor has led throughout the course of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 And front of that, you know, we`ve had ads talking about the governor`s restrictions on mask as well as talking about vaccine uh mandates across the state. And what we`ve seen is voters across the state and frankly all over the country believe in science and believe the public health outcomes of California has done throughout the course of the year. So, it`s been central to our messaging.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What`s the -- if you had to say what the governor hasn`t gotten right on COVID, what would it be?\par \par \uc2 RODRIGUEZ: Convincing Republicans that the science behind mask and vaccines works.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Do you think California schools should have been more in-person? I know there is a lot of criticism of that. Obviously, California has San Francisco and Los Angeles. It`s a huge unified school district. Those students were out of school for a very long period of time compared to some other states. Are you worried about the edge -- the learning loss in in those big school districts?\par \par \uc2 RODRIGUEZ: You know, from the beginning of this pandemic, one thing the governor has been pretty clear about is a desire to get kids back into classroom. Learning loss is something that you`ve seen across the country. And for that reason, he`s been really focused on working with public health officials to make sure that we update our classrooms, to make sure that we are following the guidance and are doing everything possible to protect our students so we don`t have the same public health outcomes that you see in states like Texas and in Florida.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There`s been particular focus among Latino voters in in California in the run-up to this. Now, again, that category is amorphous in many ways for a million reasons which of course you know. It does seem that there`s been some change in the -- in the polling at least. The latest poll, 66 percent of Latinos said they would not vote to recall Newsom.\par \par \uc2 That`s a from a few weeks ago. It`s a shift from previous polls that were tight. Does that track with what you guys have internally? What do you -- what do you think has caused that?\par \par \uc2 [20:15:23]\par \par \uc2 RODRIGUEZ: Well, listen, I think from the beginning, we`ve focused on building up a pretty like robust field operation. We`ve sent over 31 million text messages. We`ve had close to 25,000 people on the streets heading into Geo TV weekend. And our message has been simple. We`ve been focused on, you know, raising up awareness by just informing people that they have a ballot in the mail and they can turn it in or they can vote at an election site near their home.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What about this question of inequality? It is -- it is a state with -- by many measures the highest levels of inequality. It`s a state that does have fairly high taxes relative to other states in terms of top income earners but of course unhoused folks and billionaires sort of next to each other has become in some ways a sort of national image of parts of California. How central a problem does the governor see that? And does he bear responsibility for that -- for the persistence of it?\par \par \uc2 RODRIGUEZ: Yes, the symptoms of inequality are something I think you`re seeing in California, you`re seeing in other states. Over a year ago, we were anticipating close to a 54 billion dollar budget deficit. How California has fared, thanks to the work that the governor and the legislature have done. We`ve dedicated close to the $100 billion surplus and putting it back into communities and particularly the hardest-hit, helping small businesses, and providing the golden state stimulus.\par \par \uc2 I think what you will see tomorrow with this election outcome is the governor leaning in on his record both in how he`s letting those communities get back up on their feet and how he`s managed throughout the course of the pandemic. But inequality is something certainly that we`re going to have to be dealing for a very long time. And I think the governor here in California is -- has his leadership on display on what you can do right.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, Juan Rodriguez is the campaign manager for Governor Gavin Newsom, of course, the incumbent they are attempting a recall, the last day to vote tomorrow. If you`re watching this and you live in California, you can still vote. So, go do that. Thank you Mr. Rodriguez. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 There`s an emerging strategy among some Republicans where they won`t come out -- well, it`s not even emerging, they`re doing it for a while. They won`t come out as anti-facts because they know vaccines are good. But acknowledging that would go against the right-wing culture words, so instead they`re going with anti-vaccine mandate, fine line.\par \par \uc2 The problem is you can`t really separate the two. And no one failed quite as much as Florida governor Ron DeSantis today. You do not want to miss it. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:20:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WALLACE, ANCHOR, FOX NEWS CHANNEL: You say it`s a personal choice, in fact, to attend school in your state of Nebraska, children must be vaccinated against a number of diseases. Let me put them up on the screen. They must be vaccinated against diphtheria, tetanus and pertussis, polio, measles, mumps and rubella, hepatitis B, and chickenpox. Why is it that they`re not so objectionable and such a violation of personal freedom but Biden`s vaccine mandates are?\par \par \uc2 GOV. PETE RICKETTS (R-NE): Well, for all those that you just listed, there`s a long history that parents have had the opportunity to see how those things have been implemented.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, as Republican governor of Nebraska getting pushed in the fundamental political contradiction, Republicans have been trying to kind of dance around when it comes to vaccines, right? There`s a loud militant part of the party`s base, hardcore COVID anti-vaxxers who believe the shots are bad, they`re part of some global conspiracy, you know, I will not take it no matter what.\par \par \uc2 Those folks have the most energy, they yell the loudest. They`re not a majority. They`re not even a majority of Republicans as far as we know. Most Republican voters are not in that category. Certainly not Republican politicians like governors like Ricketts. All of whom, every single one of them were vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 Governors understand, I think, the efficacy and safety of vaccines, why they`ve taken the shot. And if you gave him truth serum in private, would likely all tell you yes, everyone should get vaccinated. That would be good. We would save lives.\par \par \uc2 But any Republican governor with aspirations for higher office can`t really lean into that simple message because if they are too vocal about their support for vaccines, they risk a backlash from the hardline fringe of the party. So, the way they have tried to square that circle is to mostly just stay silent. They`ll occasionally give support for the vaccines to kind of just get on the record. Like, yes, you should get the vaccine, but also, standing up against vaccine mandates which kind of allows them to have their cake needed too. Oh, I support the vaccine but not mandates.\par \par \uc2 In that same interview with Fox News, Nebraska`s governor said he plans to challenge President Joe Biden`s vaccine mandate for large companies in court. In Texas, Republican Governor Greg Abbott banned vaccine mandates in the state last month. In Florida, Republican Governor Ron DeSantis is now threatened to fine local officials after the city of Gainesville passed a vaccine mandate for city employees.\par \par \uc2 That`s in addition to his notorious and ludicrous attempt to stop cruise ships, cruise ships of all places, from imposing vaccine requirements for everyone who gets on the ship. That was ultimately struck down in court, thank God.\par \par \uc2 Now, Republican politicians and their allies and right-wing media know what they`re doing here. Just to be clear, they are cynically mobilizing the energy of the anti-vax movement for their own political benefit. But I can`t remember an example of this entire illusion being quite as flagrantly shoved in the faces of everyone as today in Florida.\par \par \uc2 Governor DeSantis, who much like Donald Trump, got vaccinated in private and who plainly harbors presidential ambitions and therefore cannot risk alienating his base, held an event today to protest local vaccine mandates in Gainesville, right? No mandates. And it featured a number of city employees who were refusing the shots.\par \par \uc2 And surprise, surprise, his anti-vaccine mandate event quickly turned into something of a well, anti-vaccine event.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): Thousands and thousands of people could be losing their jobs not because they haven`t performed well, in fact many of them performed heroically, but simply because of a very intrusive and I think illegal policy.\par \par \uc2 DARRIS FRIEND, EMPLOYEE, CITY OF GAINESVILLE: The vaccine changes your RNA, so for me, that`s a problem. So, I`m here with you folks. We don`t want to have the vaccine. It`s about our freedom and liberty. It`s not about the vaccine. They`re taking away our freedom and liberty little by little. It`s -- they`re using a vaccine for cover.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [20:25:13]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Oh, man, so much going on there. So, clearly, someone told him to make the argument that it`s not about the vaccine, it`s about freedom. But before he could get to that part, he had to say that it alters your RNA, which it does not, just to be very clear since we put that on our air. COVID vaccines do not alter your genetic code at all. That is false and dangerously so.\par \par \uc2 Take another look at Governor DeSantis there as he`s saying that. He is not a stupid man. He is an incredibly shrewd political operator. He understands what this man is saying. To an audience with his imprimatur standing up there is complete nonsense, he probably even understands it is dangerous destructive nonsense. But what does he do during that press conference? Absolutely nothing. Just complete and total abject cowardice and weakness.\par \par \uc2 Later, a spokesperson told a local reporter the comments do not reflect the Governor`s "opinion." Interesting, so the governor`s opinion is that they don`t alter your genetic code. So, there you go.\par \par \uc2 Next, the governor of Florida, you`ve got the guy giving the most wildly inaccurate anti-vax talking points to a massive audience. And that in a nutshell is why we are where we are as a country. There are dozens of Republican governors pulling similar stunts as their state`s battle COVID. And as Sheryl Gay Stolbergpoints out in a sweeping new piece, these governors come from some of the states with the strictest vaccine mandates in the country. She joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:30:33]\par \par \uc2 GOV. TATE REEVES (R-MS): If you look at history, every single time tyrants have tried to place an emphasis on their individuals in their country, they`ve always said, oh, we`re doing it because it`s in the best interest of our citizens.\par \par \uc2 The fact is we have a representative democracy in America and if the president wants to do this, then he should get the elected representatives to vote on it.\par \par \uc2 STUART VARNEY, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK HOST: Governor, did you just call the President of the United States a tyrant?\par \par \uc2 REEVES: I did not, Stuart. What I said was if you look back in history, that this is nothing but a tyrannical-type move by the president.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST: Republican Governor Tate Reeves in Mississippi is upset over the president`s COVID vaccine mandate. He says he will sue to stop it, calling it unconstitutional and terrifying.\par \par \uc2 He declared on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , we still believe in freedom from tyrants even though as Sheryl Stolberg points out in the New York Times there is a deep inconsistency in that argument. Mississippi has some of the strictest vaccine mandates in the nation, which have not drawn opposition from most of its elected officials. Not only does it require children to be vaccinated against measles mumps and seven other diseases to attend school, but it goes a step further than most states by barring parents from claiming religious, philosophical or conscientious exemptions.\par \par \uc2 In fact, every single state in our nation has some kind of legally mandated vaccine for children to go to school and yet, there are nearly two dozen Republican governors who have vowed to block President Biden`s vaccine requirement.\par \par \uc2 Sheryl Gay Stolberg Washington correspondent for the New York Times, author of the aforementioned piece titled GOP seethes as Biden mandate even in states requiring other vaccines joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Sheryl, can you lay out a little bit -- I thought it was a great piece of reporting how this has been received in states.\par \par \uc2 SHERYL GAY STOLBERG, WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT, NEW YORK TIMES: Well, as you mentioned, Republican governors are really up in arms over President Biden`s vaccine mandates in particular, his requirement that businesses with a hundred or more employees either mandate vaccination or require mandatory testing.\par \par \uc2 But what those governors are not telling you is that their own states require vaccination and have vaccine mandates as you read in the intro every state in this country has vaccine mandates for kids, some have them also for certain adults and health workers and some of these Republican states have some of the toughest mandates in the nation.\par \par \uc2 Mississippi which has one of the lowest COVID-19 vaccination rates in the nation has one of the highest, if not the highest childhood vaccination rate.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s interesting. I mean, clearly, obviously, requirements are a huge lever here. There`s two big differences here, right? Just to sort of give the argument that folks make, right?\par \par \uc2 One is that, well, it`s children as opposed to adults and the other this is new and those are not new.\par \par \uc2 STOLBERG: Right, so a couple of things about that. First of all, all vaccines were new at one point and when some of those other vaccines like for measles and mumps, etcetera were new, there was pushback over vaccine mandates but over time, they became accepted.\par \par \uc2 The second thing that Republican governors will point out and correctly so is that the president does not have authority to issue a national vaccine mandate but President Biden has not issued a national vaccine mandate. He is grounding his mandates in federal law, notably the workplace safety law that established OSHA which guarantees federal health and safety standards for workers, so these are -- these are grounded in law.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, and it`s -- one thing that`s striking about that is that the regulated entities here do not mean -- do not seem to be the ones flying into fits of histrionics, so you`re seeing a lot of Republican governors and officeholders saying this is tyranny. They`re not actually the regulated entities, this doesn`t tell states they`re doing -- they have to do anything. It`s in -- you know, employers with a hundred employees and I`m sure some employers don`t like it but, by and large, you have not seen some huge rebellion.\par \par \uc2 STOLBERG: Right and in fact, some employers do and I can share with you a forthcoming study by \uc2 The Rockefeller Foundation\uc2 , it`s going to be released at midnight tonight. They surveyed more than 1,100 employers and they found that 93 percent -- 93 percent, more than nine out of 10 are either requiring vaccination or strongly encouraging it.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:02]\par \par \uc2 STOLBERG: So, you`re not hearing a lot of pushback, a lot of screaming from the business community. And in fact, some of the major business organizations support what President Biden is doing.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Finally, I mean, the subtext here, right, the background reality is that vaccination rates are lower in parts of the country that are more conservative have voted for Trump at higher levels, just as a matter of correlation, not cause.\par \par \uc2 But you know, Charles Gaba has been running these charts, a bunch of people have been pouring through this data, you find this very intense correlation. And it`s pretty clear that these political leaders feel the need to sort of speak to that sentiment in a certain part of their constituency.\par \par \uc2 STOLBERG: That`s right. But you know what else? The political leaders themselves have been actually encouraging people to get vaccinated. Governor Reeves of Mississippi who you saw before has encouraged people. The governors of Alabama and other states, other Republican governors who are pushing back on these mandates have actually encouraged vaccination.\par \par \uc2 And there`s some thought that really what Biden is doing is giving them cover, right, by requiring it. He`s basically taking the heat off Republican governors, they don`t have to push constituents to do it. The big bad president of the United States can be the -- you know, can be the bad guy.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, it`s funny, when you think about requirements -- I mean, one of the things there`s been all these fights about, you know, vaccine shaming and persuasion, to the extent it`s required, right, it just stops being a kind of discourse and just starts being what people have to do.\par \par \uc2 STOLBERG: Yes, and what public health experts will tell you is that in a pandemic, individual liberty kind of takes a backseat to the collective, to the community. And then unless everybody is vaccinated, it`s like nobody is vaccinated because you need high levels of either vaccination or natural immunity to prevent an infectious disease from spreading.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s really important and getting vaccinated is something that we do for the collective. And that`s the public health theory behind it.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Sheryl Gay Stolberg, great reporting. Thanks so much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 Next, the threat level of the Capitol with protective barriers going back up ahead of another pro-Trump rally, police make a disturbing discovery near the DNC headquarters. What we know about the man with the machete after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:41:24]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Earlier this morning, a man was arrested in Washington D.C. for possession of a slew of prohibited weapons, things like machete and a bayonet. They found them in his truck, which was parked outside the headquarters of the \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I`m going to go show you some images of his car and what they found but I should tell you, ahead of time, his truck had a number of really disturbing white supremacist symbols all over it.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Capitol Police\uc2  say an officer noticed this Dodge Dakota around midnight outside the DNC. It had no license plate, just a U.S. flag image in the plate holder where -- and here`s where it gets really disturbing, swastika is painted on the side mirror. Another one inside the driver`s door by the window.\par \par \uc2 Capitol Police say the man had a machete and bayonet, both legal weapons in D.C. along with a number of knives. The suspect, a 44-year-old man from California allegedly told police he was on patrol and expressed white supremacist ideology is set to face a judge tomorrow morning.\par \par \uc2 This all comes as Capitol Police are already on high alert. Today, the new Capitol Police Chief just briefed congressional leaders on the preparations ahead of Saturday`s pro-Trump justice for January 6 rally, an event calling the rioters jailed and charged in connection with the assault in the Capitol called political prisoners.\par \par \uc2 Today, when House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy was asked if he thinks any members of his caucus will attend the rally, he responded, I don`t think anyone is.\par \par \uc2 Olivia Beavers is Politico`s congressional reporter and is covering the lead up to Saturday`s rally. In her most recent piece, she reports that Trump-supporting members of Congress will not attend and she joins me.\par \par \uc2 Now, I saw that reporting, I want to ask you about it because I was actually surprised. First, just tell us what the event is and why the briefing today from Capitol security.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA BEAVERS, CONGRESSIONAL REPORTER, POLITICO: So, there is a former Trump official who is planning this rally basically to claim that some of the rioters were arrested on January 6, our political prisoners because they`re being somehow treated unfairly in the federal court system.\par \par \uc2 There is no evidence to support that they`re being treated unfairly. But you still have a group of, you know, Trump supporters coming and protesting, suggesting that the riot that led to 600 arrests was somehow predicated on fairness.\par \par \uc2 So, that`s what we`re expecting to see Saturday. And officials are bracing for the potential for violence. So, we are seeing the fence -- a temporary fence going back up around the Capitol, should things get out of control.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, what was the briefing today from and who got the briefing?\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: Top congressional leaders. So, you saw Speaker Pelosi, Kevin McCarthy, Mitch McConnell and Chuck Schumer, all four of them received a briefing about the potential the threat of the rally.\par \par \uc2 Pelosi and Schumer both walked out and said that they believe that they feel much more prepared for this compared to January 6 where we know that the intelligence reports suggested that the -- that the intelligence wasn`t being shared properly, and that led to the security failures.\par \par \uc2 I also talked to Mitch McConnell walking in and I asked him, I said do you think that leaders should be encouraging their members not to be attending this and I didn`t get a response.\par \par \uc2 But those are the questions you saw Kevin McCarthy saying that he doesn`t think any of his members are. They`re not entirely speaking out against the event or saying don`t attend the event, but they are trying to kind of, you know, basically be a little bit more quiet ahead of the rally without upsetting the base or members by speaking out against it.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:03]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, so, this is interesting. I would have figured (INAUDIBLE) so we`ve seen Marjorie Taylor Greene, I think Paul Gosar and Matt Gaetz, doing things like showing up the Department of Justice at some point, sort of demanding to see the political -- the "political prisoners". I would have guessed they would go to this. It strikes me that there was some back and forth in that caucus about this as maybe not a great look to attend.\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: Well, you know, I don`t know if there have been any kind of formal issuing of don`t attend for McCarthy. There haven`t been as far as I`ve heard.\par \par \uc2 But that case, I would -- we mentioned him, I have contacted his office repeatedly. There are some who are not telling me that they are not going to attend. But Marjorie Taylor Greene and Madison Cawthorn, who have both been sort of on the forefront of claiming that there are political prisoners, do not intend to go.\par \par \uc2 I think that there is always a risk politically if this does turn violent. Lawmakers want to be very careful about not having their names attached to it, especially after what we saw with January 6, despite the organizer promising that they`re working very closely with law enforcement to ensure that there is not violence.\par \par \uc2 But one thing that I do think is an underlying problem for Republicans that this rally just underscores is that part of the base is just -- they still believe the January 6 is justified, they still believe the election is stolen. And that is where these Republicans are being put in uncomfortable position.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Olivia Beavers, who covers Congress very well, thank you very much, appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: Thanks, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Fresh from undermining Roe v. Wade, the Supreme Court Justice Amy Coney Barrett is outraged or concerned people see the court as partisan.\par \par \uc2 Elie Mystal and Dahlia Lithwick on the call that`s coming from inside the House right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:58]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: For the last number years, conservatives in the Supreme Court have among many other things allowed, unlimited dark money in political campaigns. They`ve gutted the Voting Rights Act in one crucial provision, essentially rendering it moot, dramatically weakened in another. They made it much harder for unions to collect dues.\par \par \uc2 Most recently, they declined to block Texas`s near ban of abortions, which is currently in effect and preventing, according to one estimate, one in 10 women of childbearing age in the U.S. from exercising their constitutional right to an abortion.\par \par \uc2 But whatever you do, it is important, says the court`s newest justice that no one perceive the court as acting in a partisan matter, no matter what it is actually doing.\par \par \uc2 As the Associated Press reports, "Supreme Court Justice Amy Coney Barrett expressed concerns Sunday, the public may increasingly see the court as a partisan institution. Justices must be hyper-vigilant to make sure they`re not letting personal biases creep into their decisions, since judges are people, too. Barrett said in a lecture hosted by the University of Louisville`s McConnell Center."\par \par \uc2 Wait, that McConnell? Oh yes, it continues. Introduced by Senate Republican Leader Mitch McConnell, who founded the center and played a key role in pushing through her confirmation in the last days of the Trump administration. Barrett spoke at length about her desire for others to see the Supreme Court as nonpartisan.\par \par \uc2 Yes, both she and McConnell are not dumb. They both know what they`re doing.\par \par \uc2 You may remember, Amy Coney Barrett`s nomination was so rushed, the Trump White House held a COVID super spreader ceremony announcing her selection before Justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  had even been buried.\par \par \uc2 And Barrett voiced these concerns in an event where she was introduced by Mitch McConnell, who of course, completely blocked Barack Obama from appointing a Supreme Court justice 10 months before his term was up, only to turn around and push Barrett`s nomination through in the waning days of Donald Trump`s first term.\par \par \uc2 Justice Barrett expressed concern, people might think the court has become too partisan. Imagine that.\par \par \uc2 Elie Mystal is a Justice Correspondent for The Nation, Dahlia Lithwick is a Senior Editor and Legal Correspondent at Slate and they both join me now.\par \par \uc2 I feel like this event was designed to produce the reaction we`re going to get it, so I feel a little bit baited here. But I also feel like I have to take the bait, Dahlia. And to me this is, actually, there`s there`s a deep point here.\par \par \uc2 You know, there was a reason that Roger Ailes chose, you know, we report, you decide and fair and balanced for Fox News, because it actually was an important part of the branding to have it be the fact that like, we`re not conservative, we don`t have a point of view. This is just -- this is just the truth. And I think people on the court understand that same principle.\par \par \uc2 DAHLIA LITHWICK, SENIOR EDITOR AND LEGAL CORRESPONDENT, SLATE: I`m actually kind of glad, Chris, that we`re just bracketing the insane onion quality of, you know, standing at Rick`s cafe (PH) giving a speech about how there`s no gambling going on, because that`s just straight-up trolling, right? I mean, let`s not waste our time.\par \par \uc2 One thing that I really, really noticed and I think kind of got lost in the irony today is the way in which she went to an event as you said, it was a Mitch McConnell event and the press was not advised that it was happening, there`s no recordings of it. A handful of reporters that were there were allowed to take notes. They were moved to the back of the room according to Jess Clark who reported it for a local paper.\par \par \uc2 And then, she slammed the press, then she said that the press misrepresents what the court does. It is the most astonishing lack of self-knowledge to get up and say that you shouldn`t listen to reporters, you should read our incredible (AUDIO GAP) and just don`t trust the press because they`re lying to you, trust me we`re nonpartisan, is next level cynical.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is such a great point too because my first thought when I saw this, I was like, oh we -- because I produce a, you know, make a television show. I was like, we should get the video of that then, it`s like, oh, well, there`s no video of that, it doesn`t exist.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Because again, like, she can get this message out and she can stand next to Mitch McConnell in the photos but video is a bridge too far in terms of this like, crazy bit of gaslighting, Elie.\par \par \uc2 ELIE MYSTAL, JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT, THE NATION: Yes, but then look at what the press actually did. Dahlia is exactly -- actually right. But then, look at what the press did, they gave her what she wanted. Because the A.P. as you just headlined, just dutifully copied her notes, sent out her press release, put out that headline without the context of her dripping hypocrisy in the headline. That headline should have been a partisan hack afraid people might notice hackery, that would have been an accurate headline but the press didn`t do that, did they?\par \par \uc2 And this is -- Amy Coney Barrett`s entire career is built on the press being unable or unwilling to call her out when she`s coming, or even accurately report her views and her intentions on the court.\par \par \uc2 In her first year on the Supreme Court, as you pointed out, she`s taken away labor rights, she`s taken away voting rights, she`s taken away abortion rights, she promised to do this to get the job. And yet, the mainstream media refused to call her out on it then and even today as she stands there and basically spits in their face, they still write the headline that she required, that she desired them to write.\par \par \uc2 That`s the real -- you know, those people need to -- need to also like grow up and take some personal responsibility for what they`re doing to the country.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Let me just make this point, I don`t know who wrote the headline. The piece itself I thought was quite good and was pretty clear about the inherent hypocrisy intention here just in terms of the writing.\par \par \uc2 But I also think that like, here`s the thing, Amy Coney Barrett saying this, it`s not just like conservatives say this. This is -- this is like dogma for liberals as well. Like, that the court is not partisan and that, you know, look at all these -- look at all the 9-0 decisions on like, obscure questions of statutory interpretation with the Railroad Commission or whatever, like, what do you people talking about? They`ll cite that chapter and verse.\par \par \uc2 And then, as part of that is Breyer going around selling his book, being like, who, me retire? Here`s what he had to say recently, which also made me kind of lose my mind. This is Bryer on not intending to die in the court. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN BREYER\uc2 , ASSOCIATE JUSTICE, SUPREME COURT: I don`t intend to die on the court. I don`t think I`ll be there forever. But I`ve said a few of the considerations previously, there are many factors, in fact, quite a few. And the role of the court and so forth is one of them. And the situation, the institutional considerations are some.\par \par \uc2 And I believe I can`t say I`d take anything perfectly into account. But in my own mind, I think about those things.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So, why didn`t you retire?\par \par \uc2 BREYER: I didn`t retire because I decided on balance, I wouldn`t retire.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Dahlia, what do you think about the I don`t intend to die on the court?\par \par \uc2 LITHWICK: I mean, and I feel like we`ve talked about this before, Chris. I think that we have to just accept that the nine justices live in a world in which they believe they`re magic. They`re wizards. And they are oracular. And they are just brains in vats, that`s how they sleep at night.\par \par \uc2 Because if they believe they were political hacks, they would run for Congress., right? So, they believe this. And I think the question that Elie`s asking, certainly the question that I`m asking is, why are we spending so much time repeating this? I mean, why do we keep saying, wow, I guess we must believe them, because they tell us that they really get along at the lunch table. So, there`s nothing political going on. Like they`re not stabbing each other with shrimp forks in the eye. And I just think it`s such a bizarre conversation, like, you want to believe your magic. Awesome, have at it. But why do we have to collude with that? And why is it that we`re expanding energy talking about that instead of the fact that the court is just decimating as Elie has said, voting rights, reproductive rights, you know, union rights in front of our very eyes? Isn`t that the conversation we should be having?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Elie?\par \par \uc2 MYSTAL: Look, do you require a duck to be self-aware that it`s a duck before you call it a duck? I mean, is that really the level that we`re at? Because that`s where the press seems to be.\par \par \uc2 Unless the duck says, quack, quack, I`m a duck, then we can`t call it that. If the duck think he`s actually rich, and you know, is a media mogul like Scrooge, then the media has to say Scrooge today, you know, wanted some bread. Like that`s how they report it. It`s very frustrating.\par \par \uc2 But the other thing that I want to bring up before we go, is there`s another person in the story that can stop what we saw from Barrett last night. And that is Chief Justice John Roberts, as the great Julius Campbell said in Remember the Titans, attitude reflects leadership.\par \par \uc2 [21:00:02]\par \par \uc2 MYSTAL: And John Roberts can say, you know what, Barrett, we`re not doing that because you know what, I do care about the -- looking political, and so, you`re not going to go to the McConnell Center.\par \par \uc2 Brett Kavanagh, you`re not going to go to the federal society dinner. We`re going -- \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2 , you`re not going to go on Fox News. We`re going to be above the fray.\par \par \uc2 And if John Roberts said that, they`d do it. But he doesn`t, does he?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Good point.\par \par \uc2 MYSTAL: Because he is just as complicit in this as all the rest of them\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Elie Mystal, Dahlia Lithwick, thank you both. That is ALL IN on this Monday night. "THE RACHEL MADDOW SHOW" starts right now. Good evening, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020210914eh9d00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION WITH \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2  for September 12, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Yasmin Vossoughian, Al Sharpton, Richard Lui, Donna Edwards, David  Jolly \par \uc2 6,314 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 September 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [17:00:04]\par \par \uc2 YASMIN VOSSOUGHIAN, MSNBC HOST: So, keep using that voice. It is so important. And keep wearing that bow tie because you certainly know how to wear it. Grady Knox, great to have you this afternoon. Good luck ahead, Grady.\par \par \uc2 GRADY KNOX, TENNESSEE STUDENT: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: That wraps up the afternoon, everybody. I`m Yasmin Vossoughian. I will be back next Saturday and Sunday at 3:00 P.M. Eastern. I want to turn it over now to Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  and POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 REV. AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Good evening, and welcome to POLITICSNATION. Tonight`s lead, code red.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , U.S. PRESIDENT: We have got to listen to the scientists and the economists and the national security experts. They all tell us this is code red. The nation and the world are in peril.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Right now, I fear for the world my three-year-old -- just about three-year-old grandchild will inherit, and by that, I mean, the planet itself. In ten days, the hottest summer in recorded history for much of the world will come to an end.\par \par \uc2 But not after thousands have died from extreme heat, or the effects of extreme weather. America has certainly not been spared. The federal announcement coming this weekend that this past summer was our hottest ever documented between the two coasts.\par \par \uc2 As more record-setting wildfires in California have fed so much smoke into the air that neighboring states have been debilitated. Many of which are suffering under yet another brutal heat wave, the kind that ignited the pacific northwest in ways un-thought of a year ago.\par \par \uc2 And New York and New Orleans both remain scarred by the same fatal storm. President Biden touring the aftermath of tropical storm Ida this week, sounding the alarm on climate change and touting his infrastructure plan as the nation`s way forward.\par \par \uc2 As his \uc2 environmental protection agency\uc2 , operating under a mandate to now tackle environmental racism released new data suggesting that communities of color won`t have to wait to feel the worst effect of climate change because they already are.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now for his first MSNBC primetime interview is Administrator of the Environmental Protection Agency, \uc2 Michael Regan\uc2 . He is the first black man and second person of color to lead the \uc2 EPA\uc2 . He is also the first \uc2 EPA\uc2  administrative to have graduated from historically black college and university.\par \par \uc2 Administrator Regan, I know I speak for our audience when I tell you how much we appreciate having you here tonight. But at this moment, because of this week the \uc2 EPA\uc2  released its 2021 report on climate change and social vulnerability in the United States specifically focusing on, quote, the disproportionate and unequal risks that climate change is projected to have on communities that are least able to anticipate, cope with, and recover from adverse impacts, end of quote.\par \par \uc2 Among those impacts, black Americans are 40 percent more likely than non- blacks to live in areas with the highest projected increases in mortality rate due to climate-driven changes and extreme temperatures. We also are 34 percent more likely to live in areas with the highest projected increases in childhood asthma due to air pollution caused by climate change.\par \par \uc2 But it`s not just us, and not just about immediate death or disease. The study adds Hispanic-Americans are 43 percent likely than non-Hispanics to live in areas with the highest projected labor losses due to extreme weather. Of course there are more disturbing metrics in this report. But can you tell us more broadly what these numbers mean, Mr. Regan?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL S. REGAN\uc2 , ADMINISTRATOR, ENVIRONMENTAL PROTECTION AGENCY: Well, Reverend Al, thank you for having me today. And, you know, \uc2 EPA\uc2  released the most advanced environmental justice study to date. All of the facts and figures that you laid out are stark fact and figures that we must grapple with today.\par \par \uc2 When you zoom out and take a look at this information, really, it puts the data right in front of every American information that we have known for quite some time that minorities and low-income communities have born a disproportionate impact to pollution for generations.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:15]\par \par \uc2 And now we add climate change on top of that.\par \par \uc2 This is a stark reminder that minority communities, low-income communities, and communities all across this country are now feeling the impacts of climate change. And we expect that intensity to continue.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why the president has really pointed out that a laser focus on climate change, a laser focus on environmental justice and equity are imperative if we are to provide health protections and environmental protections for all people, but also if we are to remain globally competitive as a country.\par \par \uc2 These issues are here, in front of us now. And s you heard the president ask earlier this week, not just to listen to the economists, the national security advisers and the scientists. But people should believe what they are seeing and what they are experiencing. Climate change impacts are real. The data proves it. The science proves it. And now we are all living with current impacts.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: In addition, you mentioned President Biden. In addition to yesterday`s commemoration at ground zero the president was also here -- there in New York. I`m in Houston, Texas, tonight. But he was in New York this week to damage -- to tour the damage left by the tropical storm Ida. Tying that damage to climate change and promoting his ambitious infrastructure plan.\par \par \uc2 Now, we`ve all seen all summer where the public needs have been really crippled by extreme weather. Is the president`s plan as it currently stands truly up to the task of modernizing flooded subways here around the country and the east coast, or streets and power lines that have literally melted due to extreme heat? Are we seeing these things out west? I mean, can we see from these plans some real relief?\par \par \uc2 REGAN: Reverend Al, absolutely. When you look at the president`s build back better agenda, it is very aggressive and very ambitious. And when we look at the bipartisan infrastructure deal, which is one component of the build back better agenda, we are looking at historic investments in infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 One example is $55 billion invested in our water infrastructure. A significant amount of that solely focused on eradicating lead pipes in this country which we know disproportionately impact communities of color and low income communities.\par \par \uc2 We are looking at billions of dollars of investment in infrastructure that would help curtail flood waters and help bolster the security of our water infrastructure, wastewater, sewer, and the like.\par \par \uc2 We also are looking at historic investments in cleaning up legacy pollution at your super fund sites and our brown fill sites all of which are threatened by the impacts of climate changes.\par \par \uc2 We are also looking at strong investments in mitigating climate pollution, around transportation, methane, power plants.\par \par \uc2 And so the president`s build back better agenda is a shot in the arm when we look at protecting our infrastructure from the impacts of climate change but also mitigating the pollution that we are seeing that exacerbate climate change impacts.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes. The interesting term, you said legacy pollution. Let`s pivot to the Ida response at where it first landed. In Louisiana right now, the New Orleans saints are playing their football season up in Jacksonville, Florida, because their home city is still recovering from Hurricane Ida and it is not yet ready to host a football game.\par \par \uc2 And of course we remember that 16 years ago the saints played their entire season away from home because extensive damage done to the city and the super dome by Hurricane Katrina. It does leave one to wonder whether enough has been done in the past decade and a half to build up New Orleans` infrastructure so that it is prepared for the major storms that now seem to occur more frequently than ever.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:00]\par \par \uc2 Is that a fair criticism? And what can be done about it?\par \par \uc2 REGAN: Well you know, what we do know is when we take a look at Hurricane Katrina and the devastation that was left -- fast forward to today. The investments that were made did curtail the impacts. So it was a good start. But the stark realities are, we have to do a lot more.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why the president`s build back better agenda is so important. And that`s why we need Congress to act. We need to continue to invest in our infrastructure. And we can`t put humpty dumpty back the same way every time.\par \par \uc2 We have got to look at the science. We have got to look at the data, the intensity of these storms that are projected, the heat that`s projected. We have to invest resources and an infrastructure that is not only resilient today but prepared for the future intense storms that we will see.\par \par \uc2 And we are going see those storms all over the country. We saw them in the gulf. We saw them in the southeast recently with Hurricane Ida. We saw historic flooding at a very quick pace in New York and New Jersey, tornadoes. We are looking at wildfires out west. We are looking at flooding in the middle of our country as well.\par \par \uc2 We need for Congress to move forward very quickly so that these agencies can get these much-needed resources to begin to invest in our aging infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, earlier this year, President Biden himself addressed a concentration of toxic sites in and around minority communities. One area he mentioned by name was Louisiana`s so-called cancer alley, which is home to several industrial and chemical plants, also where a plastics company wants to build a nearly $10 billion complex in the heart of St. James Parish where the black population is nearly 50 percent and where cancer rates are 50 times the national average. That`s according to the \uc2 EPA\uc2 , the \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  ordering an environmental review last month, five months after the \uc2 United Nations human rights office\uc2  condemned the air quality.\par \par \uc2 Is the administration prepared to get directly involved in such projects as the -- or for the -- for most of plant in Louisiana? If not, how the administration blocks concentrations of toxic sites in vulnerable communities?\par \par \uc2 REGAN: Well, you know, this project is on my radar. I have already spoken with the governor of Louisiana. I have had conversations with the secretary of the Department of Environmental Quality. I have also spoken with the house members that represent that district.\par \par \uc2 We will continue to focus on this area. We will continue to offer up the technical capabilities that the \uc2 EPA\uc2  can offer to help do the proper environmental justice, air quality, water quality analyses that need to be done. We are taking a very close look at cumulative impact more broadly.\par \par \uc2 And so I will do what I have done in other circumstances across the country. In Chicago, where we saw an issue with cumulative impact and legacy pollution I partnered with the mayor to take another closer look at the car shredding facility that was proposed in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 Just this week I issued a letter on behalf of Mayor \uc2 Sylvester Turner\uc2  in Houston, taking a look at the pacific railroad legacy pollution that is plaguing cash near gardens in Houston, Texas. Our job will be to carefully and thoroughly analyze the environmental impacts, but also partner with the mayors, the governors, the locally elected officials to be sure that we are adequately protecting the community, public health rights, and environmental resources.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes, I`m in Houston as I said tonight and I`m aware of your letter with Mayor Turner. Let me ask you this, Mr. Regan. You are from North Carolina. The south, like half of black America -- I spend a lot of time in the south where a lot of poor elderly black people are. I preach to. And they are facing a stark choice every year to either run up their electric bills on fixed incomes or just deal with the kind that has been killing people all over the country and the world this summer. Going back to your recent report, my producer who worked on this segment is from Raleigh, North Carolina, and he`s been wondering if you ever think about the climate debate in those terms as a black father from Goldsboro who grow up in the woods.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:04]\par \par \uc2 REGAN: Absolutely I do. I think about it from the lens of looking at, number one what you laid out which is people struggling to pay their electricity bills. One of the great things about the president`s build back better agenda, it`s looking at the investments that will go into infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 And what the reality is that many of these homes need to be focused on in terms of insulation and weatherization. And energy efficiency programs that can help cut the cost of their electric bills while also reducing the demand for the dirty electricity that`s been generated by coal plants and other fossil fuels.\par \par \uc2 And so I think about it in a couple of ways. The first way is thinking about how do we have everyday citizens see themselves in our vision? And typically that`s through the lens of their packet book. And we can reduce the cost of electricity if we ingest in infrastructure, if we invest energy efficiency.\par \par \uc2 And we can also help them produce the amount of electricity that they are pulling off the grid that`s fueled by fossil fuels which would help mitigate these climate change impacts that they are seeing as well.\par \par \uc2 Again, our communities are disproportionately impacted by the climate change impacts, the pollutants, the legacy pollutants that we face in this country. And financially they are suffering as well.\par \par \uc2 So when we look at the president`s build back better agenda, it is investing in human infrastructure as well as physical infrastructure and mitigating these climate change impact that are exacerbated by pollution.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, lastly, I know that one of your major concerns is the issue of drinkable water. Tell us about that.\par \par \uc2 REGAN: Was a huge concern. As I travel all across the country, Chicago, Detroit, Durham, North Carolina, Milwaukee. We are looking at a failing, aging water infrastructure system. Most of the countries are familiar with flint and the topic of lead. But that`s just the tip of the sphere. There are lots of communities that are close to the tipping point as it relates to water quality and water quality solution.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why I am excited in the bipartisan infrastructure deal that there is $55 billion focused on our aging water infrastructure. And by the way, when we get those resources at \uc2 EPA\uc2 , we are looking at all of our environmental issues through the lens of environmental justice, meaning those who need the resources the most will get them first.\par \par \uc2 But I`m also proud of the president`s, you know, just 40 initiative which pledges that 40 percent of all of the investments that are coming from the federal government will go to communities that are in most need. And that those resources will stay in those communities not only investing in the human and physical infrastructure but also in economic development, education, and jobs as well. So we are laser focused on this topic. And water quality is one that is of significant concern. But help is on the way.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, well, EPA Chief \uc2 Michael Regan\uc2  thank you for your time tonight.\par \par \uc2 Coming up on POLITICSNATION, the impasse over infrastructure. Why some of the usual suspects in the Democratic Party are holding up the process to get something down.\par \par \uc2 Plus a new poll suggests California Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  may survive the recall election but just barely.\par \par \uc2 But first my colleague, Richard Lui, with today`s other top news stories. Richard?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LUI, MSNBC HOST: Rev, good Sunday to you. Some of the stories we are watching for you this hour, the U.S. reached 41 million COVID cases this weekend. More than 663,000 people have died. In Wisconsin, two-thirds of adults there have received one shot. Other states with lower vaccine rates struggle right now, like Tennessee and West Virginia, facing high infection rates because of the delta variant.\par \par \uc2 Two astronauts aboard the international the international space station conducted a spacewalk today. It lasted six and half hours that included one astronaut from Japan and one from France. They were putting in a modification kit to prepare for future solar installation work on the station.\par \par \uc2 And tropical storm Nicholas is on its way from the Gulf of Mexico this morning, that is projected to reach Texas and Louisiana this week, this just weeks after 26 people were killed by Hurricane Ida. It`s expected to drop five to ten inches of rain on an already soaked gulf coast.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  right after this break.\par \par \uc2 [17:20:01]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back. Now to my political panel, joining me now, two former members of Congress, David Jolly, former Republican Congressman from Florida, who has since left the party, and Donna Edwards, former Democratic Congresswoman from Maryland. Both of my guests are MSNBC Political Analysts.\par \par \uc2 Democrats have started to make the mock-up process for the $3.5 trillion infrastructure package which includes things like Medicare expansion, child care programs, and so much more, paid for in a large part by an extremely popular provision raising taxes on the very wealthiest Americans.\par \par \uc2 And yet some Democrats, including \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 , whose state is 50th in the nation in infrastructure quality, seem to be signaling late objections.\par \par \uc2 [17:25:06]\par \par \uc2 Donna, is this thing going to get done?\par \par \uc2 DONNA EDWARDS, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I think it`s going to get done. But I have to say, a warning to Democrats who oppose both the president`s agenda and also the agenda that has been supported by the American people, that they run the risk of losing both the House and the Senate when they choose to run against and to vote against and not support President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and his agenda. And so I think it is going to be a difficult road ahead.\par \par \uc2 Keep in mind that Democrats in the Senate, Senator Sanders, had already pared down what was been almost $6 trillion proposal to $3.5 trillion. And so it has been pared down. And what \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  is asking to reduce it even further they may as well not do it at all given the complexities and the challenges facing working people and the American people for child care, for health care, for community college education. All of those needs that are shared by the American people. And we cannot let a couple of Democrats stand in the way of that. It will be at our peril.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: \uc2 Rolling Stone Magazine\uc2  -- let me bring this up. The \uc2 Rolling Stone Magazine\uc2  is reporting that President Biden is ready to pressure some Democrats on filibuster reform to pass sorely needed voting rights protections saying to Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , quote, Chuck, you tell me when you need me to start making phone calls. And I will be in Washington tomorrow with the Martin Luther King III and Arndrea King, meeting with others including Senator Schumer and delivering a hand- delivered letter to Mitch McConnell`s office.\par \par \uc2 Some Democrats have been working on a voting rights bill that will have all 50 of them on board. That language could be announced as soon as tomorrow. David, do you think that will be enough? Will any Republican sign on, let alone ten of them?\par \par \uc2 DAVID JOLLY, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes, Reverend, and this is a big development reported by rolling stone that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is willing to lean in and kind of put his mark on the table and unleash some political forces to try to rescind the filibuster. The question is, will it work?\par \par \uc2 You still have members like Manchin and Sinema and others who have said they are not interested in touching the filibuster. And then you have to wonder, some of these packages that Manchin said he`s negotiated that can bring ten Republicans, does that all blow up if the filibuster goes away?\par \par \uc2 Because you still have, even without the filibuster you still have to get to 50 votes on a number of these high priority items of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And Senator Manchin has said he`s not going to go along with the transportation bill, with the voting rights bill, as it was drafted. So look, I think the tough thing as Donna --\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: But he has indicated in the past that he could go with the John Lewis bill. And clearly, if the president leans in as many of us in the civil rights community, I certainly been very public about it in the last few days calling on the president to do exactly this. And if they can do carve-outs for other things, why wouldn`t all of the Democrats and some of Republican do carve-outs for something as basic as voter protection?\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: And look, I think that could get done by leaving the filibuster in place, the notion that you could get ten Republicans to come along. And that probably is the direction they should go. I think if \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  suggests let`s get this done by rescinding the filibuster and all way in on it, look, all bets are off.\par \par \uc2 I mean at this point I think Democrats -- Donna is right, they face a big question, do we go big knowing we might only have two years.\par \par \uc2 The challenge is, \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  represents a Republican state, the fourth most Republican state in the country. It is because he was a Democratic able to win West Virginia that Chuck Schumer is the Majority Leader today. And so \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  has not shown a willingness to move towards more progressive in the agenda.\par \par \uc2 But look, if you can get the John Lewis Act done within Republican, if you can get a modified infrastructure bill done with ten Republicans, do it. Listen to the direction \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  is go. And I know it is not the first priority of progressives but he has said he is willing to work with Democrats.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes. But I don`t know that it is moving towards progressives as much as moving toward democracy. And you know, I understand Joe Manchin`s situation in West Virginia. But let`s deal with the situations of the targeted underlining of blacks and others to be able to vote, which I think should far outweigh any one political figures, one political options in a state.\par \par \uc2 But let me go to another state. The president will head to California tomorrow to campaign for Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  as recent \uc2 U.C. Berkeley\uc2  polls show 60 percent of Californians intend to vote against the recall.\par \par \uc2 [17:30:06]\par \par \uc2 That change in polling has happened as residents have learned more about the challenges and approaches to the pandemic. There are a lot of anti- vaxxers and anti-maskers running to unseat Newsom. Donna, do you this California race is going to be think this referendum on Democratic versus Republican COVID action? And could this be a road map for 2022 mid terms and beyond?\par \par \uc2 EDWARDS: I think it`s really tough to predict what is going to be a road map or what hales for Democrats in 2022. What I will say, though, is that it`s really clear that by exposing the anti-vaxx, anti-mask, anti- of the opponents, that \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  has put himself in a really strong position to withstand this recall.\par \par \uc2 It has been by that exposure of some of these pundits who are in the absolute extreme. And I think Democrats at large can learn a lot from that about what it means to uncover and unearth the anti-vaxx, anti-mask, anti- science premise of these new-age Republicans. And I think that will bode them well.\par \par \uc2 And I think it stands to President Biden`s leadership and the testimony to his leadership that the majority of Californians are now leaning toward making certain that they continue to uphold science in California.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, yesterday we marked the 20th anniversary of the September 11th terrorist attacks. And while \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  commented on it very slightly, he commentated a pay-per-view boxing match. \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , who was of course the president at the time, made time in his speech for warning about domestic violent extremists. I am rarely in agreement with the former President Bush, but take a listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEORGE W. BUSH\uc2 , FOMER U.S. PRESIDENT: There is little cultural overlap between violent extremists abroad and violent extremists at home. But then there is disdainful pluralism, and their disregard for human life, in their determination to defile national symbols. They are children of the same foul spirit. And it is our continuing duty to confront them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Donna, then David. I want both of you on this, briefly, please. Do you think current members of the Republican Party will listen to those words from the 43rd president? Will they confront the violent extremists in their ranks?\par \par \uc2 EDWARDS: Well, I think the short answer to that, Reverend Al is no. They haven`t demonstrated that coming out of January 6th and in the weeks and months that have followed. And I thought that President Bush`s comments were incredibly pressing and important as we begin to look not just to the extremism abroad but to the extremism at home and the importance of us getting a handle on that.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: David?\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: Yes, Rev, I`m glad you focused on Bush 43`s calling that we have a duty to act. Because it was remarkable that he tied the thread from overseas terrorism and equated it to domestic terrorism. He`s right. But then he said that creates a duty for all of us to act.\par \par \uc2 Today`s Republicans will not respond to that. But it is an affirmation for a lot of former Republican and former Republican performing voters that you know what, those are the principles I believed in. And it drew a contrast that you can`t find those principles in today`s Republican Party. It is now one of the reasons that Democrats now represent a majority in the country.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: David Jolly and Donna Edwards, thank you both. We have got more POLITICSNATION on the other side of the break. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:35:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: There is still more to come here on POLITICSNATION. As the debate heats up over the state of reproductive rights in the country, the nation`s second catholic president finds himself at odds with church leaders over his stance on abortion.\par \par \uc2 Speaking of religion, the former guy`s latest grudge is with Catholics and Jews who didn`t vote for him in 2020. We will pray for him as we get into this conversation with my next guests. And later, Congresswoman Barbara Lee and Democratic National Committee Chair Jamie Harrison join my colleague Alicia Menendez to discuss the GOP`s cries about vaccine freedoms as the party continues its assault on women`s rights and their bodies. That`s at 6:00 P.M. on AMERICAN VOICES right here on MSNBC. But we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:40:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Texas`s restrictive abortion law has brought one of America`s longest-running tensions in the spotlight, religion and politics. Many catholic leaders are cheering the new anti-abortion law. They face criticism, though, from a fellow catholic, President Biden, the second president in U.S. history to be of the same faith.\par \par \uc2 But as the current president faces tension with members of his own religion, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , a man who has always been cagey about his own religious beliefs, is complaining that not enough Catholics and Jews voted for him in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now to help unpack this all is Dr. Obery Hendricks Professor at Columbia University, Visiting Lecturer at Yale University, and author of the book, Christians Against Christianity, How Right-Wing Evangelicals Are Destroying Our Nation And Our Faith.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Hendricks, I want to start with this religious bind that President Biden may feel that he is in. On one hand, he has spoken out about giving women the right to choose. On the other hand, he identifies as a man of faith. And now is feeling heat from some fellow Catholics who say that being pro-choice is anti-catholic. Talk about the tension you see there, and how the president can navigate it.\par \par \uc2 DR. OBERY M. HENDRICKS, ADJUNCT PROFESSOR, COLUMBIA UNIVERSITY: Yes. Thanks, Reverend Al, and good to be back with you again. Yes. You know there are some things that we have to -- a couple of things that we have to keep in mind. First, catholic moral teaching teaches that abortion is a sin, and therefore is murder. But that`s their doctrinal and ideology position.\par \par \uc2 Biblically it doesn`t say anything about abortion is a sin or a murder.\par \par \uc2 [17:45:04]\par \par \uc2 Anyone who teaches differently is not telling the truth. And so even though he is contradicting the teaching of his faith biblically, which is posing on some important thing, he is on firm grounds.\par \par \uc2 But not only that, he has taken an oath of defending the constitution and the constitution particularly the 13th amendment says no one has the right to enslave anyone else`s body, to lord over them, to transgress their sovereignty over their own body.\par \par \uc2 So when we get down to it, Biden is on strong moral ground. And more -- and even more than that, he`s doing the things that are most important for a Christian, looking out for poor people, so the least of these. He is looking out for workers and for the children of immigrants. So I think President Biden is on firm ground here. And he should not really be attacking --\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You know -- it`s funny because I remember when I was a kid and we had the first catholic President John Kennedy, the question was, are you going to listen to your religion and the pope, or are you going to uphold the constitution? Now the reverse is being asked of President Biden.\par \par \uc2 But let me go into this. Republicans in the Trump years seemed to coalesce around their base of evangelical Christian voters while the Democratic Party is much more religiously diverse including groups of many faiths and many Americans who aren`t religious at all according to a study conducted by the \uc2 Pew Research\uc2  people, 64 percent of registered voters considered themselves to be Christian, a number that has steadily declined since 2008.\par \par \uc2 In 2019, 30 percent of those who identified as Democrat or left leaning said they were not affiliated with any religion, whereas only 15 percent of Republicans or light-leaning voters said the same thing. How do you think these different faith make ups affect the politics of our two political parties?\par \par \uc2 HENDRICKS: Yes, yes. Well, you know, the question is, what Christianity are these people talking about? Because right-wing evangelicals, they seem to -- like Paul says in his letters in the bible, they seem to cleave to another gospel. You know, the right wing is not concerned about looking out for the least of these. They are not concerned with taking care of poor people. They are not concerned about fighting for justice. They are not even mentioning justice.\par \par \uc2 And so many people, particularly young people are becoming disenchanted with it, with Christianity. They don`t want anything to do with the religion that is presented to them as so mean-spirited. And the right wing has allowed us voices in the public sphere right now. So the hate head that they spew the divisiveness that they spew and the fact that they support a walking mAclange of the seven deadly sins in Trump turns these young people off.\par \par \uc2 I think that`s the reason why we are seeing people turn away. Now, people are not turning away are the gospel itself. They are not turning away from love and justice. They are turning away from the hatred of the right wing.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Let me switch for a minute to former President Trump. On a recorded phone call with religious leaders on his new national faith advisory board last week, he complained about losing catholic and Jewish voters in 2020 calling them, quote, ungrateful. He also went on the belly ache about how he did so much for both groups.\par \par \uc2 However, in 2019, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  reported Jews were the most targeted religious group in the country in regards to religiously motivated hate crimes. Cases of anti-Semitism has also been also on the rise. All of this occurring while Trump was still president. Additionally to a Pew Research Poll, roughly one-third of Catholics in the U.S. are Latino. And we know how Mr. Trump treated the Latino community for years.\par \par \uc2 Religious voters want to feel protection, not division, and Trump did not offer this. Am I right? And I read your excellent book. You covered some of this and questioned about evangelicals that seem blind to these facts.\par \par \uc2 HENDRICKS: Yes. Well, they are not only blind. They, some just don`t care. We must remember that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  began his campaign with hate. He began his political ascend antsy with hate against -- aligned against President \uc2 Obama\uc2 . He has a long record of racism and these people cleave to the hatred that he spewed and the lies he spewed.\par \par \uc2 [17:50:03]\par \par \uc2 And so it is not surprising at all that we have seen a rise of hatred because Trump has appealed to hatred. And when you look at the statistics, the counties that he campaigned in this last campaign, the incidents of hate crimes increased exponentially in those districts where he campaigned.\par \par \uc2 So the people, they come to him because of hatred. And they`re sticking with him because of hatred. And, but those who are leaving him, Catholics and Jews, are leaving him because they`re seeing through him. They`re seeing he is unreligious and many ways he`s just anti-catholic.\par \par \uc2 The smart folks are the ones who left him. The ones who aren`t smart are the ones still following his lies and foolishness and divisiveness and hatefulness.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. Well, thank you for being here. We have to leave it there. Dr. Obery Hendricks, Author of Christians Against Christianity, a new book that you should look at.\par \par \uc2 HENDRICKS: Thank you. Thank you Reverend Al.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Up next, my final thoughts. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: As I stated, I spent today in Houston, Texas, preaching this morning at Lily Grove Baptist Church whose Pastor Reverend Terry Anderson, is one of the outstanding faith leaders in the country and meeting with some of the courageous Texas Democrats that trying to stop the voting bill restriction, restrictive bill here in Texas.\par \par \uc2 And we talk a lot on this show about the threats that we talked about here in Texas today, the threats to our voting rights, threats to women`s rights, threats of police brutality toward minorities.\par \par \uc2 But if you saw the top of the show today, you also heard about how right now, climate change is the most consequential threat facing humanity. Everywhere we look, we see massive fires that consume entire towns. Unprecedented flooding that inundates major urban areas. Record heat waves and drought that increase water scarcity and devastate food supplies. We all feel it. We all see it. We all suffer from it. And now, we know that without action, the situation is only going to get worse.\par \par \uc2 Add to that the \uc2 EPA\uc2  just detailed all of the ways climate change will hit U.S. racial minorities the hardest. And it`s a long list. Just as an example, black people are 40 percent more likely than other groups to live in places where, extreme temperatures will cause more deaths.\par \par \uc2 African-Americans are more -- are 34 percent more likely to live in areas where childhood asthma diagnoses are likely to be exacerbated by climate change. We commend the Biden administration, Biden/Harris administration, who took ownership of this new \uc2 EPA\uc2  report, and elevated it to action, specifically because of its focus on climate justice.\par \par \uc2 But now, it`s time for the White House and Congress to address this historic environmental inequity and enact legislative proposals that would pour billions of dollars into areas that suffer the most. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:00:00]\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020210913eh9c00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION September 11, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Richard Lui, Juanita Tolliver, Susan del Percio \par \uc2 6,502 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 September 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 YASMIN VOSSOUGHIAN, MSNBC HOST: I appreciate your time this afternoon, gentlemen. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 That wraps up the hour for me, everybody. I`m Yasmin Vossoughian. I`ll be back in the chair tomorrow, 3:00 p.m. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 "POLITICSNATION" with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  starts right now.\par \par \uc2 [17:00:12]\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Welcome to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s lead: Ripple effects. The nation has seen a full generation mature in the shadow of an indiscriminate tragedy. And right now, the White House is still commemorating the 20th anniversary of the September 11 attacks. President Biden punctuating his tour of the attack sites from that awful day with calls for national reflection, and at this point, unity.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D), PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The core of who we are is not divided. Everybody says, "Biden, why do you keep insisting on trying put your country together?" That`s the thing that`s going to affect our wellbeing more than anything else how the rest of the world respond to us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: President Biden`s predecessor striking a less unifying tone today, releasing a video where he talks about 9/11 for less than 30 seconds, and spends a full minute and a half attacking Biden by name for an Afghanistan withdrawal policy that began during his own presidency.\par \par \uc2 The former guy made his own separate visit to New York City first responders this afternoon, and we will follow that up by participating in a pay-per-view boxing match later this evening.\par \par \uc2 Later in the show, I`ll talk to New York mayoral candidate Eric Adams and filmmaker, Michael Moore about what the nation has learned or not from 9/11, because the ripple effects of that day, when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was still a senator are certainly haunting his White House today at the worst possible time with the aftermath of a forever war in Afghanistan, a rise in organized right-wing extremism and the response to it diverted by politics, and a Republican Party that has now fully refined the use of racism to gain electoral advantage, the heart of which being Texas where G.O.P. lawmakers enacted their controversial voter suppression laws this week, upstaging every other state legislating to keep minorities from voting ahead of next year`s midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 Of course, Texas is also singular in its repression of abortion rights. The Justice Department announcing that it will now sue the state over its severe six-week abortion ban, as other red states emulate its example, posing a generational legal threat to reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 And so we start tonight in Texas as POLITICSNATION and MSNBC continue to mark the 20th anniversary of 9/11.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Congressman Mark Veasey, Democrat of Texas. Congressman, thank you for joining us tonight. Obviously, I want to get to the pain in your home state of Texas, but it is only appropriate that I ask you what you`re thinking about today, watching the nation that you serve as it processes, the 20 years that have passed since 9/11.\par \par \uc2 REP. MARK VEASEY (D-TX): Yes, it is, you know, one of those days, obviously, that you know, that you never forget. I mean, I remember exactly where I was that day, and I pray for the families, I pray for the first responders, our friends in New York City, those brave souls on the flight in Pennsylvania, everyone that was a part of that day, the close to 40,000 lives that we lost is something that we should never forget.\par \par \uc2 And we should always be vigilant about that there are still forces that would like to be disruptive and, and do grand events like what happened on 9/11, and we need to stand vigilant against them and take them seriously as we remember what happened to our country 20 years later today.\par \par \uc2 And I will continue to pray for these families and the victims and for our country, and I hope that this really serves as a reminder of how precious freedom and democracy really is of our country.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You are the co-chair of the Congressional Voting Rights Caucus and you are one of the faces in your state`s Democratic delegation. Throughout the legislative standoff over voting rights in the Texas legislature and many of the Texas legislators that went to Washington -- I`m going to Houston tonight, they will be with me and service tomorrow morning. I`m preaching to Lily Grove Baptist Church, Reverend Anderson in Houston, and I think those legislators were heroes.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:17]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: But despite all of what they did earlier this week, Governor Greg Abbott signed the State Senate Bill those Texas Democrats were trying to impede triggering now, a third lawsuit in Federal Court over the laws restriction, which would disproportionately affect minority voters. Because we`ve been inundated with suppressing legislation in the last year, I don`t want this audience to lose sight that this is a national, and that it is very national in its endgame, but can you illustrate what this law means for black and brown voters in Texas, Congressman?\par \par \uc2 VEASEY: Yes, absolutely, and what a bad week for Texas. I think the Governor has done everything he can possibly do to embarrass our great state, first with the abortion law, and now with this sweeping anti-voter law, and this is bad for a lot of different reasons.\par \par \uc2 If you look at Harris County, for instance, the largest county in Texas, the fourth largest city in this country, they put together a great program where people regardless of their party affiliation could vote easily by having 24-hour early voting, by not having to get out of your car during COVID-19, so you can stay safe while you vote. And the Governor signed this into law, and outlawing those things.\par \par \uc2 The other thing that worries me, and Reverend Al, I think about my own grandmother. You know, you`re talking about feeling blessed. My grandmother died two years ago, she was 106 years old and she remembers -- I keep a copy of their poll taxes, my grandparents` poll taxes from 1948, I keep a copy in my congressional office, and my grandmother never missed an election because she remembers having been born in 1911-1912, she remembers how difficult it was for black people to vote in this country.\par \par \uc2 And I also think about her organizational system, particularly as she got older, and she didn`t drive anymore. She would keep documents and boxes and things like that. And if you look at this bill, particularly for the vote by mail, now you have to put the last four digits of your driver`s license or you have to put the last four digits of your Social Security number on your vote by mail application.\par \par \uc2 And when you mail your ballot in, you have to put those on there and they have to match up, and if they don`t, they throw your ballot out. And I think about you know, my grandmother that had, you know, the last 20 years of her life, you know, had you know, poor vision, stopped driving when she was 85 years old. So, she never went back to renew her driver`s license after she turned 85, and how much trouble she would have today if she was still alive, just to simply cast her right to vote and for her to have to see that all over again, that sort of attempt to make it harder for black people to vote.\par \par \uc2 You know, it absolutely pains my heart that in Texas, you know, after the 1960 Voting Rights Act bill was passed, we actually put together some of the most -- some of the best policies in the country if you look at it, and now we`re going backwards and making it so much difficult for people to vote, and I think it`s an embarrassment.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Well, we can`t let it stick. But in addition to triggering a Justice Department lawsuit, the six-week abortion ban is also an issue there. It was signed into law this week in Texas, and it has become a North Star for other red states looking to institute similar restrictions.\par \par \uc2 So since we`re talking another national fight, what are congressional Democrats prepared to do legislatively to complement the D.O.J.`s led litigation?\par \par \uc2 VEASEY: Yes, absolutely. Reverend Al, let me say this. Thank you to all of the activists out there, all of the good Democrats, people that care about women`s rights regardless of their party affiliation that have gone on social media and that have given information out so people that are poor, the women that are poor, that if they need to seek abortion services that there is information out there that will allow them to do that. I just wanted to give them thanks on that.\par \par \uc2 Legislative-wise, you know, the Justice Department right now, the Biden administration is suing the State of Texas and I welcome this suit against our state because this is obviously the absolutely worst thing that could happen when it comes to reproductive rights in this country.\par \par \uc2 And then I also know that a Representative Judy Chu out of California that she has a bill that she is working on.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:07]\par \par \uc2 VEASEY: But Reverend Al, I think the biggest thing that we can obviously do is that we need to make sure that we continue to mobilize. The reason why we have the six-three decision is obviously, you know, because of electoral problems that we had, and we need to make sure that we are getting everybody out to the polls in 2022 and beyond, because now we can see the seriousness and the consequences of this.\par \par \uc2 Think about that. Look at what`s happening in voting in this country, because of the Voting Rights Act being dismantled the way that it was under the Roberts` Court. And now, you look at this six-three decision when it comes to women`s reproductive rights. It`s serious, there`s a reason why Republicans have been able to mobilize their voters around these issues, and we need to make sure that we can mobilize our voters around who sits on that Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Right, that is why these issues to me are connected, because we must protect the vote so we can put the right people in office. Let`s not forget the Supreme Court is stacked by two Presidents that lost the popular vote, but won in the Electoral College.\par \par \uc2 Thank you --\par \par \uc2 VEASEY: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And that`s George Bush and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . But thank you, Congressman Mark Veasey.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Democratic candidate running for Mayor of New York City and former police officer in New York City, Eric Adams.\par \par \uc2 And Mr. Adams, today is the 20th anniversary of 9/11, a very raw and painful time, not only in our nation`s history, but for all Americans on a personal level, who remember that dark day. And for those who don`t know, as I do, you and I go back 35 years. You were an NYPD police officer at that time.\par \par \uc2 Two decades later, and we still see so many first responders who helped save lives on 9/11 suffer from illnesses stemmed from being present that day, Republicans on the Hill have continuously blocked additional measures to give these heroes the aid that they need.\par \par \uc2 What would you do to make sure those brave men and women get necessary assistance to cover all their medical treatments?\par \par \uc2 You`re muted. We don`t hear you mic. I think we have a problems with --\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS (D), NEW YORK CITY MAYORAL CANDIDATE: Yes, I`m sorry.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. I know they did try to mute you for 35 minutes.\par \par \uc2 ADAMS: I stated that at the time, I was a lieutenant in the \uc2 New York City Police Department\uc2 . And I recall that day on Ground Zero, where we saw the ground was smoldering. Many of the military personnel, firefighters, police officers were covered in soot. It was a challenging time for our country.\par \par \uc2 But I believe the day that we should really acknowledge is September 12th. On September 12th, we got up, teachers taught, our builders built, retailers sold their goods and services, and it just shows the resiliency of our country during a significant time. It was our Pearl Harbor moment, and we responded accordingly.\par \par \uc2 And part of that response was our first responders, the men and women who responded to the scene, and those who were there that didn`t wear a uniform. The worst thing we can do is not give them the necessary medical benefits that they need to ensure that we did not forget them when they stood up for our country.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, as the start of the school year begins, the mask mandate debate heats up. In New York, Governor Hochul has made it so that all students statewide must wear a mask at all times indoor in order to attend in-person classes. Compare this to Florida where Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  attempted to ban masks from classrooms.\par \par \uc2 What is the number one thing we need to do to protect our children from getting sick?\par \par \uc2 ADAMS: It is so important because nothing brings out a greater level of emotion than how we feel about our children. You know, as a dad of a son, and I know your two daughters, we think about all the time, are we doing the right things for them?\par \par \uc2 And the best way to do the right thing is to allow science to direct our actions of what our medical professionals take, a combination of the C.D.C., Department of Health and Mental Hygiene, our Department of Health here in the state, and we allow the science to dictate how to better protect our families and prevent a further increase in COVID cases particularly of the delta variant which is impacting children, we will make the right decisions and we will take the emotions out of it so that we can do what`s best for our children and families.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:05]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, New Yorkers are still recovering after the remnants of Hurricane Ida left a path of destruction, including massive flooding and numerous deaths. Many streets, apartments, and subway stations were damaged from the storm.\par \par \uc2 If elected Mayor, what will you do to ensure that infrastructure gets the upgrade needed to avoid this happening again, especially in areas dominated by communities of color?\par \par \uc2 ADAMS: Well, you know, I am in a government buildings, so I`m not going to go too far down the line on being Mayor. But as the Borough President here in the borough of Brooklyn, we were hit hard as well as Queens. I visited locations on Staten Island, as well.\par \par \uc2 First, we have to be honest with ourselves of what is needed is intervention and prevention, a term I use often. Prevention, those are the long term things. Everything from closing our peaker plants, cycling buses off from using diesel fuel, building out a green infrastructure, what I like to say, a real green bond job initiative to show up our waterfront.\par \par \uc2 But Reverend Sharpton, we also have to be honest with ourselves that this did not come from high tide, it came from rain. Mother Nature is making it clear that we can`t take shortcuts, and I believe some of the intervention that we can do now as we invest in building our sewer systems, but also let`s look at what other countries are doing.\par \par \uc2 I`m in contact with the leaders over in the Netherlands, and I want you to see what they`re doing, as they have done a successful job of how do we live with water, now how do we keep water out?\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, and we`ll have to leave it there. Thank you very much for being with us, Eric Adams on this day.\par \par \uc2 Coming up on POLITICSNATION, the 9/11 attacks were meant to divide us, but now more than ever, we need to rise up and come together.\par \par \uc2 Plus, my political panel weighs in on Donald Trump`s bizarre 9/11. The grand finale of the former President`s day is just a few hours away.\par \par \uc2 But first, my colleague, Richard Lui with today`s top news stories -- Richard.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LUI, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Rev, a good Saturday. Some stories we are falling for you this hour. For the 20th anniversary of 9/11, President Biden and the First Lady traveled to New York City, Shanksville, Pennsylvania, and The Pentagon to commemorate those who lost their lives that day.\par \par \uc2 The President joined former Presidents \uc2 Obama\uc2 , Bush, and Clinton at Ground Zero for the National Day of Mourning. Former President Trump made his separate visit to New York today to visit first responders.\par \par \uc2 And the U.S. is closing in on 41 million confirmed coronavirus cases. The virus has killed more than 662,000 people.\par \par \uc2 In Idaho, hospitals are reaching maximum capacity. Friday had over 1,000 new cases there leaving only six ICU beds available statewide.\par \par \uc2 And Utah is suffering from unprecedented poor air quality. Smoke from California`s wildfires blanket areas of the state with a haze. Utah also suffers from severe drought. The Great Salt Lake has reached its lowest point ever measured says the \uc2 U.S. Geological Survey\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:22:31]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: For this week`s "Rise Up," I want to briefly reflect on the 20th anniversary of the September 11th attacks, the aftermath, and American unity.\par \par \uc2 In the days following that tragedy, Americans pulled together under a common model, "United we stand." It was emblazoned on signs, t-shirts, and in the hearts of most Americans. There was a notable and shameful spike in Islamophobic rhetoric and even violence in some corners, but the majority pulled together in solidarity and community.\par \par \uc2 Historically, most Americans have agreed that the September 11th attacks changed this country permanently, but there has been a striking shift in the perception of that change.\par \par \uc2 A year after the attacks, Americans were still standing united and a majority of us thought that change could be for the better.\par \par \uc2 Ten years after the attacks, we pretty evenly were split on whether the change would make us better or worse.\par \par \uc2 And now, 20 years later, nearly half of Americans think we have been permanently changed for the worse. Only one-third think we have been changed for the better.\par \par \uc2 There has been a change in this country over the last 20 years and that change is perhaps best displayed in how we respond to tragedy.\par \par \uc2 Two years before the September 11th attacks, children`s television host and American icon, Fred Rogers gave an interview that contained one of the most lasting pieces of wisdom.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FRED ROGERS, CHILDREN`S TELEVISION SHOW HOST: My mother used to say, a long time ago, whenever there would be any really catastrophe that was in the movies or on the air, she would say, "Always look for the helpers. There will always be helpers."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Mr. Rogers was right. Even as the ruins in New York, Washington, and Pennsylvania were still smoldering, thousands of Americans lined up to help firefighters, police officers, EMS workers, and even random passersby put their lives on the line, at risk to return to the smoking ruins in an attempt to rescue strangers.\par \par \uc2 [17:25:09]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Meanwhile, charities and blood banks across the country faced weeks of crowds as Americans lined up by the millions to give anything they could.\par \par \uc2 In tragedy, Americans turn to one another, united.\par \par \uc2 But somehow, in the intervening decades, some of us have lost that sense of camaraderie and responsibility and community. Sometimes, loving thy neighbor isn`t as demanding as running into a collapsing high rise.\par \par \uc2 Sometimes, it`s as simple as getting a vaccine or wearing a mask to stop a pandemic that has already taken more than 650,000 American lives.\par \par \uc2 Today, it is no longer enough to look for the helpers. We must rise up together and be them.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:30:06]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION. Moving on now to my political panel.\par \par \uc2 Juanita Tolliver is a Democratic strategist and Susan del Percio, a Republican strategist, both are MSNBC political analysts.\par \par \uc2 Now while every other living President under the age of 95 attended the memorials today, on the 20th anniversary of 9/11, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  issued a video attacking Biden, then stopped at an \uc2 NYPD\uc2  precinct near Trump Tower where he continued to spread his big lie about the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 And later tonight, he will provide commentary for a pay-per-view boxing match. I want to let both of you briefly react to this stunt.\par \par \uc2 I mean, Susan, then Juanita, what does this say about the disgraced former President?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN DEL PERCIO, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, Rev, not once did this President ever think it was his job to serve the country. He only wants to figure out how the country could serve him, and these actions today are just more of a reflection of it.\par \par \uc2 If I didn`t know better, I think he hated this country. I think that he hates the country that did not get him re-elected, but I think he hates the principles of being there, supporting, and valuing human kindness, and all he wants to do is be a bitter, angry, petty, disgusting little man.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Juanita?\par \par \uc2 JUANITA TOLLIVER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Look, it was reality TV show Trump today and that`s been who the country has always engaged with. That`s how he always responded to the needs of this country. And as Susan said, he hates any type of human interaction he has to have, any humanity that he has to display. And so this fully tracks with the disgrace that he has essentially displayed all of his adult life.\par \par \uc2 So, we shouldn`t expect anything different from him. If anything, he is likely going to use the same type of tone and speech as he runs in 2024 as he alluded to in his last minute visit, which I think he only put on the books after he got a lot of blowback after it was clear he was going down to Florida for this boxing match.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Susan, let`s turn to Texas. One of the most underhanded aspects of the unconstitutional Texas abortion ban is the enforcement mechanism. They`ve turned ordinary Texans into vigilantes in part to make it harder to sue to overturn the law.\par \par \uc2 Now, this week, the Department of Justice stepped in seeking an injunction in a Texas court, with this likely to end up before the Supreme Court, and with a conservative majority seemingly eager to overturn Roe, what happens now? Will there be political blowback?\par \par \uc2 DEL PERCIO: There won`t be political blowback for the state legislators in Texas. They do this to actually get re-elected. It`ll be interesting to see, as far as does the Supreme Court take up the Mississippi case that we`ve discussed and make however it decides there, settle the case and the issues in Texas. I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the D.O.J. seems to have an interesting way in tackling this much like they did in Arizona on the immigration issue. But I think again, it`s just going to have to take its way up to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 The blowback could be against Republicans nationwide, especially -- I mean, if you notice, Rev, you don`t hear Republicans praising the Texas law.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: No.\par \par \uc2 DEL PERCIO: They`re staying far away from it because they know it`s barbaric to make a woman carry the rapist`s baby to term. That`s disgusting, and they know it.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Juanita, let`s turn to the pandemic. Following President Biden`s announcements regarding vaccine requirements in Federal and private workplaces, Republican governors once again proved they care more about political posturing than saving lives.\par \par \uc2 Take \uc2 Bill Lee\uc2  of Tennessee who tweeted about his opposition to COVID mitigation, even as his state has broken hospitalization records every day of September so far. Most eligible Americans are vaccinated, why do Republicans seem so desperate to infect their own shrinking base?\par \par \uc2 TOLLIVER: Rev, you and me are asking the same question because it makes no sense. Especially, I was raised in Memphis, Tennessee, so I`m looking at this, like, yes, this is the same type of Republican tone that we get out of Texas, out of Florida, out of other states where Republican leaders have refused to enforce any safety protocols around this pandemic, have refused mask mandates, have refused vaccination requirements, and these are the exact type of Republican leaders who are perpetuating this pandemic.\par \par \uc2 [17:35:11]\par \par \uc2 TOLLIVER: As you said, Rev, Tennessee is breaking records every single day with new COVID hospitalizations. But I do want to emphasize what President Biden mentioned that this is the type of Republican that his executive action is looking to get out of the way so this pandemic can get under control.\par \par \uc2 And so Republicans are acting out because this is the narrative, this is the fight that they`ve been pushing. It fully aligns with what Trump was saying at the start of this pandemic and they are holding to it like a vise grip because they know it resonates and riles up their base.\par \par \uc2 But like you said, Rev, their base is dwindling. Hundreds of thousands of people have already lost their lives in this pandemic, hundreds of thousands more also stand to lose their lives if they continue down this track.\par \par \uc2 So, they are losing lives. They are ruining people`s lives, all for a political narrative, and it makes no sense.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now the California gubernatorial recall election is next week, and though Democratic Governor Gavin Newson is looking better in recent polling, the party isn`t taking anything for granted.\par \par \uc2 Vice President Harris campaigned with the Governor this week, and President Biden will head to California to rally supporters in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 Susan, what is at stake for California and the rest of the country in this recall election?\par \par \uc2 DEL PERCIO: Well, if Newsom should lose and the leading Republican candidate out there should win, it will be a topsy-turvy, you know, once in a blue moon, as we said, although \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  got elected, no chance of happening, but it can happen. It will be dramatic, except he will be -- he will have his hands cuffed behind his back because the state legislature will be able to override anything that he does. So, it will be in their power.\par \par \uc2 You know, Newsom is in this problem, though, because of COVID and part of his COVID response. I actually think the President`s mandate, even to the Republican governors was a gift. They should be sending them gift baskets because this will help every state with the mandates, get COVID deaths under control and stop the spreading.\par \par \uc2 They may complain about it, those Republican governors as Juanita said, to their base, but you know what? I think they are welcoming it because the businesses in their communities are also welcoming it.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes. All right, Juanita Tolliver and Susan del Percio, thank you both.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the man who documented this country`s darkest days and its aftermath, reflecting on tragedy and honoring heroes 20 years later. Michael Moore joins me after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:41:26]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: The September 11th attacks created the war on terror, which quickly became a war on Islamic terror, on all but name. And while the George W. Bush administration was focused on the foreign threat, the number of domestic hate groups rose every year for a decade after the attacks, peaking some 20 years later under President Trump and feeding the extremist presence at the January 6 insurrection.\par \par \uc2 Now, one of the most strident critics of the Bush era contends that what happened at the \uc2 Capitol\uc2  on 1/6 was set in motion by 9/11.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Michael Moore, Oscar-award winning director of the 2004 film "Fahrenheit: 9/11" and host of the podcast "Rumble with Michael Moore."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Moore, Brother Michael, we appreciate your voice, specifically with us being here tonight. And as we mark this anniversary, you were one of the most visible critics of the war on terror and your film "Fahrenheit: 9/11" remains an essential document of that period. That initial sense of it, not unity, and shared experience for many Americans, but that was followed immediately by military response that resulted in dual destabilization that stretched for decades and near decades.\par \par \uc2 And here, you had an explosion of bigotry and scrutiny that Muslim-American communities like those in your home state contend with to this very day.\par \par \uc2 You told my colleague Brian Williams this week that \uc2 Osama bin Laden\uc2  ultimately won because of what happened to our constitutional rights, our privacy.\par \par \uc2 I wonder if you saw American race relations and Civil Rights included in that victory, and if so, explain how.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MOORE, FILMMAKER: Oh, absolutely. I remember the morning of 9/11 after the towers fell. I`m watching TV, and immediately without -- there had been no investigation or anything, everybody jumped to the fact that these had to be Muslims or Arabs. And while that was true, though, we didn`t know it. It was just the first instinctive reaction to say that, even though just two years prior to this, we had the Columbine massacre committed by two young white men, and six years before that, the bombing of Oklahoma City committed by two ex-military, white guys, white Americans.\par \par \uc2 So because we had those two horrific events before 9/11, it might have been okay to consider the possibility, but it didn`t exist.\par \par \uc2 When you said that that I was one of the few voices at that time to speak out against this and to slow the rush to war. That is not -- that is not an understatement in the sense that there was nobody allowed on TV that had a dissenting voice, and there was Barbara Lee, the only Member of Congress to vote against the invasion of Afghanistan, and myself, and a few others, but most Democrats if you remember, 29 Democratic senators voted in favor of giving Bush the War Powers for Iraq and all the Democrats in the Senate and the House with the exception of Barbara Lee voted in favor of the invasion of Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 [17:45:18]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes, I remember that, and frankly, for two or three years, they kept supporting the war on Iraq. When I ran in the Democratic primary for President, I was one of maybe two or three candidates out of nine that was against the war as late as 2004.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: But picking up from the last question, Michael, could you draw a line from 9/11 to January 6.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Because I saw what you tweeted after Afghanistan`s fall last month, quote -- I`m quoting you, "Their \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , our \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , everybody`s got a \uc2 Taliban\uc2 . They`re at their best when they confiscate the halls of power."\par \par \uc2 MOORE: All right, so --\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I thought about that statement, because according to the \uc2 Southern Poverty Law Center\uc2 , you had a continuous increase in the number of organized hate groups every year after the 9/11 attacks, a number that appeared to peak under President Obama`s first term, dipping under his second, before, of course, spiking under President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Do you think the insurrection happens without some of the cultural forces that were unleashed 20 years ago?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: There`s a direct line between the two. Race is always the factor.\par \par \uc2 I mean, just think about this, since the Germans were defeated in May of 1945, I think every single war, with the exception of maybe the Serbian war that we were involved in, which the old Yugoslavia had many, many Muslim citizens, every war has been against people of color, whether it was Korea, whether it was Vietnam, whether it was the Gulf War, the Iraq War, we don`t drop bombs on white people anymore.\par \par \uc2 And, this day -- there was an excellent piece of "Frontline" on \uc2 PBS\uc2  this week, where they very bravely drew the connection between a generation that grew up knowing that Bush and Cheney and Rumsfeld have lied about the reasons to take us to Iraq. And that so many people after that where so many National Guard people that were sent over there, they signed up for the National Guard to put out forest fires. They were sent over there to die.\par \par \uc2 And they came back, a lot of them, those who survived came back with a hatred of the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 , and a lot of Americans started then not trusting whatever the government is telling us. Don`t believe them, and they drew the line on "Frontline" this week to Bush, essentially generating this lack of trust and believing the government is lying to how easy it was to get so many people to believe that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  didn`t win the election, that Trump won -- Trump is still their President -- and that we have 80 million Americans who still haven`t gotten vaccinated, because they believe \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2  has put a chip inside the vaccine, and he is going to track us.\par \par \uc2 The madness of this belief in the unreal and the untrue began back then, after 9/11. The day after 9/11, on 9/12, Bush had not met in his whole first eight months, his head of counterterrorism, Richard Clarke. Finally, Clarke gets a meeting the day after 9/11, and Bush doesn`t want to talk about anything but Iraq.\par \par \uc2 He said he testified, that`s all they want to talk about, invading Iraq. Iraq didn`t invade us, Clarke tries to tell them. They didn`t want to hear it. They wanted to go after \uc2 Saddam Hussein\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Everything that we witnessed during that time with Bush and Cheney that was the beginning of this, and that has led us, yes, to January 6th, that has led us to this awful division, this sort of so far, hopefully, so far cold, civil war between each other.\par \par \uc2 But we`re not going to make it as Americans if we keep this fight going with each other. We could have our disagreements, but that`s not what`s been happening now. Now, it`s turned violent and the violence is encouraged by the former guy who held office for the last four years.\par \par \uc2 And now we`ve got a President trying to get us vaccinated, trying to save our lives, and trying to stand up against all the lies that are being told. And when he said that this week, $300 million a day, Al, $300 million a day is what we spent for 20 years on this war.\par \par \uc2 What would our cities look like today if we had that $300 million a day for 20 years?\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: What would our children have? What would our healthcare system look like? What -- I just put down the list and this is what I meant by essentially, bin Laden ended up winning because we`ve destroyed ourselves. We destroyed our social safety net. We`ve taken away our Constitution rights and the things we have.\par \par \uc2 [17:50:10]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And we are moving away from our constitutional rights. I`m going to have to leave it there.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Always great to have you on.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Sorry, we ran so long.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Michael Moore, thank you, as always ...\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: ... for your time.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Thank you, Al.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Up next, my final thoughts, stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: As I reflect on this, the 20th anniversary of 9/11, one of the things that occurs to me is those terrorists, those attackers that brought down the Twin Towers here in Manhattan, and the plane in Pennsylvania went down because of them and the attack on The Pentagon, they saw all Americans as the enemy, they went after all of us.\par \par \uc2 They didn`t sent in an e-mail telling blacks don`t go to work tomorrow, there will be an attack. They didn`t send in a message to Latinos or Asians saying, go to work late, there`s something that`s going to happen in the morning.\par \par \uc2 [17:55:08]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: They saw all of us as one that they had to go at all of us to make their point and to deal with what they felt was right. The question is, we don`t see all of us as one. Yes, we have political differences, I`m on my way to Texas now, but we are not each other`s enemies.\par \par \uc2 And we all should be fighting for what will help all of us protect all of our rights to vote equally, protect all of what we`re about.\par \par \uc2 We learned 20 years ago that we can die together. Now the challenge is, can we live together?\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That does it for me. Thanks for watching.\par \par \uc2 Please join me tomorrow at 5:00 p.m. Eastern for another live hour of POLITICSNATION where I`ll be joined by the administrator of the Environmental Protection Agency, \uc2 Michael Regan\uc2 . We will discuss President Biden`s warning of a climate change Code Red.\par \par \uc2 And hear Mr. Regan`s take on the very real threat of environmental racism as the first black man to lead the agency.\par \par \uc2 All of that and more tomorrow at 5:00 p.m. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 My colleague, Alicia Menendez picks up our news coverage now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 [18:00:01]\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020210912eh9b00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Last Word with Lawrence O`Donnell for September 9, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Ali Velshi, Joyce Vance, Stephanie Ruhle, Kavita Patel \par \uc2 8,153 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 September 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:15]\par \par \uc2 ALI VELSHI, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 That story you had about the flight leaving from Kabul, I think especially in this week where we`re going to be marking the anniversary of 9/11, we remember that flight crews, the pilots and the people on those planes exist for passenger safety, not to, you know, give you things on the plane. That`s bravery.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST, "TRMS": Yeah.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Taking those flights in and out of Kabul with those \uc2 Taliban\uc2  flags and \uc2 Taliban\uc2  on the tarmac. I mean, that`s never going to be normal. It shouldn`t be normal. But to be able to, you know, to be on that plane, land it, get passengers off, put passengers on and take off, a thought for all of those pilots and copilots and flight attendants and everybody on those planes who are helping people get to freedom tonight. So thank you for telling us about that.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Yeah, exactly. And you have to believe that this has got to be volunteers. You couldn`t put somebody in this circumstance, and tell them it`s part of their job. These are people that must have volunteered for this, despite those dangers in this absolutely unprecedented and intense situation there. The fact that this may happen on a daily basis going forward makes it more remarkable.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: I`m glad you pointed that out. Have yourself a great evening, and we`ll see you on Friday, being Friday eve tonight. Thanks, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Thanks, Ali.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI; Well, President Biden striking back at Republicans, calling out their efforts for all intents and purposes to extend the coronavirus, which we`re going to get to later this hour, lots of development in the fight against COVID.\par \par \uc2 But he`s also taking some Republicans to court, quote, open defiance of the Constitution, end quote. That`s how the Justice Department described the Texas abortion ban in a brand new lawsuit that argues that the legislation is unconstitutional and should be struck down. This is the first major move against this law from the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  said the law, which bans abortions after six weeks, which is before many women would know that they are pregnant with no exceptions for rape or incest violates Supreme Court precedent by denying women their constitutional rights. And he said the provision that effectively deputizes private citizens to be bounty hunters was a, quote, unprecedented scheme end quote to evade responsibility.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , ATTORNEY GENERAL: This kind of scheme to nullify the constitution of the United States is one that all Americans, whatever their politics or party should fear. If it prevails, it may become a model for action in other areas by other states.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: And that`s the point. One should be fearful of this regardless of where you stand on abortion. The Supreme Court has not yet ruled on the constitutionality of the Texas law but the conservative majority on the Supreme Court allowed it to go into effect anyway.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2  told NPR today that he thought quote the majority was very, very wrong to do that. That`s easy to see why because most abortion providers in Texas have now already closed their doors out of fear of being sued by vigilantes or worse. The Texas anti-abortion law allows anyone to become a citizen bounty hunter able to take abortion law enforcement into their own hands and it comes at a time when we`re seeing a massive increase in political violence in America, almost exclusively fueled by the right.\par \par \uc2 Some reminders, January 6th, when a violent mob hunted members of Congress and brutally attacked police. A rally later this month in Washington, September 18th. In support of the jailed insurrectionists which many are worried would bring more violence, parents across the country harassing educator advocating for wearing masks in schools.\par \par \uc2 There was a plot to kidnap the governor of Michigan because of COVID restrictions that she put in place. Public officials are being targeted in their homes over election lies, and "Reuters" just identified more than 100 threats of death or violence against election officials inspired by Trump`s lies. Texas has codified violence in its Republican voter suppression bill which empowers partisan potentially armed poll watchers to roam polling locations and intimidate voters, and now, it`s codified vigilante justice by incentivizing right wing anti-abortion extremists to go after anyone they choose and potentially claim a bounty. It`s hard for some of us so fathom, especially those of us who have never known the United States before Roe v. Wade, but this ban is having a real impact on real Americans.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:00]\par \par \uc2 Not everybody is going to be equally burdened by it, as abortion providers point out to "The Texas Tribune", quote, the new restrictions won`t establish a de facto ban on abortion for all Texans. Instead they argue, state lawmakers have created a system that will most heavily affect teenagers and Texans with disabilities, as well as further disadvantaged poor women of color, end quote. This Republican ban will hurt the women who need the help the most.\par \par \uc2 And now, laws just like it may soon hurt the same groups in other states. "The Washington Post" reports that Republican officials in at least seven states, including Arkansas, Florida, South Carolina, and South Dakota have suggested they may review or amend their states laws to mirror Texas`s legislation. Kentucky, Louisiana, Oklahoma, Ohio, and more are expected to follow.\par \par \uc2 Attorney General Garland vowed to fight similar lawsuits against states that copy the Texas ban.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GARLAND: The obvious and expressly acknowledged intention of this statutory scheme is to prevent women from exercising their constitutional rights. The United States has the authority and responsibility to ensure that no state can deprive individuals of their constitutional rights through a legislative scheme specifically designed to prevent the vindication of those rights.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Leading off our discussion tonight, Texas State Representative Jasmine Crockett. She is a civil rights attorney. And Joyce Vance, a former federal prosecutor, a professor at the University of Alabama School of Law, and MSNBC legal contributor.\par \par \uc2 Thank you to both of you for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Representative Crockett, we have had some opportunities to speak in the last few months, many of them about the voter suppression laws in Texas. But now, we`ve got this, which took some people by surprise. We didn`t think that this Texas law would be the one that the Supreme Court would stumble over.\par \par \uc2 But here we have it, and I think that point that abortion providers have made to "The Texas Tribune", this doesn`t affect everybody. Women with means will still be able to go to another state, ultimately another country, but women of limited means, poor women, families, poor families, they will be the ones affected by this because they will not risk somebody getting fined or being taken to task by the vigilantes who are now empowered by this law?\par \par \uc2 STATE REP. JASMINE CROCKETT (D), TEXAS: Yeah, no, absolutely right. First of all, good evening to you, and this is a story of the haves versus the have notes. Sadly enough, that`s what we continue to see out of the state of Texas is that we want to make sure that the privileged few are literally that, few. And so, we`re going to have people that are in financially strained situations. We`re going to have those that, you know, I know there`s been this big debate about rape, right, but you`re going to have people in very desperate situations for very different reasons, and they`re not going to be able to access the health care that we need.\par \par \uc2 And so what I need the Republicans to say with me is that abortion is health care, say it with me, guys, I know that it`s hard for you to understand because you fail to possess a uterus, but maybe you should start listening to those of us that do.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: That`s a wise point to make. Joyce Vance, you wrote a column two days ago which I have tweeted out because what happened in Texas vis-a-vis the Supreme Court is complicated. There are a lot of nuances with it, and what has happened today with the Department of Justice is also complicated because there`s the discussion of the merits of Roe v. Wade and the Texas law, and then there`s a discussion of process and procedure and what \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  calls a scheme. I`m going to ask you in the limited way in which we can do on television to explain what the Department of Justice is now doing in the face of what happened at the Supreme Court last week.\par \par \uc2 JOYCE VANCE, MSNBC LEGAL CONTRIBUTOR: Well, you`re right, Ali, this is a little bit nuanced, but maybe this will help folks understand. What DOJ did today was they threw the Supreme Court`s decision in the case last week back in its face. Last week, the Supreme Court said, gee, we`ve never seen this sort of private enforcement scheme before where Texas has turned anyone who wants to be a vigilante against abortion into a vigilante who can go out and find these women and claim a $10,000 bounty if they sue the people around them who aided and abetted them in having an abortion.\par \par \uc2 And the Supreme Court just sort of said, yeah, Texas, you have outsmarted us. We`re not going to keep this law from going into effect because we don`t know how to respond to this novel approach that you have taken. Well, DOJ`s response today was very clear. They said because Texas has effectively tied the Supreme Court`s hands with these private plaintiffs who wants to challenge the abortion law, DOJ has standing, has the ability, has the legal authority to step in and challenge the provision.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:07]\par \par \uc2 They made their case that much stronger because of what the Supreme Court did last week.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Representative Crockett, Texas has not ceased to surprise us a lot in recent months. We have become accustomed to some of the weird stuff that goes on in Texas, but one of the things that was most interesting, you mentioned this in your last response to me about rape, there`s some weird conversations going on about rape, and particularly pregnancies that may result from rape. Your governor has said that`s okay because we`ll just eliminate rape, which is a fantastic goal. But it is a little confounding that that was his response.\par \par \uc2 CROCKETT: It was flippant, and it was ridiculous, and the sad part was this was part of his priority agenda, so he should have had an actual response ready, whatever that was going to be. But we know this idea that he can wave a magic wand and somehow get rid of rape, my question to the governor is why haven`t you gotten rid of rape before, and you need to tell everybody else in this country how to do it.\par \par \uc2 You know, but what we do know is when it comes to policies, there`s no money that the governor has actually prioritized for the purpose of processing rape kits. We know that he`s not prioritized rapes in general, and honestly, when you start talking about a rape kit, what does that mean? That means that the rape has already occurred.\par \par \uc2 And so this idea that he is somehow all knowing, and some god is going to be able to get rid of something that has existed since the beginning of time is ridiculous. We should be ashamed that this is the leader of the second largest state in this country, and these are the type of responses that we get about something that is so fundamental as a constitutional right.\par \par \uc2 We should be ashamed that this man was on our Supreme Court. We should be ashamed that this man was our attorney general. He obviously doesn`t understand the very basics of constitutional law, and therefore he never should have served in those capacities, and honestly I can`t wait for somebody to kick him out in this upcoming election.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Joyce Vance, there are a number of cases that the department of justice is making but one of them has to do as Representative Crockett just said about constitutionality, and about something called the supremacy clause. Can you explain to me what the department of justice is saying about the fact that the federal government in our case has opined on the legality of an abortion, what does the Department of Justice want done about this?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: DOJ is relying on constitutional principles that say that when the federal government has occupied the space, occupied this area as they allege they have in this complaint with the 14th Amendment and the Roe versus Wade guarantee to women of abortion rights that the state can`t interfere with those rights, that that would violate the supremacy clause. There`s really good case law that supports this, Ali. It goes back to the 1940s and the 1950s where there were covenants that prohibited people who were African Americans from purchasing homes and ultimately when private residents sued to try to enforce those clauses, the Supreme Court said that`s too much. States you can`t use private citizens, in this case, it was Missouri, you can`t use your private citizens to try to enforce these unconstitutional aims.\par \par \uc2 So, DOJ will draw a straight line from that well-established body of case law, and use that to make the argument, hear that Texas cannot use private citizens to enforce its unconstitutional aims.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: I`m clear on a lot of this thanks to you two tonight. Thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 Texas State Representative Jasmine Crockett and Joyce Vance, we appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, big news on the delta variant, President Biden has said enough is enough with the explosion of case counts, hospitalizations, and deaths driven by unvaccinated people. The administration making it much, much harder for people to avoid doing the right thing -- that`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:18:06]\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: President Biden had a message today for the 80 million Americans who still haven`t been vaccinated against COVID. The time for waiting and excuses is over.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Over 200 million Americans have gotten at least one shot. We`ve been patient, but our patience is wearing thin. And your refusal has cost all of us. So please do the right thing.\par \par \uc2 This is a pandemic of the unvaccinated. And it`s caused by the fact that despite America having unprecedented and successful vaccination program, despite the fact that for almost five months free vaccines have been able in 80,000 different locations, we still have nearly 80 million Americans who have failed to get the shot. And to make matters worse, there are elected officials actively working to undermine the fight against COVID-19. Instead of encouraging people to get vaccinated and mask up, they`re ordering mobile morgues for the unvaccinated dying from COVID in their communities. This is totally unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: The president`s message to Republican governors and GOP activists pushing anti-public health policies was just as harsh.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The path ahead even with the delta variant is not nearly as bad as last winter, well, what makes it incredibly more frustrating is we have the tools to combat COVID-19 and a distinct number of Americans, supported by a distinct number of elected officials are keeping us from turning the corner. These pandemic politics are making people sick, causing unvaccinated people to die. We cannot allow these actions to stand in the way of protecting the large majority of Americans who have done their part, who want to get back to life as normal.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [22:20:08]\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: In short, a vocal conservative minority is extending the length of the pandemic. So, the president is fighting back. Today, he announced new vaccine mandates for all federal workers, millions of federal government contract workers, more than 17 million hospital, home health care, and other medical workers, and employers with 100 or more workers. These businesses will either force their employees to get vaccinated or have those unvaccinated employees undergo weekly COVID testing.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is my former partner in crime, Stephanie Ruhle, NBC News business correspondent and host, senior business correspondent and host of the 9:00 a.m. hour here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 Stephan, I have seen press releases going out from companies and organizations, some not loving this, most overwhelmingly are embracing this. Most companies want this cover because they have been trying to figure out how to get their employees vaccinated and now the federal government has extended that awning over them just a little bit more.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, NBC NEWS BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Bingo, Yahtzee, amen, and hallelujah. Is this administration going to get sued for this? Sure, without a doubt. But bet your bottom dollar these lawsuits are not going to go far.\par \par \uc2 Overwhelmingly, the business community is saying thank you for the air cover. This is what they want. Remember, CEOs are not political, and if they are, they keep politics to their own private life. They want to get business done. They want customers to want to do business with them. They want their employees in their offices and safe and they know that this is the way to do it.\par \par \uc2 And by the administration or excuse me, the Labor Department really enacting this, it gives companies the ability to say I`m not asking you to do it. It`s the law, and remember because it`s OSHA, they can fine a company, they`re the regulator, it can actually cost them. It can hurt their bottom line.\par \par \uc2 They want COVID behind us so they can move forward, so if we want to move forward and be healthy as a country and have our economy be healthy, this is the fastest way to do it.\par \par \uc2 Ali, we have to remember, it was a three-pronged approach. The government developed the vaccine. The medical community are the ones who educated us. And it`s businesses who hold the carrot to influence the majority of the American people to actually get vaccinated and that`s how we`re going to move on.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: That makes a lot of sense. Steph, good to see you my friend, people tweet me, why are you and Stephanie not on TV together. We`re going to be together from eleven to two.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: We don`t get along.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: There`s that. We`re going to be together from 11:30 to 2:00 Eastern on Saturday for the commemoration of 9/11. I`m looking forward to being back with you my friend.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: There`s nobody I would rather do it with.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: And joining us now -- thanks, friend.\par \par \uc2 Joining us is Representative \uc2 Kim Schrier\uc2 , Democrat from Washington state. She is the first pediatrician to become a member of Congress. And, Dr. Kavita Patel, former Obama White House health policy director, MSNBC medical contributor, and someone I have relied on for years for health policy discussions.\par \par \uc2 Thanks to both of you for being here. Representative Schneider, thank you for being here, and halleluiah, we got a pediatrician in Congress.\par \par \uc2 This is sort of a moment to hear from pediatricians who happen to be legislators, we have a unique issue in this country, we are not able to vaccinate our youngest. They`ve got to go to school, so we kind of -- the rest of us who can get vaccines need to do our utmost to protect them. They`re not immune from COVID, and they`re not immune from dying of COVID.\par \par \uc2 REP. KIM SCHRIER\uc2  (D-WA): That is exactly right, Ali. I want to thank you for saying that because I was listening to President Biden talk about this being a pandemic of the unvaccinated, and I was thinking in of my pediatrician and also children. They are susceptible to all of the choices we all make. They have not -- if they`re under 12, they don`t have the option of being vaccinated, they are really, you know, at the whim of how responsible we all decide to be.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Kavita, all the stuff that happened today, it might seem incremental, certainly to a lot of my viewers who are vaccinated, wondering how this has to happened. When taken together, all federal workers, federal contractors, companies with more than a hundred people, that`s a big move of the needle. There are a whole lot of people who tomorrow morning are going to be able to say if you are part of my organization, you need to get vaccinated or be subject to a whole lot of testing.\par \par \uc2 KAVITA PATEL, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: Ali, I can confidently tell you this might be go down as the single important action to help decrease the impact of the pandemic and put the worst of it behind us. We`re not going to eliminate it, of course, I actually think what the president did today, and it`s so much more than even we have the time to kind of cover, you peel it away, and it mandates, teachers, and school officials who were withheld for salaries, just because they stood up for masks.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:09]\par \par \uc2 I mean, there`s so much there. I really do think that this plan today could define a moment. I thought it would be when the vaccines were discovered, approved and researched that will definitely go down in history. This will be one of the president`s actions that will go down in history for helping us beat this pandemic.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: And one of the things that happened, Dr. Schrier this evening, we heard from the L.A. school district, one of the largest in the country, they`re going to require students 12 and older to be vaccinated against COVID-19.\par \par \uc2 Talk to me about, there`s a patchwork of things going on in schools across this country. Some are openly defying their governors who say we can`t have mask mandates. What does success look like for schools across the country right now?\par \par \uc2 SCHRIER: Let me first say, bravo to L.A. Unified. I think that is a really smart decision. They are looking out for the best interest of their students. I was meeting with them yesterday, and I`m excited to see this progress.\par \par \uc2 Look, we require all kinds of vaccinations to go to school, chickenpox, measles, mumps, hepatitis B, they`re all required, and COVID right now is the single biggest threat to our children and should absolutely be required.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Kavita, how much coverage, Stephanie and I were just talking about this, the stuff that happened today has given cover to some companies that want people to be vaccinated. How much does it break the back of the resistance? Because at some point, it needs to stop, right, people have got to figure out if you want to function in this world, and we want to move forward, we have to do that.\par \par \uc2 The president was very, very firm about where the resistance, there`s always some legitimate resistance to vaccinations and drugs but the president was very clear that a lot of this is politicized.\par \par \uc2 Does that make a difference, is it going to make people sit there and say I need to get vaccinated?\par \par \uc2 PATEL: I think it is, Ali. Look, if we go to other countries where they have had something similar, France, Italy, Greece, parts of the U.K., Ireland, these types of requirements and mandates have been effective in overcoming the resistance, including the United States, we`re going to continue to see the GOP outcry, you can`t force us to do this, it`s government overreach, Ali, I can promise you, as people see cases dropping, less children in ICUs and schools opening and the laughter of children all around us and normal workplace environments, that resistance is going to be overcome.\par \par \uc2 I would say he didn`t go far enough. I would have loved to have seen him but I know it was a step too far to have a domestic travel mandate for vaccinations to actually do what New York state is doing, a lot of leisure activities that we want to be a part of immediately require vaccination. You know what? I think the president`s actions today give more and more municipalities and states, and your point, private entities the authority to do that.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Yeah, I have gone to restaurants in New York where they ask for proof of vaccination. I feel safer in the environment as a result of them doing it.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Schrier, I want to ask you, the president is drawing a bit of a distinction between people who have hesitancy or reasons to not want the vaccine, and I don`t know how you say it politely, BS, the horse deworming nonsense, and all of this kind of stuff.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know what you in Congress can do about this, but disinformation and misinformation is ruining our democracy. It has affected our elections but it`s actually killing people right now.\par \par \uc2 SCHRIER: It is incredibly frustrating, especially as a pediatrician, to see this. And look, in 20 years as a pediatrician, I met with lots and lots of parents who were anxious about vaccines, and we sat down and had conversations and built trust and we got those children vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 This is a whole different animal. And what I would love to see, frankly, is for everybody who`s worried about the vaccine to just get in touch with their doctor. Talk to their trusted physician who they developed a relationship with and get the information from them. Not from crazy online.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: That is good advice. Thanks to both of you for being here.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Kim Schrier\uc2  is a United States member of Congress, and Dr. Kavita Patel is the former member of the Obama White House policy, she`s a former White House policy director and MSNBC medical contributor.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, does good public health policy makes for good politics? We`re going to ask the Democrat in charge of helping Democrats to keep the House in 2022. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:33:42]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Right now, local school officials are trying to keep children safe in a pandemic while their governor picks a fight with them and even threatens their salaries or their jobs. If these governors won`t help us beat the pandemic, I`ll use my power as president to get them out of the way.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: President Biden`s making sure American voters know exactly who`s preventing us from getting out of this pandemic. People who are unvaccinated by choice and Republican leaders who are actively putting people including young children who are not yet eligible for the vaccine at risk.\par \par \uc2 The most recent Republican president suggested that Americans might inject bleach into themselves to prevent COVID, and now some Republican lawmakers and conservative media are out there claiming that taking horse de-wormers will cure it.\par \par \uc2 But America is not a 50/50 country when it comes to COVID. The people taking de-wormers and burning masks and screaming about freedom at flight attendants are out of step with what most Americans want, which is not to get COVID. 75 percent of adults have received at least one vaccine shot. 70 percent of parents are ok with K to 12 children wearing masks in schools.\par \par \uc2 [22:34:54]\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: You can`t get 70 percent of people to agree on anything in this country including what day it is. These are huge majorities in a polarized electorate. It might be why Democrats have decided to directly confront Republican obstruction to ending this pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now Congressman \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2 . He`s the chair of the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee. Representative Maloney, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 Republicans, sir, are betting on the fact that Americans are weary of pandemic restrictions and masks and mandates and all this. And I think Democrats are betting that Americans are weary of coronavirus.\par \par \uc2 REP. SEAN PATRICK MALONEY\uc2  (D-NY): Well, you know, what I`m willing to bet is that people who want this government to do what it takes to end the pandemic, and that understand that the only reason we`re still dealing with this is that a bunch of Americans have been fed a lie by the former president and bye his friends in the media and other politicians.\par \par \uc2 First about how serious this pandemic was because they never took it seriously enough. Secondly about the importance of mask wearing, and they`re still fighting that. And most recently they`ve been undermining at every step our efforts to get people vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 And so enough. I`m glad to see the president strengthening the requirements. I think it`s past due. And I think it`s our job to make sure that people understand that the Republican Party has been reckless and irresponsible on the pandemic and they have been feeding a lie to millions of Americans who unfortunately have believed it, and that`s why we`re still dealing with this pandemic.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: You are on the Transportation and Infrastructure Committee. It was one of the few things that Republicans and Democrats were actually able to come together on to talk about infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 Now we`ve got the second bill, which a whole bunch of Democrats didn`t want to get done, you know. They wanted it to get done ahead of the infrastructure bill.\par \par \uc2 We have people including one of your front line colleagues, Stephanie Murphy of Florida who said today she`s not ready to vote on this bill. She tweeted out, "Without the full text of the Ways and Means portion of the bill I simply cannot make an informed substantiative decision. But I`ll keep working to get this bill to a place where I can support it and become law."\par \par \uc2 Talk to me in your job, which is responsible for keeping the House majority, how you moderate this matter between some moderate Democrats and some progressive Democrats who don`t want the same thing.\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: Well, let me give you a guide to the whole thing, which is Democrats are going to deliver. And there was a recent back and forth with some of our own members about the best way forward.\par \par \uc2 And in the end, what happened? 100 percent of us came together, and voted to advance the president`s agenda. We are going to deliver results. And members have all kinds of opinions and that`s as it should be.\par \par \uc2 And they`re going to all get a chance to voice them. But we are going to pass this infrastructure bill. It will be done on a bipartisan basis. And we`re going to pass the Build Back Better Plan so that we also invest in pre-K and in child care. And make that child tax credit permanent. And make sure community college is available to all Americans. And invest in our people in a way we haven`t done in far too long.\par \par \uc2 And we`re going to get these things done as a party that believes in results. And we`re going to let the other side spin their webs and try and divide Americans and really hope that the country fails and think that it`s easier to be critics than to be problem solvers.\par \par \uc2 But we`re going to get these things done. And we`re going to let the results speak for themselves.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: To our most recently NBC News/Wall Street Journal poll taken in mid-August about who should control Congress gives Democrats the slight edge but it`s within the margin of error. The margin of error is 3.1 percent. Democrats get 47, Republicans get 46.\par \par \uc2 Look, some people say it`s stupid to be talking about this in September of 2021. But what has to happen for your purposes to move those numbers in your favor? How do you get more Americans say that Democrats deserve to keep control of Congress?\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: Look, you know, I spent the day touring the still fresh damage around my district from the recent storms. We`ve got dozens of people dead here in New York -- failing infrastructure, climate change events that are becoming more and more evident every day. So what we`re going to do is focus on real problems like that, and we`re going to get them done.\par \par \uc2 What I can tell you is the military guys say the only way out is through. And the only way through these problems for us politically is to deliver on the policy, to get results for the American people, to go big, to do it now, to make sure people feel it, to tell them we did it.\par \par \uc2 And to make sure they know that the red team has lost their minds, and they are engaged in things that are racist, and that are violent, and that are dangerous, like conspiracy theories and the kinds of nonsense we have seen from that party.\par \par \uc2 But we`re going to fix hard problems, and we`re going to do it because it`s the right thing to do. And we`re going to do it because it`s the best politics in the end.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Congressman, good to see you. Thanks for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: My pleasure.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Congressman \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we`re going to talk about a new effort by Republicans to claim victory for Trump in a state where \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  beat him in 2020, and what that means for both 2022 and 2024.\par \par \uc2 We`re also going to show you one of the best footnotes ever which happens to come from my hometown paper. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:59]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Republicans can conduct fake audits, and recounts and re-recounts until the 2024 presidential election. But they`re not going to find widespread voter fraud from the 2020 election because it doesn`t exist. There simply was none.\par \par \uc2 Many Republicans know that. They`re still fighting the results because the twice-impeached former president wants them to do. And so today Pennsylvania Republicans held a hearing about quote "an investigation on the 2020 general election" end quote, an investigation only opened because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  pushed them to open it.\par \par \uc2 When Republicans in Arizona began their partisan review of ballots in Maricopa County, Arizona, the secretary of state there \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  referred to it as a fraud-it because it wasn`t legitimate. It was just a spectacle to mollify Trump.\par \par \uc2 [22:44:59]\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: But what`s happening in Pennsylvania isn`t legitimate either. And "The Philadelphia Inquirer" is refusing to play the GOP`s democracy- destroying game.\par \par \uc2 Here`s how "The Inquirer" explains what`s going on, quote, "The Inquirer is not currently referring to attempts by Pennsylvania Republicans to investigate the 2020 presidential election as an audit because there`s no indication that it would follow the best practices or the common understanding of an audit among nonpartisan experts.\par \par \uc2 When asked by The Inquirer, lawmakers leading the effort have not explained how it would actually be run, including whether best practices would be followed. Who would be involved, including what role partisan politicians would play. How the review would be documented, how election equipment and ballots would be secured, and what the scope of any review would be. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won Pennsylvania by more than 80,000 votes." End quote.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Pennsylvania Attorney General \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2 . Attorney general, that`s actually a good start, if media organizations around the country decided to call the BS out for what it is. These are not audits, they`re nonsense.\par \par \uc2 JOSH SHAPIRO\uc2 , PENNSYLVANIA ATTORNEY GENERAL: They`re absolute nonsense. And \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  is right, it`s a fraud-it. I think it`s a charade and a sham and I think what is important for Pennsylvanians to know is that these Republican Pennsylvania senators are using taxpayer dollars to undermine the faith in our democracy with this charade.\par \par \uc2 And I`ve been hearing for weeks, actually months that this would be some kind of big blockbuster hearing today. Instead, today`s hearing was a complete and total dud that produced zero evidence in their attempts to satisfy the very people that they have been lying to for the last ten months.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Well, let`s show our viewers what we mean. Let`s play a little bit from this hearing. What you`re going to hear from is the Fulton County, Pennsylvania commissioner, Stuart Ulsh, he`s a Republican; and the Pennsylvania state senator, Vincent Hughes, who is a Democrat. Ulsh was the only person to testify today. Let`s hear a little of the exchange.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STATE SENATOR VINCENT HUGHES (D-PA): In your investigation, was there any fraudulent voting ever found out -- found in the votes?\par \par \uc2 STUART ULSH, FULTON COUNTY COMMISSIOER: My report come back -- it`s in our report, but no, nothing was found. Everything was ran in Fulton County normally.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HUGHES: Everything was square, up and up, no fraudulent voting.\par \par \uc2 ULSH: That`s what our report says.\par \par \uc2 HUGHES: That`s what the report says.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Well, that`s not a wildly successful first outing for this investigation into the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 SHAPIRO: No, but first off that was a great question by Senator Hughes. And it`s the truth, that is that there was no fraud in this election. We had a free and fair, safe and secure election that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won by just over 80,000 votes.\par \par \uc2 The problem is these Republican state senators continued to put conspiracies in front of our state constitution, continue to put important issues like climate change and job creation and educating our kids, dealing with gun violence on the back burner so they can instead focus on perpetuating the big lie.\par \par \uc2 Understand that before they had these hearings, they didn`t check with the taxpayers. They didn`t check with the people. They checked with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and asked him if he was comfortable with the direction that they were going.\par \par \uc2 And now they find themselves caught in the middle of their news max talking points and the reality of what happened here in Pennsylvania, again, that we had a free and fair, safe and secure election.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: But a lot of people say, look, it`s not going to happen. I mean what you`re seeing in Arizona or Georgia or Texas can`t happen in Pennsylvania because you`ve got a Republican governor who`s not going to let some of this nonsense go through in terms of some of the changes.\par \par \uc2 SHAPIRO: Democratic governor.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: I`m sorry, Democratic governor -- that`s correct -- who`s not going to let the nonsense go through. But that`s not really the right assurance we should be having. We shouldn`t be -- we need to figure out ways to not entertain this kind of nonsense because at some point, in every state, there will be a Democratic governor there won`t be a Democratic governor. That`s not the point, right.\par \par \uc2 The point is how do we prevent absolutely unlawful, mythical, made up stuff from possibly coming close to becoming law?\par \par \uc2 SHAPIRO: Speaking truth, and by not giving these folks who continue to perpetuate the big lie a platform to share it.\par \par \uc2 Look, they have been spoon-feeding lies to the good people of Pennsylvania for the last ten months. And so I understand why some in Pennsylvania have reacted with questions about our elections, questions about our democracy. Not because there`s any legitimate reasons for them to feel that way but because they have been lied to by these elected officials, lied to by the modern-day Republican Party who`s not beholden to truth but rather beholden and subservient to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [22:50:02]\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Pennsylvania Attorney General \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2 , thanks for joining us as always tonight.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, what America is doing to help the Afghan refugees. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Today the first civilian passenger flight to leave Afghanistan, to leave Kabul Airport since the U.S. military evacuation ended arrived in Doha, Qatar. More than a hundred passengers departed from Kabul.\par \par \uc2 This is actually the Kabul departure right now. This is at an airport fully-controlled by the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  flying \uc2 Taliban\uc2  flags. This is them taking off from Qatar.\par \par \uc2 The people on that flight included Canadian, Ukrainian, German, British and U.S. citizens. From Doha they will go to their final destinations.\par \par \uc2 That flights marks a new stage in the effort to get Americans and Afghan allies out of the country.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:51]\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Members of Wisconsin`s congressional delegation, including our next guest, Congressman \uc2 Mark Pocan\uc2  met this week with Afghan refugees being housed at Fort McCoy in Wisconsin. Here`s what Congressman Pocan said after the meeting.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. MARK POCAN\uc2  (D-WI): I can very confidently say that there`s an excellent job that`s happening right here at Fort McCoy by all the agencies involved and that we`re going to help an awful lot of people who helped the United States in finding places in this country. And it`s our obligation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Joining us now is Democratic Congressman \uc2 Mark Pocan\uc2  of Wisconsin. Congressman, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 I want to put up a poll, it`s an NPR/Ipsos poll which shows what Americans think about evacuating Afghans who worked with the United states and resettling them in the United States. 69 percent support it, 19 percent oppose it. And again, 69 percent of support is something in this politicized environment is actually quite a big deal.\par \par \uc2 There`s no question amongst 69 percent at least of Americans these folks helped us, they put themselves on the line, they`re in danger, we need to evacuate them and bring them here.\par \par \uc2 POCAN: Absolutely, Ali. Thank you so much for having me here.\par \par \uc2 You know, we went there. It`s a really well-run operation. By the weekend already I think they`re going to have some people going into communities throughout the country and this is our obligation and it`s also our opportunity to thank people who protected our soldiers who were in Afghanistan for 20 years. They and their families now are out of any harm`s way with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  and we`re going to make sure that they`re part of this country.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Interestingly enough, there are some conservatives who were putting -- I didn`t want to put the pictures up because they`re frankly offensive - - but pictures of these groups of refugees. I don`t know if they`re real or they`re just random Muslims that they put pictures up of and instilling fear.\par \par \uc2 "You really want these people here? You really want these people in this country?" Our history in America with refugees across the board has been overwhelmingly positive. People forced out of their own countries who are allowed into the United States kiss the ground that they come to and generally make themselves very hard working and successful in America.\par \par \uc2 POCAN: Yes, no question. In fact, they`ve been really great at Fort McCoy, nothing but great stories. They`re getting vaccinated. In fact, the COVID vaccine out of near capacity -- with the capacity of 13,000 people there, one person has turned down a COVID vaccine. Wouldn`t you like to see that with the United States population.\par \par \uc2 So you know, they`re ready to be a part of this country. They served and protected our soldiers for many, many years. And any kind of rhetoric about whatever background they may have, we have to remember if we want to ever be able to work with another country in a future situation, we`re going to have to deliver, and I think right now this country certainly is lifting both its hands and hearts to those people.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: So those you saw, the 12,000 people who were there -- you know some (INAUDIBLE) you had some sense of how pleased they are to be in America. Did you get a chance to talk to any of them about what`s going on back home and how they feel about the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  having taken over Afghanistan?\par \par \uc2 POCAN: We weren`t allowed to talk to them directly. We did talk to one of the interpreters who shared some stories with us. But, you know, this is something where we really are doing our best right now. They`re doing their best at the facility, you know. It`s going near perfect in the transition.\par \par \uc2 And now, you know, they`re finding 50,000 places across the country to host folks. So, you know, people will start going throughout the country.\par \par \uc2 But this is really important that we get it done right. I think we`re on the right track. And you know, I give President Biden a lot of credit because he made a very tough, but important decision to leave Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 And I think at the end of the day you look at polling there as well, people agree with that decision.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: What do you think as a member of Congress we need to be thinking about as next steps with Afghanistan? We`ve heard from \uc2 Tony Blinken\uc2 , the secretary of state, that we do not think we`re going to be recognizing or any time soon recognize the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But, you know, these flights that are taking off out of Kabul, there`s a certain amount of coordination that is going to have to be done because, like it or not, the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  are now the government of Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 Yes. I think we`re going to have to see how the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  decides to proceed. If they`re going to really be a more responsible government or if we`re going to have additional issues.\par \par \uc2 But I think what`s important is we need to double our efforts of getting more folks out of Afghanistan, especially some of the interpreters, you know. I think there is an issue with some of the families still are in Afghanistan. And since they`re helping us with interpreting right now, we really have to do more to get those folks out.\par \par \uc2 But, again, I`ve been really impressed with the effort despite the fact that the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  took over so quickly. I think \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  got things done by August 31st, as he said, and we`ve got ourselves out of this situation.\par \par \uc2 And I just hope that the next time someone decides they`re going to go into a country that`s very different than the United States, that they don`t do what \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2  and George Bush did which is think that we`re going to leave with a bunch of Dunkin` Donuts and \uc2 Disney\uc2  Lands.\par \par \uc2 That was never going to happen and it`s the right thing to get out of there. But let`s make sure we pause next time someone looks at nation- building in a country like this.\par \par \uc2 [22:59:59]\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Congressman \uc2 Mark Pocan\uc2 , thanks for joining us tonight. Congressman \uc2 Mark Pocan\uc2  of Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH BRIAN WILLIAMS" begins right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020210910eh9900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Rachel Maddow Show for September 9, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Rachel Maddow, Barbara McQuade \par \uc2 7,222 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 September 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Rachel Maddow Show\par \uc2 TRMADS\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [21:00:00]\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Chris. Thanks, my friend. Much appreciated it.\par \par \uc2 And thanks to you at home for joining us this hour.\par \par \uc2 You start in the summer of 1964. You go through the summer of 1965, and you get to the whole shocking climax of the thing by the summer of 1966.\par \par \uc2 It was a short period of time. It was world shaking stuff, but it happened fast and definitively and it all spooled out over the course of those two years -- `64 to `66. Start in the summer of `64.\par \par \uc2 July 2nd, 1964, President Lyndon Baines Johnson signs the Civil Rights Act. He signs the Civil Rights Act in 1964 into law. That`s the Civil Rights Act that had been filibustered by segregationists for 54 straight days.\par \par \uc2 But in the end, their efforts to stop it failed. LBJ signed it. The Civil Rights Act of 1964, of course, banned segregation in public facilities. Also in private facilities to which the public had access.\par \par \uc2 The segregationists at the time screamed bloody murder. It`s unconstitutional. You can`t tell private entities what to do. Actually, yes, yes you can.\par \par \uc2 In the summer of 1964, President Johnson made that law of the land. Then the following summer, July 1965, LBJ actually went out on the road this time for an unusual signing ceremony. He went to Missouri to President Truman`s presidential library in Independence, Missouri, to sign into law the bill that created Medicare and Medicaid.\par \par \uc2 He went to Truman`s Presidential Library because it was Truman who had first proposed that the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  should commit to providing health coverage to all the elderly people in the country. Truman couldn`t get it done, while he was president, but in 1965, LBJ got it done. He signed the bill creating Medicare in July 1965.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, the conservatives at the time screamed bloody murder. A high profile conservative activist at the time named \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  denounced this new program, this Medicare program as the end of America, as the end of freedom. It would mean a communist takeover of America. Be very, very afraid.\par \par \uc2 LBJ was not afraid, and it turns out, giving health insurance coverage to all the old people in the country doesn`t turn us into Russia or China. We`re still America. We`re just an America where old people get to see the doctor and get their prescriptions. Turns out it`s not the end of freedom.\par \par \uc2 At the time what the Johnson administration was actually most worried about, what serious critics and not just demagogues were worried about, was that there would be a flood of new patients into America`s hospitals because old people who couldn`t previously afford to see a doctor, now they would have health insurance coverage, they would be able to see a doctor. There were worries this new tide, this new flood of elderly patients suddenly accessing health services would swamp the nation`s hospitals.\par \par \uc2 That was the sort of substantive concern about the implementation of Medicare, and in the end that fear didn`t really materialize either, but the worry that it might and the worry about all the other things it would take to implement that big new change made LBJ and the Johnson administration build in a bit of lead time before the program actually started. So as I said, LBJ signed the Medicare bill in July 1965 but gave the country until July 1966 before Medicare would actually go into effect. So he basically gave the country, gave his administration a year to get it rolled out, right? We`ve got a year. Ready, steady, go.\par \par \uc2 Funny thing, though -- more than halfway through that yearlong wind-up, more than halfway through that yearlong on-ramp that LBJ had given himself to get implemented, nine months into that year that he had given the administration and the country to prepare for it, the Johnson administration suddenly realized what that Johnson signed into law in 1965, the Medicare bill, and what Johnson signed into law in 1964, the civil rights bill, they had something important to say to each other, which was maybe going to be a big problem in terms of implementing Medicare. Thanks to Medicare, every single old person in America was about to start having their medical bills paid for by this new federal program. Medicare was about to start a huge, huge new stream of federal government funding into hospitals all over the country.\par \par \uc2 But at that time all over the south, hospitals scoffed at the Civil Rights Act of 1964.\par \par \uc2 [21:05:04]\par \par \uc2 Hospitals considered themselves immune from that kind of federal government meddling and hospitals all over the south were absolutely positively rigidly segregated. There were literally thousands of whites only hospitals across the south. And the Johnson administration came to this revelation during that year they had given themselves to roll out Medicare.\par \par \uc2 He signed it in the July 1965. It was due to go into effect in July 1966. But in March 1966, just four months before Medicare is due to start, due to go into practice, the Johnson administration sent out a polite but firm letter to hospitals throughout the country making clear to them that if they wanted to participate in this new Medicare program, if they wanted to qualify to get any money under this huge new program, ensuring every old person in America they could not discriminate on the basis of race at their facilities.\par \par \uc2 Now, that`s not going to be a problem, right? Everybody, that`s not going to be a big dealing right? No issue? The letter went out in March 1966. The program was supposed to be implemented July 1st that same year. By may 1966, "the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " was reporting on its front page on an emergency meeting that had been convened by the Johnson administration, convened at the White House, to get the hospitals to move it, or to prepare for the consequences if they did not.\par \par \uc2 This is from "the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " may 24, 1966. Quote, top official of state hospital associations in 20 states were called in today for an emergency conference down the hall from Health Secretary John Gardner`s office. Although the meeting was closed H.E. Hamilton, president of the Louisiana hospitals association told reporters what the greatest problem is. He said, quote, it`s the requirement that Negros and whites be permitted to share the same hospital room, he said.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know what hospitals will do, but I see some of them don`t see how they could comply with that. You know what? Turns out they could comply with that.\par \par \uc2 That was late May 1966. By the following month, June 1966, "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " was still estimating that 2,000 whites only hospitals in the American south wouldn`t be compliant with the civil rights law and so they wouldn`t be certified to participate in the Medicare program. But by the month after that, by July, when Medicare went into effect, those hospitals had dug deep and found it within themselves to start complying with the Civil Rights Act after all.\par \par \uc2 Around the 50th anniversary of Medicare in 2015, there was some renewed attention to that quiet but definitive way President Johnson and his Health Secretary \uc2 John Gardner\uc2  desegregated American hospitals overnight. There were headline like this one in "The Washington Post", the massive role that Medicare played in racial integration. This one in "U.S. News", desegregation, the hidden legacy of Medicare. I only point out the news coverage from 2015 just so to the say that you didn`t have to be there yourself in 1964, 1965, 1966 to see this happen firsthand. This isn`t a secret history nobody knows about.\par \par \uc2 This is what we did as a country in our modern history. But you`d never know it from the born-yesterday commentary in response to President Biden`s actions today. Today, President Biden announced a muscular new approach by the federal government to contend with the delta variant of the coronavirus, to try to finally wrench this pandemic to the ground and defeat it.\par \par \uc2 The immediate chorus from Republican governors and members of Congress and right wing commentators all over the country was, he can`t do that. He can`t tell private entities what to do. Really? Why does your car have a seat belt? Maybe the company that made your car, that private entity, didn`t want to put in seat belts. But nevertheless, your car has a seat belt. Why is that?\par \par \uc2 Why is the ground beef you bought for dinner tonight government inspected? Meat producers do, in the private sector, have every incentive to only produce meat that people like and doesn`t make anybody sick, right? We don`t need the government sticking its nose in and telling you that`s actually cow you`re eating and not road kill. Why is there government inspection of that privately produced product?\par \par \uc2 This is a link and pin coupling system. This used to be the industry standard for how you linked railway cars together.\par \par \uc2 [21:10:02]\par \par \uc2 And for whatever reason, this is what railroad companies liked. This is what they built for their cars. Even though brakemen not infrequently got their fingers ripped and their hands ripped off while they were trying to operate the stupid system to tying rail cars together. The companies liked it. This is what they used. This is what they had been using for decades. Why change? It would be expensive to have to do it another way.\par \par \uc2 Until 1893 when the federal government banned those types of couplings and forced railroad companies to use something that wouldn`t rip the hands off their employees of that was 1893, the Safety Appliance Act.\par \par \uc2 Today, President Biden announced that the Occupational Safety and Health Association, OSHA, part of the Labor Department, will issue new rules for employers in the United States that have 100 or more employees. The rule will say employees have to be vaccinate against COVID-19 or if they`re not they have to face mandatory COVID testing every week on the job.\par \par \uc2 And when President Biden announced that today, it was a plain chant one- note chorus from Republican politicians from cost to coast. President Biden can`t do that. Only a dictator could tell companies to do something like that.\par \par \uc2 Behold, President Richard Nixon. In December 1975, signing into law the creation of the agency that was built and established in this country to do exactly that, to do exactly what President Biden is employing it to do as of tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RICHARD NIXON, FORMER PRESIDENT: We see a bill that represents in its culmination the American system at its best -- Democrats, Republicans, the house, the senate, the whiteouts house, business, labor, all cooperating in a common goal -- the saving of lives, the avoiding of injuries, making the places of work for 55 million Americans safer and more pleasant places. This is certainly a great goal.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: President Biden today is in fact putting the muscle of the federal government and his authority as president behind trying to make our national offense against COVID more intense, more thorough, more rational, less porous, less helter-skelter and regionally divided.\par \par \uc2 Yes, he`s using the huge leverage of Medicare and Medicaid funding to tell health-care providers, if you get Medicare and Medicaid funding as your hospital, your facility, your staff must be vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 The chorus on the right was instant -- oh, that`s controversial! The commentary on the press on this -- that`s a very controversial thing to do. Some staff won`t want to be vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 Yes, that`s true. You think that`s less of a reach than LBJ using Medicare to forcibly desegregate 2,000 whites-only hospitals in the south overnight? Do you think this is an unprecedented thing President Biden is doing?\par \par \uc2 That hospitals and health facilities order, that staff have to be vaccinated, that builds on what he already ordered several months ago in terms of nursing home staff. Nursing homes that get federal funding and, they basically all do, the condition is nursing home staff have to be vaccinated. This is an extension of that to apply to hospitals and health facilities.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is also using his authority over the federal work force to require vaccinations among federal workers and among federal contractors.\par \par \uc2 But, but, but, he can`t tell federal contractors how to do business. Yes, he can. Yes, he can.\par \par \uc2 The same way he`s requiring federal contractors to pay a $15 an hour minimum wage. This is not a new dictatorial power he`s assuming. This is how the government works. Wake up.\par \par \uc2 And yes, he`s using OSHA to require large private employers to vaccinate employees or test them. Educational institutions that he has direct authority over, like schools operated by the \uc2 Defense Department\uc2 , Head Start programs, early Head Start programs, he`s using his authority to ensure teachers and staff at those programs will have to be vaccinated, too. That said, for the vast majority of schools, the president doesn`t have to authority to directly order something like that. In those cases he`s recognizing the limits of his authorities and instead calling on states to put similar rules in place -- get school staff vaccinated to protect kids.\par \par \uc2 He`s calling on states to put those rules in place everywhere, even as he`s calling out the red state governors and other elect officials who are trying to build their political futures on blocking the COVID response at all costs.\par \par \uc2 [21:15:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Despite America having unprecedented and successful vaccination program, despite the fact that for almost five months, free vaccines have been available in 80,000 different locations, we still have nearly 80 million Americans who have failed to get the shot, and to make matters worse, there are elected officials actively working to undermine the fight against COVID-19. Instead of encouraging people to get vaccinated and mask up, they`re ordering mobile morgues for the unvaccinated dying from COVID in their communities. This is totally unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 The vast majority of Americans are doing the right thing. Nearly three- quarters of the eligible have gotten at least one shot. But one quarter has not gotten any. That`s nearly 80 million Americans not vaccinated, in a country as large as ours, that`s 25 percent minority. That 25 percent can cause a lot of damage, and they are.\par \par \uc2 The unvaccinated overcrowd our hospitals, overrunning emergency rooms and intensive care units, leaving no room for someone with a heart attack or cancer.\par \par \uc2 My message to unvaccinated Americans is this -- what more is there to wait for? What more do you need to see? We`ve made vaccinations free, safe, and convenient. The vaccine has \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval.\par \par \uc2 Over 200 million Americans have gotten at least one shot. We have been patient, but our patience is wearing thin. And your refusal has cost all of us, so please do the right thing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: We have been patient, but our patience is wearing thin. Your refusal has cost all of us.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere in his remarks today, the president said, what makes this incredibly frustrating is we have the tools to combat COVID-19, but a distinct minority of Americans supported by a distinct minority of officials are keeping us from turning the corner. These pandemic politics are making people sick, causing unvaccinated people to die. We can`t allow these actions to stand in the way of the large majority of Americans who have done their part and want to get back to life at normal.\par \par \uc2 President Biden today basically hitting the reset button. A new no-holds- barred approach to get our country to the vaccination rates we need to stop overfilling our hospitals, to stop 1,500 Americans dying each and every day, to get transmission down to the point where we can some day see the end of this thing and we can stop deploying active duty military teams to rural hospitals around the country to bolster their staff that is falling by the wayside in exhaustion and despair.\par \par \uc2 And the reaction on the right today and tonight and in much of the beltway press is, oh, presidents can`t do stuff like this -- that is a born yesterday canard, an ahistorical, deliberate, head in the sand canard about which President Johnson, President Nixon, President Roosevelt, who was it who signed the Lincoln railway coupling ban? President Harrison -- would like to have a word with anybody screaming today that president Biden doesn`t lead a federal government with these type of authorities. These are absolutely the authorities of the federal government. His choice is he`s going to use them to try to save lives and get us past that pandemic that is otherwise just not quitting.\par \par \uc2 The more substantive question here is not about, you know, whether this represents us becoming communists like Reagan said about Medicare, right? People are going to talk about this to whatever political advantage that he want to try to turn it to. The real substantive question is, how is this all going to work, and is this in fact a path to end the pandemic?\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Dr. David Kessler. He`s the co-chair of President Biden`s COVID advisory board. He`s former FDA commissioner. Dr. Kessler, it is an honor to have you with us tonight. Thanks you so much for being here.\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID KESSLER, CO-CHAIR, PRESIDENT BIDEN`S COVID-19 ADVISORY BOARD: It`s my pleasure, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: The president today talked explicitly about how science needs to lead. They gave the American people assurances for example, on the question of booster shots and the approval of vaccinations for kids under the age of 12, he gave the public assurances today that science will lead, that the doctors will make data base decisions and that will set government policy.\par \par \uc2 As a senior scientist advising the president, are you in favor of the approach that President Biden laid out today, and do you believe it is enough to put us in a materially different position to ending the pandemic?\par \par \uc2 KESSLER: I do, Rachel, and what the president announced today, this meets the moment. This is an unprecedented pandemic. The president has led today.\par \par \uc2 I`m a doc, pediatrician. I`ve listened to what he said. We have been patient, 75 percent of adults have rolled up their sleeves. They have done the right thing, but we have 80 million who have not.\par \par \uc2 The president is taking these steps to ensure that more people get vaccinated. It`s about one thing and one thing only -- saving lives.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: The president had previously announced other approaches to vaccination using his leverage over the federal work force. He talked about encouraging vaccination among the federal work force by requiring vaccination or allowing people to test out of it.\par \par \uc2 Now he`s saying federal workers and indeed employee of federal contractors will have to be vaccinated. Was anything learned from the previous order in terms of how to implement that sort of thing well?\par \par \uc2 I mean, it`s a vast federal work force, but it does seem like it was on again off again from agency to agency and sub work force to sub work force in whether or not the previous role was effective.\par \par \uc2 KESSLER: We now know that mandates work. We have seen the impact they have across businesses, universities, and hospitals. Every institution that has implemented mandates have seen their vaccination rates increase. They work. That`s why the president did what he did today.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Is that also true in terms of nursing home staff? I mentioned that the president had previously used his leverage over federal funding for nursing home facilities to require nursing homes, essentially to get all of their staff vaccinated. As we`ve talked about before, unvaccinated staff ended up being the lynch pin to explaining so many of the deadly and tragic outbreaks in congregate care and elderly care facilities like nursing homes.\par \par \uc2 Did the previous mandate he announced about nursing homes also create a meaningful uptick in the amount of staffing for those facilities, that is vaccinating?\par \par \uc2 KESSLER: You certainly see increases over time. Nursing homes, you saw previous steps with veterans administration. You saw it with federal workforce. In some ways this is just an arc.\par \par \uc2 No doubt that we are upping the ante, but the stakes are very high.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: The other thing that the president talked about today, which is something I feel like has fallen out of the national discussion is testing. He said that he will use the Defense Production Act to increase the supply of rapid at-home tests and make them more affordable. He said places like \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , and \uc2 Kroger\uc2  will begin to supply them at cost as soon as next week.\par \par \uc2 Can you talk from a scientific perspective about how testing fits into this muscular new approach? How Americans in our private lives, particularly if we have access to tests we can buy ourselves at an affordable rate, how we should think about testing in our lives now moving forward?\par \par \uc2 KESSLER: I can talk just personally -- I mean, as a pediatrician, as a grandfather, I get a phone call, it`s 9:00 at night. My grandchild has a fever, has some symptoms. You know, kids get runny noses, fevers always all the time, but you want to know. You want to know whether this is COVID, because there are things you can do.\par \par \uc2 And the fact is, having at-home tests -- especially -- I mean, this is a big deal getting a real discount by these companies that you can order these in-home tests. You want to have an in-home test. You run it, and you can know in 15, 30 minutes whether this is COVID or this is just a typical cold.\par \par \uc2 It`s very important. We have to give people the tools, especially when it comes to our kids. You know, we have to create environments that protect them. Most important thing to protect them is having communities that are vaccinated, families that are vaccinated. These tests can make a real difference in people`s lives, in families` lives.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Dr. Kessler, one of the other reasons that testing is so important, and you and I talked about this. I think you and I had one of the earliest televised conversations on this topic since the start of the pandemic. But if you do know that you have COVID early enough, if you can catch it before you become seriously ill enough that you need to be hospitalized, you do have a treatment option that you don`t have if you wait until you get sicker, later on in the course of the disease, and that is these monoclonal antibody treatments.\par \par \uc2 [21:25:14]\par \par \uc2 And you and I talk months ago about how effective they are, about how difficult it`s been to ensure a lot of uptake. People, again, need to take those treatments before they`re too terribly sick.\par \par \uc2 The president today talked about increasing the pace of monoclonal antibody shipments across the country, upping by 50 percent. We`ve seen demand for these treatments go way up over the summer. How do you think that part of the pandemic response is going, and how hopeful do you feel about those treatments?\par \par \uc2 KESSLER: I`m very hopeful about those treatment treatments. Those treatments work. The fact is, on July 1st, we`re shipping 10,000 of these regimens a week. We increased that dramatically to over 100,000, and we`re committed to sending out 150,000 a week.\par \par \uc2 So, a big uptake. Obviously the downside of that is delta is requiring us to use more. We want to get that curve down. But if you test positive and you are at risk for hospitalization, if you are at high risk, you should get monoclonal antibodies.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Dr. David Kessler, co-chair of President Biden`s COVID advisory board, former FDA commissioner -- sir, thank you for your time and as always for your clarity tonight. It`s an honor to have this time with you.\par \par \uc2 KESSLER: My pleasure, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: All right. We`ve got much more ahead tonight. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [21:30:48]\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: In 2010, the state of Arizona passed into law the most aggressive anti-immigrant legislation that at that point can pass anywhere in the country. You may remember it as the "show me your papers" law. One of its requirements is that it made law enforcement officials in the Arizona ask for proof of legal immigration status from anyone they suspected might be an undocumented immigrant regardless of why they expected it.\par \par \uc2 The show me your papers provision was just one piece of a sweeping radical anti immigrant bill in Arizona that. That law made it a crime to be undocumented in Arizona. A criminalize providing shelter to an undocumented immigrant.\par \par \uc2 In 2010, three weeks before that law was due to go into effect, the \uc2 U.S. Justice Department\uc2  under President \uc2 Obama\uc2  sued Arizona over that proposed law, they argue Arizona was trying to do there conflicted with existing federal law around immigration, they contended that Arizona did not have the right to effectively undo federal law with its own actions at the state level. That was the legal approach they took in tackling that Arizona bill.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, for the most part anyway, the Supreme Court agreed. In 2012, the Supreme Court struck down most of that radical immigration law in Arizona and they struck it down on the grounds that they undermines existing federal law.\par \par \uc2 Well, now, today, that would appear to be the relevant legal history we all need to remember to understands the huge news, that the Biden Justice Department announced this afternoon, because they are apparently going to run that same play again. Today, the Justice Department under Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , they sued Texas over the Texas`s new law that essentially bans abortion, in their lawsuit, DOJ accuses Texas of passing its abortion ban, quote, in open defiance of the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 The Constitution, under current president protects a woman`s right to get it abortion no matter what state she lives in. Their argument here is a direct echo of the same argument the DOJ used in 2010, to great effect that Arizona case, that argument, that legal protections, trying to federally force all by the U.S. Constitution, under state law that tries to get around those protections.\par \par \uc2 Because the Texas abortion ban ignores those federal constitutional protections, \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  and the Biden Justice Department now say the court should strike down the Texas banned altogether. That is the type of argument they`ve decided to take. That argument did succeed once in the Supreme Court. In blocking parts of that nightmarish Arizona immigration law.\par \par \uc2 Could it work here to? Is this the right approach to take to try to on ban abortion in the state of Texas?\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is our friend Barbara McQuade. She`s a former U.S. attorney. She first tipped us off to this parallel history, and we`ve been researching it and looking into every since.\par \par \uc2 Barb, thank you for being here. Thanks for your help thinking about this, this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA MCQUADE, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Oh, you bet. My pleasure.\par \par \uc2 I saw this case it was really excited to see. It because regardless of the way this case comes out on the merits, this at least is a victory for the rule of law, because now the Justice Department can get this statute before a federal judge.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: So, that`s the important thing. And I think not a lot of lawyers don`t appreciate here, is that part of the way that Texas was able to sneak its abortion ban into law, despite Roe v. Wade, supposedly protecting any state from banning abortion, is they created a scheme. In their law, that effectively prevented any sort of obvious approach to a lawsuit. Who would be sued? Who would have standing to sue them? On what grounds should the federal courts take that on? That was the way that the Supreme Court justice got the law to stand. This takes on that problem directly.\par \par \uc2 MCQUADE: Yes. I think over the past several days, that you`ve seen probably many legal analysts, including me sort of scratching our head saying, man how did they get this case before the court? In fact, the legal director, for Right to Life of Texas said, that this law was written deliberately to stymie judicial review, and so really the only one actor that can do anything about this. The one entity that has the power to sue a state is the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  says hold my beer and he`s filed this lawsuit today. will -- many people have been frustrated with some of his inaction overtime. But this is a big action, and this will -- as we said, at least tee up this case before a court.\par \par \uc2 With this makeup of the court might do to that long-standing 