ave been out for the committee? I know today was the deadline to receive a lot of information from government and companies, records related to that attack.\par \par \uc2 LOFGREN: Well, I talked to the staff a few hours ago. And quite a bit of information has come in. And it is still coming in. The deadline extends to, I think, midnight tonight. So I can`t really tell you what has come in except that there is a great amount of material.\par \par \uc2 And we will be learning more tomorrow about what`s in it. And the staff may have an announcement, later this evening. I don`t know for sure. But it is clear that agencies responded.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: The House Republican leader, Kevin McCarthy, had a striking response to the request to top telecom companies in particular, asking them to retain records, including records related to members of Congress who participated in the rally that participated preceded the attack on the Capitol. He basically threatened the companies, saying that if they complied with these requests, they would lose their ability to operate in the United States. And when Republicans were in the majority they would not forget companies that comply with this request for information.\par \par \uc2 I have to ask what you make of that. I think anybody else telling companies not to respond to congressional inquiries would be considered attempting to tamper with that investigation. I don`t know if that designation applies to the Republican leader here. But I want to get your reaction to that.\par \par \uc2 LOFGREN: I was astonished, frankly, by his statements. Number one, there is no basis in law for it. Number two, as you point out, eighties a felony to obstruct a congressional investigation, 1505 of the 18 USC. Now, whether he has a defense or not, I don`t know. Sometimes there is a speech or debate clause by the doesn`t protect against criminal activity.\par \par \uc2 The real point is, what is he hiding? What is he trying to keep the proof from coming out? I think the leaders of our country, and Kevin is one of them, should be stepping forward and trying to get to the bottom of everything that led up to January 6th. People who are not doing that are a mystery to me, unless they have participated.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know. I can`t imagine what his reason for that is, if there is a benign reason for these statements.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: California Congresswoman \uc2 Zoe Lofgren\uc2 , chair of the House Administration Committee, member of the January 6th Select Committee -- Congresswoman Lofgren, it`s nice to have you here with us, thanks for helping us understand.\par \par \uc2 LOFGREN: Thanks, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: All right. We`ll be right back. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [21:54:00]\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: I`m not sure if we should have seen this coming, I certainly didn`t see this coming. But look, this plane, you are going to see on your screen right here, this is a \uc2 Qatar Airways\uc2  Boeing 777. That is the first passenger flight to depart from the main airport in Afghanistan, the Kabul airport, since U.S. troops withdrew from Afghanistan last month.\par \par \uc2 The scene at Kabul airport was markedly different from what we saw just weeks ago. Instead of U.S. Marines, we had \uc2 Taliban\uc2  fighters patrolling the runways, the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  white flags on the flagpoles. Despite the fact that U.S. forces are in control there, and the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  clearly are, today, 113 people, including Americans, Canadians, Brits, Germans, Ukrainians all left on that \uc2 Qatar Airways\uc2  Boeing 777. The flight flew out of Kabul and landed in Doha in Qatar.\par \par \uc2 More flights out, the fight to get out again have started.\par \par \uc2 [21:55:01]\par \par \uc2 Maybe not just this one, maybe more to come.\par \par \uc2 In a statement today, Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  said today that today`s flight was the result, quote, of the department