ut we`ll use bounty hunters to enforce, it would have taken a pair of seconds for the Supreme Court to overturn that.\par \par \uc2 So, I have to honestly tell you, I think it`s completely sneaky and disingenuous that they`ve looked the other way, I have absolutely no tolerance for it, and my guess would be -- my legal opinion and guess is that when the Mississippi case comes up this term, they will overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Cynthia Alksne, Julia Ainsley, thanks to both of you. For Julia, your reporting, and Cynthia your perspective. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, we`re taking you live to New Jersey with President Biden looking at the aftermath of Hurricane Ida, dozens of towns in the northeast and parts of New York City still dealing with the devastation.\par \par \uc2 Plus, later concerns of another wave of COVID infections and deaths after a busy Labor Day weekend, one county out west now declaring COVID misinformation a public health crisis. Our one-on-one on that later in the show. You are watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Welcome back. We want to take you out to Louisiana where more than 415,000 households still do not have power, even nine days, nine since Hurricane Ida made landfall there.\par \par \uc2 People don`t have electricity, people don`t have clean water to drink, and now there`s another big concern. People don`t have classes that are open to send their kids back to. In places like Jefferson Parish, you`re going to see schools stay close this week. NBC`s Josh Lederman is in Harvey, Louisiana with more on this. Hey Josh.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:08]\par \par \uc2 JOSH LEDERMAN, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Hallie. On a normal day, this would be the lunch hour here at Helen Cox High School but instead, the students are gone -- it`s still busy here but instead, the folks who are here are lining up for basic supplies. They`re here looking for food, for water, for tarps as the