k doing what they could to maintain air conditioning. Another one said you know it`s great, they`re giving us these meals ready to eat but if you try to heat them up, we have an all-electric heating and gas in our -- and stoves in our house, I have no way to cook that. So, this guy was trying to heat up his meals ready to eat given out by eight groups by putting them on the hood of his car, Hallie.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: It`s tough to hear. Josh Lederman, I know that crews are taking a pause for that storm, you should take a pause as well as make sure you and your crew are safe. Thank you very much. We wanted to make sure to get to talk to Josh before that storm rolled through.\par \par \uc2 But what`s happening now in the northeast, President Biden as we speak, getting briefed on the aftermath of that same storm, as it made its way through New Jersey and New York. He`s hearing from officials in Hillsborough Township, New Jersey. Here`s what he said just a couple of minutes ago, watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The losses that we witness today are profound. Dozens of lost lives, homes destroyed. My thoughts are with all those families affected by the storms and all those families who lose someone they love.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: In less than an hour from now, the President is scheduled to look at the devastating storm damage in other parts of New Jersey and in Queens, New York.\par \par \uc2 Ida killed at least 45 people in those two states alone. You have thousands of people working to try to clean it up.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DANETTE RIVERA, RESIDENT, QUEENS, NEW YORK: This is the most fearful thing that have ever happened to me in my life. I never went through an experience like this before where I was actually so scared for my own life.\par \par \uc2 KAREN PAYEUR, RESIDENT, NEW JERSEY: We were watching people getting picked off their roofs on 