e not just living in two worlds, right? We were talking about the pandemic of the unvaccinated, and I think that created just a lot more confusion to be honest in retrospect. So I think the way to talk about this trajectory is that, number one, this pandemic is global and it`s not over. And number two, this is something that is actually different than last year, Hallie, within our grasp.\par \par \uc2 We can see that rapid declining cases and we can continue to actually have hopefully a more normal prospectus on the fall. I know we`re going to talk about that because of our ability to get vaccinated. The amazing thing that`s different is the effectiveness of the vaccine. There is just no question. There might be breakthrough infections, yes, and you`re right.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 PATEL: The "New York Times" talking about how kind of overestimating that effect is true. But at the same time, take a step back. Our trajectory can absolutely be altered unlike last year because of our ability to vaccinate.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: You talk about the effectiveness of the vaccine, Dr. Patel. There`s now this question of this Mu variant, right? And is it -- what`s the term the professionals use? Evasive. Can it evade the vaccine basically? Is it resistant potentially to the vaccine?\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Right.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: We don`t know much. When will we know more about that? And when you talk to people, how do you frame the level of concern that you have?\par \par \uc2 [13:35:03]\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Yes. So I think about everything in levels of concern that, one, I`m always concerned when I see variants where we have this kind of potential for evading immunity. But that just means that we need to keep an eye on it, we don`t want to get blindsided by it. But then, number two, kind of the way I talk about it with family, friends, and patients, is that the majority of what we`re seeing right now is Delta, and our vaccines work against Delta.\par \par \uc2 So we have to get data about Mu, that`s exactly right and on point, Hallie. And we also need to know that we`re surveilling the country to watch for Mu which we are doing. So we`re now taking about 10 percent of those samples of COVID tests and doing genetic analysis on it. That`s good news because we can catch things way before Alpha and some of the other variants where we got a little bit blindsided by it.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PATEL: But I do think it`s critical to remind people we`re in the thick of it with Delta, right now, and that Mu actually is getting dwarfed by Delta. So the good news is that globally cases of Mu are decreasing. And remember, that means that where else are we seeing cases? It`s with the Delta variant. But we do have three effective vaccines against it right now.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: You`re right. That is the predominant variant, that is what we`re seeing the most, and our vaccines are resistant to that. Can you talk about booster shots for a sec, Dr. P? Because we are now like two weeks-ish, right? We don`t know specifically when those are going to be made available, probably just for \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  recipients at first.\par \par \uc2 Do you think doctors are getting what they need for this to happen? Because, you know, a year ago, or I guess it was the spring, I should say, it feels like a year, mass vaccination sites were opening up, convention centers, football stadiums, right, to get people what they needed. How does that look later this month when it comes to boosters?\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Yes, Hallie, I smiled not because it`s funny, but because you`re asking the questions that all of us are also asking internally as health professionals. When are we going to get more information about boosters? Who, what, when and where? We`re already hearing about millions who are finding their way to getting boosters, whether they`re supposed to or not. People who can walk in to a pharmacy today and get one with no questions and then some who are being challenged and asked about their medication lists.\par \par \uc2 What this means in pharmacies and clinics across the country is that September 20th, our priority is going to be to make sure that we have enough vaccines for anyone who`s unvaccinated and that we give boosters as recommended once we get guidance from the FDA and CDC. I expect, like you said, \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  will be the first with guidance. That means, Hallie, millions of people who received Moderna, myself included, are not going to be able to get boosters September 20th.\par \par \uc2 We need better understanding of how to talk to patients about this. And I think, number two, I just want people to hear this. Just because you`re eight months out from your second dose, doesn`t mean that at eight months and 14 days you are not protected.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: It`s a good point.\par \par \uc2 PATEL: We likely have protection for anywhere from eight to probably, Hallie, about 12 months past that second dose. We just want to make sure high-risk people, long-term care workers, nursing home residents, health care workers, people who have immune conditions can get boosters when they need it. I think that`s critical. So I`m not scared but I do think it would be nice if we could get a little bit more guidance.\par \par \uc2 What we`re telling patients, though, is that you`re protected. For now, you are protected until evidence tells us otherwise. And I have seen no evidence that tells us that our current vaccines are not protecting us.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Dr. Patel, always great to talk with you. Thank you very much for that good information. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next on MEET THE PRESS DAILY, today is the last day to request a mail ballot for next week`s gubernatorial recall in California which will decide Governor Gavin Newsom`s political future. It comes as we`re learning new information about when Joe Biden might go out to stump for him. Where does the race stand? We`ve got Steve Kornacki at the big board, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:41:47]\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Welcome back. We are live here and not too far from Capitol Hill, just a couple of blocks away where you know lawmakers are looking at some big deadlines coming up. Over the course of the next few weeks as the House and Senate get back into session, Democrats plan on voting on that $550 billion infrastructure bill and a $3.5 trillion budget plan, plus, the debt limit will have to be extended to prevent a global economic collapse. Oh, and the government is set to shut down at the end of the month unless Congress can agree on funding.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Leigh Ann Caldwell is on the hill following the latest on this for us.\par \par \uc2 Leigh Ann, I hope you had a nice and relaxing August because your September is going to be bananas. Democrats have a lot to get done. Are lawmakers -- are they worried at all about these deadlines coming up?\par \par \uc2 LEIGH ANN CALDWELL, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Hallie, it sure is going to be a busy September and perhaps busy October as well. It`s probably the understatement of the year. And those are just the things that crises that we know about. This doesn`t include the things that are unpredictable. But, yes, there is a lot to be done, and Democratic leaders are very well aware of the challenges they have.\par \par \uc2 As far as the human infrastructure bill and the bipartisan infrastructure bill, this is going to be very difficult for Speaker Pelosi and Democratic Leader Schumer to keep their party together, to pass this within the next three weeks. The Senate doesn`t even come back into session until Monday. The House does not come back into session until September 20th. And that`s why you hear some moderates, including Senator Joe Manchin who said just last week that Democrats should take a pause, especially on this major $3 trillion reconciliation human infrastructure bill.\par \par \uc2 So -- and Hallie, I also just want to say that these are also things that are part of the president`s priorities, when you come to government spending, then the government shuts down at the end of the month if they don`t by chance or if they`re not able to fund the government, those are the things that Congress has to do. So this fall is mixed with things that the president and Democrats want to do and things that Congress has to do in order for the country to function, Hallie.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Leigh Ann Caldwell live for us on the Hill. Leigh Ann, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Just down the street, down at Pennsylvania Avenue, President Biden next week will depart from there to California at some point to campaign with Governor Gavin Newsom ahead of next week`s recall election. The press secretary saying that trip is going to happen. She didn`t say specifically when but it will be early next week. And it comes as Democrats try to line up some star power for this final stretch before that recall. And their closing message is all about COVID.\par \par \uc2 New ads making a final push for Governor Gavin Newsom as a pro-science, pro-vaccine candidate. It`s a notable approach, considering his handling of the pandemic is a big reason why this recall is even happening in the first place.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Steve Kornacki is at the big board.\par \par \uc2 Hey, pal, great to see you. Give us the numbers. Right? Where does the race stand at the moment?\par \par \uc2 STEVE KORNACKI, NBC NEWS NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Hallie. I think it`s changed potentially a little bit in the last few days. Let me take you through it. Of course the backdrop here in California, this was when Newsom got elected. This was just back in 2018, it was a landslide for him, 24 points over his Republican opponent.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:06]\par \par \uc2 It`s almost always a landslide for Democrats in California, a state that Joe Biden carried by 29 points over 