ou, Steve Kornacki, thank you, my friend. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: You got it.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: And I`ll be seeing you in the days to come.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Coming up here, a major move by one county out West -- speaking of the West Coast -- as COVID cases among unvaccinated people keep going up, along with the spread of misinformation online.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Welcome back. As states all across the country gear up for the possibility of COVID surges after Labor Day, health officials are trying to find some new ways to battle pandemic misinformation with millions of Americans still not vaccinated. So in California, here`s what they`re doing. San Diego County declared COVID misinformation a public health crisis this past week, a move that prompted praise from the U.S. surgeon general.\par \par \uc2 And joining me now is San Diego County supervisor Nathan Fletcher who authored this resolution.\par \par \uc2 Nathan, good morning. Good afternoon, forgive me, and thank you for being on the show.\par \par \uc2 NATHAN FLETCHER, SAN DIEGO COUNTY SUPERVISORS CHAIRMAN: Thank you, Hallie. Thank you for having me on.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: And I suppose it`s morning where you are. So I can get away with that.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: There you go. A couple of key questions for you here. I want to get to what you specifically and concretely hope to see from this declaration. But I have to get to the backlash of it because the county meeting at which this happened lasted 15 hours the day the resolution passed. That is a very long time for a county meeting. Let me play a little bit for our viewers to see what it was like that day. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Last I checked you aren`t my health care provider. The Constitution, the right to privacy guarantees us this. That`s why this is America and you guys can`t make your camps for the unvaccinated.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: All you do is spread misinformation, then you want to call us terrorists and say we`re a fricking public health threat.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You`re not working for the people, so you must be working against us for some foreign interest.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Basically, this is Chinese censorship, propaganda, forced vaccination. You guys just call it what it is.\par \par \uc2 (END OF VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: How do you respond to those folks? I know that there are no penalties, right, in place with this, in part so you can protect free speech. But what do you say to folks who are saying to you, you know, you`re a tool of China, you`re the one who`s spreading misinformation?\par \par \uc2 FLETCHER: You know, Hallie, we sat there 15 hours and listened to so many people who demonstrated the need for us to take this action. We heard that. We heard folks say I`m a doctor and I did my own research. And you`re not a medical doctor, you`re a chiropractor. And the research you`re touting has been debunked many times over. But the reality is this anti-vax, anti- science segment that so often was relegated to the fringes of our society has grown.\par \par \uc2 It`s now a plank in today`s Republican Party. And we will not defeat COVID- 19 if we don`t beat back misinformation. And it`s not enough anymore, Hallie, to just promote the science. We`ve been doing that for 18 months. We have to aggressively and intentionally tackle the misinformation that is taking hold and it`s costing people their lives.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: So how do you do that, right? Because with all due respect, you obviously don`t run \uc2 Facebook\uc2 . Right? Like you can`t get out there and take stuff down off social media.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:02]\par \par \uc2 So how do you hope to do it? And what do you want to see out of it?\par \par \uc2 FLETCHER: Well, the surgeon general -- look, this is a national problem.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 FLETCHER: And I want to commend the surgeon general. It was their report on misinformation that got us moving, and it was their recommendations that we put into our action here. But a national problem is going to require local action. In San Diego, we`ve seen tremendous success, more than 75 percent of our eligible folks are fully vaccinated. And the way we got there was intentionally engaging the communities, we addressed concerns.\par \par \uc2 Now we have to shift that focus. The number one barrier to people getting vaccinated today, they know it`s available, they know it`s free, it`s they have bought into this. And so we are engaging with health care experts. We are directly confronting. You may have heard this. This is why it`s not true. So much of our success in using trusted messengers is being replicated here.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to (INAUDIBLE) board meetings, where after 15 hours of misinformation we will go to a panel of doctors that will point by point refute the things that were said that are factually inaccurate. And we`re going to go out and do everything we can to save every life possible.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: You talked about trust in messengers. I know that`s so important when it comes to combating misinformation because people who feel attacked shutdown and turn away. Right? Like that is not the way to get people who are taking in this misinformation not getting vaccinated to change their minds.\par \par \uc2 I wonder what percentage of the people in your county who currently right now, let`s say, are not vaccinated? Right? Do you think are -- to use a political work, quote-unquote, "persuadable"? In other words, how many of those people, what percentage of those people do you think you can reach with this new effort on misinformation?\par \par \uc2 FLETCHER: Well, I think in many ways, Hallie, the best messengers are going to be those that came out of tragedy. We saw a story this weekend 