other Republicans are trying to hide something but what is the strategy? Is this a PR strategy, for instance, ahead of the midterms, because they know, the timing of the release of the findings of this investigation could very well come right before the midterms. And they certainly want to look like they`ve been selling this thing from the get go to their supporters?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN DEL PERCIO, REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST: Well, I think McCarthy has two things he has to do. One, he has to please Trump because he wants to raise money, and he needs Donald Trump support to potentially take back the House in the 2022 midterms.\par \par \uc2 But most of all, I see this all as a delay tactic, trying to delay things as much as possible. It`s exactly what Donald Trump did when he was president and in the private sector. This is to delay, delay, deny, deny, and hope that just keep going forward with that narrow, keep throwing things out there throw out big tech, because why not? I mean, they can get the name of -- the head of one of the company.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: I know. That`s not great.\par \par \uc2 DEL PERCIO: Whatever strategy they may be trying to pursue, I don`t have a whole lot of faith in that as far as their competency. But I think that`s all they can do. It`s just trying to talk and talk. And most of all trying to please Donald Trump. I don`t think it will be successful. I think McCarthy is driven out of pure fear. I think he knows that there`s other things besides what murse (ph) may have done, there may be lines connecting people to following the money to following people back into certain districts that could be very scary for him, especially as he`s looking at raising a lot of money nationally. So there`s a lot at stake here and he is afraid.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: I just want to mention \uc2 Marissa Mayer\uc2 , ex-CEO of Yahoo, who actually got one of these letters. She didn`t step down yesterday, by the way. She stepped down in 2017. That was quite some time ago, way before all of our lives changed dramatically, especially with the pandemic and the election and everything else that has happened since then.\par \par \uc2 [23:10:00]\par \par \uc2 Neal Katyal, there is this idea cited in the Hill about the fact that the President could feasibly claim executive privilege when calling for some of these documents, is that a risk? The former president actually say, by the way.\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: I mean, he can try and he`ll wail about executive privilege. He already has all he wants. But the fact is, he`s lost the presidency. And the final decision under the law about who can invoke executive privilege is the current incumbent president. And seeing as that President has allowed former Trump DOJ officials already to testify in this investigation. If I were Trump, I wouldn`t be holding my breath.\par \par \uc2 But again, I think that what was said is right, these are delay tactics. In some ways, it`s worth noting, they`ve already worked. I mean, today is September 6, that is eight months to the day, from when this attack on our nation happened. And we still don`t have answers to basic questions because of Republican obstruction, they first wanted to block a committee altogether. And now they`re invoking all these, you know, fake privileges to try and hide the truth from the American people. It`ll eventually not work. But I think it`s a real shame that here we are eight months later, we don`t know the answers to a lot of basic questions.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: So talk about January six. Let`s talk about September 18, right. There is this rally planned on Saturday, September 18. I`m not going to be covering the rally live for folks if in fact, it happens. And it seems as if it`s supposed to be a celebration of the insurrectionists plan by a former Trump campaign operative. I want to play a portion of what Michael Brainard has said about this September 18 rally.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MATT BRAYNARD, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, LOOK AHEAD AMERICA: We`d have our next rally planned is in the middle of September, September 18. And it`s going to be huge. And what`s going to define it is where it`s going to take place. We`re going back to the Capitol, right where it started. We`re going to have members of Congress speak. We`re going to continue to raise the volume push back against this phony narrative and demand justice for these political prisoners.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Juanita, what do you make of this?\par \par \uc2 TOLLIVER: It really fully aligns with the lies that we`ve heard from members of Congress trying to retell the story even though we have video footage of the way that these violent attackers because that`s what they were, violent attackers scaled the walls of the Capitol building, and threaten the lives of every person in that building, and was well as in the surrounding community.\par \par \uc2 And what I make of all of this is a degree of frustration. Because while this organizer sees this as celebrating and marking the date, and marking the location of the Capitol, what`s frustrating to me is the trauma that was endured by everyone who was in that building and in the surrounding communities, because let`s be real, people forget that DC is comprised of regular human beings trying to go about their day to day lives.\par \par \uc2 And so I`m concerned about the trauma that individuals around the Capitol and in the capital will feel and even recalling what happened on January 6, but I am glad to hear that law enforcement officials and federal agencies are taking notes and taking this as a serious potential threat and taking preparations as needed.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Susan, it doesn`t seem like these people are necessarily concerned with recognizing the trauma that folks felt on January 6, when those insurrectionists stormed the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 DEL PERCIO: No, and obviously, this is about grandstanding. But I do have one other concern outside of the Capitol. You know, almost a year ago, militia were arrested for trying to kidnap and kill Governor Whitmer of Michigan. These militia groups have been growing, as we saw from last October into the insurrection.\par \par \uc2 But now there`s also a national network of these or just within the state. So I do hope that you know, capitals are being fortified, and that there`s a lot of attention being paid to the groups in individual states, in addition to the capital, because I do see that as a very big potential dangerous situation for many years.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Neal, can we talk about that for one moment? Because with all that has been going on in our government, right, in our country, it seems as if, especially the administration has been inundated, right, with so much to do when it comes to infrastructure, obviously, when it comes to the reconciliation bill, to the pandemic, to Afghanistan, and so on and so forth.\par \par \uc2 But there is this kind of pervasive problem that we haven`t necessarily talked about since January 6, or has kind of put on the backburner it. It`s what Susan just talked about these militia groups that are that are huge throughout the country, and that are growing. Is this government doing enough?\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: Well, I do think the White House is doing a lot with respect to the militia stuff. They appointed Josh Geltzer at the National Security Council to run this. And they`ve had that, you know, they have extensive work being done in this area. So I`m actually pretty happy with the White House and how they`ve reacted.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 KAYTAL: I`m a little worried about Congress and the security there. I was just talking to just a college intern yesterday, and I asked him, you know, what was the most surprising thing about your internship over the summer, and I thought he`d answer like Ben Sasse or something like that. He said, a lack of security --\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: -- at the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 [23:15:00]\par \par \uc2 He said he can just walk around there go anywhere, they barely check his ID. I mean, this is eight months after January 6, though, I`m very worried about that. And what`s going on in terms of security at both the U.S. Capitol and then as we said before, also state capitol building.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes, I`ve heard that as well. Neal Katyal, Juanita Tolliver, thank you guys both. Susan Del Percio, I`m going to see you again at the top of the next hour so thanks for sticking with us.\par \par \uc2 Coming up. Everybody knew backlash over GOP governors fighting safety measures for students as the Delta variant surges across the south.\par \par \uc2 Plus the Biden Justice Department stepping up to protect women`s rights after that anti-choice law in Texas we`re going to explain.\par \par \uc2 And then later on a big update on the criminal probe into the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Welcome back, everybody. Let`s talk about the pandemic. The U.S. surpassing 40 million total COVID cases this weekend as millions of kids are heading back to school. A lot of students in the south they`ve already been in classrooms for weeks now, in Texas, for instance, where Republican Governor Greg Abbott just lost his legal fight to ban mask mandates in schools.\par \par \uc2 [23:20:10]\par \par \uc2 More than 40,000 students are being affected by school closures in at least 45 districts. Philadelphia`s La Salle University just cancelled all in- person classes this week and made a startling rise in cases despite having nearly 90 percent of the campus fully vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 And then you go to Florida, 15 Miami-Dade County School staffers have died from COVID in just 10 days.\par \par \uc2 And then nationally, a pillar of the COVID safety net ending this weekend expanded unemployment insurance expiring, leaving millions in limbo, heading to the fall with so much still unsure.\par \par \uc2 And to the head of the \uc2 American Teachers Federation\uc2  lashing out now, at some governors.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RANDI WEINGARTEN\uc2 , AMERICAN FEDERATION OF TEACHERS PRESIDENT: We got to get these governors to stop their madness. Stop their irresponsibility. Stop there, moreover, hence, you know, they are morally reprehensible for not allowing for making us fat these fights about mask mandates.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: All right, joining me now is Mark Gay with the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  editorial board and Dr. Natalie Azar, an MSNBC medical contributor.\par \par \uc2 Mara, I`m going to have you weigh in first on what we just heard from \uc2 Randi Weingarten\uc2  essentially saying, and she`s talking about right, Texas Governor Greg Abbott, Florida Governor Ron DeSantis, politicizing the mask mandate. I mean, they are getting so many cases in those states, so many children hospitalized now schools closed, do you think this could come back to bite them politically, especially as we get further, further into the winter season?\par \par \uc2 MARA GAY, THE \uc2 NEW YORK TIMES\uc2  EDITORIAL BOARD: I do. I think unfortunately, as people continue to get sick, to suffer, to see their loved ones die, I do believe that you will eventually see a sea change, even in some more conservative areas about the vaccine or at least about mask mandates and other safety precautions.\par \par \uc2 But as per usual, unfortunately, these Republican governors are playing the short-term game and thinking of their own short-term political self- interest. They also don`t want to become the target, right, of right-wing campaigns against them. And I think that is a factor that should also be considered. Now that`s not to give them credit, they should actually just do their job. But that is the environment that we`re living in.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Dr. Azar, let`s talk about the science of all this, right. I`m about to send my kiddos back to school, September 13. That`s when New York state public schools are back in session. I`m worried. And I think a lot of parents are worried. When you look at the \uc2 American Academy of Pediatrics\uc2  saying one in five new COVID cases just in the last week of August, they were in kits. How worried should parents be right now?\par \par \uc2 DR. NATALIE AZAR, MSNBC MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I wish that I could give you reassurance about the statistics, Yasmin, but as long as we have, you know, a pandemic that`s, that`s out of control and adult, we`re going to see that filter down into our kids. You know, the hospital rates have also increased fivefold in the last couple of months in children. And there appears also to be an inverse relationship to those hospital -- hospitalization rates and ER visits with the rates of vaccination in the community. So not surprisingly, again, that, you know, the adults in the room need to do their part and become vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 You know, and to your point specifically about those kids who are too young to be vaccinated, that, of course, is the biggest concern. Hence, the \uc2 American Academy of Pediatrics\uc2  has urged what they refer to as layered mitigation. So anyone who is eligible for vaccination, including staff and teachers and everything like that, and kids over the age of 12 need to be vaccinated and all the other things need to be done, mask mandates and ventilation and frequent testing and all the stuff that we`ve talked about now, ad nauseum.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Ad nauseum, right. We felt like it was over this type of conversation and suddenly here we are, once again at the beginning of the school year, we thought we were going to catch a break for a moment.\par \par \uc2 I found with Dr. Vivek Murthy, the Surgeon General said really interesting. And I want to read it to you, Dr. Azar. He said this, it is really important that we convey that success does not equal no cases. He was telling Politico this in an interview, success looks like very few people in the hospital and very few dying.\par \par \uc2 I see this two ways and I want to kind of get your assessment, but the way I`m seeing it is A, we`re going to see more and more breakthrough cases is what I`m hearing from him here. And B, we`re going to live with COVID for a long time.\par \par \uc2 AZAR: Yes, I think both of those things are true. You know, I think we`ve all now sat back and had a moment to think about the impact of the extraordinarily effective and high efficacy numbers that we quoted for both the mRNA vaccine Very, very early on when they rolled out.\par \par \uc2 [23:25:03]\par \par \uc2 And that seemed to sort of equate with an expectation that if you were protected, you know, 95 percent against symptomatic infection that you would never get a sniffle. And I had this conversation very frequently now even with my own husband, as well as patients. When we talk about the third dose, rather than calling it a booster, we`re trying to sort of switch the nomenclature a little bit to say perhaps that we need a third dose to complete the series for COVID-19 vaccination and say, how much more protected from hospitalization and death do you need to be? if it`s greater than 90 percent right now, that`s really, really good.\par \par \uc2 And I think we all do need to get comfortable with the idea that we might experience a flu-like illness with COVID-19, but we won`t end up in the hospital.\par \par \uc2 Now, there`s a lot of nuance there, right? Because if you`re living with someone who`s immunocompromised, or an elderly parent, or grandparent that mild, mildly symptomatic infection, of course, has holds much more significance. But yet, I think it is important to start shifting the idea of how we -- what our relationship to COVID-19 is when and with very likely will become an endemic infection and a part of our lives.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: So Mara, then you have this raging Delta variant right now. There is this new variant that we`re going to talk about in just a moment, the new variant that seems like it`s coming on the horizon is now a variant of interest. I`m going to ask Dr. Azar to weigh in on that in a moment.\par \par \uc2 You`ve got the end of unemployment benefits in certain states, some of them cutting them off earlier than others. You got this news out of CNBC saying 26 states ended federal unemployment benefits. But the data is suggesting it is not getting people back to work. Right.\par \par \uc2 There was this theory that the second they cut off extended unemployment benefits folks who get back to work. Well, that`s not what happened. And I`m wondering if there is a sense that this is also just a fear factor, because we are still in the throes of this thing.\par \par \uc2 GAY: You know, I don`t believe that the persuasion campaign to get Americans who are unvaccinated, vaccinated should end. I do you think that there is a majority of Americans who are saying to themselves, I am not going back to work, or to school necessarily, unless I know that those around me are vaccinated and are taking COVID seriously. And I think in some ways, this is part of what is driving those numbers that you`re talking about.\par \par \uc2 A majority of Americans do actually fear this virus, and understand the science they respected. They`ve taken steps to protect themselves and their families, and they have a healthy fear. And I think, you know, part of what needs to shift is, we need to understand not only the vet the anxieties of those who are vaccine hesitant --\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAY: -- or resistant, but we also have a healthy respect for everybody, first of all, but also for the lives and health of everybody else, because I think people have a right to be protected from the unvaccinated. And we are not really talking about that. That is that is a serious concern for millions of Americans.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Dr. Azar last word to you. And I just kind of want you to give us really the ABCs of this new variant, what we need to know how worried we need to be this variant that could possibly evade vaccines.\par \par \uc2 AZAR: Right. So remember that when a variant becomes a variant of interest or a variant of concern, it`s displaying that it`s doing a couple of things or more one thing at all, increased transmissibility, causing more severe illness or evading the protection from a vaccine or from natural immunity.\par \par \uc2 The combination of the mutations that they`re seeing in the new variant is concerning for evasion of protection, as you mentioned, Yasmin, but a very, very important thing to remember is that we have not seen that yet in n real life. This is something that there`s just sort of studying the biology of this variant and this is what they are seeing. This is how it starts. And it`s something that Dr. Fauci has assured all of us that are watching very closely.\par \par \uc2 If we start to see significantly increased, transmissibility with this variant, then you`ve got two out of the three problems happening and that becomes much more of a concern. If it kind of burns out, then it won`t really make a big difference, but it`s certainly something that the experts are watching very closely right now.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: I feel like everybody`s watching took as big of a breath as I just did and listening to this, this variant. Sometimes you just got to go. It`s going to be OK. It`s going to be OK. Mara Gay, Dr. Azar, thank you guys both. Appreciate it. Happy Labor Day. And by the way, thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Saturday marks 20 years since the attacks on September 11. What is the future of Afghanistan look like now?\par \par \uc2 Also today on this holiday Attorney General Garland made major news on protecting women`s rights, all that and news on the Trump Org criminal case, stay with us a lot more coming up folks.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:33:54]\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Welcome back. The Texas law demolishing women`s legal rights in Texas is facing a national fight. Today Attorney General Merrick Garland are releasing a statement vowing federal law enforcement protection when an abortion clinic or reproductive health center is under attack.\par \par \uc2 The feds in Texas and across the country they`re on notice with Congress coming back. Speaker Pelosi vowing a vote on an abortion rights bill. The question is how can this move in the Senate?\par \par \uc2 And what about those GOP lawmakers supporting this Texas law as they demand no mask mandates to fight COVID? Where are they on the rights and freedoms for women? This issue just one of many hugely important pieces to President Biden`s agenda coming into focus as a summer break ends.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Senator Mazie Hirono, a member of the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2 . Senator, thanks so much for joining us on this Labor Day. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MAZIE HIRONO (D-HI): Good to see you, Yasmin.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Great to see you. First give me a reaction to the attorney general statement that he released today on the Texas law.\par \par \uc2 HIRONO: Well, I`m glad that he`s saying that they`re better not be any kind of violent or any other kind of efforts to stop women from getting other health care services. But, you know, there is such fear and the chilling effect and the chaos that`s resulting in Texas as a result of what they`re doing over there, followed by other states that want to follow suit.\par \par \uc2 [23:35:11]\par \par \uc2 By the way, this is an attack on women`s health care. That`s how I see it.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: It seems as if the Supreme Court decision, the majority is at the Supreme Court decision basically said, Listen, they found a loophole, right, Texas found a loophole, and other states, as you mentioned, will likely be following suit other conservative states.\par \par \uc2 But Laurence Tribe wrote today in an op-ed, that it seems as if the Attorney General did not go far enough. He didn`t necessarily say that in his op-ed. But Laurence Tribe wants to go further saying this the need to disarm those who cynically undermine constitutional rights while ducking all normal avenues for challenging their assault on the rule of law becomes paramount.\par \par \uc2 He cites Section 241 center of the Criminal Code, making it an even more serious crime for two or more persons to agree to oppress, threaten or intimidate anyone in the free exercise or enjoyment of any right or privilege secured to him by the Constitution or laws of the United States. Does the Attorney General in his statement go far enough in protecting women`s rights in the state of Texas?\par \par \uc2 HIRONO: One, I`m really glad that both the president as well as the attorney general is looking at using whatever tools that they have. You know, until Roe v Wade is overturned by the Supreme Court that women`s right to an abortion should still be protected. So I`m hopeful that Merrick Garland will use whatever he can.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, there is another case that is before the Supreme Court, that is going to give them the chance to overturn Roe v Wade directly. And I would like the Attorney General to weigh in. I think he should intervene in that case and argue against whatever the state is doing. I think it`s Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HIRONO: And their restrictive laws. So there are things that we can do at the same time. What should be happening is that every state should be codifying Roe v. Wade as Hawaii did a number of years ago, because these restrictive laws are being enacted by state legislators all across the country. And that`s where we need to take the fight in addition to, of course, clarifying the Roe v. Wade, in the federal law.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Let`s talk about codifying Roe v. Wade, and then federal and I just want to mention you`re talking about the state of Mississippi the case that is on the Supreme Court docket banning abortion after 15 weeks. Obviously, the Supreme Court will be hearing that case in the fall and we`ll see where they land on that.\par \par \uc2 Codifying Roe v. Wade, when it comes to federal law. Obviously, Speaker Pelosi said she`s going to bring this thing to a vote. Right. What is the likelihood, though, that it could get through this Senate at this point? I think that`s what a lot of folks that are in supportive making this law. That`s what they`re worried about.\par \par \uc2 HIRONO: This is another reason that we should get rid of the filibuster so that we don`t have to have a super majority to get anything important done in the Senate. So that`s what we ought to be pursuing. Yet another reason, you know, voting rights and so many other issues are being stymied in the Senate because of the need for a super majority. That means that we have to get 10 Republicans --\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HIRONO: -- to vote with a Democrat on these issues. And that ain`t happening anytime soon. So filibuster reform is once again, yes, that should be on our agenda.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: So you`re talking about filibuster reform, obviously, this was talked about when it came to voting rights as well. Senator Joe Manchin coming out, Kyrsten Sinema coming out and saying no, no. filibuster is staying in place. The President even supporting the filibuster staying in place, are their phone calls being made, are there conversations being had I know, there`s a lot of conversations being happened with Manchin, when it comes to reconciliation bill and his most recent opinion that came out over that. Are those conversations happening when it comes to filibuster now writing on voting rights, and this Texas bill?\par \par \uc2 HIRONO: I certainly hope so. And I expect that these conversations are happening because the Democrats want to get these issues addressed if we want to do the American families plan that`s going to enable women to get back to work, and that`s going to expand Medicare. So we want to get these things done.\par \par \uc2 And my hope is that, that at some point, Joe Manchin will also say, yes, we got to get these things done. And we can`t wait around for the Republicans to see the light because they are not going to see the light. They are going to do everything they can to stop these bills from being enacted. That`s so clear. Everybody did it.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Have you ever had a conversation with Senator Joe Manchin about this?\par \par \uc2 HIRONO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: And do you think he`ll change his mind?\par \par \uc2 HIRONO: Still working on it.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: A political answer from a senator to be expected on this Labor Day. Senator Mazie Hirono, thank you, as always, for joining us this evening.\par \par \uc2 HIRINO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 HIRONO: You`re welcome.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: All right ahead, everybody. Residents in New York, New Jersey morning loved ones lost in unprecedented flooding, what to expect from the President`s trip there tomorrow. But first as we approach the 20th anniversary of 9/11 a sobering view of the chaotic us withdrawal from Afghanistan. We got a special guest on that coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:44:10]\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Welcome back. So this Saturday we`ll mark 20 years since the September 11 terror attacks. It is the first since the US pulled out of Afghanistan. And it is the first since 9/11 on which the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  flag will fly over Kabul. The war in Afghanistan cost $2.3 trillion. And over 20,000 U.S. troops were wounded or killed. Joe Biden finally ending the war after what critics say was a chaotic withdrawal, a war that spanned four presidencies.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEORGE W. BUSH, FMR. U.S. PRESIDENT: The United States military has begun strikes against al Qaeda terrorist training camps and military installations of the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  regime in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 The war against terror will be long.\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA, FMR. U.S. PRESIDENT: This war distracts us from every threat that we face. And so many opportunities we could seize.\par \par \uc2 It is in our vital national interest to send an additional 30,000 U.S. troops to Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 [23:45:04]\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FMR. U.S. PRESIDENT: Target the terrorist and criminal networks that so violence and chaos throughout Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, U.S. PRESIDENT: I will not repeat the mistakes we`ve made in the past, mistake of staying and fighting indefinitely. I will not pass this responsibly now to a fifth president.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: I wonder if then President George W. Bush knew just how long. Joining me now is Spencer Ackerman, correspondent at The Daily Beast and author of his new book "Reign of Terror, How the 911 Era Destabilized America and Produced Trump." Spencer, thank you for joining us on this.\par \par \uc2 My first question to you is, do you think the United States learned its lesson that nation building just does not work?\par \par \uc2 SPENCER ACKERMAN. THE DAILY BEAST CONTRIBUTING EDITOR: No, the United States is determined to learn absolutely no lessons of Afghanistan, even if we`re going to say that`s the lesson of Afghanistan. I don`t think that`s the lesson of Afghanistan at all. I think the lesson of Afghanistan was never to have fought the war in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: So what do you --\par \par \uc2 ACKERMAN: I also want to say one other thing?\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Go ahead. Yes, please.\par \par \uc2 ACKERMAN: In the (INAUDIBLE) you put up and you talk about the costs of the war.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ACKERMAN: Another important measure that very frequently gets ignored is that the war killed by a conservative estimate 160,000 Afghans, and we must never remember that. We must never forget that.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes, you`re absolutely right. It is not just the loss of Americans that served overseas there, but the countless innocent lives, Afghan innocent lives that were lost as well throughout this 20 year war.\par \par \uc2 Spencer, I want to read a quote from your book, because I think it`s very telling as to what we`re dealing with right now. And you write this, Trump recognize the 9/11 era is grotesque subtext -- the perception of non-whites as marauders even as conquerors from a hostile foreign civilization was its engine.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we`re seeing this type of thing that you write about play out nationally, locally, politically in Washington. We`re seeing it and Board of Education meetings, as schools are getting back. What do you make? Can you expand on this a little bit for me and how so much of the word Afghanistan fit into that as well?\par \par \uc2 ACKERMAN: More than the war in Afghanistan, the war on terror in general, was a doorway to the most violent, the most nativist and most racist currents in American history, given the cover of patriotic emergency, and rushed into power after 9/11 and staying there throughout. Among the things that that power grab enabled was the transformation and disfigurement of the American security structure that violated the law and the constitution in order to construct an apparatus of surveillance, indefinite detention, torture, and immigration suppression.\par \par \uc2 All of those currents, particularly as the wars became more and more obviously disastrous, created in those particularly on the right who believed that America was omnipotent. This very volatile condition of feeling at neither peace nor victory, and Trump came along and recognized that the subtext of the war on terror was all that was really necessary to harness that feeling and use it for his political benefit.\par \par \uc2 And as long as the war on terror remains, as long as the authorities remain, the tools remain the operations perhaps not necessarily so central, then that danger to our democracy is going to remain. There will be more Trump`s. They will be more confident, they will have more opportunities, and they will grow in ambition.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: So how do you change the cycle?\par \par \uc2 ACKERMAN: You have to break the war on terror entirely. You have to as Colin Powell once said, of the Yurok army, cut it off and then kill it. You have to organize and force real political pressure on politicians into a binary choice between mate and heat -- between maintaining power and maintaining the war on terror.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: It`s interesting, though, because we talked first about nation building, right and united has been guilty of nation building for as long as I can remember and much before that, as well. And they continue to be in this bad cycle, right, of continuing to make the same mistakes over and over again. So who`s to say the cycle of the war on terror can also be broken, considering the history?\par \par \uc2 ACKERMAN: Well, just because history exists and weighs upon us and shapes our reality doesn`t mean that it`s destiny. And I think to resign (ph) yourself to the idea that the war on terror truly is forever --\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ACKERMAN: -- is to resign yourself to a future of repression of the erosion of democracy and the mass death of so many millions overseas.\par \par \uc2 [23:50:03]\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Spencer Ackerman, thank you great conversation. I appreciate it. And again, the new book is "Reign of Terror."\par \par \uc2 Coming up next everybody. Biden approves federal aid for the New York area after deadly flooding. We`re going to have that report coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: All right, so to the ongoing recovery efforts in the New York City area after Hurricane Ida, at least 45 dead in the New York City area. Our Gabe Gutierrez is in Queens ahead of President Biden`s visit tomorrow. Gabe.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Yasmin, this is one of the hard hidden neighborhoods in Queens where residents are still a cleaning up and the death toll here in New York State has actually gone up now to 18. 13 people died here in the city of New York and we`re also starting get a clearer picture of how horrifying it was for some of these residents inside some of these basement apartments.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NYPD\uc2  putting out a video showing its officers desperately diving into a flooded basement trying to find a family that was inside but they actually had to wait because of live electricity and rising water. They have to wait for a specialized rescue team from the fire department. By the time that team got there it was too late and three people drown.\par \par \uc2 Now today, the mayor toured this neighborhood in Queens as well as other politicians and some residents were upset that they didn`t get more warning about this storm that officials weren`t better prepared for this storm.\par \par \uc2 [23:55:14]\par \par \uc2 They say and they wonder what will happen next the next time a major storm hits that rebuilding and retrofitting these buildings to withstand these types of floods, that the cost is astronomical. We spoke with one woman Dinette Rivera, who says that she desperately tried to survive and was trapped inside her basement as the water was rising. She barely got out alive when her son who happens to be blind actually yanked her out through a window. Take a listen to what she had to say.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Did you think you were going to make it out of there?\par \par \uc2 DINETTE RIVERA, NEW YORK CITY RESIDENT: When the water was still under my chest, I did. When it started to go above my head I thought I was going to die.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: President Biden has just approved a major disaster declaration for several counties in New York State as well as several counties in n New Jersey. He is set to tour this region and see some of this devastation tomorrow, Yasmin.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: All right, thank you to Gabe Gutierrez for that. Ahead, in our next hour, everybody. Stick with me. The Trump Org criminal probe is intensifying with reports on new tax related crime charges. Also on this Labor Day, the massive wealth gap and what the President is pressing to even the playing field. And what is going on here. We`re going to explain this Trump Mount Rushmore sculpture.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020210907eh9600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION WITH \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2  for September 05, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Richard Lui \par \uc2 7,262 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 September 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [17:00:00]\par \par \uc2 REV. AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Good evening, and welcome to this very, very special edition of POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s lead, tenth anniversary. Right now I`m a mix of emotions because in the last -- in the midst of last week`s news of every kind, I celebrated my tenth year as the host of this program, POLITICSNATION with \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And before we begin tonight, I just want to thank you, the audience, who has followed me throughout the years and the different time slots. You stayed with me. I assure you that every one of us here at POLITICSNATION strives to bring you the most profound insight we can.\par \par \uc2 Of course, it starts with our guests. And as always, we have a stellar group of voices tonight, the right voices, to articulate where our current political life affects us all, yet understanding that the black community remains the epicenter of our nation`s oldest and most recent traumas, case in point, the COVID-19 pandemic, which continues to generate disparate outcomes for black people, even amidst of plague that no socializing American can escape this late today weekend.\par \par \uc2 As the Biden administration finds itself beset with a crisis of every kind, the backlash of COVID`s surge and Afghanistan`s collapse reflecting in President Biden`s approval ratings, while his signature infrastructure plan is in peril from within his own party again.\par \par \uc2 My sympathies aside, this White House must remember that it was the black vote that made it possible. And so the community will continue to press for the voter protections and police reform that the president continues to endorse and the Senate Republicans continue to choke.\par \par \uc2 Lots of news to cover on this POLITICSNATION tenth anniversary show, so let`s begin.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Democratic Representative from New York, \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , he is the number three ranked Democrat in the House in his capacity as chair of the caucus. Congressman, many things for you joining us on this festive occasion.\par \par \uc2 Last week, Congressman, I marched on Washington with Martin Luther King III and 50,000 of us to fight for voting rights as we near the mid terms. The house passed voting rights legislation last month in hopes of protecting Americans as they head to the ballot box in the face of so many new voter restrictions being passed in red-dominated states. But will this be enough, Congressman Jeffries?\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES\uc2  (D-NY): Well, great to be with you, Reverend Al, and congratulations on ten years of speaking truth to power. I certainly think that your advocacy, the march`s advocacy is an important step in the right direction to keep the pressure on the Congress and specifically the Senate to do the right thing as it relates to the John Robert Lewis Voting Rights Advancement Act.\par \par \uc2 I remain hopeful and optimistic that the Democratic senators understand the urgency of passing legislation to protect the right to vote in the face of the epidemic of voter suppression that we have seen after the January 6th insurrection. Instead of running toward democracy, Republicans have run away from democracy. And that`s why this legislation is so important.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now we also face, on Labor Day, over 7 million Americans will lose unemployment benefits as the federal program is set to expire. The country`s unemployment rate has gone down, but hiring has slowed as the delta variant causes an uptick in coronavirus cases across the country.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:05]\par \par \uc2 It`s clear that we are still in a pandemic. Is it too soon to be ending this relief while so many continue to be struggling?\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Well, I think it is important that we continue to monitor the situation and be prepared to act if necessary. Right now, President Biden is advancing an ambitious agenda connected to both the bipartisan infrastructure package that we want to get over the finish line in the next few weeks as well as the build back better act that would invest substantially in our working families, our middle class folks, those who aspire to be part of the middle class, home care, health care, child care, and elder care and making sure we invest in America`s families in a meaningful way.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, as you point out Reverend Al, the pandemic will not be over for any of us until it is over for all of us both in the context of the public health side of the crisis and the economic side of the crisis. And to the extent that Congress has to act with respect to unemployment and all the areas. We should be prepared to do that.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Congressman, on top of losing additional unemployment benefits some Americans are also facing eviction as the Supreme Court ruled 6-3 to block the extension of the moratorium on evictions, allowing landlords to resume evictions across the country. According to U.S. census data from last month, approximately 3.5 million Americans are at risk right now of being evicted in the next two months. What can -- if anything, lawmakers do right now to ensure that no one becomes homeless?\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Well, Supreme Court is out of control right now, to the extent they have any credibility left they continue to shred it with decision after decision after decision to take us backward as opposed to moving us forward.\par \par \uc2 One of the things that we need to do is to make sure that the approximately $46 billion in relief that Congress allocated to provide emergency rental assistance to tenants at risk of eviction as well as their landlords in terms of making sure that those back payments are made -- we have got to get that out the door.\par \par \uc2 Only a few billion dollars of that aid has actually made it out. And so we are working with the Biden administration, the treasury department to ease some of the restrictive language that may have been an obstacle.\par \par \uc2 But we also have to urge state after state after state to continue to do what`s necessary. Here in New York State we are thankful that Governor Hochul and the state legislature came together to extend the moratorium through the early part of next year. That should provide sufficient time get the relief out.\par \par \uc2 But in other states, like Texas or Florida, you have got people at risk of eviction. And that`s why I think getting this money out the door is going to be so critical.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: It is very critical. It`s great what is happening here in New York, but that does not mean anything in other states. Thank you for being with us, Congressman \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 . I am sure we will see you again on POLITICSNATION often. Many thanks again.\par \par \uc2 With me now is Democratic Representative from Florida and \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  Candidate Val Demings. Congresswoman, let me go right to this question, the Supreme Court this week denied to hear about the Texas anti-abortion law which bans all abortions procedures after six weeks, regardless of the pregnancy is cause by rape or incest, plain in simple, are you concern that the future of Roe versus Wade is at high risk of being in jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 REP. VAL DEMINGS (D-FL): Reverend Sharpton, first let me congratulate you on your tenth anniversary. It is an honor to be with you today.\par \par \uc2 Of course I am concerned. When you look at all that is going on in our nation. We just ended America`s longest war. You just spoke with our chair about evictions people being put out of their homes, and no longer receiving unemployment benefits.\par \par \uc2 But the war on women continues. I think it is shameful and I think Chairman Jeffries was absolutely correct. Where is the Supreme Court? Because they were always that body that body historically that would make wrongs right.\par \par \uc2 But Texas the law is out of control. And I know you`ve already heard that Florida is already looking their chops, looking at something that could be similar. But Reverend Sharpton, we are not going to let this go. The majority of the electorate in Florida are women. And I believe this will bring us together.\par \par \uc2 We have been looking at that issue. Republicans and Democrats believe that women should be able to make their own reproductive choices.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:03]\par \par \uc2 And we are going to fight this with every ounce of energy and influence that we have.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And I want to get your reaction on your Republican colleague GOP House Leader Kevin McCarthy`s behavior this week. He went after the house select committee looking into the January 6th attack by telling private companies that they were reach out to, to do what they had to comply with the investigation -- they were reached out to by the select committee.\par \par \uc2 He went after them about them handing over communications and social media records. I mean, actually, in a threatening way, that if they cooperated with a select committee of Congress investigating something that could lead to criminal behavior. I mean, what`s your reaction to a member of Congress coming in this way at people who have been asked to cooperate with a select committee of Congress?\par \par \uc2 DEMINGS: Well, Reverend Sharpton, leadership will show up during times of crisis. And what happened on January 6th was certainly a crisis. Look, Kevin McCarthy has a problem. In spite his efforts to shut down any investigation at all, voted against the independent commission, and now to have the audacity to threaten the social media companies to say that -- because all the chairman -- Chairman Thompson, the head from the select committee was to ask that those records be preserved.\par \par \uc2 That`s what you would do in any investigation within Congress, criminal or otherwise, ask that the records are preserved. And look, it was McCarthy who shared that he had had a conversation with the president on January 6th begging and pleading with the president to talk to the nation, talk to the insurrectionists and get them to stop it.\par \par \uc2 And so his threats are useless, they`re ridiculous. And he needs to be careful because I know that he will be a major part of this information. And so I think he will be questioned enough. He does not need to give the committee additional questions about his efforts to obstruct the release of any records.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And just for clarity, when you say that he talked to the president on January 6th, you were talking about President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . This was before Biden was sworn in later that month?\par \par \uc2 DEMINGS: That`s absolutely correct. On January 6th when they were really trying to stop President Biden and Vice President Harris from being sworn in.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now the 20th anniversary of September 11th is coming up. As a member of Homeland Security Committee and as a former Police Chief when you reflect on that dark, tragic day, what are you thinking about, Congresswoman?\par \par \uc2 DEMINGS: Reverend Sharpton, I was assigned to the Orlando international airport on 9/11. And I will never forget that morning, shortly before 9:00, one of my police officers walked into my office and said that a plane had just flown into one of the world trade center towers.\par \par \uc2 And as we walked back to the break room to continue to watch on television, we, along with, I think, the world, watched the second plane fly.\par \par \uc2 And so this is something that I have been involved in that capacity from the very beginning working with the airport authority, the international airport authority, and national airport authority that I was involved in during that time really trying to make sure that the traveling public was safe and that an attack of this nature would never happen again.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, as we reflect, it is a sad time. But we are also looking at the progress that we made in the hearing just this week we talked about the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  and some of the changes that have been made to that department, but changes that still need to be made.\par \par \uc2 The bottom line, Reverend Sharpton, we have got to make sure America is never attacked again in such a vicious and cowardly way.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Well, thank you Congresswoman Val Demings. And please come back and be a guest as often as you can on POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 DEMINGS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now we bring in one who I have respected probably more than most, if I could count three people that I say have a good word that have been in public life she would be two of them. \uc2 Valerie Jarrett\uc2 , the former senior adviser to President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Thank you for joining us this evening, Ms. Jarrett.\par \par \uc2 And I want to go back the Texas with you. And you`ll know why as I mentioned earlier, the state has made every Texan a vigilante in enforcing their unconstitutional abortion rights ban. You are on the board the ride sharing company, \uc2 Lyft\uc2 , which announced it would cover 100 percent of legal fees and for any driver sued under SB-8, which is this state measure for driving someone to on abortion procedure, plus a $1 million donation to Planned Parenthood.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:20]\par \par \uc2 Since this unconstitutional law relies on private citizens to enforce it. Are we going to have to rely on private companies to fight this, Ms. Jarrett?\par \par \uc2 VALERIE JARRETT\uc2 , FORMER SENIOR ADVISER TO PRESIDENT \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 : First of all, Reverend Sharpton, good evening, and congratulations on ten years on the air. And I think back to your early days on your show. And I appeared on your show. I don`t think either you or eye would have thought back then that Texas or any other state would pass such an egregious law that puts into the hands of the people basically forfeiting the responsibilities of government, but says everybody can go and enforce this law.\par \par \uc2 And so imagine you are driving a \uc2 Lyft\uc2  and somebody gets in your car and you plug in the address and you take them there. Are you supposed to say, are on your way to the abortion to get an abortion? Are you six weeks along? If you are sick weeks along, well you have to get out of the car or I could be fined up to $10,000.\par \par \uc2 That`s what`s going through the minds of our drivers. And so I am very, very proud of \uc2 Lyft\uc2  for stepping up to the plate saying this is unacceptable and we have to protect our drivers. And they going another step further and saying we have to protect organizations that are providing basic health care to women many of who without planned parenthood wouldn`t have access to health care.\par \par \uc2 And this law is particularly egregious, Reverend Sharpton, against women who are poor. Women who are wealthy can go to another state. They can -- although we are seeing an increasing number of states trying the pass these laws. But for the poor women in Texas this is outrageous. It`s outrageous.\par \par \uc2 And so yes, the answer to your question is, business needs to step up to the plate. Local elected officials in Texas, you just heard Congresswoman Demings say how many there are. Women in office in Texas, they need to step up to the plate. All of our voices have to be heard on this issue.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, this is a Supreme Court where five of the nine justices were appointed by presidents who did not win the popular vote. Justice Alito and Chief Justice Roberts were appointed by \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  in his first term. And Justices Gorsuch, Kavanaugh and Barrett were appointed by Trump. They along with Justice Thomas seem ready to gut voting rights, LGBTQ rights, abortion rights, and more. Is there any way to stop this or are we stuck in this anti-Democratic court for another generation?\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: You know what, Reverent Sharpton, elections have consequences. And nobody really liked to talk about judges over the last decades and how important they are. But every American is impacted by the decisions that the courts make, including the very important highest court, the Supreme Court. And the president has the start to do just what the presidents who appointed this bench have done. And they have been confirmed by the Senate.\par \par \uc2 And so we are stuck with them and the lifetime appointments. So this is why, one of the many reasons why we have to encourage people to turn out and vote. And you mentioned voting rights. It is absolutely under attack. We have got to get Congress to pass laws that will protect it. They will put back in place what was gutted when the case was decided by the Supreme Court that got rid of section five. We have to put that back and make sure that those states right now today are passing laws we know are intended to disenfranchise people of color, we know are intended to disenfranchise young people. We need guardrails at the federal level to keep that from happening.\par \par \uc2 And then we also have to have laws that affirmatively make it easier for people vote. All of these cases end up before the Supreme Court if there is a constitutional issue that needs to be addressed. And so, yes, vote for the president. But also vote for members of Congress.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Right.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Vote for your state prosecutors. Up and down the ballot, it is important to show up and vote. Or your live could be at stake.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, as you mentioned, this is the tenth year, the tenth anniversary show of POLITICSNATION. But were look very different now than it did a decade ago as a frequent guest, a friends of this show, do you have any advice for us for the next ten years?\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: I sure do, Reverend Sharpton, please keep doing exactly what you are doing. It is so important that your viewers are informed that you have guests that come on and you have a very dynamic, constructive conversation so that they can make decisions on their own. And so your platform provides a wonderful forum for people to get the information they need to go out and try to be a force for good and make our country and make our world better.\par \par \uc2 [17:20:04]\par \par \uc2 So I say to you Reverend Sharpton just please keep doing exactly what you are doing. Your voice and the voice of your guests are necessary for American people to hear.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Well thank you for that. And I might take personal privilege and say one of the proudest things we had in the ten years is the last sitting interview that President \uc2 Obama\uc2  did before the election that led to him being a lame duck president you arranged to be done on this show. And that`s one of the highlights of our ten years. He sat with us in North Carolina as he was trying to get voters out right before Election Day, right before he officially became the lame duck president and we had a president-elect in 2016. And we will never forget you for arranging that.\par \par \uc2 Thank you former \uc2 Obama\uc2  Senior Adviser \uc2 Valerie Jarrett\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 There`s a lot more coming on this special edition of POLITICSNATION, a look back at some uplifting moments from our weekly rise up. But, first, a message from our friends at Morning Joe.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE SCARBOROUGH, MSNBC HOST: Hey, Rev, we just want to congratulations you on ten years on POLITICSNATION. What an extraordinary run. And it`s so important. We all know you work for the hardest working man in show business in James Brown. You are now the hardest working Baptist preacher in civil rights. And you spread the good news every weekend on POLITICSNATION. And we are grateful for that around, aren`t we, Willie?\par \par \uc2 WILLIE GEIST, MSNBC HOST: Absolutely, Rev, I would ask you what your secret is but I already know. You wake up at 3:30 in the morning to work out. You drink whatever that tall green juice is. You do the Lord`s work all day and then end it with a nice cigar. Congratulations, Rev.\par \par \uc2 MIKA BRZEZINSKI\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: It is amazing ten years and Rev you bring empathy, humanity, heart and soul to POLITICSNATION. Thank you. We love you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Wow. A big thanks to the Morning Joe team. Now, let`s turn to my colleague, Richard Lui, with today`s other top news stories. Richard?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LUI, MSNBC HOST: Rev, that`s tough to top, congratulations to you on your tenth anniversary as well from me.\par \par \uc2 Stories we are watching this hour, some Louisiana residents may have to wait until the end of September to get power restored. Hundreds of thousands are living without power in Louisiana after Hurricane Ida. Utility company \uc2 Entergy\uc2  says it is exploring every option to restore electricity sooner.\par \par \uc2 As of today more than 40 million cases of COVID have been reported in the U.S. The delta variant drove the most recent spike. Hospitals are seeing an average of 100,000 COVID patients per day. They are also facing a nursing crisis. Many nurses are quitting or retiring due to exhaustion from the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 And college grads, well, they`re starting salaries are at an all-time high, the average for the class of 2020 with over $55,000. According to the national association of colleges and employers, majors with the highest salaries, well, those are petroleum engineering and computer programming.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  right after a break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:25:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST: One thing I think people don`t appreciate enough with Rev Al is that the man is always on, always working, always serving the public, and doing it in ways that nobody else is doing.\par \par \uc2 I mean, and it`s the stuff that you see, it is hosting marches, and giving sermons, and out organizing everyone even half his age while also still finding time to work out. But on top of all of that, hosting this incredible TV show that really is always bringing new stuff to the table, always covering stories nobody else is talking about until Rev Al brings them to the table and starts the conversation.\par \par \uc2 My friend, if there is a secret to how you get it all done all so well without ever visibly breaking a sweat, I would pay good money for that secret. I have a feeling it is kinds of just you being you, but let me in on some of that.\par \par \uc2 Rev, congratulations on ten years here. It is an absolute honor to call you a colleague?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That was the one and only Rachel Maddow with some kind words about the tenth anniversary of POLITICSNATION. Rachel`s show makes 13 years on the air this coming week. So, let me be the first to say congratulations and here is to many more years to come to my friend, sister, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 Now for this week`s Rise Up, I wanted to do a quick look back over the last seven months. When the former president was voted out of the White House, we knew it was time to retire the memo to Trump segment and replace it with a segment of purpose.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 Today I am introducing a new segment, called Rise Up. It will be a recurring call to action.\par \par \uc2 The history of black America is a history of rising up against bondage, segregation, violence and disenfranchisement. The battlefield for racial justice is as widespread as we are, in the streets, and in the courts, and at the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 There are people alive today because we marched in peaceful protest.\par \par \uc2 And here`s the hard truth about fighting for justice. You don`t get to opt out when the times get tough.\par \par \uc2 Only by rising up together and declaring our solidarity with one voice can we finally begin the process of eradicating the scourge of racism and white supremacist hatred in this country.\par \par \uc2 Add your voice to the call for reform on social media. Leave no room for bigotry on your page or in your life.\par \par \uc2 The assault on ballot box access is a five-alarm fire, and it`s time we start acting like it.\par \par \uc2 [17:30:09]\par \par \uc2 We can douse the flames of suppression with the power of collective action, but only if we keep rising up together.\par \par \uc2 Get organized. Get elected. And get even.\par \par \uc2 Make your voice heard, because the future of your children and this country depends on it.\par \par \uc2 We will only get out of this pandemic if we all rise up together and get that shot.\par \par \uc2 Together, we can relegate the death penalty to the dust bin of history, where it belongs.\par \par \uc2 It has been over 200 years since our founders ratified the Constitution and countless lives have been lost in the struggle to ensure that we, the people, means all the people.\par \par \uc2 American history is littered with shameful policies but it is also filled with freedom fighters.\par \par \uc2 To teach our children anything else is to rob them of their heritage and the truth, and they deserve better.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: We all deserve better. We deserve the truth. We deserve our votes. And we deserve the life, liberty and pursuit of happiness promised to us in that declaration of independence. And if we keep rising up for ten more years and beyond, we can continue the long march toward justice together.\par \par \uc2 After the break, the current state of criminal justice reform and the fight to end brutality.\par \par \uc2 But, first I`m told my colleague and little sister, \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2 , has a message for me.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOY REID\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Big brother Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2 , what can I say about ten years of POLITICSNATION? Well, basically what I can say is that it has allowed viewers on MSNBC to get just a taste of the advocacy and love for our community that you have shown those of us who came up in New York and lived in New York and really were rescued by you for so many years. It`s allowed people to see that you not only can do the activism in the streets but that you can talk policy and politics on TV just as well.\par \par \uc2 Big brother Rev, I love you. I`m so proud of you. I`m so excited about POLITICSNATION for another ten years. And I just want to say thank you for being you, for being my personal hero and for doing what you do. Congratulations. I love you, big brother, Rev.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:35:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, MSNBC HOST: For MSNBC and for me personally, Reverend Al and, of course, POLITICSNATION has been such a powerful voice for equal justice, police reform and critically needed changes in civil society. No one has done more to try to complete the centuries-old promise of equal justice under the law and hold candidates and elected officials accountable.\par \par \uc2 We need you Reverend Al, now more than ever. So congratulations. Our deepest thanks to you, Rev, for all you do, and for your passion for giving voice to those who cannot speak for themselves.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Well, thank you so much to NBC veteran Andrea Mitchell for your support. And thank you for joining us for this special tenth anniversary edition of POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Our next two guests are familiar faces on this program. Gwen Carr, the mother of the late Eric Garner and Dr. Michael Eric Dyson distinguished university professor of African-American studies at \uc2 Vanderbilt University\uc2 . He is also the Author of the forthcoming book Entertaining Race, Performing Blackness In America.\par \par \uc2 Let me go to you first, Gwen, because I`m honored that you joined us tonight. For the past decade we have tried to give people a voice who were sidelined by the political process. You have been in the center of the fight for police reform since the killing of your son, Eric Garner, by a choke hold by New York City police. Where have you found the strength to keep going?\par \par \uc2 GWEN CARR, MOTHER OF ERIC GARNER: Well, first of all, Rev, I want to congratulate you on your ten years. But, actually, it was you, just getting my strength from listening to you, and hearing the things you had to say, giving us history lessons, black history lessons every week. And I got inspired and empowered. And I says, you know, I just want to keep up the fight. I want to keep my son`s name alive. And I think this is the way we do it. So here we are, seven years later we are together.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Seven years ago you lost your son and you have been a leader in your own right. And you were there with us every Saturday at National Action Network and we have often done this show. You have become a force. Dr. Dyson, you didn`t know I was going to promote your upcoming book but, you know, we stay on top of one another`s careers.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL ERIC DYSON, PROFESSOR, VANDERBILT UNIVERSITY: Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And you have been one of the most powerful voices for black Americans in recent years. And you are about to release this book, the 24th book you have written, this one called Entertaining Race. What inspires you. 24 books, all over the world lecturing. What inspires you doing this important work?\par \par \uc2 DYSON: Well, first I want to join Sister Carr, and always great to see her and be in her presence and God bless her for her stalwart activity.\par \par \uc2 But you know, and now I want to talk about here by be and the GOAT, the greatest of all time. We have talking too one of the GOATs of our community and the Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2 . You are indeed an inspiration.\par \par \uc2 Ain`t nobody getting up earlier than you.\par \par \uc2 [17:40:00]\par \par \uc2 Ain`t nobody going to bed later than you, like your mentor the Reverend Jesse Jackson, you are indefatigable, the inexhaustible resolver of goodwill that you have, high intelligence, rhetorical theory, the ability to concentrate serious intelligence upon intractable social issues and to bring to bear upon them a broad range of historic legacies, of social justice and resistance is inspiration to all of us.\par \par \uc2 In fact, one of the chapters in my book is on the Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  because you continue to inspire us, up lift us, to call us to responsibility. And in my own tradition and trajectory as an intellectual, a public intellectual, I started late in college. I was a teen father. I didn`t go to school until I was 21 years old.\par \par \uc2 Hustled on the streets, lived on welfare. Didn`t get my degree until I was 26 years old, and then went to graduate school. But since that time I tried to make up for lost time as a professor, as a preacher, as a social activist, as a public intellectual and this book is the manifestation of that intense involvement with the traditions of social resistance and the determination to use literally and the word to uplift us.\par \par \uc2 But there has been no stronger soldier of justice, truth, and democracy in our own time. What you are doing is unprecedented. A black leader, the black leader in our community, with also a television show, and a radio show to amplify the voices of those who are most vulnerable and those who are least recognized is an extraordinary testimony to your genius and to your commitment to our community.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Well, thank you for that. And I am old enough to remember when somebody calling you a GOAT would be insulting. But now it means something else. Let me say, as we face these challenges that we face today, Gwen, you were with us last Saturday, Martin III and Arndrea King as we led this march around voting rights, tens of thousands into \uc2 CBS\uc2  said were 50,000 -- you are there every rally, every march.\par \par \uc2 We are facing critical times where the very democracy that this country claims is at risk here. And do you think people really understand how serious times are when we are looking at voting being undermined, police being given a way out of being held accountable? Do you think people really understand the danger that we are in?\par \par \uc2 CARR: Well, Reverend Al, some people do. I think most don`t. And this is why we have to get out here and we have to push. We have to let them know the danger, the criticalness that is for us to vote.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 CARR: For us to register to vote. Because I don`t think people are walking around saying voting doesn`t matter. Voting matters in every aspect of the word. And they just don`t understand. They are walking around with their heads in the clouds. But they have got to get their heads out of the clouds?\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I am out of time. But Michael Eric Dyson, are we in a real existential crisis here?\par \par \uc2 DYSON: There is no doubt. An existential crisis, a political crisis, a spiritual crisis and a moral crisis, that`s why leadership of your ilk brand and variety is a especially meaningful to us as we uplift the great truths of our democracy and of our people together.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right Gwen Carr and Michael Eric Dyson, thank you both for being with us on this anniversary show.\par \par \uc2 Okay. We are not taking a break. It`s time now for a look back at the last ten years of POLITICSNATION. But not before we first hear from my good friends and colleagues, \uc2 Jonathan Capehart\uc2 , followed by MSNBC`s Ali Velshi.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Congratulations, Reverend Al, on ten years of POLITICSNATION. In that time, you have brought your voice and moral clarity to our national political discourse. I have been covering you and your voice for nearly 30 years back in my days at the Daily News when you were challenging the establishment, speaking truth to power even as your stature grew. And that`s the secret sauce of your success and the longevity of POLITICSNATION, staying true to your values and remaining focused on what`s right, fair, and justice. Here`s to many more years, my friend.\par \par \uc2 ALI VELSHI, MSNBC HOST: All right. So Reverend Al`s impact on the country on MSNBC, that`s well documented, nothing for me to add. But on a very personal level, particularly in the last few years when we really needed his analysis any time I have asked or called the Reverend to come on and provide the kinds of analysis that we need and that I rely on, he has done so. He has called from the gym, from a hotel room. I think he called from a subway car once. So, here`s to all that you do for us, and here`s to another great ten years and beyond for you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [17:45:]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Well, thank you both Jonathan and Ali for your messages.\par \par \uc2 Now, I made a promise on my very first POLITICSNATION show to say what I mean and mean what I say. A decade later -- a decade of shows later, I haven`t strayed from that promise. Here are ten years in four minutes.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome to POLITICSNATION, I`m \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2 . Tonight`s lead.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s lead.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s lead.\par \par \uc2 I`m live tonight in Orlando, Florida.\par \par \uc2 From Selma, Alabama.\par \par \uc2 Downtown Minneapolis.\par \par \uc2 From Columbia, South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Live from the legendary Apollo Theater in Harlem, New York City.\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: Progress is never made overnight. Yes, there are going to be times where compromises need to be made.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: The sensitivity, I think that we have, is whoever selected that black women be clearly seen as qualified to be vice president.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , U.S. PRESIDENT: I assure you that over the more than a dozen women that I have taken an initial look at there are significantly more than one black women that`s going to be considered by this group.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: She`s the Vice president of the United States, the honorable \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , U.S. VICE PRESIDENT: Don`t let COVID get us. Let`s get the vaccine instead.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): Reverend Sharpton, always an honor to be with you, thank you for your question about your children.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Can we get rid of the filibuster? Or is that something the caucus has to debate?\par \par \uc2 SEN. CHUCK SCHUMER (D-NY): Yes, we will put our heads together and decide how the best way of getting change is, but change, we must.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: How do you assess \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in a Kingian kind of analysis as one that walked shoulder to shoulder with Dr. King?\par \par \uc2 FMR. \uc2 REP. JOHN LEWIS\uc2  (D-GA): I don`t believe this man really understand what \uc2 Martin Luther King Jr\uc2 . was all about.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Stevie Wonder, where do you start in you have done it all.\par \par \uc2 Spike lee, thank you.\par \par \uc2 SPIKE LEE, U.S. DIRECTOR: Always.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right.\par \par \uc2 LEE: Brooklyn is in the house.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: In the house.\par \par \uc2 The queen of soul, Ms. Aretha Franklin. Thank you so much for being here.\par \par \uc2 ARETHA FRANKLIN, SINGER AND SONGWRITER: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Policing is the critical civil rights issue aside from voting protection. Those two issues are the issues of 21st century.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN CRUMP, CIVIL RIGHTS ATTORNEY: Absolutely, Reverend Al. And, you know, this will be George Floyd`s legacy.\par \par \uc2 LEWIS: Because they can do what they want but I am going to always be seeking the truth.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN LUTHER KING III, HUMAN RIGHTS ADVOCATE: To one of the things dad did, as you know, Rev, was if somebody was 90 percent bad, he didn`t focus on the 90 percent bad but he focused on the 10 percent good and extracted that from the people.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It is the tenth annual Revy Awards from Rockefeller Center. Here is your host, \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I`m handing out blueberry pie slices. I decided to hand out a few extra slice awards this year.\par \par \uc2 This week`s memo to Trump, what would you, \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 , know about strength? You were born with a silver spoon in your mouth and then spent every day of your pathetic life whining that it wasn`t gold.\par \par \uc2 Even though you and I were born and raised in the same city, here is one way we are different. You can dish it but you certainly can`t take it.\par \par \uc2 I got you.\par \par \uc2 By the way, I got you.\par \par \uc2 I got you.\par \par \uc2 If our voices don`t matter, they wouldn`t be trying so hard to silence them.\par \par \uc2 Social justice is a team sport.\par \par \uc2 Now that we know exactly how effective we can be together, we have to keep going, keep rising up together, marching peacefully and demanding that this country finally deliver on its essential promise, liberty and justice for all.\par \par \uc2 That does it for me. Thanks for watching. I will see you back here tomorrow at 5:00 P.M. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Up next, my final thoughts on the next ten years to come. But we can`t go to break without hearing from someone near and dear to me. That`s Tiffani Cross.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TIFFANI CROSS, MSNBC HOST: Reverend Sharpton, I want to wish you a wonderful, happy ten-year anniversary on POLITICSNATION. I remember when you got this show and how proud and excited I was to see you. And I have to say, you have always been such a mentor to me. You are my big brother. You helped me navigate so many spaces. You shared your platform with me when we co-hosted your radio show on SiriusXM every Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 And I cannot thank you enough for not only what you`ve meant for what you have meant to me as a colleague and coworker at MSNBC, but truly what you have meant to the community.\par \par \uc2 [17:50:00]\par \par \uc2 You have always been an unapologetic champion for the people and I don`t think you get enough credit for that. So, thank you so much for everything you have done and I want to wish you a wonderfully happy ten-year anniversary.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: As we go forward, the next ten years, we have real, real danger of losing voting rights, women`s rights, LGBTQ rights, and the battle for police reform. So we must stay focused and stay on the mark, and that`s what I have tried to do on this show. I want to thank Phil Griffin, who was president of MSNBC and brought this show and me onboard.\par \par \uc2 I don`t think when I came in this building, 30 Rock, anyone but Phil, an event mildly thought I would last days. I want to thank Rashida Jones, who is now the president.\par \par \uc2 [17:55:00]\par \par \uc2 I`m so proud. I wish \uc2 Shirley Chisholm\uc2 , the congresswoman, who I was a volunteer for her presidential campaign and my mother could have lived to see us in a building that a black woman is the president of a network who has kept that vision and kept driving us going forward.\par \par \uc2 I want to thank the team of POLITICSNATION. You see me, but we had a great team ten years, our producer, Matt Saal, and the great Marsha Ernestine (ph) and the team. I want to thank the team at National Action Network that lets me multitask, Mike Hardy and the team I`ve had for ten years. I want to thank the audience that tolerated even when I had to learn the teleprompter in the first few months, and stayed with us and gave us the audience that sustained us. I want to thank my family and my friends. And too many to name.\par \par \uc2 But most of all, I want to thank you all that watch, get informed and get active. And as a Baptist minister, I want to thank God, for he never let me down, even when I let him down.\par \par \uc2 That does it for me. Thanks for watching this special edition of POLITICSNATION. I`ll see you back here next weekend at 5:00 P.M. Eastern on MSNBC. Coverage picks up at the top of the hour.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:00:00]\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020210906eh9500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION September 4, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Richard Lui \par \uc2 6,965 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 September 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [17:00:00]\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Good evening and welcome to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s lead: Our rights under attack. Millions of women in Texas woke up this week to the news that the Supreme Court inaction had allowed their constitutional right to an abortion to be eroded, essentially out of existence. And in the following days, millions of other people in other Republican controlled states watched as their own legislatures rushed to follow Texas`s lead.\par \par \uc2 In a blistering dissent, Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  called the court`s order, stunning. But while the Texas law and the court`s decision might have stunned us with this brazenness, we cannot be surprised.\par \par \uc2 The current court with five of its nine members appointed by Republican Presidents who did not win the popular vote, while they`ve shown nothing but disdain for the fundamental rights of America`s most marginalized people, this court has continuously disregarded not just the right to choose, but also the single most right of citizens in a so-called democracy, the right to vote and choose their own leaders.\par \par \uc2 The two are inextricably connected, both fundamental issues about the right to do some self-determination. In a truly free society, people should get to decide whether and when to become parents without interference from the government, just as we should have guaranteed access to the ballot box without fear of intimidation, suppression, or retaliation.\par \par \uc2 That`s why we marched on the Capitol last Saturday, last week today, and why we will not stop. We cannot wait and watch as our rights are stripped away. By next year`s midterm election. It may be too late.\par \par \uc2 Conservatives on the court in Congress and in state legislatures across the country are determined to defile the sanctity of the voting booth and the privacy of your doctor`s office.\par \par \uc2 And so we start tonight in the State of Texas where without Federal election, they have so far succeeded in both.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Congressman Al Green, Democrat of Texas and member of the Homeland Security and Financial Services Committees.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, first of all, thank you for joining me. I want to start in your home state where the Supreme Court inactions has allowed the most draconian abortion law in nearly a half a century to stand.\par \par \uc2 I particularly want to ask you about the part of the law that turns ordinary people into vigilantes, because the state does not enforce this ban, but rather deputizes Texans into suing anyone involved in aiding and abetting the procedure for $10,000.00.\par \par \uc2 What will this look like on the ground? And do you expect to see Republicans try this trick for things other than abortion?\par \par \uc2 REP. AL GREEN (D-TX): But thank you for having me, Reverend, and I`m wearing my mask for identification purposes simply because I have staff working with me in our office. We have a policy of wearing our mask when we`re in the office together.\par \par \uc2 Thank you also for your intro. I would like to associate myself with your comments and the portion where you said that we should not be surprised is entirely correct, because after all, Texas is where Roe versus Wade took place. This was up in Dallas County. The Wade and -- Roe vs. Wade was Henry Wade, the District Attorney up there who was sued.\par \par \uc2 And Texas is also the place, Reverend, where we have to white primaries. So, you`re right that is moving to voting as well. And after white primaries were outlawed by the Supreme Court, Texas had white pre- primaries.\par \par \uc2 Texas has been a bad actor for a long time. Yes, I believe that there are those who will seek to have this reward accorded them. They will go out and try to find ways to literally bring people before the bar of justice in court in a civil lawsuit, to try to acquire $10,000.00 because they may suspect that someone has sought to get an abortion within this six weeks period.\par \par \uc2 I will also say to you that I don`t understand how rape and incest can be eliminated. Because if a young teenage daughter is raped, what parent would not want to help that daughter receive the choice that she might have of not carrying to term.\par \par \uc2 It`s just amazing to me and I support totally the opposition to this. As Speaker Pelosi has said that we would codify this law so that it would not become something that the Supreme Court might act on in this arbitrary capricious way. I support that. We need to codify the law.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:07]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, and as you mentioned, this law says, even in the case of rape or incest, you can`t get an abortion if you have been pregnant over six weeks, and many women don`t even know they are pregnant in the first six weeks. But other states are looking to follow Texas`s example on this.\par \par \uc2 South Dakota Governor \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2  has already directed her staff to look into emulating this attack on the constitutional right to choose, and other Republican-led states aren`t far behind. What actions can Congress take to stop this assault on reproductive justice since the Supreme Court has refused to step in?\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Yes, sir. Of course, we can codify laws that protect the rights of women. We already have such a law pending and Speaker Pelosi has already indicated that we will have a vote on it.\par \par \uc2 I`m convinced that we can pass it in the House and I`m optimistic about the Senate because I believe that there are Republicans who are concerned about the lack of an opportunity to deal with incest and rape.\par \par \uc2 And I`m also eager to say to you, Reverend, there is a higher court than a Supreme Court that has to rule and has to make his opinion known, and that is the court of public opinion. It`s time for the court of public opinion to issue its thoughts. It`s time for people to do what is lawful, and that Reverend, is engaged in peaceful protest.\par \par \uc2 If the public says this is not something that we want, the Supreme Court eventually will come around, maybe not immediately, but eventually.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Congressman, let me turn to the aftermath of the January 6 insurrection. Freshman Republican Congressman Madison Cawthorn of North Carolina referred to insurrectionists as, quote "political prisoners" at a party event this week. He also falsely claimed the recent election rigged and said there would be bloodshed. This is a quote, he said, "bloodshed" despite denial from his office that seems like a pretty clear threat to me.\par \par \uc2 What is it like to come to work every day with someone like Cawthorn? Will there be consequences for his threat, Congressman?\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Reverend, allow me to note because this is such an important issue. He also said he would "dread having to pick up arms against a fellow American." That`s pretty strong language, Reverend, and it does make it difficult for persons to do the things that we have to do to pass legislation.\par \par \uc2 We like to believe that we can visit with each other and we can talk to each other, and that we don`t have any intentionality associated with harming another American when these conversations are taking place.\par \par \uc2 This is very much regrettable. It`s a very sensitive issue, and I would hope that this member would retract this post haste so as to let the other members know that this is not something that we should be concerned about.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, the threats aren`t limited to freshmen Republicans like Cawthorn. This week, House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy threatened American technology companies that have been asked to preserve their phone records relating to January 6 and the insurrection. Why is Republican leadership so desperate to keep the truth from coming out?\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Reverend, that is the question why, as opposed to what are they doing, but why are they doing this? Reverend, it is obvious that they are attempting to cover something up, some would say white wash; others would say that this is an obstruction of Congress, an official congressional investigation. Others might even say this obstruction of justice.\par \par \uc2 Here is what I say. I say that this is a part of the effort to bring back a President that they have been trying to protect since he left office. Remember, Mr. McCarthy did not want the committee to be established, and he has fought putting persons on the committee. This is an effort to look forward to 2022 when there will be an effort to take the House back, but they will fail, and in 2024 when there will be an effort to reclaim the presidency, wherein they must fail.\par \par \uc2 We have to take to the streets. But more importantly, we`ve got to vote. This is a time for every person to register and vote notwithstanding Senate bills here in Texas that thwart our efforts, can`t get 24-hour voting.\par \par \uc2 In Texas, we ban things we choose not to understand and I say choose not to because there is no one so blind as he who refuses to see and we ban a 24- hour voting. We have banned drive-thru voting. We`ve made it difficult to vote generally speaking, and when this happens, you`ve got to conclude at some point that this is about upcoming elections, and they, I believe are preparing for what they perceive as the return of a President who was impeached twice and should not be returned to office.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:12]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Congressman Green, briefly, please. As a member of the House Homeland Security Committee, I wonder if you could corroborate on reporting Friday that Homeland Security is concerned about far-right groups praising the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  in their online platforms, where the takeover of Afghanistan has been cast as a model for the kind of Civil War many of these groups are trying to foment domestically.\par \par \uc2 Because it struck me in the reading that 20 years after 9/11 and all the anti-Islamic sentiment that it channeled from the right-wing, organized white supremacists are now looking past race and religion and seeing a violence success story in Afghanistan, even as they demonize Afghan refugees as part of a racial replacement campaign.\par \par \uc2 Have you heard these concerns from D.H.S., Congressman?\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Well, the concerns I am aware of have not gotten it directly from D.H.S., but the concerns I`m aware of and concerned about, and here`s why. The insurrection that took place has been something that has inspired these white supremacists. They don`t see this as the last attempt to assault the Capitol and many of them believe that what happened was a good thing.\par \par \uc2 And I believe that we must do all that we can to make sure that the Capitol is protected. Never again should such a thing occur.\par \par \uc2 Speaker Pelosi has done an outstanding job in bringing on new personnel, new people to help with the Capitol Police. So, I`m looking forward to doing what we can as Members of Congress to not only say that it shouldn`t happen again, but Reverend, we should memorialize January 6, the same way we more memorialized 9/11.\par \par \uc2 Congress passed a resolution, the President signed it. We need to memorialize January 6th so that people will know where the Congress stands on an insurrection that took place on our watch.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Thank you for being with us, Congressman, and thank you for marching and speaking with thousands of us last Saturday in Washington, Congressman Al Green.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, former Republican strategist and founder of Rilax Strategies is Rina Shah, and podcast host and founder of Woke AF Nation, Danielle Moodie.\par \par \uc2 Rina, let me start with you. I want to start with the Texas anti-abortion law that has rattled the nation. The Supreme Court denying to step in by five to four vote as women now face being turned down an abortion after only six weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The conservative-leaning Supreme Court decision was a direct consequence of the Trump presidency, which led to three Supreme Court appointments. Is this just a taste of what is to come during this battle for a fight for women`s right to choose?\par \par \uc2 RINA SHAH, FORMER REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST: Well, this story really epitomizes what the Republican Party is today, Rev, and it is a party of hypocrisy. On the one hand, they say, our body our choice, no masks; on the other, well, women, we want to tell you what to do with your body.\par \par \uc2 Women make up over half this country and what Texas legislators have told us is that freedom isn`t free for everyone. And so what I really feel today is disappointment, because I`m not surprised. I knew this. I knew when Senator Collins said what she said, I knew that in the back of her head she knew better. And if we had \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  today, no doubt would the Supreme Court have had a different decision.\par \par \uc2 But don`t lose faith, anyone listening, please don`t. Our democracy demands that we get out there now and we educate and we advocate for those who cannot do for themselves.\par \par \uc2 I`m a lover of freedom. I may be pro-life myself, but I`m pro-choice for other women. And that I believe is a view of that many more Republicans out there than we know. So, I hope that they use this time to get out there and actually share their stories of stories of when they needed a medically necessary abortion.\par \par \uc2 Most insurance companies now cover genetic testing. During both my pregnancies, that was covered. Genetic abnormalities show up and make a pregnancy non-viable. Share stories.\par \par \uc2 Anybody right of center that is conservative, you know that this is not a black and white issue. You know, we need to share and we need to do this work now because we`ve seen what happens. We see what they`ll do in the dead of night.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And you`re right. You could be pro-life yourself, but still fight for people to have the right of choice.\par \par \uc2 Danielle, it is not lost on me that in Texas people have more of a right to spread deadly virus and not wear a mask than women are to control over their -- have control over the own body. I mean this week, the Biden administration is punching back, criticizing the Supreme Court and calling the anti-abortion law almost un-American.\par \par \uc2 As we near the midterms, will this force Democrats to get their act together to prevent more laws like this?\par \par \uc2 [17:15:19]\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE MOODIE, PODCAST HOST AND FOUNDER OF WOKE AF NATION: I mean, the reality is, is that nobody should be shocked and especially Democrats should not have been shocked about where Texas has gone. We have been in a constant battle to preserve Roe v. Wade, we`ve been in a constant battle to preserve our democracy, and we are at a place where we knew what was going to happen when \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  decided to jam through the appointment of Amy Coney Barrett three weeks before the election.\par \par \uc2 Three Supreme Court Justices that \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  was able to put on the courts because guess what? Democrats don`t talk about the courts. And it`s one of the most important things that we need to talk about.\par \par \uc2 When we`re talking about voting rights, when we`re talking about LGBTQ rights, when we`re talking about women`s rights, where does that come from? It doesn`t come from Congress and Congress alone. It comes from the courts, and \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  was able to appoint 300 -- over 300 Federal justices, and we have -- we have now the most conservative court that we have ever seen.\par \par \uc2 And so yes, I wish that Democrats would stop playing defense and start playing offense and use the power that the people gave them in the midst of a pandemic in 2020 and came out in a historic election.\par \par \uc2 Look, voting is extremely important, we know that, but also the conversations and whose feet we need to lay this battle at is the feet of Republicans, and Democrats do not do that. So, I hope that we see a sense of urgency that we have yet to see.\par \par \uc2 We are not in a collaborative place. We are not in a let me talk to my friend from across the aisle type of place. We need to start utilizing the power that the people gave the Democrats and move forward because we are running out of time.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now Rina, private companies are fighting back against Texas`s oppressive anti-abortion law, S.B. 8 is the title of it. The ride-sharing app, \uc2 Lyft\uc2 , has issued a statement saying they have created a legal defense fund that will cover 100 percent of all drivers` legal expenses if sued by Texas for taking a passenger to an abortion clinic.\par \par \uc2 They also announced they will be donating $1 million to Planned Parenthood. Why is it that when democracy fails, it is up to private companies to step in to fight?\par \par \uc2 SHAH: To me, this isn`t even enough, and this is because the Texas abortion law is so cruel. I mean, they`re talking about civil lawsuits against people who even drive a woman to an abortion. This is just so absolutely gray and beyond the pale that of course, these companies have to step in, because so many of us use rideshare opportunities nowadays.\par \par \uc2 And so I`m glad they did, but we need to see far more corporations do something. The cruelty of saying that even in the case of incest and rape, this is going to be illegal for women to seek out abortions after six weeks. Again, as was mentioned in the segment that women oftentimes don`t even know they`re pregnant at six weeks.\par \par \uc2 For both my pregnancies, I didn`t know until well after six weeks, I was really surprised. And I was married at that point.\par \par \uc2 So you know, this is just something to say that corporations owe us more. And yes, they do have to step in, it seems in this era when Republicans are doing many people very dirty. But we need more corporations to actually put their money where their mouth is, actually stop funding some of these lawmakers and to stop giving money to their PACs because we`re watching, we know what you do.\par \par \uc2 And these corporations need to step up and step up for the American women because we hold the pocket.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Danielle and Rina, please stay with us. We`ll bring you back, both of you in a bit.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, POLITICSNATION, tonight, Republicans say their war on reproductive rights is pro-life. I`m about to call their bluff and tell you how you can rise up and fight back.\par \par \uc2 And later, the state of Civil Rights in the country on the heels of some startling hate crime numbers from the F.B.I. The one and only \uc2 Ben Crump\uc2  joins me later on the show tonight.\par \par \uc2 But first my colleague, Richard Lui with today`s top news stories -- Richard.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LUI, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Rev, a good Saturday to you. Stories that we`re watching for you this hour, in New Jersey, at least one-third of the people killed by Ida drowned, trapped in their cars. Ida caused at least 25 deaths in the state. Tuesday, President Biden will visit New Jersey and New York to survey the damage there.\par \par \uc2 The hurricane season remains in full swing. Hurricane Larry is expected to be a Category 4 storm with winds up to 140 miles an hour. It could threaten Bermuda, but will stay east of the U.S., as it is expected.\par \par \uc2 August was the deadliest pandemic month for Florida. Over 5,700 people died in the state says the C.D.C. The previous high was January before vaccines were widely available.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 General Motors\uc2  is halting production for up to two weeks at most of its North American plants. GM saying it`s due to a global computer chip shortage. The pandemic and other disasters had disrupted supply chains in the automotive industry.\par \par \uc2 [17:20:05]\par \par \uc2 LUI: Longtime "Today" show weatherman, Willard Scott, has passed away at 87. He spent 65 great years at NBC. Scott launched the tradition of celebrating fans turning 100 years old. He`s semi-retired in 1996.\par \par \uc2 Al Roker who then stepped in on today called Scott an inspiration. Roker also added there`s only one Willard.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  right after a short break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: For this week`s Rise Up, I want to talk about the all-out assault on reproductive freedom that has been underway for decades in this country. It finally came to a head this week as the conservative wing of the Supreme Court allowed a draconian Texas law to stand, one that will effectively outlaw abortion In the Lone Star State, in open defiance of the constitutional precedent set by Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 [17:25:08]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: This development is a violation of the bodily autonomy of millions of Texans, and it will disproportionately hurt women of color, disabled women, and poor folks.\par \par \uc2 I`ll leave the details of the law for someone else to parse. It`s been covered very well by my colleagues on this network. Instead, I want to address I want to really get to the hypocrisy of the party that calls itself quote, "pro-life," but only when it comes to abortion, because these are the same folks who have consistently been pro-gun, pro-war, and pro- death penalty.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, once these pregnancies come to turn, these people are nowhere to be found for the rest of that child`s life. No paid family leave. No food stamps. No universal childcare. No eviction moratorium. No funding for public education. And now no mask or vaccine mandates to keep those children safe from coronavirus.\par \par \uc2 And yet, they have the gall to call themselves pro-life. This is the party who spent decades stacking the courts with anti-abortion judges, all while lying to the American people about their real goals.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. JONI ERNST\uc2  (R-IA): I think the likelihood of Roe v. Wade being overturned is very minimal. I don`t see that happening.\par \par \uc2 SEN. LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): This is not a referendum on Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 SEN. SUSAN COLLINS (R-ME): I do not believe that \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  will overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE\uc2 , FORMER VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I would never presume how Judge Amy Coney Barrett would rule on the \uc2 Supreme Court of the United States\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D), PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The point is that the President also is opposed to Roe v. Wade. That`s on the ballot as well.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: You don`t know it is on the ballot? Why is it in the ballot?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Because you said --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: It`s not on the ballot. You don`t know her view on Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: They`ve been lying to us for years because they had to. The American public does not want abortion to be banned. In fact, a resounding majority of Americans have wanted abortion to be legal in some or all circumstances since Gallup began asking the question in 1975.\par \par \uc2 And do you know why? Because the American people know that banning abortions does not stop people from getting abortions. It stops them from getting safe abortions.\par \par \uc2 We cannot let these so-called pro-life laws drag us back to the days of back alley death. So, here`s what we can do about it.\par \par \uc2 If you truly want to see fewer abortions in this country, the answer is not banning the procedure, but advocating for conditions that will make it less necessary. Things like paid family leave, universal prenatal care, easy access to contraception, and comprehensive sex education.\par \par \uc2 Make your voice heard and let your elected representatives know that pro- choice voters are every bit as passionate about the issue as those who oppose abortion rights. And if they don`t take action to undo the damage done by the court, particularly this court, we will hold them accountable at the ballot box because reproductive justice is not just a woman`s issue, it will take all of us to rise up together and stop this assault on the bodily autonomy of half the population and we can do it together.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION. Let`s bring back my political panel. We have a lot to talk about with podcast host, Danielle Moodie and former Republican strategist, Rina Shah.\par \par \uc2 Danielle, on Tuesday, House G.O.P. leader Kevin McCarthy warned multiple companies not to cooperate with the House Select Committee looking into what occurred on January 6th. Now this comes after the committee asked 35 communication and social media companies like \uc2 Verizon\uc2  and \uc2 Twitter\uc2  to preserve their records since it may be valuable in their investigation.\par \par \uc2 The reason may be to look into how G.O.P. lawmakers were communicating with former President Trump leading up to the violence.\par \par \uc2 Could turning over these records be why Leader McCarthy is so against it? I mean, what does he have to hide?\par \par \uc2 MOODIE: Rev, he has everything to hide, as does every single Republican that was vocal at this Stop the Steal rally that was held right before the insurrection occurred. I mean, the reality is, is that we need to know whether or not our leaders were in conversation with insurrectionists, provided them with plans of the Capitol so that they knew where to go and who to attack and when.\par \par \uc2 We need to understand how this Republican Party is currently operating. We`re supposed to be in a democracy, right, that is supposed to have a two- party system that is about working for the betterment of America, and what we have come to understand about this political party under Trump and before is that they do not want America to be better.\par \par \uc2 If they did, then they would tell people to go get vaccines. If they did, then they would tell their constituents to wear a mask. If they did, then they would be about doing everything that they can do in order to get us out of a public health pandemic, so that we can become economically stable and viable again.\par \par \uc2 But that is not what they want. They want Biden to fail. They want America to fail so that they can bring in and usher in their authoritarian regime.\par \par \uc2 This is not something new. This is the playbook that Republicans have been using for quite some time.\par \par \uc2 And so with Leader McCarthy, we need to ask, why don`t you want us to see your phone records? Why don`t you want to tell us, Jim Jordan, what conversation you had with President Trump and when? Did you talk to him that day? Did you talk to him the day before? What were those conversations and why?\par \par \uc2 [17:35:19]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Well, I think it even goes -- it even goes deeper than that, Rina, we already know that Leader McCarthy was a person of great interest during former President Trump`s second impeachment trial. McCarthy is known for having close communications with Trump.\par \par \uc2 Is it ethical for him to be encouraging private companies not to comply with the House committee`s investigation? Because what I really want to underscore here is if a committee investigating what happened that could lead to crimes, he is interfering what could be a criminal procedure, which could in itself be a crime.\par \par \uc2 SHAH: That`s right, Rev. This is what is really at stake here. He knows it is unethical, number one, but here, he is also basically breaking U.S. Code, and people are saying he can be referred to the Department of Justice for breaking a Code. And that Code essentially says you cannot threaten anybody trying to comply with the orders of the select committee.\par \par \uc2 This select committee has reached out to 35 corporations, as far as I know, that they want to know these records, they want to get these records. And I think as Americans, we should want to see these records.\par \par \uc2 If we have people who`ve been sitting in Congress who said questionable things that were part of that rioting, the insurrection on January 6th, we, American taxpayers deserve to know because they`re getting protection every day in the Halls of \uc2 U.S. Congress\uc2  with our dime. So, that`s number one.\par \par \uc2 And I do hope that this goes a step further, because what we saw on January 6th, that was extremism, and if Leader McCarthy chooses to go this route, we need to be extreme with him and these 11 others who sent a threatening letter to 13 corporations, including \uc2 Google\uc2 , \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , \uc2 Verizon\uc2 , and \uc2 AT&T\uc2 , this is a threatening letter. Why? Republicans threatening corporations here? What is going on? You think you have that much power, I really hope Democrats do it.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Threatening about -- threatening them about cooperating with a committee investigation of the \uc2 U.S. Congress\uc2 . Let`s be real clear, emphasizing on what he is threatening them about. He is really telling them to defy the request of a committee.\par \par \uc2 But Danielle, let me ask you this. The recall election in California is just 10 days away with Democratic Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  battling it out to stay in power. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  will be campaigning for Newsom in the final stretch ahead of the election.\par \par \uc2 Californians must also vote on who should replace him if they choose to vote yes to the recall. California has gone red before. What do you see happening this time?\par \par \uc2 MOODIE: I mean, Rev, I`ve to say that I`m really concerned. I`m concerned with the radical right that they have on the ballot in California. I`m concerned with the Trumpism that they are willing to follow.\par \par \uc2 And the reality is this, everybody in California has got to get out to vote. Everybody in California has got to show up. Everyone in California has got to say that they are not about this recall, that \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  should continue as the Governor of this state, because we cannot lose a Senate seat, right, because that is exactly what will happen.\par \par \uc2 We cannot lose the State of California. We are at a very critical time in our democracy. I don`t think that folks understand the urgency that where you`re in and I hope that Texas is raising the alarm that needs to go around the country, not just people shrugging off and saying, well, at least I don`t live in Texas.\par \par \uc2 No, what is happening in Texas will happen everywhere, and folks need to pay attention to what is happening there and what is happening in California.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Danielle Moodie and Rina Shah, thank you both for being with us tonight.\par \par \uc2 After the break, new numbers from the F.B.I. show hate crimes are at their highest level in years. What this means in the ongoing fight for Civil Rights? That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:42:06]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: As we prep for our 10th Anniversary edition tomorrow, we learned of a grim milestone this week. The F.B.I. releasing its annual hate crime statistics for 2020 and finding that our nation has reached a 12-year high in reports of bias motivated attacks. The most chilling numbers being the exponential increases in attacks on the black and AAPI communities fueled by a year of COVID conspiracy theories and historic clashes over criminal justice.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Civil Rights attorney general of black America, \uc2 Ben Crump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Attorney Crump, thank you for being with us. We have a lot to talk about, starting with this new F.B.I. report that found hate crimes rose again in 2020, the sixth time in the last seven years, the highest level since Barack Obama`s election 12 years ago, nearly 7,800 criminal hate crimes reported last year by more than 15,000 law enforcement agencies, a six percent increase over 2019 and about 40 percent increase in just the last six years.\par \par \uc2 But then you add this offenses targeting black people, up approximately 42 percent, while attacks against people of Asian descent are up more than 70 percent. We`ve heard from Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  that the prosecution of hate crimes is being enhanced by the Justice Department and I am certainly understanding the targeting of extremist groups.\par \par \uc2 But I wonder from a law enforcement perspective, how do you stop individual actors before they can actually commit their crimes? Because this report tells me that the last six to seven years have emboldened both.\par \par \uc2 You are the Attorney General of black America. How do you see this?\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN CRUMP\uc2 , CIVIL RIGHTS ATTORNEY: Well, first of all Reverend Al, I want to say congratulations on 10 years of POLITICSNATION, one of the most consequential and important television shows for people of color, especially African-Americans. And as I congratulate you, that is a segue into answering your question about the rise in hate crime.\par \par \uc2 We have seen over and over again that minority communities that have been marginalized in America have been engaged and been empowered. We saw that with the election in Georgia, turning it blue, and so there is a projected sense that black and brown people are becoming more populous.\par \par \uc2 The Americans that we see is becoming more diverse and so you have an egregious or rabid reaction by those on the far-right following the six or seven years of this rhetoric of make America great again.\par \par \uc2 [17:45:11]\par \par \uc2 CRUMP: Well, does that mean that they thought that making it great again when they were lynching black people? And then we saw in 2020 Ahmaud Arbery was black, when they were discriminating overtly against Asian-Americans, and that we see this rise in hate crime against Asians. And so I believe this is their response because they are afraid of a more diverse America, Reverend Al.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Attorney Crump, as one of the attorneys for Ahmaud Arbery`s family, could you elaborate on the charges now facing the former Georgia District Attorney, who was initially tasked with prosecuting his killing last year by three white men, one of whom was revealed to be a retired veteran investigator in her office.\par \par \uc2 Because the indictment of Jacqueline Lee-Johnson by Georgia`s Attorney General, this week alleges that she violated her office and obstructed the investigation into Arbery`s death by, quote, "showing favor and affection to a former police officer, Greg McMichael," and directing that his son Travis McMichael should not be placed under arrest.\par \par \uc2 Both men now stand accused of felony murder. I know you, Attorney Lee Merritt and Ahmaud Arbery`s parents held a press conference yesterday. How does this indictment impact this case?\par \par \uc2 CRUMP: It is so important not only for this case, Reverend Al, but also for other cases where the message is sent loud and clear that when prosecutors abuse their authority to put their hands on the scales of justice to show an utter lack of disrespect, and use their position to make sure that minority victims, black and brown victims are not given the same due process of the law that should be enjoyed by all American citizens, that they will be held accountable.\par \par \uc2 PA Jackie Johnson, that was clear from Attorney General \uc2 Chris Carr\uc2  of Georgia, he sent the message that she may not have killed Ahmaud Arbery, but I believe she certainly played a starring role in the cover up, Reverend Al, that you and I know that with their attorney, we sought over and over again to say that they saw the video on day one, we didn`t see it until 72 hours later. They saw on day one that those murdering duo, father and son had lynched this young black man for jogging while black.\par \par \uc2 And so this is huge. This is huge, Reverend Al.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes. And Ben, as you mentioned, POLITICSNATION is celebrating 10 years on the air. And looking back, it would be an understatement to call you a friend of the show and we`ve always been grateful to have your insights on Civil Rights abuses.\par \par \uc2 Going back to the first case that really gave our program its voice, the delayed investigation into the death of Trayvon Martin in the spring of 2012 and the eventual murder trial for that death. You`re no stranger to television, you brought me that case when it wasn`t even really nationally covered. And I hope that you recognize what we`ve tried to do here on this show to set ourselves apart for the last decade.\par \par \uc2 The audiences we`ve tried to speak to and speak for, of course, you`re a lawyer, so I`ll let you be the judge here.\par \par \uc2 CRUMP: Well, Reverend Al, I think without you and POLITICSNATION, the world would have never come to know Trayvon Martin and raise the consciousness of America leading into the \uc2 Black Lives Matter movement\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And we went from Trayvon Martin to George Floyd, and just thinking that decade, practically, how many families that we gave a voice to, Reverend Al. You as America`s Civil Rights leader and me as a lawyer championing a cause for justice.\par \par \uc2 I can think of Michael Brown in Ferguson. I can think both of Botham Jean in Texas, Terence Crutcher in Tulsa, Oklahoma, Pamela Turner in Houston, Texas -- so many families that really, Reverend Al, can never, ever say thank you enough and saying that their lives, their loved ones lives matter and Black Lives Matter.\par \par \uc2 So, I`m forever your pupil, you are my mentor. POLITICSNATION is a staple for leading the cause, making sure that America live up to its highest promise of life, liberty, and the pursuit of happiness.\par \par \uc2 [17:50:06]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Black America`s Attorney General, \uc2 Ben Crump\uc2 . Thank you, as always.\par \par \uc2 Up next, my final thoughts. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: A week ago today, last Saturday, tens of thousands of people marched with Martin Luther King, III and Arndrea King and I around voting rights in Washington to the U.S. Capitol Building. \uc2 CBS\uc2  estimated 50,000, I thought 20,000 when we started and it swelled.\par \par \uc2 Before the week was out though, before we got to the middle of the week, the Supreme Court refused to step in to protect women`s right to choose in Texas.\par \par \uc2 Now, some would say, what does one have to do with the other? Now, I`d say everything. Because the reason the Supreme Court could make that decision is because a President named \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  put three people on the Supreme Court and scores of people on the Federal bench.\par \par \uc2 Why? Because of voting.\par \par \uc2 If you don`t vote, people that are against our interest, whether we are women, whether we are LGBTQ, whether we are black, whether we are Latinx, whether we are Asian, Native American, they will operate against the interest of all of us.\par \par \uc2 That is why those that are fighting for women`s rights and voting rights and police reform must come together. We do not have to agree on all of the issues.\par \par \uc2 [17:55:13]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: But we have to agree that we must stand together to protect the interests of all Americans that are under assault by the same forces that want to limit our right to vote, limit women`s right to choose, and limit other people`s right to life, liberty, and the pursuit of happiness.\par \par \uc2 It is no accident. Texas was where voting rights and women`s rights were stationed that we see the most egregious activities.\par \par \uc2 That does it for me. Thanks for watching.\par \par \uc2 I`ll see you back here tomorrow at 5:00 p.m. Eastern for a special edition of POLITICSNATION, marking 10 years on the air.\par \par \uc2 It`s been a long journey, but I`m not done yet. We have some special guests and a few surprises in store.\par \par \uc2 I`ll see you there.\par \par \uc2 MSNBC news coverage continues at the top of the hour.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 YASMIN VOSSOUGHIAN, MSNBC HOST: Hi, everybody, welcome. Thank you for joining us. I`m Yasmin Vossoughian. The future of abortion rights is front and center and under attack.\par \par \uc2 [18:00:09]\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020210905eh9400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 All In for September 3, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes, Antonia Hylton \par \uc2 7,737 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 September 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [21:01:44]\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST: Welcome back to a special extended version of ALL IN. I am Chris Hayes.\par \par \uc2 This is day three of what is essentially the post Roe v. Wade era in America. For almost 50 years, the supporters of abortion rights, pro-choice forces, have been playing defense to protect abortion rights from a massive mobilized effort to destroy them. But in some ways the battle has switched literally overnight.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court basically waived through this Texas ban on abortion, so now the mobilization needs to switch in practical terms on the ground. Texas Republicans and the conservative justices on the Supreme Court have done something the majority of Americans do not want them to do.\par \par \uc2 I`m not even sure a majority of Texans would support this law, do support this law to be honest. I mean according to 2020 exit polling out of Texas, nearly 80 percent of voters think abortion should be legal, at least available in some instances. Only 15 percent say absolutely no abortions, which is pretty close to what this law does.\par \par \uc2 The law bans abortions once cardiac activity can be detected in the embryo, something that usually happens and six weeks, and that`s 18 weeks earlier than the legal standard set by Roe v. Wade in 1973 in that landmark 7-2 decision. It wasn`t a 5-4 decision, that Roe v. Wade decision. It was 7-2, easy enough to \uc2 Google\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And banning abortions after six weeks means essentially banning all abortions because many women do not find out they are pregnant in the first six weeks. In fact, when you have a legal threat hanging over people, it is unclear anyone is going to perform any kind of abortion no matter how early.\par \par \uc2 Believe you me, right now in Texas, it is an almost certainty there`s a teenage girl somewhere in that state who is pregnant by her abuser or by a family member or by someone who sexually assaulted her, she might be 14 or 15, who cannot get an abortion. That is a near certainty.\par \par \uc2 There are likely thousands of women who are pregnant who now cannot get an abortion because there are no exemptions in this law, none. This is functionally the most extreme kind of ban, the first of its kind. Essentially it is a complete ban, no exceptions for rape or incest. It is not enforced by state government, the people elected to enforce laws. We don`t want citizens running around enforcing murder laws or anything like that, right?\par \par \uc2 No, it is enforced by private citizen bounty hunters with a $10,000 check being dangled by the Texas government.\par \par \uc2 To help carry out the new law, the state`s largest anti-abortion group put out this completely creepy dystopian tip line.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We need your help. If you are on the sidewalks at the abortion mills or if you hold evidence of an abortion occurring after the baby`s heartbeat is detectable, you can anonymously report that at prolifewhistleblower.com. That`s prolifewhistleblower.com.\par \par \uc2 You can stop abortion by protecting women and children from unsafe criminal abortions. Those who worship at the altar of child sacrifice tried to shut down prolifewhistleblower.com, but the site is functioning and awaiting your findings.\par \par \uc2 [21:05:00]\par \par \uc2 Through civil enforcement, the Texas Heartbeat Act escapes the talons of activist judges by relying on the public to enforce the law on any abortionist who commits an abortion after the child`s heartbeat is detected is liable to be sued by almost anyone.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I`m going to say a few things about that. First of all, for a movement that talks about freedom and about how it is tyranny or Marxism to put a mask on, what would you describe a society in which your neighbors creep around spying on you to report out your health care decisions? Does that sound like a free society to you?\par \par \uc2 Now, I know it is hard to believe that video we played you is real. So is that e-mail. The group is trying to deputize private citizens to sue anyone who performs or aids an abortion or maybe intends to aid as the law says. The website she shared is for reporting people to be sued. Of course, it would be a shame if people spam it to complete ineffectiveness.\par \par \uc2 But this is all actually happening. I should tell you as we mentioned a couple of hours ago, a judge stopped that group with that incredible video from suing abortion providers and workers at Planned Parenthood under the new law. The ruling however does not affect the abortion ban in general. That`s still in place.\par \par \uc2 But again, things have changed now. It is incumbent upon people who value this right, whether they be rioters, intellectuals, activists, lawyers, voters, citizens, anyone who believes in the right to bodily autonomy and fully quality for women to fight this, to bring debate to the opposition to force them to defend this law.\par \par \uc2 We`re starting to see movement on that. A lot of people on the right recognize it is unpopular. They`ve opened themselves up. The law is indefensible. The mechanics of the law are indefensible. The actual law itself, complete abortion ban, as complicated as most people`s views on abortion might be, they are not complicated on how horrible the law is.\par \par \uc2 Today, Democrats in the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  announced they would hold a hearing to examine the Texas abortion ban and the Supreme Court`s abuse of its shadow docket.\par \par \uc2 Democratic Senator Mazie Hirono of Hawaii is a member of that committee and she joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Senator, what is the agenda for Senate Democrats to deal with this law?\par \par \uc2 SEN. MAZIE HIRONO (D-HI): Well, one of the things they`re going to do is to have a hearing on the Supreme Court`s abuse of power by abusing the shadow docket to do what they did. They`re letting the Texas law stand. I`m glad you used the word "abused" because that`s exactly what the Supreme Court is doing.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Speaker Pelosi said there will be a vote on a piece of legislation co-written by Ayanna Presley who was my guest on the program last night that would codify the Roe standard into law, into federal law. Is there appetite for that in the Senate? Obviously, we need to pass it in both houses for it to become law.\par \par \uc2 HIRONO: There`s an appetite for that and the companion bill that 48 Democratic senators signed. I am one of them. So when we have this hearing on the abuse of the shadow docket, what is going to come out I think is the need for Supreme Court reform and that --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What do you mean by court reform?\par \par \uc2 HIRONO: Court reform, the fact we have a Supreme Court abusing its powers so we can talk about term limits for the Supreme Court, we can talk about applying ethics provisions to the Supreme Court, we can increase the number on the Supreme Court, we can cycle circuit court judges through the Supreme Court. So I think what is going to happen is when it comes to -- when it becomes obvious that the Supreme Court, which, by the way, has used the shadow docket 28 times during the Trump years and only four times during the entire Bush and Obama terms, this is an abuse.\par \par \uc2 So you use the shadow docket where you don`t have to even say who is voting, who is, you know, doing the position that uses the shadow docket and there`s no hearing. There`s nothing. So there`s no accountability they hope, but in this case we have four justices who filed dissents on what the five other justices did.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Forty-eight, I can`t help but notice, is not 50.\par \par \uc2 HIRONO: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That seems to me -- well, that is sort of the issue, right? You have the filibuster but before you get to the filibuster, you can`t beat 52 votes with 48. I wonder if it changes the calculation of any of your Senate colleagues.\par \par \uc2 HIRONO: I hope so, because to me we need filibuster reform also. At least you have a shot at it if you don`t have to have 60 votes to codify Roe v. Wade at the federal level. We also need to codify it at the state level such as what the state of Hawaii did many years ago. There are a number of states that have codified Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 That should be happening at the state level, which is why all of the people I see marching now in the streets, having awakened to what is happening to women all across the country, I hope that they will make sure that their states codify Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 [21:10:06]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yeah, you make a good point here which is that in the same way that very conservative states have taken huge steps constantly to sort of push against abortion rights and abortion restrictions, there are opportunities for states that have elected representatives who believe in abortion rights to expand access and to enshrine and protect it.\par \par \uc2 HIRONO: Yes. And so this is why we need to be mobilized at all levels. It is not just Congress that can take action, but it is all of the state legislatures. And, as I said, when it becomes clear that this is actually a very activist, conservative Supreme Court where, you know, the rule of law and precedent out the window when they feel like it.\par \par \uc2 So \uc2 Citizens United\uc2 , that was -- you know, that opened the door to billions in dark money being spent in the Shelby County that has a major part of the voting rights law, to Janus which stymied the public sector unions and their ability to collect dues. These are all done by a very activist court peopled with folks like the Trump nominees, Trump appointees, all of whom I voted against.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Do you think -- there`s polling that indicates liberals and Democrats think highly of this court, they think highly of John Roberts. They approve of the court. They particularly approve of John Roberts. What do you think about that?\par \par \uc2 HIRONO: John Roberts at least cares about the institution of the Supreme Court. I think he cares that the Supreme Court is not seen as just some kind of ideological right wing court. I think he cares about that. But his colleagues do not, and clearly, the three people that are the Trump appointees, they basically do not. They are very ideologically driven in my view.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Senator Mazie Hirono of Hawaii, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 HIRONO: Thank you. Aloha.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: In 2017, even though Donald Trump Republicans controlled both chambers of Congress, they could not repeal the Affordable Care Act because of the intense public pressure whipped up by grassroots progressive groups like Indivisible, which took to the streets, called out their elected officials. So, what can people who support women`s rights do now?\par \par \uc2 Meagan Hatcher-Mays is director of democracy policy at Indivisible, and Amy Hagstrom Miller is the founder of Whole Woman`s Health. That`s the organization that challenged the Texas abortion ban in the Supreme Court and they both joined me now.\par \par \uc2 Amy, let me start with you because you are in Texas. There are so many people that want to do something, that feel so enraged and upset, rightly so, about this. What should they do? What should people who want to support you be doing?\par \par \uc2 AMY HAGSTROM MILLER, FOUNDER AND CEO, WHOLE WOMAN`S HEALTH: I think there`s a few things that we can ask people to do. The first would be to donate to Texas abortion funds that are helping folks get access to abortions in Texas and outside of Texas, AOC/ActBlue, and I think the other thing that seems a little more subtle but I think is extremely powerful is for us all to begin to talk about abortion through an affirmative, positive framework, about how access to safe abortion makes our communities healthier, how it has added value to people`s lifts, how people we know and love have had access to abortion that has made them be able to build and plan a future and to have healthy families.\par \par \uc2 I think that`s incredibly important. I think the Women`s Health Protection Act would be amazing, if we could use this momentum and this impetus to really get Congress to step in, and protect those of us who live in states where these kind of politics are constantly happening so that we can really secure the rights and really flesh out the full access for all people.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Meagan, what do you see as the sort of points of pressure here to react to this?\par \par \uc2 MEAGAN THATCHER-MAYS, DIRECTOR OF DEMOCRACY POLICY, INDIVISIBLE: Yeah, I think Amy is right. You know, the best thing you can do is -- I mean the best thing Congress can do is pass the Women`s Health Protection Act. As Senator Hirono was saying, not only do we have all 50 Democrats on it but we don`t have a commitment from all 50 to change the rules to get it done. Yet again the filibuster is being used to block a popular thing from going into effect.\par \par \uc2 But more importantly, this court is a disaster. The way that this court is currently made up cannot continue. So there really needs to be a serious conversation, not just about the shadow docket which nobody knows what that is, but about the court itself. So I`m glad to hear that Senator Hirono said they would be looking at expanding the number of seats on the court because there`s no universe in which these six conservatives are going to wake up one day and say, wait, I changed my mind about reproductive health care, I changed my mind about abortion, I`m okay with it now.\par \par \uc2 This has been a 50-year-long project on the part of the right to get us to this moment and now they`re here.\par \par \uc2 [21:15:04]\par \par \uc2 They`re not going to just change their minds. There has got to be something done in Congress about this court, and so that is going to be a really critical piece of the fight beyond codifying Roe at the federal level, making sure we can keep it by adding seats to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Amy, I want to ask you a question that you may not be at full liberty to discuss because of obviously the legal environment that you now find yourselves in because the Supreme Court has decided to allow this law to stand, but I will ask it anyway, which is what are the conversations among reproductive service providers like yourself like in Texas right now? What is your understanding of what you can and can`t do and are women -- are there any -- is there any place women are allowed to get abortions, can get abortions in Texas, or is the entire state essentially right now under a ban?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: I appreciate that question, Chris. It is complicated. I want folks to know that abortion is still legal in Texas, that clinics are still open all across the state including the four Holmes health clinics in Texas. We are offering care to the new legal limit of about six weeks into pregnancies and then we are also helping folks have ultra sounds and figure out how many weeks they might be into the pregnancy and trying to assist them to get the care that they deserve.\par \par \uc2 Nobody is more compassionate, more kind or more informed with the actual information that people need than the clinics in Texas and then the abortion funds in Texas to help people. There are some websites like Need an Abortion, and Avow and the Little Fund and a whole bunch of folks are keeping up the information that people need.\par \par \uc2 Our staff, our values, our commitment to this work hasn`t changed just because this law has changed. We have seen lots of people come in since this law was passed. Heartbreakingly, we have had to turn away quite a lot of people and it has been really, really difficult for our staff members who are there because of their commitment to this work, because of their commitment to the human rights and justice issue that is abortion, having to look people in the eyes and deny them care that they could provide just two or three days ago.\par \par \uc2 It is really -- it is really heartbreaking for all of us to be put in this position. It is unjust. It is cruel to put clinic workers and clinic staff in this position after all they have done during this pandemic as front line health care workers, to keep access to safe abortion available to people all across the state.\par \par \uc2 So I think it is encouraging to see how people are outraged. It is encouraged to see how many folks really know that this doesn`t represent the majority of people in Texas. This small group of people who are elected who have this power don`t represent the feelings and beliefs of the majority of people in this country, and surely not people in Texas. I think it is high time that the real Texas was paid attention to and our voices were heard, and we can respect and empower the activists on the ground in Texas who are doing some amazing work to bring about justice.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right. Meagan Hatcher-Mays and Amy Hagstrom Miller. Thank you both. I really appreciate it. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 MILLLER: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: This week, "\uc2 ProPublica\uc2 " uncovered a widespread movement of hard-core Trump supporters and conspiracy theorists organizing to take over election. In a piece called "Heeding Steve Bannon`s call," election deniers organized to cease control of the GOP and reshape America`s elections. And here`s the thing, thousands have been successful.\par \par \uc2 Next, the amazing details in that piece with one of the reporters who broke the story. You don`t want to miss it.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [21:22:30]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: We have talked at length on this show about how the insurrection did not end on January 6th and how Republican lawmakers are laying the ground work to overturn the next election. But a new report by "\uc2 ProPublica\uc2 " reveals how the effort is manifesting itself in unexpected ways.\par \par \uc2 They report at the urging of former Trump adviser and pardon recipient Steve Bannon and others, thousands of Trump supporters have taken over local election positions in swing states across the country.\par \par \uc2 According to the report, quote, the new movement is built entirely around Trump`s insistence that the electoral system failed in 2020 and that Republicans can`t let it happen again. The result is a nationwide groundswell of party activists whose central goal is not merely to win elections but the reshape their machinery. They feel President Trump was rightfully elected president and it was taken from him, said Michael Barnett, the GOP chairman in Palm Beach County, Florida, who has enthusiastically added 90 executive committee members this year.\par \par \uc2 They feel their involvement in upcoming elections will prevent things like this happening again.\par \par \uc2 So, what does it mean for the 2022, 2024 elections?\par \par \uc2 Isaac Arnsdorf is one of the "\uc2 ProPublica\uc2 " reporters who broke this story and he joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Isaac, it`s a great piece of reporting. First, let`s just start with Bannon`s role in all of this, what he has been kind of fomenting explicitly that people heeded the call for.\par \par \uc2 ISAAC ARNSDORF, \uc2 PROPUBLICA\uc2  REPORTER: The idea here is that because the 2020 election was stolen, Trump supporters need to take over the Republican Party to prevent that from happening again, and people listening are probably thinking, well, wait a minute, take over the Republican Party? Doesn`t Trump already control the Republican Party?\par \par \uc2 But according to this version of history, the reason that Trump isn`t president anymore was not just the widespread fraud, that was just the beginning, but the seconds piece is that Trump was sold out by the Republican Party, by the state legislators who didn`t throw the Electoral College votes to Trump, by the Republican members of Congress who didn`t vote to overturn the Electoral College results on January 6th, but the senators who voted -- by the members of Congress who voted to impeach him for inciting the insurrection.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, people like Brad Raffensperger in Georgia who certified the election results that Trump insisted was fraudulent, and so the idea here is that they need to take over the party from the bottom up, from the local level, giving them the power to elect the party officers higher up the chain, at the county, district, state and national level.\par \par \uc2 [21:25:01]\par \par \uc2 They need to get rid of everyone like Brad Raffensperger who doesn`t agree that the election was stolen, and they need to use these party powers to pass new voting restrictions like the laws in Georgia and Arizona, to have more audits like the one in Arizona, to expand the role of Republican- controlled state legislatures in running elections and controlling the outcome of elections.\par \par \uc2 And the way that they do this is through these precinct positions, which, you know, most voters have probably never heard of before, but these are just the lowest level positions in the party organization and they`re the worker bees of the party, so to speak. They`re the ground game. They`re the field organization. They`re knocking on doors. They`re making phone calls.\par \par \uc2 But not only that, they have these formal powers that give them a direct role in how elections are run, and the details are a little bit different in every state. But sometimes it is picking the poll workers, sometimes it is picking the members of the boards that oversee the elections, sometimes it is having a say in who actually gets on the ballot either because they`re collecting signatures or because they`re voting directly in nominating conventions instead of public primaries. So this was the plan that Bannon highlighted in February, and after he put it on his podcast, which is one of the most popular podcasts on apple, it has tens of millions of downloads.\par \par \uc2 After Steve Bannon`s podcast featured this, it went viral all across far- right media. I don`t mean FOX News and \uc2 Facebook\uc2 . I am talking about people like Steve Bannon, Michael Flynn, Lin Wood, Sidney Powell, sites like Telegram, and Gab, and QAnon talk shows, and this became the it thing everyone was talking about.\par \par \uc2 The key thing is they weren`t just talking about it on the internet. Thousands of people actually went out and did it. So what we found by calling around to dozens of key counties in competitive states, we`re talking about the states that decide presidential elections and who controls Congress, Arizona, Georgia, Florida, Texas, Pennsylvania, Michigan, Wisconsin, and we asked these county chairs, hey, all of a sudden are tons of people calling you asking to be precinct officials and mostly they said, yeah, we`ve never seen anything like this before.\par \par \uc2 Sometimes they frankly had no idea why it was happening, and sometimes they figured out that it was because of this viral episode of Steve Bannon`s podcast. Sometimes actually the county chair was a new county chair who had been elected by a lot of these newcomers to the party. So you are already seeing them start to put those powers to use and starting to have effects up the chain of the party organization.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So here is a key point I think that your article lays out and I think people need to understand, is that party positions are actually part of the election supervision architecture at the ground level in the U.S. So there will be these sort of -- you know, the county will have like a three and three, the Democrats and Republicans have six people that oversee, you know, these sort of -- these bipartisan boards.\par \par \uc2 So if the party official sitting there, you know, usually it is an equal amount for both parties. When you are talking about party positions, it is not just they`re party positions. They`re the people that end up playing these sort of official roles of election supervision.\par \par \uc2 ARNSDORF: That`s right. It is traditionally a gate keeping function. The reason that we have this entrenched two-party system and there aren`t third parties is because the parties have this entrenched legal, formal role over the nuts and bolts of American politics, and the actual running of elections. That`s exactly what this movement is targeting.\par \par \uc2 The lesson that they learned from last time is that the reason that Trump is not president anymore is not actually because of the widespread fraud by the Democrats, but it is because the Republicans, even though they knew the election was stolen, didn`t use these powers to stop it. The reason that they`re focused on taking over these positions now is to change that next time.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And we saw this. I mean, the place where this was most highlighted to me was in Michigan last time around because you had the Wayne County board of electors, whatever they are, right? Again, it was one of nose -- those, you know, I think it was three and three, three Republicans, three Democrats.\par \par \uc2 ARNSDORF: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Again, they`re playing -- the party is the one that assigns the people a role, but they`re playing this official role in the county. We remember, right, two of the Republicans voted not to certify the results, and Donald Trump called to congratulate them. They ended up backing down. Then it went up to the state board of elections I think.\par \par \uc2 [21:30:03]\par \par \uc2 And, again, there were several different Republicans I think who voted against certifying the results and one who did who I think subsequently got threatened and all of this stuff. I didn`t really think about the role, the leverage point, the kind of leverage point that these random party, you know, officials are in making sure an election gets certified.\par \par \uc2 ARNSDORF: Absolutely. I want to give you another example. So right next door in Wisconsin, the county parties actually nominate the poll workers. So the people who actually work in the polling place running the election. Historically, you know, and the law actually says whoever the party nominates, the county clerk who runs the election has to hire those party nominees.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Oh, wow.\par \par \uc2 ARNSDORF: Now, historically this really hasn`t been an issue because the parties kind of didn`t bother. You know, they were more interested in, well, we`re going to use our people on Election Day, you know, getting out the vote and that kind of thing. But now --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 ARNSDORF: -- several county parties have seen this and they said, you know what? We are going to stack these rolls with really hard-line Republicans and we`re going to make sure that they are in those polling places on the lookout for fraud.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yikes.\par \par \uc2 Yeah. You go to vote and you have the QAnon Shaman welcoming you in. It could be a bad scene.\par \par \uc2 Isaac Arnsdorf, really good reporting. Thanks for laying it out so clearly. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 ARNSDORF: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still ahead, the escalating fight in Texas over the way students can learn about race and slavery in the classroom, that`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [21:35:46]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The anti-abortion legislation in Texas is not the only example of the state Republicans` extremist right-wing politics. Republican Governor Abbott signed an extensive ban on critical race theory earlier this summer, but state Republicans weren`t satisfied with that. So they sent a second bill restricting how race and history are taught in Texas schools to the governor`s desk.\par \par \uc2 This culture war has been bubbling under the surface of Texas politics for a while now. You might remember one incident a few years ago from a town of Southlake, Texas, a small down in Dallas/Ft. Worth suburbs. And a video of white teenagers shouting the N-word went viral.\par \par \uc2 In response to that incident, as well as others, Southlake decided it would take action. It held listening session and created the Council on Diversity. And after all of the work, the school board unveiled a new proposal last summer, which would require diversity and inclusion training for all students. It would amend the student code of conduct to ban micro aggressions.\par \par \uc2 The backlash was swift. Outraged parents accused the school board of promoting Marxism and leftist indoctrination and the small Texas town became a microcosm for the larger fight over how we teach the legacy of racism in this country. The story of which is masterfully outlined in "Southlake" a new six-part podcast series from NBC News.\par \par \uc2 Antonia Hylton is an NBC News correspondent, one of the hosts of "Southlake" podcast, along with an NBC reporter Mike Hixenbaugh.\par \par \uc2 Antonia, welcome and congrats. It is a phenomenal piece of work. People should listen to it and binge it. It`s really, really good.\par \par \uc2 Tell us just a little bit about this town and how you started getting into the story.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA HYLTON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Thank you, Chris.\par \par \uc2 So, look, Southlake is one of the ground zero communities where this entire fight began. Because their fight got started in 2018, long before most people knew what the phrase critical race theory meant and long before we were all seeing these fights play out at school board meetings across the country. Initially this council comes together, it has conservative, liberal parents, everything in between and they get ready to present this diversity plan and it takes almost two years of work.\par \par \uc2 The plan is ready right in the months of George Floyd`s murder in which the political conversation around how to talk about race history in the classroom is entirely different than it was in 2018. So this document, despite how it had been worked on for so long by members of the community, is now in that politicized context and there`s a massive backlash. Parents raised hundreds of thousands of dollars and even launched a lawsuit that leads to a restraining order that stops the district from implementing any kind of diversity and inclusion training for kids in this large and very well-known school district in Texas.\par \par \uc2 So this opposition effort here though in this community in Southlake has really become representative of the movement we have seen around the community because the Republicans there, many of them are extremely well- connected. This is a town full of, you know, American airlines executives, bankers, and many of them are closely tied to politics in Texas.\par \par \uc2 And they launched this effort with a PAC on the local level, but they`re explicit about the fact they see school board seats as being a critical part of the political infrastructure, as a huge part of the way in which they can wield power and influence. They talk about that very openly in these months after the plan comes out.\par \par \uc2 I want to introduce you to one of the parents involved in this effort, this opposition work. His name is Juan Salvidar, a former army officer with a child in the school district there. He essentially believes that systemic racism is not real, that incidents like the video you saw there where kids were chanting the "N" word can be addressed on a case-by-case basis and he essentially refutes a lot of what the black and Asian minority families in town have been trying to tell about their experiences over the last couple of years.\par \par \uc2 So, I want you to listen to the conversation I had with him featured in our upcoming episode.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: I`ve spoken to several black families over the last couple of days, many of them are considering leaving town over this. When you hear that, does that not make you think there might be something systemic going on?\par \par \uc2 JUAN SALDIVAR: I think that every incident should be investigated to the fullest to find out exactly what`s going on.\par \par \uc2 [21:40:05]\par \par \uc2 But no one has any basis to make assumptions about what is in another person`s heart.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: What you heard Juan say there is pretty representative of what the opposition movement in Southlake has said about this plan and about its supporters all along, that essentially the plan is not going to be necessary in a community like Southlake and that they feel that even despite the testimonies that people have come forward, and in some cases hundreds of testimonies of racist incidents like that video you just saw there, that essentially the community already has the tools in its hands to deal with that and no comprehensive plan is needed in Southlake -- Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Can you talk about the racial makeup of the school and what you heard from black students and parents in the school district?\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: Well, this is a really fascinating suburb because I think people recognize it in their own communities all over the United States. This is a majority white town. The school district used to be 80 something percent white and over the last several years has gone down to 60 percent something white so it is rapidly diversifying, especially with Latino and South Asian families and an influx of some black families there.\par \par \uc2 What you hear when you speak to minority families, as more minority families have moved in the tension increased in the town. They hear from some white residents in Southlake that they feel the identity of Southlake, the true Texas roots of Southlake are now being threatened. As they come forward and try to have a dialogue about some of the racist harassment their students have faced at school, the comments, the exclusion that their kids complain about -- I mean, student after student has spoken to me and my co-host, Mike Hixenbaugh, and they often use the word isolation. They go through the school district and they feel completely alone the entire time.\par \par \uc2 They keep trying to tell these stories and they feel that their neighbors essentially have told them, you know, either get with the program, the culture of this town, or get out.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What does this sort of backlash mean in terms of the way the school is dealing with issues now? Have they managed to essentially curtail this proposed curriculum?\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: Well, the district`s hands are tied at the moment because the restraining order is in place that bars any kind of work on a diversity and inclusion plan. I mean, when you talk to administrators, even when you try to speak to them just on background they are afraid to talk to you about the details of the plan.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: I had an exclusive interview with the superintendent of the district, Dr. Lane Ledbetter, who has kind of become the face of the fight in town agent both sides expect him to bring unity despite the fact there`s this restraining order in place. So, he is in this incredibly difficult position in which he is charged with making students feel safe in this town but also is told he cannot do anything new, can`t bring any new diversity programming in to address the very clear and direct concerns students of color have brought forth.\par \par \uc2 So, he often feels like he is essentially walking into something like a land mine. You know, I asked him point-blank in our interview, for example, is there racism in Southlake. I didn`t say is all of Southlake racist, just is there any racism here and he essentially declined to answer the question and sort of gave me an answer about how people have different perspectives and we need to have empathy about that.\par \par \uc2 There have been multiple incidents of students using the "N" word in the district. His answer to the question, is there racism here, his inability to address it tells you about how toxic the discourse has gotten there, how challenging it is for people in positions of leadership who are dealing with parents who in many cases are refusing to communicate with each other, and somehow they`re supposed to forge the path forward.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Antonia Hylton, thank you coming on. The two episodes of "Southlake" are available now. Episode three comes out on Monday and you should check it out wherever you get your podcast.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Joe Manchin`s latest threat as the president visits Louisiana and surveys the damage from Hurricane Ida. The latest after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [21:48:18]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: President Biden traveled to Louisiana today to tour the damage left behind by Hurricane Ida. Nearly a million people are still without power and the heat index just topped 100 degrees. The president met with local leaders and emergency officials, and at a round table with Governor John Bel Edwards, he reiterated the need to address the climate crisis.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We have a significant piece of legislation, both infrastructure bill and a budget thing, a reconciliation bill, that calls for significant investment in being able to deal with what is about to come. In other words, for example, when you guys are putting back up those high-tension wires again and that`s working, you`re not going to put up the same exact system. You guys are going to have to build it better, and it`s resiliency.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That budget reconciliation bill the president mentioned included vital pieces of his climate initiative. It faces however new challenge. Democratic Senator Joe Manchin of West Virginia penned an op-ed in the "Wall Street Journal" yesterday of all places calling for a pause to the $3.5 trillion framework which he voted to pass just three weeks ago or at least continue ahead.\par \par \uc2 Greg Sargent writing in "The Washington Post" says Manchin`s move could have dire long-term consequences especially if it is not resolved before the November Climate Conference in Scotland. Quoting a foreign affairs expert, Sargent warns that a lack of commitment to the bill`s clean energy standard would send a signal to the world the U.S. isn`t taking this seriously and would embolden other nations to choose not to engage.\par \par \uc2 I want to turn to someone who has written extensively about the climate crisis, is currently leading a major research project on climate change and the future of cities, professor of sociology, Eric Klinenberg.\par \par \uc2 Eric, I`ve been thinking about you for the last few days because this is really the work that you devoted yourself to.\par \par \uc2 The first thing I want to say is, one thing that`s so striking when an extreme weather event happens is all sorts of features of the infrastructure become apparent in a way they weren`t before. So, in New York, you know, I never thought about the fact that the BQE in certain parts is down and the expressway is below. When it floods and those services become lakes, I hadn`t thought of that until I saw it turn to lakes. It gives you a hint of the scope of what has to be thought through as we think about all of our built infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 ERIC KLINENBERG, PROFESSOR OF SOCIOLOGY, NEW YORK UNIVERSITY: It can feel overwhelming when you are looking at one city like New York. The issue in the United States now is we have yet to do any comprehensive climate planning with respect to mitigation, converting our energy systems, so we depend more on renewables. Or when it comes to adaptation, we do things project by project, neighborhood by neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 And we notice, that place is really low. It`s a flood risk. We have to look granularly. We also have to look at the big picture.\par \par \uc2 This is a once in a lifetime moment we are in right now to turn things around. I heard Senator Manchin say, Congress needs to take a pause this week. I don`t know how your week was. In New York City, it didn`t feel like climate change took a pause. In the United States, it doesn`t feel like the health crisis has taken a pause. Now is our time to act.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: One other aspect of this that`s striking is the human toll. We have got I think up to 40 or over 40 people that lost their lives in this. That`s in the part of the country that wasn`t -- the one that wasn`t getting ready for the storm, down in the Gulf. This is in the Northeast.\par \par \uc2 Your first book was about this incredibly deadly heat wave in Chicago in which hundreds of people lost their lives. It points to how vulnerable human life can be to extreme events, particularly those that we don`t view as the big ones that we have to prepare for.\par \par \uc2 KLINENBERG: That`s right. There`s so many disasters that have been invisible. We haven`t recognized them. In some cases, they don`t have spectacular imagery. People die in heat waves not just because it`s very hot but also because we have a lot of very poor and very old and very isolated people who tend to suffer invisibly every day. They are at risk when a heat wave comes around.\par \par \uc2 Why did so many people die in New York City? Why did people drown in the floods? It`s partly because we have tens of thousands of people in New York City who live in effectively basement apartments. We call them garden apartments.\par \par \uc2 It`s a story about social class and social protection. It has become a kind of line in progressive circles that climate change is a social justice issue. I think that`s absolutely right. But we need to show the substance of that. We can`t just deal with climate as a weather problem. It`s connected to the everyday inequalities that organize American life. Chris, I think that`s why it`s so important that we own up to this moment with a policy agenda.\par \par \uc2 You know, when I hear some members of Congress say, let`s slow down a put the brakes on infrastructure, I hear them saying, let`s get through this one particular crisis we`re having this month and do everything we can to get back to the status quo. Let`s conserve the order of things now. I think there`s another big rising part of America. I think it`s the majority saying, we have seen enough. It`s time to hit the reset button.\par \par \uc2 We need a new model for how we get energy. We need a new model for how we do planning. We need a new model for how we deal with inequality. Frankly, Chris, I think that`s what`s at stake in this battle over infrastructure. That`s why this issue about how Manchin and Sinema are going to vote is so important.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Finally and quickly, I think we can focus on gloom and doom and it is terrible. But it is an opportunity, it`s a cliche, to build new stuff and try new things and reconfigure space. You have a great piece that I loved about a landscape engineer who is trying to grow a lot of oysters around New York, which turn out to help with dealing with storm surges. It just made me think, there are so many ways to re-conceptualize what our infrastructure is. It`s exciting in that way.\par \par \uc2 KLINENBERG: I mean, we should be asking ourselves, how happy were with our cities, our neighborhoods, the way we were living five, ten years ago? Before we really had this reckoning we are in.\par \par \uc2 [21:50:02]\par \par \uc2 I think a lot of us wanted do something different. Kate Orfa (ph) about whom I wrote, she`s one of the many designers who is saying, seizing the moment and doing something different around climate change is actually a way for us to make better cities, to make better communities, to give ourselves lives that are more meaningful and enriching than the ones before.\par \par \uc2 Maybe we are spending too much time going for speed and efficiency and the latest technology. Maybe we need to find better ways to be with each other. Her work is designed do that thing. This is a moment for thinking differently and building differently. I think that`s what`s exciting about being alive right now. It`s terrifying but there are great possibilities.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Eric Klinenberg, that piece is really a great piece. Thank you so much for making time tonight. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 KLINENBERG: Thank you, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is ALL IN for this evening. We`ll see you next week. Please have a great holiday weekend.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020210904eh930002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for September 3, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes, Paul Butler \par \uc2 9,113 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 September 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: That`s tonight`s REIDOUT. "ALL IN WITH CHRIS HAYES" starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (voice-over): Tonight on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It`s a continuing pandemic of the unvaccinated.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: While Republican governors for efforts to stem the carnage, there`s now indisputable proof that masks work. Dr. Anthony Fauci joins me live tonight.\par \par \uc2 Then as hospitals start to fill up around the country, the economy takes a hit too.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: There`s no question that Delta variant is why today`s job report is stronger.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Plus the urgent need to stop the destruction of Roe v. Wade, how the majority can step up to protect women`s rights. And as rioters get sentenced for storming the Capitol on January 6 --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He had a fondness for Trump that was not unlike the first love a man may have for a girl.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Steve Bannon is laying the groundwork to help steal the next election when ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. I saw COVID stat today that kind of blew my mind, I got to say. The vaccine, as we know, has been widely available for free to the public for about six months. And right now as I speak to you, current fatalities from COVID in this country are running roughly double what they were a year ago, before we had vaccines.\par \par \uc2 It seems an unspeakable tragedy. It is an unspeakable tragedy. It`s maddening and it`s confusing. I think it`s worth taking a second to understand where we are in the battle against this virus right now. About 62 percent of those eligible are fully vaccinated in this country, which means we have a whole nation within a nation of unvaccinated folks, right, tens of millions of people who aren`t vaccinated, not to mention children. And unvaccinated folks are often clustered in areas around each other because vaccination is not evenly distributed throughout the population.\par \par \uc2 There are areas like Gaines County, Texas, where less than 25 percent of people are fully vaccinated. And there are places like Middlesex County, Massachusetts where more than 75 percent of people are fully vaccinated. We also have a variant which we know from the data is more aggressive and more transmissible coupled with basically a total absence of public health measures to stop the spread.\par \par \uc2 I mean, if you ever wanted an informal study of the fact that yes, all those interventions last year that made life so constrained and difficult but they did save lives, well, we`ve gotten rid of all of them, and now twice as many people are dying.\par \par \uc2 But it is to an extent understandable the situation we find ourselves in because here`s the thing, once vaccines came along, it became very difficult to justify things like for instance, shutting down bars and nightclubs or concert venues or not having in-person school. I mean, school particularly, right?\par \par \uc2 But these are all important, valuable things. Shutting them down came at great cost. We felt that cost, a great cost to children, first and foremost. More broadly, a great cost the economy, a great cost to our mental well-being, massive societal disruption. So, when a vaccine came along, policymakers could no longer really justify the massive cost of those shutdowns.\par \par \uc2 But what that means is the unvaccinated in this country are currently living through a full pandemic, without any interventions. And this is what it looks like. It`s a horror show. And by the way, if you wanted to actual study that these interventions do work, we`ve got one of those too.\par \par \uc2 The largest randomized study of its kind looking at 340,000 people across 600 villages in Bangladesh found that yes, masks work at stopping the spread of the virus. I`ll say it again. Masks work. Of the 170,000 or so people encouraged to wear masks in the study, researcher saw masking rates increased 30 percent. And as NBC News reports, after the program was instituted, the researchers reported an 11.9 percent decrease in symptomatic COVID, right? So, masking goes up 30 percent, COVID goes down 12 percent. It`s pretty clear.\par \par \uc2 But this is the conundrum we`re in because access to vaccines, man, you can`t really go back to these massive large-scale social interventions. It`s harder. And so, the disease is just going to run rampant among the unvaccinated and it will end up infecting some vaccinated folks too.\par \par \uc2 Higher levels of unique transmission mean higher levels of breakthrough infections. It`s just a number game. And more breakthrough infections mean more of those rare cases where the fully vaccinated to get severely ill, and sometimes even die. This I think has become the fundamental challenge facing the Biden administration and our society in a way I think much of Washington and the political press corps have failed to grasp.\par \par \uc2 Because the problem here, right, there`s a mobilized core of the country that`s been whipped up into a deadly frenzy to oppose all interventions to stop COVID. And that`s well, unless it`s an intervention that is explicitly not being recommended, like a deworming agent, then they`re all into it.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:12]\par \par \uc2 For the first several months of this administration, the Biden -- the Biden folks had a very clear job. It`s their responsibility to work out the logistics to get the supply of vaccines out and into arms. And that was a very difficult task, and they 100 percent aced that test. They did.\par \par \uc2 And then the test got even more difficult, because it turned to the question of demand and human psychology and sociology and trust and whether people wanted to take the vaccine. And there they have run into a brick wall. A few days ago, on the show, we showed you some charts which show the COVID desk are significantly higher in counties that voted for Donald Trump. But now we have even more information that demonstrates that point.\par \par \uc2 For various reasons, it`s missing some data from Alaska, Nebraska, and Florida, the states that don`t report out by county. The takeaway, though, is pretty clear. Just look at that. It`s rare you get data looking like that. The little blue bars on the left, those are the per capita deaths in the counties that went the most for Joe Biden. And as we go from left to right, the counties get more conservative.\par \par \uc2 The big red bars on the right are the per capita deaths in counties that voted overwhelmingly for Donald Trump. And it`s just clear as day. The more support for Trump, the more people that are dying of COVID right now. That can change in the future. It`s possible. Partly this is geographical about where the Delta virus started and where it`s going. But right now, that`s the picture of the last few months. That is the picture of the havoc being reached by the Delta variant.\par \par \uc2 And given all that, it`s also important not to give up hope. It`s also important to remember this. Every day in this country, we`re vaccinating an average of more than 900,000 people, which means there are folks two days ago who decided to get vaccinated and three days ago and four days ago and woke up this morning and wanted to get vaccinated, almost a million of them.\par \par \uc2 There are people coming to this day by day by day. And the polling shows the amount of people that are going to definitely not get the vaccine is now at its lowest level. Also, very encouraging, it`s gone down to only 20 percent even when it feels like the situation is just overwhelming and defeating. Also, remember, there are people in the ground every day doing the crucial work. And there has been progress.\par \par \uc2 But we are now approaching the fall, schools are reopening in-person across the country. We all remember what last month looked like. The former FDA Commissioner is warning the worst is still yet to come.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 , FORMER COMMISSIONER, FDA: I think our true Delta waves going to start to build after Labor Day here in the northeast of northern part of the country. This is going to be a highly regionalized epidemic. And so, I do think that Labor Day and the return to school are going to be incubators for spread that`s going to lead to that delta wave. I don`t think that we`re done with this. I hope we are. But I don`t think that we can conclude that just because the cases are coming down from the mini surge that we saw over the summer.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, now, this is the biggest test yet for the American public, for the Biden administration, for all levels of government, Republican, Democrat, to find a way to have a safe and thriving fall and winter. Dr. Anthony Fauci is the chief medical adviser to President Joe Biden, Director of the National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases. For the past 18 months, he`s been the face of public health guidance in this country as we enter a potentially troubling fall and winter.\par \par \uc2 Let`s start, Dr. Fauci, with Scott Gottlieb`s view on this and whether you share it that we are in for the regional centers of this to move out of the sort of south and southwest that they`ve been in into the Northeast, and that it`s going to be a bad fall and winter.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY FAUCI, CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISER TO THE PRESIDENT: Well, what he said I agree with. I hope that`s not the case. But it does have the makings. When you get over a holiday weekend, like the Labor Day weekend, and then start going into the fall, you have children coming back to school on mass -- you know, there`d be hundreds of thousands if not millions of kids coming back to school. All of those ingredients together could actually lead to a resurgence of cases. And that`s what we`re concerned about.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What`s the magic number? I mean, I looked at number -- this has been this question all along, right? The thing I think we all want is for COVID to end up like polio and smallpox or like chickenpox, right? Just a thing that we don`t worry about anymore, extinguished, stamped out by the miracle of vaccination.\par \par \uc2 And there`s some number at which that happens. We are very clearly not there. I saw Singapore is up to 80 percent they seem to be repelling it. Like, do we know what that number is? And is that even a thing that we`re still thinking about as the policy, explicit policy North Star goal?\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: You know, Chris, we don`t know what that number is. But it`s -- you know, it`s interesting and it doesn`t mean to be facetious about it, but you`ll know what when you see it. You know, when you`re dealing with a disease like measles, it`s a very contagious, very transmissible disease. But the level of vaccination that you need in the community to get individuals to be free in the sense of having what we generally refer to as the concept of herd immunity, where you don`t have any vulnerable targets, or if you have very, very few, then the virus has no place to go. That`s generally what you talk about when you say herd immunity.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:26]\par \par \uc2 For measles, that`s over 90 percent. I don`t know what it is for SARS-CoV- 2. But you will know what it is when you get the overwhelming majority of people vaccinated, and you start to see the number of cases plummet. And when they do, and go down to the point of almost disappearing, whatever that percentage is, that`s what it is.\par \par \uc2 But rather than trying to figure out what the number is, I think we`ve got to do, what you were hinting at in your -- in your introduction to the show, is to say, we`ve got to get as many people vaccinated as we possibly can. We have somewhere around 80 million, 75-80 million people who are eligible to be vaccinated in this country who are not yet vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got to get them vaccinated because you see all the numbers show the vulnerability of unvaccinated people, which in fact, can spill over as you suggested into the vaccinated people. Because if you keep a lot of the virus dynamically circulating in the community, you`re going to get breakthrough infections among the vaccinated people, and some of them, very few we know, but some of them are going to get seriously ill.\par \par \uc2 So, it cuts both ways. You have the vulnerability of the unvaccinated and the threat by keeping the dynamics of the virus alive in the community, you have a threat to everyone, including the vaccinated people.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Let`s talk about the one population of unvaccinated Americans that are a topic of tremendous concern right now, which are children under 12.\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: Right.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: First of all, there`s been some criticism. The FDA is too slow on this. I imagine you don`t want to second guess the FDA. Lots of people on the internet like doing that. But do we have a sense of a timeline on children -- vaccination for children under 12?\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: Yes. And you have to look at it for -- let`s take two products, the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  product and the Moderna product. If you look at the clinical trials that are ongoing now with a data being collected for both safety and immunogenicity which is a surrogate of predicting that you would be protected against infection, right around the end of middle of end of September, very soon in the next few weeks, end of September or so, you`ll have enough data for \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 , for them to apply for an emergency use authorization.\par \par \uc2 Moderna is a little bit behind. They`re probably going to be the end of October, the beginning of November. At that point, when they submit to the FDA, remember, you can`t criticize the FDA for moving slowly. Unless the companies submit the data to the FDA, for example, to apply for emergency use authorization, when they do the FDA is going to make a determination, is this truly an emergency situation? And if it is, they`ll seriously consider an EUA.\par \par \uc2 That being the case, if they do that -- and that`s an if -- I don`t want to second guess them, then you could probably have approval for at least one of the products as you get into October. But again, you can`t get ahead of the FDA, but that`s at least the possibility.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: OK, that`s a useful -- the CDC releasing some data, I think, tomorrow which they previewed today on children and delta. And I want to read from part of it. The weekly COVID-19 associated hospitalization rate per 100,000 children adolescents during the week ending August 14, was nearly five times the rate during the week ending June 26 among children aged zero to four years. The weekly hospitalization rate during the week ending August 14 was nearly 10 times that during the week ending of June 26.\par \par \uc2 There`s a question here of is it the case that this is more dangerous for kids than previous variants? And is that what the data is saying?\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: It doesn`t necessarily say that, Chris, what it says we know Delta is a bad actor. It transmits extremely efficiently. So, you are seeing more people, everyone adults and children getting infected. The more children getting infected, a certain proportion of them are going to require hospitalization. And the more children get infected, the more kids you`re going to see that are going to require hospitalization.\par \par \uc2 We do not know yet. We don`t have enough data, Chris, to say that on a child by child basis, it`s much more severe Delta. It might be there`s some hints that it is more severe in adults. So, you might be able to extrapolate that maybe it`s going to be more severe in children. But from the data we have, the only thing we know is that more children are getting infected. And the more kids that get infected, more of them are going to wind up in the hospital. And that`s what that MMWR tells you.\par \par \uc2 [20:15:22]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I want to be really clear on this because it`s something parents are thinking about a lot. What I just read is the -- is the total hospitalization rate for all children. What you`re saying is that is going up because all children are getting much more COVID. COVID is moving through children because Delta is more transmissible. But it is not the case, it has not been definitively established that the percentage of children getting COVID -- there`s of kids that get COVID, there`s a higher likelihood of being hospitalized with COVID compared to previous rounds of the disease, which is the thing that parents are worried about, and as of yet is not definitively established by the data.\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: You are correct, Chris. That has not been definitively established by the data. What has been definitively established is what you said a moment ago and what I said that more children are getting infected, and therefore more of them are going to get hospitalized. We are struggling to get enough data to be able to make a determination of whether or not, as you said, for a number of kids infected is you getting relatively speaking more that are requiring hospitalization. We don`t know that yet.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Let`s talk about boosters. There`s been a little back and forth on this. And again, I want to be sympathetic to people that are making determinations under conditions of uncertainty and emerging data. There was -- it appeared an announcement of a recommendation for boosters for people that had had it been immunized I think nine months, after nine months.\par \par \uc2 Today, top federal officials have told the White House to scale back that plan to offer Coronavirus booster shots to the general public later this month saying that regulators need more time to collect and review all the necessary data according to people familiar with the discussion. What is your understanding of where this stands?\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: Well, we had a plan and you described it correctly that the plan was if all other things fell into place, that we could get boosters ready to roll out for people around eight months after the time that the earliest ones were vaccinated, and that would start rolling out the week of September the 20th.\par \par \uc2 We had hoped that we would be able to do it simultaneously with \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  and with Moderna. As it turns out now, the information necessary to do that with \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  is there. It still has to go through the recommendation of the \uc2 Advisory Committee on Immunization Practices\uc2 . It does not appear from a timing standpoint that the information that you would need for Moderna is going to be in time for September the 20th. It may be a delay for a few weeks, we don`t know.\par \par \uc2 But there is now with discordance between the two products as to what would be ready to be approved for a boost.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, Dr. Anthony Fauci, as always, I appreciate you taking the time to talk through this stuff with us and our viewers. Thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 FAUCI: Good to be with you, Chris. Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, the Texas abortion bill left in place by a narrow 5-4 majority the Supreme Court this week, is so extreme that Republicans won`t talk about it. And other states are playing to replicate the radical Texas law. There is now an urgent need for those supporting abortion rights to press their political advantage to stop these law from spreading because Texas is likely just the beginning. What can be done? After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: For decades, public opinion on abortion in America has been complicated and difficult to pin down. And it can be very, very finicky and responsive to how you ask the question. But for the most part, all right. Those who believe abortion should be illegal has consistently over the years been ahead of those who think it should be outlawed.\par \par \uc2 So, what abortions -- what opponents of abortion have tried to do when they`re sort of waging their battle for public opinion, is to focus on edge cases, for example, so-called partial-birth abortions, abortions that are very, very late-term, because that has less public support, and it brings the public more to their side.\par \par \uc2 But because the Texas law is so extreme, so cruel, and so dystopian, we`re seeing it become essentially indefensible. Texas appellate lawyer Raffi Melkonian makes this point on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  by highlighting the fact that according to the law, the statutory language, you can be sued if you intend to engage in the conduct of helping someone get an abortion.\par \par \uc2 Wait a second, think about that for a second. If you intend to engage in it? If you`re in Texas right now, and you`re sitting there and you`re thinking, I want to help someone get an abortion, you can be sued? That is, as Raffi Melkonian rightly points out, bananas when applied to constitutional rights.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, that can`t work. That`s not going to stand. And I think this is part of why most Republicans are not out there celebrating and defending the law, even the ones who helped write the damn thing. \uc2 BuzzFeed\uc2  news contacted all of Texas Republicans in the House of Representatives, and both of the state`s senators, out of a total of 25 officials -- OK, these are the congressional Republicans, just four responded. Only one referred to the case.\par \par \uc2 Now, the question is, how do those who believe in abortion rights want to push the issue on this odious bill and the ones that are sure to follow? \uc2 Nancy Northup\uc2  is the President and CEO of the Center for Reproductive Rights who had filed an emergency request to block the Texas abortion ban. Emily Kane is the executive director of Emily`s List, an organization that helps elect Democratic women in favor of abortion rights. And they both join me now.\par \par \uc2 I slightly misspoke above on the folks who are in the Texas congressional delegation. Obviously, they didn`t write this bill. That`s the Texas State House and state senate, so just wanted to correct that.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, we`ve got some news. There is actually a state court judge that has issued a sort of injunction directed to this specific class of people that just happened about an hour ago. Can you explain what just happened in the state court?\par \par \uc2 NANCY NORTHUP\uc2 , PRESIDENT AND CEO, CENTER FOR REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS: Sure. Well, let me just start by saying, of course, it`s been a devastating two days since the Supreme Court turned its back on the constitution and the people in the state of Texas and allowed this abortion ban -- near ban to go into effect. And so, it`s been chaos for people trying to get access to care. And of course, we`re looking at every legal option.\par \par \uc2 And today, in a lawsuit with Planned Parenthood and its affiliates filed in Texas against Texas Right to Life, they got an injunction today that stops Texas Right to Life from engaging in these lawsuits with respect to Planned Parenthood. So, it`s an interim ruling, but it`s a first step in showing that this law is absolutely out of bounds.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:40]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Emily, I want to read you from the Wall Street Journal op-ed today which I thought was interesting. This is the supporter of abortion restrictions, and in fact, getting rid of Roe v. Wade, who write this. Texas Republicans have handed Democrats a political grenade to hurt the anti-abortion causes. This is the editorial board.\par \par \uc2 Pro-life groups has spent nearly 50 years arguing that abortion is a political question to be settled in the States by public debate. Yet now, Texas, they want to use the courts via civil litigation to limit abortion. I do think this law is so frankly odious that more Republicans and those who oppose abortion rights should have to own it. And I wonder if you folks who work in the trenches of the political opinion on this feel the same way.\par \par \uc2 EMILY CAIN, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, EMILY`S LIST: Absolutely, Chris. Let`s start with the fact that the overwhelming majority of Americans are on the side of those who want to see and keep Roe versus Wade as the law of the land. That is what keeps women safe, what keeps people safe who needs reproductive care. This Texas law goes too far.\par \par \uc2 And what I would say to your listeners or your watchers is, do you know who your state senator is? Do you know who your state representative is? Do you know who your governor is and where they stand on this? Because Republican governors and legislators across the country are looking at this not as a grenade but as a playbook. They`re thinking, what can we do here in our state to rollback these important rights that affects so many families.\par \par \uc2 At Emily`s list, we`re working to hold them to account and I believe we believe that every single member of the legislature needs to be asked, where do you stand on this? They should be asked now, they should be asked again next year, and they will be asked by voters when voters come out just as they did after \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  was confirmed to the court in 2018. They will come out and be held to account at the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Nancy, you have been working in the legal trenches on this issue for years. And you know, I truly hate it when talking heads such as myself, you know, like spout off on like strategy. You`re like why don`t people do this. But just indulge me for a second because I have been thinking about this all day.\par \par \uc2 It just seems untenable to allow this to stand. Meaning, this blatant bad faith hack put together procedurally by the crafters of the law so that there`s no one to enjoin because it`s enforced civilly, and yadda, yadda, yadda. And then the five conservative justice say like, oh, yes, well, nothing to do here, and then no abortions in Texas. Like, it feels that the issue needs to be forced. It feels like someone needs to violate the law. Like, it can`t just be an abstraction. Is that crazy for me to think that that like, just to let this sort of stand seems nuts?\par \par \uc2 NORTHUP: Oh, well, we`re not going to let this just stand. As I said, we`re looking at every legal option. And as well, you know, it`s just been the change of events in the last, you know, 36 hours, because we now have, you know, the Biden administration, the president came out very strongly yesterday calling for a whole of government response.\par \par \uc2 We then heard from the Vice President, we heard from the HHS Secretary, we heard from the attorney general, we heard Nancy Pelosi say she is going to call for a vote on the Women`s Health Protection Act, which is a response that has been advocated for years in the Congress to this whack a mole that goes on in the States.\par \par \uc2 And we`re seeing people stand up. I just read that \uc2 Lyft\uc2  has said that they will cover the legal fees of drivers who take women to the clinics. People are funding, supporting the abortion funds in the state of Texas, they`re supporting the people in the clinics who are on the ground. So, all of that is tremendously important.\par \par \uc2 So, no one is just letting the stand. It is now the Supreme Court may have let us down. But from the federal government, to the grassroots, people are stepping up to ensure that we get abortion access restored in the state of Texas.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Emily, it also seems to me that there`s a salient issue here, which is, is abortion has been very high salience for a motivated part of the Republican base. And I think because Roe was the law of the land, they were sort of the insurgents to deconstruct the status quo. Those who are protecting the right to choose were defending what was the status quo even it was chipped away and Whole Woman`s Health and a whole -- through a variety of means.\par \par \uc2 But that seems like flip now. It just seems to me that every Democratic lawmaker should be talking about this law whenever they can. Like it`s a disgusting, despicable, bad faith piece of garbage that everyone in America should know about.\par \par \uc2 EMILY CAIN, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, EMILY`S LIST: I couldn`t agree more with your very technical description of the law. It`s terrible. It`s a -- it`s bad for women, it`s bad for families, it`s bad for communities. And it is the greatest threat to Roe that we`ve seen.\par \par \uc2 I mean, just this year, in 2021, we`ve seen hundreds of anti-abortion restrictions introduced at the state legislative level, more than any other year since Roe became the law of the land.\par \par \uc2 That is -- that is proof that this is not just arbitrary. This is, let`s throw it all and see what sticks, they got something to stick in Texas.\par \par \uc2 But now, they`re going to be asked about it because it is bananas to your point earlier. It is dangerous, it is reckless and it`s going to come in the form of all of the strategy that Nancy talked about on the policy side, on the legal side.\par \par \uc2 But it`s also going to come in the politics, and it`s going to come in voters because it`s not just Democrats who want people to be able to have safe and legal access to abortion, it`s independence. And it`s even some Republicans. It`s the majority of Americans.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why we`re going to see not only people stepping up to advocate and to this call of action moment for healthcare in America, but they`re going to show up at the ballot box and they`re going to organize and we will not back down.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: \uc2 Nancy Northup\uc2  and Emily Cain, thank you both for making time tonight.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Ahead, as one of the most well-recognized faces or torsos of the January 6th attack pleads guilty, will the enablers in Congress face any consequences? That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:36:06]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Perhaps one of the most recognizable insurrectionists from January 6th is named Jacob Chansley, you probably recognize him there on your screen. He`s of course the guy that stormed the Capitol with no shirt in the middle of winter, pretty fit dude, wearing face paint and a furry horned hat. He`s known as the QAnon shaman.\par \par \uc2 Chansley is an infamous QAnon conspiracy theorist who believed that Donald Trump would dismantle a mythical, fictitious though believe to be real by him, secret Society of pedophiles within the Democratic Party and Hollywood and the deep state, it goes on and on.\par \par \uc2 Images quickly circulated of Chansley inside the Senate Chamber. I mean, how can you not photograph that at the Senate desks (PH) and elsewhere around the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 A reporter for the New Yorker in fact caught Chansley on video sitting at the seat Vice President Mike Pence had just evacuated moments before.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACOB CHANSLEY, JANUARY 6TH INSURRECTIONIST: Any chance I could get you guys to leave the Senate wing?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We will. I been making sure they aren`t disrespecting the place.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: OK, just want to let you guys know, this is like the sacredest place.\par \par \uc2 CHANSLEY: I know, hey, I`m going to take a -- I`m going to take a, sit in this chair, because Mike Pence is traitor.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Got to do it for those \uc2 Instagram\uc2  photos, you know, when you`re there. Jacob Chansley was arrested just three days after the insurrection. He`s been in jail ever since. He originally pleaded not guilty to charges including violent entry, disorderly conduct and obstruction of official proceeding.\par \par \uc2 Although, given all that photographic evidence a little tough to keep that going. Today, he accepted a plea deal to a single charge of felony obstruction of an official proceeding. He will be sentenced to November. His lawyer maintains his client was nonviolent and peaceful and is dealing with genuine mental health issues.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALBERT WATKINS, DEFENSE ATTORNEY FOR JACOB CHANSLEY: He had a fondness for Trump, that was not unlike a first love a man may have for a girl or a girl for a man or a man for a man. (INAUDIBLE) The first love always, always maintains a tender and soft spot in the heart of the lover.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I guess you get whatever lawyer you can if you`re Jacob Chansley. Chansley is one of nearly 600 defendants charged in connection with the deadly Capital riot, one of the foot soldiers that`s going to pay the price.\par \par \uc2 Well, the people again, who encouraged the insurrection helped organize it have so far gotten off scot free.\par \par \uc2 Ryan Reilly is the HuffPost Senior Justice Reporter who has been covering these cases perhaps closer than anyone else. Paul Butler is a former federal prosecutor, professor of law at Georgetown University and an MSNBC Legal Analyst.\par \par \uc2 Ryan, as these cases have come in, this case, obviously has gotten a lot of attention because of how sort of instantly iconic the picture of Mr. Chansley was. But where does it stack up in terms of the spectrum of seriousness of the offense? And how the -- how the plea deal has worked?\par \par \uc2 RYAN REILLY, SENIOR JUSTICE REPORTER, HUFFPOST: Yes, so he -- I mean, he sort of gotten that more serious charge that interference with Congress essentially, because he entered that Senate well, and because you know, some of the speech and the actions that he took.\par \par \uc2 But I think this is a really interesting case because, you know, there`s been a number of defendants who have been arrested since this point who haven`t spent time behind bars pretrial.\par \par \uc2 And you really got to wonder like, if he wasn`t arrested at that time, perhaps if he had a different lawyer, whether he would have been incarcerated for this entire length of time.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know ultimately what the sentence is going to be. The sentencing guideline range the prosecutors expressed was in the range of 41 to 51 months, which, you know, given this conduct, which is non-violent, I think is -- you know, is a significant, significant sentence that, you know, is nothing to -- nothing to sneeze at.\par \par \uc2 The first defendant who of course went -- it got -- and pled guilty to a similar charge, the same charge in fact, ultimately got eight months in prison. And that`s basically what this defendant has already done at this point.\par \par \uc2 So, we`re really kind of at an infliction point where it`s kind of like, OK, if there`s going to be a deal that you`re probably going to reach soon, because ultimately, he could be sentenced to something that`s perhaps less time than he`s actually served in jail up until this point.\par \par \uc2 [20:40:01]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, a lot of folks have been released pending their trials. He has not.\par \par \uc2 Paul, I mean, just to take it back -- step back and think about sort of justice in a broader sense. I mean, I have to say 41 months seems excessive to me for someone that did not plan it and did not engage in violence there. It seems to me like those are things you should reserve for folks that did that kind of -- those kinds of actions, he`s done nine months. As someone who -- you know, was a prosecutor, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 PAUL BUTLER, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: I`m not mad about Chansley`s potential sentence. He was originally charged with six felonies and face up to 20 years in prison. He pled guilty to one and now he`s looking at four or five years. That`s a pretty good deal, because, make no mistake, Chansley is a stone-cold thug.\par \par \uc2 According to documents that were filed in court, he talked about hanging government officials who he considered traitors. At the insurrection, he used a bullhorn to demand police, turn over the Congresspeople to the insurrectionist.\par \par \uc2 And then, Chris, when he got dressed up in that fur and horns, he put himself out there. And now, prosecutors are making an example of him. They want the world to know who`s going to prison for four or five years, that`s a long time, it`s called deterrence. It`s an important message to anybody else who might be considering trying to overturn an election with a violent mob.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I guess it`s a good point, right, that if you -- to the extent that you like sort of go out of your way to become this sort of leader, symbolize, you also enhance the level of return value that comes with your sentence, because people will know of you and they`ll know what you did.\par \par \uc2 There is to me though -- and I forget who said this, it might have been Adam Serwer who was talking about Abu Ghraib when this all went down, where, of course, the folks that were actually in that prison, in Abu Ghraib, like they all faced charges, and they got sort of run through the criminal justice system, Donald Rumsfeld didn`t get anything.\par \par \uc2 Like, people at the top sort of walked away. And it does feel a little bit like that right now, Ryan, where you`ve got these folks that were, you know, not to excuse them at all, but these were people that were urged to go down to Capitol Hill by the president of the United States that were whipped into a frenzy by a whole bunch of folks who so far have escaped accountability while they`re the ones who are before the judge.\par \par \uc2 REILLY: Yes, I think that you definitely got a great point there. Because, you know, ultimately, if he gets 41 to 51 months, that would be a very, very significant sentence and I think probably more than maybe necessarily would be warranted given his behavior, which is -- which is overwhelmingly non-violent.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, this isn`t also the biggest, I think, probably the biggest, you know, issue in the country overall in terms of the criminal justice system.\par \par \uc2 You know, one case that I always think of whenever I -- whenever I sort of cover this is Josh Williams from Ferguson, who I think, you know, you probably know pretty well from back in the Ferguson days who --\par \par \uc2 You know, that`s a kid who was 18 years old, 19 years old. And I mean, he`s still in prison now. And he did eight years for a trash can. A trash can. He burned down a trash can. Like, that`s what we`re talking about here.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, meanwhile -- so, I mean, you know, comparison to what you`re talking about there, of course, it can`t be like sort of an eye to an eye situation, and you have to have make sure that the penalty is sort of in line with exactly, you know, the conduct and the behavior here.\par \par \uc2 And ultimately, there are a lot bigger, you know, offenders than this, you know, QAnon shaman who I think, you know, you can essentially compare to, you know, the Naked Cowboy in Times Square, who`s basically sort of this -- sort of the spirit animal or perhaps sort of mascot for the QAnon movement, but like, how serious was yet was he planning? Was he going ahead of -- you know, was he involved in any plans for storming the Capitol? Or was he just sort of like, you know, grabbing selfies.\par \par \uc2 And he`s sort of like a failed actor who wanted to get really involved in a lot of this and, you know, get a little attention for himself and, ultimately, you know, committed crimes. But is three to four years going to be, you know, something we really need to throw the book at someone for I don`t know?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, and I think the deterrent question really is quite live and operational right now, Paul, to your point, right? Because it`s like, none of this has gone away.\par \par \uc2 The big lies being like, cultivated every day. The idea that like, Democrats are going to steal it again. I`m watching, you know, in the California recall, there`s these wild viral rumors.\par \par \uc2 Like, you do got to create some strong messages to people that this kind of behavior will lead to very serious punishment, just as a just basic means of preserving civil peace.\par \par \uc2 BUTLER: Absolutely. So, five years sentence, it`s a long time in federal prison. I`m not sure it`s actually enough time for QAnon when we know that 50 percent of people who are locked up in federal prison are there for non- violent drug crimes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 BUTLER: Many serving way more time than five years. So, QAnon and the other insurrectionists are not the best faces for sentencing reform.\par \par \uc2 But think of three groups in the insurrection. So, they`re the MAGA tourists who illegally entered the Capitol, but they didn`t wild out once they were there. And then, there are instigators and organizers and leaders who have more culpability, they should suffer more severe consequences.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:06]\par \par \uc2 BUTLER: That group I think does include QAnon. But it also might include some Republican Congressmen, their complicity still hasn`t been fully investigated. And then they`re the people who plan and finance the insurrection, they should get the most time.\par \par \uc2 And finally, Chris, there`s the former president of the United States, I don`t think Trump necessarily has to be charged with treason or sedition. But it`s a crime to incite a riot and Trump committed that crime on national television. Every day that he`s not held accountable is an insult to equal justice under the law.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well said. Paul Butler and Ryan Reilly, thank you so much for making time tonight. Have a good weekend.\par \par \uc2 Next, the latest jobs numbers came in far lower than expected, what the Delta surge had to do with it and what that means for the rebounding a COVID economy, coming.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:03]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Today, the first Friday of the month is when the federal government releases the latest jobs report and it details hiring and unemployment in the past month, August, among other measures. And the top-line number we got was not great. The economy added just 235,000 jobs last month, that`s almost half a million jobs fewer than the 720,000 that economists expected.\par \par \uc2 While that number may be disappointing, it was not exactly surprising, as the president pointed out in his remarks this morning.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: There`s no question that Delta variant is why today`s job report isn`t stronger. I know people were looking and I was hoping for a higher number.\par \par \uc2 But next week, I`ll lay out the next steps that are going to -- we`re going to need to combat the Delta variant to address some of those fears and concerns.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Betsey Stevenson is a former member of President Obama`s Council of Economic Advisers. She`s a Professor of Public Policy and Economics at the University of Michigan`s Ford School of Public Policy, and she joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Betsey, I guess we`ll just start with just the basic idea here that Joe Biden said a ton on the campaign and as president, which is, you can`t -- like, there is no get the economy back without dealing with the pandemic and the pandemic flared up again. And so, then here we are.\par \par \uc2 BETSEY STEVENSON, FORMER MEMBER, PRESIDENT OBAMA`S COUNCIL OF ECONOMIC ADVISERS: Right, the virus drives the economics here, it always has, and it`s going to continue to do that. And when we see case numbers that are rising -- hospitalizations that are rising, I actually think the surprising thing is how well the economy is doing in the face of that if you compare that to how the economy did at the beginning of this pandemic, when we saw, you know, case rates that were much lower than even what we`re seeing right now.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I mean, that speaks, I think, to the sort of Catch-22, I articulated at the top of the show, which is that because of the presence of widely available vaccines, we`re not seeing those big policy, you know, interventions, like closed down all the bars in the city or closed down venues, because that just seems unfair, right? When people can choose to get vaccinated, you could do it relatively safely.\par \par \uc2 So, in that respect, I mean, I saw that airline numbers were down -- August, travel was down like 33 percent from 2019. And I thought to myself, I think that`s less of a drop than I would have guessed, you know, that if we`re still two-thirds of pre-pandemic, that`s obviously a big decline, but it`s bigger than I think I would have guessed.\par \par \uc2 STEVENSON: Well, you know, I think the thing to realize is that it`s never really been about what the governors or the local policy decisions were. It`s really about what people do to keep themselves safe.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Correct.\par \par \uc2 STEVENSON: And I think there`s a lot of people out there that are vaccinated, but they`re hearing about breakthrough cases. And maybe they`re not worried about themselves, because we know those breakthrough cases are unlikely to land you in the hospital and lead to death. So, we`re insured if you`re vaccinated against the worst outcomes.\par \par \uc2 But if you`re like me, and you have kids who aren`t eligible vaccinated, well, then you want to be extra cautious, because you don`t want to bring COVID home.\par \par \uc2 So, I think what we`re seeing is people starting to pull back in terms of what they`re doing. And we`re seeing companies worried about what people are going to choose to do. And so, they are hesitating to bring people back in the office.\par \par \uc2 And that airline travel you`re talking about, I think that people are pulling back on that because it`s just more exposure than they want, given how rampant and contagious the Delta variant is.\par \par \uc2 And so, we`re going to have to get either a higher vaccination rate, start to vaccinate our kids or get enough people who`ve had COVID, that we actually get something that brings this a little bit more under control before we see the economy pick up again.\par \par \uc2 It`s going to pick up again, but we`re in a little bit of a lull right now, because cases are really growing.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And there`s a real concern about unemployment insurance. I mean, you know, we had this situation where there was the unemployment insurance benefit, which is a bonus above what you would be -- what the calculated U.I. would be. It was $600, it went down to $300. And now, it`s going to phase out.\par \par \uc2 And you know, it`s going to phase out at a time when like, the economy is not roaring, and there`s worry that people just going to cause a lot of misery. Where are you at on that?\par \par \uc2 STEVENSON: Well, first of all, I think everybody`s focused on that $300 too much. I`m focused on the people are going to lose benefits 100 percent.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, let me be clear, there`s two -- yes, there`s two issues here, there`s the -- there`s the end of the bonus. But there`s a bunch of people who have been on an extension of U.I. that`s pandemic related past the normal expiration date that are going to hit an expiration days cliff.\par \par \uc2 STEVENSON: That`s the majority of the people on U.I. right now. So, let`s be clear, we`re not talking about a small group of people. We`re talking about the majority of people getting U.I. are people who are on one of these expanded programs that covers more people. People who aren`t typically covered either, because their earnings weren`t quite high enough to be covered or because they`re an independent contractor, they`re self- employed. There`s reasons why they`re not being covered.\par \par \uc2 And I think that it`s really important to realize that when you go down to zero, it`s very hard to pay your rent and put food on the table.\par \par \uc2 So, those are the people I am absolutely most worried about.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:10]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, and that`s also like, I mean, obviously, it`s a human story, because those people are in trouble. It`s also not great macroeconomically, because it`s just a lot of money flowing into the economy for things like paying rent or buying clothes or whatever. That`s not there.\par \par \uc2 The one -- the one kind of bizarre upside to me -- excuse me, is that I keep worrying the Feds going to hike rates, and there`s going to be pressure. And I`ve seen some of the Fed governors saying, like, well, we`re getting to the point where we got to hike rates.\par \par \uc2 And I do think in a weird way, what ends up happening is these kinds of job reports in the long run, sort of punt off, you know, fed tightening in a way that might end up being salutary for the long-term trajectory of the recovery.\par \par \uc2 STEVENSON: I mean, I think it`s going to put off Fed tightening wisdom, put on Fed tightening, because the economy`s not that strong.\par \par \uc2 So, I`m not glad that the economy is not that strong, this is going to put on Fed tightening. I`m glad that the Fed is so responsive and is able to really respond on a dime to the kind of news that comes out today and say, hey, you know what, the economy still need some support, we`re not really ready to do the kind of tightening that we`d like to be able to do going forward.\par \par \uc2 You know, I -- so I think the good news there is that we have flexible monetary policy. I love us to have flexible fiscal policy. I`d love us to have an unemployment insurance system that understood that we`re just not ready to kick a lot of people off of unemployment insurance. And that is going to end up hurting us more than it helps.\par \par \uc2 Now, if you think about sort of the balance of unemployment insurance is we want to create incentives for people to get out there and get jobs. I get that, I think it`s really important we create those incentives for people to get jobs. I also think we make sure that people can eat and that our economy has enough money flowing into it.\par \par \uc2 And when we have the pandemic on the rise in cases surging, we need to prioritize making sure people can eat and making sure that there`s enough money flowing through our economy, that they can pay their rent, that we don`t see evictions. Those should be our priorities right now.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Totally agree with that. Betsey Stevenson, thanks so much for making time. Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 You at home -- you at home on your holiday weekend. You`re not working right now. You`re watching television. That`s cool.\par \par \uc2 Don`t go anywhere because tonight we have a rare treat, a limited edition to our ALL IN special tonight, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, the one the only Senator Mazie Hirono on the Supreme Court decision. An unsettling report of Trump supporters trying to reshape America`s elections at the ground level.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Joe Manson`s latest threat to the Biden agenda in the fight against the climate crisis. All that more coming up with yours truly after this, so do not go anywhere.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020210904eh9300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Last Word with Lawrence O`Donnell for September 2, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lawrence O`Donnell, Maria Teresa Kumar, Ali Velshi \par \uc2 6,938 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 September 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:08]\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 And I decided today why I would be a terrible Supreme Court justice. And that is I would struggle with the muted language that they use like, for example, the word "unprecedented," which the dissenters use repeatedly in their reaction to this Texas abortion law. I called it insane last night. Reduced to one word, that was my judicial opinion about the Texas abortion law.\par \par \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2 , she was pretty great because she departed from the unprecedented, gentility language. And so, I`m really thankful for that because she kind of speak of those of us who are frustrated by the limits on language and the way those guys reacted.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST: Her first line is "the court`s order is stunning".\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: And the whole dissent is amazing. It is only three and a half pages. Everybody should read it.\par \par \uc2 She says, the court should not be so content to ignore its constitutional obligation to protect not only the rights of women but also the sanctity of its precedent, and the rule of law, period. I dissent, period.\par \par \uc2 Now, Justin Kagan`s dissent will also give you blisters. But at the end of Kagan`s dissent, she says, I respectfully dissent. Sotomayor has even turned that out, actually, I just dissent.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yeah, I would forget to use that word "respectfully" a lot. I just, oops,. oops, I forgot to say respectfully, I`m very sorry. We will get into it tonight. We`re going to get into it. It is -- it`s one of those stories that continuing to -- you get a night`s sleep on this story and it is either just as bad or worse the next day. There is just more to say, more to get into about it. It is really a horrible, horrible situation.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: And in practical terms. We were discussing this with Dahlia Lithwick earlier not hour, Lawrence. I said to her bluntly, is there any reason we shouldn`t expect all the other red states to immediately enact copy cat laws to have new abortion laws in new states insistently because of this. She says there is no reason not to expect that.\par \par \uc2 So note in each passing day, whether it`s south Dakota, or Florida, or wherever else it`s going to be, the place they`re already working on it, we`re going to see red states all over the country ban abortion using this as the \uc2 Xerox\uc2  template, and it`s going to go fast.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yeah. Just start with every Republican governor who is running for president. They are demanding of their legislatures right now. Send me one of those to sign immediately.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Yeah, exactly. Even as the country doesn`t want abortion laws made more strict, let alone wants an abortion banned. I mean, there is a reason that folks in the conservative media aren`t talking about this right now, as the dog that caught the car. They like talking about trying to achieve this now that they realize the country is against this and the backlash is going to be considerable.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We will see. Thank you, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Thanks, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, Justice Sotomayor said new Texas law creates citizen bounty hunters. Here`s the way the new Texas abortion law works. If you are a girl who grows up in Texas and in college meets the person who you think is the love of your life and you have a loving way in every way that`s important to you and six months or a year into that intimacy, you share the worst thing that`s ever happened to you and you tell your partner a few years ago, your father raped you, and you became pregnant and you went to Planned Parenthood and they helped you end the pregnant, and your partner is very supporting and loving and through tears says everything right in that moment.\par \par \uc2 But for reasons having nothing to do with that moment, just the normal twists and turns of young romance or romance of any age, you eventually feel yourself drifting apart from that person and at some point you break up. And your partner does not take it well. Your partner becomes enraged.\par \par \uc2 And so, this person you thought you loved, this person who you thought loved you becomes a citizen bounty hunter and files a lawsuit in a Texas court to sue every one involved who provided abortion services to you -- the people who helped you when you needed them the most, the people who helped you in the darkest moments of your life.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:11]\par \par \uc2 Four years, every day for four years after that abortion is performed, everyone involved in performing that abortion can be sued at any time in those four years.\par \par \uc2 If the citizen bounty hunter who betrays you wins the lawsuit because four years later, the records aren`t clear on whether a test was done to establish any cardiac activity in the embryo, then everyone involved in that abortion except the pregnant woman is going to have to pay damages of at least $10,000 with no limit on those damages, all paid to a person who betrayed your confidence, who took your most painful experience to court for money.\par \par \uc2 And the people involved in performing that abortion are going to have to pay the attorneys` fees of these citizen bounty hunters, who betrayed that intimacy to go into a Texas courtroom and sue the people who ended the pregnancy that was caused by rape and incest. The Texas law does not make an exception for cases of rape or incest or both because that is a matter of Republican religion. It wasn`t always that way.\par \par \uc2 The first abortion language to appear in a Republican platform was in 1976 in the platform called for, quote, the continuance of the public dialogue on abortion and supports the effort of those who seek enactment of a constitutional amendment to restore protection of the right to life for unborn children. Now, Republicans knew they would not be a constitutional amendment. They were just trying to appeal to the anti-abortion. That`s all.\par \par \uc2 The last debate among Republicans about what their antiabortion position should be, what should be on exemptions for rape and incest, was 21 years ago when \uc2 John McCain\uc2  and \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  were running against each other for the Republican presidential nomination. By that time, the Republican platform opposed all abortions without exceptions for rape and incest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN MCCAIN\uc2  (R-AZ): George, do you believe there is an exception for rape and incest and life of the mother?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE W. BUSH\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MCCAIN: Yeah, it is interesting. Here is the one that says, that \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  supports the pro-life plank.\par \par \uc2 BUSH: I do.\par \par \uc2 MCCAIN: So, in other words, your position is that you believe there is an exception for rape and incest and life of the mother, but you want the platform you are leading to have no exception.\par \par \uc2 BUSH: I will. I will. It doesn`t talk about what specifically should be in the constitutional amendment.\par \par \uc2 Please let me finish, John. Let me finish. The platform speaks about a constitutional amendment. It doesn`t refer to how that constitutional amendment ought to be defined. John --\par \par \uc2 MCCAIN: Read the platform. It has no exceptions.\par \par \uc2 BUSH: John, I think we need to keep the platform the way it is. This is the pro life party --\par \par \uc2 MCCAIN: When you are contradicting --\par \par \uc2 BUSH: May I finish, please? Please. We need to be a pro-life party. We need to say life is precious. That`s what that platform refers to. That`s why we need to leave it the same.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: During that campaign, \uc2 John McCain\uc2  was asked by a reporter in his campaign bush if he would tell his then 15-year-old daughter that she could not have an abortion if she became pregnant, and \uc2 John McCain\uc2  said, no, I would discuss this issue with Cindy and Meghan and this would be a private decision that we would share within our family. Obviously, I would encourage her to know that that baby would be brought up in a loving family. The final decision would be made by Meghan with our advice and counsel. And I think that`s such a private matter.\par \par \uc2 And all reasonable people agreed with that answer. But immediately a panic set in among the McCain campaign staff over that answer, and \uc2 John McCain\uc2  took it back. "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " report of that day says, quote, a short time later, Mr. McCain telephoned reporters and said, "I misspoke. What I believe I was saying and intended to say is this that this is a family decision. The family decision will be made by the family, not by Meghan alone."\par \par \uc2 Eight years later, \uc2 John McCain\uc2  was the Republican presidential nominee of the party that believes in no exceptions for rape or incest or both, and he chose as his vice presidential nominee the governor of Alaska who fully embraced the Republican doctrine of no exceptions.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:00]\par \par \uc2 Four years later, the Republican nominee was \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2 , who embraced the Republican doctrine of no exceptions. When \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  was running in Massachusetts, he ran as a full supporter of Roe v. Wade. He went from completely pro-choice to completely anti-abortion with no exceptions when he became the Republican presidential nominee. He changed his position to comply with the demands of politics.\par \par \uc2 John McCain\uc2  changed his position to comply with the demands of Republican politics. George W.H. Bush changed his position from a supporter of Planned Parenthood as a Texas congressman, to the standard Republican anti-abortion position, to become Ronald Reagan`s running mater in 1980.\par \par \uc2 Those are not deeply held beliefs. Those are political briefs adopted in service of ambition. And as the Republican Party became ever more insistent on the absolutism of their no exceptions on abortion, young Republicans in law schools dreaming of becoming Supreme Court justices knew what they had to think in order to become Supreme Court justices chosen by Republican presidents, and so you have the majority of the Supreme Court tonight who have said to the women of Texas, if you are a rape victim, if you are an incest victim and if you end a pregnancy that results from those crimes, everyone involved in ending that pregnancy can be sued, and that is why you won`t be able to end those pregnancies in Texas, because abortion providers cannot run the risk of losing their homes to provide services to women in need.\par \par \uc2 People working in abortion services have been taking their lives in their hands for decades in this country, people who claim all life is precious have shot and killed abortion providers in this country. And now this. Now they want to bankrupt them.\par \par \uc2 Supreme Court justices appointed by George H.W. Bush and \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  have delivered whatever Republican presidential nominee since \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  promised their voters they would deliver. It may be that all of the Republican presidential candidates who ran as anti-abortion candidates with no exceptions did not believe what they were saying. But the Supreme Court justices chosen by Republican presidents are now enforcing the words that those presidents at least pretended to believe.\par \par \uc2 Leading off our discussion tonight are Carol Sanger, Columbia University law professor and author of the book "About Abortion: Terminating Pregnancy in the 21st Century." Also with us, Maria Teresa Kumar, president and CEO of Voter Latino. She`s an MSNBC contributor.\par \par \uc2 And, Professor Sanger, let me begin with you. This evolution of Republican thinking on abortion obviously is something that law students with judicial ambitions were paying attention to, knowing where they would have to go on this if they were to make it on to the federal bench and above. And it seems to me that the message, whether the politicians believe what they are saying or not has been accepted by the judiciary they appointed.\par \par \uc2 CAROL SANGER, LAW PROFESSOR, COLUMBIA UNIVERSITY: Well, I don`t -- I try to keep Republican and Democratic politics out of the class, but there certainly are interest groups now that Constitution Law Society and the Federal Society that support pro-life and pro-choice provisions. And I`m not sure that`s what you`re asking.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Maria Teresa Kumar, I want to get your reaction to the situation in Texas tonight with providers basically shutting down.\par \par \uc2 MARIA TERESA KUMAR, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: This is incredibly dangerous because what you`re doing is that you are putting so many women`s lives at risk, and women that you are putting at risk are might be able to go into a different state to do so. But you are putting at risk the women most vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 Let`s be clear, Lawrence. The women there seeking abortion in the second trimester are oftentimes women that have found that there is something wrong with the fetus or they find out within six months they`re pregnant and realizing the best decisions for themselves and for their family and for their existing children is to make the hard, personal decision of ending a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 What the Republicans have done today is telling women that not only do they not have agency over their body, but they`re also telling them that they do not know how to best value their priorities to make sure that their existing children and family are safe.\par \par \uc2 [22:15:00]\par \par \uc2 This is incredibly devastating, and we cannot take it lightly because the fight not only continues, but it demonstrates what the stakes are when it comes to election even in the midterm because everything that we are talking about when it comes to women`s ownership of their body, what we can do when it comes to the ability to communicate our health and our priorities has been basically tried to be stripped away. The fact that Justice Roberts said this decision was unprecedented and he sided with Sotomayor, he sided with Kagan, demonstrate that this is not only highly unusual, but they have politicized the Supreme Court in the era of the woman`s right to choose.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And, Professor Sanger, in Justice Breyer`s dissent, he referred to running the financial and other risks that come from waiting for a private person to sue them under the Texas law. And the Texas law, as I said, gives them four years. And, so, for 48 months after every abortion that might be performed, if any are performed under this law, everyone involved would be waiting the 48 months to find out if they`re going to be sued.\par \par \uc2 SANGER: Yes. And what I think we see from that is the kind of -- that has a huge societal and cultural implications. It turns -- it turns a community into something like a surveillance state.\par \par \uc2 And it`s not clear yet what kind of evidence you have to show or bring in, but we know that if you are going to get your $10,000, you`ve got to prove that this was an illegal abortion, so you have to have records. You have to have calendars. You have to have dates. You have to have testimony. All this is being assembled.\par \par \uc2 And it`s a very -- it`s very un-community-like and gives a financial incentive to hunt down your neighbor, your cousin, your friends for financial reasons or, perhaps, for true devout beliefs. But in any case, it is not the kind of culture that we pride ourselves on having.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, Breyer has that right. It is a long time to be under -- to be under suspicion. And women who have abortions even outside of Texas find that abortions come back to haunt them in future aspects of their lives, like abortion -- early abortion can come up in a family law custody fight and there are a number of examples of that. An early abortion can come up in a job interview, even though that`s not supposed to happen.\par \par \uc2 So abortion is a -- an excellent tool to hold over women`s heads at various points long after the event and that`s -- that`s one of the -- that`s one of the powerful things about having such an abortion ridden culture, is that it stays with everyone in a very negative way.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And, Maria Teresa, my point about the citizen bounty hunter as Justice Sonja Sotomayor call them, that they might not have to hunt. It could be an intimate. IT could be your sibling. It could be someone you make the mistake of trusting.\par \par \uc2 At some point in those four years and you share this secret with that person and at some point down the road for whatever reason, that person gets angry with you and decides, okay, I know how to get back of the you. I`m going to go into court and I`m going to be the person who sues over the abortion you told me about in a moment of intimate sharing.\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: Well, Lawrence, you are describing weaponizing a woman`s most vulnerable moment. That should send chills up and down everybody`s spine. The possibly that all of a sudden in the most intimate, most vulnerable moment you share something with someone that was heart wrenching for you to make that decision and then they turn around and weaponize is not only immoral in so many ways, but it also makes women vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 Folks know that oftentimes when an abortion happens, women look for other services because it is a difficult decision so they can have a conversation on mental illness and mental wellness and this will prevent many of those conversations from happening and we need to make sure we`re providing them with the full support.\par \par \uc2 And the idea that your neighbor or your lover has the ability to hold something over your head that was a very private decision, not only creates a cruelty, but it creates a gender imbalance where women once again are not on the same level playing field as men and are put instead in a place where they cannot fully exercise their agency and their ability to choose.\par \par \uc2 [22:20:16]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Maria Teresa Kumar and Professor Carol Sanger, thank you both very much for starting off our discussion tonight.\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: Thank you, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, Harvard`s constitutional law professor Laurence Tribe will join us next on the Supreme Court`s decision about the Texas abortion law. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Unprecedented, that`s the word Rachel and I were discussing at the beginning of this hour. And that`s the word that appears from the four Supreme Court justices who believe that the new Texas abortion law should be stopped in its tracks.\par \par \uc2 Unprecedented is a strong word for judges, but it has lost its meaning in public discourse, especially in the age of Trump.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:03]\par \par \uc2 Justice Sotomayor just couldn`t take it anymore. She just couldn`t go along and use language like unprecedented. And as Rachel pointed out earlier, the first sentence in her dissent is the court`s order is stunning.\par \par \uc2 And she goes on from there, quote, the extraordinary step of enlisting private citizens to do what the state could not, the Texas legislature has deputized the state`s citizens as bounty hunters, offering them cash prizes for civilly prosecuting their neighbors` medical procedures.\par \par \uc2 She then calls them, quote, citizen bounty hunters, a breathtaking act of defiance of the Constitution. This is untenable. It cannot be the case that a state can evade federal judicial scrutiny by outsourcing the enforcement of unconstitutional laws to its citizenry.\par \par \uc2 Justice Sotomayor ended with what, for the court, is a version of pounding the table in anger. Her last sentence is simply, I dissent, instead of traditional "I respectfully dissent."\par \par \uc2 And joining us is Laurence Tribe, university professor of constitutional law emeritus at Harvard Law School. He has won 35 cases in the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 . His new article for "\uc2 The Guardian\uc2 " is entitled "Roe versus Wade died with barely a whimper, but that`s not all."\par \par \uc2 Professor Tribe, thank you for joining us tonight. I want to give you an open field here to comment in any way that you want on this decision and let America know what they should be thinking about it.\par \par \uc2 The one thing I would ask is that you begin with Judge Sotomayor`s point about this peculiar deputizing of the citizenry to be the civil prosecutors of actions that have no relationship to them whatsoever. It establishes a class of plaintiffs that have never existed in American law that I`m aware of. This Texas law seems to have no relationship to American law, federal or state law, in saying that it will be citizens who enforce this law, not the law enforces of the state and the Supreme Court used that, apparently, as the reason why they couldn`t stop it.\par \par \uc2 LAURENCE TRIBE, CONSTITUTIONAL LAW PROFESSOR, HARVARD LAW SCHOOL: Well, it is truly stunning. Justice Sotomayor had it exactly not.\par \par \uc2 It is not exactly unprecedented. Back in the early slave years, fugitive slave laws were enforced by bounty hunters. They went out and sought to return even freed slaves to their so-called owners to collect a bounty. So it has a kind of ugly, dark history.\par \par \uc2 But as Justice Sotomayor said, it simply cannot be the case. Although, five justices say they think it is. It cannot be the case that the state can pass a flagrantly unconstitutional law, unconstitutional unless and until Roe v. Wade is formally overturned, and then avoid any judicial review by deputizing strangers, total strangers.\par \par \uc2 And they don`t have to be Texans. They can be from California. They can be from France. The law clearly allows anyone on the Planet Earth to come into Texas court during, as you say, that four-year period after an abortion and get $10,000 from anyone, friend, a neighbor, the ex-lover, the Uber driver, the person who funded the abortion, anybody who was involved in the abortion, unless somehow the evidence is accumulated that there was no heartbeat.\par \par \uc2 That is not only stunning, it is abominable. It`s breathtaking. And I`m hopeful that even though we know this court does not mean that Roe v. Wade should stand, that maybe one of the five justices in a lawsuit that attacks specifically the bounty hunting aspect by preventing officers of courts from actually transferring the bounties and thereby taking the financial incentive out of it, maybe a justice will be so upset by the idea of privatizing law will get a fifth vote. Not very likely.\par \par \uc2 But there`s very little else that can be done. The whole idea of legislating Roe v. Wade, making it part of statutory law, which I favored for a long time, is something that is going to happen in the House, but it looks like it will die in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 [22:30:00]\par \par \uc2 And even if it were passed, the court that just a little while ago in 2007 said that the commerce power of congress is broad enough so that congress can ban a particular late-term abortion procedure, they probably will turn around and say, oh, but now we discovered new limits to the commerce power. They can`t do that.\par \par \uc2 Just imagine if we were talking about a right that conservatives like, you know, like gun rights. Suppose Massachusetts, the state where I live, decided to disregard the Supreme Court`s precedents about guns and made it a crime for any private citizen to own a pistol without special government permission and without showing the flagrantly unconstitutional. But if they didn`t have public prosecutors enforcing it and had like an army of citizen vigilante making sure that nobody can have a gun at home, do you think this court would have applied its logic to say, oh, we`re going to let that go into effect. Not on your life.\par \par \uc2 So they are not at all consistent. All of these procedural mumbo jumbo things that they said make it very hard for us to figure out how to review this law. But that was just really concocted for the purpose of evading review.\par \par \uc2 And what was most stunning in Justice Sotomayor`s dissent was how compelling her arguments were. I mean there was emotion in it, but the logic was impeccable. She said (INAUDIBLE) she could have added to what she said.\par \par \uc2 She could have said when you deputize private citizens to enforce the law, they become arms of the state. And so you should be able to go after them in the same way you can go after other state officers.\par \par \uc2 But instead the majority said, well, we`re not sure that these judges who were sued are proper defendants. We`re not sure you can sue a judge, a state judge. There is nothing in the constitution that says you cannot have federal suits against state judges.\par \par \uc2 We`re not sure that these judges would enforce the law. What, are they going to be scoff (ph) laws? It`s all made up so that the majority is just tipping its hand. They hate Roe v Wade. They ducked. Didn`t want to say that it is overruled. They claim not to be judging that.\par \par \uc2 But at the very same time, they`re simply obliterating the rights of all the women of Texas to control their own lives and their bodies.\par \par \uc2 And I do think it is not clear, that they can leave the state without creating vulnerabilities. I mean, if I pay somebody to help them get a bus ticket to go to a neighboring state where she can get an abortion, I might be aiding and abetting an illegal abortion, even though it is not illegal at the state where she goes to.\par \par \uc2 That may not be true, as someone has said it is ambiguous. But as long as the chilling effect is there, as long as you don`t know when you are helping a woman deal with a tragic situation in her life, as long as you don`t know whether you are going to be socked for $10,000 here and $10,000 there that you`re likely not to reach out and help her as a Good Samaritan.\par \par \uc2 So this is a devastating decision. And I said in my op-ed piece in "\uc2 The Guardian\uc2 ", Welcome to Gilead -- the nightmarish place in "The Handmaid`s Tale". That`s the world that this court is creating and I think it is a disaster.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Harvard Law Professor Laurence Tribe, thank you for joining us tonight. Professor Tribe`s column about this is in "\uc2 The Guardian\uc2 ". It is entitled "Roe v Wade died with barely a whimper". But that`s not all. You should read it.\par \par \uc2 Professor Tribe, always a pleasure. Thank you very for joining us.\par \par \uc2 TRIBE: Thank you, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, the grand jury in Manhattan heard testimony from two key employees of the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  today as part of its investigation into \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his businesses.\par \par \uc2 Tim O`Brien will join us next.\par \par \uc2 [01:34:13]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: This afternoon, a New York grand jury heard testimony from Matthew Calamari, Jr. the head of security for the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  as part of the investigation into Donald Trump`s finances. His father Matthew Calamari, Sr. is the chief operating officer of the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  and has not yet been subpoenaed.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " reports Jeffrey McConney a top finance official at the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  is also believed to have testified before the grand jury today. It would be the second time that he has provided testimony in the same investigation.\par \par \uc2 In July, Manhattan district attorney, Cy Vance, charged Donald Trump`s accountant Allen Weisselberg in the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  with tax fraud.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Tim O`Brien, senior columnist for "Bloomberg Opinion" and the author of Trump Nation. He was sued by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2006 and Tim O`Brien won.\par \par \uc2 Tim, what do you make of this news about Matthew Calamari Jr. testifying today?\par \par \uc2 TIM O`BRIEN, SENIOR COLUMNIST, "BLOOMBERG OPINION": Well, you know, it`s an incremental development, Lawrence. But I think it`s significant in that it shows that the Manhattan DA is going down and up the food chain at the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  to gather information and to gather testimony and to apply pressure to anyone in the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  who might have knowledge of illegalities there.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:43]\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: If what the Calamari were asked about was consistent with everything else that the Manhattan district attorney has been probing, it was probably about whether or not they got undeclared compensation in the form of freebies like apartments and automobiles that weren`t properly declared to the \uc2 IRS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The consequence of that will -- consequences of that will matter because if -- if the Calamaris are exposed then it turns them into potential witnesses against others in the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 , including \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  himself.\par \par \uc2 It also continues to beg the question of whether or not the Trump children will be called to testify in the same set of issues and anyone else in the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  including Jeff McConney and as we know already, Allen Weisselberg.\par \par \uc2 And all of them have to be looking at what Allen Weisselberg is looking at, which is the possibility of a severe prison sentence if this goes in as bad as a direction as it might, which of course it might not.\par \par \uc2 I think the other thing to remember, when it comes to the Calamaris themselves, is I know hired Matthew Calamari because he saw them -- saw him beating up a few people at the U.S. Open and hired him as a body guard.\par \par \uc2 And then years along the way, he got transformed into Donald Trump`s chief operating officer. And it`s a reminder of how cartoonish and comically absurd the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  is as an entity.\par \par \uc2 This person who was elected because of his supposed business prowess was hiring people willy-nilly for roles that were far above their experience or expertise but he didn`t really care. And it was a mirror of what he later did in the White House, I think.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Well, I mean isn`t that just a title, chief operating officer? Isn`t that -- I mean \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the chief operating officer, along with the kids, presumably.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And that title was just there to give away to someone who wasn`t named Trump.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: 100 percent. And remember, this was essentially a mom and pop operation. This wasn`t a large, sophisticated Fortune 500 company. This was a small group of people intensely loyal to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And at the end of the day, two or three people that have called all of the shots and none of them did anything with Donald -- of significance without \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  signing off on it.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Now, Jeff McConney, if I have him placed correctly, he is in the financial structure of the operation. Is he the kind of guy who would be in the room with Allen Weisselberg a lot?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Well, he`s the kind of guy who helped the Trumps prepare their tax returns. And he`s the kind of guy who has knowledge of what the scope of their business really looked like, who some of their financial partners were, how they structured deals on paper.\par \par \uc2 I think at the end of the day, it was really Allen Weisselberg and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and -- and maybe one or two other people that had deciding roles in all this. And I don`t think that Jeff McConney was always there, but he was certainly there frequently enough that he`s a very material witness and he`s somebody whose testimony is clearly damaging and threatening to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: There is -- there is something that`s always been fascinating about this investigation and that is that the district attorney is leaving office basically at the end of the year, which is unusual in these kinds of investigations.\par \par \uc2 And so we have all had this time frame of thinking what`s going to happen will happen within this calendar year, at least get started in a serious way. And so, that`s why every time there is a witness going in, the news media thinks we`re closer and closer to that point of what happens next.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Well, I think that might be an artificial time frame, Lawrence, because Cy Vance Jr. has put in a very sophisticated team of investigators. They are being methodical as they should be. I think they`re trying to build a bulletproof case, as they should.\par \par \uc2 And I think he`s very cognizant of the fact that there is a likelihood that this case will run past his time as DA, which I think it most likely will. And I think that`s a good thing.\par \par \uc2 It may not happen quickly enough to satisfy everyone`s appetite for quick justice. But it is -- I do think it is an indicator of the kind of formidable investigative team he`s put in place.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Tim O`Brien, thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Coming up, a new poll of California voters shows Democratic Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  doing better in the recall election that will be held on September 14th.\par \par \uc2 But if more than 50 percent of California voters say \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  should be recalled, the polls show that a Republican radio talk show host will be the next governor of California. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 [22:44:48]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The first question voters must answer in California`s second- ever recall election of a governor on September 14th is -- shall \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  be recalled, removed from the office of governor?\par \par \uc2 A new poll by the \uc2 Public Policy Institute of California\uc2  found that 58 percent of likely California voters will vote no on that first question. 39 percent would vote to remove the governor. The poll also found that 53 percent approve of Gavin Newsom`s job as governor at this point.\par \par \uc2 The second question on the ballot is if the governor is recalled who do you want to replace him? A poll found that Republican radio talks show host Larry Elder, who is a full-fledged Trump supporter is leading the field with 26 percent. That 26 percent would be enough to make Larry Elder the governor of California if 51 percent of voters vote to remove the governor.\par \par \uc2 Today Governor Newsom said this.\par \par \uc2 [22:49:56]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 GAVIN NEWSOM\uc2  (D-CA): It shouldn`t surprise any of you that this recall is part of an extended narrative that started with a big lie. Extended to the insurrection of January 6th. Extends to the voter suppression laws you`ve seen all across the country. And yes, what you saw yesterday in Texas and the assaults on women`s rights and reproductive freedom in this nation.\par \par \uc2 This is a profoundly important moment to say no. Not in California.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is \uc2 Darrell Steinberg\uc2 , the Democratic mayor of Sacramento. He was the president of the California Senate during the administration of Arnold Schwarzenegger who became governor in California`s first recall of a governor.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for joining us tonight, Mr. Mayor.\par \par \uc2 My California political friends and some former operatives including Anthony Reyes who used to work as a staffer in the state senate and used to work on this show -- by the way, he`s my pipeline to California politics -- had been worried about the Democratic strategy on this, which is telling people just to vote no and don`t bother choosing a name to replace the governor, which all voters can do.\par \par \uc2 You can vote no and, you know, you don`t want the governor removed and then you can vote for whoever you think is the best person or the least bad person to go in there.\par \par \uc2 The polls now indicate that that strategy might work because the governor might beat this recall by a substantial margin.\par \par \uc2 But as we go into it, what do we make of polls on recalls? How reliable are they? Recalls are such a strange process. Do the pollsters even know how to poll this question?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR \uc2 DARRELL STEINBERG\uc2  (D-CA), SACRAMENTO: Well, thank you for having me, Lawrence, first of all.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are beginning to wake up. And that is a good thing. But there is 13 days left, and we cannot take anything for granted because as Governor Newsom said, there is far too much at stake.\par \par \uc2 Look, this governor doesn`t get enough credit for what he is doing in California. He`s doing a terrific job. He has led and is leading this state through the worst pandemic in modern history. He is following the science. And our state is doing relatively better than other states like Texas and Florida.\par \par \uc2 He has also achieved some incredible things for the people of California, whether it`s universal preschool, whether it is billions of dollars into leading on climate and setting the national standard for carbon neutrality. Whether it is attacking the homeless problem with more money and more attention than any governor in our history.\par \par \uc2 And so, this is a political hijacking -- an attempt at a political hijacking. And I think the people of California, especially the Democrats, are beginning to pay attention.\par \par \uc2 And in part, Larry Elder in some ways is helping because think about it for a moment. In 2003, when I was in the legislature, I worked with Governor Schwarzenegger when he was the governor. The recall back then was wrong, too. But Schwarzenegger and the Republican Party in those days were relatively moderate.\par \par \uc2 Today, Larry Elder taking over the state of California, he`s against vaccinations. He`s against a woman`s right to choose. He`s a climate denier.\par \par \uc2 If he were somehow to slip in with 25 percent of the vote, remember how this works, God forbid if Governor Newsom gets recalled, he would be recalled say getting 49 percent of the vote. And then on the second question, Elder would slip in with 25 percent of the vote. The process itself needs to be reformed.\par \par \uc2 But having said that, it is the choice of anybody to vote on the second question. I did not. I think Democrats ought to go to that ballot and they ought to vote no. It takes about 20 seconds and be done with this thing. Because California despite its many challenges, is actually making tremendous progress on a number of issues that President Biden and the Congress are attempting to achieve through the infrastructure bill.\par \par \uc2 Governor Newsom is leading that charge. To have this kind of political hijacking midterm would be terrible for California. It would bring government to a standstill, and it has to be turned back.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:49]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Well, it is all about Democratic turn-out. Obviously if the Democratic voters turn out, Governor Newsom is going to win by a wide margin. It is all about that turn-out.\par \par \uc2 Sacramento Mayor \uc2 Darrell Steinberg\uc2 , thank you very much for joining us tonight. I really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 STEINBERG: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We`ll be right back with tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The tail end of the downgraded Hurricane Ida battered the northeastern United States late yesterday and last night leaving a path of destruction across New York, New Jersey, Connecticut and Pennsylvania. At least 43 people have died according to NBC News.\par \par \uc2 During a briefing on the storm damage, New York Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): What`s so fascinating is that the records that were broken in Central Park, for example, 3.15 inches in one hour. It broke a record literally set one week earlier.\par \par \uc2 That says to me that there are no more cataclysmic unforeseeable events. We need to foresee these in advance and be prepared.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [22:59:58]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Governor Hochul gets tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH BRIAN WILLIAMS" starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020210902eh9200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Rachel Maddow Show for September 2, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Rachel Maddow \par \uc2 7,978 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 September 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Rachel Maddow Show\par \uc2 TRMADS\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Chris. Thanks, my friend.\par \par \uc2 And thanks to you at home for joining us this hour.\par \par \uc2 There are fires on the West Coast, there is the storm in the Northeast, and the hurricane aftermath in Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 [21:00:01]\par \par \uc2 We are in a multiple split screen moment of multiple, gigantic, climate driven disasters across huge swaths of the United States tonight. The West is spotted with multiple fires, particularly in the state of California, where more than a dozen large fires are burning tonight as we speak. The two biggest fires are the Dixie Fire and the Caldor Fire. They are so large they became the first fires in recorded history to burn from one side of the Sierra Nevada Mountain Range to the other.\par \par \uc2 The Dixie Fire is only about an hour northeast of the Camp Fire. You remember when the camp fire burned multiple towns to the ground in 2018? That felt like the worst that it could get?\par \par \uc2 Dixie is -- has already burned 1,400 square miles of land. Dixie has destroyed nearly 600 homes. It`s only about half contained at the moment. It is the second largest fire in California history.\par \par \uc2 The Caldor Fire, just outside of Lake Tahoe, has destroyed even more homes, at least 622 homes and counting. It is raging right now toward even more populated areas. Only about 25 percent of the Caldor Fire is contained.\par \par \uc2 Both of those fires have multiple evacuation orders and warnings in place. The fire season is just unrelenting and merciless. Firefighters working in basically inhuman conditions now for weeks on end fighting unprecedented fire after unprecedented fire. Just a huge devotion of resources, human and otherwise, and so far no end in sight.\par \par \uc2 In the Northeast, we have watched all day as the death toll has climbed from the floods that left behind by the trailing ends of Hurricane Ida last night. This is Hurricane Ida`s remnants hitting the most densely populated part of the country. As it stands right now we are at a death toll of 42 people in the Northeast. Many of them unable to leave ground level or basement level apartments when the floods hit so suddenly. Lots of other people trapped in their cars as water on the roads became more like rivers.\par \par \uc2 Again, that is a huge death toll already, 42 already. We expect that to continue to climb. The state of Pennsylvania reports doing thousands of water rescues last night.\par \par \uc2 The New York City Fire Department reported doing hundreds of water rescues just within New York City, itself. Buses and subway platforms in New York flooded to the point that public transit shut down. That left people stranded on flooded platforms and on subway cars wherever they were.\par \par \uc2 Central Park at New York City set a record last night for the most rain Central Park has ever recorded in a single hour. The previous record was set last month. But over 3 inches an hour in Central Park? They call them flash floods for a reason. People who were going about their lives in the Northeast were really not expecting this last night. As I say, the death toll continues to climb.\par \par \uc2 In the South, Ida hit on Sunday as a category 4 hurricane. The impact was bad enough from the initial sledge hammer blow of the storm including its very high winds. But the danger to human life and the misery in the wake of Ida has gotten worse with each passing day since the storm left. Some electric power is slowly being restored in the state of Louisiana, but we are still at nearly a million homes and businesses without power. That is a huge number of Americans coping in very difficult conditions. Some parts of Louisiana are still looking at potentially a month before the lights come back on.\par \par \uc2 We have talked to two parish officials in two southeast Louisiana counties in the past couple nights on this show both from St. John`s Parish and Jefferson Parish, Louisiana told us their parishes right now are bluntly uninhabitable.\par \par \uc2 Without power, fuel has become all the more important. Even hospitals are still relying on generators for power. That is not a long term solution. That is a stop-gap.\par \par \uc2 Nearly two-thirds of gas stations in Baton Rouge and New Orleans were out of fuel today. You need fuel to power your generator. The line at one \uc2 Costco\uc2  in New Orleans where they did still have fuel today, that line was 2 miles long. All of this while the National Weather Service has issued a heat advisory, heat index of 107 degrees today in New Orleans.\par \par \uc2 Again, I can`t stress this enough. I don`t want you to glide past it. This is a disaster in Louisiana that is getting worse by the day now not better. Even as the federal government and the state just pours resources in to try to get basic infrastructure back up and running the scale of the devastation means it is really slow going. And this means our fellow Americans, thousands and thousands of our fellow Americans are right now in day four of increasingly unlivable conditions in big swaths of southeastern Louisiana right now.\par \par \uc2 [21:05:07]\par \par \uc2 President Biden is headed to Louisiana tomorrow. Today, he did not mince words about what is happening with the concurrent and compounding disasters and what we need to do to get more resilient against these kinds of things.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The past few days of Hurricane Ida and the wildfires in the west and the unprecedented flash floods in New York and New Jersey is yet another reminder that these extreme storms and the climate crisis are here. We need to be better prepared. We need to act. When Congress returns this month, I`m going to press for their action on my Build Back Better plan.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: The Build Back Better plan he is referencing there is the president`s two-part, multi trillion dollar infrastructure package, a huge part of which is funding and getting our roads and bridges and electrical grids prepared for these kinds of disasters. But, yeah.\par \par \uc2 About that, today, Democratic Senator \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  published an op-ed on the conservative editorial page of "The Wall Street Journal" explaining why he doesn`t feel like voting for the climate focused part of the package. Good timing, Senator.\par \par \uc2 With or without that funding log jammed in Washington by Republicans and \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 , we do still have the multiple, ongoing crises we`re facing right now to take care of, and that all takes a ton of resources and a ton of coordination.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: There`s a lot of damage, and I made clear to the governors that my team at the \uc2 Federal Emergency Management Agency\uc2 , \uc2 FEMA\uc2 , is on the ground and ready to provide all the assistance that`s needed. In fact, our \uc2 FEMA\uc2  director/administrator, Deanna Criswell, will -- was a chief federal response officer after Superstorm Sandy in 2012. She knows what to do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: The name President Biden sort of coughed over there was \uc2 Deanne Criswell\uc2 . She is the FEMA administrator. She`s the head of \uc2 FEMA\uc2 , in charge of federal disaster response. In the midst of all of this, all of these overlapping and compounding disasters right now, she joins us now here live.\par \par \uc2 Administrator Criswell, you have a million things to be doing right now. Thank you so much for taking a few minutes to be here with us.\par \par \uc2 DEANNE CRISWELL\uc2 , FEMA ADMINISTRATOR: Thank you, Rachel. Thanks for having the time to have me.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: How much harder is it to get any one of these disaster responses right when you have so many to respond to all at the same time?\par \par \uc2 CRISWELL: You know, as you were just stating, the impacts that we`re seeing from these severe storms continue to grow. We`re seeing the storms intensify rapidly. Hurricane Ida, she caused destruction across four of our regions and nine states. You know, we have a good force. They`ve been working hard to support our COVID response. And we are prepared to support all of the needs that are going to come out of the disaster and the damages that Hurricane Ida has left.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: With your experience with Hurricane Sandy in the Northeast, which we just heard President Biden reference there talking about you today I just have to ask you based on looking at what happened last night and as you say the speed at which Ida came upon the Northeast right now surprising many people particularly in the tri-state area, how do you think this sort of walloping by Hurricane Ida last night compares to what we saw with Sandy?\par \par \uc2 CRISWELL: You know, no two storms are alike. And there`s a lot of differences between Sandy and Ida. I mean, Sandy had a lot of warning, right? More warning watching the storm approach from the Atlantic. We knew that this storm was going to bring severe rain and thunderstorms and tornadoes, we just didn`t know where it was going to happen until it moved over the Northeast.\par \par \uc2 So we`re seeing damages. We are seeing a lot of destruction right now in New Jersey and New York but also Philadelphia and Maryland. And we`re going to be able to support the state as they find their needs and we`ll bring in the resources to help them with their recovery.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: I`ve been looking at the releases from \uc2 FEMA\uc2  tonight about the incident management teams fanning out, you know, five of them in Louisiana, two of them in Mississippi, but now also New Jersey, New York, Pennsylvania. I know that FEMA`s got I think more than a thousand employees in Louisiana and Mississippi alone supporting the hurricane aftermath there. I have to tell you though as this week has gone on, looking at the conditions on the ground, it doesn`t feel like enough.\par \par \uc2 I am worried about how long hospitals in Louisiana can stay on generator power particularly given the fact that there was no slack in the system due to the huge surge in very sick COVID patients. How long can people live in the dark and heat?\par \par \uc2 I know there is no estimate as to when the power is coming back on in a lot of places.\par \par \uc2 [21:10:04]\par \par \uc2 But what else can be done?\par \par \uc2 CRISWELL: We are prepared and planning for the power to be out for several weeks. With the thousand employees \uc2 FEMA\uc2  has on the ground \uc2 FEMA\uc2  is just one part of the team. We also have resources in from all of the federal agencies that are supporting this, from the \uc2 U.S. Coast Guard\uc2 , the Department of Defense, the \uc2 Army Corps of Engineers\uc2 . They are also providing support to the critical infrastructure needs to help them with that.\par \par \uc2 Hospitals are the priority. I was with the governor a few days ago and he has made it very clear that supporting the hospitals is the priority. While they are on generators, we also have additional resources, additional generators that can come in and support if we find any trouble with the ones the hospitals have.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Tell me honestly here. Is there anything that you`re asking for that \uc2 FEMA\uc2  thinks it needs particularly for the Louisiana response which I`m so worried about, is there anything that you`re asking for that you`ve been told to which you`ve been told no? Any resources you`ve requested that you have been turned down for?\par \par \uc2 CRISWELL: No, not at all. We have the full force, the full support of the entire federal family. President Biden has been very clear that whatever Louisiana needs for its response and recovery, that we`ll be able to provide.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: I know you are heading to Louisiana tomorrow with President Biden. What is your top priority for that visit? Why is it important to get the president himself down there to see things with his own eyes?\par \par \uc2 CRISWELL: Well, I think it is always important for our leaders to see exactly what`s going on on the ground so they can, you know, if they do find struggles, right, that they can relate to the people and have that understanding of what they`re struggling with. So, we can keep pushing those resources in. Having President Biden go see for himself is just a demonstration of the leadership that he wants to provide that the government is behind them and we`re going to bring what they need to help with their recovery.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: FEMA Administrator \uc2 Deanne Criswell\uc2 , this is an offer I made to some of our officials dealing with the COVID crisis early on in the administration. I make it to you here as well. If you get told no, if there is something you need for response and you run into bottlenecks, please tell the public. Please tell us and let us tell the country because there is so much support for what you need to do and if you hit bottlenecks, public pressure can help.\par \par \uc2 CRISWELL: No, I understand it, and I appreciate that offer, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: All right. Thank you. Good luck. Good luck.\par \par \uc2 CRISWELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: \uc2 Deanne Criswell\uc2  is the administrator of the Federal Emergency Management Agency under President Biden.\par \par \uc2 You know, at times like this it is sometimes helpful to remember that the news gods just do this to us sometimes. They give us too much to handle. I think they enjoy it.\par \par \uc2 Too much to handle. Too much to follow. Too much to absorb. Certainly too much to report on in any sort of orderly fashion. I can prove it to you.\par \par \uc2 Today, we went back and looked up in the archives the broadcast from NBC Nightly News the night that Roe versus Wade was first decided by the Supreme Court in January, 1973. It`s not like we know now Roe was a big deal but at the time they didn`t recognize it. No. At the time they knew it was a huge deal.\par \par \uc2 Abortion was a really controversial thing then as it is now. In 1973, abortion was illegal in most states in the country. But the Roe decision came down like a lightning bolt and Roe meant of a sudden, overnight, legal abortion everywhere for all American women. It was a huge deal. They knew it was a huge deal when it happened.\par \par \uc2 So, I was expecting one thing when I went back and looked in the archives as to what NBC "Nightly News" was like that night. I was wrong. Given what a big deal it was and how big they knew it was at the time, I will tell you, I was shocked to learn that that Supreme Court decision, January, 1973, did not come up in that night`s network news broadcast until 11 minutes in, 11 minutes in to their 30-minute broadcast.\par \par \uc2 And then even when they tried to bring it up, it got pushed down by further breaking news.\par \par \uc2 Now, what was going on is that this was two days after Nixon had just been inaugurated for a second term. So that was going on. The first 11 minutes of the newscast that night was about the Paris peace talks to supposedly end the Vietnam War. That takes them through the first 11 minutes of the broadcast. In the middle of minute 11, they try to get to the roe vs. Wade decision, but more breaking news comes in on the phone.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TV ANCHOR: We have just received a bulletin from "The Associated Press" from Texas that reports that former President Lyndon Johnson is dead. Lyndon Johnson has died. According to the bulletin, former President Lyndon Johnson, he was 64 years old, has died according to his press secretary. The report only says that Johnson was stricken at his ranch in Texas. He died this afternoon at Brooke General Hospital.\par \par \uc2 [21:15:02]\par \par \uc2 Repeating that bulletin that just arrived, former President Lyndon Johnson is dead of a heart attack at the age of 64. We`ll have more details in the program as we get them.\par \par \uc2 The other major story today, aside from the death of Lyndon Johnson, the tragic death and the hopes for peace in Vietnam, is the decision of the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  that handed down an historic decision about abortion. The court said in a 7-2 decision that in the first three months of pregnancy, only the woman and her physician may decide whether she may have an abortion. In the second three months, all the state may do is regulate abortion procedures. And only in the final three months of pregnancy can the state forbid abortion. The court ruled in cases from Texas and Georgia but all 50 states are affected whatever their laws.\par \par \uc2 BETTY ROLLIN, NBC NEWS REPORTER: Today`s decision came as a shock to both anti and pro-abortionist forces.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Well, it means that January 22nd, 1973, will stand out as one of the great days for freedom and free choice. This allows a woman free choice as to whether to remain pregnant. This is extraordinary.\par \par \uc2 ROLLIN: For some women, this law will mean the difference between having an abortion or not. For many others the law will mean simply having the abortion in their own state instead of traveling to another state like New York. This New York clinic for example reports that 48 percent of its patients are from out of the state.\par \par \uc2 From the beginning pro abortionist forces have seen this issue as a question of freedom of an individual`s choice. The freedom to have an abortion is now legal in every state. The basic legal fight is in effect over.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Basic fight is in effect over. Well, it was over. It was over -- from then, 1973, until yesterday.\par \par \uc2 It`s amazing to see though. That is NBC reporter Betty Rollin, almost having her front page top of the broadcast story kicked entirely out of "Nightly News" because the day that Roe vs. Wade was decided was also a day that included the shocking death of former President Lyndon Johnson at age 64 and also maybe the end of the Vietnam War with the Paris peace talks, and also day three of a new presidency and also everything else.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the news gods sometimes do this. I can empathize because now 48 years down the road, we get the other book end on that shelf, right? Now we get the end of Roe vs. Wade, the end of legal abortion access being guaranteed in America arriving simultaneously with the harrowing and overdue end of the Afghanistan war, while also fires like no one has ever seen before rage in California, while also a hurricane kills dozens of people in the Northeast and floats away Staten Island and parts of Brooklyn and New Jersey and Philly, after that same storm swamped over a million Americans into days if not weeks without power and running water and extreme heat in the South, while also an out of control never before seen pandemic races us towards 700,000 Americans dead with over a thousand Americans dying every day now again.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it is enough to make you question your life choices you know? It is enough to make you wish the country might have taken a harder line along the way for people who tried to make sure government is ill-equipped and underresourced and derided and undermined in its abilities to tackle serious problems and surprise crises when they arrive. It is enough to make you think.\par \par \uc2 Today in response to the Supreme Court`s ruling, late last night, not just letting the Texas abortion bill stand but now saying we`re letting the Texas abortion bill stand ending the protection of Roe vs. Wade, today, President Biden made a substantive statement not just criticizing the decision but saying both what is wrong with the decision and what he plans to do about it. You may have heard he made a statement about this today. It is worth actually checking what he said here.\par \par \uc2 Quote, the Supreme Court`s ruling overnight is an unprecedented assault on a woman`s constitutional rights under Roe vs. Wade which has been the law of the land for almost 50 years. By allowing a law to go into effect that empowers private citizens in Texas to sue health care providers, family members supporting a woman exercising her right to choose, or even a friend who drives her to the hospital or clinic, it unleashes un-constitutional chaos and empowers self-anointed enforcers to have devastating impacts.\par \par \uc2 Complete strangers will now be empowered to inject themselves in the most private and personal health decisions faced by women. This law is so extreme it doesn`t even allow for exceptions in the case of rape or incest and not only empowers complete strangers to inject themselves into the most private of decisions made by a woman it actually incentivizes them to do so with the prospect of a $10,000 bounty if they win their case.\par \par \uc2 For the majority of the Supreme Court to do this without a hearing, without the benefit of an opinion from a court below, and without due consideration of the issues, insults the rule of law and the rights of all Americans to seek redress from our courts.\par \par \uc2 [21:20:04]\par \par \uc2 The impact of last night`s decision will be immediate and it requires an immediate response.\par \par \uc2 The president says, quote, I am directing my gender policy council and the White House counsel`s office to launch a whole of government effort to respond to this decision, looking specifically to the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  and to the Department of Justice to see what steps the federal government can take to ensure that women in Texas have access to safe and legal abortions as protected by Roe and what legal tools we have to insulate women and providers from the impact of Texas` bizarre scheme of outsourced enforcement to private parties.\par \par \uc2 President Biden saying he wants a whole of government response to this catastrophe in women`s rights in Texas. I have questions. First of all, what can the federal government actually do? What can those agencies, President Biden singled out, Health and Human Services, the Justice Department, what can they do that might make a difference?\par \par \uc2 Also, what does it mean? What should we infer from the fact that President Biden has put, interestingly, his White House counsel in charge of leading this whole of government effort? What does that say about how he intends to fight and what resources he thinks he can bring to bear?\par \par \uc2 Also, now that Texas has been allowed to ban abortion in this specific way, should we expect that all the red states in the country will just \uc2 Xerox\uc2  the Texas law and thereby put their own copy cat abortion bans into place as well?\par \par \uc2 Is the Supreme Court giving Republican-controlled states the green light to ban abortion that way all across the country? South Dakota Governor \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2 , for example, has already said today that she plans to do just that.\par \par \uc2 Here`s a related question. The ruling last night says explicitly the justices are not saying one way or the other whether Texas` abortion ban is constitutional or not. The ruling says they`re just letting it go into effect regardless of whether or not it is constitutional.\par \par \uc2 Does that mean that Texas` ban is only going to stand temporarily? Or should we expect it to stand for the foreseeable future?\par \par \uc2 Here is another very practical question. In the pre-Roe era like in that NBC News clip from 1973, women who could afford to, would leave their home state where abortion was outlawed and travel to a place like New York or California to get an abortion because it was legal in those states. We see that starting again already. Clinics nearby to Texas including in Oklahoma today said as of today they`re getting an influx of Texas women patients.\par \par \uc2 But does the vigilante bounty hunter part of this law also put that at risk? Will the vigilante snitch on your neighbor, sue your neighbor over her medical procedures part of this law, will that be used to target people who donate money or otherwise help women leave their state to go get an abortion elsewhere? Practical question.\par \par \uc2 Here is a Washington question. Democrats in the White House responded to the Supreme Court today by saying they plan to codify roe into law. They say they`ll try to pass a law through the House and the Senate and have President Biden sign it. Even if they can do that, Senator \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  notwithstanding, would it help? Or would the court not only undo Roe but also kill a law like that?\par \par \uc2 And here is one last question. It does now feel inevitable the same Supreme Court will go the full nine yards and formally repeal Roe this fall with this Mississippi case they`re going to hear, beyond what they did yesterday, which was just to quietly gut Roe without admitting that`s what they were doing. When that happens, I used to think if that happens but now I`m thinking when that happens. When they formally overturn Roe, how much worse will that make things? And if we`re expecting that what should we do now to plan for it?\par \par \uc2 Questions as you can see I have a lot. But answers, we have them next.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [21:28:22]\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Can we put those little questions back up on the screen? We have the ability to do that. Squeeze them up there. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 I am not a lawyer. I do not even pretend to be one on TV. But I do have questions about the law. About the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  putting us back in the days of abortion being illegal in this country.\par \par \uc2 And at least for right now, that list of questions includes these. President Biden says he is going to mount a whole of government response to defend women`s rights. What does that mean? What could he do? Is it important that he is putting his White House counsel in charge of that effort? Is that significant?\par \par \uc2 Also, should we expect all the red states to now copy the Texas ban since the Supreme Court has allowed it to become law? Is this going to be true in every other red state now?\par \par \uc2 How is the Supreme Court only legalized the Texas ban or allowed the Texas ban to stand on a temporary basis? Or should we expect it to stay in place indefinitely?\par \par \uc2 Also, since we are now back in a world where some states ban abortion, it is one now but I`m guessing more soon, can women get help to leave abortion banned states so they can travel to a state that will let them get an abortion or does the bounty hunter, vigilante parts of the Texas law be used against any of us who would donate money or otherwise provide help to a woman to cross state lines?\par \par \uc2 Could a new federal law on abortion rights help? Democrats say that is what they plan to do. Even if they can do that, would it help? And is worse coming? And if so, how do we prepare for it?\par \par \uc2 [21:30:01]\par \par \uc2 [21:30:01]\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is someone who has talked me off many a legal ledge over the years. Tonight, I think we might possibly be standing on that ledge together, however. Dahlia Lithwick is senior editor at slate.com.\par \par \uc2 Dahlia, it`s nice to see you. Thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 DAHLIA LITHWICK, SENIOR EDITOR AT SLATE.COM: Hi, Rachel. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: First of all, let me give you the option. You have special privileges here on this show. Do you want to throw all these questions out? Are these at least close to the right ones to be asking or are they not?\par \par \uc2 LITHWICK: They are good -- I will tell you when you threw them up on the screen again I had the most wicked flashback to issue spotter exams in my first year of law school and my heart started pounding. I`m in law school. I can do it. Let`s power through.\par \par \uc2 I will say that the questions about, you know, the Women`s Health Protection Act and the potential legislation and what can be done, I mean, I know that`s what \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  is pushing. That`s what Biden is talking about, President Biden I`m sorry, but I think we should be really clear, a, it doesn`t seem that that has support in the Senate. I think you said \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  not with standing. And I think there is a larger question buried somewhere in one of your questions about whether the Supreme Court would just turn around and strike it down.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, we can certainly talk about codifying Roe, which has been I think the answer to this but it is not at all clear to me, A, that happens in the Senate and, B, that the Supreme Court tolerates it.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Okay. After we speak with you tonight we`re going to be speaking with the sponsor of the Women`s Health Protection Act to try to get her take on that. She`s been -- Congressman Judy Chu has been introducing this proposed law year after year after year and we`ll talk to her about exactly those points because she thinks this is a beachhead and a way to do it. So, we`ll get back to that later in the show.\par \par \uc2 But let me ask you about the federal government part of it. I mean, President Biden said today he didn`t just condemn what the Supreme Court did. He said he wants a whole of government response. He specifically called out the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  and the Justice Department and he put his White House counsel in charge of it.\par \par \uc2 Do any of those raise your interest in terms of something the federal government might be able to do they haven`t previously done?\par \par \uc2 LITHWICK: Look, I am absolutely delighted if \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  want put together a whole government response. My sense is, look, this should have been happening from inauguration day where we had clinics in Louisiana, Mississippi, places where women were all but losing access. These are protections that are long, long overdue. By the way, that goes back to the Obama administration.\par \par \uc2 So absolutely anything that can be done, robust protections for women`s reproductive care, you`re not going to get me to pooh-pooh it. I am going to say, Rachel, the thing I`ve been saying to you for years which is this is a courts problem. And in my view where the rubber hits the road has to be thinking very, very seriously about structural court reform, what we do about courts that take it upon themselves to nullify roe in the dark of night. I don`t think these problems can be solved as a sort of whole government response and then say we`ll kick it over to the court commission and hope that they make a recommendation that in 27 years we can do to reform the courts.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Could that court commission take this more seriously from what you know about President Biden -- for our viewers who haven`t been following this closely there has been a serious proposal promoted with increasing urgency that the Supreme Court should be expanded and that other potential reforms to the way the Supreme Court operates and the way it is constituted ought to be seriously considered because of the way that Republicans in particular have really stacked the deck to make that court their own play thing.\par \par \uc2 President Biden formed a court commission to study the matter and as you say, you know, somewhat facetiously, we can`t wait for them to hear they ought to do something different in 27 years. If you could wave a magic wand and be the new convener of the court commission what would you have them do?\par \par \uc2 LITHWICK: Rachel, I want to be super clear. I have so much respect for so many of the people on that commission. The time to fix the courts was yesterday if not ten years ago. I just think that having a court commission that is not by the way seriously looking at court expansion, that is doing a kind of quasi-academic exercise trying to think about other versions of structural court reform, I just think the urgency that we are facing in terms of what this court can do and will do, not just in terms of reproductive rights but in terms of dismantling the administrative state, in terms of voting rights, which we`re not going to have a chance to talk about, but this court I don`t think can be handled with the chin stroking, let`s weigh some proposals, and think about it.\par \par \uc2 [21:35:06]\par \par \uc2 I really do think that every single day we are not talking seriously about court reform is a day that is absolutely lost.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: In the short term, Dahlia, do you expects we`ll have a lot of red states do copy cat laws that look just like the Texas law and will those effectively just go into effect as soon as they pass them and we`ll have abortion bans in multiple states?\par \par \uc2 LITHWICK: It certainly looks like it, Rachel. \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , the chief justice one of the dissenters from the order last night. The point he made was we could have enjoined. We could have enjoined it and gotten to the merits eventually, let this thing play out. The court does that all the time. By failing to enjoin it, the court has invited more.\par \par \uc2 And as you noted we`re seeing all over the country, Florida falling over itself to pass a copy-cat statute. Why wouldn`t it? They`ve now had the doors flung open. I think without a doubt we are going to see very, very soon, a checker board of states in which for all intents and purposes there is no right to abortion. Roe v. Wade is a right that exists on paper and not as a matter of fact.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: And briefly and practically, for people who are thinking about funding efforts, donating to efforts, contributing personally to efforts, volunteering to help women in abortion ban states get to states where abortion is still legal so they can have the procedure done elsewhere, are these laws designed to target people who provide that kind of help?\par \par \uc2 LITHWICK: You know, I`ve seen that answered both in the affirmative and the negative today. NPR was reporting earlier today that the law will not reach out and get you if you are simply writing a check to help somebody get out of state. It will not touch you if you are in fact treating someone out of state.\par \par \uc2 But Larry Tribe writing in "\uc2 The Guardian\uc2 " today suggested otherwise. I think the truth is that the law is sufficiently vague, Rachel, that we`re going to have to test it and test it and find out. One of the points, the very purpose of this law is to chill people not just abortion providers in Texas but to chill the entire community of people who want to help women right now into second-guessing whether they`re going to be on the hook. That was the purpose of the law. That was the law the Supreme Court let go into effect.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: I can see a proverbial mushroom cloud of rage rising above you over the United States of America as people everywhere hear those words from you, my friend. Dahlia Lithwick, senor editor, at slate.com -- Dahlia, keep writing and giving them hell. We need you more than ever.\par \par \uc2 LITHWICK: Thanks, Rachel. Thanks for having me, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: All right. Much more ahead. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LISA MYERS, TV ANCHOR: In summing up today`s decision, Justice Harry Blackmun, author of the Roe decision, wrote: Now just when so many expected the darkness to fall, the flame has grown bright. However, it`s also true that that flame can be extinguished by one more vote.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Congressional leaders said they would try to go around the Supreme Court by making abortion legal once and for all.\par \par \uc2 SEN. GEORGE MITCHELL (D), MAJORITY LEADER: The Congress must act to ensure that the fundamental right of American women to choose for themselves is not lost.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: That was June, 1992. That was the first big Supreme Court test of Roe vs. Wade and Roe survived. The ruling was close, 5-4. One vote the other direction Roe would have been overturned. You heard the Democratic leader in the Senate, George Mitchell, say that is too close for comfort. It is time to take the whole issue of protecting abortion rights out of the hands of the Supreme Court. It is too dangerous there.\par \par \uc2 He said in June, 1992 rather than leaving it up to the court, Congress should make an end run around the court. Congress should just pass a federal law that would protect a woman`s right to have an abortion in all 50 states. He promised he would bring the full \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  to a vote on the Freedom of Choice Act which would enshrine abortion rights into federal law.\par \par \uc2 In the end, he did not follow through on that promise.\par \par \uc2 But the idea of doing that, the promise to do that, has stuck around in Democratic politics ever since in a weird and kind of frustrating way. Go back to the 2008 presidential campaign, \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  then a candidate said if he were elected president he would make sure there was a vote on the Freedom of Choice Act. He said signing that bill would be, quote, the first thing I`d do as president. He was elected president but that did not happen.\par \par \uc2 As recently as 2020, almost every Democrat in the massive field of 2020 presidential contenders said the same thing. They all said they would 