elementary school teacher infected with the Delta variant spread the virus to half the students in a classroom seeding an outbreak that eventually infected 26 people.\par \par \uc2 The teacher taught for two days while symptomatic and before getting tested which itself not a great call. During which time, she read aloud without a mask to 24 students. Everyone in the front row tested positive, which fell to 80 percent in the first two rows. In the back three rows, 28 percent of students tested positive. You can see how it diffuses through the class there.\par \par \uc2 An unvaccinated teacher dropped her mask despite rules -- school rules to wear masks indoors. And within days, half of her class was positive for the virus at a school that had been conscientious about following COVID protocols. Yet despite stories like this, Florida Governor Ron DeSantis is withholding funds from two school districts that made masks mandatory in classrooms this fall, one of which is Broward County where Dr. Vickie Cartwright is the interim superintendent. And Dr. Cartwright joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Great to have you here. Before we get into mask policy, I just want to level set because I want to make sure I understand this. Pursuing to I think an executive order from the governor, you can`t have a vaccine requirement for your staff -- teachers and staff in schools, correct?\par \par \uc2 [20:55:14]\par \par \uc2 DR. VICKIE CARTWRIGHT, INTERIM SUPERINTENDENT, BROWARD COUNTY PUBLIC SCHOOLS: Well, that`s something that we would have to review coming up here in the future. We have not -- at this point in time, we have not had legal counsel to advise us as to what we are allowed to do or not allowed to do specific to vaccinations.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, you don`t have -- just to be clear, you don`t have a vaccination requirement in your school district?\par \par \uc2 CARTWRIGHT: No, we do not have the vaccination requirement at my school district at this time.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right. So, I mean, just to -- when we`re talking about sort of levels of mitigation, right, like one level would be having vaccinated teachers, hopefully, a lot of them are vaccinated. A second level would be masks. Why did you decide to require masks inside your schools, despite the fact that Governor said he would take away funding for your school district?\par \par \uc2 CARTWRIGHT: Absolutely. So, this decision was something that was very difficult for our school board to consider and to look into and to make a decision on. What happened is the numbers here in Broward County were we were starting to spike in our numbers, we continue to see spread that was happening throughout our county, we were now also starting to see where the hospitalization rates and the -- and the pediatric ICU beds were also increasing.\par \par \uc2 And so, whereas originally, you know, summertime, we were thinking we were going to be able to make it optional. It just got to the point where, unfortunately, the data for our county was very alarming and very concerning. We`re coming back to school, all back in-person. So, we knew that the mitigation strategy of being able to separate by six feet or three feet is just nearly impossible inside of the classroom.\par \par \uc2 So, with the positivity rate increasing and knowing that we needed to take as many mitigation factors into play as possible, that was why our board made that decision, and very difficult decision. I don`t want to go against the governor. But at the same time, they`re responsible -- their constitutional duties require for them to provide that safe environment here locally within Broward County.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What does it mean for the county in terms of -- I`m still a little unclear on what the actual stick the government --the governor is wielding against the school district.\par \par \uc2 CARTWRIGHT: So, what he did is actually right now we have two different sanctions that are in place. The first sanction that he is doing is withholding the school board members` salaries for each month that we are not in compliance. Now, and that`s important to also realize is that even though our board made this decision, we`re revisiting the decision after Labor Day.\par \par \uc2 So, we`re going to continue to monitor -- it`s that`s a decision that`s been made for the entire year. So, our school board members, prior to the commissioner coming out and saying that they were going to withhold their funds, they withhold -- on August 26, they withheld the funds. So, it was $35,000, a little bit over that, that they did, and withholding those funds.\par \par \uc2 And the other thing that they also put in sanctions for was with paperwork, as you might say. So, on a daily basis, every one of my schools, every one of them, that those principals at this point in time have to certify a report that basically goes into listing the names of students, the parents, information, the contact information for any student that we had to discipline or if a parent or the student themselves or a caregiver just report that the child has been harassed. And we`re doing that because even then the order they gave us was so vague.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, they`re punishing you with dumb paperwork for a masking requirement to try to protect the school. Has -- have parents been -- generally, how have parents been about this?\par \par \uc2 CARTWRIGHT: Overwhelmingly supportive. Everywhere I`m going, when I`m interacting with community members, when I`m interacting out at our school sites, of course, there are parents there as well. Every one of them, thank you. Thank you for protecting our children.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, mitigation efforts really work as a collective undertaking. That`s something that I think hasn`t really gotten through with some folks. Dr. Vickie Cartwright, thank you so much for coming on tonight. I really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 CARTWRIGHT: Absolutely. You have a wonderful evening and thank you for this opportunity.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You have a good evening, too.\par \par \uc2 That is ALL IN for this evening. "THE RACHEL MADDOW SHOW" starts right now. Good evening, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Chris. Thanks, my friend.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You bet.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: And thanks to you at home for joining us this hour. There are fires on the West Coast, there is the storm in the northeast, and the hurricane aftermath in Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020210903eh9200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for September 2, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Yasmin Vossoughian, Monica Alba, Claire McCaskill,  Gary Grumbach, Rich Lowry \par \uc2 8,860 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 September 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: Welcome to "Meet the Press Daily." I`m Chuck Todd.\par \par \uc2 We are following two historic stories that have broken within the last 24 hours, one of those stories, the ruling by the Supreme Court allowing what is apparently a near total ban on abortion in Texas to take effect.\par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 It`s a major clue that Roe`s days are likely numbered by this court and it could put the issue of abortion, front and center in every election for the foreseeable future, up and down the ballot. President buying is -- Biden is vowing a whole of the government effort to respond to this decision. We`re going to get to that in a moment. It`s been a big chunk of this show on it.\par \par \uc2 But we`re going to begin with the historic and deadly toll that the remnants of Hurricane Ida have taken on the Northeast. Just another reminder that we are living in the climate change nightmare many people warned about decades ago. The flooding in its aftermath has killed more than a dozen people including a 2-year-old boy in New York -- in New Jersey after dumping more than half a foot of rain on the region. Both states remain under a state of emergency as water rescues continue there right now.\par \par \uc2 More than three inches of rain fell each hour in Central Park, just an astonishing amount of water, breaking a record that was set just a week ago and severely limiting subway service throughout New York City. The fire department of New York City says it rescued hundreds of people from subways, buildings, and roadways as a result of this flash flooding.\par \par \uc2 In New Jersey, it wasn`t just flooding, but a tornado rare sighting there that caused widespread devastation. And this is what that tornado did to a neighborhood in Mullica Hill, New Jersey, where entire homes were ripped apart. And at least three people were killed in the Philadelphia area where water rescues are also continuing today.\par \par \uc2 Officials in Montgomery County, Pennsylvania just outside the city of Philadelphia say they`ve rescued about 500 people since yesterday. It`s more than three times as many rescues as any previous single storm. Remind you, this storm came ashore in the Gulf and had to work its way across land and this did amount of damage in the northeast folks.\par \par \uc2 Ida has now moved out of the United States, thankfully and it -- but it did cut a path of destruction from the southeast to the northeast, and includes Louisiana where 900,000 customers still have no power. OK, we`re going to be checking with the conditions there later this hour as well.\par \par \uc2 President Biden was set to visit Louisiana tomorrow to spoke about the devastation that Ida has caused in the northeast.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I want to express my heartfelt thanks to all the first responders and everyone who has been working through the night, and well into the morning to save lives and get power back. There`s a lot of damage, and I made clear to the governors that my team at the \uc2 Federal Emergency Management Agency\uc2 , FEMA is on the ground and ready to provide all the assistance as needed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Our own Yasmin Vossoughian has been assessing the damage in a Mamaroneck, New York in Westchester County. She joins me now along with our own Monica Alba at the White House.\par \par \uc2 You know, Yasmin, one of the issues as climate change fuels the stronger storms is a -- is about whether we have the infrastructure to handle the dilush of water that comes now with these storms, and I take it that`s what we`re learning in the northeast that our -- that the infrastructure may not be able to handle just in, you know, monster storms that drop rain at the rate this one did.\par \par \uc2 YASMIN VOSSOUGHIAN, MSNBC ANCHOR: It`s interesting you bring that up, Chuck because it seems like we just don`t. I mean, we got the answer last night, right? Speaking of folks in New York City, which is where I live, a lot of building folks, architects said New York`s just not built to withstand the storms like we`ve had over the last summer really.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this was the third kind of rain blowout that we`ve had this summer but this one being the worst. And the buildings, the infrastructure, the areas the restaurants, the commercial areas, they`re just not built to withstand this type of rain and this type of flooding. And I think the images here right, Chuck, they really tell the story especially what`s happened here in Westchester County which is where I am.\par \par \uc2 Now, I want to take you inside, Chuck, somebody`s home who so graciously allowed us to enter, and it`s incredibly devastating to see -- I`m going to put my mask on because I`m going into your -- indoors, and let`s walk slowly because I don`t want JP to trip, but you see this house in here Chuck, come on with me JP, and it is completely and utterly destroyed, and the line of debris that you see along -- I`m going to get to a lighter area so my camera guy can get a better view for you guys. The line of debris up here on the wall shows you just how high this water line got. So, it`s a little bit shorter than I am here.\par \par \uc2 So, this is a first floor apartment. Show the kitchen here JP. Look at this. Imagine this is your house, Chuck, and now completely done, completely destroyed. Think about how emotional this is for this family of four a mother, a father, two young children, a 4-year-old, and a 5-month- old. They woke up at 1:00 in the morning -- let`s walk a little bit this way. Here`s a bedroom over here, just gone. They woke up at 1:00 this morning, the mom told me she got a phone call from a friend. She woke up, put her feet on the ground, and the water was up to her knees.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Oh, my goodness.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: And she was sleeping in the same room as her 4-year-old child, and her -- and her 5-month-old baby, and she grabbed her kids and thankfully, JP let`s show this area where they were sleeping, was able to get out in the nick of time because as we will know by this point from the other live shots that I`ve been doing throughout the day leading up to you, the water got even higher than the water line in this apartment.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:07]\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: The water actually reached around 11 feet eventually. So, this entire floor would have been completely overtaken. They were able to get out in time before anything more devastating happened. And we know obviously now that a lot of folks lost their lives overnight because of this incredible storm.\par \par \uc2 And I think it begs to ask the question that you very much was asking about -- about climate change, and whether or not we have the infrastructure to withstand, right, these types of storms? And it seems like more and more often, especially in the northeast, we`re seeing and, obviously with what we`re seeing in the south, that we don`t, right?\par \par \uc2 We don`t have the infrastructure to withstand it. And it seems like every storm that comes past us, it is the worst of the worst. So, I think a lot of folks are saying, what`s next? And how do we rebuild from this? Because seeing this as your reality is really devastating.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And we have no idea whether that house to leave and be inhabitable because people forget, with water, comes mold, and it may be that you cannot have anybody living in those places. So, this is -- even when the water recedes, it could be devastating. On this, Yasmin, terrific reporting. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: And this is --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: -- And I don`t mean to cut you off. It`s -- so the people that have this home, this is a rental property, right, so they have to call the owners who then have to file for an insurance claim. Luckily, their cousin lives up doors, but it`s kind of this waterfall effect, right? One thing after another -- domino effect, I should say. One thing after another. It`s just not that easy to pick up your life after something like this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And if anybody, and I`m one of those who thought they had home owner`s insurance that covered water events, a lot of people are going to find out sadly the hard way that their home owner`s insurance does not cover rain events like this, and this is going to be a financial catastrophe for quite a few people as well. Anyway, Yasmin, on top of the story, I`m glad you`re getting it personalized for people so they can understand the individual devastation this stuff causes. Yasmin, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Monica, I don`t want to go -- I want to go over to you, and about the President. He`s going to go to Louisiana, but in some ways, he mentioned the fires, we see what I did in the northeast. We know that they -- look he is talked about trying to infuse all domestic policy, particularly spending policy with a climate -- with a -- with the mitigation of climate change and dealing with it as sort of a central focus. How do they go about that?\par \par \uc2 MONICA ALBA, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Precisely, Chuck. The President framing this as a climate crisis from coast to coast, talking about the devastation out west and of course, where Ida first hit in the Gulf Coast, and then as it traveled up all the way into the Northeast, causing that catastrophic flooding that we were just seeing there with Yasmin`s reporting.\par \par \uc2 And the President making the point there that this is not political. He says when it comes to these major weather events, the storms don`t care whether you`re a Democrat or Republican, whether you live in a rural or an urban area. He said just earlier this hour talking about this and the impact all over the country, but he did take the opportunity to make it a little bit political in terms of pushing his domestic agenda.\par \par \uc2 He made the case for infrastructure, now more than ever. Again, touting his bill back better plan, saying when Congress comes back, he would like them to vote on this extremely quickly because people are seeing the need for themselves.\par \par \uc2 So, he did, in one track, talk about that. But in another, he has been talking about how the federal government is equipped to deal with things like this and respond immediately, not just with FEMA, which we`ve seen already, of course, on the ground in Louisiana and Mississippi, but also things like the Department of Housing and Urban Development helping with the critical assistance of many people who have now been displaced and will have these issues.\par \par \uc2 So, he wanted to talk about all of this, but he did really broaden it out and make it a big picture pitch for what he wants to accomplish next, but you`re right, all of these issues are really intertwined. He`s talking about not just the climate challenges, but everything else in terms of infrastructure as well, and what he calls the desperate need for it and the desperate need to act now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, there`s no doubt. Our infrastructure, whether in New York, California, Louisiana, Florida, is not ready for the weather events of the 21st century. Anyway, Monica Alba at the White House for us. Monica, thank you. We`re going to keep a close eye on the emergency situation we`re seeing throughout the Northeast. All hour, we will bring you any news as it develops. Meanwhile, we`re going to turn to the day`s top political story, which in and of itself is a pretty big story.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court last night announced it would not block a new law that effectively bans abortion in Texas, though no one`s quite sure how this law is going to actually be enforced. President Biden slammed the court`s decision this morning saying it would unleash unconstitutional chaos, and he`s vowing what he calls a whole of the government effort to respond to this.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:01]\par \par \uc2 TODD: It was a five to four vote. The Justices last night denied an emergency request by women`s health providers to halt a state law in Texas that prohibits abortions after six weeks. This new law also incentivizes members of the public to police the ban. Citizens would be entitled to a bounty I guess, of $10,000 if they want a lawsuit against anyone who aids or abets an abortion after six weeks.\par \par \uc2 So, to be clear, this law was written as a way to essentially have a total ban on abortion. Many women do not even know if they are pregnant at six weeks. And the law offers zero exceptions for rape or incest. To say the court`s decision to let the law take effect is huge would be an understatement because the court appears to be signaling that it`s not a matter of if, but when the actual landmark abortion case Roe v. Wade, will be overturned officially. Most folks think yes, last night to decision effectively already overturns it. It also opens up the Pandora`s box of questions legally, culturally, and politically.\par \par \uc2 Because we`re pretty divided on this issue, but we`re pretty divided. There`s no clear answer to that. Look at these new numbers from our NBC News poll, just 8 percent of adults say abortion should be illegal without any exception. So, just 8 percent of the country agrees with this Texas law. The slights of America which support severe restrictions are older, conservative, rural Americans, very much in the minority.\par \par \uc2 Democrats and independents say abortion should be legal by sizable margins, and urban and suburban Americans say it should be legal by even bigger margins than that. Then you start to look at it by age, and there`s a giant age gap here. Young voters big time, even among -- but among 65 and over, views are more split down the middle issue. We`ll see.\par \par \uc2 So, we`re going to dive into what all this means for the broader political landscape. And I want to first dive into this specific situation in Texas, and the White House`s response. So, joining me now is the Executive Editor of the Texas Tribune, Ross Ramsey.\par \par \uc2 So, Ross, let`s just start with a simple question. This is now the law of the land. In Texas, you know, the law of the state -- of the state land there. What -- how does this get enforced? Who`s in charge of enforcing this law?\par \par \uc2 ROSS RAMSEY, EXECUTIVE EDITOR, TEXAS TRIBUNE: Well, they wrote this law to stump the courts. They are not going to put the state or any part of the state, local prosecutors, or anything like that, in the position of enforcing this. Instead, as you mentioned in your lead-in, their crowdsourcing the enforcement. They`re allowing people to go to civil means to go after anyone who aids or abet someone seeking an abortion. You can`t sue the person getting or seeking the abortion, but you can sue the doctor, you can sue the Uber driver, you can sue anybody who helps pay for it. All of those kinds of things. And the thought was that they can accomplish a ban on abortion and not put the state in the position of being sued.\par \par \uc2 When it went to the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 , the court in effect was stumped by this. That the dissents from foreign judges putting the Chief Justice said, you know you`re doing an unconstitutional thing, but you`ve confounded the court with this mechanism that you`ve got. It raises a ton of questions.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Explain the $10,000. Is it a reward? Is that a bounty? I don`t mean to -- I mean, it`s just bizarre. Why -- why would somebody who decides to take it into their own hands to stop and to, you know, help identify an illegally done abortion, according to Texas law. Why should they get rewarded with $10,000? What`s the incentive? What`s the matter?\par \par \uc2 RAMSEY: The authors of this said basically, that you still have to go through all the civil procedures and all of those things, but you have to - - and when you get to the end, if you`re correct and the court decides with you, then you can -- the court can order that your lawyer fees are paid, and up to $10,000 be paid you basically as a bounty, they didn`t call it a bounty. It`s certainly become the bounty hunting bill, you know, rhetorically in Texas now. And that`s effectively how it works.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, is there any, I mean, you look at the polling on this, and there is not support for this extreme of a measure. Most of the polling whether it`s in Texas, or nationally, as we just showed, you know, you have minorities who want either no exceptions, and all legal or everything illegal, but a majority of the country`s somewhere in the middle, right? Either those want to see legal with some restrictions or some illegal but with some exceptions. Politically, what are some of the more moderate suburban Republicans thinking with this?\par \par \uc2 RAMSEY: Well, you know, all of the Republicans in the legislature voted for this. They were unanimous. Two Democrats voted for it, but the rest of the Democrats didn`t. It`s effectively a Republican state and has been, you know, in the legislature since the early part of the century and in statewide elections, you know, for more than 25 years. There`s not an effective opposition and that nothing here really moderated. When you put something like this on the floor of the Texas House, or on the floor of the Texas Senate, the Republicans feel compelled to stick with it or they`re going to get -- or they think they`re going to get beat by conservatives in their own primaries.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:07]\par \par \uc2 TODD: What are the abortion clinics in Texas planned to do? Do they want to try to sort of challenge the law and sort of dare people to stop them believing that this is unconstitutional nationally, or are they going to try to work within the confines of the -- of the law as it`s written right now?\par \par \uc2 RAMSEY: I think that`s still taking form. The first line of defense for those guys obviously was to go to the Supreme Court, that didn`t work. Although there are still -- there`s still a lot of litigation pending. The court didn`t actually rule on the merits of the law, they just decided not to stop it. So, this has been in effect since Wednesday morning in Texas, September 1 is when the law took effect.\par \par \uc2 And there`s a combination of, you know, a lot of people are really surprised that it went this way that the court didn`t stop it. A lot of people on both sides of this argument though, probably the courts going to stop it and we`re going to litigate this for a while, now we`re in a different situation where this is in effect, and the clinics, and things, I believe there are 24 abortion providers locations left in the state of Texas, and they basically started shutting the doors at midnight, you know, the night before last.\par \par \uc2 So, at the moment, unless you are less than six weeks pregnant, you can`t get an abortion in Texas. They tell us that`s about 85 or 90 percent of cases.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Let me ask you this, where did this idea of using the public and creating a case -- a crowdsourcing bounty incentive on this? Is this modeled after some other piece of legislation we`ve never heard of on another issue? It seems to be -- certainly as the courts have pointed out a very unique and unusual way to try to get around the constitutionality or unconstitutionality of a law.\par \par \uc2 RAMSEY: Well, I think this is new. I think that`s why it confounded the courts in the way that it did. Ordinarily, in a case like this, you would say, you know, the state of Texas is going to enforce this new law, and if you don`t like the new law, you sue the state of Texas as the actor in this thing. Since you don`t have an actor, you don`t have executive or government authority, they basically crowdsource it, and until someone actually files a suit, you don`t have a test case.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s like everything with the abortion debate. At the end of the day, nobody wants to take ownership of some of these laws, they kind of want to work around the system. Anyway, Ross Ramsey, the Executive Editor of The Texas Tribune. Ross, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next. Just when you thought that the politics in the country couldn`t get more polarized, let`s throw abortion on top of the pile. Talk about away an issue that could upend our politics in 22 and 24, we`ll talk about it.\par \par \uc2 And later, an update from a leading health official in Louisiana as the storm leaves nearly a million residents across the state in the dark. That`s putting hospitals that are already grappling with the COVID surge in an increasingly dire situation.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:21:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. As we said, the Supreme Court`s decision to let Texas`s nearly total ban on abortion to go into effect could have a huge impact on our politics going forward at every level of government. Everybody`s going to have to actually take a position on this issue in a more detailed way than ever before.\par \par \uc2 And let`s take a look at where the public`s view of the issue is from our 2020 exit polls in Texas. We`ll walk you through, so it gives you a sense, right? You wouldn`t -- you`re going to see some numbers between the majority of state wants a legal abortion, legal versus illegal. But we do give a sort of categories of folks, right?\par \par \uc2 In Texas, just 18 percent believe abortion should be legal, under just about any circumstance, then you have another 30 percent legal most of the time saying with the exception of maybe no late-term abortion, things like that, then you have a group of 31 percent that wants abortion illegal, but with some exceptions, life of the mother, things like that, and then of course, it`s 15 percent illegal with most exceptions. As you could see in Texas, a majority are sort of in between the 35-yard lines here, on abortion, they`re not for either totally legal or totally illegal.\par \par \uc2 Now, let`s look at some of the swing states here that are both up and 22 and 24. Georgia, Pennsylvania, Wisconsin, Arizona got a huge Senate race here. And essentially the breakdown here, majorities in all these states -- the majority for the legal, large minority so, -- in wanting to see abortion mostly illegal. And again, it`s the extremes of the smaller numbers here. It`s in the 30 percent in each that want either some exceptions, if you make it illegal, or some other exemptions if you decide to make it all legal.\par \par \uc2 And as you can see here, three other states, we`re going to be keeping an eye on this year, Ohio, Texas, and Iowa, both are a little more Republican in states but as you can see, there`s a narrow sort of plurality in two and majority in one here, where the belief is that abortion should be more legal than illegal.\par \par \uc2 So, let`s talk about the politics of this. Joining me now, former Missouri Democratic Senator Claire McCaskill, she`s also an MSNBC Political Analyst, and National Review Editor, Rich Lowry, also an MSNBC political analyst.\par \par \uc2 So, geez, I don`t know if we can -- our polarized political electorate can get more polarized and throw another topic, but this is sort of an old- school polarization here. And Claire, let me start with you. You come from a state that I would argue sort of -- is also in this sort of, most people are within the 30-yard lines on abortion, right? Either they want it to be legal with some exceptions, or illegal with some exemptions here, where`s this headed in your view politically?\par \par \uc2 CLAIRE MCCASKILL, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I think Texas has ignited the vast majority of Americans in this country with what they have done. Let`s be clear here. This isn`t about polarization as it relates to the Texas law. This is about that 15 percent that wants abortion illegal in all cases, including a young girl who has been repeatedly raped by her father, who has no idea she`s pregnant until after six weeks.\par \par \uc2 And in Texas, they have put out private bounty hunters for her. I mean, that is beyond the pale extreme. I bet if you took a poll on that factual scenario, Chuck, it would be 90-10, that that is wrong, and that`s what the Supreme Court said was OK, last night. I think this will have major repercussions in our elections, and it should.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Rich, do you think this is a, you know, like an any of our social issues, the party that overreaches gets punished. Is this an overreach?\par \par \uc2 RICH LOWRY, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, first of all, people are acting as though Roe has been overturned, I would welcome that outcome and it may eventually be in this dobs case, but it hasn`t been overturned yet at all. This is a procedural ruling that clearly was the correct one, there was no harm here. Yet, the eight defendants had done nothing, even in joining them if that`s what happened wouldn`t have stopped this law because they hadn`t -- their government officials have nothing to do with the enforcement of the law.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:11]\par \par \uc2 LOWRY: And I tip my hat, pro-lifers in Texas who -- I don`t think anyone should have to find a way around Roe, I think Roe`s a bad law and should be tossed. But in that environment, they have found a way to avoid injunctions on what they want to do, and to find a way to restrict abortion working around Roe, but ideally, Roe goes, and then you have democratic deliberation around the country.\par \par \uc2 Some states, it`ll be entirely legal, some states it`ll be illegal, most states will be somewhere in between and the public venue question, Chuck is, what`s the -- what do people think of when I say most of the time with some exceptions? I don`t think that`s been fully probed. I don`t think people really thought that through, and it posts Roe environment, they`d have to.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I wanted those -- to drill down on something though. You`re comfortable with this sort of empowering the public to be some sort of bounty police force here. I mean, it`s sort of it, this is an odd -- an oddly written law. I mean, imagine if this were done with other issues when it comes to gun restrictions and things like this, I mean, this feels like turning the public against each other.\par \par \uc2 LOWRY: Yes. There`s some precedent for its -- obviously entirely different realm, but my understanding like the False Claims Act has kind of a provision like this. But if Roe weren`t in the way, if Roe weren`t blocking any significant expression of pro-life sentiment in Texas, or in countries around -- the states around the country, you know, you wouldn`t do it this way, you just flatly restrict abortion. So, it`s a workaround, but it`s caused by extortion from the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 There`s no right to abortion in the Constitution, the left is just used to using that spurious decision as a way to block democratic deliberation and action on this. And I don`t want the roads going to go or not, I think they`ll certainly be eroded, but I think Democrats going to have to use -- get used to winning this in the small D democratic arena, rather than just running to the courts.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Claire, is the answer, try to get Roe codified in Congress?\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: I just got to tell you my heart is beating so fast right now. I -- it is very hard for me to stay calm. To equate the False Claims Act was setting extreme anti-abortion factions after young girls who have been raped because they didn`t know they were pregnant until after six weeks, I find outrageous.\par \par \uc2 And this is not a workaround. It shouldn`t be a tip of the hat, it should be condemnation, that they are trying to take 50 years of Supreme Court precedent, and throw it out by creating a private police that can invade women`s life at the most personal, private, and difficult moment they ever face, and many of those women are very, very young.\par \par \uc2 I want bounty hunters after the people who are raping their children. I want bounty hunters after them. If this is the way we`re going to -- road we`re going to go down in America, that we`re going to hire private police in order to avoid constitutional precedent, I am so tired of conservatives talking about the value of precedent until they don`t. And I mean, really, I`m sorry to get so upset, Rich`s my friend, but he is so flat wrong here about the impact this will have on women in this country.\par \par \uc2 LOWRY: Well, it`s -- I mean, the bottom -- the crux of the issue is, is abortion immoral, wrong, or not? And if you believe the destruction of innocent life in any circumstance is wrong, well, then you have a different conclusion on this.\par \par \uc2 And I think kind of all due respect, you`re letting the rhetoric get out of -- out of hand. Bounty hunters, and private police, that`s not what this is, is at all. And of course, rape is illegal, and if that`s enforced in Texas, and forced every state around the country, but Roe, there is no right to abortion in the constitution, there just isn`t. Roe has been used to block democratic action on abortion.\par \par \uc2 Let`s have a debate. We`re having a debate right now, and then state legislature should be able to decide it`s going to be -- abortion is going to be illegal, in some states post-Roe, it`s going to be in between in others, be fully legal in others.\par \par \uc2 But if this -- if this Roe had never been on the books, this debate would have happened long ago, and there would have been a kind of equilibrium, probably wouldn`t have been to my liking, ultimately, but would have allowed more small d democratic debate and a democratic voice on this, which has been denied, totally denied illegitimately by the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Claire?\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Well, I -- this is an outrageous workaround. I mean, just read Sonia Sotomayor`s dissent that four Justices agreed with. This was a way -- this was a trick, this was trying to take it out of the state`s hands, so somehow they could go rule 50 years of constitutional precedent. I can`t wait for Kavanaugh`s hearing where he went on and on about respect for precedent. They blew up precedent on a religious freedom case just a few months ago.\par \par \uc2 This is all about situational precedent for the far right. They have gone too far on this. There will be huge political ramifications for private bounty police going after women who want to terminate a pregnancy as soon as they find out they`re pregnant, which many times is after six weeks.\par \par \uc2 LOWRY: But precedent is not the ultimate value, whether the constitutional -- the decision is actually constitutionally sound or not, and Roe just isn`t. It`s just made up. I know people agree with the policy, that`s fine, that`s their right, but the Supreme Court shouldn`t illegitimately with no constitutional warrant keep this debate from happening. And if Roe weren`t on the books, Texas would just have a restriction on these abortions. It`d be much simpler.\par \par \uc2 But it`s just a bad Supreme Court decision. That`s the problem. I hope the court overturns it, not sure it will, but it`s certainly going to be eroded and that`s a (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 TODD: Claire, before we get to the Supreme Court and that decision and whether Roe stays in name only or not, I think that this decision shows it`s been already eroded, what would you be advising -- right now Democrats have control of Congress. You try to codify it.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Well, I think they will try to codify it. And I think it will be a tough vote for many Republicans that are in those states that you just put up, Chuck. I mean, this is -- the extreme position that they have taken in Texas, that they`re hanging their hat on, is not supported by America. It`s not supported by people in Wisconsin or Ohio or Pennsylvania or Georgia or any of the battleground states, or suburban districts that will be the battleground in House races.\par \par \uc2 I am not willing to say this was a good thing in any way because it`s a terrible thing. But there will be political benefits to Democrats as a result of what the Supreme Court did late last night.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, one thing it`s going to do --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 LOWRY: Codify really have to overturn the filibuster and over the filibuster to codify Roe is not -- would not turn out well for the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Here`s what`s true on this debate even though we`re not resolving it now. There`s a lot of people who have been lawmakers on a state legislative level, congressional level, who have been able to duck specifics on their abortion position. It is now -- because this is going to be state by state, everybody is going to -- it is going to test everybody on some specifics when it comes to abortion and then I think we will find out really on the details where this is headed. But it`s going to be a political earthquake I think.\par \par \uc2 Anyway. Claire and Rich, thank you both.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we`re going to talk to one of the nation`s top observers of the Supreme Court about what last night`s move means as the high court gets ready to hear the case that could overturn Roe.\par \par \uc2 Speaking of something in name only, is John Roberts in charge of this court now in name only?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:36:42]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. As we said, the Supreme Court`s decision not to block the Texas` restrictive abortion law came in a 5-4 vote last night. In her dissent, Justice Sonya Sotomayor called the law, quote, "a breathtaking act of defiance of the Constitution, of this court`s precedents, and of the rights of women seeking abortions throughout Texas." All of this comes just a month before the court is set to hear arguments on an abortion case out of Mississippi in a new law there.\par \par \uc2 So to break down the legal implications of this decision by this court, I`m joined by "The Washington Post" Supreme Court reporter, Robert Barnes.\par \par \uc2 And Robert, I think to me the big news here, and we sort of saw this development happening over the last year in the last term of the court, John Roberts may be the chief justice but he doesn`t control this court any more, does he?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT BARNES, THE WASHINGTON POST SUPREME COURT REPORTER: You`re right about that. The chief justice sort of offered up a compromise to say let`s keep the law from going into effect while we look at the unique and, as he called them, unprecedented issues that it raises. And only two other justices went along with that. None of his fellow conservatives did.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So reading the tea leaves now, this is in Brett Kavanaugh`s hands. And listen, at least on this issue, this is Brett Kavanaugh`s court. And I guess in some ways he truly has replaced \uc2 Anthony Kennedy\uc2  as far as being the swing justice here.\par \par \uc2 BARNES: Could be. And also could be Justice Barrett. We don`t know yet enough about her inclinations and jurisprudence. She`s only been there for a short time. But I think it`s clear that the two of them are probably more in the middle of the court as the conservatives go than the other three conservative justices.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So the decision by this court to let this go, I mean, why shouldn`t we look at it as well, we know where Mississippi is headed.\par \par \uc2 BARNES: Well, it didn`t present a constitutional issue. It did in a way but that`s not what the court was deciding. The court was deciding these procedural issues, and whether or not to stop the law before it went into effect or not. The case that they`re going to take up in the fall, Mississippi`s 15-week ban on abortion directly challenges "Roe v. Wade." And so that`s going to be a case where the court is sort of directly presented with that question, and we`ll find out whether or not the court is willing to go along with that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is it likely here so that this Texas law then, even if Roe is overturned by Mississippi, this Texas law still could have problems constitutionally when it comes before the court?\par \par \uc2 BARNES: Perhaps. But I think if the court overturns Roe, that really throws the issue of abortion back to the state legislatures the way it was almost 50 years ago and the state legislatures will have a really freehand to do what it wants on abortion to the extent even of not offering it or allowing it in the state. And so we will move back to a sort of patch work state-by- state system and not the national guarantee of abortion rights that Roe provided.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:10]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is this 5-4 makeup we saw last night? Is this the new split with Roberts sort of, you know, then the middle here?\par \par \uc2 BARNES: I think it is on some questions. You know, neither all of the conservatives nor all of the liberals think alike on everything, and so there are some issues, voting rights, for instance, in which you have seen the six conservatives very much on the same page. And so in questions like that, they`ll all go one way. On other issues, though, it is more divided and there is more of a role for the chief justice and perhaps Justice Kavanaugh, perhaps Justice Barrett to play that sort of middle position.\par \par \uc2 TODD: He`s put this off, it feels like, for years. You know, he never says this, and I mean he being John Roberts. But it always seems as if he is very mindful that he doesn`t want to put the Supreme Court in the middle of a political fight. Well, we`re here now and the Supreme Court is going to get polarized publicly.\par \par \uc2 It is, believe it or not, even though in Washington people have opinions about where the court is, the public has been fairly split on its rulings over the last few years, fairly pleased with it. It feels like this is going to polarize now the court the same way everything else has been polarized.\par \par \uc2 BARNES: Yes, this is a big issue. I mean, you know, if you look at Supreme Court terms, you often have, you know, the liberals win a few, the conservatives win a few, sometimes those are very narrow wins that sort of put off the big question but, you know, in the public`s mind it looks like it`s kind of evening out, and I think that that`s what the public has reacted to.\par \par \uc2 The public does hold the court in fairly high esteem, certainly better than Congress, about even with what it thinks about the executive branch, and I think it is that sort of back and forth, one side wins one, one side wins another that keeps the public thinking that, you know, perhaps the court is sort of somewhere in the middle where they like it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And going back to the conservative five that basically decided to let this law stand, is there anybody there that we`re just -- we`re over- assuming what their position is going to be other than the two we named, which is Kavanaugh and Barrett?\par \par \uc2 BARNES: No, I don`t think so. Justice Gorsuch has supplied, you know, at least one surprise since he`s been on the court about the rights of gay and transgender employees, and so, you know, that one was one that didn`t turn out the way it was sort of thought that it might. You know, sometimes the liberals join the conservatives in a religious case that you would think that there would be a divide on. But yes, it`s -- you know, the splits are real.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Robert Barnes, the Supreme Court reporter for "The Washington Post," always an important column that I read when you write about the court.\par \par \uc2 Robert, thanks very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 BARNES: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Up next, we got a live report from Philadelphia where water rescues have been happening all day after this historic downpour and flooding. Plus another severe weather update this time from the Gulf Coast as hundreds of thousands of Louisiana residents are still waiting for the lights to get turned back on. And guess what, they may have to wait a month.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:46:56]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. We`re going to continue to track the rescues that have been taking place across the northeast right now. This is some drone video that we just got into our newsroom. This is from Bridgeport, Pennsylvania, within Montgomery County which of course is a suburban county outside Philadelphia. Montgomery County officials tell us that at least three people there have been killed from the remnants of Hurricane Ida. Crews have also rescued about 500 people since yesterday.\par \par \uc2 I mean, folks, you don`t see water like this in Philadelphia. By the way, the previous record from a single storm was 135.\par \par \uc2 Our own Gary Grumbach is at the center -- is in Center City right now in Philadelphia and he joins me now.\par \par \uc2 And Gary, again, Philadelphia, we just -- this is -- you look here and you think, oh, is this the Cajun Navy down in Louisiana? You hate to say we`re used to that. We`re not used to this.\par \par \uc2 GARY GRUMBACH, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey there, Chuck. Yes, we are at 23rd and Orange in Center City, Philadelphia. And as you can see behind me there are feet of water. We see the green garbage can, that`s about halfway up the green garbage can and further along is a giant supermarket. And further beyond that, you can see cars actually submerged in there.\par \par \uc2 Officials here in Philadelphia are calling this an active situation. And that`s exactly what this is because the banks are still rising. The water of the Schuylkill River which is about a block away from here is still rising. And it`s not supposed to crest for another few hours this afternoon. So officials say they`ve been doing water rescues, as you mentioned, throughout the morning, throughout the afternoon. And they`re going to continue until they get everyone they need to get out.\par \par \uc2 I just talked to a neighbor here who was right next to this live shot. And she said she has friends that live above the giant behind me, these friends that are trying to get out of that building, they can`t without water rescues. We`ve seen a number of military-style looking high water vehicles driving back and forth on Orange, trying to get people in and out of the situation.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, today Philadelphia public schools did not close. That was causing consternation among some of the parents that thought it should.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GRUMBACH: There`s no decision on schools tomorrow yet in Philadelphia. But we do have 50,000 people here in the city without power.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, I was just going to ask you about the power situation and then drinking water. A lot of times int these flooding situations, is the drinking water still good to go?\par \par \uc2 GRUMBACH: Right now we have no word on the drinking water. They`re more concerned about just getting the people out that need to get rescued from these cars, from these first level apartments, first -- you know, street level apartments. So no word on the drinking yet. But we do know that there is no injuries and no casualties that they know of, that the emergency office here in Philadelphia knows of yet.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, that is good news.\par \par \uc2 Gary Grumbach, in Philadelphia for us, or outside of Philadelphia, you know, actually right in downtown. Gary, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And boy, those -- whoever the school administrator was that made that decision, you never get questioned for closing. You always get questioned when you don`t.\par \par \uc2 All right, in Louisiana, more than 900,000 residents were still without power this morning as the Gulf region continues to reel from Ida`s aftermath. New Orleans Mayor LaToya Cantrell acknowledged that some progress was made yesterday in restoring power for eastern part of the city, but boy, it is coming out in a trickle.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:01]\par \par \uc2 And then officials in neighboring St. Charles Parish warned residents to expect at least a month without power. 80 percent of those in the dark get their electricity from the state`s largest utility company \uc2 Entergy\uc2 , and in a tweet yesterday, \uc2 Entergy\uc2  said getting hospitals back online is one of the company`s top priorities, while Louisiana hospitals were of course already strained under a surge of COVID patients even before Hurricane Ida tore through the state, forcing hard hit hospitals to have to evacuate hundreds of patients and run on backup generators, and it was hard to find other hospitals that had room to take up some of these patients.\par \par \uc2 Well, the chief medical officer of one of Louisiana`s largest hospital systems, Ochsner Health, joins me now.\par \par \uc2 So let me ask you about the impact from Ida here. First of all, how is the power situation for hospitals in the region?\par \par \uc2 DR. ROBERT HART, EXECUTIVE VICE PRESIDENT, OCHSNER HEALTH: Thank you. The power is getting better. \uc2 Entergy\uc2  has been working to get some of the major feeds, the major power feeds to certainly the central facilities in the area, So we are beginning to have more and more facilities come on power. Our larger facility here in New Orleans got power earlier this morning. We were able to use that.\par \par \uc2 We still have a couple of facilities that are the down, that are running on generator, but the generators are plugging along and keeping things running at those facilities.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you have limitations on what patients you can treat right now?\par \par \uc2 HART: So the limitations right now in many of the facilities are just a matter of staffing in the hospital. Our emergency departments are open and running, and of course, all of the routine types of care that come in on an auto basis, but the things that come in after a storm like this, all the cuts and falls and broken bruises, that sort of thing.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The staffing situation I know it`s the same issue that many hospitals are dealing with. How acute is this, and how concerned are you that this is going to continue to impact care?\par \par \uc2 HART: So the staffing situation is certainly acute, and it`s been a problem now for months as we had experienced that fourth COVID surge. Finding enough staff to take care of all the patients is certainly difficult.\par \par \uc2 We`re are relying on redeploying a lot of people in our facilities, to different areas. But we had -- a week ago, we had over 900 patients in our hospitals just with COVID. Thank goodness, as this storm hit, we were down, and now we`re down 600, so that frees up some bed but it is still very difficult to find the staffing to manage all of the patients right now with COVID and with the other illnesses that we`re trying to take care of.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And I`m curious, Dr. Hart, given -- you just gave us a sort of patient situation there. Do you -- is there more evidence that tells you that maybe Louisiana is at least finally plateauing here with this fourth surge?\par \par \uc2 HART: So, definitely, we`ve seen those numbers come down from the fourth surge where we had highs above 1,000 at one point in our facility. So down below 700, in the 600 range, great. My big concern, though, with this storm is that there`s going to be a lot of people who traveled, who evacuated, who are in close quarters.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 HART: And now they begin coming back as we get power and worry about another little spike perhaps in our COVID numbers.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Dr. Hart, the executive vice president of Ochsner medical facility there in Louisiana, appreciate you coming on and letting us know how things are going there. Good luck.\par \par \uc2 HART: Certainly. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And one more extreme weather update today, on the West Coast, we have thousands of acres are still on fire. California`s massive Caldor Fire is blazing towards Lake Tahoe. It`s now forcing thousands of residents and tourists to evacuate the popular region to escape an inferno that has already incinerated more than 200,000 acres. There was some hope that they could avoid this.\par \par \uc2 Nevada`s governor already declared a state of emergency as Caldor`s flames encroach on the state`s border there. As of this morning, though, it was only just 25 percent contained. And just a few miles east, a smaller, newer, but a bit more controlled wildfire called Tamarack is now burning. Firefighters are attempting to merge the two, believe it or not, because it`s an effort to get one wildfire under control.\par \par \uc2 All right. Tough day, as we have been dealing with a ton of problems in this country, let`s hope we can find the leadership to deal with it.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:01]\par \par \uc2 That does it for us this hour. We`ll be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. MSNBC`s coverage will continue with Geoff Bennett right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT, MSNBC HOST: And it is good to be with you on this Thursday. I`m Geoff Bennett, and we start with a 1 in 500-year weather event from Maryland to Massachusetts. Far more catastrophic than expected, catching much of the northeast off guard.\par \par \uc2 Officials say at least 25 people have died and a massive search and rescue mission is under way.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020210902eh9200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE RACHEL MADDOW SHOW for September 1, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Rachel Maddow \par \uc2 7,889 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 September 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Rachel Maddow Show\par \uc2 TRMADS\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Conten